{'copyright': 'Copyright (c) 2020 The New York Times Company. All Rights Reserved.', 'response': {'docs': [{'abstract': 'A Chinese-language paper ponders the future of the city comptroller, a Jewish paper explores a kosher-food controversy, and a Spanish-language paper writes about the Latino birth rate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/02/nyregion/02world.html', 'snippet': 'A Chinese-language paper ponders the future of the city comptroller, a Jewish paper explores a kosher-food controversy, and a Spanish-language paper writes about the Latino birth rate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the past month, a Jewish newspaper explored a kosher-food controversy; a Chinese-language newspaper pondered the political fate of the city comptroller, John C. Liu, the first Asian-American to hold a major citywide office; and a columnist for a Spanish-language newspaper asked a provocative question about the Latino birth rate. SAM DOLNICK', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02WORLD2/02WORLD2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 949, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02WORLD2/02WORLD2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02WORLD2/02WORLD2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Excerpts From the Ethnic Press', 'kicker': 'New York World', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Excerpts From the Ethnic Press', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Spanish Language', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chinese Language', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-01T00:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Dolnick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dolnick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Text', '_id': 'nyt://article/21a0f2b9-8868-5d82-92db-177d40e8f046', 'word_count': 749, 'uri': 'nyt://article/21a0f2b9-8868-5d82-92db-177d40e8f046'}, {'abstract': 'The baker’s weekend routine may involve a swing by the local Dunkin’ Donuts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/02/nyregion/02routine.html', 'snippet': 'The baker’s weekend routine may involve a swing by the local Dunkin’ Donuts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bartolo Valastro, universally called Buddy, is a fourth-generation Italian baker whose Hoboken storefront, Carlo’s, uses 1,000 pounds of sugar each week to produce 500 birthday cakes, 50 or 60 wedding cakes and the occasional life-size racecar framed in fondant. He stars as the “Cake Boss” on TLC and in a new spinoff, “Next Great Baker.” Mr. Valastro, 33, also published a memoir studded with recipes, in November. He lives in East Hanover, N.J., with his wife, Lisa, and their children, 7-year-old Sophia; 6-year-old Buddy; and Marco, who is 3 (a fourth is due in February). JODI RUDOREN ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02ROUTINE2/02ROUTINE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02ROUTINE2/02ROUTINE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02ROUTINE2/02ROUTINE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02ROUTINE2/02ROUTINE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Time for Gravy and Football', 'kicker': 'Sunday Routine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Time for Gravy and Football', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hoboken (NJ)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Valastro, Buddy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bakeries and Baked Products', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-01T00:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jodi Rudoren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jodi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rudoren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/35555864-6747-5097-a010-b20d1c4c7d88', 'word_count': 612, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35555864-6747-5097-a010-b20d1c4c7d88'}, {'abstract': 'Works at a show at Princeton University Art Museum center mostly on disputed land and the scorching aftermath of war.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/02/nyregion/02artsnj.html', 'snippet': 'Works at a show at Princeton University Art Museum center mostly on disputed land and the scorching aftermath of war.', 'lead_paragraph': ' It has been said that geography creates war and wars create geography. “Nobody’s Property: Art, Land, Space, 2000-2010,” at the Princeton University Art Museum, might be used as evidence, since its works, by nine artists \x97 including two artist duos \x97 center mostly on disputed land and the scorching aftermath of war.', 'print_section': 'NJ', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02ARTNJ2/02ARTNJ2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02ARTNJ2/02ARTNJ2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02ARTNJ2/02ARTNJ2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 779, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02ARTNJ2/02ARTNJ2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02ARTNJ2/02ARTNJ2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Conflict’s Many Marks on the Map', 'kicker': 'Art Review | New Jersey', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Conflict’s Many Marks on the Map', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Princeton University Art Museum', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-01T00:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Schwendener', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwendener', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8d7fc85-4555-5fc9-a182-d84c3f87f2ab', 'word_count': 961, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8d7fc85-4555-5fc9-a182-d84c3f87f2ab'}, {'abstract': 'A taxing, baffling, five-day journey into New York’s underground is meant to expose the city’s skeleton and to render visible its invisible marvels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/02/nyregion/02underground.html', 'snippet': 'A taxing, baffling, five-day journey into New York’s underground is meant to expose the city’s skeleton and to render visible its invisible marvels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT must have been the third or fourth day — time, by that point, had started to dissolve — when I stood in camping gear on Fifth Avenue, waiting as my companions went to purchase waterproof waders at the Orvis store. We had already hiked through sewers in the Bronx, slept in a basement boiler room, passed a dusty evening in a train tunnel; we were soiled and sleep-deprived, and we smelled of rotting socks. Yet no one on that sidewalk seemed to notice. As I stood among the businessmen and fashionable women, it dawned on me that New Yorkers — an ostensibly perceptive lot — sometimes see only what’s directly in front of their eyes.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02UNDERGROUND1/02UNDERGROUND1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02UNDERGROUND1/02UNDERGROUND1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02UNDERGROUND1/02UNDERGROUND1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02UNDERGROUND1/02UNDERGROUND1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02UNDERGROUND1/02UNDERGROUND1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Wilderness Below Your Feet', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wilderness\nUnderfoot', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sewers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exploration and Explorers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-01T00:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Feuer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Feuer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a63425ea-bb7e-53f7-a622-37ea2ae6d1ec', 'word_count': 3225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a63425ea-bb7e-53f7-a622-37ea2ae6d1ec'}, {'abstract': 'Sleds in Central Park gear up with the arrival of snow, but golfing on courses in the city is suspended.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/02/nyregion/02open.html', 'snippet': 'Sleds in Central Park gear up with the arrival of snow, but golfing on courses in the city is suspended.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SLEDDING IN CENTRAL PARK is dominated by two hills on the East Side: Pilgrim Hill near 72nd Street and Cedar Hill between 76th and 79th Streets.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank':