: 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/01/us/01boomers.html', 'snippet': 'On New Year’s Day, the Baby Boom Generation’s oldest reach birthdays fraught with sociological significance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In keeping with a generation’s fascination with itself, the time has come to note the passing of another milestone: On New Year’s Day, the oldest members of the Baby Boom Generation will turn 65, the age once linked to retirement, early bird specials and gray Velcro shoes that go with everything.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/01/us/JP-BOOMERS1/JP-BOOMERS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/01/us/JP-BOOMERS1/JP-BOOMERS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/01/us/JP-BOOMERS1/JP-BOOMERS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 699, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/01/us/JP-BOOMERS1/JP-BOOMERS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/01/us/JP-BOOMERS1/JP-BOOMERS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Boomers Hit New Self-Absorption Milestone: Age 65', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Boomers Hit Another Milestone Of Self-Absorption: Turning 65', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pew Research Center', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Longevity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New Year', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Boomers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-01T02:40:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Barry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/693f06af-fb85-5928-82e5-8ece40c4ae71', 'word_count': 1364, 'uri': 'nyt://article/693f06af-fb85-5928-82e5-8ece40c4ae71'}, {'abstract': 'With the New Start treaty nearly wrapped up, President Obama should begin discussing other, more far-reaching deals with Russia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/01/opinion/01sat1.html', 'snippet': 'With the New Start treaty nearly wrapped up, President Obama should begin discussing other, more far-reaching deals with Russia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even after the herculean effort required to win Senate ratification of the New Start treaty, President Obama has no time to rest. The treaty, which mandates modest cuts in long-range nuclear weapons, is on its way to approval by the Duma, the lower house of Parliament in Russia. Once that happens, Washington and Moscow should quickly begin discussing other, more far-reaching agreements. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Next Treaties', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Next Treaties', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARMS CONTROL AND LIMITATION AND DISARMAMENT', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': '