Comments on editorials may be e-mailed to letters@nytimes.com or faxed to (212) 556-3622.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-01T02:44:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8fe216a-35fd-57fd-bedf-d5835bdf77bc', 'word_count': 72, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8fe216a-35fd-57fd-bedf-d5835bdf77bc'}, {'abstract': 'Oregon’s Chip Kelly and Wisconsin’s Bret Bielema, both single, illustrate the subtle shift away from a longtime unspoken requirement for head football coaches.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/01/sports/ncaafootball/01bachelors.html', 'snippet': 'Oregon’s Chip Kelly and Wisconsin’s Bret Bielema, both single, illustrate the subtle shift away from a longtime unspoken requirement for head football coaches.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — When Arnie Bielema’s son Bret got ready to leave the family farm in Prophetstown, Ill., to attend college at Iowa and walk on with the football team, Arnie passed along some parting advice. Not surprisingly, considering that Bret Bielema’s daily chores included washing out hog pens for five hours, the talk was grounded in Midwestern sensibility.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/01/sports/BACHELOR2/BACHELOR2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 780, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/01/sports/BACHELOR2/BACHELOR2-thumbStan