rl': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/02/nyregion/02accent.html', 'snippet': 'Now that Andrew Cuomo is governor, his speaking style, including nasally vowels and a staccato rhythm, will no doubt be on conspicuous display.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Andrew M. Cuomo is asked whether he speaks like a New Yorker, he likes to shake his head. “No,” he sometimes says. “That’s a Queens accent.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02inaug1-cnd/02inaug1-cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02inaug1-cnd/02inaug1-cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02inaug1-cnd/02inaug1-cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02inaug1-cnd/02inaug1-cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/02inaug1-cnd/02inaug1-cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Speaking Manner Rooted in Queens but All His Own', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Speaking Manner Rooted In Queens but All His Own', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Governors (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Speech', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-01T20:09:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Javier C. Hernández', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Javier', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Hernández', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/507f24a0-df4b-512d-a8b8-2a809f950d60', 'word_count': 440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/507f24a0-df4b-512d-a8b8-2a809f950d60'}, {'abstract': 'The Wellington Hotel is now little more than a facade on State Street, but in the early 1980s, it was Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo’s first home in the city.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/02/nyregion/02haunts.html', 'snippet': 'The Wellington Hotel is now little more than a facade on State Street, but in the early 1980s, it was Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo’s first home in the city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ALBANY \x97 In the middle of downtown, half a block from the State Capitol, the hollowed-out remains of five brick and stone buildings stare blankly out at State Street. Only the facades remain, some of them covered by plywood. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/02/nyregion/BULL-HAUNTS1/BULL-HAUNTS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cuomo’s Albany Housing, From Old Hotel to Mansion', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cuomo’s Housing in Albany, From Old Hotel to Mansion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Governors (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HOTELS AND MOTELS', 'rank': 2, 'major':