a have to concede that his 1998 staging of Verdi’s “Traviata” was terrible. With its opulently garish sets and knee-jerk realism, the production dwarfed the cast, no matter what stars were singing. So the time has long since come for something different. And the intriguing production by the German director Willy Decker that the Met introduced on New Year’s Eve could not be more different.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/TRAVIATA/TRAVIATA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 372, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/TRAVIATA/TRAVIATA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 372}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/TRAVIATA/TRAVIATA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 784, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/TRAVIATA/TRAVIATA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/TRAVIATA/TRAVIATA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Timeless Tragedy in Modern Guise', 'kicker': 'Opera Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Timeless Tragedy in Modern Guise', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Polenzani, Matthew', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Verdi, Giuseppe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Poplavskaya, Marina', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-01T22:18:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8052beae-f3f7-5b89-9c1f-779a1c9b088a', 'word_count': 1175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8052beae-f3f7-5b89-9c1f-779a1c9b088a'}, {'abstract': 'Reflecting on a Depression-era technology that still works and a family farm known only through photographs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/02/opinion/02sun4.html', 'snippet': 'Reflecting on a Depression-era technology that still works and a family farm known only through photographs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m standing in the barnyard with the farrier. Remedy, the quarter horse, is getting his winter shoes. We’re surrounded by vast heaps of plowed snow. It’s a sparkling day, and for some reason I find myself thinking about a photograph we found after my father died. My grandfather is sitting on a high bank of snow, dressed in his work clothes, and he’s holding up a fox terrier, which looks as if it’s ready to eat the camera. This is the home farm in northwest Iowa, probably 1936.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Looking Back', 'kicker': 'Editorial | The Rural Life', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Looking Back', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Farmers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horses', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rural Areas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-01T22:19:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Verlyn Klinkenborg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Verlyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klinkenborg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/484b6b46-b523-5fe1-a983-bf623f9e80cf', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/484b6b46-b523-5fe1-a983-bf623f9e80cf'}, {'abstract': 'Retirees from the Livermore Lab have taken the University of California to court, saying it unfairly cast them out of its retiree health-care system.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/02/us/02bclivermore.html', 'snippet': 'Retirees from the Livermore Lab have taken the University of California to court, saying it unfairly cast them out of its retiree health-care system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As one of the country’s leading nuclear physicists at one of the country’s most prestigious scientific institutions, Jay Davis has worked in the golden hills of Livermore and weapons depots of Iraq. In 1988, Mr. Davis founded the renowned Livermore Lab’s Center for Accelerator Mass Spectrometry, which reveals the chemical makeup of substances and has helped advance research across many disciplines.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/02/us/02BCLIVERMORE1/02BCLIVERMORE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Livermore Retirees Sue University Over Health Care Benefits', 'kicker': 'The Bay Citizen', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Livermore Retirees Sue University Over Health Care Benefits', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lawrence Livermore National Labora