': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/NUTCRACKER/NUTCRACKER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 653, 'width': 524, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/NUTCRACKER/NUTCRACKER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/NUTCRACKER/NUTCRACKER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Nutcracker’ Riddles With Many Answers', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Nutcracker’ Riddles With Many Answers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T22:20:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4f60eb0-8cbf-5d68-abe6-1c4c79772219', 'word_count': 1349, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4f60eb0-8cbf-5d68-abe6-1c4c79772219'}, {'abstract': '“Being Polite to Hitler,” the third novel in Robb Forman Dew’s trilogy, considers how people can go on being pleasant in a world that is frequently terrible.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/books/03book.html', 'snippet': '“Being Polite to Hitler,” the third novel in Robb Forman Dew’s trilogy, considers how people can go on being pleasant in a world that is frequently terrible.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Being Polite to Hitler” opens on Oct. 5, 1953, with the Dodgers losing the World Series to the Yankees during a heat wave. Agnes Scofield, a widowed baseball fan and schoolteacher in Washburn, Ohio, has allowed her third graders to listen to the final game on the radio, and then, amid her own intense disappointment, finds herself telling a furious child that “learning how to lose is part of growing up.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/BOOK/BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 785, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Staying Civil as the World Convulses', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Staying Civil as the World Convulses', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dew, Robb Forman', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T22:21:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Suzanne Berne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Suzanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/19ec7914-fa7c-5fd4-a4d9-46d940936de7', 'word_count': 993, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19ec7914-fa7c-5fd4-a4d9-46d940936de7'}, {'abstract': 'A New Year’s Eve concert by the New York Philharmonic featured the pianist Lang Lang and Tchaikovsky galore.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/arts/music/03lang.html', 'snippet': 'A New Year’s Eve concert by the New York Philharmonic featured the pianist Lang Lang and Tchaikovsky galore.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For much of the music world the new year arrives to the opulent sounds of Johann Strauss, whose perfectly formed Viennese dance elaborations fuse nostalgia with a vibrancy and optimism suited to forging ahead. You could search long and hard without finding music better suited to the occasion; for proof look to the New York Philharmonic, where a restless shuffling of the New Year’s Eve playlist in recent seasons has offered its share of hits and misses but still hasn’t yielded a better alternative. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/LANG-JP/LANG-JP-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/LANG-JP/LANG-JP-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/LANG-JP/LANG-JP-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 647, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Need a Gala? Tchaikovsky Is a Go-To Guy', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Need a Gala? Tchaikovsky Is a Go-To Guy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lang Lang', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilbert, Alan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T22:23:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fd55565-07c6-5175-8429-1fdd583e06b0', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fd55565-07c6-5175-8429-1fdd583e06b0'}, {'abstract': 'Chuck Lamprey uses a variation of a winkle to land some extra points.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/crosswords/bridge/03card.html', 'snippet': 'Chuck Lamprey uses a variation of a winkle to land some extra points.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Saturday’s column involved a duel between the defenders and the declarer in which the defenders came out on top. In the diagramed deal the declarer had the upper hand. It featured a pretty piece of play by Chuck Lamprey of White Plains. He was partnering Arch McKellar of Dobbs Ferry, N.Y., in a pair event at the Bridge Deck in Scarsdale, N.Y., last October.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Hunt for an 11th Trick After the Clubs Break Badly', 'kicker': 'Bridge', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On the Hunt for an 11th Trick After the Clubs Break Badly', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T22:37:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb6e85c1-3c73-5af7-8fa9-f8fed99e9b8a', 'word_count': 403, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb6e85c1-3c73-5af7-8fa9-f8fed99e9b8a'}, {'abstract': 'The Muttahida Qaumi Movement said it was ending its partnership with the Pakistan Peoples Party, putting into question the fate of Prime Minister Yusuf Raza Gilani’s government.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/world/asia/03pakistan.html', 'snippet': 'The Muttahida Qaumi Movement said it was ending its partnership with the Pakistan Peoples Party, putting into question the fate of Prime Minister Yusuf Raza Gilani’s government.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISLAMABAD, Pakistan \x97 The second-largest party in the ruling coalition here announced late Sunday that it was ending its partnership with the Pakistan Peoples Party, putting into question the fate of Prime Minister Yusuf Raza Gilani’s government, which is grappling with a fragile economy as it tries to ward off political and terrorism-related troubles.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/world/03pstan1/03pstan1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/world/03pstan1/03pstan1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/03/world/03pstan1/03pstan1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Major Party Walks Out of Coalition in Pakistan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Major Party Walks Out Of Coalition In Pakistan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilani, Yousaf Raza', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pakistan Peoples Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muttahida Qaumi Movement', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T22:43:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Salman Masood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Salman', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Masood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16af2056-e2a2-5aab-be9b-da99ef66aeb6', 'word_count': 689, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16af2056-e2a2-5aab-be9b-da99ef66aeb6'}, {'abstract': 'The $4.3 billion measure to aid rescue workers sickened after the 2001 terrorist attack required the president’s signature before he returned from a Hawaii vacation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/nyregion/03health.html', 'snippet': 'The $4.3 billion measure to aid rescue workers sickened after the 2001 terrorist attack required the president’s signature before he returned from a Hawaii vacation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONOLULU \x97 President Obama took time out of his Hawaiian vacation on Sunday to sign into law one of the surprise accomplishments of the lame-duck Congress: a measure covering the cost of medical care for rescue workers and others sickened by toxic fumes and dust after the 2001 terrorist attack on the World Trade Center. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Signs Bill to Help 9/11 Workers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obama Signs Bill to Help 9/11 Workers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'James Zadroga 9-11 Health and Compensation Act', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T22:44:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheryl Gay Stolberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheryl', 'middlename': 'Gay', 'lastname': 'Stolberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0471ab08-ffdb-5a8d-b0ff-a5b79b8e4426', 'word_count': 274, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0471ab08-ffdb-5a8d-b0ff-a5b79b8e4426'}, {'abstract': 'Amar’e Stoudemire scored a game-high 26 points as the Knicks ended a two-game skid and prepared for a tough stretch against Western teams.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/sports/basketball/03knicks.html', 'snippet': 'Amar’e Stoudemire scored a game-high 26 points as the Knicks ended a two-game skid and prepared for a tough stretch against Western teams.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For Ronny Turiaf, Sunday’s game was part basketball, part wrestling. He had his eye poked, his ribs elbowed and his shoulder tugged against the Indiana Pacers. In between the bodily damage, Turiaf blocked enough shots to provide a spark and nudge the Knicks to a victory. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/sports/KNICKS/KNICKS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 712, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/sports/KNICKS/KNICKS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/03/sports/KNICKS/KNICKS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Turiaf’s Blocks Carve Out a Path for the Knicks', 'kicker': 'Knicks 98, Pacers 92', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Turiaf’s Blocks Carve Out a Path for the Knicks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foster, Jeff', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turiaf, Ronny', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stoudemire, Amare', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARENAS, GILBERT', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hibbert, Roy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Douglas, Toney', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Indiana Pacers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "D'Antoni, Mike", 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gallinari, Danilo', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chandler, Wilson', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Granger, Danny', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Stu', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Knicks', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T22:53:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Abrams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Abrams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec40140a-4fb6-5d13-ab91-4615101f5f79', 'word_count': 785, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec40140a-4fb6-5d13-ab91-4615101f5f79'}, {'abstract': 'North American ticket sales for the holiday weekend were down 20 percent compared with the same period last year, as a group of holdover releases failed to match the heat generated last winter by “Avatar.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/movies/03arts-TRUEGRITCONT_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'North American ticket sales for the holiday weekend were down 20 percent compared with the same period last year, as a group of holdover releases failed to match the heat generated last winter by “Avatar.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hollywood started the New Year with a relative whimper. North American ticket sales for the holiday weekend were down 20 percent compared with the same period last year, as a group of holdover releases failed to match the heat generated last winter by “Avatar.” For the weekend “Little Fockers” (Universal) was No. 1 with an estimated $26.3 million for a two-week total of $103.2 million, according to Hollywood.com, which compiles box office statistics. “True Grit” (Paramount), with Jeff Bridges, right, was a surprisingly close second with $24.5 million for a two-week total of $86.8 million, a phenomenal result for an adult drama. “Tron: Legacy” (Disney) chugged away in third place with $18.3 million for three-week total of $130.9 million. “Yogi Bear” (Warner) was fourth with about $13 million, bringing its three-week total to $66.1 million, while “The Chronicles of Narnia: The Voyage of the Dawn Treader” was fifth. That fantasy from 20th Century Fox sold about $10.5 million over the weekend for a four-week total of about $87 million.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘True Grit’ Continues to Lure Moviegoers Into 2011', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘True Grit’ Continues to Lure Moviegoers Into 2011', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T22:54:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fd9bde7-5480-554a-b75f-2c63889a0373', 'word_count': 172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fd9bde7-5480-554a-b75f-2c63889a0373'}, {'abstract': 'Serbian prosecutors say they believe that Goran Hadzic, a former Croatian Serb rebel leader wanted for war crimes, may have tried to raise money for his life in hiding by selling a painting by Amedeo Modigliani, The Associated Press reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/arts/design/03arts-MODIGLIANIPA_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Serbian prosecutors say they believe that Goran Hadzic, a former Croatian Serb rebel leader wanted for war crimes, may have tried to raise money for his life in hiding by selling a painting by Amedeo Modigliani, The Associated Press reported.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Serbian prosecutors say they believe that Goran Hadzic, a former Croatian Serb rebel leader wanted for war crimes, may have tried to raise money for his life in hiding by selling a painting by Amedeo Modigliani, The Associated Press reported. On Thursday Serbian police officers searching the home of one of Mr. Hadzic’s friends for clues about his whereabouts reported that they found about 50 paintings, as well as a photograph of a Modigliani. The office of Serbia’s war crimes prosecutor said in a statement that the painting was estimated to be worth over a million euros ($1.3 million) and that Mr. Hadzic had probably tried to sell it, The A.P. said. The painting was not named or described further, and it was unclear if it was stolen. A Modigliani painting from 1917, “Nude Sitting on a Divan (The Beautiful Roman Woman),” sold at Sotheby’s in November for $68.9 million, an auction record for the artist. Mr. Hadzic and the Bosnian Serb army commander Ratko Mladic are the last two suspects wanted by the United Nations War Crimes Tribunal in The Hague. Serbia’s failure to arrest the two men is a sticking point in its attempts to join the European Union.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Modigliani Painting in War Crimes Case', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Modigliani Painting In War Crimes Case', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Modigliani, Amedeo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T22:55:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b31d4446-202e-5fed-ad48-e8fc9e60ee4a', 'word_count': 204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b31d4446-202e-5fed-ad48-e8fc9e60ee4a'}, {'abstract': 'Bhutan has granted British art experts access to the country’s roughly 2,000 Buddhist temples and monasteries in an effort to preserve wall paintings from the 16th through 19th centuries, The Observer of London reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/arts/design/03arts-BHUTANGRANTS_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Bhutan has granted British art experts access to the country’s roughly 2,000 Buddhist temples and monasteries in an effort to preserve wall paintings from the 16th through 19th centuries, The Observer of London reported.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Himalayan kingdom Bhutan has granted British art experts rare access to the country’s roughly 2,000 Buddhist temples and monasteries in an effort to preserve wall paintings from the 16th through 19th centuries, The Observer of London reported. The research project, a joint effort between the Courtauld Institute of Art in London and the Bhutan Culture Department, was financed by an anonymous United States donor. The experts have finished their fieldwork and analysis, and they will publish a report. Bhutan’s art is largely restricted to veneration within the temples and monasteries, so it is little known in the West and hard to gain access to even inside Bhutan. In 2008 the government allowed some sacred artworks to leave for an exhibition organized by the Honolulu Academy of Arts on the condition that they be chaperoned by Bhutanese monks, who performed daily rituals to safeguard the works’ spiritual well-being.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bhutan Grants Access to Temples', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bhutan Grants Access to Temples', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bhutan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T22:56:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/162959bd-ce73-54fa-9457-305e68332606', 'word_count': 148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/162959bd-ce73-54fa-9457-305e68332606'}, {'abstract': 'The Schermerhorn Symphony Center in Nashville, which was badly damaged in a flood in May, reopened on Friday with a concert featuring the violinist Itzhak Perlman, The Tennessean reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/arts/music/03arts-NASHVILLEMUS_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The Schermerhorn Symphony Center in Nashville, which was badly damaged in a flood in May, reopened on Friday with a concert featuring the violinist Itzhak Perlman, The Tennessean reported.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Schermerhorn Symphony Center in Nashville, above, which was badly damaged in a flood in May, reopened on Friday evening with a concert featuring the violinist Itzhak Perlman, The Tennessean reported. The center, completed in 2006 and the home of the Nashville Symphony Orchestra, required $40 million worth of repairs after the flood filled the basement and subbasement. Most of the cost is expected to be covered by insurance and by financing from the Federal Emergency Management Agency, according to Reuters. The rest is to be made up through a fund-raising campaign. In an acknowledgment of the flood the concert included Strauss’s “Thunder and Lightning Polka”; the music director, Giancarlo Guerrero, said he had picked it over Handel’s “Water Music.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nashville Music Hall Reopens After Flood', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nashville Music Hall Reopens After Flood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nashville (Tenn)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T22:56:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c9d0d79-a46a-5817-a5f4-8d7b9d8c88a8', 'word_count': 124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c9d0d79-a46a-5817-a5f4-8d7b9d8c88a8'}, {'abstract': 'The Broadway productions of “The Lion King” and “Mary Poppins” will allow ticket holders to exchange seats for other dates through March 10 for any reason, without penalty, as many times as needed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/theater/03arts-DISNEYTESTSB_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The Broadway productions of “The Lion King” and “Mary Poppins” will allow ticket holders to exchange seats for other dates through March 10 for any reason, without penalty, as many times as needed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Beginning with performances this week the Broadway productions of “The Lion King” and “Mary Poppins” will allow ticket holders to exchange seats for other dates through March 10 for any reason, without penalty, as many times as needed. Disney Theatrical Productions announced its “worry-free winter 2011” exchange policy for the two musicals as an experiment to allow greater flexibility at the box office. Policies for Broadway plays and musicals vary for those who want to exchange tickets for another date; this pilot program is meant to help Disney evaluate how many theatergoers might use and appreciate such a policy as well as to offer peace of mind for those worried about making their shows when, say, a blizzard looms. “People have busy lives and need more flexibility when the unexpected happens and plans change,” David Schrader, executive vice president of Disney Theatrical Productions, said in a statement. “We recognize the time is right to reduce restrictions and put more power in the hands of the customer.” Customers who exchange tickets would not be able to receive a full or partial refund if a refund were offered for a specific performance. The policy covers a traditionally slow-selling stretch for many shows and comes during a time when tickets are relatively available for “Mary Poppins” and to some extent for “The Lion King,” which is the bigger hit.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Disney Tests Broadway Ticket Flexibility', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Disney Tests Broadway Ticket Flexibility', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Disney, Walt, Theatrical Productions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T22:57:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/125e4c03-fb7f-5c6c-9f0f-f2144f729a22', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/125e4c03-fb7f-5c6c-9f0f-f2144f729a22'}, {'abstract': 'ABC announced that about 9.1 million people tuned in between 10 and 11 p.m. on Friday to watch “Dick Clark’s Primetime New Year’s Rockin’ Eve With Ryan Seacrest.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/arts/television/03arts-DICKCLARKWIN_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'ABC announced that about 9.1 million people tuned in between 10 and 11 p.m. on Friday to watch “Dick Clark’s Primetime New Year’s Rockin’ Eve With Ryan Seacrest.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'ABC announced that about 9.1 million people tuned in between 10 and 11 p.m. on Friday to watch “Dick Clark’s Primetime New Year’s Rockin’ Eve With Ryan Seacrest,” allowing the program to beat NBC’s “New Year’s Eve With Carson Daly” and handing ABC an overall ratings victory for the evening. Dick Clark, 81, began hosting the program in 1972. He had a stroke in 2004 that affected his speech, and most of the M.C. duties are now handled by his co-host, Ryan Seacrest (above left, with Mr. Clark). Mr. Clark, however, still does his signature countdown to the ball drop in Times Square. The broadcast drew its biggest audience in three years and attracted 1.2 million more viewers than the last time New Year’s Eve fell on a Friday night, in 2004.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dick Clark Wins on New Year’s Eve', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dick Clark Wins On New Year’s Eve', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T22:58:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/9086a899-72bf-55be-a13c-eb692163e5df', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9086a899-72bf-55be-a13c-eb692163e5df'}, {'abstract': 'A constructor shows us his knack for stacking theme entries.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/02/song/', 'snippet': 'A constructor shows us his knack for stacking theme entries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY’S PUZZLE — Greetings and happy new year! I hope everyone has recovered sufficiently from the holiday season’s festivities and Mr. Happy Pencil cocktail toasts. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday: The Song Remains the Same', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wordplay', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T23:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/53d698d7-249f-57a9-a1ed-39e20adf8f47', 'word_count': 835, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53d698d7-249f-57a9-a1ed-39e20adf8f47'}, {'abstract': 'Kanye West’s “My Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy” is phenomenal, a choice for album of the year by many publications, but their consensus is less a measure of greatness than of social climate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/arts/music/03kanye.html', 'snippet': 'Kanye West’s “My Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy” is phenomenal, a choice for album of the year by many publications, but their consensus is less a measure of greatness than of social climate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Welcome to 2011, a year that \x97 once the last of the snow is scraped away \x97 will bring new hope, the promise of renewal, a chance to wake up in a world in which Kanye West’s “My Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy” is not the consensus pick for album of the year.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/KANYE/KANYE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 822, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/KANYE/KANYE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/03/arts/KANYE/KANYE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Album and Its Buzz', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Album and Its Buzz', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'West, Kanye', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T23:09:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c15b7ab7-ec1c-5ecd-a12d-5ba47dce7567', 'word_count': 1210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c15b7ab7-ec1c-5ecd-a12d-5ba47dce7567'}, {'abstract': 'A man charged in the murders of five teenagers points to police report inconsistencies and a conspiracy stretching to the mayor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/nyregion/03newark.html', 'snippet': 'A man charged in the murders of five teenagers points to police report inconsistencies and a conspiracy stretching to the mayor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WEST ORANGE, N.J. \x97 Lee Evans sat with his broad back to the wall of a mostly empty diner and picked at his Spanish omelet and grits on a December afternoon, his face only once breaking into a grin, to greet an acquaintance in worker’s overalls. The man smiled back, something Mr. Evans can no longer take for granted, despite having lived most of his 57 years in and around Newark, a plumber on call day and night. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/nyregion/03NEWARK/03NEWARK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 344, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/03/nyregion/03NEWARK/03NEWARK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 344}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/nyregion/03NEWARK/03NEWARK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 607, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/03/nyregion/03NEWARK/03NEWARK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/03/nyregion/03NEWARK/03NEWARK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With Murky Details, Defendant in 1978 Newark Murders Builds a Case', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With Murky Details, Defendant in 1978 Newark Murders Builds a Case', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARSON', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turner, Alvin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Newark (NJ)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McDowell, Michael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hampton, Philander', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taylor, Ernest', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pittman, Melvin', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Randy', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Evans, Lee', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T23:09:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/919d44ac-309a-5e9a-94df-766f7f072483', 'word_count': 1703, 'uri': 'nyt://article/919d44ac-309a-5e9a-94df-766f7f072483'}, {'abstract': 'The $4.3 billion measure to aid rescue workers sickened after the 2001 terrorist attack required the president’s signature before he returned from a Hawaii vacation.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/02/911-health-bill-signed-into-law-by-obama/', 'snippet': 'The $4.3 billion measure to aid rescue workers sickened after the 2001 terrorist attack required the president’s signature before he returned from a Hawaii vacation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama took time out of his Hawaiian vacation on Sunday to sign into law one of the surprise accomplishments of the lame-duck Congress: a measure covering the cost of medical care for rescue workers and others sickened by toxic fumes and dust after the 2001 terrorist attack on the World Trade Center. Read more …', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '9/11 Health Bill Signed Into Law by Obama', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-02T23:15:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheryl Gay Stolberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheryl', 'middlename': 'Gay', 'lastname': 'Stolberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b674a1df-f51a-57f8-851a-460ed98dca15', 'word_count': 56, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b674a1df-f51a-57f8-851a-460ed98dca15'}, {'abstract': 'Jason Garrett and Leslie Frazier appears to be staying, but Marvin Lewis, Tony