s Your Trash', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T12:01:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/98ed19a2-d87a-5f2f-bbac-9853708c63bf', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98ed19a2-d87a-5f2f-bbac-9853708c63bf'}, {'abstract': 'The bank agreed to pay more than $2.5 billion to resolve claims with Freddie Mac and Fannie Mae against Countrywide Financial.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/business/04bank.html', 'snippet': 'The bank agreed to pay more than $2.5 billion to resolve claims with Freddie Mac and Fannie Mae against Countrywide Financial.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bank of America announced on Monday that it had paid more than $2.5 billion to buy back troubled mortgages and resolve related claims from Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac \x97 deals that may prompt a wave of such settlements by big banks.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bank of America Buys Back $2.5 Billion in Mortgage Debt', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bank of America Buys Back $2.5 Billion in Mortgage Debt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of America Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T12:45:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess and Eric Dash', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'rol