lement our ‘real’ bags,” Sholomytska\xa0says. “So we ended up with a surreal design that references luxury but is ultimately an anti-status status symbol.” And the Birkin references don’t stop there: in a fittingly bizarre twist, there is already a three-month waiting list.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ceci N’est Pas un Birkin', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T14:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8509268d-0d94-51b3-929e-2b125353917b', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8509268d-0d94-51b3-929e-2b125353917b'}, {'abstract': 'The loser of the vote, Laurent Gbagbo, refused to leave office, leaving open the possibility of military action from outside, or internal violence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/world/africa/04ivory.html', 'snippet': 'The loser of the vote, Laurent Gbagbo, refused to leave office, leaving open the possibility of military action from outside, or internal violence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ABIDJAN, Ivory Coast \x97 African leaders came to this West African nation for the second time in less than a week, pressing Laurent Gbagbo to cede power after losing an election in November and hoping to settle a political crisis that threatens to reignite civil war. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/04/world/IVORY-COAST/IVORY-COAST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 705, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 