es and the killing of a Christian woman in her home in Baghdad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGHDAD \x97 Two members of the United States military were killed Sunday night in central Iraq, the first such deaths here this year at a time when American casualties had become a rarity. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '2 Americans Die in Iraq; Local Forces Are Attacked', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '2 Americans Die in Iraq; Local Forces Are Attacked', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civilian Casualties', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T16:41:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Leland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25e6cf59-8ff4-5a26-a23a-d2a450fe5eb7', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25e6cf59-8ff4-5a26-a23a-d2a450fe5eb7'}, {'abstract': 'An aggregation of links by the reporters and editors of Diner’s Journal.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/what-were-reading-88/', 'snippet': 'An aggregation of links by the reporters and editors of Diner’s Journal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An aggregation of links by the reporters and editors of Diner’s Journal. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T16:42:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/be9348ef-58fd-5923-9d52-de2b5a5114bc', 'word_count': 420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be9348ef-58fd-5923-9d52-de2b5a5114bc'}, {'abstract': 'With the regular season over and the clock ticking on the expiring collective bargaining agreement, the N.F.L. sent an e-mail message directly to fans Monday morning, laying out management’s position in the labor talks.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/n-f-l-makes-case-on-labor-talks-to-fans/', 'snippet': 'With the regular season over and the clock ticking on the expiring collective bargaining agreement, the N.F.L. sent an e-mail message directly to fans Monday morning, laying out management’s position in the labor talks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the regular season over and the clock ticking on the expiring collective bargaining agreement, the N.F.L. sent an e-mail message directly to fans Monday morning, laying out management’s position in the labor talks. The message, sent to about 5 million fans — and also distributed to teams so they can distribute it — mentions proposals for the 18-game season (and cutbacks in off-season workout programs that would accompany it) and for a rookie wage scale. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'N.F.L. Makes Case on Labor Talks to Fans', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T16:48:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judy Battista', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judy', 'midd