entatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Academies of the United States', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T19:52:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9cd4a450-07dd-58aa-83af-5626451b5b3f', 'word_count': 637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9cd4a450-07dd-58aa-83af-5626451b5b3f'}, {'abstract': 'A study of 18th-century rural Finlanders finds that almost everyone from a poor family born in bumper crop years went on to have at least one baby, those born in lean years far less often.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/health/research/04patterns.html', 'snippet': 'A study of 18th-century rural Finlanders finds that almost everyone from a poor family born in bumper crop years went on to have at least one baby, those born in lean years far less often.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ministers of the Lutheran Church in 18th-century Finland kept careful records of births, deaths and marriages so they could efficiently tax their congregants, but 21st-century biologists are using the data for quite a different purpose.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Patterns: When a Bumper Crop Led to a Baby Bulge', 'kicker': 'Vital Signs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'PATTERNS: When a Bumper Crop Led to a Baby Bulge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Finland', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Population', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T19:58:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Research', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Bakalar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3afafcf-dfa0-5a0a-af70-8813db95ffb5', 'word_count': 220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3afafcf-dfa0-5a0a-af70-8813db95ffb5'}, {'abstract': 'People who are distracted during a meal eat more, and are hungrier afterward, than those who concentrate on just eating.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/health/research/04behavior.html', 'snippet': 'People who are distracted during a meal eat more, and are hungrier afterward, than those who concentrate on just eating.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Catching up with e-mail while you eat lunch? Watching television? You may end the day eating more than you think.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Behavior: Distracted Eating Adds More to Waistlines', 'kicker': 'Vital Signs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'BEHAVIOR: Distracted Eating Adds More to Waistlines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T19:59:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Research', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Bakalar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4233fc98-4264-555a-b593-6fe53c2c85fb', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4233fc98-4264-555a-b593-6fe53c2c85fb'}, {'abstract': 'IPhone customers were confused and upset on Monday after the phone’s built-in alarm failed to go off for the third day in a row because of an Apple programming glitch.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/after-apple-says-otherwise-iphone-alarm-still-remains-silent/', 'snippet': 'IPhone customers were confused and upset on Monday after the phone’s built-in alarm failed to go off for the third day in a row because of an Apple programming glitch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you use your iPhone as an alarm clock, it might be time for a backup plan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Apple Says Otherwise, iPhone Alarm Still Remains Silent', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T20:01:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/407f73ed-6284-5794-bd7f-6cb2678bc92d', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/407f73ed-6284-5794-bd7f-6cb2678bc92d'}, {'abstract': 'Fried fish may be a factor in the high death rate from stroke in eight Southern states.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/health/research/04diet.html', 'snippet': 'Fried fish may be a factor in the high death rate from stroke in eight Southern states.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A wide swath of the South has long been known as the “stroke belt” because it has higher rates of stroke and other cardiovascular illnesses than the rest of the country. Now researchers are suggesting one culprit: fried fish.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Diet: Fried Fish Is Seen as a ‘Stroke Belt’ Culprit', 'kicker': 'Vital Signs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'DIET: Fried Fish Is Seen as a ‘Stroke Belt’ Culprit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stroke', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southern States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T20:03:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Research', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Bakalar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f8c9ad3-cf05-5f22-8b08-db36a801b1fd', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f8c9ad3-cf05-5f22-8b08-db36a801b1fd'}, {'abstract': 'Sylvie Cachay’s boyfriend, Nicholas Brooks, is accused of strangling and drowning her in a hotel bathtub.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/murder-charge-in-swimsuit-designers-death/', 'snippet': 'Sylvie Cachay’s boyfriend, Nicholas Brooks, is accused of strangling and drowning her in a hotel bathtub.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nicholas Brooks, the boyfriend of the swimsuit designer whose body was found in a bathtub in the fashionable Soho House hotel in Manhattan last month, has been indicted on second-degree murder charges, according to court papers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Murder Charge in Swimsuit Designer’s Death', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T20:07:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colin Moynihan and Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moynihan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/34faeb1c-5b2a-50de-af2b-2862dfaeb3ea', 'word_count': 183, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34faeb1c-5b2a-50de-af2b-2862dfaeb3ea'}, {'abstract': 'Lesson Plan | Examining maps with population statistics gleaned from the United States census to gain insights into various places in one’s state and the nation.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/connecting-the-dots-interpreting-u-s-census-data/', 'snippet': 'Lesson Plan | Examining maps with population statistics gleaned from the United States census to gain insights into various places in one’s state and the nation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Overview | How can census data provide insights into one’s community and state, as well as the nation? What are the implications of the population data provided by the census? In this lesson, students examine maps that represent census data, from the present to the past 100 years, on levels from national to state to street, then generate and find answers to related research questions. They can also design and conduct an ethnography study in their community to gain insight into the real people behind the statistics. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Connecting the Dots: Interpreting U.S. Census Data', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Census', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Maps', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Population', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Census Bureau', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T20:15:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne and Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cd6d314-eece-5ea2-8f21-6c1fcf248b55', 'word_count': 2149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cd6d314-eece-5ea2-8f21-6c1fcf248b55'}, {'abstract': 'Martin Garbus is now an attorney of record in the child custody case concerning Mel Gibson and his former girlfriend, Oksana Grigorieva. He represents Ms. Grigorieva.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/mel-gibsons-girlfriend-gets-to-keep-her-lawyer/', 'snippet': 'Martin Garbus is now an attorney of record in the child custody case concerning Mel Gibson and his former girlfriend, Oksana Grigorieva. He represents Ms. Grigorieva.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — “I am now an attorney of record in the case.” So said Martin Garbus, in a brief phone conversation after a closed-door hearing Monday in the child custody dispute between Mel Gibson and his former girlfriend, Oksana Grigorieva. Mr. Gibson’s lawyers had opposed a request by Mr. Garbus, who is from New York, to be allowed to represent Ms. Grigorieva in the Los Angeles County Superior Court case. They cited, among other things, his public remarks about the dispute during a November interview on one of Larry King’s final televised interview shows for CNN. On the program, Ms. Grigorieva described how she had recorded Mr. Gibson’s obscene rants to her because she feared he might kill her. Mr. Garbus, who appeared with her on the program, had said that Mr. Gibson did not want to pay any support for his daughter with Ms. Grigorieva.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mel Gibson’s Ex-Girlfriend  to Keep Her Lawyer', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T20:18:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/57fdc991-2643-5bd5-86ac-a5661d63a870', 'word_count': 216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57fdc991-2643-5bd5-86ac-a5661d63a870'}, {'abstract': 'The bank agreed to resolve claims with Freddie Mac and Fannie Mae against Countrywide Financial in a deal that may prompt a wave of settlements with other big lenders.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/fannie-and-freddie-continue-to-collect-on-bad-loans/', 'snippet': 'The bank agreed to resolve claims with Freddie Mac and Fannie Mae against Countrywide Financial in a deal that may prompt a wave of settlements with other big lenders.', 'lead_paragraph': '7:31 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bank of America Buys Back $2.5 Billion in Mortgage Debt', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moynihan, Brian T', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of America Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Countrywide Financial Corp', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corp', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal National Mortgage Association (Fannie Mae)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T20:30:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess and Eric Dash', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dash', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9d2d332-0ae5-5b40-91b7-960745e71188', 'word_count': 689, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9d2d332-0ae5-5b40-91b7-960745e71188'}, {'abstract': 'There is another Internet company investors can trade, and it has a stake in Facebook.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/facebooks-london-back-door/', 'snippet': 'There is another Internet company investors can trade, and it has a stake in Facebook.', 'lead_paragraph': '5:23 p.m. | Updated You want to invest in Facebook, but you’re not a high net-worth client of Goldman Sachs. There is a way — sort of. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multim