worry about the flaws in the libertarian utopia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since I wasn’t blogging over the holidays, I missed the online dust-up over Chris Beam’s big essay on libertarianism’s moment in the political sun. What earned Beam the most pushback from libertarians was probably the way he finished his piece: After briskly surveying libertarian thought, from Ayn Rand to Will Wilkinson, he concluded with an extended argument that “libertarianism ventures down some fascinating paths but usually ends up deep in the wilderness.” That wilderness, in his view, is the impossible dream of “minarchy” — a vision of government in which the state is “responsible for a standing army, local security, and a courts system, and that’s it”\xa0— which Beam dismissed with the following critique:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Specter of Minarchy', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T22:21:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/eaad2b2f-742b-500e-920c-65a9c6b9bc8c', 'word_count': 1118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eaad2b2f-742b-500e-920c-65a9c6b9bc8c'}, {'abstract': 'The so-called Rh factor makes few headlines these days, but until the middle of the 20th century it was a serious public-health concern, implicated in the deaths and severe disabilities of 10,000 babies in this country every year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/health/04first.html', 'snippet': 'The so-called Rh factor makes few headlines these days, but until the middle of the 20th century it was a serious public-health concern, implicated in the deaths and severe disabilities of 10,000 babies in this country every year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The so-called Rh factor makes few headlines these days, but until the middle of the 20th century it was a serious public-health concern, implicated in the deaths and severe disabilities of 10,000 babies in this country every year.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/04/science/SUB-FIRS/SUB-FIRS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 214, 'width': 292, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rh Factor, 1944', 'kicker': 'First Mention', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rh Factor, 1944', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T22:25:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Bakalar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a3630ad-50c7-5fca-a551-efef1393b8a2', 'word_count': 548, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a3630ad-50c7-5fca-a551-efef1393b8a2'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs from Pakistan, Australia and Austria.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/01/03/blogs/20110103POD.html', 'snippet': 'Photographs from Pakistan, Australia and Austria.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photographs from Pakistan, Australia and Austria.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'image