e', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Space & Cosmos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dennis Overbye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dennis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Overbye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/715d8f97-dc80-5423-9fc0-6943071f5c8b', 'word_count': 1892, 'uri': 'nyt://article/715d8f97-dc80-5423-9fc0-6943071f5c8b'}, {'abstract': 'A  report from the Nieslen Company on Monday shows Apple’s iPhone still in the smartphone lead, but Google’s Android is right on its heels.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/apple-mobile-operating-system-still-tops-google-android/', 'snippet': 'A  report from the Nieslen Company on Monday shows Apple’s iPhone still in the smartphone lead, but Google’s Android is right on its heels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many thought this race would be determined by the end of 2010, but as we roll into 2011 the\xa0mobile phone race is still too close to call.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Apple Mobile Operating System Still Tops Google Android', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Android (Operating System)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nielsen Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T22:44:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a00ace92-3a53-5a25-a589-82e8aa5753e5', 'word_count': 205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a00ace92-3a53-5a25-a589-82e8aa5753e5'}, {'abstract': 'The Russian American Foundation and the Bolshoi Ballet Academy are holding auditions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/arts/dance/04arts-FOOTNOTE_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The Russian American Foundation and the Bolshoi Ballet Academy are holding auditions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Russian American Foundation and the Bolshoi Ballet Academy are holding auditions to fill two spots at the school for the summer. The winning dancers, one man, one woman, will be offered scholarships to train in Moscow for what’s known as a summer intensive. The auditions will be held on Sunday at the American Musical and Dramatic Academy, 211 West 61st Street in Manhattan. More information can be found at russianamericanfoundation.org or (212) 687-6118.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Footnote', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Footnote', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T22:45:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/385f90c1-b2e1-5721-b8e2-37f6f129a511', 'word_count': 75, 'uri': 'nyt://article/385f90c1-b2e1-5721-b8e2-37f6f129a511'}, {'abstract': 'The Seattle Seahawks’ victory over the St. Louis Rams propelled NBC to No. 1.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/arts/television/04arts-FOOTBALLPUTS_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The Seattle Seahawks’ victory over the St. Louis Rams propelled NBC to No. 1.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Seattle Seahawks’ victory over the St. Louis Rams propelled NBC to No. 1 in the ratings on Sunday. While final figures for “Sunday Night Football” were not expected until Tuesday, Nielsen’s overnight audience estimate was higher than for the equivalent game a year ago, which drew 16.3 million viewers. But the audience was smaller than the one for last week’s postponed broadcast of the game between the Minnesota Vikings and the Philadelphia Eagles, which drew 23.7 million viewers on Tuesday. CBS ranked second for the night with “60 Minutes” at 7 p.m. (13.6 million) and new episodes of “CSI: Miami” (11.1 million) and “Undercover Boss” (11.8 million) at 8 and 9. “Desperate Housewives” lifted ABC into third place, with 12.1 million viewers at 9; the network drew more modest ratings for “Extreme Makeover: Home Edition” at 8 (9.3 million) and “Brothers & Sisters” at 10 (8 million). Fox was fourth with reruns of its comedy lineup.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Football Puts NBC on Top', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Football Puts NBC on Top', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T22:47:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/88d9234a-a33e-583b-ab8b-709052772a8d', 'word_count': 160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88d9234a-a33e-583b-ab8b-709052772a8d'}, {'abstract': 'Holidays, weather and sanitation crises create a perfect storm for those too lazy to move their cars.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/a-record-twelve-days-of-parking-amnesty/', 'snippet': 'Holidays, weather and sanitation crises create a perfect storm for those too lazy to move their cars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ladies and gentlemen, we think we have a modern-day record: the city announced this afternoon that alternate-side rules would be suspended Tuesday to facilitate snow removal, which will make 12 days in a row that cars have not had to be moved. The streak began Friday, Dec. 24, in honor of Christmas, which fell the next day. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Record? Twelve Days of Parking Amnesty', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T22:47:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/77cb9a00-c521-5902-af10-43cf8e32421e', 'word_count': 151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77cb9a00-c521-5902-af10-43cf8e32421e'}, {'abstract': 'Last January, as Iran’s leadership struggled to contain protests, nerves frayed to such an extent that the country’s president, Mahmoud Ahmadinejad, was slapped in the face by a military commander at one contentious meeting, according to a leaked U.S. diplomatic cable.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/ahmadinejad-was-slapped-by-general-leaked-cable-says/', 'snippet': 'Last January, as Iran’s leadership struggled to contain protests, nerves frayed to such an extent that the country’s president, Mahmoud Ahmadinejad, was slapped in the face by a military commander at one contentious meeting, according to a leaked ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last January, as Iran’s leadership struggled to contain postelection protests, nerves frayed to such an extent that the country’s president, Mahmoud Ahmadinejad, was slapped in the face by a military commander at one contentious meeting, according to a confidential Iranian source who spoke to an American diplomat in a neighboring country.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ahmadinejad Was Slapped by General, Leaked Cable Says', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T22:50:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e910e916-4470-5145-b065-04046d64b47d', 'word_count': 545, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e910e916-4470-5145-b065-04046d64b47d'}, {'abstract': 'She was scheduled to be released on Monday from the Betty Ford Rehabilitation Center in Palm Springs, Calif.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/arts/04arts-ANOTHERRELEA_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'She was scheduled to be released on Monday from the Betty Ford Rehabilitation Center in Palm Springs, Calif.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s hoping that 2011 will be better for Lindsay Lohan, below. Ms. Lohan, the 24-year-old actress, was jailed twice last year and made two trips to rehabilitation centers stemming from a 2007 drunken-driving conviction. She was scheduled to be released on Monday from the Betty Ford Rehabilitation Center in Palm Springs, Calif. “Today is the first day of the rest of my life,” she wrote in a New Year message posted on Twitter. “The future depends on what we do in the present. \x97 Mahatma Gandhi ... One step at a time.” Ms. Lohan remains under investigation over a report that she attacked a female staff member at the clinic. She must also satisfy a judge that she complied with her probation terms during her three-month stay at Betty Ford.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Another Release For Lindsay Lohan', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Another Release For Lindsay Lohan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T22:52:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/5646b38f-20dc-57a3-8537-4718e8a731e8', 'word_count': 133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5646b38f-20dc-57a3-8537-4718e8a731e8'}, {'abstract': 'The Alvin Ailey American Dance Theater offered a tribute to its artistic director, Judith Jamison.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/arts/dance/04ailey.html', 'snippet': 'The Alvin Ailey American Dance Theater offered a tribute to its artistic director, Judith Jamison.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Judith Jamison is leaving the post of artistic director of the Alvin Ailey American Dance Theater with a smile and laughter. On Sunday night the company ended its annual season at City Center with a tribute to her that lasted over three hours. Well paced, it seemed shorter; but when Ms. Jamison came to the stage at the end, one of her first utterances, delivered in teasing tones of amazement, was simply to thank the audience for staying, and applauding, that long. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 