s oceans.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/04/science/04SCIBKS1_span/04SCIBKS1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 313, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/04/science/04SCIBKS1_span/04SCIBKS1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 313}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/04/science/04SCIBKS1_span/04SCIBKS1_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 604, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/04/science/04SCIBKS1_span/04SCIBKS1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/04/science/04SCIBKS1_span/04SCIBKS1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting to Know Your Neighbors Below the Surface', 'kicker': 'Books on Science', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Getting to Know Your Neighbors Below the Surface', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oceans and Seas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fish and Other Marine Life', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T23:02:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cornelia Dean', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cornelia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dean', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19f24d6a-7213-5a24-81df-a230ccda0f9c', 'word_count': 560, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19f24d6a-7213-5a24-81df-a230ccda0f9c'}, {'abstract': 'Cineastes won’t have to leave their computers to see the work of emerging French filmmakers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/movies/04arts-FRENCHFILMFE_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Cineastes won’t have to leave their computers to see the work of emerging French filmmakers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cineastes won’t have to leave their computers to see the work of emerging French filmmakers. UniFrance, a government-sponsored association of French Film industry professionals, in partnership with AlloCiné, an online Paris film directory, have organized a new online event called My French Film Festival and are offering it around the world for two weeks starting Jan. 14. Featuring 10 full-length films and 10 shorts, all recently released in France by emerging directors, the festival will make them available individually or as a group. Visitors can go to myfrenchfilmfestival.com to stream a feature film for $2.60; a short for $1.30 or the entire slate for $18.40. The festival will be judged and voted on by the public, bloggers and critics, with the winner receiving a noncash award to be announced at the end of the festival, on Jan. 29.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'French Film Festival Offers Web Version', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'French Film Festival Offers Web Version', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T23:02:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/44e70e84-d3c0-5ed3-827c-63b6dfceccff', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44e70e84-d3c0-5ed3-827c-63b6dfceccff'}, {'abstract': 'A. O. Scott looks back at Noah Baumbach’s family drama “The Squid and the Whale.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/critics-picks-video-the-squid-and-the-whale/', 'snippet': 'A. O. Scott looks back at Noah Baumbach’s family drama “The Squid and the Whale.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this week’s Critics’ Pick, A. O. Scott revisits Noah Baumbach’s 2005 drama “The Squid and the Whale.” Set in the 1980s and told from the point of view of the 16-year-old Walt Berkman (Jesse Eisenberg), the film explores how a boy is affected by his parents’ separation. Mr. Scott praises the film’s performances, including Jeff Daniels as Walt’s teacher/novelist father Bernard and Laura Linney as his mother Joan. Have you seen “The Squid and the Whale?” Please share your thoughts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Critics’ Picks Video: ‘The Squid and the Whale’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baumbach, Noah', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daniels, Jeff', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eisenberg, Jesse', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-03T23:03:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fabfdafb-c006-5f33-b825-0f673aa