ad-b06cf192cdf3', 'word_count': 608, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f693810c-68ad-59a3-8bad-b06cf192cdf3'}, {'abstract': 'A second go-round for the Green Mountain Project’s rendition of Monteverdi’s 1610 masterpiece.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/arts/music/04green.html', 'snippet': 'A second go-round for the Green Mountain Project’s rendition of Monteverdi’s 1610 masterpiece.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Almost exactly a year ago, the New York soprano Jolle Greenleaf, the Boston violinist Scott Metcalfe and a couple of dozen close associates from around the country presented the fruit of what they called the Green Mountain Project: a performance of Monteverdi’s grand and glorious Vespers of 1610, mounted in observance of the work’s 400th anniversary. A tremendous popular and critical success, the concert might have been the culmination of a one-off labor of love.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/04/arts/GREEN/GREEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/04/arts/GREEN/GREEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/04/arts/GREEN/GREEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 804, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/04/arts/GREEN/GREEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/04/arts/GREEN/GREEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Worth a Reprise at Age 401', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Worth a Reprise at Age 401', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Mountain Project', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenleaf, Jolle', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T01:02:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a262827-5f98-5770-b6a4-0306edc3cc3a', 'word_count': 460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a262827-5f98-5770-b6a4-0306edc3cc3a'}, {'abstract': 'The city’s Sanitation Department deployed 1,100 trucks in two 12-hour shifts and pledged to collect garbage this week from every street.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/nyregion/04garbage.html', 'snippet': 'The city’s Sanitation Department deployed 1,100 trucks in two 12-hour shifts and pledged to collect garbage this week from every street.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A welcome chorus of grunts, hisses, crunches and clangs greeted New Yorkers on Monday as lumbering sanitation trucks collected trash for the first time in more than a week, their arrival a promise that tons of city garbage would finally begin moving away from curbs, not toward them.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/04/nyregion/04GARBAGE1/04GARBAGE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 672, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/04/nyregion/04GARBAGE1/04GARBAGE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/04/nyregion/04GARBAGE1/04GARBAGE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After 10 Days, Trash Pickup Resumes (Even Outside Manhattan)', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After 10 Days, Trash Pickup Resumes (Even Outside Manhattan)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snow and Snowstorms', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sanitation Department (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T01:04:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/518d259e-0b4e-5e4a-b039-562fd7d7dc9f', 'word_count': 682, 'uri': 'nyt://article/518d259e-0b4e-5e4a-b039-562fd7d7dc9f'}, {'abstract': 'Sinfonia New York chose the theme of love and madness for its concert at Bargemusic on Sunday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/arts/music/04barge.html', 'snippet': 'Sinfonia New York chose the theme of love and madness for its concert at Bargemusic on Sunday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Sinfonia New York was founded in 2007, it was meant to be principally a period-instrument Baroque and Classical orchestra. As it has turned out, Sinfonia has become a more flexible enterprise, perhaps of necessity, given the cost of running an orchestra. The Sinfonia Players, a chamber offshoot of the ensemble, presented a striking series of Pergolesi concerts last year, led from the harpsichord by the orchestra’s conductor, John Scott. And a different configuration, with the superb lutenist Grant Herreid supplanting Mr. Scott as the main continuo player, offered a program of mostly Italian Baroque chamber and vocal works at Bargemusic on Sunday afternoon. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/04/arts/Barge/Barge-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/04/arts/Barge/Barge-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/04/arts/Barge/Barge-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Link Between Love and Madness, Explored on a Small Stage', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Link Between Love and Madness, Explored on a Small Stage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bargemusic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sinfonia New York', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T01:04:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/34bcede2-f4ba-5f16-bc5a-ee49cf269607', 'word_count': 431, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34bcede2-f4ba-5f16-bc5a-ee49cf269607'}, {'abstract': 'Thirty years after Cornelius Dupree Jr. was imprisoned for rape and robbery, prosecutors in Dallas declared him innocent on Monday in light of new DNA evidence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/us/04brfs-DNAEVIDENCEC_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Thirty years after Cornelius Dupree Jr. was imprisoned for rape and robbery, prosecutors in Dallas declared him innocent on Monday in light of new DNA evidence.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Thirty years after Cornelius Dupree Jr. was imprisoned for rape and robbery, prosecutors in Dallas declared him innocent on Monday in light of new DNA evidence. Mr. Dupree, 51, has served more years in a Texas prison for a crime he did not commit than any of the other 41 people exonerated in the state in recent years. In 1980, Mr. Dupree was convicted along with a second man, Anthony Massingill, of robbing a couple and then kidnapping and raping the woman. But DNA tests completed last year on traces of semen showed that neither man committed the rape. Mr. Dupree was released on parole last summer, weeks before the DNA tests were done. Mr. Massingill, who was convicted in another sexual assault, remains in prison. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Texas: DNA Evidence Clears Man After 30 Years', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Southwest', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Texas: DNA Evidence Clears Man After 30 Years', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kidnapping', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'False Arrests, Convictions and Imprisonments', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forensic Science', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T01:07:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d399cc44-0ace-5a48-a15b-6faa5ee75593', 'word_count': 127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d399cc44-0ace-5a48-a15b-6faa5ee75593'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Takamine developed from an endearing child star into a powerful representative of the Japanese woman’s search for identity and autonomy in the years after World War II.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/movies/04takamine.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Takamine developed from an endearing child star into a powerful representative of the Japanese woman’s search for identity and autonomy in the years after World War II.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hideko Takamine, a Japanese actress who over the course of nearly 200 films developed from an endearing child star into a powerful representative of the Japanese woman’s search for identity and autonomy in the years after World War II, died on Dec. 28 in Tokyo. She was 86.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/05/arts/05takamine/05takamine-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/05/arts/05takamine/05takamine-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/05/arts/05takamine/05takamine-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hideko Takamine, Lauded Japanese Actress, Dies at 86', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hideko Takamine, 86, Acclaimed Actress', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T01:08:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Kehr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kehr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c69f575-f30a-53cf-a9e1-9253d28f70f8', 'word_count': 685, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c69f575-f30a-53cf-a9e1-9253d28f70f8'}, {'abstract': 'The N.A.A.C.P. says three prisoners were beaten by guards in retaliation for a strike, but the prison leadership denies wrongdoing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/us/04brfs-NAACPSAYSGUA_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The N.A.A.C.P. says three prisoners were beaten by guards in retaliation for a strike, but the prison leadership denies wrongdoing.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The N.A.A.C.P. says three prisoners were beaten by guards in retaliation for a strike, but the prison leadership denies wrongdoing. The N.A.A.C.P. and the inmates’ families said Monday that they planned to file a lawsuit or a complaint with the federal government after receiving photographs showing the men’s injuries. The group says that the men were handcuffed and beaten, including with a hammer, and that one man now needs a wheelchair and another apparently has brain damage. “We are a law enforcement agency and do everything possible to uphold, not break, laws,” the Georgia Department of Corrections said. Inmates in several Georgia prisons went on strike last month, seeking more food and better living conditions. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Georgia: N.A.A.C.P. Says Guards Beat Prisoners', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | South', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Georgia: N.A.A.C.P. Says Guards Beat Prisoners', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strikes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Assn for the Advancement of Colored People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Guards', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T01:11:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robbie Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robbie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/65c4eedd-8f0a-53ed-9aa9-ad67cf53d7e4', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65c4eedd-8f0a-53ed-9aa9-ad67cf53d7e4'}, {'abstract': 'The Department of Defense has identified 1,432 American service members who have died as a part of the Afghan war and related operations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/us/04list.html', 'snippet': 'The Department of Defense has identified 1,432 American service members who have died as a part of the Afghan war and related operations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Department of Defense has identified 1,432 American service members who have died as a part of the Afghan war and related operations. It confirmed the death of the following American on Monday:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Names of the Dead', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Names of the Dead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Marine Corps', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T01:14:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5445be2-de37-5027-9b87-b1d3d3da69af', 'word_count': 46, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5445be2-de37-5027-9b87-b1d3d3da69af'}, {'abstract': 'A single social program is transforming how countries all over the world help their poor.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/to-beat-back-poverty-pay-the-poor/', 'snippet': 'A single social program is transforming how countries all over the world help their poor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The city of Rio de Janeiro is infamous for the fact that one can look out from a precarious shack on a hill in a miserable favela and see practically into the window of a luxury high-rise condominium.\xa0 Parts of Brazil look like southern California.\xa0 Parts of it look like Haiti.\xa0 Many countries display great wealth side by side with great poverty.\xa0 But until recently, Brazil was the most unequal country in the world.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Beat Back Poverty, Pay the Poor', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequa