'main': 'Obama May Bypass Guantánamo Rules, Aides Say', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obama May Bypass Guantánamo Rules, Aides Say', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holder, Eric H Jr', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shutdowns (Institutional)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Detainees', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Signing Statements', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gates, Robert M', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN BAR ASSN', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T01:25:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlie Savage', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Savage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3bc882d-f8fc-5d6c-9165-c4deba4e39fd', 'word_count': 808, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3bc882d-f8fc-5d6c-9165-c4deba4e39fd'}, {'abstract': 'When it comes to the Internet, are even free-speech advocates having second thoughts?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/03/anonymity-and-the-dark-side-of-the-internet/', 'snippet': 'When it comes to the Internet, are even free-speech advocates having second thoughts?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In McIntyre v. Ohio Elections Commission (1995) the Supreme Court overturned a statute requiring any person who prints a notice or flyer promoting a candidate or an issue to identify the communication’s author by name. Justice John Paul Stevens, writing for the majority, grounded his opinion in an account of meaning he takes from an earlier case (First National Bank of Boston v. Bellotti): “The inherent worth of . . . speech in terms of its capacity for informing the public does not depend upon the identity of its source, whether corporation, association, union, or individual.” Or, in other words, a writing or utterance says what it says independently of who happens to say it; the information conveyed does not vary with the identification of the speaker.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Anonymity and the Dark Side of the Internet', 'kicker': 'Stanley Fish', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T01:30:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stanley Fish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stanley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2286e24-6e7c-5036-a7cb-a7b981c7668f', 'word_count': 1460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2286e24-6e7c-5036-a7cb-a7b981c7668f'}, {'abstract': 'The judge (who refuses to judge) said he was leaving; the governor (who nominates) refused to nominate; and the Senate (which confirms) still refused to confirm.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/nyregion/04judge.html', 'snippet': 'The judge (who refuses to judge) said he was leaving; the governor (who nominates) refused to nominate; and the Senate (which confirms) still refused to confirm.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In New Jersey’s strangest current political soap opera, Monday’s episode played out like this: the judge (who refuses to judge) said he was leaving, the governor (who nominates) refused to nominate, and the Senate (which confirms) still refused to confirm. Got that?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Tangle In Battle Over Court In Trenton', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Tangle In Battle Over Court In Trenton', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Judges', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rivera-Soto, Roberto A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallace, John E Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christie, Christopher J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Supreme Courts (State)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Patterson, Anne M', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T01:30:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Pérez-Peña', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pérez-Peña', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/729d8081-c970-5dc8-afe4-744af340c72e', 'word_count': 645, 'uri': 'nyt://article/729d8081-c970-5dc8-afe4-744af340c72e'}, {'abstract': 'With a crowd of reporters tagging along, Cathleen P. Black showcased success stories in the system.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/nyregion/04chancellor.html', 'snippet': 'With a crowd of reporters tagging along, Cathleen P. Black showcased success stories in the system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the first school Cathleen P. Black visited, students in a fifth-grade classroom had one laptop apiece, from which they received individualized lessons. In the second school, for teenagers who had been on the verge of dropping out, counselors routinely show up at the homes of students if they are absent three days in a row. The third was one of four schools in a building that once housed one; students had violin and dance classes, aside from traditional subjects like history, English and math.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/04/nyregion/CHANCELLOR/CHANCELLOR-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/04/nyregion/CHANCELLOR/CHANCELLOR-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/04/nyregion/CHANCELLOR/CHANCELLOR-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/04/nyregion/CHANCELLOR/CHANCELLOR-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/04/nyregion/CHANCELLOR/CHANCELLOR-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Touring Schools That Work, Chancellor Says New Approach Is Needed at Those That Don’t', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Touring Schools That Work, Chancellor Says New Approach Is Needed at Those That Don’t', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Black, Cathleen P', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Educa