'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Kaplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e019abcc-3bfa-576a-9c3d-64db228929db', 'word_count': 723, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e019abcc-3bfa-576a-9c3d-64db228929db'}, {'abstract': 'The country has been moved and angered by the two movies, one featuring Algerian martyrs, and the other French martyrs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/world/europe/04algeria.html', 'snippet': 'The country has been moved and angered by the two movies, one featuring Algerian martyrs, and the other French martyrs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS \x97 The French colonial experience in Algeria, marked by warfare, terrorism and torture, is a wound that never quite seems to close. Anger and guilt about Algeria infuse some of the anxiety today about the heavily immigrant and Muslim banlieues, or suburbs, about the French concern with national identity, radical Islam and veiled women.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/04/world/ALGERIA2/ALGERIA2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Films Open French Wounds From Algeria', 'kicker': 'Memo From Paris', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As 2 Movies Open, French Wounds From Algeria Ache as if New', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beauvois, Xavier', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALGERIA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bouchareb, Rachid', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T01:36:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Erlanger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Erlanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1b955da-dbb7-5e95-b52a-e448dadc1090', 'word_count': 1097, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1b955da-dbb7-5e95-b52a-e448dadc1090'}, {'abstract': 'Britain is having an unusual surge of flu cases this winter, according to the World Health Organization.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/world/europe/04briefs-Britain.html', 'snippet': 'Britain is having an unusual surge of flu cases this winter, according to the World Health Organization.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Britain is having an unusual surge of flu cases this winter, according to the World Health Organization, and British news media reports said severe cases were straining intensive care units, particularly those for children, at some hospitals. Most cases are of last year’s H1N1 swine flu pandemic strain, which has now become a seasonal flu. Relatively few Britons got flu shots this year, officials said, ascribing that to skepticism after the swine flu pandemic proved less lethal than feared. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Britain: Flu Cases Surge, Straining Some Hospitals', 'kicker': 'World Briefing  | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Britain: Flu Cases Surge, Straining Some Hospitals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Swi