0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris V. Nicholson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Nicholson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/36019945-cb91-5be1-a817-0cd83ac3774e', 'word_count': 316, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36019945-cb91-5be1-a817-0cd83ac3774e'}, {'abstract': 'How should we feel about the Seahawks? Do we adopt them as a plucky underdog, or do we root for a quick exit Saturday against New Orleans?', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/the-seattle-seahawks-deal-with-it-america/', 'snippet': 'How should we feel about the Seahawks? Do we adopt them as a plucky underdog, or do we root for a quick exit Saturday against New Orleans?', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the day that the Buffalo Bills advanced to their fourth Super Bowl of the 1990s, some fans at Rich Stadium held up a banner that read: “We’re back. Deal with it, America!” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Seattle Seahawks. Deal With It, America.', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Seattle Seahawks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T14:33:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e43f6cea-41bd-5869-9e0a-6eb476dbd7b4', 'word_count': 234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e43f6cea-41bd-5869-9e0a-6eb476dbd7b4'}, {'abstract': 'In a Q&A, Tilda Swinton, the star of “I Am Love,” talks about the travails of making indie films and her bemusement at the awards season.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/swinton-on-love-hollywood-and-fish-pie/', 'snippet': 'In a Q&A, Tilda Swinton, the star of “I Am Love,” talks about the travails of making indie films and her bemusement at the awards season.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I Am Love,” the art house Italian film starring Tilda Swinton, may have been left out of the statuette race so far, but hey –- Quentin Tarantino named it one of the top movies of last year. And come to think of it, so did some critics closer to home. Ms. Swinton is still a long-shot contender for a nomination, but she’s made the most of those odds before, by winning an Oscar for best supporting actress (for “Michael Clayton”) in 2007. The Bagger reached her recently at her home in the Scottish countryside, in the midst of a snowstorm and dinner preparations (fish pie was on the menu), to talk about moviemaking, the director she loves but can’t imagine working with (Martin Scorsese), her distance from Hollywood (asked if she was a member of the Academy, Ms. Swinton confessed she didn’t know), and winning that Oscar. “It was like a sort of theme park,” she said of her previous Oscar whirl. “I really, really enjoyed it. People wave at me at airports, and that’s all fun.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Swinton on ‘Love,’ Hollywood and Fish Pie', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T14:57:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'ty