ews_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeffrey Gettleman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gettleman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/132ad3c0-c41e-57a1-865a-dc8e91c77297', 'word_count': 396, 'uri': 'nyt://article/132ad3c0-c41e-57a1-865a-dc8e91c77297'}, {'abstract': 'Public-sector workers tend to be more educated, but there is debate over how to compare their compensation with that of private-sector counterparts.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/do-public-workers-diplomas-justify-their-pay/', 'snippet': 'Public-sector workers tend to be more educated, but there is debate over how to compare their compensation with that of private-sector counterparts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Several attentive readers of my post Monday on public workers’ compensation raised interesting questions that go to the heart of a debate flaring in many statehouses and cities. (And as my colleague Steven Greenhouse reports on Tuesday, it is a debate that is prompting some states to ponder ways to curb the power of public-sector unions.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Do Public Workers’ Diplomas Justify Their Pay?', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T17:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Powell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Powell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/faa3f532-563f-505d-bd30-812e802c9333', 'word_count': 517, 'uri': 'nyt://article/faa3f532-563f-505d-bd30-812e802c9333'}, {'abstract': 'In a unanimous vote, Volkswagen AG retained the company’s chairman through 2016, effectively endorsing his goal to surpass Toyota in gross global sales.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/volkswagen-extends-c-e-o-martin-winterkorns-contract/', 'snippet': 'In a unanimous vote, Volkswagen AG retained the company’s chairman through 2016, effectively endorsing his goal to surpass Toyota in gross global sales.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The executive who has set the goal of turning Volkswagen into the world’s largest automaker by 2018 has job security through 2016, the automaker announced on Monday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Volkswagen Extends C.E.O. Martin Winterkorn’s Contract', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winterkorn, Martin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Volkswagen AG', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T17:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4689dc40-7b24-5fcc-b280-aa15fdc02076', 'word_count': 232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4689dc40-7b24-5fcc-b280-aa15fdc02076'}, {'abstract': 'The Victory Cafe, a hole-in-the-wall coffee shop in Boerum Hill, Brooklyn, that serves as a neighborhood living room, must vacate the premises by the end of the month.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/its-quirky-its-beloved-and-its-closing/', 'snippet': 'The Victory Cafe, a hole-in-the-wall coffee shop in Boerum Hill, Brooklyn, that serves as a neighborhood living room, must vacate the premises by the end of the month.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 1:05 p.m. | The news began to trickle out just before Christmas via a flurry of horrified Facebook postings and shocked exchanges over lattes and bagels: the Victory Cafe, a tiny hole-in-the-wall coffee shop in Boerum Hill, Brooklyn, was being evicted by its landlord. It had until Jan. 31 to vacate the premises.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s Quirky, It’s Beloved, and It’s Closing', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coffeehouses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boerum Hill (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T17:02:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Gibberd', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gibberd', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0213e0bf-ebb4-511f-a28b-b3b5db2a25f4', 'word_count': 871, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0213e0bf-ebb4-511f-a28b-b3b5db2a25f4'}, {'abstract': 'The shabby little piano bar off the lobby of the Edison Hotel will be reopened by the folks who run Ward III.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/the-rum-house-rises/', 'snippet': 'The shabby little piano bar off the lobby of the Edison Hotel will be reopened by the folks who run Ward III.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Rum House was a shabby little piano bar off the lobby of the Edison Hotel on 47th Street west of Seventh Avenue, marvelous in its way as a place to stop in before a show for a whiskey sour. Irene Gandy, the legendary Broadway press agent, often held court in one corner. Tyros with musical-comedy in their blood sketched out bits in another. Always, tourists mooned. The bar was a tie to the Rialto of old – an adult-beverage version of the Polish Tea Room next door, where producers gathered to dream and scheme over tuna fish on rye, a scene out of Runyon, a simple place for a drink. And then, in the summer of 2010, after 37 years, it closed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rum House Rises', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Edison Hotel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T17:06:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Sifton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sifton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4a48933-0ca7-547d-b3fc-e07498026992', 'word_count': 316, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4a48933-0ca7-547d-b3fc-e07498026992'}, {'abstract': 'The Pulitzer Prize-winning novelist Annie Proulx recounts building her dream home in Wyoming.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/books/05book.html', 'snippet': 'The Pulitzer Prize-winning novelist Annie Proulx recounts building her dream home in Wyoming.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are two ways to describe Annie Proulx’s memoir, “Bird Cloud,” an account of her Sisyphean struggle to build her dream house on a remote and striking 640-acre stretch of land in Wyoming. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/05/arts/05book/05book-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 686, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/05/arts/05book/05book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/05/arts/05book/05book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Novelist Wills Her Dream Home Into Being', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Novelist Wills Her Wyoming Dream Home Into Being', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Proulx, Annie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wyoming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T17:13:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dwight Garner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dwight', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/29d96abd-bd10-5869-8a55-79a2b4a92ca2', 'word_count': 1018, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29d96abd-bd10-5869-8a55-79a2b4a92ca2'}, {'abstract': 'It cost some $1.4 billion to run the House last year; the new Republican majority will be shaving that by a few percentage points.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/republicans-trim-millions-from-houses-own-budget/', 'snippet': 'It cost some $1.4 billion to run the House last year; the new Republican majority will be shaving that by a few percentage points.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The budget cutting begins at home. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Republicans Trim Millions From House’s Own Budget', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T17:13:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By David M. Herszenhorn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3101d348-c576-5f26-a76e-8c9be5d9ad83', 'word_count': 547, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3101d348-c576-5f26-a76e-8c9be5d9ad83'}, {'abstract': 'The financial planner and Bucks contributor Carl Richards plans to think less about money in 2011 and think more about the present than the past or the future when he does think about money.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/resolved-think-less-about-money/', 'snippet': 'The financial planner and Bucks contributor Carl Richards plans to think less about money in 2011 and think more about the present than the past or the future when he does think about money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, our contributors, along with some of our favorite sources, are writing about their new year’s financial resolutions. This post is from Bucks artist in residence Carl Richards.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Resolved: Think Less About Money', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T17:22:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Richards', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richards', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6cf3cac-46c2-5851-b0c8-d5833b5032ba', 'word_count': 802, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6cf3cac-46c2-5851-b0c8-d5833b5032ba'}, {'abstract': 'Allen Iverson says he’s happy in Turkey and is playing basketball because he still loves the game.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/iverson-breaks-media-silence-in-turkey/', 'snippet': 'Allen Iverson says he’s happy in Turkey and is playing basketball because he still loves the game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Allen Iverson finally spoke from Turkey after dodging the Philadelphia news media, having refused interviews even when reporters came to Istanbul to see him.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iverson Breaks Media Silence in Turkey', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Iverson, Allen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Istanbul (Turkey)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T17:27:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '