'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roberts, David', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T19:10:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/621475c9-f653-5654-a0bc-57478c0cb682', 'word_count': 195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/621475c9-f653-5654-a0bc-57478c0cb682'}, {'abstract': 'Alexandre Thumerelle of the intello-chic Right Bank bookshop 0fr shares his Paris picks.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/the-insider-alexandre-thumerelle/', 'snippet': 'Alexandre Thumerelle of the intello-chic Right Bank bookshop 0fr shares his Paris picks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The daily patrons of 0fr (pronounced “oh-eff-eehrrr”), a family-run intello-chic bookshop in the Marais area of Paris, are a mixed bunch. “Our customers always vary,” the owner Alexandre Thumerelle says. “We usually have locals — students from the fine arts Grande École across the street, designers, models and artists who live in the area. We have tourists, but the ones who come here are pas mal.” Thumerelle, 39, opened his internationally eclectic store in 1995 with his younger sister, Marie. Since then, 0fr has become an indie destination spot for its assortment of vintage and newly released art books and cult fashion magazines. Small but busy, it doubles as a exhibition space for emerging artists (past artist inhabitants have included Will McBride and Mark Borthwick) as well as a publisher for Paris-centric publications (including “GuideParis,” a yearly manual filled with local favorites). “People think Parisians are snobs, but ‘GuideParis’ and 0fr prove that we’re not so bad. We don’t mind newcomers … most of the time.” To celebrate its 15 years in existence, 0fr will be releasing a retrospective book in May. Showcasing all 160 of its publications, the book will include collaborations with other Parisian insiders, such as Purple magazine’s Olivier Zahm and the designer Agnès B.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Insider | Alexandre Thumerelle', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T19:14:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Yuri Chong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Yuri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f1a00d8-b84e-5f4c-8387-8becbcefb400', 'word_count': 561, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f1a00d8-b84e-5f4c-8387-8becbcefb400'}, {'abstract': 'Eric Cantor accuses the Congressional Budget Office of misrepresenting the cost of the new health care law at the behest of Democrats.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/cantor-hills-budget-office-fudged-health-costs/', 'snippet': 'Eric Cantor accuses the Congressional Budget Office of misrepresenting the cost of the new health care law at the behest of Democrats.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The incoming House majority leader, Representative Eric Cantor, Republican of Virginia, accused the nonpartisan Congressional Budget Office on Wednesday of misrepresenting the cost of the recently enacted health care law at the behest of\xa0 Democrats in Congress.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cantor Says Budget Office Miscounted Health Costs