ommercial/05invest.html', 'snippet': 'In many big cities, there is suddenly a lively market for investors in office buildings.', 'lead_paragraph': ' After a two-year hiatus, the investment market for office buildings has sprung back to life, with some properties commanding prices reminiscent of the boom years. Bidding for well-occupied, high-quality buildings in major cities like New York and Washington has been so fierce that some buyers have been priced out of the market. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/05/business/Invest1/Invest1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/05/business/Invest1/Invest1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/05/business/Invest1/Invest1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After Hibernation, the Office Market Is Waking Up', 'kicker': 'Square Feet', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Hibernation, the Office Market Is Waking Up', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Office Buildings and Commercial Properties', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T20:28:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'subsection_name': 'Commercial Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Terry Pristin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Terry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pristin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3779096b-2d11-5ed7-b15d-a6c3c5738736', 'word_count': 1284, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3779096b-2d11-5ed7-b15d-a6c3c5738736'}, {'abstract': 'Traveling in winter comes with a particular packing challenge: namely, what to do with the coat?', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/a-coat-that-becomes-a-tote/', 'snippet': 'Traveling in winter comes with a particular packing challenge: namely, what to do with the coat?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Traveling in winter comes with a particular packing challenge: namely, what to do with the coat? MaxMara’s solution is the Cube, a reversible, convertible, collapsible Siberian goose-down puffer. Should the destination require no more than a bathing suit, the jacket folds up into its own little zippered tote. Or for cold-climate options, the Cube can be restyled with interchangeable hood trims and cuffs (in fabrics like linen and suede) or shortened with the help of hidden buttons. While the winter collection stuck to city-friendly black, the spring line, in stores now, comes in four styles and eight slope-worthy colors, from pink to orange. Prices range from $750 for a vest to $4,350 for a fur-lined coat; extra cuffs and trimmings are $175 to $475. At MaxMara, 813 Madison Avenue; (212) 879-6100.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Coat That Becomes a Tote', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T20:30:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ba11270-4120-5704-bff9-26fc02cb2e58', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ba11270-4120-5704-bff9-26fc02cb2e58'}, {'abstract': 'Lesson Plan | Considering predictions made in 1931 about 2011, and developing informed ideas about what may happen in the future.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/the-future-is-now-analyzing-and-making-predictions/', 'snippet': 'Lesson Plan | Considering predictions made in 1931 about 2011, and developing informed ideas about what may happen in the future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Overview | What did historical figures imagine our lives would look like today? How can we make informed predictions about the future? In this lesson, students consider and discuss predictions about life in 2011 that were written in 1931 by prominent thinkers of the day, and then draw on New York Times articles to develop their own predictions about the future. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Future Is Now: Analyzing and Making Predictions', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'History', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T20:35:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Kavanagh and Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kavanagh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/883dbfab-cee3-54c8-9b60-3e91bc7f691b', 'word_count': 1467, 'uri': 'nyt://article/883dbfab-cee3-54c8-9b60-3e91bc7f691b'}, {'abstract': 'Lea Michele, who portrays Rachel Berry on “Glee,” is featured in a campaign from PETA that seeks to end the carriage horse trade in New York City.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/glee-star-joins-peta-cause/', 'snippet': 'Lea Michele, who portrays Rachel Berry on “Glee,” is featured in a campaign from PETA that seeks to end the carriage horse trade in New York City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'People for the Ethical Treatment of Animals, known as PETA, has long been skillful at attracting the buzzed-about celebrities in the popular culture to support its causes. Now, the organization has enlisted a star of the hit TV series “Glee” to help in one of its efforts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Glee’ Star Joins PETA Cause', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Michele, Lea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'People for the Ethical Treatment of Animals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T20:37:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b01b5ea9-fc9b-566a-bc69-b2a769420fb1', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b01b5ea9-fc9b-566a-bc69-b2a769420fb1'}, {'abstract': 'Guest blogger Amy Ernst writes about the Congolese men who stand by their women, despite rape.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/notes-from-a-young-american-in-congo-a-good-man/', 'snippet': 'Guest blogger Amy Ernst writes about the Congolese men who stand by their women, despite rape.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paluku was standing at the edge of a patchwork field of sugar cane when it happened. “I saw the soldiers catch my wife,” he says, recounting what he saw, while in his daughter’s small pharmacy in a North Kivu village, which is still over-run with soldiers. The metal doors are mostly closed, putting the man in relative darkness, but I can still see the white speckles in his short beard and the flash of his eyes. “I saw how my wife screamed.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Notes From a Young American in Congo: A Good Man…', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T20:51:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Ernst', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ernst', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bf892e4-5769-5ca6-b80e-761e4bbb5443', 'word_count': 778, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bf892e4-5769-5ca6-b80e-761e4bbb5443'}, {'abstract': 'In the Schools: Foreign language instruction seems to be serious business for Cathleen P. Black, and Mandarin the new Spanish.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/focusing-on-languages-mainly-mandarin/', 'snippet': 'In the Schools: Foreign language instruction seems to be serious business for Cathleen P. Black, and Mandarin the new Spanish.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During her visit to High School for Violin and Dance in the Bronx on Monday, one of the stops in five-borough tour that worked as her formal introduction to her new job, New York City’s schools chancellor, Cathleen P. Black, gathered around a table with students and alumni, discussing career paths, opportunities and plans. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Focusing on Languages (Mainly Mandarin)', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chinese Language', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Principals (School)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Black, Cathleen P', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'HARLEM (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T20:58:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fernanda Santos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fernanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Santos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3f6e32c-c6d3-5fcb-a864-85c8786aaa60', 'word_count': 721, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3f6e32c-c6d3-5fcb-a864-85c8786aaa60'}, {'abstract': 'Aides are likely to recommend that President Obama sharply criticize a bill’s restrictions on transferring detainees without claiming authority to bypass the rules.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/us/politics/05gitmo.html', 'snippet': 'Aides are likely to recommend that President Obama sharply criticize a bill’s restrictions on transferring detainees without claiming authority to bypass the rules.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON \x97 President Obama’s aides, confronting a bill that places new restrictions on the transfer of Guantánamo detainees, are likely to recommend that Mr. Obama sharply criticize the limitations when he signs the legislation, a White House official said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Aides Lean Against Bypass of Guantánamo Rules', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obama Aides Lean Against Bypass of Guantánamo Rules', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guantanamo Bay Naval Base (Cuba)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Signing Statements', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Detainees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T20:59:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlie Savage', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Savage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2731baa7-ec22-5782-9f06-273ea7f53304', 'word_count': 505, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2731baa7-ec22-5782-9f06-273ea7f53304'}, {'abstract': 'Toronto’s DeMar DeRozan may be a one-dimensional scorer, but at least he is developing that dimension.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/torontos-derozan-develops-his-one-dimension/', 'snippet': 'Toronto’s DeMar DeRozan may be a one-dimensional scorer, but at least he is developing that dimension.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The league’s craving for versatility has all but buried the classic basketball archetype of the one-dimensional scorer. Such players can still be found on struggling teams with few other options, but those with the luxury of choice shy away from shoot-first types with little else to offer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Toronto’s DeRozan Develops His One Dimension', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toronto Raptors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Toronto (Ontario)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T21:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Mahoney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mahoney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/295990cb-fe5d-558b-940e-27a40d1008a0', 'word_count': 789, 'uri': 'nyt://article/295990cb-fe5d-558b-940e-27a40d1008a0'}, {'abstract': 'The not quite world-wide web attacks.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/trouble-in-the-tubes/', 'snippet': 'The not quite world-wide web attacks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m in an undisclosed location, and it’s great — but our fine subtropical Internet provider is turning out to be a sometime thing. So if there are stretches of silence, it’s not because I have nothing to say, it’s because my wit and wisdom can’t get through …', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Trouble in the Tubes', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T21:02:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9149ff9b-4219-522e-91f1-da74e033ebc4', 'word_count': 48, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9149ff9b-4219-522e-91f1-da74e033ebc4'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs from Israel, Pakistan and the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/