s-brooklyn-cemetery-says/', 'snippet': 'At Washington Cemetery in Midwood, a line of gravestones near the street line were knocked over by a massive mound of snow plowed in from the street by city plows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 6:10 p.m. | On Sunday, Yana Zhuravel and her family went to visit the grave of Ms. Zhuravel’s grandmother at a cemetery in Brooklyn. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Snow Plowed by City Toppled Gravestones in Brooklyn Cemetery', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T22:51:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/16fc966d-a1ab-5ea2-99aa-ba59b57668be', 'word_count': 309, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16fc966d-a1ab-5ea2-99aa-ba59b57668be'}, {'abstract': 'The nominee for state environment commissioner has been quoted as urging caution in state decisions on fracking, a controversial type of natural gas drilling.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/04/cuomo-picks-open-space-advocate-for-environment-chief/', 'snippet': 'The nominee for state environment commissioner has been quoted as urging caution in state decisions on fracking, a controversial type of natural gas drilling.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Andrew Cuomo is receiving plaudits from environmental groups for nominating Joseph Martens as the new commissioner of New York State’s Department of Environmental Conservation. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cuomo Picks ‘Open Space’ Advocate for Environment Chief', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chemicals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drilling and Boring', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hazardous and Toxic Substances', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydraulic Fracturing', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water Pollution', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grannis, Alexander B', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Department of Environmental Conservation', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Natural Resources Defense Council', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Open Space Institute', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T22:56:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leslie Kaufman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leslie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaufman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e937777-73d1-5c22-b38a-0e9de1fe8ef1', 'word_count': 565, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e937777-73d1-5c22-b38a-0e9de1fe8ef1'}, {'abstract': 'A New York chef who returned to Finland to perfect ruis, his native whole-grain rye bread, is now selling it locally.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/dining/05bread.html', 'snippet': 'A New York chef who returned to Finland to perfect ruis, his native whole-grain rye bread, is now selling it locally.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Like many other Finns, Simo Kuusisto, a chef who trained at the French Culinary Institute, is devoted to his native whole-grain rye bread. He began baking it in New York, then went back to Finland to work with a master baker. Though he is still the chef at the Canadian mission to the United Nations, he and his brother, Tuomas (left), turn out their Nordic breads at night, in a bakery in Queens. They are increasing production, which stands at 2,500 breads a week, because of demand on the Internet.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/05/dining/05stuff-rye/05stuff-rye-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 385, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/05/dining/05stuff-rye/05stuff-rye-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 385}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/05/dining/05stuff-rye/STUF-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/05/dining/05stuff-rye/05stuff-rye-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/05/dining/05stuff-rye/05stuff-rye-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dark, Dense Nordic Bread, Made for Butter or Cheese', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dark, Dense Nordic Bread, Made for Butter or Cheese', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bakeries and Baked Products', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bread', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T23:08:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34dda067-9959-5f04-9180-8c4ca2c642aa', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34dda067-9959-5f04-9180-8c4ca2c642aa'}, {'abstract': 'Piennolo tomatoes are grown organically in the rich soil of Mount Vesuvius National Park.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/dining/05tomato.html', 'snippet': 'Piennolo tomatoes are grown organically in the rich soil of Mount Vesuvius National Park.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new preserved tomato can go a long way to brighten a pasta sauce or a pizza in the cold months. The exotic piennolo is a large, deeply flavored, fleshy cherry tomato, ripened in clusters, and at once richly sweet and tart. These imports preserved in jars are grown organically in the rich soil of Mount Vesuvius National Park. Unlike the famous San Marzanos, the iconic plum tomatoes from Southern Italy that stand up to long simmering, piennolos have uncommon freshness for a product in a jar and are best with only brief heating. For pasta, simply warm them very gently with olive oil and garlic. They are ideal when quickly baking fish. And lightly crushed, they can go directly onto pizza without being turned into sauce.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/05/dining/05stuff-tomato/05stuff-tomato-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 724, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/05/dining/05stuff-tomato/05stuff-tomato-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/05/dining/05stuff-tomato/05stuff-tomato-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Preserved Tomatoes Bring a Fresh Note to Winter', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Fresh Note for Winter Pasta', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tomatoes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-04T23:09:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/84b5de28-f449-58d9-8e33-84da9ea2d4b4', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84b5de28-f449-58d9-8e33-84da9ea2d4b4'}, {'abstract': 'In opening a restaurant that he estimates will be around for only nine months, the chef John Fraser can — and must — keep his investment low.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/dining/05temp.html', 'snippet': 'In opening a restaurant that he estimates will be around for only nine months, the chef John Fraser can — and must — keep his investment low.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JOHN FRASER’S new restaurant is doomed. It may make a splash at the start, but by this time next year it will be over, done with, kaput.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/05/dining/05temp2/05temp2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'x