ue': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T15:47:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Azam Ahmed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Azam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahmed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/56acc029-99a7-55ce-a070-fafc6f4dcb5f', 'word_count': 758, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56acc029-99a7-55ce-a070-fafc6f4dcb5f'}, {'abstract': '“The Town,” “Inception” and “Black Swan” are among the Art Directors Guild nominees. Alas, no “The Kids Are All Right” or “Yogi Bear.”', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/the-favorites-show-up-in-production-design-nods/', 'snippet': '“The Town,” “Inception” and “Black Swan” are among the Art Directors Guild nominees. Alas, no “The Kids Are All Right” or “Yogi Bear.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Art Directors Guild announced its nominees for excellence in production design today, broken up into three categories – period, fantasy and contemporary film – and guess what? “Yogi Bear” did not get a single nod, even though it could arguably fit in any of those slots. Among other films not doing that well with the Rotten Tomatoes crowd, “Tron: Legacy” and “The Chronicles of Narnia: the Voyage of the Dawn Treader” did earn nominations, in the fantasy category. They’re joined there by the better-reviewed “Alice in Wonderland” and “Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows: Part 1.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Favorites Show Up in Production Design Nods', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T15:58:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/92f5b857-74ac-5ba5-8621-650d4e9a834e', 'word_count': 193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92f5b857-74ac-5ba5-8621-650d4e9a834e'}, {'abstract': 'Moktada al-Sadr, whose followers waged deadly warfare against American troops, returned to Iraq after three years in Iran.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/world/middleeast/06iraq.html', 'snippet': 'Moktada al-Sadr, whose followers waged deadly warfare against American troops, returned to Iraq after three years in Iran.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGHDAD \x97 Moktada al-Sadr, the populist cleric who emerged as the United States’ most enduring foe in Iraq, returned Wednesday after more than three years of voluntary exile in Iran in a homecoming that embodied his and his movement’s transition from battling in the streets to occupying the halls of power.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/06/world/06iraq_337-395/IRAQ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/06/world/06iraq_337-395/IRAQ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/06/world/06iraq_337-395/IRAQ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Anti-U.S. Cleric Returns to Iraq, and to Power', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Anti-U.S. Cleric Returns to Iraq, and to Power', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'S