'Pasadena, Calif., Jan. 9 -- The Surveyor 7 spacecraft made a safe landing on the moon tonight, providing a triumphal conclusion to a seven year program that led to mechanized reconnaissance for a manned landing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Surveyor Lands Gently On Moon', 'kicker': 'January 10, 1968', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T16:31:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': '<h5> By Gladwin Hill</h5>\n<h5>Special to The New York Times </h5>', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43fbcd52-60f5-504d-a4e0-b97ec6635608', 'word_count': 858, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43fbcd52-60f5-504d-a4e0-b97ec6635608'}, {'abstract': 'the first American president to resign from office following his involvement in the Watergate scandal', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/learning/general/onthisday/bday/0109.html', 'snippet': 'the first American president to resign from office following his involvement in the Watergate scandal', 'lead_paragraph': 'To millions of Americans, Richard Milhous Nixon was the most puzzling and fascinating politician of his time. He was a man of high intelligence and innovative concepts whose talents, especially in international affairs, were widely respected by both friend and foe. Yet he was so motivated by hatreds and fears that he abused his powers and resorted to lies and cover-ups.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 37th President; In Three Decades', 'kicker': 'OBITUARY', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T16:31:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By JOHN HERBERS', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'HERBERS', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7aeae78c-e4c2-5a79-a1e9-e8966fd70e83', 'word_count': 7435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7aeae78c-e4c2-5a79-a1e9-e8966fd70e83'}, {'abstract': 'Western tourists visiting the hyperinflation-plagued country are snatching up trillion-dollar Zimbabwean notes for their novelty.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/zimbabwean-dollars-are-finally-worth-something/', 'snippet': 'Western tourists visiting the hyperinflation-plagued country are snatching up trillion-dollar Zimbabwean notes for their novelty.', 'lead_paragraph': '… as economic souvenirs. The Associated Press reports that Western tourists visiting this hyperinflation-plagued country are snatching up trillion-dollar Zimbabwean notes for their novelty, not their value as a means of exchange:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Zimbabwean Dollars Are Finally Worth Something', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T16:35:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/df64da31-1e4e-5056-82b3-16181916c4e1', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df64da31-1e4e-5056-82b3-16181916c4e1'}, {'abstract': 'Joel and Ethan Coen, riding high on the box-office success of “True Grit,” recount the toughest shoot of their careers and marvel at being Hollywood insiders', 'web_url': 'https:/