2 losses ago.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/06/sports/06michigan/06michigan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/06/sports/06michigan/06michigan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/06/sports/06michigan/06michigan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 741, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/06/sports/06michigan/06michigan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/06/sports/06michigan/06michigan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Looking to Start Over, Michigan Fires Rodriguez', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Looking to Start Over, Michigan Fires Rodriguez', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Michigan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Big Ten Conference', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mississippi State University', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T17:03:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bunkley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bunkley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e47373c-7372-5c52-87d0-7b751219c773', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e47373c-7372-5c52-87d0-7b751219c773'}, {'abstract': 'Why President Obama was right to hail the quarterback’s post-prison second chance.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/the-example-of-michael-vick/', 'snippet': 'Why President Obama was right to hail the quarterback’s post-prison second chance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Another Christmas-season controversy I missed was the burst of outrage — culminating in some unfortunate hyperbole from Tucker Carlson — over the phone call that Barack Obama made to the Philadelphia Eagles’ owner thanking him for giving Michael Vick a second chance. If you remember my earlier thoughts on Vick’s return to the N.F.L., you won’t be surprised to learn that I agree with Michael Gerson, who penned a persuasive column yesterday arguing that “Obama’s instinct on this issue is entirely correct.” While “Vick is not representative,” Gerson wrote, “he is symbolic” of the 700,000-odd felons who get out of prison every year and attempt to reintegrate into society. And that symbolism matters:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Example of Michael Vick', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T17:05:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bdc4781-9ab3-568b-b086-3d921981f19d', 'word_count': 412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bdc4781-9ab3-568b-b086-3d921981f19d'}, {'abstract': 'Toshiba is unveiling a 3-D video technology that you can watch without glasses.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/3d-without-the-glasses/', 'snippet': 'Toshiba is unveiling a 3-D video technology that you can watch without glasses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LAS VEGAS — For those of you interested in watching 3-D video but who hate those wearing funny-looking glasses (and isn’t that most of us?), Toshiba has something to show you at the Consumer Electronics Show — televisions and a laptop that can display 3-D video without requiring the viewer to put on glasses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '3-D Without the Glasses', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Consumer Electronics Show', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Laptop Computers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Three-Dimensional Devices and Effects', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TOSHIBA CORP', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T17:06:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Brustein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brustein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6da57a72-acba-5ea9-b4d7-9bfffa73470f', 'word_count': 319, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6da57a72-acba-5ea9-b4d7-9bfffa73470f'}, {'abstract': 'Mitch Daniels, Indiana’s Republican governor and a potential 2012 presidential candidate, has often rejected conservative orthodoxy, but lots of conservatives are fond of him.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/why-do-so-many-conservatives-like-mitch-daniels/', 'snippet': 'Mitch Daniels, Indiana’s Republican governor and a potential 2012 presidential candidate, has often rejected conservative orthodoxy, but lots of conservatives are fond of him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mitch Daniels presents something of an enigma for conservatives.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Do So Many Conservatives Like Mitch Daniels?', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T17:12:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd16d11a-aea2-5620-b75d-425b516c7bbb', 'word_count': 416, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd16d11a-aea2-5620-b75d-425b516c7bbb'}, {'abstract': 'The rising star of “Tron: Legacy” values his privacy. “I learned at a very young age just to keep my mouth shut,” he said.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/asked-answered-garrett-hedlund/', 'snippet': 'The rising star of “Tron: Legacy” values his privacy. “I learned at a very young age just to keep my mouth shut,” he said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s no turning back for Garrett Hedlund. The 26-year-old, who left his parents cattle farm in Roseau, Minn., in 2003 to pursue an acting career in Los Angeles, is bound for Hollywood stardom, thanks to his breakout roles in “Tron: Legacy” and “Country Strong,” which opens in theaters this Friday. The Moment met up with the actor in a nondescript Brooklyn diner, the kind of place that Hedlund says he pictured himself working in if the whole acting thing hadn’t worked out.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Asked & Answered | Garrett Hedlund', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T17:15:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marcus Chang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marcus', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b726dc62-178f-5b27-b877-bb3e8f091fe6', 'word_count': 936, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b726dc62-178f-5b27-b877-bb3e8f091fe6'}, {'abstract': 'As the Commodity Futures Trading Commission proposes hundreds of rules for banks, hedge funds and other big financial players, trading limits for wheat and corn are among the most contentious.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/c-f-t-c-inches-toward-compromise-on-commodity-trading-limits/', 'snippet': 'As the Commodity Futures Trading Commission proposes hundreds of rules for banks, hedge funds and other big financial players, trading limits for wheat and corn are among the most contentious.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As regulators propose hundreds of new rules for banks, hedge funds and other big financial players, trading limits for wheat and corn are proving among the most contentious. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inching Toward Compromise on Commodity Trading', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T17:16:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'las