gton from the American embassy in Tel Aviv in late 2008, Israeli officials told American diplomats “on multiple occasions that they intend to keep the Gazan economy on the brink of collapse without quite pushing it over the edge.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Israel Planned to Keep Gaza’s Economy ‘on the Brink of Collapse,’ Leaked Cable Says', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T18:15:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa96ca4b-bd87-50a0-b458-f1fb86a33d2d', 'word_count': 543, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa96ca4b-bd87-50a0-b458-f1fb86a33d2d'}, {'abstract': 'Procter & Gamble seeks to burnish its corporate image by expanding a campaign declaring the company to be a “proud sponsor of moms.”', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/procter-gamble-expands-its-moms-campaign/', 'snippet': 'Procter & Gamble seeks to burnish its corporate image by expanding a campaign declaring the company to be a “proud sponsor of moms.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Procter & Gamble, the nation’s largest advertiser, is further expanding a campaign it introduced last year that declared the maker of household staples to be a “proud sponsor of moms.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Procter & Gamble Expands Its ‘Moms’ Campaign', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Special Olympics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T18:30:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0829396-5bc9-5141-ab87-d30d57f4bc33', 'word_count': 350, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0829396-5bc9-5141-ab87-d30d57f4bc33'}, {'abstract': 'The last episode of the HBO documentary “24/7: Penguins/Capitals” marks the end of a singular experience for fans, players and coaches.', 'web_url': 'https://slapshot.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/a-bittersweet-farewell-to-hbos-247/', 'snippet': 'The last episode of the HBO documentary “24/7: Penguins/Capitals” marks the end of a singular experience for fans, players and coaches.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Wednesday at 10 p.m., HBO will show the final episode of its “24/7″ series on the Penguins’ and Capitals’ “Road to the Winter Classic,” and hockey fans will be watching as they have the previous three episodes of this superb, engrossing documentary. This episode will be about the New Year’s night outdoor game, and many at Heinz Field in Pittsburgh last Saturday were already saying they couldn’t wait to see it. But when it is over, a lot of fans will be very sad to see it go.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Bittersweet Farewell to HBO’s “24/7″', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T18:31:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6fe800a-4257-52c3-80a6-b1b4732c24ad', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6fe800a-4257-52c3-80a6-b1b4732c24ad'}, {'abstract': 'Representative John A. Boehner of Ohio was elected the 61st House speaker on Wednesday and Representative Nancy Pelosi of California was elected leader of the Democratic minority.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/and-the-winners-are/', 'snippet': 'Representative John A. Boehner of Ohio was elected the 61st House speaker on Wednesday and Representative Nancy Pelosi of California was elected leader of the Democratic minority.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Representative John A. Boehner of Ohio was elected the 61st House speaker on Wednesday and Representative Nancy Pelosi of California was elected leader of the Democratic minority.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'And the Winners Are…', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T18:43:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By David M. Herszenhorn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/048e6fa1-08b4-508c-a485-f84766cfdb9f', 'word_count': 233, 'uri': 'nyt://article/048e6fa1-08b4-508c-a485-f84766cfdb9f'}, {'abstract': 'You won’t find it in the discretionary budget.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/the-spending-that-matters/', 'snippet': 'You won’t find it in the discretionary budget.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HotAir’s Allahpundit has good rundown of why House Republicans are backing off their pledge to cut $100 billion from the federal budget this year:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Spending That Matters', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T18:44:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f3af608-50f6-548c-a08a-c8ebcd262554', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f3af608-50f6-548c-a08a-c8ebcd262554'}, {'abstract': 'The owners of failed businesses have a million stories about why they failed. Here are some of them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/business/smallbusiness/06sbiz.html', 'snippet': 'The owners of failed businesses have a million stories about why they failed. Here are some of them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In business, numbers usually tell the story. If you use them to judge 2010, it was probably a better year for small businesses than 2009. In the first quarter of 2010, for example \x97 the most recent period for which the data are available \x97 there was a net loss of 96,000 companies with fewer than 100 employees, according to the Bureau of Labor Statistics. In 2009, the loss was 400,000 companies.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/06/business/smallbusiness/06sbiz-inline/06sbiz-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 614, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/06/business/smallbusiness/06sbiz-inline/06sbiz-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/06/business/smallbusiness/06sbiz-inline/06sbiz-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Six Companies Failed to Survive 2010', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Six Companies Failed to Survive 2010', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wesabe.com', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T18:47:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eilene Zimmerman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eilene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zimmerman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/020736bb-bf63-5997-be05-927dafd26fb2', 'word_count': 1292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/020736bb-bf63-5997-be05-927dafd26fb2'}, {'abstract': 'The post-holiday blues kick in for the music industry.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/05/swift-stays-on-top-during-anemic-sales-week/', 'snippet': 'The post-holiday blues kick in for the music industry.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The record industry’s traditional post-holiday doldrums were reflected in this week’s Billboard album chart as sales of Christmas-related CDs dropped sharply and even nonholiday fare experienced a decline. Taylor Swift’s “Speak Now” continued as No. 1, for the third week in a row, but its sales fell 72 percent, to 77,000, from the previous week. Sales of the records that had been holding down the No. 2 and 3 slots leading into Christmas, “The Gift” by Susan Boyle and the “Glee” cast’s “Christmas Album,” plunged more than 90 percent, dropping both of those CDs out of the Top 10.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Swift’s Album Stays on Top in Anemic Sales Week', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recordings and Downloads (Audio)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Swift, Taylor', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-05T18:56:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/69656aff-ec9f-5cd1-bd5f-7754e7c2c5db', 'word_count': 395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69656aff-ec9f-5cd1-bd5f-7754e7c2c5db'}, {'abstract': 'The new Converse flagship and the new Dr. Martens store offer customizing and new options.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/fashion/06CRITIC.html', 'snippet': 'The new Converse flagship and the new Dr. Martens store offer customizing and new options.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FIRST, some words about the Chuck Taylor, a real shrug of a shoe.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/06/fashion/06critic_4/06critic_4-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/06/fashion/06critic_4/06critic_4-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01