erial': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/72ae3334-4ac3-561f-9672-23a5f2397b7b', 'word_count': 169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72ae3334-4ac3-561f-9672-23a5f2397b7b'}, {'abstract': 'Because of a legal quirk, those protesting the detention of Kremlin opponents must keep their distance from one another, or their political opposites, or face arrest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/world/europe/06moscow.html', 'snippet': 'Because of a legal quirk, those protesting the detention of Kremlin opponents must keep their distance from one another, or their political opposites, or face arrest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW \x97 Over the past three days, after the detention of Boris Y. Nemtsov, a prominent opposition figure, at a New Year’s Eve rally, a cat-and-mouse game has been unfolding on Moscow’s streets, still festooned with holiday decorations.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/06/world/06moscow_337-395/MOSCOW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/06/world/06moscow_337-395/MOSCOW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/06/world/06moscow_337-395/MOSCOW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Russia, Dissent Turns Into a Solo Act', 'kicker': 'Moscow Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dissent Turns Into a Solo Act', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Prisoners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moscow (Russia)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T03:17:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew E. Kramer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Kramer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1db8cb87-c052-564a-a039-9d5f992ca534', 'word_count': 703, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1db8cb87-c052-564a-a039-9d5f992ca534'}, {'abstract': 'Mats Zuccarello scored in overtime, the first N.H.L. goal of his career, and provided a happy finish to the first half of the Rangers’ season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/sports/hockey/06nhl.html', 'snippet': 'Mats Zuccarello scored in overtime, the first N.H.L. goal of his career, and provided a happy finish to the first half of the Rangers’ season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are times when being short can help a hockey player, as Mats Zuccarello, the 5-foot-7 Norwegian rookie, demonstrated Wednesday night with the overtime goal that gave the Rangers a 2-1 victory over Carolina at Madison Square Garden.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/06/sports/subNHL/subNHL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 741, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rangers’ Rookie Makes First Goal a Winner', 'kicker': 'N.H.L. Roundup', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rangers’ Rookie Makes First Goal a Winner', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Rangers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carolina Hurricanes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Hockey League', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T03:28:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/875d9e19-aa50-5930-9a34-d8f7f82aecf5', 'word_count': 566, 'uri': 'nyt://article/875d9e19-aa50-5930-9a34-d8f7f82aecf5'}, {'abstract': 'An anarchist group linked to earlier letter bombs claimed responsibility on Wednesday for an explosion that damaged a court building in Athens last week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/world/europe/06briefs-Anarchists.html', 'snippet': 'An anarchist group linked to earlier letter bombs claimed responsibility on Wednesday for an explosion that damaged a court building in Athens last week.', 'lead_paragraph': ' An anarchist group that has claimed responsibility for sending letter bombs to foreign embassies in November claimed responsibility on Wednesday for an explosion that damaged a court building in Athens last week. The group, the Conspiracy of the Cells of Fire, said in a statement that the blast was in protest of the coming trial of 13 people suspected of belonging to the group. In the statement, published by the online Indymedia news network, it threatened the judges and vowed that “for every year in prison given to our brothers, we will plant a kilogram of explosives in your front yards, in your cars, in your offices.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Greece: Anarchists Claim Court Blast', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE'