, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Public Workers: The New Scapegoats?', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Public Workers: The New Scapegoats?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Employees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T04:35:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1acafbf-2207-57de-8bc0-c6143e95fdc1', 'word_count': 849, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1acafbf-2207-57de-8bc0-c6143e95fdc1'}, {'abstract': 'Getting the government out of the housing market would mean saying goodbye to the 30-year mortgage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/opinion/06mclean.html', 'snippet': 'Getting the government out of the housing market would mean saying goodbye to the 30-year mortgage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AS we all move forward with our New Year’s resolutions, it’s a good time to remember the promises our politicians have been making about the American mortgage market. The Obama administration, at a conference last August on the future of housing finance, pledged to have, come January, a plan for Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, the mortgage giants that are now wards of the government. Congressional Republicans, in their recent position paper, made an even bolder resolution: to build a mortgage market that “does not rely on government guarantees” and “does not make private investors and creditors wealthy while saddling taxpayers with losses.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/06/opinion/0106oped_mclean/0106oped_mclean-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/06/opinion/0106oped_mclean/0106oped_mclean-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/06/opinion/0106oped_mclean/0106oped_mclean-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Wants a 30-Year Mortgage?', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Wants a 30-Year Mortgage?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal National Mortgage Association Fannie Mae', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corp', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T04:35:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bethany McLean', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bethany', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McLean', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/ecd19c31-1884-5826-8406-3dc011ee5334', 'word_count': 1151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ecd19c31-1884-5826-8406-3dc011ee5334'}, {'abstract': 'A reader responds to a recent op-ed that called for reform of the laws governing material support for terrorism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/