6/learning-with-the-ipad/', 'snippet': '6 Q’s About the News | Why are educators, parents and scholars divided about the use of iPads in schools?', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOW is Roslyn High School on Long Island using iPads in a pilot program?HOW would you feel if your school adopted these devices for the classroom?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Learning With the iPad', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T08:56:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/56978edb-3dec-59ff-8811-0f2de1b9e2c5', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56978edb-3dec-59ff-8811-0f2de1b9e2c5'}, {'abstract': 'Student Opinion | What do you think about a new edition of “Huckleberry Finn” that replaces the 219 instances of a racial epithet with the word “slave” instead?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/should-the-racial-epithets-be-removed-from-huck-finn/', 'snippet': 'Student Opinion | What do you think about a new edition of “Huckleberry Finn” that replaces the 219 instances of a racial epithet with the word “slave” instead?', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new edition of “Huckleberry Finn” removes the 219 uses of the word “nigger” in the novel and replaces them with “slave.” The professor who proposed the idea said he did it because he was hesitant to pronounce the word when he was teaching the book, and because he wanted an edition “not for scholars, but for younger people and general readers.” What do you think about tinkering with a literary classic like this? Have you studied “Huck” in school? How did your teacher handle the language? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Should the Racial Epithets Be Removed From ‘Huck Finn’?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T09:34:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fabd6bc7-aeeb-5788-b5d9-e616da1880f7', 'word_count': 564, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fabd6bc7-aeeb-5788-b5d9-e616da1880f7'}, {'abstract': 'Obama administration envoys met with their Chinese counterparts amid Seoul’s rejection of a North Korean offer for peace talks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/world/asia/07korea.html', 'snippet': 'Obama administration envoys met with their Chinese counterparts amid Seoul’s rejection of a North Korean offer for peace talks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING \x97 On the heels of a North Korean plea for negotiations to end the crisis on the Korean Peninsula, Obama administration envoys central to stalled six-party talks met Thursday with their Chinese counterparts, and one said he was hopeful that serious talks on North Korea would begin soon.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/world/07KOREA/07KOREA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/world/07KOREA/07KOREA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75