le/326d5228-1a62-5a82-a8e9-63a4fcdf457c', 'word_count': 482, 'uri': 'nyt://article/326d5228-1a62-5a82-a8e9-63a4fcdf457c'}, {'abstract': 'Iran’s state-controlled media said the woman had espionage equipment concealed in her teeth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/world/middleeast/07iran.html', 'snippet': 'Iran’s state-controlled media said the woman had espionage equipment concealed in her teeth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TEHRAN \x97 Two state-controlled media outlets reported Thursday that Iranian authorities had arrested a 55-year-old American woman who entered the country from Armenia for spying, after finding a microphone in her teeth. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iran Detains American Woman as Spy, Reports Say', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iran Reports Detaining an American Woman for Spying', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T14:22:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Yong and Alan Cowell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b1719da-118f-51c5-af9c-91b0590684a6', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b1719da-118f-51c5-af9c-91b0590684a6'}, {'abstract': 'A French minister said the case appeared to involve the automaker’s electric car technology.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/business/global/07renault.html', 'snippet': 'A French minister said the case appeared to involve the automaker’s electric car technology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS \x97 The French government said on Thursday that it would seek to bolster industrial secrecy rules after the automaker Renault suspended three executives, including a member of its management committee, who are suspected of compromising the electric car technology on which the company is betting its future.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/business/global/07renault-inline/07renault-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/business/global/07renault-inline/07renault-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/07/business/global/07renault-inline/07renault-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Executives at Renault Suspended in Secrecy Breach', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Renault Suspends 3 Suspected of Revealing Secrets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Renault SA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Vehicles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Industrial Espionage', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T14:26:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Saltmarsh and David Jolly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saltmarsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jolly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/80916510-6251-5514-bea2-822570310160', 'word_count': 877, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80916510-6251-5514-bea2-822570310160'}, {'abstract': 'While others debate the merits of changing Mark Twain’s novel, the contrarian comedian argues that the alterations do not go far enough.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/colbert-sells-revised-huck-finn-down-the-river/', 'snippet': 'While others debate the merits of changing Mark Twain’s novel, the contrarian comedian argues that the alterations do not go far enough.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Earlier at Room for Debate, nine writers including Jane Smiley and Francine Prose pondered the merits of an Alabama publisher’s revised edition of “Adventures of Huckleberry Finn” that replaces Mark Twain’s use of a racial epithet with the word “slave” on 219 occasions. And on Wednesday night Stephen Colbert added his contrarian voice to the mix, arguing on “The Colbert Report” that the changes to “Huckleberry Finn” do not go far enough. “Who knows,” Mr. Colbert asks, “what other words it contains that are O.K. now that someday might be offensive?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Colbert Sells Revised ‘Huck Finn’ Down the River', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clemens, Samuel Langhorne', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T14:26:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_n