://article/599d2141-99d3-504a-88d3-b9678448680c'}, {'abstract': 'According to a report in Vanity Fair, Julian Assange threatened The Guardian with a lawsuit when it made plans to release the leaked State Department cables before he was ready.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/vanity-fair-wikileaks-deal-was-nearly-derailed/', 'snippet': 'According to a report in Vanity Fair, Julian Assange threatened The Guardian with a lawsuit when it made plans to release the leaked State Department cables before he was ready.', 'lead_paragraph': 'According to a report in Vanity Fair written by Sarah Ellison and published on Thursday, Julian Assange, the founder of WikiLeaks, threatened the British newspaper The Guardian with a lawsuit when it made plans to release State Department cables before he was prepared to do so.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vanity Fair: WikiLeaks’s Deal Was Nearly Derailed', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classified Information and State Secrets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assange, Julian P', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Guardian (British Newspaper)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T15:16:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Carr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a83894c-16f9-53d7-a274-3403ef4de84b', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a83894c-16f9-53d7-a274-3403ef4de84b'}, {'abstract': 'A pricey pair of headphones from Monster, with a Miles Davis legacy.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/monster-meets-miles-again/', 'snippet': 'A pricey pair of headphones from Monster, with a Miles Davis legacy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The “Head Monster” was decidedly restrained at this year’s Consumer Electronics Show in Las Vegas. And that might be a first.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monster Meets Miles, Again', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Headphones and Headsets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Miles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Monster Cable', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T15:24:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/35082d6c-869d-56b4-adbe-545bc723b801', 'word_count': 294, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35082d6c-869d-56b4-adbe-545bc723b801'}, {'abstract': 'The founder of Backpacker Concierge, a travel consultancy specializing in the Middle East, shares his favorite Cairo addresses.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/the-insider-eric-monkaba/', 'snippet': 'The founder of Backpacker Concierge, a travel consultancy specializing in the Middle East, shares his favorite Cairo addresses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though he started out in advertising in Manhattan, Eric Monkaba has always had a bit of wanderlust. While vacationing in Morocco a few years back, Monkaba shared a\xa0taxi\xa0in the High Atlas Mountains with a few British tour managers who opened his eyes to a different career path. Soon enough he was leading tours through the deserts of Egypt, Jordan and Morocco. After studying Arabic at the University of Damascus and a stint working with street children in\xa0Yemen, he started Backpacker Concierge, a personalized travel consultancy specializing in responsible tourism in the Middle East, mainly in Egypt and Jordan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Insider | Eric Monkaba', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T15:30:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maura Egan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7194e30e-1f4f-534a-bc33-b33dedd77aed', 'word_count': 448, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7194e30e-1f4f-534a-bc33-b33dedd77aed'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s post pairs the poem “Tomato Pies, 25 Cents” with a 2007 article about Sunset Park, Brooklyn, “A Neighborhood Balks at a Chain Restaurant.”', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/poetry-pairing-jan-6-2011/', 'snippet': 'This week’s post pairs the poem “Tomato Pies, 25 Cents” with a 2007 article about Sunset Park, Brooklyn, “A Neighborhood Balks at a Chain Restaurant.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In our weekly “Poetry Pairing” series, we collaborate with the Poetry Foundation to feature a work from its American Life in Poetry project alongside content from The Times that somehow echoes, extends or challenges the poem’s themes. Each poem is introduced briefly by Ted Kooser, a former United States poet laureate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poetry Pairing | Jan. 6, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T15:32:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/138fa062-b2af-5e7c-ad01-ce33d4184257', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/138fa062-b2af-5e7c-ad01-ce33d4184257'}, {'abstract': 'The latest data on food prices, niche dating sites and other consumer-focused items from Wednesday’s Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/thursday-reading-rising-food-prices/', 'snippet': 'The latest data on food prices, niche dating sites and other consumer-focused items from Wednesday’s Times.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thursday Reading: Rising Food Prices', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T15:32:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Saranow Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'Saranow', 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/487afc29-9119-5605-a131-8fd7f2e231eb', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/487afc29-9119-5605-a131-8fd7f2e231eb'}, {'abstract': 'Quentin Tarantino’s top 10 list of 2010’s movies omitted a certain “Somewhere,” by Sofia Coppola, even though it won the top prize at a film festival he judged.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/tarantino-forgets-a-friend/', 'snippet': 'Quentin Tarantino’s top 10 list of 2010’s movies omitted a certain “Somewhere,” by Sofia Coppola, even though it won the top prize at a film festival he judged.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After all the attention generated by his list of top movies of 2010, Quentin Tarantino is now sheepishly admitting he forgot one: “Somewhere,” directed by his friend (and ex) Sofia Coppola, which received the Golden Lion at the Venice Film Festival under his watch as president of the jury. The folks at Vulture contacted Mr. Tarantino to ask him about the omission. “I’m a little embarrassed by it, actually,” he told them, explaining that he did not include the films from Venice in his considerations because “the world knows how I feel about those films already.” Mr. Tarantino was also surprised by how much attention his list generated. “If I’d known it was going to get out, I’d have put it in my top 10,” he said. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tarantino Forgets a Friend', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venice International Film Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coppola, Sofia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tarantino, Quentin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T15:34:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/549feb0f-b914-54d7-a63f-e01da42e3bed', 'word_count': 260, 'uri': 'nyt://article/549feb0f-b914-54d7-a63f-e01da42e3bed'}, {'abstract': 'Still a big bust.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/trend-versus-cycle-texas-edition/', 'snippet': 'Still a big bust.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I see that various people, including Ryan Avent, are going on about how Texas is a great success story of this recession despite an unemployment rate about the same as New York’s. I think what’s going on is a misunderstanding of the difference between trends and cycles.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Trend Versus Cycle, Texas Edition', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T15:36:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe97900c-f4c5-5e3b-9b93-1d1a451d13af', 'word_count': 240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe97900c-f4c5-5e3b-9b93-1d1a451d13af'}, {'abstract': 'A retiree reflects on his dependency on home health aides.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/the-miracle-workers/', 'snippet': 'A retiree reflects on his dependency on home health aides.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some of the most compelling first-person accounts of family caregiving I’ve ever read come from the “Narrative Matters’’ section of the policy journal Health Affairs. Often these stories are written by medical professionals who have found all of their training and expertise useless when faced with responsibility for a parent or spouse. What they thought they knew they didn’t, and what they did know got lost in the emotional upheaval of caring for a loved one rather than a patient.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Miracle Workers', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Handicapped', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Home Health Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Health Affairs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T15:38:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Gross', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gross', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6818591-3f81-537b-b689-64beba9b6763', 'word_count': 894, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6818591-3f81-537b-b689-64beba9b6763'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. De Niro, a co-founder of the Tribeca Film Festival, has twice served as head of the Cannes jury, and starred in two films, “Taxi Driver” and “The Mission,” that won the Palme d’or.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/anytime-anywhere-robert-de-niro-to-serve-as-president-of-cannes-jury/', 'snippet': 'Mr. De Niro, a co-founder of the Tribeca Film Festival, has twice served as head of the Cannes jury, and starred in two films, “Taxi Driver” and “The Mission,” that won the Palme d’or.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yes, Robert De Niro is already a co-founder of the Tribeca Film Festival, but do you want to be the one to tell him he can’t be involved in more than one cinematic gala at a time? The organizers of the Cannes Film Festival clearly didn’t mind Mr. De Niro’s other affiliation, announcing on Thursday that he will serve as the president of the jury for its coming 64th annual run, which will be May 11-22. Mr. De Niro, the quintessential New York actor whose films run the gamut from “Mean Streets” to “Little Fockers,” served as the president of the Cannes jury twice in the 1980s, and starred in two films that won the festival’s Palme d’or prize: Martin Scorsese‘s “Taxi Driver,” in 1976, and Roland Joffe’s “Mission,” in 1986.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Anytime, Anywhere: Robert De Niro to Serve as President of Cannes Jury', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cannes International Film Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'De Niro, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T15:39:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8924304-2bbc-56b9-ba5a-fb29893c4014', 'word_count': 259, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8924304-2bbc-56b9-ba5a-fb29893c4014'}, {'abstract': 'Artists across a variety of disciplines lecture on oddball subjects upon which they had fixated.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/nocturnalist-fixations-in-abundance/', 'snippet': 'Artists across a variety of disciplines lecture on oddball subjects upon which they had fixated.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nocturnalist | Fixations in Abundance', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subj