'_id': 'nyt://article/b5d1a4d3-a890-5037-a953-c70729c6fea1', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5d1a4d3-a890-5037-a953-c70729c6fea1'}, {'abstract': 'The English comedian Daniel Kitson explores questions about loss and love and the pursuit of meaning that are too complicated to answer completely.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/theater/07kitson.html', 'snippet': 'The English comedian Daniel Kitson explores questions about loss and love and the pursuit of meaning that are too complicated to answer completely.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Daniel Kitson was about 9, he told his teacher that he was thinking of killing himself. At some point over the next few decades, he forgot this had ever happened, until just over a week ago when his mother reminded him. Asked in an interview if he really was suicidal or if it was just a plea for attention, he said with a merry chuckle, “I have no idea.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07kitson_1/07kitson_1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07kitson_1/07kitson_1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07kitson_1/07kitson_1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Exploring That Old Knee-Slapper, Suicide', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Exploring That Perennial Knee-Slapper, Suicide', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Under the Radar Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T21:35:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Zinoman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinoman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfd5efca-63a4-5398-87e0-583c3a7c7078', 'word_count': 1085, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfd5efca-63a4-5398-87e0-583c3a7c7078'}, {'abstract': 'The actor will receive the Producers Guild of America’s humanitarian award, while the Directors Guild will celebrate its 75th anniversary.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/producers-not-paparazzi-to-honor-penn/', 'snippet': 'The actor will receive the Producers Guild of America’s humanitarian award, while the Directors Guild will celebrate its 75th anniversary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sean Penn will be celebrated for his humanitarianism (towards everyone but paparazzi) by the Producers Guild of America, which will bestow him with the Stanley Kramer Award, the group announced on Thursday. Named for the producer and director who made “High Noon,” “The Caine Mutiny” and “Guess Who’s Coming to Dinner,” and was apparently known as “Hollywood’s Conscience,” the prize honors “a motion picture, producer or other individual, whose achievement or contribution illuminates provocative social issues in an accessible and elevating fashion.” It will be given to Mr. Penn at the guild’s ceremony on Jan. 22. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Producers, Not Paparazzi, to Honor Penn', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kramer, Stanley', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Penn, Sean', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Directors Guild of America', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Producers Guild of America', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T21:40:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c2b29ab-72b5-5d35-9090-ba81d950f7fc', 'word_count': 270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c2b29ab-72b5-5d35-9090-ba81d950f7fc'}, {'abstract': 'Philosopher Hedge Fund King | The Mystery Fourth Fund | S.E.C Paper Pileup', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/buzz-tracker-4/', 'snippet': 'Philosopher Hedge Fund King | The Mystery Fourth Fund | S.E.C Paper Pileup', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Philosopher King  The best-performing hedge fund in 2010 was Structured Portfolio Management, which is run by a former philosophy teacher. The hedge fund, with $2 billion in assets under management, returned 50 percent in the first 10 months of 2010, reports Bloomberg Markets magazine.Bloomberg Markets ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Buzz Tracker', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T21:40:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7c7aa15-af60-5262-859c-d272f2aa2a2e', 'word_count': 340, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7c7aa15-af60-5262-859c-d272f2aa2a2e'}, {'abstract': 'Questions about writing thank-you notes in the voice of toddlers and hotel dining room breakfasts in pj’s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/fashion/09Social.html', 'snippet': 'Questions about writing thank-you notes in the voice of toddlers and hotel dining room breakfasts in pj’s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My 2-year-old attends birthday parties for her preschool pals. She then receives thank-you notes for the gifts we bring, written “by” her friends and addressed to her, even though none of them can speak (much less write): “I especially loved the markers since I color every day after my nap.” I expected to make my kids write thank-you notes once they could write their own names, but should I be writing them in the meantime, in my daughter’s voice, to kids who can’t read? ', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2009/10/25/style/25social-1/thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2009/10/25/style/25social-1/thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Babes in Letters', 'kicker': 'Social Q’s', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Babes in Letters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T21:42:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip Galanes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Galanes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ecf9211-f296-507d-90c4-5e390a26ddf8', 'word_count': 553, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ecf9211-f296-507d-90c4-5e390a26ddf8'}, {'abstr