iod rooms in the American Wing.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07rohlfs_span/ROHLFS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07rohlfs_span/ROHLFS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07rohlfs_span/ROHLFS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 711, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07rohlfs_span/ROHLFS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07rohlfs_span/ROHLFS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Marriage of Design, Aged in the Wood', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Marriage of Design, Aged in the Wood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chairs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Furniture', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tables', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T22:47:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fc887ca-1cca-5b74-91b4-7d37729ae8b8', 'word_count': 983, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fc887ca-1cca-5b74-91b4-7d37729ae8b8'}, {'abstract': 'The New York State Board of Regents has named a 16-member advisory group to address the controversial issues surrounding the selling of art and artifacts by museums to cover operating expenses, known as deaccessioning.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/arts/design/07arts-PANELTOADVIS_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The New York State Board of Regents has named a 16-member advisory group to address the controversial issues surrounding the selling of art and artifacts by museums to cover operating expenses, known as deaccessioning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York State Board of Regents has named a 16-member advisory group to address the controversial issues surrounding the selling of art and artifacts by museums to cover operating expenses, known as deaccessioning. The list of members, reported on Wednesday at artsjournal.com, includes Thelma Golden, director of the Studio Museum in Harlem; Martin E. Sullivan, director of the National Portrait Gallery; Anne W. Ackerson, director of the Museum Association of New York; and Henry Lanman, associate general counsel of the Museum of Modern Art. The group, the Ad Hoc Advisory Committee on Deaccessioning, has met once so far, on Dec. 15, and is led by Carole Huxley, who for 24 years served as deputy commissioner for cultural education. “The Regents really want to hear what the issues are and really want to get it right,” said Michael Botwinick, director of the Hudson River Museum in Yonkers. Debate over the question of how to handle deaccessioning prompted the creation of the Regents committee, said Merryl H. Tisch, the chancellor of the Board of Regents, in a telephone interview on Thursday.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Panel to Advise Regents on Sales by Museums', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Panel to Advise Regents On Sales by Museums', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T22:49:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/0537da79-0c07-534f-af9a-ab44edfec61a', 'word_count': 183, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0537da79-0c07-534f-af9a-ab44edfec61a'}, {'abstract': 'A selected guide to classical music and opera performances in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/arts/music/07classical.html', 'snippet': 'A selected guide to classical music and opera performances in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Classical', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Classical Music/Opera Listings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Listings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salzburg Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T22:50:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0efe267-ab29-53f4-936b-0f6a4adca459', 'word_count': 1375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0efe267-ab29-53f4-936b-0f6a4adca459'}, {'abstract': 'Eiko & Koma and members of the Paul Taylor Dance Company are among the performers scheduled to appear next Friday at the Brooklyn Academy of Music in honor of the 80th birthday of Charles L. Reinhart.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/arts/dance/07arts-FOOTNOTES_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Eiko & Koma and members of the Paul Taylor Dance Company are among the performers scheduled to appear next Friday at the Brooklyn Academy of Music in honor of the 80th birthday of Charles L. Reinhart.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eiko & Koma and members of the Paul Taylor Dance Company are among the performers scheduled to appear next Friday at the Brooklyn Academy of Music in honor of the 80th birthday of Charles L. Reinhart, president of the American Dance Festival. The evening is free but reservations are required: (212) 586-1925. ... The Works & Process series is trying something new for its latest installment on Sunday, a performance by members of Pacific Northwest Ballet at the Guggenheim Museum. The sold-out event, featuring excerpts from the company’s coming new staging of “Giselle,” will be live-streamed on the Web. Viewers can also take part in an online chat at the same time. The performance and chat will be at ustream.tv/channel/worksandprocess.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Footnotes', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Footnotes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T22:51:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb8ebcbd-1568-5cb1-bbc8-d434aefce93c', 'word_count': 122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb8ebcbd-1568-5cb1-bbc8-d434aefce93c'}, {'abstract': 'As the “Modern Family” star Eric Stonestreet, below, wrote on his Twitter account on Thursday morning, “Beating the People’s Choice Awards in the ratings is kinda better than winning a People’s Choice Award.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/arts/television/07arts-FEWERVIEWERS_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'As the “Modern Family” star Eric Stonestreet, below, wrote on his Twitter account on Thursday morning, “Beating the People’s Choice Awards in the ratings is kinda better than winning a People’s Choice Award.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the “Modern Family” star Eric Stonestreet, below, wrote on his Twitter account on Thursday morning, “Beating the People’s Choice Awards in the ratings is kinda better than winning a People’s Choice Award.” That remark came the morning after “Glee” bested “Modern Family” in the Favorite TV Comedy category at the People’s Choice Awards on Wednesday night, and after the awards show itself was beaten by several other prime-time programs, including “Modern Family.” A two-hour episode of “Law & Order: SVU” (10.7 million viewers) helped lift NBC to the top of the ratings on Wednesday night, according to Nielsen’s estimates, and ABC had strong performances from its comedies “The Middle” (9.3 million) and “Modern Family” (11.8 million). CBS was second for the night, but its new reality competition “Live to Dance” (7.8 million), with Paula Abdul, declined in its second outing, and the network’s broadcast of the 37th annual People’s Choice Awards (9.5 million) was down considerably from last year. ABC ranked third for the night, and Fox finished in fourth place with back-to-back episodes of “Human Target” (6.3 million).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fewer Viewers Honor People’s Choice Awards', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fewer Viewers Honor People’s Choice Awards', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T22:52:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/dcffee9a-30ec-5d9f-b923-6b734e300e2e', 'word_count': 182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dcffee9a-30ec-5d9f-b923-6b734e300e2e'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers said an extinct, flightless bird that came from Jamaica and belonged to the ibis family used its wings as a powerful club.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/science/11obbird.html', 'snippet': 'Researchers said an extinct, flightless bird that came from Jamaica and belonged to the ibis family used its wings as a powerful club.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An extinct bird from Jamaica used its wings as a powerful clublike weapon, according to a new study. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/11/science/11obwings/11obwings-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 909, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/11/science/11obwings/11obwings-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/11/science/11obwings/11obwings-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bony Wings That Went ‘Pow! Smack! Whomp!’', 'kicker': 'Observatory', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bony Wings That Went ‘Pow! Smack! Whomp!’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paleontology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fossils', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T22:53:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sindya N. Bhanoo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sindya', 'middlename': 'N.', 'lastname': 'Bhanoo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93256c23-c58c-51fc-8148-b40a8f389b47', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93256c23-c58c-51fc-8148-b40a8f389b47'}, {'abstract': 'In “The Cape,” a new superhero series on NBC, privatization is one of the villains.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/arts/television/07cape.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Cape,” a new superhero series on NBC, privatization is one of the villains.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Gotham of Christopher Nolan’s Batman movies (“The Dark Knight,” “Batman Begins”) is a bleak-looking place where the light is charcoal, and the mood is caught between 1970s Manhattan and Dante’s “Inferno.” Crime rates are high; so too is the general misery index. A sense of grim deterioration is ever present.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07cape_span/CAPE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07cape_span/CAPE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargehe