The Listings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cannes International Film Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMALS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T22:55:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/feeb82e9-7f23-52f0-a50b-d824227fa448', 'word_count': 2946, 'uri': 'nyt://article/feeb82e9-7f23-52f0-a50b-d824227fa448'}, {'abstract': '“Bob’s Burgers,” which has its debut on Sunday, has a lackadaisical vibe; its humor, no matter how anarchic, slides by in a deadpan monotone.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/arts/television/07burgers.html', 'snippet': '“Bob’s Burgers,” which has its debut on Sunday, has a lackadaisical vibe; its humor, no matter how anarchic, slides by in a deadpan monotone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Watching “Bob’s Burgers,” Fox’s new Sunday night cartoon, is a good-news, bad-news proposition. The bad news is that the show, the network’s latest dysfunctional-animated-family comedy, is at best mildly funny, at least in the first episode (the only one provided to critics). The good news? It’s not another grating half-hour from the mind of Seth MacFarlane, who already occupies three of the five slots in Fox’s “Animation Domination” lineup.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07burgers_span/BURGERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 325, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07burgers_span/BURGERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 325}}, {'rank': 0