ly under threat from resurgent rivals. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/business/7TOYOTA/7TOYOTA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 927, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/business/7TOYOTA/7TOYOTA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/07/business/7TOYOTA/7TOYOTA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Toyota Aims to Remain King of the Hybrids', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Toyota Aims to Remain King of the Hybrids', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Vehicles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hybrid Vehicles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toyota Motor Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T22:56:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ecacaaac-21c7-5131-bba8-934d52392cf2', 'word_count': 1254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ecacaaac-21c7-5131-bba8-934d52392cf2'}, {'abstract': 'Once an elegant stretch of brick and marble houses, St. Marks Place is now famously funky. But a few of the old beauties survive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/realestate/09scapes.html', 'snippet': 'Once an elegant stretch of brick and marble houses, St. Marks Place is now famously funky. But a few of the old beauties survive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN the 1830s St. Marks Place was one of New York’s best addresses, a dignified street of chaste Federal-style mansions for the city’s first families. Today, just three remnants of the street’s golden age still stand, and it is a fair contender for the title of bong capital of the world, with its tumbledown shops for vintage records, punk clothing and drug paraphernalia. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/realestate/streets-1/streets-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/realestate/streets-1/streets-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/realestate/streets-1/streets-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/realestate/streets-1/streets-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Be There or Be Square, 1830 to the Present', 'kicker': 'Streetscapes | St. Marks Place', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Be There or Be Square, 1830 to the Present', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East Village (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T22:57:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Gray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff1135aa-74a7-5ad2-9b02-fdc85035bcfb', 'word_count': 822, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff1135aa-74a7-5ad2-9b02-fdc85035bcfb'}, {'abstract': 'A selected guide to the visual arts in New York, the area and the East.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/arts/design/07art.html', 'snippet': 'A selected guide to the visual arts in New York, the area and the East.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Art', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Museum and Gallery Listings for Jan. 7-13', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Listings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vietnam War', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Buddhism', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Spanish Civil War (1936-39)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T23:01:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/278af58e-264c-52c6-b200-220701f1003c', 'word_count': 6227, 'uri': 'nyt://article/278af58e-264c-52c6-b200-220701f1003c'}, {'abstract': 'The aftermath of Hurricane Karina is explored through music and dance in a show from the company Universes.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/under-the-radar-5-questions-about-ameriville/', 'snippet': 'The aftermath of Hurricane Karina is explored through music and dance in a show from the company Universes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s not much to sing and dance about when it comes to Hurricane Katrina and the devastating path it took through New Orleans. But that’s exactly what happens in “Ameriville,” a piece written and performed by the company Universes (Gamal Abdel Chasten, Mildred Ruiz, William Ruiz and Steven Sapp) and directed by the playwright Chay Yew. The show, part of the Under the Radar Festival, uses hip-hop choreography and assorted musical genres — blues, gospel, jazz — to examine issues of race and class as they played out across the country after Katrina hit. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Under the Radar: 5 Questions About ‘Ameriville’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T23:02:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b806d60e-7b5d-51ff-8c20-f7430c4e330c', 'word_count': 595, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b806d60e-7b5d-51ff-8c20-f7430c4e330c'}, {'abstract': 'A selected guide to jazz performances in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/arts/music/07jazz.html', 'snippet': 'A selected guide to jazz performances in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jazz', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jazz Listings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Listings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Winter Jazzfest', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'NIGHTCLUBS AND CABARETS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T23:04:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc811ca8-492a-5437-8a37-2f7130780cc1', 'word_count': 2817, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc811ca8-492a-5437-8a37-2f7130780cc1'}, {'abstract': 'A day after the return of Moktada al-Sadr, supporters spoke with certainty about their future.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/world/middleeast/07iraq.html', 'snippet': 'A day after the return of Moktada al-Sadr, supporters spoke with certainty about their future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NAJAF, Iraq \x97 It was fewer than seven lines of handwritten Arabic on a small piece of paper, bearing his stamp. But with it, Moktada al-Sadr, son of a revered ayatollah and scourge of the American occupation, made clear on Thursday that he was in charge again of a group that has begun shaping Iraq as the United States withdraws.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/world/07IRAQ1/07IRAQ1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 695, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/world/07IRAQ1/07IRAQ1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/07/world/07IRAQ1/07IRAQ1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sadrists Project Confidence in Iraq', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cleric’s Followers in Iraq Project New Confidence in Sadrist Movement', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shiite Muslims', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sadr, Moktada Al-', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maliki, Nuri Kamal al-', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T23:08:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Shadid', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shadid', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5672231c-13bc-5211-85ad-d831448d10fa', 'word_count': 1112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5672231c-13bc-5211-85ad-d831448d10fa'}, {'abstract': 'Three new series — “Downton Abbey, “Shameless,” and “Episodes” — continue the Britishification of American TV.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/arts/television/07shameless.html', 'snippet': 'Three new series — “Downton Abbey, “Shameless,” and “Episodes” — continue the Britishification of American TV.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Watching “Downton Abbey,” a thoroughly enjoyable British series on PBS about masters and servants in a stately home on the eve of World War I, is a bit like buying a trunk at Restoration Hardware. Both are copies, of course, but so shrewdly and exactingly recreated that they are almost as gratifying as the original.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07shameless_span/SHAMELESS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 355, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07shameless_span/SHAMELESS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 355}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07shameless_span/SHAMELESS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07shameless_span/SHAMELESS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07shameless_span/SHAMELESS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sincerest Forms of Flattery for British Shows', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sincerest Forms of Flattery for British Shows', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting Service', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Showtime', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T23:10:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alessandra Stanley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alessandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stanley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bc4a5ad-88ec-5cfb-8d54-21eb5d0c4eea', 'word_count': 1289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bc4a5ad-88ec-5cfb-8d54-21eb5d0c4eea'}, {'abstract': 'In Abel Ferrara’s “Go Go Tales,” a Manhattan strip club owned by Willem Dafoe and run by Bob Hoskins faces eviction by Sylvia Miles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/movies/07gogo.html', 'snippet': 'In Abel Ferrara’s “Go Go Tales,” a Manhattan strip club owned by Willem Dafoe and run by Bob Hoskins faces eviction by Sylvia Miles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Heaven, David Byrne likes to sing, is a bar where the band plays your favorite song and everyone leaves the party at the same time. Mostly, though, “heaven is a place, a place where nothing, nothing ever happens,” which pretty much describes the usual scene at Ray Ruby’s Paradise Lounge. A gorgeously tawdry nirvana set in Manhattan, Paradise Lounge is an old-school strip club run by a soulful dreamer, Ray Ruby (a wonderful Willem Dafoe), who with love and not enough money is struggling to keep his people and parts in play — a beautiful metaphor for the filmmaking hard times faced by the likes of Abel Ferrara.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07gogo_1/07gogo_1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07gogo_1/07gogo_1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07gogo_1/GOGO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07gogo_1/GOGO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07gogo_1/GOGO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Bar Where Everybody Knows Your Pole Dance', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Go Go Tales'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Bar Where Everybody Knows Your Pole Dance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ferrara, Abel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'NIGHTCLUBS AND CABARETS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dafoe, Willem', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hoskins, Bob', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T23:11:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/96100600-e406-5e2b-a9fc-220b6f938a99', 'word_count': 972, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96100600-e406-5e2b-a9fc-220b6f938a99'}, {'abstract': 'Twitter users in Japan sent a staggering 6,939 Tweets per second over New Year’s Eve, breaking previous usage records on the service.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/56505/', 'snippet': 'Twitter users in Japan sent a staggering 6,939 Tweets per second over New Year’s Eve, breaking previous usage records on the service.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It didn’t take long for Twitter to break its own record as we rolled into 2011. The company said on Thursday that a mere four seconds after midnight in Japan, users set a new all-time record of the number of Tweets sent per second across the social network.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Record-Breaking New Year’s Eve for Twitter', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T23:12:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/44e244e0-495f-568b-89be-74b46cd8fb46', 'word_count': 126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44e244e0-495f-568b-89be-74b46cd8fb46'}, {'abstract': '“The Jewel Thief,” a show at Skidmore College, messes with given definitions of painting, drawing, sculpture and architecture, and by doing so breaks those forms open.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/arts/design/07thief.html', 'snippet': '“The Jewel Thief,” a show at Skidmore College, messes with given definitions of painting, drawing, sculpture and architecture, and by doing so breaks those forms open.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SARATOGA SPRINGS, N.Y. — In the echo chamber that is the New York art world, where one voice can give the illusion of being many, the crusading cry of late is “We need more painting!” As if there were a dearth. Is there? Walk through any of the city’s art museums, and what do see? Paintings everywhere. Visit contemporary galleries all over town, and what do you find? They’re painting packed. But still the cry goes on.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07thief_1/THIEF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 965, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07thief_1/07thief_1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07thief_1/07thief_1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New Sparkle for an Abstract Ensemble', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Sparkle for an Abstract Ensemble', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Skidmore College', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T23:14:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/89a4f59a-0488-5d72-8ffa-ba4625cf517f', 'word_count': 1277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89a4f59a-0488-5d72-8ffa-ba4625cf517f'}, {'abstract': 'Guarantees complicated by changes in the Truth in Lending Act \x97 which add consumer protections but also lengthen the loan-application process.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/realestate/mortgages/09mort.html', 'snippet': 'Guarantees complicated by changes in the Truth in Lending Act \x97 which add consumer protections but also lengthen the loan-application process.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AS mortgage rates have edged higher, many borrowers have been locking in loan rates for a home purchase or refinancing.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/realestate/09mort-graphic/09mo