, except Marshall McLuhan, the subject of this book, figured it out 50 years before anybody ever updated his Facebook page or posted his whereabouts on Twitter.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/Carr-t_CA0/Carr-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 389, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/Carr-t_CA0/Carr-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 389}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/Carr-t_CA0/Carr-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 925, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/Carr-t_CA0/Carr-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/Carr-t_CA0/Carr-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Marshall McLuhan: Media Savant', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Media Savant', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McLuhan, Marshall', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coupland, Douglas', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T23:46:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Carr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ef4036e-1a78-52e3-aa0c-1f8a32b2e3ce', 'word_count': 1759, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ef4036e-1a78-52e3-aa0c-1f8a32b2e3ce'}, {'abstract': 'In Elia Suleiman’s film “The Time That Remains,” a son views his father’s life and experiences in the first Arab-Israeli war.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/movies/07time.html', 'snippet': 'In Elia Suleiman’s film “The Time That Remains,” a son views his father’s life and experiences in the first Arab-Israeli war.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Time That Remains” is described by its subtitle as the “chronicle of a present absentee,” a paradoxical formulation that reveals a lot about the temperament of its director, Elia Suleiman. Mr. Suleiman, an Arab born in the Israeli city Nazareth in 1960 and currently living in Paris, has an exquisite eye for the conflicts and contradictions that bedevil his native city, but he examines them without polemics or sentimentality. “The Time That Remains” has the scope of a historical epic with none of the expected heaviness. It presents a half-century of tragedy and turmoil as a series of mordant comic vignettes. Imagine a heroic poem boiled down to a flurry of witty epigrams, or a martial statue made of origami, and you will have some idea of the improbable way this filmmaker folds big themes into delicate forms.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07time_span/TIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07time_span/TIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07time_span/TIME-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 554, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07time_span/07time_span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07time_span/07time_span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Nazareth, Human Comedy as Wind Rustles the Olive Branches', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'The Time That Remains'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Nazareth, Human Comedy as Wind Rustles the Olive Branches', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Suleiman, Elia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-06T23:52:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/188f4054-455c-5a14-81fe-ac4395e06eeb', 'word_count': 887, 'uri': 'nyt://article/188f4054-455c-5a14-81fe-ac4395e06eeb'}, {'abstract': 'Unlike its struggling rival Borders, Barnes & Noble, the nation’s largest book chain, said on Thursday that it finished the holiday season with stronger sales than expected, reporting its highest comparable store sales increase since 1997.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/barnes-noble-up-borders-down/', 'snippet': 'Unlike its struggling rival Borders, Barnes & Noble, the nation’s largest book chain, said on Thursday that it finished the holiday season with stronger sales than expected, reporting its highest comparable store sales increase since 1997.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Unlike its struggling rival Borders, Barnes & Noble, the nation’s largest book chain, said on Thursday that it finished the holiday season with stronger sales than expected, reporting its highest comparable store sales increase since 1997. That increase was 9.7 percent over the same nine-week period the year before, while overall store sales increased 8.2 percent, to $1.1 billion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Barnes & Noble Up, Borders Down', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T00:01:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman and Michael J. de La Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de La Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/22ab8f6d-b799-5c50-ac0d-7062ea00a3ce', 'word_count': 419, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22ab8f6d-b799-5c50-ac0d-7062ea00a3ce'}, {'abstract': 'Sufism, Pakistan’s most popular branch of Islam, has seen a rise of attacks against its worshipers at shrines.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/06/the-islam-that-hard-liners-hate/', 'snippet': 'Sufism, Pakistan’s most popular branch of Islam, has seen a rise of attacks against its worshipers at shrines.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KASUR, Pakistan — In Pakistan’s heartland, holy men with bells tied to their feet close their eyes and sway to the music. Nearby, rose petals are tossed on tombstones. Free food is distributed to devotees.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Islam That Hard-Liners Hate', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'PAKISTAN', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sufism (Religion)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T00:03:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Huma Imtiaz and Charlotte Buchen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Huma', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Imtiaz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Charlotte', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buchen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/86b50025-a304-565a-9b14-2df329b95f43', 'word_count': 847, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86b50025-a304-565a-9b14-2df329b95f43'}, {'abstract': 'In the comedy “Americatown,” our guides are Roosevelt Microsoft (Cory Howard) and Plymouth Rayban (Jonathan Guggenheim).', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/movies/07america.html', 'snippet': 'In the comedy “Americatown,” our guides are Roosevelt Microsoft (Cory Howard) and Plymouth Rayban (Jonathan Guggenheim).', 'lead_paragraph': 'The doldrums between the beginning of the year and the Oscars is often a dumping ground for bottom-of-the-barrel movies that should probably have stayed there. “Americatown” is no exception: this bizarre sort-of satire featuring insane characters doing incomprehensible things might be forgivable if it were even mildly amusing. It’s not.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07america_span/07america_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 335, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07america_span/07america_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 335}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07america_span/07america_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07america_span/07america_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07america_span/07america_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'These United States', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Americatown'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T00:05:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeannette Catsoulis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeannette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Catsoulis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8be3c3bc-3a76-5c61-ba2a-2d9b2a1bb6b0', 'word_count': 291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8be3c3bc-3a76-5c61-ba2a-2d9b2a1bb6b0'}, {'abstract': '“Season of the Witch” is a 14th-century road movie with 21st-century cuss words.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/movies/07season.html', 'snippet': '“Season of the Witch” is a 14th-century road movie with 21st-century cuss words.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By this point most of us have stopped trying to figure out Nicolas Cage’s career logic. With a résumé that encompasses Oscar winner, action star and indie darling, this supremely talented actor continues to confound us with choices like “Season of the Witch,” a 14th-century road movie with 21st-century cuss words.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07season_span/ROUNDUP-SEASON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 335, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07season_span/ROUNDUP-SEASON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 335}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07season_span/ROUNDUP-SEASON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07season_span/ROUNDUP-SEASON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07season_span/ROUNDUP-SEASON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Deserters From the Crusades', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Season of the Witch'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cage, Nicolas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sena, Dominic', '