title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e614218-675a-50d0-9fa2-224a06925af6', 'word_count': 373, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e614218-675a-50d0-9fa2-224a06925af6'}, {'abstract': 'Adam Bock’s “Small Fire” at Playwrights Horizons, about a woman who falls victim to a mysterious disease, has a crisp economy and precise focus.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/theater/reviews/07small.html', 'snippet': 'Adam Bock’s “Small Fire” at Playwrights Horizons, about a woman who falls victim to a mysterious disease, has a crisp economy and precise focus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Life’s darkest terrors and most visceral delights both play a role in “A Small Fire,” a new drama by Adam Bock that opened Thursday night at Playwrights Horizons. This low-key, quietly moving play features a protagonist who falls victim to a mysterious, debilitating disease — cue the violins — but also features a frank, strenuous sex scene of daring honesty, making for a theatrical combo plate that proves unusually satisfying.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07small_span/SMALL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07small_span/SMALL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07small_span/SMALL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/arts/07small_span/SMALL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name'