 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/us/07emit.html', 'snippet': 'The new Republican governor, Susana Martinez, had said the state regulations were burdensome for industry and harmful to the economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Acting on a campaign promise, New Mexico’s new Republican governor, Susana Martinez, has scuttled a state regulation requiring annual 3 percent cuts in greenhouse gas emissions. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '2 Environment Rules Halted in New Mexico', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '2 Environment Rules Halted in New Mexico', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Mexico', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martinez, Susana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T03:33:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicity Barringer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barringer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec2dbde3-2ca3-50a9-aea2-e2cea336e000', 'word_count': 502, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec2dbde3-2ca3-50a9-aea2-e2cea336e000'}, {'abstract': 'The currency crisis of 1997 taught South Korea how to bounce back from economic hard times, and the lessons learned then might help the United States now, economists say.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/world/asia/07seoul.html', 'snippet': 'The currency crisis of 1997 taught South Korea how to bounce back from economic hard times, and the lessons learned then might help the United States now, economists say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea \x97 When the global financial crisis struck more than two years ago, customers disappeared from the Dongdaemun market, a cramped maze of clothing and fabric shops in the shadow of a medieval city gate. But in contrast to the economic conditions in the United States and Europe, business quickly rebounded here and in the rest of this vibrant, technology-driven nation, a resilience that many South Koreans attribute to their bitter experience of having survived an even worse downturn, the currency crisis of 1997. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/world/07SEOUL/07SEOUL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/world/07SEOUL/07SEOUL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/07/world/07SEOUL/07SEOUL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lessons Learned, South Korea Makes Quick Economic Recovery', 'kicker': 'Memo From Seoul', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lessons Learned, South Korea Makes Quick Economic Recovery', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T03:34:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Fackler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fackler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c143a062-8a7f-50c0-a7c8-670a7922b499', 'word_count': 1079, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c143a062-8a7f-50c0-a7c8-670a7922b499'}, {'abstract': 'Another conservative economic miracle bites the dust, with implications for the nation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/opinion/07krugman.html', 'snippet': 'Another conservative economic miracle bites the dust, with implications for the nation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'These are tough times for state governments. Huge deficits loom almost everywhere, from California to New York, from New Jersey to Texas. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Texas Omen', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Texas Omen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T03:35:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/52c51baf-8c74-5b76-897e-927332918505', 'word_count': 829, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52c51baf-8c74-5b76-897e-927332918505'}, {'abstract': 'Members of the House might have thought they were bringing the Constitution alive by reading it aloud. But they made a crucial error by excising its history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/opinion/07fri2.html', 'snippet': 'Members of the House might have thought they were bringing the Constitution alive by reading it aloud. But they made a crucial error by excising its history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Members of the House might have thought they were bringing the Constitution alive by reading it aloud on Thursday. But they made a crucial error by excising its history. When they chose to deliberately drop the sections that became obsolete or offensive, and which were later amended, they missed a chance to demonstrate that this document is not nailed to the door of the past. It remains vital precisely because it can be reimagined. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The United States Consti ...tion', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The United States Consti ...tion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitutional Amendments', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitutions', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T03:39:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/102fa836-f316-59f1-93b6-e140a298cf78', 'word_count': 441, 'uri': 'nyt://article/102fa836-f316-59f1-93b6-e140a298cf78'}, {'abstract': 'Pope Benedict XVI said Thursday that God’s knowledge, love and “inexhaustible creativity” — not chance — lay at the origins of the universe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/world/europe/07briefs-italy.html', 'snippet': 'Pope Benedict XVI said Thursday that God’s knowledge, love and “inexhaustible creativity” — not chance — lay at the origins of the universe.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Pope Benedict XVI said Thursday that God’s knowledge, love and “inexhaustible creativity” \x97 not chance \x97 lay at the origins of the universe. Scientific concepts suggesting the formation of the universe was accidental, like the Big Bang theory, “only arrive at a certain point,” the pope told worshipers in St. Peter’s Basilica on Thursday. The church no longer says that the process of evolution violates its teachings, but for the church, God remains the ultimate creator. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Italy: Pope Favors Design, Not Accident', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Italy: Pope Favors Design, Not Accident', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benedict XVI', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evolution', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T03:43:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabetta Povoledo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabetta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Povoledo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f4099fb-573a-5950-93c5-f2a820569665', 'word_count': 76, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f4099fb-573a-5950-93c5-f2a820569665'}, {'abstract': 'To get the Legislature to agree to shut unneeded prisons, Governor Cuomo will have to push back hard against the unions that have thwarted reform.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/opinion/07fri3.html', 'snippet': 'To get the Legislature to agree to shut unneeded prisons, Governor Cuomo will have to push back hard against the unions that have thwarted reform.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Andrew Cuomo struck just the right tone on both adult prison reform and juvenile justice reform in his first State of the State address on Wednesday. He said that New York could no longer afford to keep hugely expensive but unneeded facilities open to serve as “an employment program” for upstate residents. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Good Place to Start Cutting', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Good Place to Start Cutting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the State Message (NYS)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Correctional Association of New York', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pataki, George E', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T03:44:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6b3e8b4-26e5-53e2-9632-9eaaae169d0a', 'word_count': 399, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6b3e8b4-26e5-53e2-9632-9eaaae169d0a'}, {'abstract': 'Kazakhstan’s senate voted unanimously on Thursday to ask President Nursultan Nazarbayev to extend his current term in office until 2020.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/world/asia/07briefs-Kazakh.html', 'snippet': 'Kazakhstan’s senate voted unanimously on Thursday to ask President Nursultan Nazarbayev to extend his current term in office until 2020.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Kazakhstan’s senate, generally considered a rubber-stamp chamber, voted unanimously on Thursday to ask President Nursultan Nazarbayev to extend his current term in office until 2020 by holding a referendum on continuing his rule rather than a scheduled election. Mr. Nazarbayev, who has already ruled for almost 20 years, would be 80 years old in 2020. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kazakhstan: Leader’s Long-Term Lease', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | ASIA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kazakhstan: Leader’s Long-Term Lease', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nazarbayev, Nursultan A', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kazakhstan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T03:46:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew E. Kramer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Kramer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e52f4591-dd6c-50ad-8f94-383cff811d91', 'word_count': 56, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e52f4591-dd6c-50ad-8f94-383cff811d91'}, {'abstract': 'The man recognized by foreign powers as the winner of the recent presidential election here, called for a raid to seize the incumbent, Laurent Gbagbo, and “take him somewhere else.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/world/africa/07briefs-Ivory.html', 'snippet': 'The man recognized by foreign powers as the winner of the recent presidential election here, called for a raid to seize the incumbent, Laurent Gbagbo, and “take him somewhere else.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The man recognized by foreign powers as the winner of the recent presidential election here, Alassane Ouattara, called Thursday for a “special operations” raid to seize the incumbent, Laurent Gbagbo, and “take him somewhere else.” Mr. Gbagbo refuses to give up power nearly six weeks after an election the United Nations, the United States and African nations say he lost. Meanwhile, the United States Treasury announced it was freezing Mr. Gbagbo’s assets, and the United Nations said some 31 people had died in Ivory Coast in post-election violence in the last week, bringing the total to more than 200. The United Nations peacekeeping chief in New York called for reinforcements to bolster the embattled mission of close to 10,000 here, which Mr. Gbagbo is seeking to expel. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ivory Coast: Election Victor Wants Raid', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | AFRICA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ivory Coast: Election Victor Wants Raid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ouattara, Alassane D', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gbagbo, Laurent', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ivory Coast', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T03:49:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Nossiter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nossiter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f45b20d-b110-5f2c-9fad-9047223ce442', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f45b20d-b110-5f2c-9fad-9047223ce442'}, {'abstract': 'A tribute to one of America’s great scholarly librarians.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/opinion/07fri4.html', 'snippet': 'A tribute to one of America’s great scholarly librarians.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MORRIS, the name of the online catalog of the Yale Law School Library, is not an acronym. It’s a tribute to Morris Cohen, the school’s longtime librarian and law professor, who died in December at 83 and was one of America’s great scholarly librarians.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2009/10/24/opinion/Bug-Opinion-Appreciations/Bug-Opinion-Appreciations-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2009/10/24/opinion/Bug-Opinion-Appreciations/Bug-Opinion-Appreciations-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Morris Cohen', 'kicker': 'Editorial | Appreciations ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Morris Cohen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libraries and Librarians', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yale University', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T03:50:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lincoln Caplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lincoln', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c0e7354-a745-5b61-bec0-89ca1d6613a6', 'word_count': 361, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c0e7354-a745-5b61-bec0-89ca1d6613a6'}, {'abstract': 'The Department of Defense has identified 4,422 American service members who have died since the start of the Iraq war.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/us/07list.html', 'snippet': 'The Department of Defense has identified 4,422 American service members who have died since the start of the Iraq war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Department of Defense has identified 4,422 American service members who have died since the start of the Iraq war. It confirmed the deaths of the following Americans on Thursday:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Names of the Dead', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Names of the Dead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T03:51:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/af32e707-7134-56fa-8893-442516caed36', 'word_count': 60, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af32e707-7134-56fa-8893-442516caed36'}, {'abstract': 'Egypt’s president told Israel to “adopt tangible confidence-building measures” toward the Palestinian Authority in order to allow a resumption of peace talks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/world/middleeast/07briefs-Egypt.html', 'snippet': 'Egypt’s president told Israel to “adopt tangible confidence-building measures” toward the Palestinian Authority in order to allow a resumption of peace talks.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Egypt’s president, Hosni Mubarak, told Israel on Thursday to “review its positions and policies and to adopt tangible confidence-building measures” toward the Palestinian Authority in order to allow a resumption of peace talks, an Egyptian official told reporters after talks between Mr. Mubarak and Israel’s prime minister, Benjamin Netanyahu. Mr. Netanyahu asked Mr. Mubarak to try to persuade the Palestinians to return to “direct, intensive and serious” negotiations, the Israeli government said in a statement. Israeli-Palestinian talks have been stalled since September. Mr. Mubarak also warned Israel not to carry out a new offensive in Gaza, according to Egypt’s official news agency. The Israeli military said that at least 10 rockets and mortar shells had been fired from Gaza against southern Israel since Jan. 1. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt: A Chat About Arab-Israeli Peace', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | MIDEAST', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Egypt: A Chat About Arab-Israeli Peace', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T03:52:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isabel Kershner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isabel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd79a342-fa98-5613-a91e-3febfc76a425', 'word_count': 129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd79a342-fa98-5613-a91e-3febfc76a425'}, {'abstract': 'Responding to the latest in a series of violent student protests in recent months, President Abdullah Gul met with college youth representatives on Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/world/europe/07briefs-Turkey.html', 'snippet': 'Responding to the latest in a series of violent student protests in recent months, President Abdullah Gul met with college youth representatives on Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Responding to the latest in a series of violent student protests in recent months, President Abdullah Gul met with college youth representatives on Thursday. Urging them to stay away from violence, the President promised that his office would listen to their concerns over what they perceive to be government interference in academia. The protests became more vehement, after a 19-year-old pregnant student was beaten by riot police officers, allegedly causing her to lose her baby in mid-December. Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdogan has expressed concern that subversive groups hostile to the government have been trying to co-opt the students to destabilize the country. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Turkey: President Meets With Students', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | EUROPE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Turkey: President Meets With Students', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gul, Abdullah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T03:54:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sebnem Arsu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sebnem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arsu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/735212f8-0cfb-59e9-ac10-2aa1708a4bd5', 'word_count': 103, 'uri': 'nyt://article/735212f8-0cfb-59e9-ac10-2aa1708a4bd5'}, {'abstract': 'Fox Sports will televise the inaugural title game for what will become the Pacific-12, signaling Fox’s determination to rebuild its college sports portfolio.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/sports/ncaafootball/07foxbowl.html', 'snippet': 'Fox Sports will televise the inaugural title game for what will beco