://article/a47575c7-b5ba-563f-bdb0-baf33f5d198b', 'word_count': 126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a47575c7-b5ba-563f-bdb0-baf33f5d198b'}, {'abstract': 'Guy Pearce, who plays Edward VIII in “The King’s Speech,” chats about preparing for the role, as well as Nazis, sex and the Queen Mum.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/finding-the-voice-of-an-abdicating-king/', 'snippet': 'Guy Pearce, who plays Edward VIII in “The King’s Speech,” chats about preparing for the role, as well as Nazis, sex and the Queen Mum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Harvey Weinstein promises, he delivers (usually). Just a few weeks ago, he announced to the Bagger that everyone associated with “The King’s Speech” would turn up for promotional duties eventually; this at a screening with Eve Best and Jennifer Ehle, who worked just a few days on the film between them but were charming to talk to about it nonetheless. Up next on the hype machine: Guy Pearce, who plays Edward VIII, whose abdication to marry Wallis Simpson forced his stuttering brother, George (Colin Firth), to step up to the royal mic more than he would’ve liked. Mr. Pearce lives in Australia but had been flown to Los Angeles by HBO to do some work for its upcoming mini-series “Mildred Pierce,” and of course the Weinstein Company took advantage of his presence in an American time zone. He spoke by phone with the Bagger for far longer than the 15 minutes a publicist allotted, about why David, as Edward was known to his family, never wanted to be king and how his brother, known as Bertie, resented him forever because of it. There was also some talk about sex, Nazis, and the Queen mum.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding the Voice of an (Abdicating) King', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Royal Family', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pearce, Guy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Simpson, Wallis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weinstein, Harvey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T14:28:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b89cf1d-e0c2-5e9c-a099-d1d47a91373a', 'word_count': 1082, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b89cf1d-e0c2-5e9c-a099-d1d47a91373a'}, {'abstract': 'The economy added jobs in December, but still has a long way to go before it fully recovers from the recent recession.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/comparing-recoveries-job-changes-2/', 'snippet': 'The economy added jobs in December, but still has a long way to go before it fully recovers from the recent recession.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Source: Bureau of Labor Statistics. Chart by Amanda Cox. Horizontal axis shows months. Vertical axis shows the ratio of that month’s nonfarm payrolls to the nonfarm payrolls at the start of recession. Note: Because employment is a lagging indicator, the dates for these employment trends are not exactly synchronized with National Bureau of Economic Research’s official business cycle dates.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Comparing Recoveries: Job Changes', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T14:30:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': Non