 335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1b2d296-9b8a-5d40-b774-f16bd715af82'}, {'abstract': 'Is it redundant, or just a cliché?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/magazine/09FOB-onlanguage-t.html', 'snippet': 'Is it redundant, or just a cliché?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Keith Otis Edwards e-mails: ‘‘In the Dec. 12 On Language column, I see that hackneyed phrase treasure trove. Since a trove is defined as ‘a store of valuable or delightful things,’ isn’t treasure trove redundant? Has anyone ever spoken of a garbage trove?’’', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Treasure Trove’', 'kicker': 'On Language', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Treasure Trove', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T14:36:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Zimmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zimmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5f95d91-4f22-5421-87b3-111f73b17ad7', 'word_count': 537, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5f95d91-4f22-5421-87b3-111f73b17ad7'}, {'abstract': 'When it came down to it, Stu Rothenberg’s team did very well in forecasting the 2010 Congressional election. Early in the cycle, though, he put a foot very wrong.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/how-not-to-take-responsibility-for-a-failed-forecast/', 'snippet': 'When it came down to it, Stu Rothenberg’s team did very well in forecasting the 2010 Congressional election. Early in the cycle, though, he put a foot very wrong.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The House race forecasts issued by Stu Rothenberg and his team on the eve of last November’s elections performed extremely well.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Not To Take Responsibility For a Failed Forecast', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T14:46:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/24ff5f28-cde3-5e9b-9dba-b9087a3885ca', 'word_count': 1921, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24ff5f28-cde3-5e9b-9dba-b9087a3885ca'}, {'abstract': 'The domestication of the female midlife crisis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/magazine/09fob-wwln-t.html', 'snippet': 'The domestication of the female midlife crisis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the mid-1970s, women began to take flight.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09wwln_span/09wwln_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 416, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09wwln_span/09wwln_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 416}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09wwln_span/09wwln_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09wwln_span/09wwln_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09wwln_span/09wwln_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fear (Again) of Flying', 'kicker': 'The Way We Live Now', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fear (Again) of Flying', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T14:52:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Warner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Warner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/03038e4a-809d-524c-b14a-883547ee41b0', 'word_count': 1069, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03038e4a-809d-524c-b14a-883547ee41b0'}, {'abstract': 'Commandeering four leftover prime rib roasts, chopping them up and sauteing them with onions got one cook his first job.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/getting-hired-thanks-to-hash/', 'snippet': 'Commandeering four leftover prime rib roasts, chopping them up and sauteing them with onions got one cook his first job.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hash Week continues: Stay At Stove Dad has a guest post from Steve Flax, a journalist who got a job at Harvest, in Cambridge, Mass., based on the strength of his roast beef hash. The secret, he said, was raiding the walk-in refrigerator and commandeering four leftover prime rib roasts. “I owed it all to using such great leftovers rather than to any special skill as a chef,” he writes. At the bottom of the post, there’s a talk-through recipe; Julia Child is mixed up in the tale somewhere, too.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting Hired, Thanks to Hash', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T14:56:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pete Wells', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pete', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wells', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a188c2b9-739f-5d69-96ea-90289fd16b5d', 'word_count': 90, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a188c2b9-739f-5d69-96ea-90289fd16b5d'}, {'abstract': 'The U.S. surgeon general knows what’s good for you: dancing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/magazine/09FOB-Q4-t.html', 'snippet': 'The U.S. surgeon general knows what’s good for you: dancing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the United States surgeon general, you just released a report on tobacco smoke that claims, not completely convincingly, that one puff of one cigarette can trigger inflammation and a fatal heart attack. Is that intended as a slogan?  Well, it’s more than a slogan. The scientific basis is that tobacco smoke is a toxic mix of more than 7,000 chemicals and chemical compounds. When you inhale the smoke, the chemicals can reach your lungs very quickly and enter the bloodstream. Whether it’s secondhand smoke or if it’s from a cigarette, it damages the lining of the blood vessels; it makes your blood more likely to clot.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09questions_1/09questions_1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09questions_1/09questions_1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Doctor’s Orders', 'kicker': 'Questions for Regina Benjamin', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Doctor’s Orders', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benjamin, Regina M', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T14:57:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Deborah Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Deborah', 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/71c009cb-0178-552a-ae25-158b01d4dc63', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71c009cb-0178-552a-ae25-158b01d4dc63'}, {'abstract': 'Fair-weather and fleeting gym users.', 'web_url': 'https://schott.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/gym-hares/', 'snippet': 'Fair-weather and fleeting gym users.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fair-weather and fleeting gym users.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gym Hares', 'kicker': 'Schott&#039;s Vocab', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T15:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Schott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3157110-5037-5c8e-938c-5ec8d9af5c2e', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3157110-5037-5c8e-938c-5ec8d9af5c2e'}, {'abstract': 'Not only do they isolate you, but they may also be making you deaf.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/magazine/09FOB-medium-t.html', 'snippet': 'Not only do they isolate you, but they may also be making you deaf.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One in ﬁve teenagers in America can’t hear rustles or whispers, according to a study published in August in The Journal of the American Medical Association. These teenagers exhibit what’s known as slight hearing loss, which means they often can’t make out consonants like T’s or K’s, or the plinking of raindrops. The word “talk” can sound like “aw.” The number of teenagers with hearing loss — from slight to severe — has jumped 33 percent since 1994.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09medium_span/09medium_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09medium_span/09medium_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09medium_span/09medium_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09medium_span/09medium_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09medium_span/09medium_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Against Headphones', 'kicker': 'The Medium', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Against Headphones', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Headphones and Headsets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ears and Hearing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T15:01:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Virginia Heffernan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Virginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heffernan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc68db1a-14e6-58dc-b6cb-0abdb96222cd', 'word_count': 1073, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc68db1a-14e6-58dc-b6cb-0abdb96222cd'}, {'abstract': 'Required reading; required reporting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/magazine/09FOB-Ethicist-t.html', 'snippet': 'Required reading; required reporting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My company now requires all employees to complete a self-directed training course on the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act, relevant to our overseas staff but not to my job. This training takes about four hours, time better spent on something more productive, and was not a condition of my employment. Until I transfer into a job that involves me with foreign officials or companies, am I ethically obligated to complete the training? J.H., BOSTON', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/03/18/magazine/ethicist75/ethicist75-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2010/03/18/magazine/ethicist75/ethicist75-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Doing What the Company Wants', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Doing What the Company Wants', 'name': None, 