ame': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T15:05:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer B. McDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2255cc3-6879-5459-abda-bfb3317efb14', 'word_count': 194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2255cc3-6879-5459-abda-bfb3317efb14'}, {'abstract': 'Tim Donaghy, the most notorious ref in N.B.A. history, served 15 months for betting on games. Now comes the hard part.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/magazine/09FOB-Encounter-t.html', 'snippet': 'Tim Donaghy, the most notorious ref in N.B.A. history, served 15 months for betting on games. Now comes the hard part.', 'lead_paragraph': ' I ﬁrst meet Tim Donaghy on a cold November evening in Sarasota, Fla., a year after he left prison. He looks leaner than he did as an N.B.A. ref, almost gaunt, with a long face and prominent, rabbity teeth. Tonight is family-dinner night — he has chosen an Italian joint in the town’s upmarket minimall — and around Donaghy sit his newish girlfriend, Carolyn; her 12–year–old daughter; and his own preteen girls, Bridget and Molly. His two other daughters, both teenagers, declined the invitation, wanting nothing to do with their father or this dinner. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09encounter_span/09encounter_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09encounter_span/09encounter_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09encounter_span/09encounter_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09encounter_span/09encounter_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09encounter_span/09encounter_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After the Buzzer', 'kicker': 'Encounter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After the Buzzer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Officiating (Sports)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Donaghy, Tim', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T15:06:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pat Jordan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pat', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jordan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da886b95-5d2f-5f49-9ada-56bb28b94b64', 'word_count': 889, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da886b95-5d2f-5f49-9ada-56bb28b94b64'}, {'abstract': 'A victim of the recession gets out of his pajamas to take a stand.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/magazine/09lives-t.html', 'snippet': 'A victim of the recession gets out of his pajamas to take a stand.', 'lead_paragraph': ' I’m just a guy in pajamas, chuckling about a “Yo Gabba Gabba!” episode my year-old daughter and I were watching, when I see a guy with a machete out my window. He’s hacking away at branches near my fence as two other men hammer spikes in my lawn. They undoubtedly work for the city. This type of thing ticks me off. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09Lives_1/09Lives_1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 904, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09Lives_1/09Lives_1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/magazine/09Lives_1/09Lives_1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sewer-Project Blues', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'SEWER-PROJECT BLUES', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sewers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T15:08:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By M. O. Walsh', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4c98c1d-62ae-50b2-bba2-20318e97206c', 'word_count': 777, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4c98c1d-62ae-50b2-bba2-20318e97206c'}, {'abstract': 'Scott Starrett came up with the idea for Jorno, a foldable keyboard for smartphones, while traveling for work. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/technology/personaltech/1248069535519/scott-starrett.html', 'snippet': 'Scott Starrett came up with the idea for Jorno, a foldable keyboard for smartphones, while traveling for work. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scott Starrett came up with the idea for Jorno, a foldable keyboard for smartphones, while traveling for work. ', 'source': 'The New York Times