icle', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Saranow Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'Saranow', 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a4c9a82-4b65-5f62-8d01-368667c9b99b', 'word_count': 123, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a4c9a82-4b65-5f62-8d01-368667c9b99b'}, {'abstract': 'Oliver Strand revisits one of last-year’s most controversial columns, which wondered why Paris doesn’t have better coffee.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/ristretto-paris-coffee-improving-sort-of/', 'snippet': 'Oliver Strand revisits one of last-year’s most controversial columns, which wondered why Paris doesn’t have better coffee.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last year, I wrote a column that wondered why Paris doesn’t have better coffee. Or, to quote Duane Sorenson of Stumptown Coffee Roasters, “Why does the coffee in Paris suck so bad?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ristretto | Is Coffee in Paris Improving?', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T15:30:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Oliver Strand', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Oliver', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strand', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/412fc9dd-1ef0-5d04-9642-65aa9fdbe02b', 'word_count': 681, 'uri': 'nyt://article/412fc9dd-1ef0-5d04-9642-65aa9fdbe02b'}, {'abstract': 'Send your questions about Sudan to Nicholas Kristof and former President Jimmy Carter.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/question-a-president-and-me-too-abo