e.jpg', 'height': 394, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/08/business/08bernanke-cnd/08bernanke-cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 394}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/08/business/08bernanke-cnd/08bernanke-cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/08/business/08bernanke-cnd/08bernanke-cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/08/business/08bernanke-cnd/08bernanke-cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bernanke Expects a ‘Moderately Stronger’ Recovery', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bernanke Expects a ‘Moderately Stronger’ Recovery This Year', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bernanke, Ben S', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T15:40:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sewell Chan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sewell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7318971a-7aee-527f-8a0e-1d62039af423', 'word_count': 591, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7318971a-7aee-527f-8a0e-1d62039af423'}, {'abstract': 'A critic tries to pick the Top 10 composers of Western classical music. You’re invited to help.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/arts/music/09composers.html', 'snippet': 'A critic tries to pick the Top 10 composers of Western classical music. You’re invited to help.', 'lead_paragraph': 'YOU know that a new year has truly arrived when critics stop issuing all those lists of the best films, books, plays, recordings and whatever of the year gone by. These lists seem to be popular with readers, and they stir up lively reactions. Like other critics I enjoy recalling the pieces and performances that struck me as exceptionally good, or exceptionally bad, during the year in classical music.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/05/arts/Composer-cover/Composer-cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/05/arts/Composer-cover/Composer-cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/05/arts/Composer-cover/Composer-cover-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/05/arts/Composer-cover/Composer-cover-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Greatest', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Greatest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Handel, George Frederick', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bach, Johann Sebastian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T15:42:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '0', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34ef5721-4882-5956-b050-2f0c18765055', 'word_count': 1679, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34ef5721-4882-5956-b050-2f0c18765055'}, {'abstract': 'Choreographers will be paired with directors and dramaturges.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/joyce-theater-to-use-grant-money-for-dance-theater-teams/', 'snippet': 'Choreographers will be paired with directors and dramaturges.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Grant money for dance is flowing through the Joyce Theater. The Joyce said on Friday that it would allot $625,000 toward helping 10 choreographers — and possibly more — work on projects. Six will be given partners from the world of theater — directors or dramaturges — to help them “embrace new methods of creating work by introducing them to the theater model in which projects go through multiple workshops,” the Joyce said. The Rockefeller Foundation, Rockefeller Brothers Fund and Andrew W. Mellon Foundation are giving the grants.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Joyce Theater to Use Grant Money for Dance-Theater Teams', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barnes, Monica Bill', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Joyce Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T15:46:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/eadce398-473f-508c-903f-fa5319a34475', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eadce398-473f-508c-903f-fa5319a34475'}, {'abstract': 'Organizers of the North American International Auto Show are using cautious analogies to describe the improving prospects for the auto business.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/automobiles/autoshow/09SHOW.html', 'snippet': 'Organizers of the North American International Auto Show are using cautious analogies to describe the improving prospects for the auto business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BY any conventional measure, the auto show season has been under way for months, with last fall’s major exhibitions in Paris and Los Angeles showcasing dozens of new vehicles. Yet for many who follow the car industry, the annual cycle of introductions and corporate chest-beating doesn’t really start until the Motor City lays out the welcome mat for the world’s automakers. ', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/autoshow/09show-span/09show-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 240, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/autoshow/09show-span/09show-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 240}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/autoshow/09show-span/09show-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 410, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/autoshow/09show-span/09show-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/autoshow/09show-span/09show-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s Time for a Checkup: Vital Signs Seem Stronger', 'kicker': 'Detroit Auto Show Preview', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It’s Time for a Checkup: Vital Signs Seem Stronger', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'North American International Auto Show', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T15:52:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'subsection_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jerry Garrett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garrett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ccafdc3-55d8-55e2-b870-cdb7ba8d7a00', 'word_count': 1219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ccafdc3-55d8-55e2-b870-cdb7ba8d7a00'}, {'abstract': 'This week, we’ve been asking you to identify locations included in our Where to Go 2011 issue. Here is the place that made the number one spot.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/guess-where-this-is-day-4/', 'snippet': 'This week, we’ve been asking you to identify locations included in our Where to Go 2011 issue. Here is the place that made the number one spot.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, we’ve been asking you to identify locations included in our Where to Go 2011 issue, which will be online later today. Many readers correctly guessed the last three: the first, posted on Facebook, was Miami; the second Salonika, Greece; and the third Reykjavik, Iceland.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Guess Where This Is, Day 4', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_ki