', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noah Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f676c51a-21f6-5498-a586-6732b74d61d1', 'word_count': 902, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f676c51a-21f6-5498-a586-6732b74d61d1'}, {'abstract': 'The automaker is promoting its remade midsize sedan as the “naughty S60.” Happily, they’re right.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/automobiles/autoreviews/09volvo.html', 'snippet': 'The automaker is promoting its remade midsize sedan as the “naughty S60.” Happily, they’re right.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VOLVO’S boxy years are long gone. But even as the company has bent its 90-degree designs into slinkier shapes, there’s something a bit square about its safety obsession.', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/WHEE/WHEE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/WHEE/WHEE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/WHEE/WHEE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 448, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/WHEE/WHEE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/WHEE/WHEE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After Its Safety Dance, Volvo Learns to Tango', 'kicker': 'Behind the Wheel | Volvo S60', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Its Safety Dance Volvo Learns to Tango', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reviews', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Volvo Car Corp', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T16:20:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'subsection_name': 'New Cars', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lawrence Ulrich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lawrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ulrich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0981be5-0267-591c-bb81-b29f0f8a7a6c', 'word_count': 1516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0981be5-0267-591c-bb81-b29f0f8a7a6c'}, {'abstract': 'An internist offers tips for negotiating prices with health care providers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/08/health/policy/08patient.html', 'snippet': 'An internist offers tips for negotiating prices with health care providers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'READERS of this column have been advised more than once to negotiate prices with health care providers for things like an M.R.I. scan, surgery and office visits. With patients paying more out of pocket for their health care than ever before \x97 in the form of higher co-payments and co-insurance, high deductibles and uncovered and out-of-network treatments \x97 negotiating with doctors and other providers has become commonplace. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/06/kullgren480/kullgren480-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/06/kullgren480/kullgren480-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/06/kullgren480/kullgren480-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 691, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/06/kullgren480/kullgren480-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/06/kullgren480/kullgren480-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Talk With the Doctor May Help Patients Afford Care', 'kicker': 'Patient Money', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Talk With the Doctor May Help Patients Afford Care', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T16:20:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Money & Policy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Walecia Konrad', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Walecia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Konrad', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d926d5cd-9b01-54ae-98ee-948ecd565c74', 'word_count': 1232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d926d5cd-9b01-54ae-98ee-948ecd565c74'}, {'abstract': 'Negotiating with your health care provider has become commonplace. But how do you begin?', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/how-to-haggle-with-your-doctor/', 'snippet': 'Negotiating with your health care provider has become commonplace. But how do you begin?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this week’s Patient Money column, Walecia Konrad asks Dr. Jeffrey Kullgren, an internist at the University of Pennsylvania who researches consumer-driven health care, for advice on how to negotiate with a doctor or other medical provider. “Your physician may be just as uncomfortable with these conversations as you are,” he said. “That’s because — and I can tell you firsthand — doctors are simply not trained for this. I was trained to give the very best care for my patients, regardless of cost.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Haggle With Your Doctor', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T16:21:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/39756eb8-f935-5a7e-9dd8-70f08993d300', 'word_count': 220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39756eb8-f935-5a7e-9dd8-70f08993d300'}, {'abstract': 'Judd Apatow’s real-life wife, Leslie Mann, and Paul Rudd will reprise their semi-happily married spouses from “Knocked Up” for a film planned for 2012.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/the-second-time-around-new-apatow-comedy-will-revisit-knocked-up-couple/', 'snippet': 'Judd Apatow’s real-life wife, Leslie Mann, and Paul Rudd will reprise their semi-happily married spouses from “Knocked Up” for a film planned for 2012.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The actress Leslie Mann is lucky to have almost a whole year to figure out what to get her husband, the comedy director Judd Apatow, for the 2011 holiday season, because she’ll need the time to top Mr. Apatow’s latest gift to her: another movie role. Ms. Mann, who has been featured in all of her husband’s films including “The 40-Year-Old Virgin” and “Funny People,” is set to reunite with Paul Rudd to reprise the semi-happily married couple they portrayed in Mr. Apatow’s 2007 hit “Knocked Up.” Variety reported that the as-yet untitled film, to be written and directed by Mr. Apatow, would be a follow-up of sorts to “Knocked Up” – in which Ms. Mann played the sister of Katherine Heigl‘s newly pregnant character, and Mr. Rudd, another Apatow regular, played Ms. Mann’s mushroom-ingesting, fantasy-baseball-obsessed spouse – though not necessarily a direct sequel or prequel to that comedy. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Second Time Around: New Apatow Comedy Will Revisit ‘Knocked Up’ Couple', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'APATOW, JUDD', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T16:30:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/01b74be6-508f-501d-9e7c-be3b2cae9955', 'word_count': 214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01b74be6-508f-501d-9e7c-be3b2cae9955'}, {'abstract': 'The new S60 sedan plays up, and plays on, the curves.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/01/09/automobiles/autoreviews/20110109-volvo.html', 'snippet': 'The new S60 sedan plays up, and plays on, the curves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The new S60 sedan plays up, and plays on, the curves.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/autoreviews/20110109-volvo-slide-ALMW/20110109-volvo-slide-ALMW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/autoreviews/20110109-volvo-slide-ALMW/20110109-volvo-slide-ALMW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/autoreviews/20110109-volvo-slide-ALMW/20110109-volvo-slide-ALMW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '2011 Volvo S60', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reviews', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T16:31:07+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'subsection_name': 'New Cars', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/ec3a1873-b524-5d59-a210-b7722aee212e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/ec3a1873-b524-5d59-a210-b7722aee212e'}, {'abstract': 'The city advises families who have a child starting kindergarten next September that the deadline is March 4.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/kindergarten-admission-season-opens/', 'snippet': 'The city advises families who have a child starting kindergarten next September that the deadline is March 4.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Trying to get your preschooler into kindergarten at a popular public school across the street? Wondering whether you will have priority over a former neighbor who moved to a different borough but who already has a child there?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kindergarten Admission Season Opens', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T16:33:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sharon Otterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sharon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Otterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/60691d91-5f30-5d8d-a9ca-9744776a36b6', 'word_count': 434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60691d91-5f30-5d8d-a9ca-9744776a36b6'}, {'abstract': 'The effort that goes into dressing a prize-winning red-carpet clotheshorse often feels greater than what one can properly say about the ugly dress or indeed a second-rate event. This week’s People’s Choice Awards illustrated the problem.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/dressing-a-listers-for-the-red-carpet/', 'snippet': 'The effort that goes into dressing a prize-winning red-carpet clotheshorse often feels greater than what one can properly say about the ugly dress or indeed a second-rate event. This week’s People’s Choice Awards illustrated the problem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The effort that goes into dressing a prize-winning red-carpet clotheshorse often feels greater than what one can properly say about the ugly dress or indeed a second-rate event. This week’s People’s Choice Awards illustrated the problem. After deploying references to Adderall, Xanax, “Pride and Prejudice” and fancy pillowcases to describe the awfulness of the clothes, Amy Odell of New York magazine’s The Cut blog received a lecture from readers about her misuse of Jane Austen. There are no milkmaids in “Pride and Prejudice,” she was told, certainly no “slovenly milkmaids.” Ms. Odell might wish she had just said cow.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dressing A-Listers for the Red Carpet', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Celebrities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T16:34:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/271b827d-d69d-596f-8a51-9fac7ac126ab', 'word_count': 656, 'uri': 'nyt://article/271b827d-d69d-596f-8a51-9fac7ac126ab'}, {'abstract': 'An article in Education Life asks: If memoir writing is tough, even for professionals, why are 17-year-olds expected to nail it?', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/the-almighty-essay/', 'snippet': 'An article in Education Life asks: If memoir writing is tough, even for professionals, why are 17-year-olds expected to nail it?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the latest edition of Education Life, The Times’s quarterly supplement, to be published this weekend, Trip Gabriel wonders why the personal essay is so important to admissions officers. The essay is viewed as the one place where a student’s own voice can be heard. In his story, “The Almighty Essay,” Mr. Gabriel writes:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Almighty Essay', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None