7/something-in-the-air-is-brewing/', 'snippet': 'Six Point Craft Ales is making a beer with the wild yeasts that blow in from New York Harbor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It seems that no part of Brooklyn can be left untouched by its ever-striving culinary scene, even its microorganisms.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Something in the Air Is Brewing', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:16:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Simonson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Simonson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1bd88bf-ac73-5c71-bd78-27d785ae78cc', 'word_count': 522, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1bd88bf-ac73-5c71-bd78-27d785ae78cc'}, {'abstract': 'Officials at the New York Fire Department could not immediately identify the victim, who appeared to be an adult.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/fatal-fire-on-lower-east-side/', 'snippet': 'Officials at the New York Fire Department could not immediately identify the victim, who appeared to be an adult.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Update, 2:34 p.m. | A 72-year-old woman died early Friday after a fire swept through the second floor of a tenement building on Manhattan’s Lower East Side, the authorities said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fatal Fire on Lower East Side', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fires and Firefighters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fire Department (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lower East Side (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:17:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Zraick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zraick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3eb5c4aa-a631-5742-be14-7daf38514ade', 'word_count': 169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3eb5c4aa-a631-5742-be14-7daf38514ade'}, {'abstract': 'Thanks to technology, particularly Skype, campus-to-campus relationships go on, and on, and on.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/a-love-distance-affair/', 'snippet': 'Thanks to technology, particularly Skype, campus-to-campus relationships go on, and on, and on.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Generations of high school sweethearts have stayed together into college, but the connection tended to unravel by Thanksgiving. That’s no longer the case, Abigail Sullivan Moore writes in an article in Education Life. Now, thanks to technology, particularly Skype, campus-to-campus relationships go on, and on, and on. Breaking up is hard to do. Should you? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A ‘Love’-Distance Affair', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:22:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c18a33bf-636f-5398-a66a-0129df43f36b', 'word_count': 57, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c18a33bf-636f-5398-a66a-0129df43f36b'}, {'abstract': 'The WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange wants his “highly personal” book to “become one of the unifying documents of our generation.”', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/wikileaks-the-book-the-shoes-the-u-f-o-s/', 'snippet': 'The WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange wants his “highly personal” book to “become one of the unifying documents of our generation.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the lull before the media frenzy that will no doubt strike again next week, when Julian Assange returns to court in London to continue his fight against extradition to Sweden, his British publisher is trying to drum up interest in his book, which is due out in April.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'WikiLeaks: The Book', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:28:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/34f4e74f-e619-5b18-804e-ffb878de6245', 'word_count': 566, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34f4e74f-e619-5b18-804e-ffb878de6245'}, {'abstract': 'Some brands are focusing North American marketing efforts in more populous — and, to the brands’ thinking, more influential — markets like New York and Los Angeles.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/nissan-and-mitsubishi-among-detroit-auto-shows-notable-absentees/', 'snippet': 'Some brands are focusing North American marketing efforts in more populous — and, to the brands’ thinking, more influential — markets like New York and Los Angeles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once again, a number of automakers will skip the Detroit auto show, which opens to the press on Monday and to the public on Jan. 15.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nissan and Mitsubishi Among Detroit Auto Show’s Notable Absentees', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'North American International Auto Show', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Aston Martin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fisker Automotive', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mitsubishi Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nissan Motor Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Porsche AG', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rolls-Royce Motor Cars', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Suzuki Motor Corp', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:30:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Stenquist', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stenquist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/866a6156-d669-577f-bf9b-597407b866dd', 'word_count': 392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/866a6156-d669-577f-bf9b-597407b866dd'}, {'abstract': 'Which N.F.L. team has the right makeup to reach the Super Bowl? After watching our editors debate, vote or make your own prediction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/sports/football/20110108-nfl-feature.html', 'snippet': 'Which N.F.L. team has the right makeup to reach the Super Bowl? After watching our editors debate, vote or make your own prediction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Which N.F.L. team has the right makeup to reach the Super Bowl? After watching our editors debate, vote or make your own prediction.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/14/sports/football/20110108-nfl-feature-web1/20110108-nfl-feature-web1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 262, 'width': 395, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Road to the Super Bowl', 'kicker': 'Point/Counterpoint', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Patriots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Falcons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brady, Tom', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:31:18+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/0a405fd2-29fd-50e1-bcfa-4259927ebb41', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/0a405fd2-29fd-50e1-bcfa-4259927ebb41'}, {'abstract': 'An exploration of fundamental changes in family and class relationships, and in the very idea of what it is to be Indian.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/books/review/Bahadur-t.html', 'snippet': 'An exploration of fundamental changes in family and class relationships, and in the very idea of what it is to be Indian.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the middle of his accomplished book, “India Calling,” Anand Giridharadas tells of meeting a Maoist revolutionary in Hyderabad. The city, nicknamed Cyberabad, serves as a base for both the globalized Indian economy and an armed insurgency at war against the country’s inequalities, rooted and new. India’s Maoist — or Naxalite — movement began as a rural struggle against exploitative landlords in a caste-conscious, socialist nation but has now arrayed itself against the forces of global capitalism reshaping India. When Giridharadas pushes the Naxalite — What does one fight have to do with the other? — the man answers with a striking notion: globalization is reducing people to their specific economic task, stripping them of their humanity, just as caste had done. And software engineers in gated communities have become the new Brahmins. Giridharadas follows the curve of this argument, allowing it to seduce us. Then, he reveals that this rebel, although waging revolution by night, reports by day for a newspaper he himself describes as a shill for the multinational transformation of India. “I have to earn my lunch,” the man explains. “I’m not a whole-timer for revolution.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/BAHADUR/BAHADUR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/BAHADUR/BAHADUR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/BAHADUR/BAHADUR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/BAHADUR/BAHADUR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/BAHADUR/BAHADUR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Homeland Revisited', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Homeland Revisited', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indian-Americans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Giridharadas, Anand', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:31:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gaiutra Bahadur', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gaiutra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bahadur', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1b8a171-6f5e-573d-b4a7-b5d24de0edde', 'word_count': 1026, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1b8a171-6f5e-573d-b4a7-b5d24de0edde'}, {'abstract': 'In her first work of nonfiction in more than 20 years, Annie Proulx chronicles her efforts to build a house on a 640 acre preserve in Wyoming.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/books/review/Fuller-t.html', 'snippet': 'In her first work of nonfiction in more than 20 years, Annie Proulx chronicles her efforts to build a house on a 640 acre preserve in Wyoming.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her early fiction about Wyoming, Annie Proulx rewrote the state with a smash-and-grab intensity all her own. In the story collections “Close Range,” “Bad Dirt” and “Fine Just the Way It Is,” it was as if Proulx had broken the language of classic Wyoming (Owen Wister’s novel “The Virginian,” Jack Schaefer’s “Shane,” Mary O’Hara’s “My Friend Flicka”) and used the shards to make something so subversively new that there was only one word for what she had done: Proulxian.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/0109-BKS-Fuller/0109-BKS-Fuller-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 663, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Shelter Beneath an Open Sky', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shelter Beneath an Open Sky', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wyoming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Proulx, Annie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:35:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Fuller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fuller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cacba51d-010e-5cd9-86a1-d53dad4a1ae5', 'word_count': 1186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cacba51d-010e-5cd9-86a1-d53dad4a1ae5'}, {'abstract': 'At 89, Richard Wilbur still cuts a straight path through the shifting landscape of American poetry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/books/review/Orr-t.html', 'snippet': 'At 89, Richard Wilbur still cuts a straight path through the shifting landscape of American poetry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s tough to be a Grand Old Man of — well, anything, really. At this point Joe Paterno must feel less like a person than a physical feature of the Penn State campus, like the Nittany Lion Shrine. In his elegy for Yeats, Auden claims that the poet, upon dying, “became his admirers,” but it would’ve been more accurate to say that like most Grand Old Men (and not just the ones who actually are men), Yeats had much earlier become shorthand for an array of generalizations. Stick around long enough and, to most people, you’re no longer an artist; you’re a brand.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/ORR/ORR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 850, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/ORR/ORR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/ORR/ORR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Formalist', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Formalist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilbur, Richard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:37:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Orr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Orr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/166f5e33-242c-5ecf-972a-66944020dd60', 'word_count': 1268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/166f5e33-242c-5ecf-972a-66944020dd60'}, {'abstract': 'Melanie Thernstrom, known for her journalism based on her own life, says she always asks permission before writing about someone she’s close to.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/books/review/Upfront-t.html', 'snippet': 'Melanie Thernstrom, known for her journalism based on her own life, says she always asks permission before writing about someone she’s close to.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Melanie Thernstrom is known for her powerful first-person journalism based on her own life, whether the subject is the murder of her best friend (recounted in her first book, “The Dead Girl”); her own struggles with chronic pain (the inspiration for her latest, “The Pain Chronicles”); or the birth of her children via a complex process of third-party reproduction, described in last Sunday’s cover article for The Times Magazine.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/0109-BKS-Upfront/0109-BKS-Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 607, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/0109-BKS-Upfront/0109-BKS-Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/0109-BKS-Upfront/0109-BKS-Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front: Melanie Thernstrom', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thernstrom, Melanie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:37:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34f91582-1fb9-59a4-b4f3-9f026b2dd7da', 'word_count': 206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34f91582-1fb9-59a4-b4f3-9f026b2dd7da'}, {'abstract': 'A writer recollects her schizophrenic mother, to whom she remains emotionally captive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/books/review/Thernstrom-t.html', 'snippet': 'A writer recollects her schizophrenic mother, to whom she remains emotionally captive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the center of Mira Bartok’s memoir about her schizophrenic mother is a beautiful metaphor: a memory palace. The idea, she explains, derives from the sixth-century B.C. Greek poet Simonides, who was attending a party at a palace and stepped outside just before the building collapsed. Because he could recall where all the other guests had been standing, Simonides alone could identify the mangled bodies. Inspired by this tale, a 16th-century Jesuit priest recommended a mental technique by which scholars could build an imaginary palace to keep their memories safe, creating a visual image for everything they wanted to recall and creating a particular place for the image inside the mental palace.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/THERNSTROM20110109123322jpg-THERNSTROM/THERNSTROM20110109123322jpg-THERNSTROM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 717, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/THERNSTROM20110109123322jpg-THERNSTROM/THERNSTROM20110109123322jpg-THERNSTROM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/THERNSTROM20110109123322jpg-THERNSTROM/THERNSTROM20110109123322jpg-THERNSTROM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Power of Recall', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Power of Recall', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bartok, Mira', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Memory', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Schizophrenia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:37:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melanie Thernstrom', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thernstrom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d066ca16-a3bb-518a-8adc-b1a185eaf967', 'word_count': 1179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d066ca16-a3bb-518a-8adc-b1a185eaf967'}, {'abstract': 'The daughter of the poet Anne Sexton describes her own harrowing struggle with mental illness.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/books/review/Russo-t.html', 'snippet': 'The daughter of the poet Anne Sexton describes her own harrowing struggle with mental illness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Linda Gray Sexton’s first memoir, “Searching for Mercy Street,” described a childhood dominated by the mental illness of her larger-than-life mother, the poet Anne Sexton. Linda, the older of Sexton’s two daughters, wrote frankly about her mother’s terrifying breakdowns, fugue states and drunken rages, as well as demands for closeness that at times slid into molestation. Linda Sexton grew up intellectually privileged — in high school she had private poetry tutorials with her mother’s best friend, Maxine Kumin — but emotionally lost, bound to her glamorous, suffering, overbearing parent in what the first of many psychiatrists called a “destructive symbiosis.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/Russo-1293639720356/Russo-1293639720356-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 704, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/Russo-1293639720356/Russo-1293639720356-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/Russo-1293639720356/Russo-1293639720356-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Suicide’s Shadow', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Suicide’s Shadow', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suicides and Suicide Attempts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Depression (Mental)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sexton, Anne', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sexton, Linda Gray', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:37:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Russo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Russo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad6ed41a-2d4c-536c-8f92-078e3ccaeb65', 'word_count': 931, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad6ed41a-2d4c-536c-8f92-078e3ccaeb65'}, {'abstract': 'Nigel S. Wright, the top adviser to the country’s prime minister, plans to return to his old job at the private equity shop Onex in two years.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/in-canada-doubts-about-prime-ministers-top-adviser/', 'snippet': 'Nigel S. Wright, the top adviser to the country’s prime minister, plans to return to his old job at the private equity shop Onex in two years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As President Obama names a JPMorgan Chase executive, William Daley, to be his right-hand man, another new chief of staff is creating controversy. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Canada, Doubts About Prime Minister’s Top Adviser', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daley, William M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harper, Stephen J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AIRBUS INDUSTRIE', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boeing Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Onex Corp', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:38:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Austen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/396a85db-e0fb-5685-ac9c-94e5cdb85653', 'word_count': 662, 'uri': 'nyt://article/396a85db-e0fb-5685-ac9c-94e5cdb85653'}, {'abstract': 'How Africa put its stamp on America’s food traditions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/books/review/Grimes-t.html', 'snippet': 'How Africa put its stamp on America’s food traditions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In one of the personal vignettes that punctuate “High on the Hog: A Culinary Journey From Africa to America,” the food historian and cookbook author Jessica B. Harris takes a trip with her mother to Houmas House, near New Orleans. One of a string of former plantations along Louisiana’s River Road, the estate bears witness to a cruel history. Harris, who is black, speculates aloud that much of the place was built by slaves. The remark draws an unexpected response. “What artistry,” her mother says. “What beauty they created for people who thought we were nothing but goods, not even human beings!” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/GRIMES/GRIMES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/GRIMES/GRIMES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/GRIMES/GRIMES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 748, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/GRIMES/GRIMES-thumbSt