 in rural China and Taiwan, challenged the conventional wisdom about health and nutrition by espousing the benefits of a plant-based diet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nutrition Advice From the China Study', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Campbell, Colin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:40:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b3d3e58-60f2-5b76-bbb2-e51363e30b24', 'word_count': 1127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b3d3e58-60f2-5b76-bbb2-e51363e30b24'}, {'abstract': 'In this first novel, a woman reimagines the life and times of her polar-exploring ancestor and his faithful wife.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/books/review/Lear-t.html', 'snippet': 'In this first novel, a woman reimagines the life and times of her polar-exploring ancestor and his faithful wife.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sleeping, dreaming, cooking, commuting: much of “The Still Point” lazes in this register. Julia and Simon, together for 10 years, their marriage veering toward the rocks, have recently moved from London to Julia’s ancestral country home. When Simon, an architect, leaves for the city, Julia, an unemployed archivist, returns to cataloging the artifacts that fill the house, the remnants of her great-great-uncle, Edward Mackley, and his fatal journey to the North Pole.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/LEAR-1293640290064/LEAR-1293640290064-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/LEAR-1293640290064/LEAR-1293640290064-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/LEAR-1293640290064/LEAR-1293640290064-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beyond the Edge of the World', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beyond the Edge of the World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Pole', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exploration and Explorers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:40:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sophia Lear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sophia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lear', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ff1ed0a-7e14-5f51-8016-28114c891c33', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ff1ed0a-7e14-5f51-8016-28114c891c33'}, {'abstract': 'A luminous tale of passion and betrayal, set amid unrest and violence in Sierra Leone.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/books/review/Mengiste-t.html', 'snippet': 'A luminous tale of passion and betrayal, set amid unrest and violence in Sierra Leone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An aged and dying man, Elias Cole, tells a story about a woman he once loved, as a British psychologist, Adrian Lockheart — who has left behind a wife and daughter to come to Sierra Leone — listens on. This premise alone might have been enough to sustain a book called “The Memory of Love,” an ambitious new novel by Aminatta Forna. Yet Forna has achieved something more startling and impressive here. Hers is a luminous tale of passion and betrayal, encompassing the political unrest that racked Sierra Leone in the late 1960s and the ruinous civil war of the 1990s, as well as the days of tenuous quiet when those who managed to stay alive struggled to cope with the physical and mental scars of those years.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/mengiste/mengiste-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/mengiste/mengiste-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/mengiste/mengiste-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 759, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/mengiste/mengiste-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/mengiste/mengiste-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Between Pain and Peace', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Between Pain and Peace', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sierra Leone', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War and Guerrilla Warfare', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Forna, Aminatta', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:41:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maaza Mengiste', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maaza', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mengiste', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1729867-c07a-56f2-bc0f-a90d38ab9678', 'word_count': 966, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1729867-c07a-56f2-bc0f-a90d38ab9678'}, {'abstract': 'In this French novel of essentially one endless sentence, a secret agent exposes his guilt, and that of the West.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/books/review/Burn-t.html', 'snippet': 'In this French novel of essentially one endless sentence, a secret agent exposes his guilt, and that of the West.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Near midnight on a Friday in April 1854, Gustave Flaubert wrote one of his many letters to Louise Colet. Flaubert had spent days hidden away in his Croisset retreat, researching theories of clubfoot and discarding pages from the manuscript of “Madame Bovary,” and he told Colet that he had come to the conclusion that “the books from which entire literatures have flowed, like Homer, Rabelais, are encyclopedias of their time. They knew everything.” This conception — the novel that knows everything — would come to obsess Europe’s modernist writers, who dreamed that a narrative of infinite detail and esoteric knowledge could blur the boundaries between traditional genres, with fiction shading into nonfiction, poetry bleeding into history.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/Burn-sub/Burn-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/Burn-sub/Burn-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/Burn-sub/Burn-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'River of Consciousness', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'River of Consciousness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mandell, Charlotte', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:42:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Burn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/96618607-9a4d-57bf-8f90-8af60263183e', 'word_count': 1230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96618607-9a4d-57bf-8f90-8af60263183e'}, {'abstract': 'A listing of cultural events this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/arts/09weekaheadweb.html', 'snippet': 'A listing of cultural events this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dance', 'source'