t': 276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23320487-6a71-5e77-912d-355c80c03fc3'}, {'abstract': 'Letter in response to Ed Park’s essay “One Sentence Says It All.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/books/review/Letters-t-LATINRHYTHMS_LETTERS.html', 'snippet': 'Letter in response to Ed Park’s essay “One Sentence Says It All.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Latin Rhythms', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Latin Rhythms', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:45:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1f33026-d33a-576b-9774-7494673e52bc', 'word_count': 195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1f33026-d33a-576b-9774-7494673e52bc'}, {'abstract': 'Charles Portis’s “True Grit” is back on the best-seller list, thanks to the Coen brothers’ film adaptation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/InsideList-t.html', 'snippet': 'Charles Portis’s “True Grit” is back on the best-seller list, thanks to the Coen brothers’ film adaptation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BONANZA: Charles Portis’s “True Grit” (1968) has popped up on the trade paperback list — it’s No. 11 this week — thanks to the Coen brothers’ film adaptation.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/16insidelist/16insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/16insidelist/16insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:45:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64ccb83a-4fbc-5d2b-8428-72c270c4466a', 'word_count': 518, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64ccb83a-4fbc-5d2b-8428-72c270c4466a'}, {'abstract': 'This first novel explores the emotional turmoil within the family of an intersex child.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/books/review/DErasmo-t.html', 'snippet': 'This first novel explores the emotional turmoil within the family of an intersex child.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Seeing double isn’t all that easy, especially when it comes to questions of gender and sexuality. In her excellent study “Vice Versa,” Marjorie Garber argues that bisexuality isn’t a third category but a category that undoes identity categories, and that sexuality itself is “a narrative that changes over time rather than a fixed identity, however complex.” In her memoir “Conundrum,” Jan Morris, who was James Morris before a sex-change operation in 1972, writes: “I do not mind my continuing ambiguity. . . . I see myself not as man or woman, self or other, fragment or whole.” She concludes her autobiography not with a “solution,” but with a question: “What if I remain an equivocal figure?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/DErasmo-t_CA0/DErasmo-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 767, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/DErasmo-t_CA0/DErasmo-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/DErasmo-t_CA0/DErasmo-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Announcing Her Existence', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Announcing Her Existence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transsexuals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:45:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': "By Stacey D'Erasmo", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stacey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "D'Erasmo", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/af0f58b1-fcf0-5888-93a2-fe676e15b262', 'word_count': 1164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af0f58b1-fcf0-5888-93a2-fe676e15b262'}, {'abstract': 'Letter in response to Alan Wolfe’s review of Olivier Roy’s “Holy Ignorance.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/books/review/Letters-t-WHYWECELEBRA_LETTERS.html', 'snippet': 'Letter in response to Alan Wolfe’s review of Olivier Roy’s “Holy Ignorance.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why We Celebrate', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why We Celebrate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:45:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c66a3e0-ee06-59d0-876a-99d47920b099', 'word_count': 120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c66a3e0-ee06-59d0-876a-99d47920b099'}, {'abstract': 'Letters in response to Dale Peck’s review of Thomas Bernhard’s “My Prizes: An Accounting” and “Prose.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/books/review/Letters-t-BERNHARDTHEA_LETTERS.html', 'snippet': 'Letters in response to Dale Peck’s review of Thomas Bernhard’s “My Prizes: An Accounting” and “Prose.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bernhard the Alienator', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bernhard The Alienator', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:45:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/30fc2657-3f16-5b73-8c4e-e80cf7e8181f', 'word_count': 335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30fc2657-3f16-5b73-8c4e-e80cf7e8181f'}, {'abstract': 'Mystery novels by Charles Todd, Anders Roslund and Borge Hellstrom, T. Jefferson Parker and R. Scott Bakker.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/books/review/Crime-t.html', 'snippet': 'Mystery novels by Charles Todd, Anders Roslund and Borge Hellstrom, T. Jefferson Parker and R. Scott Bakker.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s 1920 at the opening of A LONELY DEATH (Morrow/Harper\xadCollins, $24.99), the latest of Charles Todd’s masterly novels about England’s slow and painful recovery from the Great War. Ian Rutledge, the Scotland Yard inspector at the center of this series, is one of the walking wounded who never speak of their experiences but share a silent bond. It’s not entirely surprising, then, that Rutledge is so unnerved by the suicide of a friend, a despondent former artillery captain, that he packs a loaded service revolver when he makes his first return visit to the French battlefield where he was buried alive under the bodies of the soldiers he commanded.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/Crime-t_CA0/Crime-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 519, 'width': 369, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/Crime-t_CA0/Crime-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/Crime-t_CA0/Crime-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Buried Past', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Buried Past', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roslund, Anders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hellstrom, Borge', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parker, T Jefferson', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Todd, Charles (Author)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:45:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/93701548-bf1a-59bb-b713-9dc313384196', 'word_count': 875, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93701548-bf1a-59bb-b713-9dc313384196'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/books/review/PaperRow-t.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON THIN ICE: The Changing World of the Polar Bear, by Richard Ellis. (Vintage, $18.95.) Ellis has written some of the most important natural history books of the past decade, including “No Turning Back” (a history of extinction) and “The Empty Ocean.” Here he surveys polar bear history, lore, biology and politics, and the complexity of human-polar bear relations: we love the polar bear’s seeming cuddliness, admire its strength and are incapable of breaking the carbon-fuel addiction that’s threatening its survival as a species. In REWILDING THE WORLD: Dispatches From the Conservation Revolution (Picador, $18), Caroline Fraser follows proponents of “rewilding” — an ecological idea that stresses the restoration of animal habitats, the importance of migration corridors and the need for large carnivores in healthy ecosystems.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/books/review/09paperrow/09paperrow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 