o contacts, no direction and no confidence.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'This post is part of a occasional series profiling Web sites and blogs made in New York. Have a profile suggestion? E-mail NewYorkOnline@nytimes.com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tonic for Dispirited Career-Hunters', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Careers and Professions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Craigslist', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LinkedIn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MONSTER.COM', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:49:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexis Mainland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mainland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/85000714-9d66-53b8-bda1-3b578dd424f5', 'word_count': 615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85000714-9d66-53b8-bda1-3b578dd424f5'}, {'abstract': 'Thumping all-wheel-drive units in Fords and oil leaks in Hondas are among the mechanical ailments covered in recent technical service bulletins.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/automobiles/09TSB.html', 'snippet': 'Thumping all-wheel-drive units in Fords and oil leaks in Hondas are among the mechanical ailments covered in recent technical service bulletins.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THUMPING all-wheel-drive units in Fords and oil leaks in Hondas are among the mechanical ailments covered in recent technical service bulletins. ', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/TSB1/TSB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 313, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/TSB1/TSB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 313}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/TSB1/TSB1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 534, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Growling Vans, Sagging S.U.V.’s', 'kicker': 'Motoring', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Growling Vans, Sagging S.U.V.’s', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recalls and Bans of Products', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:54:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Sturgis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sturgis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdd3a7fa-3150-54ec-960c-76f7005d49d4', 'word_count': 772, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdd3a7fa-3150-54ec-960c-76f7005d49d4'}, {'abstract': 'Walter Braunfels, a composer suppressed by the Third Reich, has been enjoying a revival, at the Los Angeles Opera and the Pittsburgh Symphony Orchestra.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/arts/music/09braunfels.html', 'snippet': 'Walter Braunfels, a composer suppressed by the Third Reich, has been enjoying a revival, at the Los Angeles Opera and the Pittsburgh Symphony Orchestra.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TRUTH or fable? The incident shows up neither in the New Grove Dictionary of Music and Musicians nor in the research materials of the Orel Foundation, dedicated to composers suppressed by the Third Reich, and the parties involved are long gone. But according to family lore, something thrilling happened when a tin-pot thug and demagogue named Hitler called on Walter Braunfels, the composer of the runaway hit “Die Vögel” (“The Birds”), in Munich. It was 1923, the year of the Beer Hall Putsch, an early Nazi attempt to overthrow the Weimar Republic, and Hitler wanted Braunfels to write an anthem for the brownshirts on the march. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/arts/BRAUNFELS3/BRAUNFELS-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 677, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/arts/BRAUNFELS3/BRAUNFELS-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/arts/BRAUNFELS3/BRAUNFELS-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Silenced Voice, Heard Again', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Silenced Voice, Heard Again', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Braunfels, Walter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Opera', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pittsburgh Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:56:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '0', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Gurewitsch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gurewitsch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bc3bdbc-1d8a-55a9-9246-761408863138', 'word_count': 1590, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bc3bdbc-1d8a-55a9-9246-761408863138'}, {'abstract': 'HBO has ordered a full season of “Girls,” a comedy series created by and starring Lena Dunham, the star and director of “Tiny Furniture.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/another-big-break-for-tiny-furniture-creator-an-hbo-series/', 'snippet': 'HBO has ordered a full season of “Girls,” a comedy series created by and starring Lena Dunham, the star and director of “Tiny Furniture.”', 'lead_paragraph': ' Were you already jealous of Lena Dunham, the 24-year-old wunderkind writer, director and star of the sleeper-hit comedy “Tiny Furniture”? Prepare to grow even greener with envy: on Friday HBO announced that it had ordered a full season of a new comedy series that Ms. Dunham created and will star in for that cable channel. HBO said in a news release that the series, called “Girls,” will offer “a comic look at the assorted humiliations and rare triumphs of a group of girls in their early 20s,” with a cast that will also feature Jemima Kirke (of “Tiny Furniture”), Allison Williams (“American Dreams”), Adam Driver (of the HBO movie “You Don’t Know Jack”) and Zosia Mamet (“Mad Men”). Lest she be labeled an underachiever, Ms. Dunham also wrote and directed the show’s pilot episode, and the series will be produced by Jenni Konner and Judd Apatow (who’s keeping plenty busy himself these days). A premiere date for “Girls” wasn’t immediately announced.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Another Big Break for ‘Tiny Furniture’ Creator: An HBO Series', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dunham, Lena', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T17:56:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb705332-3ab9-5f72-b7ea-12c38836980a', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb705332-3ab9-5f72-b7ea-12c38836980a'}, {'abstract': 'The Mitsubishi Lancer Sportback Ralliart is a middling member of the hot compact class, which is exactly why it’s the smartest buy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/automobiles/autoreviews/09mitsubishi.html', 'snippet': 'The Mitsubishi Lancer Sportback Ralliart is a middling member of the hot compact class, which is exactly why it’s the smartest buy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TESTED 2011 Mitsubishi Lancer Sportback Ralliart', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/BLOC/BLOC-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 265, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/BLOC/BLOC-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 265}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/automobiles/BLOC/BLOC-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 414, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',