rst full-length show to the United States as part of the Under the Radar Festival.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Under the Radar: 5 Questions About ‘Phobophilia’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T18:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/1136120f-ca35-500c-8732-7384d6f94c98', 'word_count': 595, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1136120f-ca35-500c-8732-7384d6f94c98'}, {'abstract': 'The pace of job creation is now only barely fast enough to keep up with population growth.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/painfully-slow-jobs-progress/', 'snippet': 'The pace of job creation is now only barely fast enough to keep up with population growth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The job market is getting better — but oh so slowly, especially given how far it still has to go.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Painfully Slow Jobs Progress', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T18:03:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualif