 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Blast in Egypt, Many Deride Their Church as Too Timid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Coptic Orthodox Church of Alexandria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hosni', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T18:04:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Slackman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Slackman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04ca0610-bc0d-5bb8-b33a-5a6dc79b93dc', 'word_count': 911, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04ca0610-bc0d-5bb8-b33a-5a6dc79b93dc'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama is expected to pursue new initiatives that do not carry a big price tag that would add to the growing national debt.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/08/business/economy/08obama.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama is expected to pursue new initiatives that do not carry a big price tag that would add to the growing national debt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON \x97 President Obama went to a busy window-manufacturing plant near here on Friday to promote his economic policies and his new team of advisers as the monthly jobs report reflected only modest employment growth.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Promises Full Recovery for Employment', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obama Promises Full Recovery for Employment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sperling, Gene', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daley, William M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T18:05:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jackie Calmes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jackie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calmes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aeb7f73e-5059-567d-8934-3f7aaf9b2797', 'word_count': 873, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aeb7f73e-5059-567d-8934-3f7aaf9b2797'}, {'abstract': 'A computer prodigy drops out of Harvard and builds one of America’s hottest companies. Goldman Sachs plays a key role in the eventual I.P.O. This isn’t Mark Zuckerberg in 2011. It’s Bill Gates in 1986.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/the-facebook-microsoft-parallels/', 'snippet': 'A computer prodigy drops out of Harvard and builds one of America’s hottest companies. Goldman Sachs plays a key role in the eventual I.P.O. This isn’t Mark Zuckerberg in 2011. It’s Bill Gates in 1986.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A computer prodigy drops out of Harvard and builds one of America’s hottest companies. He brings on an M.B.A. to help him think about things other than programming. He wants to keep his very profitable company private. But as his company grows, he begins distributing shares to his ever-increasing employee base. This gives his company more than 499 shareholders and forces him to consider an initial public offering. The investment bank Goldman Sachs plays a major role in the I.P.O. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Facebook-Microsoft Parallels', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gates, Bill', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuckerberg, Mark E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook.com', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corp', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T18:06:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/080edfd2-e24e-54a0-a516-f3c09e2a72f4', 'word_count': 742, 'uri': 'nyt://article/080edfd2-e24e-54a0-a516-f3c09e2a72f4'}, {'abstract': 'The S60 is surprisingly sporty for a Volvo, especially the T6 model.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/reviewing-the-volvo-s60/', 'snippet': 'The S60 is surprisingly sporty for a Volvo, especially the T6 model.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Sunday’s Automobiles section, Lawrence Ulrich reviews the new Volvo S60, the first new model from the company since Ford sold Volvo to the Chinese automaker Geely. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reviewing the Volvo S60', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reviews', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T18:09:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/de4f365c-f7c6-537b-81be-8c9d57033c61', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de4f365c-f7c6-537b-81be-8c9d57033c61'}, {'abstract': 'Higher floors often get poor reception because most telephone carriers have installed antennas on building roofs relatively low to the ground, to reach users on the sidewalks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/realestate/09posting.html', 'snippet': 'Higher floors often get poor reception because most telephone carriers have installed antennas on building roofs relatively low to the ground, to reach users on the sidewalks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN the 19th century, as buildings got taller across New York, engineers struggled to find ways to pump water to the uppermost floors. Now, with the mysteries of water pressure long since resolved, a new quandary is facing the city’s tallest residential buildings: poor cellphone reception. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/realestate/posting/posting-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/realestate/posting/posting-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/realestate/posting/posting-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Improving Cellphone Reception in Tall Buildings', 'kicker': 'Posting', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Too Lofty For Cell Service', 'name': N