he city’s Department of Consumer Affairs, Mr. de Blasio warned that the Broadway musical “Spider-Man: Turn Off the Dark” may be violating consumer protection laws by not clearly distinguishing its preview period (when the show is still considered a work in progress) from its post-opening performances (when the show is a finished product), and could face fines. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Public Advocate Says ‘Spider-Man’ May Violate Consumer Protection Laws', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T19:56:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a764078-d98a-5efa-81f5-abe43251d141', 'word_count': 1016, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a764078-d98a-5efa-81f5-abe43251d141'}, {'abstract': 'The Golden Globe Best Actor nominee cuts a swath in a three-piece suit — accessorized, naturally, with a Windsor knot.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/look-of-the-moment-colin-firth/', 'snippet': 'The Golden Globe Best Actor nominee cuts a swath in a three-piece suit — accessorized, naturally, with a Windsor knot.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Look: Royal Flash: The three-piece suit,\xa0a favorite of monarchs and kings of industry alike, reigns again. (Accessorized, naturally, with a Windsor knot.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Look of the Moment | Colin Firth', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Firth, Colin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T20:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/439bdbd4-83c3-58fc-a383-d1d74d85f935', 'word_count': 75, 'uri': 'nyt://article/439bdbd4-83c3-58fc-a383-d1d74d85f935'}, {'abstract': 'A cappella hits a high note.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/education/09cappella-t.html', 'snippet': 'A cappella hits a high note.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COLLEGIATE a cappella is enjoying a moment — perhaps its biggest since the Yale Whiffenpoofs were founded in 1909. It may be fitting that this year’s ’Poofs, who have the longest tradition, were eliminated early on this season’s “Sing-Off,” the hit a cappella version of “American Idol.” The most popular a cappella belies tradition for pop stylings and beatbox percussion, and stardom is just a viral video away. A few hits:', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/education/09cappella-t_CA0/09cappella-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/education/09cappella-t_CA0/09cappella-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/education/09cappella-t_CA0/09cappella-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/education/09cappella-t_CA0/09cappella-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/education/09cappella-t_CA0/09cappella-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Cappellooza', 'kicker': 'Blackboard | Singing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Cappellooza', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ohio University', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yale University', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tufts University', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Straight No Chaser', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of California, Berkeley', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T20:01:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisa Mala', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mala', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3c9b41b-5d7f-5650-8bda-5470e539b65b', 'word_count': 339, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3c9b41b-5d7f-5650-8bda-5470e539b65b'}, {'abstract': 'The reasonability of the Democratic proposals.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/on-filibuster-reform/', 'snippet': 'The reasonability of the Democratic proposals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the health care debate, I wrote a series of posts defending the filibuster — you can find them here, here, and here, with a much later follow-up here — and arguing for the virtues of a supermajority hurdle for controversial, big-ticket legislation. I stand by all of those arguments, but I also agree with Daniel Foster that the filibuster reform proposal the Senate Democrats are putting forward looks pretty reasonable.\xa0 Essentially, for the filibuster to work as I’d like it to work, it needs to be available as a check on temporary majorities without becoming a routine tactic for obstruction, which is how it’s increasingly employed. Ultimately, restoring this balance requires more collegiality, comity, and good manners: There’s no rule you can change that explicitly limits the filibuster to particularly sweeping and controversial pieces of legislation. (I mean, no reasonable rule, since I suppose you could imagine a provision stating that “60 votes shall only be required for cloture when Gallup shows that a majority of Americans oppose the legislation in question,” or something similarly implausible.) But the revisions the Democrats are contemplating — which would limit the filibuster to substantive measures (no filibustering motions to proceed) and require actual speechifying from filibustering senators — would at least make the filibuster feel more extraordinary than it does today. And that seems like a step in the right direction.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Filibuster Reform', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T20:01:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f37ade5-b85f-5679-8f9b-ed2848fddf31', 'word_count': 311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f37a