ook at some of the actor’s past performances.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/theater/01092011-BEDFORD-slide-AIMQ/01092011-BEDFORD-slide-AIMQ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stage Scenes: Brian Bedford', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bedford, Brian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T21:25:34+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Theater', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/d5b1cd06-5eaf-5ad1-a144-b5cbf6e661b5', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/d5b1cd06-5eaf-5ad1-a144-b5cbf6e661b5'}, {'abstract': 'The Tristano School, with its harmonically daring and super-fluent music, has become influential among a younger generation of jazz players.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/arts/music/09tristano.html', 'snippet': 'The Tristano School, with its harmonically daring and super-fluent music, has become influential among a younger generation of jazz players.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HAD he enjoyed a different sort of jazz career, you might say that Ted Brown was finally making a comeback. A tenor saxophonist drawn to a light and lyrically swinging style, Mr. Brown turned 83 last month, with just a handful of albums to his name. For the better part of 30 years, from the early 1960s on, he made his living as a computer programmer. “I’m not good at going out and getting gigs,” he said recently, sounding resigned and matter of fact. By his account his last booking in New York as a bandleader was in 1976 at the short-lived Midtown branch of George Wein’s Storyville club.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/arts/TRISTANO1_span/TRISTANO-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/arts/TRISTANO1_span/TRISTANO-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/arts/TRISTANO1_span/TRISTANO-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 821, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/arts/TRISTANO1_span/TRISTANO-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/arts/TRISTANO1_span/TRISTANO-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tristano School, Back in Session', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tristano School, Back in Session', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tristano, Lennie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T21:26:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '0', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14444e68-cf47-5a8a-adc5-9ff1f663afd6', 'word_count': 1609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14444e68-cf47-5a8a-adc5-9ff1f663afd6'}, {'abstract': 'Looking back at some of the music that got lost in the shuffle of 2010, including albums from Khaira Arby, Pantha du Prince and Jefferson Airplane.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/arts/music/09playlist.html', 'snippet': 'Looking back at some of the music that got lost in the shuffle of 2010, including albums from Khaira Arby, Pantha du Prince and Jefferson Airplane.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Farewell 2010? Not quite yet. There’s always the nagging-conscience pile: the music that didn’t get timely attention as the year rushed by. Here are some stragglers.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/arts/PLAYLIST1_span/PLAYLIST-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/arts/PLAYLIST1_span/PLAYLIST-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/arts/PLAYLIST1_span/PLAYLIST-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 817, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/arts/PLAYLIST1_span/PLAYLIST-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/arts/PLAYLIST1_span/PLAYLIST-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Melodic Wanderings, Starting in Timbuktu', 'kicker': 'Playlist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Melodic Wanderings, Starting in Timbuktu', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Prince', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Toure, Ali Farka', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Slick, Grace', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jefferson Airplane', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T21:26:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '0', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5f22137-68b6-5d51-b9d6-2a20089e5071', 'word_count': 793, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5f22137-68b6-5d51-b9d6-2a20089e5071'}, {'abstract': '“The Game,” which is returning to television on BET, was an inadvertent precursor to reality television about the semi-famous.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/arts/television/09game.html', 'snippet': '“The Game,” which is returning to television on BET, was an inadvertent precursor to reality television about the semi-famous.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DURING its first three seasons “The Game,” the dramedy about professional football players and the women who barely tolerate them, overflowed with cameos by black celebrities. There were singers like Musiq, rappers like Soulja Boy, athletes like Chad Ochocinco and more. Robin Givens and Rick Fox played themselves in recurring roles. These appearances added verisimilitude to a series already steeped in it. Few shows on television were as specific to their moment as “The Game.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/arts/GAME1_span/GAME-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xla