ittee proceedings anyway.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'House Cleans Up After Swearing-In Snafu', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T21:34:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Hulse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hulse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fce6e4d6-8ba2-51bb-b000-f6a22fa5b542', 'word_count': 299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fce6e4d6-8ba2-51bb-b000-f6a22fa5b542'}, {'abstract': "DealBook's Evelyn M. Rusli talks to Scott Kessler, an equities analyst at Standard & Poor's, about whether Facebook deserves the valuation it received from Goldman Sachs.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/business/1248069537388/is-facebook-worth-50-billion.html', 'snippet': "DealBook's Evelyn M. Rusli talks to Scott Kessler, an equities analyst at Standard & Poor's, about whether Facebook deserves the valuation it received from Goldman Sachs.", 'lead_paragraph': "DealBook's Evelyn M. Rusli talks to Scott Kessler, an equities analyst at Standard & Poor's, about whether Facebook deserves the valuation it received from Goldman Sachs.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/07/business/10927_1_db-facebook-kessler_75x75.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/07/business/10927_1_db-facebook-kessler_75x75.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Facebook Worth $50 Billion?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook.com', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuckerberg, Mark E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Standard & Poor's Corp", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rusli, Evelyn M', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T21:36:00+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'Mac William Bishop', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mac', 'middlename': 'William', 'lastname': 'Bishop', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/41968231-10e4-534b-9b68-c42f824807af', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/41968231-10e4-534b-9b68-c42f824807af'}, {'abstract': 'Just days after its keynote speech at the Consumer Electronics Show, Verizon invites reporters to a Tuesday event in New York, raising expectations that it will announce that the iPhone will finally be available on its network.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/is-it-finally-time-for-the-verizon-iphone/', 'snippet': 'Just days after its keynote speech at the Consumer Electronics Show, Verizon invites reporters to a Tuesday event in New York, raising expectations that it will announce that the iPhone will finally be available on its network.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just yesterday, Verizon’s top executives had the attention of the world’s technology press during a keynote speech and news conference at the Consumer Electronics Show in Las Vegas. The events left some disappointed: Verizon had little new to announce, and in particular, there was no announcement about an iPhone running on Verizon Wireless.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headl