'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T22:45:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ac2823e-77c4-5387-b541-3ca150848dd2', 'word_count': 309, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ac2823e-77c4-5387-b541-3ca150848dd2'}, {'abstract': 'There is some consensus among brokers that the market is improving, based on sales data over the last year.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/big-deal-a-slow-recovery-in-the-hamptons/', 'snippet': 'There is some consensus among brokers that the market is improving, based on sales data over the last year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Figuring out the state of the real estate market in the Hamptons can be tricky.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Deal | A ‘Slow Recovery’ in the Hamptons', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hamptons (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T22:48:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Kershaw', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershaw', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d5b4d36-bb99-532e-9338-b82f230fe291', 'word_count': 831, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d5b4d36-bb99-532e-9338-b82f230fe291'}, {'abstract': 'In the pre-weekend round-up, the Academy announces its technical winners, Bafta winnows its lists and the makers of “True Grit” conduct a thought experiment about “Guess Who’s Coming to Dinner.”', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/the-kings-speech-advances-and-the-coens-ponder-another-remake/', 'snippet': 'In the pre-weekend round-up, the Academy announces its technical winners, Bafta winnows its lists and the makers of “True Grit” conduct a thought experiment about “Guess Who’s Coming to Dinner.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Academy Awards aren’t all celebrity glitz and glam: the Bagger spent all day hanging out at an outer-borough elementary school, and it was still part of the Oscar beat. (Stay tuned to find out why.) Also today, the Academy announced the winners of its technical awards, which appear as a blip on the telecast but get to enjoy what is probably a pretty fun ceremony, usually hosted by a pretty young actress (Jessica Alba is totally scientific, right?). The honorees at the nerd Oscars will include Dr. Mark Sagar, for “influential facial motion retargeting solutions,” a.k.a. a way to transform motion-capture into a stable of animated characters; Mark Noel and John Frazier, for creating a winch system that allows cars, planes and other large props to be flown on wires; and Eric Tabellion and Arnauld Lamorlette, for “the creation of a computer graphics bounce lighting methodology that is practical at feature film scale,” as seen in “Shrek 2” and described in their paper “An Approximate Global Illumination System for Computer Generated Films.” What if the Bagger covered these Oscars instead? The mind boggles.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The King’s Speech’ Advances and the Coens Ponder Another Remake', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-07T22:49:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a11e41b8-10dc-536c-865e-61e3c6c37e55', 'word_count': 375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a11e41b8-10dc-536c-865e-61e3c6c37e55'}, {'abstract': 'At Landmark, a college for the learning disabled, studying is one skill, just getting to class another.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/education/edlife/09landmark-t.html', 'snippet': 'At Landmark, a college for the learning disabled, studying is one skill, just getting to class another.', 'lead_paragraph': 'RAYNE NELSON, a 21-year-old sophomore at Landmark College in Putney, Vt., does not let her attention deficit hyperactivity disorder throw her off track.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/education/edlife/09landmark-1/09landmark-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/education/edlife/09landmark-1/09landmark-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/education/edlife/09landmark-1/09landmark-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/education/edlife/09landmark-1/09landmark-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/ed