f9a0f0'}, {'abstract': 'The head of the House immigration subcommittee will be Elton Gallegly of California, not Steve King of Iowa.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/surprise-choice-for-immigration-panel/', 'snippet': 'The head of the House immigration subcommittee will be Elton Gallegly of California, not Steve King of Iowa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The new Republican chairman of the House Judiciary Committee, Lamar Smith of Texas, has passed over an outspoken immigration hard-liner and member of the Tea Party caucus for chairman of the immigration subcommittee.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Surprise Choice for Immigration Panel', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-08T01:28:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/775f6cd8-4529-57ca-8719-d5801b382672', 'word_count': 443, 'uri': 'nyt://article/775f6cd8-4529-57ca-8719-d5801b382672'}, {'abstract': 'An early television-writing gig unravels, and the author hits the bottle — which hits back.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/sauce-for-the-goose-take-a-gander/', 'snippet': 'An early television-writing gig unravels, and the author hits the bottle — which hits back.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To this day I don’t know what made me do it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sauce for the Goose? Take a Gander', 'kicker': 'Dick Cavett', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-08T01:30:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dick Cavett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cavett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2679d8e-25f4-5fc4-90e5-9a063d8cb8de', 'word_count': 1266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2679d8e-25f4-5fc4-90e5-9a063d8cb8de'}, {'abstract': 'Conditional cash programs provide a lifeline to those who need it most.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/07/helping-the-worlds-poorest-for-a-change/', 'snippet': 'Conditional cash programs provide a lifeline to those who need it most.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tuesday’s Fixes column was about conditional cash transfers, a new and highly successful large-scale anti-poverty program.\xa0 It pays poor women as long as they meet certain responsibilities, like keeping their children in school and taking their families for regular medical care.\xa0 It’s in use in Brazil, Mexico and 38 other countries.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Helping the World’s Poorest, for a Change', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Third World and Developing Countries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Bank', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-08T01:30:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e936432-ae7f-5c95-9ff5-ddf6f50ecd57', 'word_count': 1935, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e936432-ae7f-5c95-9ff5-ddf6f50ecd57'}, {'abstract': 'The Supreme Court will decide whether states may forbid government officials from voting on matters where they appear to have a conflict.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/08/us/politics/08scotus.html', 'snippet': 'The Supreme Court will decide whether states may forbid government officials from voting on matters where they appear to have a conflict.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON \x97 The Supreme Court on Friday agreed to decide whether states may forbid government officials to vote on matters where they appear to have a personal conflict. The case, which concerns a member of a city council in Nevada, was one of seven the court added to its docket.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Justices to Hear Case on Recusal Laws', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Justices to Hear Case on Recusal Laws', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conflicts of Interest', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-08T01:31:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/70ec3b54-7183-56b5-b21f-2fc79ac0eb87', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70ec3b54-7183-56b5-b21f-2fc79ac0eb87'}, {'abstract': 'A search for the pretty girl in the back and the boy with polio, among others in a gifted class in Brooklyn, yielded many success stories.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/nyregion/09class.html', 'snippet': 'A search for the pretty girl in the back and the boy with polio, among others in a gifted class in Brooklyn, yielded many success stories.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR most of my life I wondered what had become of them: the pretty girl in the back; the boy who used to be my best friend; the teacher, Mr. Goldman, with his blondish hair and falling pompadour. There were 35 of us in a special class of gifted children — 9 S-P — at Junior High School 149 in East New York, Brooklyn. Two little girls who turned boys’ heads, Faith Hurwitz and Marilyn Bloom, held a sign in our graduation picture reading, “June 1950.”', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/nyregion/09CLASS1/09CLASS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/09/nyregion/09CLASS1/09CLASS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/09/nyregion/09CLASS1/09CLASS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/09/nyregion/09CLASS1/09CLASS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Class of 1950, Primed for Success', 'kicker': 'New York Story', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Class of 1950, Primed for Success', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'v