: 409, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec30f44a-1b42-5742-9124-318eb42f4f70'}, {'abstract': 'A look at the implications of a planet with 7 billion people, the population later this year.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/08/7-billion-and-counting/', 'snippet': 'A look at the implications of a planet with 7 billion people, the population later this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'National Geographic magazine has kicked off a yearlong series of articles related to humanity’s remarkable growth spurt, with the first story focusing on the consequences of hitting 7 billion humans later this year and the basics of demography, from Malthus forward. The piece was written by Robert Kunzig, a senior editor there who was a colleague of mine at Discover Magazine in the late 1980s. I caught up with him via Skype a couple of days ago and here’s our chat (I hope you’ll excuse the lag and fuzziness): ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '7 Billion and Counting', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Geography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Population', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohen, Joel E', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Neill, Brian", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Geographic', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wilson, Woodrow, International Center for Scholars', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-08T14:06:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5e6fdbe-a160-5b87-ac22-da9ad1531eba', 'word_count': 574, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5e6fdbe-a160-5b87-ac22-da9ad1531eba'}, {'abstract': 'Mitch Daniels, Indiana’s Republican governor, says the bursting stock-market bubble of a decade ago erased the budget surpluses that President Bill Clinton bequeathed President George W. Bush. That is not accurate.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/08/bush-the-bubble-and-the-deficit/', 'snippet': 'Mitch Daniels, Indiana’s Republican governor, says the bursting stock-market bubble of a decade ago erased the budget surpluses that President Bill Clinton bequeathed President George W. Bush. That is not accurate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wolf Blitzer was kind enough to read a portion of a recent Economix post on CNN this week. Here he is, interviewing Mitch Daniels, Indiana’s Republican governor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bush, the Bubble and the Deficit', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daniels, Mitchell E Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-08T14:30:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc320448-3b15-58e7-bebb-df98619d3bf2', 'word_count': 331, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc320448-3b15-58e7-bebb-df98619d3bf2'}, {'abstract': 'Citrusy and packed with vitamin C, but it might not slake your thirst.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/08/urban-forager-pine-tree-tea/', 'snippet': 'Citrusy and packed with vitamin C, but it might not slake your thirst.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back in the mid-1960s, decades ago, the foraging forefather Euell Gibbons, Grape Nuts spokesman and author of “Stalking the Healthful Herbs,” asked millions of Americans if they had ever eaten a pine tree. While I’ve never consumed a pine tree per se, I was interested in sampling some pine needles for a wholesome tea. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Urban Forager | Pine Tree Tea', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-08T14:41:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ava Chin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ava', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8a2c904-b8e7-53d0-9143-6e65629d4385', 'word_count': 384, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8a2c904-b8e7-53d0-9143-6e65629d4385'}, {'abstract': 'To a rapturous welcome, the populist Iraqi cleric Moktada al-Sadr delivered his support for an Iraqi state that he had once derided as a tool of the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/world/middleeast/09iraq.html', 'snippet': 'To a rapturous welcome, the populist Iraqi cleric Moktada al-Sadr delivered his support for an Iraqi state that he had once derided as a tool of the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NAJAF, Iraq \x97 To a rapturous welcome that conflated the religious and political, the populist Iraqi cleric Moktada al-Sadr delivered his support on Saturday for an Iraqi state he had once derided as a traitorous tool of the United States and that his followers had battled in the streets of Iraq’s most important cities only a few years before.', 