le', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Wines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da1f74c6-b9c2-5454-a075-6b893523a9b1', 'word_count': 313, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da1f74c6-b9c2-5454-a075-6b893523a9b1'}, {'abstract': 'Vietnam’s economy is growing, but rising food prices are punishing the working class, and the country’s currency is consistently falling below the official exchange rates.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/business/global/10iht-viet10.html', 'snippet': 'Vietnam’s economy is growing, but rising food prices are punishing the working class, and the country’s currency is consistently falling below the official exchange rates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HO CHI MINH CITY, Vietnam \x97 Hammer and sickle flags are flying here as Ho Chi Minh City, the seemingly irrepressible bastion of Vietnamese capitalism, dutifully marks the start on Tuesday of the Communist Party’s National Congress, an event held every five years to chart the course of a country that has witnessed an economic miracle in recent decades. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/10/world/VIETNAM_span/VIETNAM-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/10/world/VIETNAM_span/VIETNAM-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/10/world/VIETNAM_span/VIETNAM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'lega