there, and I’m very grateful that he’s taking the time to answer reader questions. This is our first batch of responses — stay tuned for more in the days to come. And there’s still time to pose your own questions. Now let me kick this off by asking the first question of President Carter:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Answering Your Sudan Questions, Take 1', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-09T23:26:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd9cf353-b07e-5f95-8e62-4d983ddca7f7', 'word_count': 1369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd9cf353-b07e-5f95-8e62-4d983ddca7f7'}, {'abstract': 'Universal Music has donated a huge archive of recordings made from 1926 to 1948, including the chitchat between takes, to the Library of Congress.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/arts/music/10masters.html', 'snippet': 'Universal Music has donated a huge archive of recordings made from 1926 to 1948, including the chitchat between takes, to the Library of Congress.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Library of Congress has begun taking possession of a huge donation of recordings, some 200,000 metal, glass and lacquer master discs from the period 1926 to 1948 that have been languishing in the subterranean vaults of Universal Music Group, the largest music conglomerate in the United States.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/10/arts/MASTERS/MASTERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 922, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Library of Congress Gets a Mile of Music', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Library Of Congress Gets a Mile Of Music', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Universal Music Group', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recordings and Downloads (Audio)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Archives and Records', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Library of Congress', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-09T23:26:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2888fc6-1705-594a-8ae6-1cdc9f7a8c70', 'word_count': 1255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2888fc6-1705-594a-8ae6-1cdc9f7a8c70'}, {'abstract': 'Billie Joe Armstrong, the Green Day frontman, brings a jolt of rock-god electricity to the Broadway musical “American Idiot.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/theater/10idiot.html', 'snippet': 'Billie Joe Armstrong, the Green Day frontman, brings a jolt of rock-god electricity to the Broadway musical “American Idiot.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rock star impersonators are not exactly rare creatures on Broadway today. An imitation Elvis and Jerry Lee and friends are stirring up trouble in “Million Dollar Quartet” at the Nederlander Theater. Flesh facsimiles of Paul, John, George and Ringo are running through the Beatles’ greatest hits nightly in “Rain” at the Neil Simon.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/10/arts/IDIOT/IDIOT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 569, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/10/arts/IDIOT/IDIOT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/10/arts/IDIOT/IDIOT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Idiot’ Welcomes Back a Bad Influence', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Idiot’ Welcomes Back a Bad Influence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARMSTRONG, BILLIE JOE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Day', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-09T23:29:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02c18169-4b0e-5625-9404-23ded00639d3', 'word_count': 893, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02c18169-4b0e-5625-9404-23ded00639d3'}, {'abstract': 'The Green Day front man talks about the pressures of being in a Broadway musical and helps stave off anxiety by putting on a red sequined dress.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/welcome-to-a-new-kind-of-tension-billie-joe-armstrong-on-his-american-idiot-role/', 'snippet': 'The Green Day front man talks about the pressures of being in a Broadway musical and helps stave off anxiety by putting on a red sequined dress.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Billie Joe Armstrong plays many roles in “American Idiot,” and that’s before he even takes the stage at the St. James Theater. Mr. Armstrong, the 38-year-old Green Day front man and guitarist and a co-writer of that Broadway musical adapted from his band’s hit album of the same title, rejoined the show on Jan. 1 to resume the role of St. Jimmy, the pugnacious, drug-pushing alter ego of the protagonist, Johnny (portrayed by John Gallagher Jr.). (Charles Isherwood’s review of Mr. Armstrong in “American Idiot” appears in Monday’s New York Times.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Welcome to a New Kind of Tension: Billie Joe Armstrong on His ‘American Idiot’ Role', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARMSTRONG, BILLIE JOE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Day', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-09T23:31:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/026848b4-1ac1-5958-996e-1b8e2a871da7', 'word_count': 1924, 'uri': 'nyt://article/026848b4-1ac1-5958-996e-1b8e2a871da7'}, {'abstract': '“No One Killed Jessica,” a film by Raj Kumar Gupta, revisits a notorious murder case in India.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/movies/10noone.html', 'snippet': '“No One Killed Jessica,” a film by Raj Kumar Gupta, revisits a notorious murder case in India.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1999 Jessica Lal, a model tending bar at an elite nightspot in New Delhi, was shot dead by a customer who was refused a drink. A trial in 2006 acquitted the man, the son of a wealthy former government minister, but he was later found guilty by the Delhi High Court and sentenced to life imprisonment.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/10/arts/NOONE/NOONE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 308, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/10/arts/NOONE/NOONE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 308}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/10/arts/NOONE/NOONE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 526, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/10/arts/NOONE/NOONE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/10/arts/NOONE/NOONE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Indian Murder Reverberates on Film', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'No One Killed Jessica'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Indian Murder Reverberates on Film', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-09T23:39:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7811d19d-5dee-5e28-901e-0829317fb1bb', 'word_count': 422, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7811d19d-5dee-5e28-901e-0829317fb1bb'}, {'abstract': 'Polina Semionova will join American Ballet Theater as a guest artist in two performances during its 2011 season at the Metropolitan Opera House.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/arts/dance/10arts-POLINASEMION_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Polina Semionova will join American Ballet Theater as a guest artist in two performances during its 2011 season at the Metropolitan Opera House.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Polina Semionova, above, will join American Ballet Theater as a guest artist in two performances during its 2011 season at the Metropolitan Opera House, the company has announced. Ms. Semionova, a principal dancer with the Berlin State Opera Ballet since 2002, will dance the role of Kitri in “Don Quixote,” opposite David Hallberg, a principal with American Ballet Theater, as Basilio, on May 21. On July 2 Ms. Semionova will dance the dual roles of Odette and Odile in “Swan Lake,” again opposite Mr. Hallberg as Prince Siegfried. Ms. Semionova has performed both roles for the Berlin State Opera Ballet.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Polina Semionova to Dance at the Met', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Polina Semionova To Dance at the Met', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-09T23:41:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/45b45bd6-1f2e-5247-beb0-9f04d34ab90f', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45b45bd6-1f2e-5247-beb0-9f04d34ab90f'}, {'abstract': 'The National Book Foundation, which presents the National Book Awards, has organized a retrospective of its past poetry winners.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/books/10arts-POETRYGOESDI_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The National Book Foundation, which presents the National Book Awards, has organized a retrospective of its past poetry winners.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The National Book Foundation, which presents the National Book Awards, has organized a retrospective of its past poetry winners, with daily blog posts by emerging poets about the winners and a series of public programs in New York; Minneapolis; and Portland, Ore., the foundation announced. The blog posts, which will also include a biography of each poet, will begin on Feb. 14 with an original essay about William Carlos Williams, the first winner of the National Book Award for Poetry in 1950. On Feb. 24 Elizabeth Alexander, who was selected to read at President Obama’s Inauguration in 2009; Stephen Burt; Tony Hoagland; James Longenbach; Maureen N. McLane; and Susan Stewart will begin the series of programs with the panel discussion “Lineage: American Poetry Since 1950,” moderated by Craig Morgan Teicher at the New School in Manhattan. They will also read from their own work on Feb. 25 at the Center for Book Arts in Chelsea. The retrospective is financed by the National Endowment for the Arts and will culminate in a digitized literary archive on the foundation’s Web site, nationalbook.org, that will include curatorial appreciations of the past winners’ books, images of the original covers, portrait photographs, and related information and links.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poetry Goes Digital', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Poetry Goes Digital', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Book Foundation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-09T23:41:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3bb8fd1-5d1d-5a5f-b2de-65c34cb3a3b7', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3bb8fd1-5d1d-5a5f-b2de-65c34cb3a3b7'}, {'abstract': 'The Broadway production of “The Merchant of Venice” has earned back its original capitalization costs of $3.45 million, the producers announced.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/theater/10arts-MERCHANTOFVE_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The Broadway production of “The Merchant of Venice” has earned back its original capitalization costs of $3.45 million, the producers announced.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Broadway production of “The Merchant of Venice” has earned back its original capitalization costs of $3.45 million, the producers announced, becoming one of the few Broadway shows during each theater season to turn a profit. Starring Al Pacino as Shylock and Lily Rabe as Portia, this “Merchant” was first mounted by the nonprofit Public Theater last summer at its stage in Central Park, where tickets were free. Directed by Daniel Sullivan, the play proved hugely popular with critics and audiences, and the Public \x97 along with the producers Jeffrey Richards, Jerry Frankel and others \x97 transferred the production to the Broadhurst Theater for a commercial Broadway run in mid-October. “Merchant” begins a three-week hiatus this week to accommodate a film commitment of Mr. Pacino’s; the play is set to resume on Feb. 1, with the cast largely intact, and run through Feb. 20. “Merchant” was regularly the top-grossing play on Broadway this fall and winter; one other new production of a play that began on Broadway this fall, “Driving Miss Daisy,” starring Vanessa Redgrave and James Earl Jones, has recouped its capitalization costs of $2.6 million.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Merchant of Venice’ Recoups Investment', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Merchant of Venice’ Recoups Investment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-09T23:42:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/355bad59-99ef-5bf0-95f3-35e676d8d391', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/355bad59-99ef-5bf0-95f3-35e676d8d391'}, {'abstract': 'An exhibition that focuses on Adolf Hitler at the German Historical Museum in Berlin has been extended by three weeks because of high attendance, Agence France-Presse reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/arts/design/10arts-SHOWONHITLER_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'An exhibition that focuses on Adolf Hitler at the German Historical Museum in Berlin has been extended by three weeks because of high attendance, Agence France-Presse reported.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Hitler and the Germans: Nation and Crime,” an exhibition that focuses on Adolf Hitler at the German Historical Museum in Berlin, has been extended by three weeks because of high attendance, Agence France-Presse reported. The show features displays of Nazi propaganda, like swastika Christmas decorations and Nazi figurines, alongside a show that highlights the realities of what the Nazis represented, like war and genocide. The exhibition has been billed as the first in Germany to focus exclusively on Hitler since the end of World War II. Rudolf Trabold, a museum spokesman, said that over 170,000 visitors had attended since the show opened in October. It was to close on Feb. 6 but will now remain on view through Feb. 27.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Show on Hitler Extended', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Show on Hitler Extended', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hitler, Adolf', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-09T23:43:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/324c271f-aea7-5753-a200-313be2c08076', 'word_count': 122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/324c271f-aea7-5753-a200-313be2c08076'}, {'abstract': 'Key Porter Books, a prominent Canadian publishing house in Bolton, Ontario, announced it would temporarily suspend publishing operations, The Globe and Mail of Toronto reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/books/10arts-CANADIANPUBL_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Key Porter Books, a prominent Canadian publishing house in Bolton, Ontario, announced it would temporarily suspend publishing operations, The Globe and Mail of Toronto reported.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Key Porter Books, a prominent Canadian publishing house in Bolton, Ontario, announced it would temporarily suspend publishing operations, The Globe and Mail of Toronto reported. The company, which releases around 100 books a year, laid off almost two-thirds of its staff members in September and said on Friday that it would halt production of all new titles. It would continue to market and distribute books already in print and would consider restructuring options, according to a company statement. “Key Porter Books has played a leading role in giving a voice to the Canadian story, and we will do everything possible to ensure that voice continues to be heard,” Jordan Fenn, the vice president and publisher, said in the statement. Several authors and literary agents have expressed concern over the status of their books and frustration with the company’s lack of communication. Julie Devaney, who planned to have her first book released by Key Porter in April, told The Globe and Mail that finding “as good of a fit” with another publisher would be difficult. Key Porter was founded by the author and publisher Anna Porter as a joint venture with Key Publishers in 1979 and acquired by H. B. Fenn & Company in 2004. It has represented authors including Margaret Atwood, Joan Barfoot and Conrad Black.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Canadian Publisher Halts Production', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Canadian Publisher Halts Production', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-09T23:44:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3709432-efae-5d0e-82b0-273e588df14a', 'word_count': 219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3709432-efae-5d0e-82b0-273e588df14a'}, {'abstract': '“True Grit” continued its tear at North American theaters over the weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/movies/10arts-TRUEGRITSUST_BRF.html', 'snippet': '“True Grit” continued its tear at North American theaters over the weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': '“True Grit” (with Jeff Bridges and Hailee Steinfeld, above), a sober western and an Oscar hopeful, continued its tear at North American theaters over the weekend, beating the overtly commercial “Little Fockers” for first place at the box office. “True Grit” (Paramount) was No. 1 with about $15 million in ticket sales for a three-week total of $110.4 million, according to Hollywood.com, which compiles box-office statistics. “Little Fockers” (Universal) was second with $13.8 million, bringing its three-week total to about $124 million. “Season of the Witch,” a newcomer starring Nicolas Cage, sold about $10.7 million in third place for Relativity Media. “Tron: Legacy” (Walt Disney Studios) sold about $10 million in fourth place, bringing its four-week total to about $148 million. Heavy television advertising helped “Black Swan” (Fox Searchlight) take fifth place. That Oscar hopeful, starring Natalie Portman, sold $8.4 million to lift its six-week total to $61.5 million. The only other new release of note, “Country Strong” (Sony) with Gwyneth Paltrow, opened quietly in sixth place with $7.3 million.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘True Grit’ Sustains Its Oscar Push', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘True Grit’ Sustains Its Oscar Push', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-09T23:45:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b28fdf00-2768-5edf-af3e-f74a4eea93f5', 'word_count': 176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b28fdf00-2768-5edf-af3e-f74a4eea93f5'}, {'abstract': 'New York Winter Jazzfest, an annual event, packed clubs in Greenwich Village.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/arts/music/10jazzfests.html', 'snippet': 'New York Winter Jazzfest, an annual event, packed clubs in Greenwich Village.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I saw a lot of the following at the 2011 New York Winter Jazzfest, held in five clubs within a three-block radius in Greenwich Village on Friday and Saturday nights: Female musicians. Hyperarticulate trumpet players. Music from foreign lands — Iraq, India, Cuba and the lost continent of prog-rock. Bands with a Fender Rhodes keyboard. A fascinating range of drummers, very fine to clunky-passing-as-cool. And other people’s backs.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/10/arts/JAZZFEST/JAZZFEST-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 343, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/10/arts/JAZZFEST/JAZZFEST-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 343}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type':