as no exception.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Large retailers sent 15 percent more promotional e-mails per subscriber in 2010 than in 2009, according to Responsys, an online marketing firm. Responsys arrived at the figure by setting up dummy accounts to track the e-mail marketing practices of some 100 of the largest online retailers. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In the In-Box, More Retail Promotions', 'kicker': 'Drilling Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In the In-Box, More Retail Promotions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronic Mail', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T00:42:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Mindlin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mindlin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/74406d84-a3ed-5e3e-9734-23873f12c245', 'word_count': 148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74406d84-a3ed-5e3e-9734-23873f12c245'}, {'abstract': '6 Q’s About the News | What tragic events took place on Saturday in Tucson, Arizona?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/an-american-tragedy/', 'snippet': '6 Q’s About the News | What tragic events took place on Saturday in Tucson, Arizona?', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHO is Representative Gabrielle Giffords?WHO is Judge John M. Roll?WHO is Jared Lee Loughner?WHO else was hurt or killed in Saturday’s shootings in Arizona?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An American Tragedy', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T00:45:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/241491ab-fbac-5fe1-8061-a0df35f96c1e', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/241491ab-fbac-5fe1-8061-a0df35f96c1e'}, {'abstract': 'Student Opinion | Did a toxic political environment contribute to this tragedy? If so, what should be done?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/will-the-arizona-shootings-change-our-political-culture/', 'snippet': 'Student Opinion | Did a toxic political environment contribute to this tragedy? If so, what should be done?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an apparent attempt on Saturday to assassinate Representative Gabrielle Giffords of Arizona, a troubled 22-year-old college dropout shot 20 people, killing six of them. Both political parties have now begun a “wrenching process of soul-searching about the tone of political discourse,” wondering if a “lack of civility contributed to the bloodshed in Tucson.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Will the Arizona Shootings Change Our Political Culture?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T00:46:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fef897e-fc31-5f3a-b586-14f39b0f6b64', 'w