ine.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/hey-mr-critic-19/', 'snippet': 'Sam Sifton offers advice on where to dine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The mail brought among other things questions of religious difference and financial management, requests for reservation advice and for a place to watch television while eating well. I tried my best to answer. As ever you can send your own questions to dinejournal@nytimes.com. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hey, Mr. Critic!', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kosher Foods', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fornino Park Slope (NYC Restaurant)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Le Relais de Venise', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Midtown Lunch', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Momofuku Ssam Bar', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T13:47:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Sifton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sifton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e7b5fb8-d08c-5379-928f-31860fd983b7', 'word_count': 1637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e7b5fb8-d08c-5379-928f-31860fd983b7'}, {'abstract': 'As the season heads in an awards-heavy week,the National Society of Film Critics offers up some surprises, including Olivia Williams as best supporting actress.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/ghost-writer-makes-a-move/', 'snippet': 'As the season heads in an awards-heavy week,the National Society of Film Critics offers up some surprises, including Olivia Williams as best supporting actress.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Social Network” may have continued its sweep of professional opionators on Saturday when it earned the top prize (and more) from the National Society of Film Critics, but there were some surprises among the winners. The group, consisting of 61 writers from publications like Time, The Village Voice and The New Yorker, gave the best supporting actress award to Olivia Williams for her role as a political wife in Roman Polanski’s “The Ghost Writer.” She creamed her competition, getting substantially more votes than Amy Adams and Melissa Leo for “The Fighter” or Jacki Weaver for “Animal Kingdom.” “The Ghost Writer” also placed in the screenplay and director category, which may mean that it has a better-than-a-dark-horse chance at the Oscars (a dappled horse?). The society does reveal its final tally, an unusual openness in the world of trophy-giving. For a closer look at what their numbers mean, check out The Hollywood Reporter’s take. As Tim Appelo notes there, if Ms. Williams does win an Oscar, she will have to hand it over to Mr. Polanski – as she promised she would – because he’s got a pretty clear vision for his performers. “He acts the scene for you,” she said. “And if you don’t copy what he does, he goes, ‘No! Like this,’ — pause, movement, intonation, the works. So the prize for any performance really does go to Roman.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Ghost Writer’ Makes a Move', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Polanski, Roman', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Society of Film Critics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T13:59:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/68f92998-40b1-5c55-a48a-78ab1a7e0244', 'word_count': 286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68f92998-40b1-5c55-a48a-78ab1a7e0244'}, {'abstract': 'The Seahawks used brute force, in the form of Marshawn Lynch’s touchdown run, and  a little trickery, when John Carlson faked a flop and scored, to help beat the Saints.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/lynchs-brilliant-run-and-carlsons-fake-flop/', 'snippet': 'The Seahawks used brute force, in the form of Marshawn Lynch’s touchdown run, and  a little trickery, when John Carlson faked a flop and scored, to help beat the Saints.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the greatest postseason runs in N.F.L. history. … Can we remove the word “postseason” and call it one of the greatest runs in N.F.L. history?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lynch’s Brilliant Run, and Carlson’s Fake Flop', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carlson, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lynch, Marshawn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Orleans Saints', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Seattle Seahawks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T13:59:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cc3d5c1-a4e7-5d19-8f6c-601d8c95cb