 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/11/sports/y-fast-1/y-fast-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 628, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/11/sports/y-fast-1/y-fast-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/11/sports/y-fast-1/y-fast-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rushing Games Rise for the Playoffs', 'kicker': 'N.F.L. Fast Forward', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rushing Games Rise for the Playoffs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Super Bowl', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lynch, Marshawn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Indianapolis Colts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T16:50:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judy Battista', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Battista', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6cbaf3b-90ec-54c5-9423-700a30c1017d', 'word_count': 1078, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6cbaf3b-90ec-54c5-9423-700a30c1017d'}, {'abstract': 'Lee Badgett, an expert in policy issues that affect same-sex couples and their families, shares her financial resolutions for the new year.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/resolved-make-some-financial-choices/', 'snippet': 'Lee Badgett, an expert in policy issues that affect same-sex couples and their families, shares her financial resolutions for the new year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week on the Bucks blog, our contributors, along with some of our favorite sources, are writing about their new year’s financial resolutions. Here, Lee Badgett, research director of the Williams Institute, a research group that studies sexual orientation policy issues, shares her own plans. She is also a professor of economics and director of the Center for Public Policy and Administration at the University of Massachusetts, Amherst.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Resolved: Make Some Financial Choices', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New Year', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T17:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lee Badgett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lee', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Badgett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/db6248b9-e9a2-5938-aa0e-c33bc9641b07', 'word_count': 506, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db6248b9-e9a2-5938-aa0e-c33bc9641b07'}, {'abstract': 'Not the same.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/economics-and-morality/', 'snippet': 'Not the same.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mark Thoma directs me to Eric Schoeneberg, who argues that the right is winning economic debates because people believe, wrongly, that there’s something inherently moral about free-market outcomes. My guess is that this is only part of the story; there’s more than a bit of Ayn Randism on the right, but there’s also the appeal of simplicity: goldbuggism is intellectually easy, Keynesianism is intellectually hard, as evidenced by the inability of many trained economists to get it. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Economics and Morality', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T17:03:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/82fb7b82-b38d-50c9-93e1-132203b194e3', 'word_count': 515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82fb7b82-b38d-50c9-93e1-132203b194e3'}, {'abstract': 'Why the burgers sell — but the burger joint doesn’t.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/can-this-business-be-sold/', 'snippet': 'Why the burgers sell — but the burger joint doesn’t.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, NBC Nightly News featured the story of Ann Price, who is proprietor of Ann’s Snack Bar in Atlanta and has been struggling to sell her business. While Ann’s is a beloved regional icon, both the NBC Nightly News feature and this New York Times video that was published in November (along with this article) offer some wonderfully visual examples of classic problems that can make it very hard to sell a small business.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can This Business Be Sold?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Price, Ann', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Atlanta (Ga)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T17:04:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Barbara Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Barbara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d193ecd3-6736-57ce-bac3-18dc3ec5497e', 'word_count': 738, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d193ecd3-6736-57ce-bac3-18dc3ec5497e'}, {'abstract': 'Haydn, Mozart, Beethoven and Schubert: What made Vienna such a hotbed of musical creativity?', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/top-10-composers-the-vienna-four/', 'snippet': 'Haydn, Mozart, Beethoven and Schubert: What made Vienna such a hotbed of musical creativity?', 'lead_paragraph': 'For any attempt to determine the top 10 classical composers in history, like the one we embarked on in the Arts & Leisure section on Sunday, the Viennese Classical period presents a special challenge. If such a list is to be at all diverse and comprehensive, how could 4 of the 10 slots go to composers — Haydn, Mozart, Beethoven and Schubert — who worked in Vienna during, say, the 75 years from 1750 to 1825? What on earth was going on there to foster such achievement?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Top 10 Composers: The Vienna Four', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haydn, Franz Joseph', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mozart, Wolfgang Amadeus', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T17:04:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0270bf5d-fd74-5fe6-bf25-1f0bd1ff10bd', 'word_count': 972, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0270bf5d-fd74-5fe6-bf25-1f0bd1ff10bd'}, {'abstract': 'The model, a longtime sales leader for Ford’s European operations, will be built in the United States, and will be offered as both a hybrid and plug-in hybrid.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/c-max-ford-europes-family-truckster-gets-hybrid-power-for-u-s-market/', 'snippet': 'The model, a longtime sales leader for Ford’s European operations, will be built in the United States, and will be offered as both a hybrid and plug-in hybrid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Introduced on Monday: 2012 Ford C-Max', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'C-Max: Ford Europe’s Family Truckster Gets Hybrid Power for U.S. Market', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'North American International Auto Show', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T17:04:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Stenquist', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stenquist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c94220d7-f787-5ffe-b3d9-5f36f2f49070', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c94220d7-f787-5ffe-b3d9-5f36f2f49070'}, {'abstract': 'Public support for stricter gun control has fallen significantly over the past 20 years, and there is little evidence to suggest that major gun crimes change opinions on the issue.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/support-for-gun-control-has-dropped-in-recent-years/', 'snippet': 'Public support for stricter gun control has fallen significantly over the past 20 years, and there is little evidence to suggest that major gun crimes change opinions on the issue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the wake of Saturday’s shooting in Arizona, there are likely to be new polls out this week measuring the public’s support for stricter gun control laws. Until they surface, it is worth noting that support for stricter gun control has significantly dropped over the last couple of decades, and there is little evidence to suggest that major gun crimes change opinions on the issue.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Support for Gun Control Has Dropped in Recent Years', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T17:05:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dalia Sussman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dalia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sussman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce7f48bd-efef-50a8-81e8-0ad6896cf57c', 'word_count': 319, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce7f48bd-efef-50a8-81e8-0ad6896cf57c'}, {'abstract': 'Locals in Brooklyn have been host to a delicious, charred secret. Till now, anyway.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/now-smoking-in-flatbush-peppas-jerk-chicken/', 'snippet': 'Locals in Brooklyn have been host to a delicious, charred secret. Till now, anyway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Locals in Flatbush, Brooklyn, know the routine well: Get off the\xa0Q train at the Parskide stop any time after 10 p.m. and you emerge into a cloud of smoke, as\xa0if some sort of remarkable flavored fireworks display has gone off. The source is\xa0Peppa’s Jerk\xa0Chicken,\xa0just around the corner on Flatbush Avenue.\xa0Open for more than six years, Peppa’s, despite the smoke signals, remains delightfully off the radar. For now, anyway.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Smoking in Flatbush | Peppa’s Jerk Chicken', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T17:10:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jordan Michelman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jordan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Michelman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/608a2731-14f3-536c-ad60-1e436354222d', 'word_count': 227, 'uri': 'nyt://article/608a2731-14f3-536c-ad60-1e436354222d'}, {'abstract': 'Goldman Sachs’ $450 million investment in Facebook has raised many questions. Here is a guide to understanding the legal issues raised by the deal.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/the-legal-issues-in-the-goldman-facebook-deal/', 'snippet': 'Goldman Sachs’ $450 million investment in Facebook has raised many questions. Here is a guide to understanding the legal issues raised by the deal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Goldman Sachs’s $450 million investment in Facebook has raised many questions. Here is a guide to understanding the legal issues raised by the deal. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Legal Issues in the Goldman-Facebook Deal', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name'