original': "By Anahad O'Connor", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anahad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connor", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c1235f5-0f4d-577a-9a46-9402c7bf7068', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c1235f5-0f4d-577a-9a46-9402c7bf7068'}, {'abstract': 'As the Jets try to avenge a regular-season rout by the Patriots, fans will see and hear a lot about Jets Coach Rex Ryan this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/sports/football/11leading.html', 'snippet': 'As the Jets try to avenge a regular-season rout by the Patriots, fans will see and hear a lot about Jets Coach Rex Ryan this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'America, you are about to enter a world New York has been stomaching (pun intended) for two seasons now. You are about to get a full plate (pun still intended) of Rex Ryan. The Jets beat the Colts to advance to the American Football Conference divisional playoffs, where they will face the big bad Patriots. Make no mistake, this will be all about Rex. If you think a 45-3 shellacking at the hands of the Patriots in December would quiet the bluster, well, you would be wrong. His every bombastic word will be repeated on every “SportsCenter” and Internet sports site. You will hear about his dislike for Patriots quarterback Tom Brady, his disdain for everything New England up to and including, we believe, clam chowder. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/11/sports/11leading-web1/11leading-web1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 897, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/11/sports/11leading-web1/11leading-web1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 