the blurriest of the troubled peripheral countries.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Portugal? O Nao!', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T17:52:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2522b6a-56bd-5c1b-b36b-ea3916ba1a15', 'word_count': 204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2522b6a-56bd-5c1b-b36b-ea3916ba1a15'}, {'abstract': 'Nasrin Sotoudeh, a female human rights lawyer, has been sentenced to 11 years in jail for crimes that include “activities against national security” and “propaganda against the regime.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/world/middleeast/11tehran.html', 'snippet': 'Nasrin Sotoudeh, a female human rights lawyer, has been sentenced to 11 years in jail for crimes that include “activities against national security” and “propaganda against the regime.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'TEHRAN \x97 A human rights lawyer has been sentenced by the Iranian authorities to 11 years in jail for crimes that include “activities against national security” and “propaganda against the regime,” family members confirmed Monday. In addition to her jail sentence, the lawyer, Nasrin Sotoudeh, was barred from practicing law and from leaving the country for 20 years, her husband, Reza Khandan, said in a telephone interview on Monday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iran Sentences Human Rights Lawyer to 11 Years in Jail', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iran Sentences Human Rights Lawyer to 11 Years in Jail', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T17:53:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Yong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f249769-fe5a-5dbe-9f92-d134a6a7d0e6', 'word_count': 465, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f249769-fe5a-5dbe-9f92-d134a6a7d0e6'}, {'abstract': 'Like many of New York’s apartment dwellers, chefs are forced to make do with small kitchens.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/nyregion/11appraisal.html', 'snippet': 'Like many of New York’s apartment dwellers, chefs are forced to make do with small kitchens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While some New Yorkers eat out or order in so often that they have been known to use kitchen cabinets as shoe storage, real estate brokers continue to talk up the merits of apartments that feature “chef’s kitchens.” The term conjures up visions of professionals using acres of counter space and island stoves to perfect their braising and baking \x97 like Julia Child’s kitchen memorialized in the Smithsonian. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/11/nyregion/Appraisal/Appraisal-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/11/nyregion/Appraisal/Appraisal-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/11/nyregion/Appraisal/Appraisal-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/11/nyregion/Appraisal/Appraisal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/11/nyregion/Appraisal/Appraisal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Even for Chefs, Home Kitchen Can Be Tight Fit', 'kicker': 'The Appraisal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Even for Chefs, the Kitchen At Home Can Be a Tight Fit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chefs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kitchens', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T17:54:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Haughney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haughney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9a3c685-561d-5a23-ab5f-38bec7ce4918', 'word_count': 719, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9a3c685-561d-5a23-ab5f-38bec7ce4918'}, {'abstract': 'Fans of Riccardo Tisci for Givenchy rejoice: his collection of women’s ready-to-wear is finally available on Barneys.com, tomorrow.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/chic-peek-barneys-com-and-givenchy/', 'snippet': 'Fans of Riccardo Tisci for Givenchy rejoice: his collection of women’s ready-to-wear is finally available on Barneys.com, tomorrow.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fans of Riccardo Tisci for Givenchy rejoice. Givenchy women’s ready-to-wear is finally available on Barneys.com, and to commemorate the event, the label has tapped the London-based director and Showstudio.com alumna Ruth Hogben (known for her recent films for Gareth Pugh and Celine, among others) to create a short film based on the spring/summer 2011 collection. In this exclusive snippet which appears above, attention-grabbing leopard prints share the spotlight with the Italian model Mariacarla Boscono, as styled by Panos Yiapanis. The complete film airs tomorrow on Barneys.com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chic Peek | Barneys.com and Givenchy', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T17:56:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By T Magazine', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e4179ba-b0b5-59fb-b852-8f035961b623', 'word_count': 86, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e4179ba-b0b5-59fb-b852-8f035961b623'}, {'abstract': 'Several work force experts and accounting executives say offering paid maternity leaves, telecommuting and the like can pay off for companies.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/workplace-flexibility-and-the-bottom-line/', 'snippet': 'Several work force experts and accounting executives say offering paid maternity leaves, telecommuting and the like can pay off for companies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For several years now, many women’s advocates have been pushing hard for more workplace flexibility to help workers — not just women, but also men — have an easier time juggling work and family responsibilities. But to some people, the whole notion of workplace flexibility — paid maternity leaves, more flexible schedules, telecommuting — is nice-sounding mumbo-jumbo that might makes employees feel good, but doesn’t translate into dollars and cents on the bottom line.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Workplace Flexibility and the Bottom Line', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACCOUNTING AND ACCOUNTANTS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fringe Benefits', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T17:56:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/617ef302-0a45-5531-8c37-a2e29226a1a0', 'word_count': 453, 'uri': 'nyt://article/617ef302-0a45-5531-8c37-a2e29226a1a0'}, {'abstract': 'Politicians, VW brand representatives, company brass and a chart-topping pop group were on hand to fête the four-door at a teaser party.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/before-debut-volkswagen-throws-coming-out-party-for-passat/', 'snippet': 'Politicians, VW brand representatives, company brass and a chart-topping pop group were on hand to fête the four-door at a teaser party.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — On the eve of the Detroit auto show, inside a onetime firehouse now festooned with the motorized light canons and digitally enhanced subwoofers of an Ibiza nightc