g supercar and a hydrogen-powered fuel cell car.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/mercedes-hedges-its-e-bets-with-hydrogen-and-batteries/', 'snippet': 'At the Detroit auto show, Mercedes-Benz showed an electric gullwing supercar and a hydrogen-powered fuel cell car.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — For Mercedes-Benz, the pair of cars it displayed here Monday represented healthy green fantasies in sickly yellow paint. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mercedes Hedges Its E-Bets With Hydrogen and Batteries', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Vehicles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydrogen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mercedes-Benz', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T22:03:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lawrence Ulrich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lawrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ulrich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d920ed88-4d1e-5b35-86e7-ce90795529f6', 'word_count': 495, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d920ed88-4d1e-5b35-86e7-ce90795529f6'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. White will keep Marea, Osteria Morini and Ai Fiori, while Mr. Cannon gets Alto and Convivio.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/making-it-official-michael-white-and-chris-cannon-split/', 'snippet': 'Mr. White will keep Marea, Osteria Morini and Ai Fiori, while Mr. Cannon gets Alto and Convivio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael White and Chris Cannon have officially dissolved their high-flying, high-end restaurant partnership, following two months of silence after blog items and unceasing speculation in the food world fueled the rumors of a breakup.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Making It Official, Michael White and Chris Cannon Split', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T22:05:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/01fdd68b-d923-58a2-a905-e405eb925702', 'word_count': 644, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01fdd68b-d923-58a2-a905-e405eb925702'}, {'abstract': 'Varied menus for sustaining a well-fed world later this century.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/varied-menus-for-sustaining-a-well-fed-world/', 'snippet': 'Varied menus for sustaining a well-fed world later this century.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On many issues relevant to charting a smooth human journey in this century, arguments are often framed between camps seeking to promote and spread “sustainable” behaviors and those pushing to advance and/or disseminate “better” technologies. In such polarized discussions, it’s hard to find acknowledgment that a variegated world heading toward roughly 9 billion people by 2050 will almost assuredly require “all of the above.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Varied Menus for Sustaining a Well-Fed World', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Population', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sustainable Living', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krugman, Paul', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ridley, Matt', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stone Barns Center for Food and Agriculture', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Iowa', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T22:06:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b3187a3-0e3d-5423-a8a3-45cf7a66e2eb', 'word_count': 2782, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b3187a3-0e3d-5423-a8a3-45cf7a66e2eb'}, {'abstract': 'The Fed’s $80 billion profit | Progress Energy lobbyist found dead | Carlyle, the corporate citizen | Madoff son’s will request nixed', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/buzz-tracker-6/', 'snippet': 'The Fed’s $80 billion profit | Progress Energy lobbyist found dead | Carlyle, the corporate citizen | Madoff son’s will request nixed', 'lead_paragraph': 'A sampling of what’s making the rounds in the deal-making ecosystem:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Buzz Tracker', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Madoff, Mark', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carlyle Group', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Duke Energy Corp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Progress Energy Incorporated', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T22:10:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d34fc26-6e09-580d-8c33-1bbd2ae3afec', 'word_count': 294, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d34fc26-6e09-580d-8c33-1bbd2ae3afec'}, {'abstract': 'Known as Kepler 10b, it is only 40 percent larger than the Earth and about 4.6 times as massive, making it about as dense as Mercury.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/science/space/11planet.html', 'snippet': 'Known as Kepler 10b, it is only 40 percent larger than the Earth and about 4.6 times as massive, making it about as dense as Mercury.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEATTLE — In a down payment on riches to come, scientists from NASA’s Kepler satellite announced Monday that they had discovered the smallest planet yet found outside our solar system and the first that was unquestionably rocky, like the Earth.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kepler Finds an Exoplanet, Rocky, Small and Very Hot', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kepler Finds an Exoplanet, Rocky, Small and Very Hot', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Aeronautics and Space Administration', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Planets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Astronomy and Astrophysics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T22:11:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Space & Cosmos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dennis Overbye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dennis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Overbye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66e570cd-053f-5d4b-b8a2-c094b83d7acc', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66e570cd-053f-5d4b-b8a2-c094b83d7acc'}, {'abstract': 'The Directors Guild finalists include David O. Russell and Tom Hooper, but the list spells not-so-great Oscar news for the Coen brothers, Danny Boyle and Lisa Cholodenko.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/10/directors-guild-offers-some-oscar-clues/', 'snippet': 'The Directors Guild finalists include David O. Russell and Tom Hooper, but the list spells not-so-great Oscar news for the Coen brothers, Danny Boyle and Lisa Cholodenko.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Conventional wisdom holds that the best picture Oscar nearly always goes to the best director winner. And Oscarologist wisdom holds that the Academy’s best director is nearly always the same as the Directors Guild of America’s choice; the two prizes haven’t matched up only six times in 62 years. So when the D.G.A. announced its nominees on Monday, the following people were probably doubly celebrating: Darren Aronofsky, for “Black Swan;” Tom Hooper, for “The King’s Speech;” Christopher Nolan for “Inception;” David Fincher for “The Social Network” and David O. Russell for “The Fighter.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Directors Guild Offers Some Oscar Clues', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARONOFSKY, DARREN', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boyle, Danny', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cholodenko, Lisa', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hackford, Taylor', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nolan, Christopher', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Russell, David O', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Directors Guild of America', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T22:13:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fc4738d-d06a-51f0-bd10-fb1f3a386b37', 'word_count': 456, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fc4738d-d06a-51f0-bd10-fb1f3a386b37'}, {'abstract': 'The new Comedy Central show “Onion SportsDome” takes a satirical approach to its note-for-note re-creation of ESPN’s “SportsCenter.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/arts/television/11onion.html', 'snippet': 'The new Comedy Central show “Onion SportsDome” takes a satirical approach to its note-for-note re-creation of ESPN’s “SportsCenter.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’re looking for an argument for the continued relevance of print, you could do worse than “Onion SportsDome,” the latest product of the Onion satire factory.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type':