eo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T22:51:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b25cbe6-49c0-599b-8ef4-6c03d9c66b34', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b25cbe6-49c0-599b-8ef4-6c03d9c66b34'}, {'abstract': 'A typical La Niña would bring dry conditions to the Southern United States. So how to explain a recent deluge in Southern California?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/science/earth/11nina.html', 'snippet': 'A typical La Niña would bring dry conditions to the Southern United States. So how to explain a recent deluge in Southern California?', 'lead_paragraph': ' For meteorologists and climate scientists, the flooding that has affected the Australian state of Queensland has not been unexpected. It is seen as collateral damage from the current La Niña, a lowering of sea-surface temperatures in the tropical eastern Pacific Ocean.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'California’s Heavy Rains Counter La Niña’s Ways', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California’s Heavy Rains Counter La Niña’s Ways', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-10T22:53:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Henry Fountain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Henry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fountain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13d9ab70-39a0-5b54-a63c-b310468b5984', 'word_count': 676, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13d9ab70-39a0-5b54-a63c-b310468b5984'}, {'abstract': '“Lights Out,” a new FX series, is a first-rate tale about a boxer’s comeback that includes sly, convoluted twists on family politics, class and New Jersey corruption.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/arts/television/11lights.html', 'snippet': '“Lights Out,” a new FX series, is a first-rate tale about a boxer’s comeback that includes sly, convoluted twists on family politics, class and New Jersey corruption.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the Cinderella myth is to be believed, women feel oppressed by class, but men seem most daunted by age. That’s why the two sexes cling to such different versions of that fairy tale. For women, it survives as a rescue fantasy — the prince who lifts a young woman up from lower-class obscurity. Men usually prefer a “Cinderella Man” story, the return of a broken-down has-been who battles ruin and ridicule to win back his honor — and fortune.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/11/arts/LIGHTS/LIGHTS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/11/arts/LIGHTS/LIGHTS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/11/arts/LIGHTS/LIGHTS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 753, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo