riginal': 'By Paul E. Kanjorski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Kanjorski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/02c7a01e-9f92-5c12-a38f-dffef96601ff', 'word_count': 671, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02c7a01e-9f92-5c12-a38f-dffef96601ff'}, {'abstract': 'A case against British environmental activists comes apart amid accusations of an agent provocateur.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/world/europe/11britain.html', 'snippet': 'A case against British environmental activists comes apart amid accusations of an agent provocateur.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON \x97 The unmasking of an undercover police officer who spent seven years infiltrating environmental protest groups in Britain and more than 20 other countries caused the collapse on Monday of a trial involving activists accused of planning to occupy and immobilize Britain’s largest coal-fired power station.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Charges Dropped Against British Protesters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Charges Dropped Against British Protesters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scotland Yard (Metropolitan Police Service)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T04:08:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By John F. Burns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9656cbe-3ce0-521a-b29c-f14e652cfc1d', 'word_count': 808, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9656cbe-3ce0-521a-b29c-f14e652cfc1d'}, {'abstract': 'Members of Congress are concerned about their safety. But they should be no less worried about the effect of their inaction on gun control on the safety of Americans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/opinion/11tue1.html', 'snippet': 'Members of Congress are concerned about their safety. But they should be no less worried about the effect of their inaction on gun control on the safety of Americans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Glock 19 is a semiautomatic pistol so reliable that it is used by thousands of law enforcement agencies around the world, including the New York Police Department, to protect the police and the public. On Saturday, in Tucson, it became an instrument of carnage for two preventable reasons: It had an oversize ammunition clip that was once restricted by federal law and still should be; and it was fired by a disturbed man who should never have been able to purchase it legally.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Assault on Everyone’s Safety', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Assault on Everyone&#8217;s Safety', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tucson Shooting (2011)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Rifle Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Loughner, Jared Lee', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCarthy, Carolyn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Giffords, Gabrielle', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tucson (Ariz)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Glock GmbH', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T04:11:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a1a1cc2-d3a0-595d-82a5-86267ac911be', 'word_count': 642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a1a1cc2-d3a0-595d-82a5-86267ac911be'}, {'abstract': 'Beijing would be wise to stop tailoring economic policy to satisfy its exporters and start thinking about the big picture, starting with its own people.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/opinion/11tue2.html', 'snippet': 'Beijing would be wise to stop tailoring economic policy to satisfy its exporters and start thinking about the big picture, starting with its own people.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While the Obama administration has been pressing China to stop artificially cheapening its currency, Chinese leaders still resist, insisting that that would damage exports and risk the country’s economic health and possibly its social order. Spiraling inflation \x97 exacerbated by the cheap currency policy \x97 could threaten both. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Not So Cheap for China', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Not So Cheap for China', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Yuan (Currency)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value':