ters Group PLC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Technologies Corp', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T13:52:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0739c6d-61ae-510f-b5cd-5cef136bf001', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0739c6d-61ae-510f-b5cd-5cef136bf001'}, {'abstract': 'Tom Hooper, the director of “The King’s Speech,” sought to subvert audience expectations, creating a London that was filthy, ugly and covered in smog.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/subverting-the-royal-film/', 'snippet': 'Tom Hooper, the director of “The King’s Speech,” sought to subvert audience expectations, creating a London that was filthy, ugly and covered in smog.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Everything felt very proper – chin up, crusts off — at the lunch for “The King’s Speech” on Monday, held at a private club in Manhattan that resembled an English manor house, complete with a bust of Churchill and an actual Churchill, Edwina Sandys,\xa0granddaughter of that British\xa0prime minister, who said she’d seen the movie three times. Tina Brown and Harry Evans were also there, along with Trudie Styler, Geoffrey Fletcher (the Oscar-winning screenwriter of “Precious”) and a sizable contingent of people who talked about literature, opera, and, one presumes, fox hunting or tea (or kinky sex). Also present, though less proper: Harvey Weinstein, who suggested a shot to the Bagger’s illustrious producer, Jeremy Beiler, whispering, “Close-up!” as our pal focused his camera. (Anyone who wondered whether Mr. Weinstein’s filmmakers get final cut might have an answer now.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Subverting the Royal Film', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T13:55:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/bda569ea-0251-5f79-9651-35ed47a8addc', 'word_count': 433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bda569ea-0251-5f79-9651-35ed47a8addc'}, {'abstract': 'Monday’s product debuts suggested an industry that rediscovered its footing in 2010 — if not its swagger.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/a-revitalized-detroit-auto-show/', 'snippet': 'Monday’s product debuts suggested an industry that rediscovered its footing in 2010 — if not its swagger.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Revitalized Detroit Auto Show', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'North American International Auto Show', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T13:56:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b33b6f6e-ebe7-578c-a635-ad256adf7dab', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b33b6f6e-ebe7-578c-a635-ad256adf7dab'}, {'abstract': 'This week, I am not going to discuss the error of using a singular verb in a relative clause like this: “He is one of the people who [make/makes] my life miserable.” But variations on that problem are still fair game. And here are several (courtesy of colleagues and a careful reader):', 'web_url': 'https://afterdeadline.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/disagreeable/', 'snippet': 'This week, I am not going to discuss the error of using a singular verb in a relative clause like this: “He is one of the people who [make/makes] my life miserable.” But variations on that problem are still fair game. And here are several (courtes...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Notes from the newsroom on grammar, usage and style. (Some frequently asked questions are here.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Disagreeable', 'kicker': 'After Deadline', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T13:57:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Topics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94e2187f-2102-5e8e-88be-dd8aaa22432e', 'word_count': 1033, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94e2187f-2102-5e8e-88be-dd8aaa22432e'}, {'abstract': 'This week, I am not going to discuss the error of using a singular verb in a relative clause like this: “He is one of the people who [make/makes] my life miserable.” But variations on that problem are still fair game. And here are several (courtesy of colleagues and a careful reader):', 'web_url': 'https://topics.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/disagreeable/', 'snippet': 'This week, I am not going to discuss the error of using a singular verb in a relative clause like this: “He is one of the people who [make/makes] my life miserable.” But variations on that problem are still fair game. And here are several (courtes...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Notes from the newsroom on grammar, usage and style. (Some frequently asked questions are