', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T13:59:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Dunlap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dunlap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac46a96c-addc-5ce8-8cb9-05b6e12f1470', 'word_count': 72, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac46a96c-addc-5ce8-8cb9-05b6e12f1470'}, {'abstract': 'There really is nothing new under the sun. On Thursday, “The Master of the Blue Jeans” opens at Didier Aaron in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/vintage-jeans/', 'snippet': 'There really is nothing new under the sun. On Thursday, “The Master of the Blue Jeans” opens at Didier Aaron in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There really is nothing new under the sun. On Thursday, “The Master of the Blue Jeans” opens at Didier Aaron in New York. Originally organized by Gerlinde Gruber, a curator at the Kunsthistorisches Museum in Vienna, for Galerie Canesso in Paris, the exhibition includes seven late-17th-century Italian paintings that show poor people dressed in denim fabric, which was made then in Genoa and known by the French name for that city, Gênes. The paintings, which had once been attributed to artists like\xa0Velázquez and de la Tour, are now thought to be the work of a single, unknown artist. The catalog even has a brief essay by that 20th-century master of stonewash, François Girbaud. For more information, go to didieraaron.com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vintage Jeans', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T14:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pilar Viladas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pilar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viladas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bbfb9b3-29ca-550b-a495-becc2eb77742', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bbfb9b3-29ca-550b-a495-becc2eb77742'}, {'abstract': 'Roger Ailes said Fox News would try to cool the heated rhetoric.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/ailes-to-fox-anchors-tone-it-down/', 'snippet': 'Roger Ailes said Fox News would try to cool the heated rhetoric.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Roger Ailes, the president of Fox News, on Monday embraced the idea of a more civil public discourse in the wake of the shootings in Arizona.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ailes Tells Fox Anchors to ‘Tone It Down’', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tucson Shooting (2011)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AILES, ROGER E', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox News Channel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T14:02:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9597e378-ce97-546e-bf83-988845f9f3b5', 'word_count': 214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9597e378-ce97-546e-bf83-988845f9f3b5'}, {'abstract': 'Can’t happen. So?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/more-thoughts-on-equality-of-opportunity/', 'snippet': 'Can’t happen. So?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lots of good comments on this post. But I think there’s a bit of an intellectual trap lurking here. As I pointed out, the typical conservative line about equality of opportunity, not results, really implies the need for a radical restructuring of our society, which doesn’t offer anything remotely resembling equal opportunity. At this point, however, there’s a tendency to think about what that restructuring would involve — and because it’s basically impossible, to throw up one’s hands.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Thoughts on Equality of Opportunity', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T14:15:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5e56eb4-f7bf-5007-9949-a5a91608c687', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5e56eb4-f7bf-5007-9949-a5a91608c687'}, {'abstract': 'The S.& P. 500-stock index has gained 88 percent since its March 2009 low.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/business/12markets.html', 'snippet': 'The S.& P. 500-stock index has gained 88 percent since its March 2009 low.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Standard & Poor’s 500-stock index on Tuesday rebounded from a three-day slump after Sears Holdings’ profit forecast and Lennar’s earnings beat estimates. Higher oil prices drove up energy shares.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wall Street Manages to Close Higher', 'kicker': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'S.&