icle/6ef0d190-9388-5d97-b110-51675afcb401'}, {'abstract': 'A manufacturer estimates that more than three times as much wind power capacity was installed in China last year than in the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/chinas-galloping-wind-market/', 'snippet': 'A manufacturer estimates that more than three times as much wind power capacity was installed in China last year than in the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A corporation that makes wind-machine components estimates that more than three times as much wind power capacity was installed in China last year than in the United States and that China now constitutes the world’s largest wind energy market.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China’s Galloping Wind Market', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Renewable Energy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recession and Depression', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wind Power', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Superconductor Corp', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T15:23:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f13c0a5e-51d6-5349-bb34-d61e8873679e', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f13c0a5e-51d6-5349-bb34-d61e8873679e'}, {'abstract': 'Hours after snow began to fall on New York on Dec. 26, the mayor’s private plane took off from his island retreat. But was he on it?', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/blizzard-mystery-solved-air-bloomberg-seen-in-bermuda/', 'snippet': 'Hours after snow began to fall on New York on Dec. 26, the mayor’s private plane took off from his island retreat. But was he on it?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 12:10 p.m. | On the day after Christmas, a request came into the Bermuda airport: a private plane needed to be pulled out of its hangar and readied for takeoff.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Blizzard Mystery Solved? Air Bloomberg Was Seen in Bermuda', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snow and Snowstorms', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bermuda', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T15:25:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Barbaro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barbaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0d6e1f6-aa5c-590b-960d-6aee4178512b', 'word_count': 619, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0d6e1f6-aa5c-590b-960d-6aee4178512b'}, {'abstract': 'An economist argues that proclamations of a “new normal” for the economy are almost comically frequent, and generally hooey.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/the-new-normal-is-actually-pretty-old/', 'snippet': 'An economist argues that proclamations of a “new normal” for the economy are almost comically frequent, and generally hooey.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Everything old is new again, including normality.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The ‘New Normal’ Is Actually Pretty Old', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T15:27:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/17609dc5-cb39-5a8f-a8c6-d9030dea892a', 'word_count': 457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17609dc5-cb39-5a8f-a8c6-d9030dea892a'}, {'abstract': 'A facialist’s facialist if ever there was one, Chin has figured out how to buff, squeeze and laser even the most recalcitrant skin into its best showing.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/vain-glorious-christine-chin-saves-face/', 'snippet': 'A facialist’s facialist if ever there was one, Chin has figured out how to buff, squeeze and laser even the most recalcitrant skin into its best showing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’ve ever wondered why some women have skin that glows, it may be time to invest in the services of Christine Chin. A facialist’s facialist if ever there was one, Chin has figured out how to buff, squeeze and laser even the most recalcitrant skin into its best showing. She’s not been called “Mean Christine” for no reason: her facials are rigorous rather than soothing and may include the meticulous (and time-consuming) lancing of what she affectionately refers to as “doughnuts” – those sebaceous bumps under the skin that give it a regrettably bumpy appearance but are rarely bothered with by more massage-happy hands. Chin’s knowledge and expertise don’t come cheap and her Orchard Street salon isn’t cozy or even spacious, but she’s worth the trek. And now there’s another reason to seek her out, even if you’ve never tried her services: Chin has come out with a new line — the Christine Chin Celebrity collection — that reinforces her beliefs. The result of four years of closely supervised work with a Swiss lab, the products include Nano Gold, a new type of gold particle, as well as active plant cells from Sequoia, Blue Lotus and Argan. They range in price from $48 for the superb cleanser and toner to $120 for the debris-busting gommage. For the complete list of available products for sale online, go to christinechin.com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vain Glorious | Christine Chin Saves Face', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T15:30:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daphne Merkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daphne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Merkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c4b6ca9-b1ff-5600-b422-711fe43e880a', 'word_count': 230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c4b6ca9-b1ff-5600-b422-711fe43e880a'}, {'abstract': 'Evidence against paying students for good grades, a look at chefs’ home kitchens and other consumer-focused items from today’s Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/tuesday-reading-paying-for-good-grades/', 'snippet': 'Evidence against paying students for good grades, a look at chefs’ home kitchens and other consumer-focused items from today’s Times.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tuesday Reading: Paying for Good Grades', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T15:30:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Saranow Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'Saranow', 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0e28bf4-0d77-59a9-9730-919d07f8c34a', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0e28bf4-0d77-59a9-9730-919d07f8c34a'}, {'abstract': 'There are rules.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/notes-to-commenters/', 'snippet': 'There are rules.', 'lead_paragraph': '1. Obscenity will get your comment deleted; I suspect that a fair number of commenters don’t even realize they’re doing it, because that’s the way many of us #$%^! talk these days. But think about it, and don’t waste your time or mine.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Notes to Commenters', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T15:37:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bda7678-7e40-536b-b52f-539b39d1b3ef', 'word_count': 92, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bda7678-7e40-536b-b52f-539b39d1b3ef'}, {'abstract': 'At an event in New York, Verizon Wireless is expected to announce a new addition to its smartphone lineup — Apple’s iPhone.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/live-blogging-the-verizon-iphone-announcement/', 'snippet': 'At an event in New York, Verizon Wireless is expected to announce a new addition to its smartphone lineup — Apple’s iPhone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At an event in New York, Verizon Wireless is announcing a new addition to its smartphone lineup — Apple’s iPhone. It will be the second carrier in the United States to offer the phone; AT&T has had the exclusive so far. Members of the news media have been invited to Jazz at Lincoln Center for the unveiling. Stay tuned for updates as Jenna Wortham and I live-blog the press conference and relay information about data plans, pricing and multitasking. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Live Blogging the Verizon iPhone Announcement', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Verizon Communications Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T15:46:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Grobart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grobart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed0da094-9752-5812-9754-4b1c86379dd9', 'word_count': 999, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed0da094-9752-5812-9754-4b1c86379dd9'}, {'abstract': 'Leilani Münter, a driver for Li-ion Motors, a Nevada-based builder of electric vehicles, addressed the press on Tuesday at the Detroit auto show.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/a-green-racecar-driver-with-a-clean-conscience/', 'snippet': 'Leilani Münter, a driver for Li-ion Motors, a Nevada-based builder of electric vehicles, addressed the press on Tuesday at the Detroit auto show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — Steve McQueen probably never lost sleep over his carbon footprint. Not so for racecar driver Leilani Münter, a spokes-driver for Li-ion Motors. But she said her her relationship with Li-ion, an X Prize-winning Nevada electric-car company, helps her offset racing emissions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Green Racecar Driver With a Clean Conscience', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'North American International Auto Show', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T15:54:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Moran', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moran', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/47b948f6-1a34-5cf2-9db4-9e2707e55bd0', 'word_count': 101, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47b948f6-1a34-5cf2-9db4-9e2707e55bd0'}, {'abstract': 'Searching for clues and trends heading into rematch weekend in the N.F.L. playoffs.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/circular-logic-and-the-jets-prospects-in-new-england/', 'snippet': 'Searching for clues and trends heading into rematch weekend in the N.F.L. playoffs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is payback weekend in the N.F.L. playoffs, with every game a rematch of at least one regular-season meeting. Both of the divisional-round pairings in the A.F.C. (Jets-Patriots and Ravens-Steelers) are just that — divisional games. In the N.F.C., the Seahawks will try to repeat their October win in Chicago, and the Packers will try to avenge a November loss in Atlanta.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Circular Logic and the Jets’ Prospects in New England', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Patriots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T16:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Das', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Das', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ecab3b7-a2a5-5f18-aab0-ac84312ac7e6', 'word_count': 294, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ecab3b7-a2a5-5f18-aab0-ac84312ac7e6'}, {'abstract': 'The country’s choice for an Oscar this year is “Carancho,” which like last year’s winner, “The Secret In Their Eyes,” is a thriller starring Ricardo Darín.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/a-sequel-for-argentinian-noir/', 'snippet': 'The country’s choice for an Oscar this year is “Carancho,” which like last year’s winner, “The Secret In Their Eyes,” is a thriller starring Ricardo Darín.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The surprise winner in last year’s Oscar competition for best-foreign-language film was\xa0“The Secret in Their Eyes,” an Argentine\xa0neo-noir thriller starring Ricardo Darín. The Argentine submission this year? “Carancho,” a neo-noir thriller starring Ricardo Darín.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Sequel for Argentine Noir?', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trapero, Pablo', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARGENTINA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T16:03:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b3ea406-5a23-5d21-be71-8e10f3ceb562', 'word_count': 876, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b3ea406-5a23-5d21-be71-8e10f3ceb562'}, {'abstract': 'The panel calls for a significant expansion of regulatory staff, a long review before drilling permits are granted and lifting the $75 million cap on corporate liability for damages from a spill.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/spill-commission-issues-recommendations/', 'snippet': 'The panel calls for a significant expansion of regulatory staff, a long review before drilling permits are granted and lifting the $75 million cap on corporate liability for damages from a spill.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A presidential commission has issued its final report on the causes of the Gulf of Mexico oil spill and laid out its recommendations. Here is John Broder’s article.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spill Commission Issues Recommendations', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Halliburton Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transocean Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gulf of Mexico', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T16:14:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0af0d270-3154-5c82-9e66-e0cb7cf631f7', 'word_count': 28, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0af0d270-3154-5c82-9e66-e0cb7cf631f7'}, {'abstract': 'Shooting on the new films is to start next month.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/mckellen-re-enlists-as-gandalf-for-hobbit-films/', 'snippet': 'Shooting on the new films is to start next month.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hobbit fans can breathe a sigh of relief. Ian McKellen has agreed to reprise his role as Gandalf in Peter Jackson’s two-picture adaptation of J.R.R. Tolkien’s novel “The Hobbit.” Mr. McKellen portrayed Gandalf, the tall (especially when compared with a Hobbit) wizard in the “Lord of the Rings” trilogy, and garnered an Oscar nomination for best supporting actor in the first of the three films, “The Fellowship of the Ring.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'McKellen Re-Enlists as Gandalf for ‘Hobbit’ Films', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Peter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McKellen, Ian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tolkien, J R R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Zealand', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T16:35:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b38c31d-3ebf-54ba-8656-b71ef7fa488e', 'word_count': 147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b38c31d-3ebf-54ba-8656-b71ef7fa488e'}, {'abstract': 'Is the iron hand of Chinese parenting the future for Americans raising their kids?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/magazine/16fob-wwln-t.html', 'snippet': 'Is the iron hand of Chinese parenting the future for Americans raising their kids?', 'lead_paragraph': ' There was bound to be some push back. All the years of nurturance overload simply got to be too much. The breast-feeding through toddlerhood, nonstop baby wearing, co-sleeping, “Baby Mozart” co-watching; the peer pressure for never-ending singsong-voiced Mommy niceness, the ever-maddening chant of “good job!”; compulsory school “involvement” (that is, teacher-delegated busywork packaged as a way to Show Your Child You Care), the rapt attendance at each and every school performance, presentation, sporting event — the whole mishmash of modern, attuned, connected, concerned, self-esteem-building parenting. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16fob-wwln-t_CA0/16fob-wwln-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 423, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16fob-wwln-t_CA0/16fob-wwln-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 423}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16fob-wwln-t_CA0/16fob-wwln-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 717, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': 