A “world without fossil fuels” is “simply not America’s energy reality,” an official says.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The top energy official at the United States Chamber of Commerce, the powerful lobbying group, urges lawmakers to focus on expanding fossil fuel energy production, not “high-cost energy sources” like wind and solar. “Having people really understand our energy reality, rather than energy as we would like it to be, is incredibly important, particularly at this juncture in terms of our economy,” the official, Karen Harbert, a former high-level official at the Department of Energy under President George W. Bush, says in an interview. “It may be lovely to think about a world without fossil fuels, but that simply is not America’s energy reality.” [The Hill]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: U.S. Chamber Backs Growth of Fossil Fuels', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Energy and Power', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BP Plc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Chamber of Commerce', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colombia', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T17:03:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Collins Rudolf', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'Collins', 'lastname': 'Rudolf', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5ed106e-62b1-5f0d-ac17-66e60fd31880', 'word_count': 214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5ed106e-62b1-5f0d-ac17-66e60fd31880'}, {'abstract': 'Brent Musburger’s line at the end of Auburn’s victory in the B.C.S. title game made viewers stop and say, ‘What?’ Then it went viral.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/sports/12leading.html', 'snippet': 'Brent Musburger’s line at the end of Auburn’s victory in the B.C.S. title game made viewers stop and say, ‘What?’ Then it went viral.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If college football’s national championship game gave you a queasy feeling, you were not alone. There were the obvious reasons, including the fact that the winning team’s quarterback was once shopped around by his father like a side of beef, drawing nothing more than an official N.C.A.A. shrug. Even those who chose to ignore the ethical dilemmas inherent in Auburn’s appearance and patiently waited through the epoch between the end of the regular season and the mythical title game were rewarded not with the promised offensive bonanza but instead with a rather grinding game with one signature play. They then endured ESPN’s Brent Musburger proclaiming, “This is for all the Tostitos.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/sports/12leading/12leading-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/12/sports/12leading/12leading-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/sports/12leading/12leading-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': Non