e of the best neologisms to bubble up during the Internet era is “moasting,” a verb that combines the words moaning and boasting. Everyone moasts, sometimes, but media creatures on Twitter have perfected the form: “Racing from Washington for a ‘Charlie Rose’ taping. What’s happened to the quiet car on the Acela?” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/BOOK/BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/BOOK/BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/BOOK/BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 462, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Throwing Mud and Calling It Beautiful', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Throwing Mud and Calling It Beautiful', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Levy, Bernard-Henri', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Houellebecq, Michel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T22:05:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dwight Garner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dwight', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5bfe84dc-02c6-5692-a2da-5e1e3e41b0c3', 'word_count': 1141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5bfe84dc-02c6-5692-a2da-5e1e3e41b0c3'}, {'abstract': 'Two veterans of “Rent” revisit that show but also perform some of their original material.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/arts/music/12town.html', 'snippet': 'Two veterans of “Rent” revisit that show but also perform some of their original material.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Adam Pascal and Anthony Rapp, original cast members of “Rent,” joined voices to sing its anthem “What You Own” on Monday evening at Town Hall, the only thing that had dated about the song was its actual date. “You’re living in America at the end of the millennium,” goes this proclamation of survival in the age of AIDS. “You’re living in America, where it’s like the Twilight Zone.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/TOWN/TOWN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 374, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/TOWN/TOWN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 374}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/TOWN/TOWN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 713, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/TOWN/TOWN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_n