Helft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Miguel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Helft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/93eb1975-055b-5ea4-a0fe-566bd7e38270', 'word_count': 1150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93eb1975-055b-5ea4-a0fe-566bd7e38270'}, {'abstract': 'Comparatively few politicians attended this year’s Detroit auto show. On Tuesday, Michigan’s new governor addressed the press.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/for-gov-rick-snyder-little-competition-for-detroit-spotlight/', 'snippet': 'Comparatively few politicians attended this year’s Detroit auto show. On Tuesday, Michigan’s new governor addressed the press.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — With all eyes on the bleak state of the Michigan economy, the state’s newly elected governor, Rick Snyder, addressed the press here on Tuesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Gov. Rick Snyder, Little Competition for Detroit Spotlight', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Snyder, Rick', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'North American International Auto Show', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Corker, Robert Phillips Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pelosi, Nancy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T22:19:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tamara Warren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tamara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Warren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3867b08b-9845-5a3d-84c9-39990a40a509', 'word_count': 243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3867b08b-9845-5a3d-84c9-39990a40a509'}, {'abstract': 'The country duo Steel Magnolia celebrated their debut album at the Bowery Ballroom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/arts/music/12steel.html', 'snippet': 'The country duo Steel Magnolia celebrated their debut album at the Bowery Ballroom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You can absolutely judge a band by its covers, a cursory examination of the classics played on Monday night by the young country duo Steel Magnolia showed. There was “After the Fire Is Gone,” the Loretta Lynn-Conway Twitty lament; the feisty Stevie Nicks scold “Stop Draggin’ My Heart Around”; and “Jackson,” popularized by Johnny and June Carter Cash, which masks deep resentment beneath pep and winks.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/STEEL/STEEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/STEEL/STEEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/STEEL/STEEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Songs Say One Thing, Actions Another', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Songs Say One Thing, Actions Another', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bowery Ballroom', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T22:20:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/300ee02a-2b44-5a13-ba10-0c080e596db5', 'word_count': 637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/300ee02a-2b44-5a13-ba10-0c080e596db5'}, {'abstract': 'The special treatment of derivatives in bankruptcy can interfere with the use of Chapter 11 to resolve cases involving nonbank financial institutions.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/derivative-safe-harbors-vs-chapter-11/', 'snippet': 'The special treatment of derivatives in bankruptcy can interfere with the use of Chapter 11 to resolve cases involving nonbank financial institutions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I have noted before on DealBook, derivative contracts have a broad exception to the normal rules of bankruptcy, often called a safe harbor. Derivatives are not subject to the automatic stay, collateral collected on the eve of bankruptcy cannot be attacked as a preference, and derivative contracts are not subject to a debtor’s ability to assume or reject contracts unless the nondebtor counterparty consents.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Safe Harbors for Derivatives vs. Chapter 11', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Derivatives (Financial Instruments)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Deposit Insurance Corp', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Skadden Arps Slate Meagher & Flom', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T22:22:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen J. Lubben', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Lubben', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/64831504-df87-5a89-92c9-55081bfcfd8f', 'word_count': 696, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64831504-df87-5a89-92c9-55081bfcfd8f'}, {'abstract': 'The award for best diss at the New York Film Critics Circle went to Darren Aronofsky this year, with Michelle Williams as the lovely runner-up.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/a-ceremony-for-the-cantankerous/', 'snippet': 'The award for best diss at the New York Film Critics Circle went to Darren Aronofsky this year, with Michelle Williams as the lovely runner-up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As awards ceremonies go, the New York Film Critics Circle is unique, in that – amid the thanking of producers and Columbia film professors, a sweet touch – there is always a bit of tension between movie people and the folks that judge them. Last year, even that master of off-the-cuff charm George Clooney got in a few jabs at Rex Reed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Ceremony for the Cantankerous', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARONOFSKY, DARREN', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'White, Armond', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'WILLIAMS, MICHELLE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Film Critics Circle', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T22:26:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena R