 Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/3c480bfb-f9fc-5d98-a6be-5cd06c109f8c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/3c480bfb-f9fc-5d98-a6be-5cd06c109f8c'}, {'abstract': 'The goal of men’s “replacement shopping” is to find the perfect chinos, briefs and socks and buy them again and again. Why not add white dress shirts?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/fashion/13TRADE.html', 'snippet': 'The goal of men’s “replacement shopping” is to find the perfect chinos, briefs and socks and buy them again and again. Why not add white dress shirts?', 'lead_paragraph': 'ZEROING in on the most basic materiel in the men’s wear arsenal \x97 white T-shirts, blue jeans, khakis \x97 has made hip and happy campers out of clothing makers and their customers alike. It has done wonders for big companies like Calvin Klein, Ralph Lauren and American Apparel and small players like Jean Shop and Save Khaki, all of which have created items that have, in turn, created repeat customers. In the little-discussed realm of retail known as “replenishment” or “replacement shopping,” the goal is to perfect basic garments expressly so that the time-crunched or shopaphobic guy can whiz in and out of a store as if on a jet-fueled sortie, halting only briefly behind enemy lines to grab three/six/nine of his tried-and-true chinos/briefs/socks. In, out, nobody gets hurt. ', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/11/fashion/20110111-TradingUp-slide-GB0G/20110111-TradingUp-slide-GB0G-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1022, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/11/fashion/20110111-TradingU