ating hard cheeses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'RUSSELVILLE, Ark.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12united-span/12united-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 292, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12united-span/12united-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 292}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12united-span/12united-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 513, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12united-span/12united-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12united-span/12united-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How the Microplane Grater Escaped the Garage', 'kicker': 'United Tastes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Grater That Escaped the Garage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T23:43:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By JOHN T. EDGE', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'T.', 'lastname': 'EDGE', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13811af3-b330-5bef-ad44-1680ba007fcf', 'word_count': 1267, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13811af3-b330-5bef-ad44-1680ba007fcf'}, {'abstract': '“Reservoir” by Eric Henry Sanders, brings “Woyzeck,” that classic of a soldier gone mad, up to date.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/theater/reviews/12reservoir.html', 'snippet': '“Reservoir” by Eric Henry Sanders, brings “Woyzeck,” that classic of a soldier gone mad, up to date.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Woyzeck,” Georg Büchner’s 1837 play about a soldier driven to madness and murder by poverty, exploitation and jealousy, is sometimes considered the first modern tragedy and is a primary source of absurdist theater. In recent years the title character has become shorthand for society’s brutalized, alienated souls, with some depictions even playful. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/RESERVOIR/RESERVOIR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 363, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/RESERVOIR/RESERVOIR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 363}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/RESERVOIR/RESERVOIR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 637, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Soldier Returns to Pressures at Home and Chaos Within', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Reservoir'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Soldier Returns to Pressures at Home and Chaos Within', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Drilling Co', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buchner, Georg', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T23:43:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel M. Gold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/16aa080b-0c9e-5641-8142-1169188fa67d', 'word_count': 393, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16aa080b-0c9e-5641-8142-1169188fa67d'}, {'abstract': 'Cut Brooklyn, a workshop in Gowanus, sells hand-forged, limited-edition knives and butcher blocks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/dining/12knives.html', 'snippet': 'Cut Brooklyn, a workshop in Gowanus, sells hand-forged, limited-edition knives and butcher blocks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hand-forged knives made by Joel Bukiewicz, left, are all beautifully finished, limited-edition pieces that have been sold online. Mr. Bukiewicz has now opened his pleasant workshop to visitors who can buy or order the knives, each of which comes with a lifetime guarantee for sharpening and conditioning. Buyers will learn the best way to maintain their knives, which are usually made of carbon steel. Unlike stainless steel, carbon steel can stain and rust, but the upside is that it holds a better, sharper edge than stainless.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12stuff-knives/12stuff-knives-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 365, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12stuff-knives/12stuff-knives-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 365}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12stuff-knives/STUFF3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12stuff-knives/12stuff-knives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12stuff-knives/12stuff-knives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Knives and Butcher Blocks, Together Again', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Knives and Butcher Blocks, Together Again', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Knives', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T23:43:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e783deb3-7538-5582-9ea5-a7b5b5d0d567', 'word_count': 225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e783deb3-7538-5582-9ea5-a7b5b5d0d567'}, {'abstract': 'Brazilian street food like acarajé and the national dish, feijoada, can be found at this two-year-old restaurant in Astoria.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/dining/reviews/12under.html', 'snippet': 'Brazilian street food like acarajé and the national dish, feijoada, can be found at this two-year-old restaurant in Astoria.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THERE’S nothing dainty about an acarajé. Peeled and mashed black-eyed peas are fried in palm oil, after which the fritter (pronounced ah-cah-rah-JEH; $5) is sliced and stuffed. Overstuffed, inevitably, with vatapá, a creamy paste flavored with seafood and nuts; caruru de quiabo, an onion-and-ginger-laced okra gumbo; and bacalao salad. It’s a taste of Brazilian summer, down to the last licked finger.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12under-span/12under-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12under-span/12under-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12under-span/UNDER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12under-span/12under-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12under-span/12under-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Point Brazil', 'kicker': '$25 and Under', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brazilian Summer In Snowy Queens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Astoria (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T23:44:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'subsection_name': 'Dining & Wine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Cook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cook', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2d58133-55c3-5b62-b2ca-ba766611381e', 'word_count': 681, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2d58133-55c3-5b62-b2ca-ba7666113