36c', 'word_count': 262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0525134c-3610-5eeb-820c-15305e3f536c'}, {'abstract': 'Cabbage is often an unloved, homely vegetable. But those who can get past their initial aversion know it as one of winter’s quiet overachievers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/dining/12vege.html', 'snippet': 'Cabbage is often an unloved, homely vegetable. But those who can get past their initial aversion know it as one of winter’s quiet overachievers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHEFS praise cabbage. They embrace its sweetness. They delight in its crunch in raw slaws and its melting smoothness in cold-weather braises.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12vegie-span/12vegie-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12vegie-span/12vegie-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12vegie-span/SUB-VEGIE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12vegie-span/12vegie-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/12/dining/12vegie-span/12vegie-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cabbage’s Sweet Side', 'kicker': 'The Temporary Vegetarian', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cabbage’s Sweet Side', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cabbage', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T23:44:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Louie', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Louie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f0acf28-8ba4-527d-b755-2a455ef0ef1e', 'word_count': 633, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f0acf28-8ba4-527d-b755-2a455ef0ef1e'}, {'abstract': 'If you’re looking for parity, look to the baseball playoffs.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/for-playoff-parity-take-m-l-b-over-n-f-l/', 'snippet': 'If you’re looking for parity, look to the baseball playoffs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I love the N.F.L., and especially the playoffs. Every game is an elimination game, and every game starts at a reasonable hour on the East Coast. Of the 11 N.F.L. postseason games every year, only two begin after 8 p.m. Baseball playoff games tend to start later, and of the last 43 postseason series, stretching back to the 2004 World Series, only five have produced a winner-take-all game.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Playoff Parity, Take M.L.B. over N.F.L.', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T23:46:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Kepner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kepner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/413acd09-12a1-5858-943f-f799345459ed', 'word_count': 367, 'uri': 'nyt://article/413acd09-12a1-5858-943f-f799345459ed'}, {'abstract': 'The president is adopting a model for his message much like the one that President Bill Clinton delivered after the Oklahoma City bombings.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/11/obama-speech-to-focus-on-serving-country/', 'snippet': 'The president is adopting a model for his message much like the one that President Bill Clinton delivered after the Oklahoma City bombings.', 'lead_paragraph': ' President Obama will focus his speech at a memorial service in Tucson on Wednesday evening on the victims of the attack and on the idea of service to the country, avoiding any overt commentary on the debate over violence and the nation’s political culture.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Speech to Focus on Serving Country', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T23:50:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helene Cooper and Jeff Zeleny', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zeleny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1150e02-12df-5a6d-a789-99a0cd91a50a', 'word_count': 679, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1150e02-12df-5a6d-a789-99a0cd91a50a'}, {'abstract': 'Mark A. Landis is one of the most prolific forgers American museums have encountered in years. But he does not seem to be in it for the money.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/arts/design/12fraud.html', 'snippet': 'Mark A. Landis is one of the most prolific forgers American museums have encountered in years. But he does not seem to be in it for the money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'His real name is Mark A. Landis, and he is a lifelong painter and former gallery owner. But when he paid a visit to the Paul and Lulu Hilliard University Art Museum in Lafayette, La., last September, he seemed more like a character sprung from a Southern Gothic novel. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/FRAUD1/FRAUD1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 344, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/FRAUD1/FRAUD1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 344}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/FRAUD1/FRAUD1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/FRAUD1/FRAUD1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/FRAUD1/FRAUD1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Elusive Forger, Giving but Never Stealing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Elusive Forger, Giving but Never Stealing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forgery', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-11T23:58:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstn