desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Zeller Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zeller', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d693ac4-a6f0-5c92-9f92-1c7fbd67025a', 'word_count': 862, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d693ac4-a6f0-5c92-9f92-1c7fbd67025a'}, {'abstract': 'The Somali police detained sailors on board a boat carrying charcoal on Tuesday in the capital, Mogadishu, a week after their boat was seized by the Somali Navy, officials said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/world/africa/12somaliabrf.html', 'snippet': 'The Somali police detained sailors on board a boat carrying charcoal on Tuesday in the capital, Mogadishu, a week after their boat was seized by the Somali Navy, officials said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Somali police detained sailors on board a boat carrying charcoal on Tuesday in the capital, Mogadishu, a week after their boat was seized by the Somali Navy, officials said. The boat, which was leased by Somali businessmen and had at least 14 sailors on board, was carrying charcoal to the United Arab Emirates when the navy attacked off the coast of Somalia early this month. The boat had previously been in Merca port, in an area controlled by the Shabab, an Islamist group that has been waging relentless attacks in an effort to overthrow the government. The export of charcoal has been a flourishing business in southern Somalia, but the government banned it last year, citing environmental preservation. Officials said the seizure of the boat was a sign that the navy was becoming more capable, at a time when piracy in Somali waters has been a substantial problem.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Somalia: Sailors on Seized Boat Are Detained', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Africa', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Somalia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Al-Shabab', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T05:15:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mohamed Ibrahim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mohamed', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ibrahim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7dd6c5d5-8e0f-5256-8d80-9d9cea98e9db', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7dd6c5d5-8e0f-5256-8d80-9d9cea98e9db'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Gross, known for his fluid style and easy erudition, was the editor of The Times Literary Supplement in London and a book critic for The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/arts/12gross.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Gross, known for his fluid style and easy erudition, was the editor of The Times Literary Supplement in London and a book critic for The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Gross, the editor of The Times Literary Supplement in London in the 1970s and a book critic for The New York Times in the 1980s who was known for his fluid style and easy erudition, died on Monday in London. He was 75.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/GROSS-obit/GROSS-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 709, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/GROSS-obit/GROSS-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnai