dia', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'None None', 'person': [{'firstname': 'None', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'None', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/b384c0c6-227b-57b9-9281-e8f693618192', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/b384c0c6-227b-57b9-9281-e8f693618192'}, {'abstract': 'Despite all the calls for more sane political dialogue, we’re getting the same crazy rhetoric.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/does-moderation-work/', 'snippet': 'Despite all the calls for more sane political dialogue, we’re getting the same crazy rhetoric.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gail Collins: David, we can’t not talk about the shootings in Tucson. I know you’ve been wanting a more moderate and sane political dialogue. But I am sorry to admit that one of my thoughts is that the aftermath of Tucson proves how moderation doesn’t work.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Does Moderation Work?', 'kicker': 'The Conversation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tucson Shooting (2011)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beck, Glenn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Giffords, Gabrielle', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Limbaugh, Rush', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tucson (Ariz)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T17:55:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks and Gail Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Gail', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/43f864c0-4b03-5217-8368-47efc77200b5', 'word_count': 1160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43f864c0-4b03-5217-8368-47efc77200b5'}, {'abstract': 'A new video process promises to drastically improve the picture quality of Blu-ray technology.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/better-than-blu-ray/', 'snippet': 'A new video process promises to drastically improve the picture quality of Blu-ray technology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To get the best picture quality from your 1080p-capable HDTV, you need to watch content emanating from a Blu-ray disc; only Blu-ray can deliver the 1080p resolution that your TV can potentially display.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Better Than Blu-ray?', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Digital and High-Definition Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T17:56:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric A. Taub', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Taub', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6aff1c1-9a8c-5a26-9360-515fe4bb48a2', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6aff1c1-9a8c-5a26-9360-515fe4bb48a2'}, {'abstract': 'A Marvel Comics editor creates the ideal character to foil the evil forces that leave mounds of snow in their wake.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/tights-cape-and-shovel-todays-perfect-superhero/', 'snippet': 'A Marvel Comics editor creates the ideal character to foil the evil forces that leave mounds of snow in their wake.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Right around sunrise, Nick Lowe, 31, a senior editor of X-Men comic books at Marvel Comics was shoveling the sidewalk in front of the two-story house his apartment is in on 29th Street in Long Island City, Queens.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tights, Cape and Shovel. Today’s Perfect Superhero.', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snow and Snowstorms', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lowe, Nick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T17:57:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca White', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'White', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/872a4163-4b2f-55e1-b241-f33a16d09c9c', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/872a4163-4b2f-55e1-b241-f33a16d09c9c'}, {'abstract': 'First Quality Music, based in Louisville, Ky., wrapped up 2010 with an $80,000 loss, and experts said the company needed a radical overhaul.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/business/smallbusinessemail/13sbiz.html', 'snippet': 'First Quality Music, based in Louisville, Ky., wrapped up 2010 with an $80,000 loss, and experts said the company needed a radical overhaul.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FIRST QUALITY MUSIC is a family-owned supplier of stringed musical instruments, including guitars and mandolins, and parts and accessories. Founded in Louisville, Ky., in 1970, the company also makes banjos and game calls for hunters. The nine-employee operation includes a retail showroom, a small