unt': 237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a46f133-d4af-5499-a151-041231ccd6cb'}, {'abstract': 'Billboard.com said Ms. Swift had the lowest sales figure — 52,000 copies — for a No. 1 album since Nielsen SoundScan began keeping track in 1991.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/despite-soft-sales-taylor-swift-keeps-top-spot/', 'snippet': 'Billboard.com said Ms. Swift had the lowest sales figure — 52,000 copies — for a No. 1 album since Nielsen SoundScan began keeping track in 1991.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Taylor Swift’s “Speak Now” is at No. 1 for the sixth week on the Billboard album chart this week. But the weekly sales figures have earned it what Billboard.com calls the “dubious honor” of having the lowest sales — 52,000 copies — for a No. 1 album since Nielsen SoundScan began keeping track in 1991. The previous low was set by the “Dreamgirls” soundtrack on the Jan., 27, 2007, chart, with slightly more than 60,000 albums sold. Back to 2011: Nicki Minaj’s “Pink Friday” climbed to No. 2 with 39,000, and Bruno Mars moved up a spot to No. 3 with “Doo-Wops & Hooligans,” which sold 38,000. Daft Punk’s “Tron: Legacy” soundtrack is fourth with sales of 34,000, and Eminem’s “Recovery” slipped three notches to No. 5, with sales of 33,000.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Despite Soft Sales, Taylor Swift Keeps Top Spot', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Swift, Taylor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T19:23:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/daf995e0-9549-5023-a0a1-a7ef6465a16e', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/daf995e0-9549-5023-a0a1-a7ef6465a16e'}, {'abstract': 'Accel’s Jim Breyer is turning his attention to New York, where he sees significant opportunity in social media, e-commerce and entertainment start-ups.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/an-early-facebook-investor-is-bullish-on-new-york/', 'snippet': 'Accel’s Jim Breyer is turning his attention to New York, where he sees significant opportunity in social media, e-commerce and entertainment start-ups.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Long before Goldman Sachs sunk hundreds of millions of dollars into Facebook at a soaring $50 billion valuation, an Accel partner, Jim Breyer, made a controversial bet on a Harvard dropout.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Early Facebook Investor Is Bullish on New York', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venture Capital', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T19:24:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0067d7fd-fd83-5e0e-9c4f-3608c9d4767e', 'word_count': 1235, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0067d7fd-fd83-5e0e-9c4f-3608c9d4767e'}, {'abstract': 'A $99 gadget offer remote access between two computers.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/remote-access-made-easy/', 'snippet': 'A $99 gadget offer remote access between two computers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Simple and wireless: we love encountering those two words in product description.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Remote Access Made Easy', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T19:30:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7242698b-f9c8-5c73-946b-862f313d6004', 'word_count': 193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7242698b-f9c8-5c73-946b-862f313d6004'}, {'abstract': 'Increasingly, research is focusing not on how much exercise people get — but on how much of their time is spent in sedentary activity, and the harm that it does.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/the-hazards-of-the-couch/', 'snippet': 'Increasingly, research is focusing not on how much exercise people get — but on how much of their time is spent in sedentary activity, and the harm that it does.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many of us sit in front of a computer for eight hours a day, and then go home and head for the couch to surf the Web or watch television, exchanging one seat and screen for another. Even if we try to squeeze in an hour at the gym, is it enough to counteract all that motionless sitting?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hazards of the Couch', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T19:43:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roni Caryn Rabin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roni', 'middlename': 'Caryn', 'lastname': 'Rabin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba8bcc6c-e258-5217-8bac-90da50e84c70', 'word_count': 776, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba8bcc6c-e258-5217-8bac-90da50e84c70'}, {'abstract': 'The 41-story former Deutsche Bank building is now the former 41-story Deutsche Bank building. Within a month, it should be cleared away completely.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/ten-years-after-911-the-wounded-deutsche-bank-tower-vanishes/', 'snippet': 'The 41-story former Deutsche Bank building is now the former 41-story Deutsche Bank building. Within a month, it should be cleared away completely.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 41-story former Deutsche Bank building opposite the World Trade Center site is now the one-story former Deutsche Bank building.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '10 Years After 9/11, Deutsche Bank Tower Vanishes', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Buildings (Structures)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deutsche Bank Building (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Trade Center (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schick, Avi', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lower Manhattan Development Corp', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T19:44:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Dunlap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dunlap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e762a361-7212-5d63-bc8b-2b5e97ebed37', 'word_count': 756, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e762a361-7212-5d63-bc8b-2b5e97ebed37'}, {'abstract': 'When a guest has to cancel, Edward Schneider quickly reconsiders dinner, and uses the ingredients for meatballs to make a ragù for future use.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/the-flexibility-of-meatball-ingredients/', 'snippet': 'When a guest has to cancel, Edward Schneider quickly reconsiders dinner, and uses the ingredients for meatballs to make a ragù for future use.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My wife, Jackie, and I were grinding beef, veal, pork and prosciutto to make meatballs, enjoying the background aroma of finely chopped vegetables sweating in olive oil. There were a number of routes we could take at this point. Meatballs as planned, or meatloaf, or any of various varieties of ragù: all could be made from the same blend of meats and the same aromatic mixture of onions, carrots, celery and garlic, with sage and parsley.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Flexibility of Meatball Ingredients', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T19:47:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Schneider', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schneider', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d134dee-869b-5f91-86d1-8ea868f557c2', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d134dee-869b-5f91-86d1-8ea868f557c2'}, {'abstract': 'A familiar fallacy, although short of zombiehood.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/the-doctrine-of-immaculate-transfer/', 'snippet': 'A familiar fallacy, although short of zombiehood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dave Altig at the Atlanta Fed weighs in on Martin Feldstein’s much-quoted paper arguing that the United States and China will soon reduce or eliminate their current account imbalances. I think it’s worth saying a bit more about this, because there’s a common fallacy here — not one Feldstein has fallen into, but which many others do. And talking about that fallacy is also a way to see why current yuan policy is a problem for the world.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Doctrine of Immaculate Transfer', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T19:55:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f40074f8-634a-5118-becc-a63c5edcc1c4', 'word_count': 557, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f40074f8-634a-5118-becc-a63c5edcc1c4'}, {'abstract': 'The union president, Bob King, said carmakers would be better off financially and morally if they allowed their American workers to unionize.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/business/global/13uaw.html', 'snippet': 'The union president, Bob King, said carmakers would be better off financially and morally if they allowed their American workers to unionize.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT \x97 The United Automobile Workers union this year is renewing its effort to organize workers at plants that foreign automakers operate in the United States and will portray companies that resist as “human rights violators,” the union’s president said Wednesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.A.W. to Renew Organizing Efforts at Foreign-Owned Plants', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.A.W. to Renew Organizing Efforts at Foreign-Owned Plants', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Investments', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Automobile Workers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T19:59:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bunkley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bunkley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b73c6af-19b8-5342-a7eb-f1b6aff021b9', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b73c6af-19b8-5342-a7eb-f1b6aff021b9'}, {'abstract': 'Youths in China who spend all of their monthly salary.', 'web_url': 'https://schott.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/moonlight-clan/', 'snippet': 'Youths in China who spend all of their monthly salary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Youths in China who spend all of their monthly salary.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Moonlight Clan', 'kicker': 'Schott&#039;s Vocab', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T20:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Schott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d9a2db6-a76e-5db0-8dff-86f186a2ff1a', 'word_count': 271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d9a2db6-a76e-5db0-8dff-86f186a2ff1a'}, {'abstract': 'Standouts included cocoon coats, jersey dresses with subtle sunburst pleats and a new brocade adapted from a pattern in a very old book of wallpaper samples.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/just-one-look-rochas-pre-fall-collection/', 'snippet': 'Standouts included cocoon coats, jersey dresses with subtle sunburst pleats and a new brocade adapted from a pattern in a very old book of wallpaper samples.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Pre-Fall Collections, being shown in New York over the next several weeks, arrive in stores in May to fill the gap between bikinis and furs.Trunk shows for Rochas regularly bring in 25-year-old princesses as well as ladies of a certain age. And it was easy to see how Marco Zanini’s ’30s-inspired pre-fall collection could charm both ends of the spectrum. Standouts included cocoon coats, jersey dresses with subtle sunburst pleats and a new brocade adapted from a pattern in a very old book of wallpaper samples. “I love to add unusual touches to the very French allure of Rochas,” Zanini said. “This time, why not play a little with Bloomsbury romance?” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Rochas Pre-Fall Collection', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T20:02:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alix Browne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a90b818-0bbb-5e5b-ae96-0bc733fad903', 'word_count': 118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a90b818-0bbb-5e5b-ae96-0bc733fad903'}, {'abstract': 'Washington Coach Lorenzo Romar acknowledged one of his players is under investigation after a sexual assault allegation, but gave no specifics.', 'web_url': 'https://thequad.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/washington-basketball-player-accused-of-sexual-assault/', 'snippet': 'Washington Coach Lorenzo Romar acknowledged one of his players is under investigation after a sexual assault allegation, but gave no specifics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lorenzo Romar, coach of the Washington men’s basketball team, acknowledged Tuesday that one of his players was being investigated by the police in Seattle after an accusation of sexual assault by a 16-year-old girl. The police have not identified the player and have not filed charges. Romar addressed the investigation with reporters at his regular weekly news conference, but said he could not provide any specifics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Washington Basketball Player Accused of Sexual Assault', 'kicker': 'The Quad', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romar, Lorenzo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Seattle (Wash)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T20:08:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/451c46c1-6b24-5c86-bdc6-ab3140665a67', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/451c46c1-6b24-5c86-bdc6-ab3140665a67'}, {'abstract': 'From Miami to Jaipur, new hotels hope to be noticed for distinctive touches.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/travel/16hotels.html', 'snippet': 'From Miami to Jaipur, new hotels hope to be noticed for distinctive touches.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE global economic forecast may be dim, but travelers can look forward to distinctiveness among the class of 2011 hotel openings. In Hong Kong, the city’s tallest skyscraper will become home to a hotel. In India, a resort made of box-shaped tents will take shape. And in New York, a Latin import will have its debut. These and three other newcomers aim to inspire travel strictly for the in-house amenities, activities and design.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/travel/16hotels-span/16hotels-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/16/travel/16hotels-span/16hotels-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/travel/16hotels-span/Hotels-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 646, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/travel/16hotels-span/16hotels-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/travel/16hotels-span/16hotels-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '8 Hotels to Look Out for in 2011', 'kicker': 'Openings', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Miami to Jaipur: New Rooms That Stand Out', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Marrakesh (Morocco)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jaipur (India)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HOTELS AND MOTELS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Miami (Fla)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Miguel de Allende (Mexico)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T20:09:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Glusac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/638333ed-3f51-5b2a-9b34-7b57752f8ce7', 'word_count': 1034, 'uri': 'nyt://article/638333ed-3f51-5b2a-9b34-7b57752f8ce7'}, {'abstract': 'When designer labels showed variations of long underwear on their fall runways, it seemed more like a styling affectation than a real trend with commercial legs. And yet retailers are giving some credence to a potential revival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/fashion/13ROW.html', 'snippet': 'When designer labels showed variations of long underwear on their fall runways, it seemed more like a styling affectation than a real trend with commercial legs. And yet retailers are giving some credence to a potential revival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT was one thing, while picking through the detritus of fall fashion for end-of-season bargains, to discover that Club Monaco has been trying to convince men that the latest things to be wearing are long johns. There were waffle-knit thermals, thin-ribbed sweats and, displayed boldly on one mannequin, a very expensive cashmere “onesie” in a navy-and-white Fair Isle knit.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/fashion/13ROW-span/13ROW-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 328, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/13/fashion/13ROW-span/13ROW-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 328}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/fashion/13ROW-span/Z-ROW-A-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 328, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/fashion/13ROW-span/Z-ROW-A-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/13/fashion/13ROW-span/Z-ROW-A-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Cold Is It? Enough to Make Long Johns Stylish', 'kicker': 'Front Row', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Cold Is It? Enough to Make Long Johns Stylish', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lingerie and Underwear', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T20:10:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6235ff40-1d4a-551b-908c-b7f9f6270815', 'word_count': 528, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6235ff40-1d4a-551b-908c-b7f9f6270815'}, {'abstract': 'File-sharing sites like Dropbox and Box have iPad apps that allow videos to be uploaded and then played with one tap on the screen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/technology/personaltech/13askk.html', 'snippet': 'File-sharing sites like Dropbox and Box have iPad apps that allow videos to be uploaded and then played with one tap on the screen.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Streaming Video to an iPad', 'kicker': 'Q&A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Streaming Video to an iPad', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Telephones and Telecommunications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPad', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recordings and Downloads (Video)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cable Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T20:13:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. D. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_