graph': 'Last year, Google tried to buy Yelp, the local business rating and review site. Since then, it has focused on building its own Yelp competitor with Google Places and Hotpot.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Google Takes On Yelp With New Mobile App', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T21:37:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Cain Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': 'Cain', 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/306ceb3c-ecb0-5f2c-864f-1057be98fdb5', 'word_count': 371, 'uri': 'nyt://article/306ceb3c-ecb0-5f2c-864f-1057be98fdb5'}, {'abstract': 'Governor Chris Christie of New Jersey said that if Sarah Palin continues to avoid exchanges with reporters and the public, “she’ll never be president.”', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/christie-says-palin-needs-to-go-unscripted-to-contend-for-2012/', 'snippet': 'Governor Chris Christie of New Jersey said that if Sarah Palin continues to avoid exchanges with reporters and the public, “she’ll never be president.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Chris Christie of New Jersey says it’s way too early to handicap the field of his fellow Republicans who might run for president in 2012, but on Wednesday he voiced a few sharp words about the most famous one.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Christie Says Palin Needs to Go Unscripted to Contend for 2012', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T21:43:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Pérez-Peña', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pérez-Peña', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4a4d503-def5-5391-81d5-fe7b47a88512', 'word_count': 391, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4a4d503-def5-5391-81d5-fe7b47a88512'}, {'abstract': 'A study by Swedish researchers found that male chess players took more risks, with worse results, in games against attractive female players.', 'web_url': 'https://gambit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/to-play-better-against-attractive-women-men-need-to-avert-their-eyes/', 'snippet': 'A study by Swedish researchers found that male chess players took more risks, with worse results, in games against attractive female players.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Do men get distracted by and play differently against attractive women than against other opponents? Yes, according to a study by Swedish researchers. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Play Better Against Attractive Women, Men Need to Avert Their Eyes', 'kicker': 'Gambit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beauty', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chess', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T21:43:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Chess', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dylan Loeb McClain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dylan', 'middlename': 'Loeb', 'lastname': 'McClain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0708c259-188d-55dd-89be-59c966876b34', 'word_count': 392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0708c259-188d-55dd-89be-59c966876b34'}, {'abstract': 'When Nick Washington scored 43 points for the Wizards Tuesday, he provided a test case for the hot hand theory.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/washingtons-young-tests-the-hot-hand-theory/', 'snippet': 'When Nick Washington scored 43 points for the Wizards Tuesday, he provided a test case for the hot hand theory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nick Young took a prime opportunity –- an overtime game against one of the league’s worst defenses -– and made a great night out of it. The Wizards barely nudged by the Kings in Tuesday night’s game, but that was no fault of Young, who poured in a career-high 43 points on 14-of-22 shooting. Young was insanely efficient (to score 43 points on 22 shots is a ridiculous accomplishment) even though his game was clearly oriented to the perimeter; as is typically the case with Young, he subsisted on long jumpers. The only difference was that on Tuesday night, Young put up a ton of attempts from behind the three-point line (more than twice his season average in three-point attempts), and that regardless of where he shot from, Young made everything. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Washington’s Young Tests the Hot Hand Theory', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Wizards', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T21:45:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Mahoney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mahoney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2999b6