main': '‘Chinaberry Sidewalks’', 'kicker': 'Excerpt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T22:24:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rodney Crowell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rodney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crowell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'First Chapter', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c0b98aa-3355-535e-b3c3-3907021e1cd2', 'word_count': 2022, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c0b98aa-3355-535e-b3c3-3907021e1cd2'}, {'abstract': 'Margaret Whiting, a daughter of Hollywood’s golden age, was a loving caretaker of the American Songbook.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/arts/music/13whiting.html', 'snippet': 'Margaret Whiting, a daughter of Hollywood’s golden age, was a loving caretaker of the American Songbook.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A largely overlooked pleasure of Nora Ephron’s 2009 film “Julie & Julia,” is the rich, sensual voice of the young Margaret Whiting, singing the Sammy Cahn-Jule Styne standard “Time After Time” over the end title credits. Immediately after seeing the film I telephoned a friend of Ms. Whiting’s, who told her about it. Until then she had no idea that her recording was used in the film. The friend reported back her tearfully overjoyed amazement. She hadn’t been forgotten. It meant everything.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/arts/whiting/whiting-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 742, 'width': 596, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url':