: 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/world/13SHANGHAI1/13SHANGHAI1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/13/world/13SHANGHAI1/13SHANGHAI1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'China, in a Shift, Takes On Its Alzheimer’s Problem', 'kicker': 'The Vanishing Mind', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China, in a Shift to Acceptance, Takes On Its Alzheimer’s Problem', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Alzheimer's Disease", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dementia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing Homes', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T22:42:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Barboza', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barboza', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e405a0a7-5dbd-55b1-9c74-280411010716', 'word_count': 1126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e405a0a7-5dbd-55b1-9c74-280411010716'}, {'abstract': 'Photographers who are worried that fashion has nothing significant to say have turned to gender-bending and exotically erotic models as a way to express their creative powers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/fashion/13NOTEBOOK.html', 'snippet': 'Photographers who are worried that fashion has nothing significant to say have turned to gender-bending and exotically erotic models as a way to express their creative powers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THREE times this week I’ve heard someone refer to “a parallel world.” The first was on Sunday, when the wife of a college friend said that she did not pay much attention to the celebrity-media culture. My friend, a real estate investor, and his wife, a doctor, had left Manhattan for Darien, Conn., shortly after 9/11 and were raising two girls in a neighborhood of two-story Colonials and adjoining lawns. I had the distinct sense that she did not mind not knowing who Snooki was (nor did her husband or their elder child, a polite and articulate eighth grader with braids), but also that at one time she did not feel quite so separated from mass culture. ', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/fashion/13NOTEBOOK-A/Z-JP-NOTEBOOK-A-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 769, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/fashion/13NOTEBOOK-A/Z-JP-NOTEBOOK-A-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/13/fashion/13NOTEBOOK-A/Z-JP-NOTEBOOK-A-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pushing Fashion Boundaries in an Era Without Any', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pushing Boundaries That No Longer Exist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Magazines', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T22:44:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a3fb39e-9391-5616-94f1-ce5575059847', 'word_count': 1247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a3fb39e-9391-5616-94f1-ce5575059847'}, {'abstract': 'Another breakup wave is hastening the end for some conglomerates.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/business/13views.html', 'snippet': 'Another breakup wave is hastening the end for some conglomerates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Another breakup wave is hastening the end for some conglomerates. Motorola, Fortune Brands and possibly Sara Lee are doing the splits. Now the ITT Corporation, an offshoot of a three-way division in 1995, is breaking in three again. The financial benefits of specialization have long been popular. But resurgent animal spirits explain the current swell of activity.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Time Is Right for Breaking Up', 'kicker': 'Reuters Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Time Is Right for Breaking Up', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ITT Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T22:46:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Cyran, Peter Thal Larsen and Neil Unmack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cyran', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'Thal', 'lastname': 'Larsen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Unmack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca38ebff-827e-5803-bc94-82824039ca47', 'word_count': 728, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca38ebff-827e-5803-bc94-82824039ca47'}, {'abstract': 'Oprah Winfrey’s hairdresser has a new product line, and there’s a cheaper solution to split ends.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/fashion/13SPOTS.html', 'snippet': 'Oprah Winfrey’s hairdresser has a new product line, and there’s a cheaper solution to split ends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HAIR AND FEEDING This month, Shu Uemura Art of Hair (shuuemuraartofhair-usa.com) introduced a medium-hold styling product called Yokan Craft. Developed for short to medium-length hair, the jellylike substance is made with natural ingredients, including rice, green tea, honey and algae, and features a fragrance with a hint of juniper. Yokan Craft is also claimed to improve texture and sheen ($33).', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/fashion/13SKIN-spots1/Z-SKIN-G-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 835, 'width': 893, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/fashion/13SKIN-spots1/Z-SKIN-G-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/13/fashion/13SKIN-spots1/Z-SKIN-G-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beauty Spots', 'kicker': 'Skin Deep', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beauty Spots', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hair', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beauty Salons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T22:47:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Howard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Howard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0dd0840-bf9c-5d30-bdbb-b2fc04159976', 'word_count': 219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0dd0840-bf9c-5d30-bdbb-b2fc04159976'}, {'abstract': 'Add to the list of buyers of arty film festival movies ... the USA Network?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/movies/13arts-USAACQUIRES9_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Add to the list of buyers of arty film festival movies ... the USA Network?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Add to the list of buyers of arty film festival movies ... the USA Network? That top-rated cable channel stocks its schedule with slick and sexy original dramas (“Royal Pains”), reruns of popular shows like “NCIS” and major Hollywood movies. But Bonnie Hammer, the NBC Universal executive who runs USA, plans to announce on Thursday the channel’s first festival acquisition: “The Space Between,” a drama starring Melissa Leo as a flight attendant who forms a bond with a 10-year-old Pakistani-American boy traveling by himself on 9/11. (Ms. Leo, now generating Oscar buzz for her chain-smoking mother in “The Fighter,” is on something of a tear these days.) “The Space Between,” written and directed by Travis Fine, had its premiere in the spring at the Tribeca Film Festival. The purchase price was not disclosed.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'USA Acquires 9/11 Film', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'USA Acquires 9/11 Film', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T22:48:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/259c2c25-193a-5d4e-8c1f-4d5d5f39563f', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/259c2c25-193a-5d4e-8c1f-4d5d5f39563f'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs from Australia, Haiti, India and Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/pictures-of-the-day-australia-and-elsewhere-2/', 'snippet': 'Photographs from Australia, Haiti, India and Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photographs from Australia, Haiti, India and Washington.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pictures of the Day: Australia and Elsewhere', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T22:50:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Merrill D. Oliver and Karly Domb Sadof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Merrill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc702cb1-8dbe-5895-86f7-1a793680bff2', 'word_count': 7, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc702cb1-8dbe-5895-86f7-1a793680bff2'}, {'abstract': 'The Ranters Theater is making its American debut with a show about two men and a children’s pool.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/coil-festival-5-questions-about-holiday/', 'snippet': 'The Ranters Theater is making its American debut with a show about two men and a children’s pool.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Australian company Ranters Theater is making its American debut at the Coil Festival with “Holiday.” The setting for the show is pretty simple: accompanied by a Baroque score and video installation, two men consider life while sitting in and around a children’s pool.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Coil Festival: 5 Questions About ‘Holiday’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T22:51:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0864649-7a20-5ce2-9ee8-135ed0ec436f', 'word_count': 358, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0864649-7a20-5ce2-9ee8-135ed0ec436f'}, {'abstract': 'Men who color their hair often keep some gray, balancing the look of experience with an impression of youth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/fashion/13SKIN.html', 'snippet': 'Men who color their hair often keep some gray, balancing the look of experience with an impression of youth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LIKE bartenders, hairstylists get an earful from regulars about their woes, and during the economic downturn Jeff Swaner, who owns Epic Salon in Los Angeles, has been hearing from middle-age men who lost their jobs.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page'