team.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chip Kelly, Oregon’s football coach, found inspiration in his friend and fellow New England native Micky Ward, a former junior-welterweight champion. Early in the season, he showed his team film of Ward in the ring. Ward also met with the players, most recently on Monday night, before the Ducks’ 22-19 loss to Auburn in the national championship game in Glendale, Ariz.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Fighter’ Was Rooting for Oregon', 'kicker': 'The Quad', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Chip', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ward, Micky', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T22:52:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vincent M. Mallozzi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vincent', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/619b27b7-debc-5394-976e-0ad83bbcabd6', 'word_count': 573, 'uri': 'nyt://article/619b27b7-debc-5394-976e-0ad83bbcabd6'}, {'abstract': 'Karin Sander, a conceptual artist, added two floors an old factory in Berlin to create a flexible living-and-working space.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/01/13/greathomesanddestinations/20110113_location.html', 'snippet': 'Karin Sander, a conceptual artist, added two floors an old factory in Berlin to create a flexible living-and-working space.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Karin Sander, a conceptual artist, added two floors an old factory in Berlin to create a flexible living-and-working space.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Expanding Vertically in Berlin', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T22:52:18+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Great Homes & Destinations', 'section_name': 'Great Homes & Destinations', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/df7870d4-de6a-544c-a6d7-0af7620af929', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/df7870d4-de6a-544c-a6d7-0af7620af929'}, {'abstract': 'CBS’s hit sitcom “The Big Bang Theory” has been renewed through the 2013-14 television season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/arts/television/13arts-CBSRENEWSBIG_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'CBS’s hit sitcom “The Big Bang Theory” has been renewed through the 2013-14 television season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CBS’s hit sitcom “The Big Bang Theory” has been renewed through the 2013-14 television season, the network and the show’s studio, Warner Brothers, jointly announced on Wednesday. “Big Bang,” which started in 2007, was the top-rated scripted show on television last season among 18- to 49-year-old viewers, and its ratings are up about 15 percent in that demographic since being shifted last fall to Thursday nights from Monday. The stars of the show, Kaley Cuoco, Johnny Galecki and Jim Parsons, reportedly negotiated new contracts last fall that tripled their salaries, to roughly $200,000 each per episode.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CBS Renews ‘Big Bang’', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'CBS Renews ‘Big Bang’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T22:52:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca19157a-06d7-5059-b239-e0ef905dcf6c', 'word_count': 98, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca19157a-06d7-5059-b239-e0ef905dcf6c'}, {'abstract': 'CBS dominated the ratings on Tuesday as “NCIS” delivered its largest audience ever.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/arts/television/13arts-NCISDELIVERS_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'CBS dominated the ratings on Tuesday as “NCIS” delivered its largest audience ever.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CBS dominated the ratings on Tuesday as “NCIS” delivered its largest audience ever, 21.9 million viewers, according to Nielsen’s estimates. The show easily outperformed its combined competitors at 8 p.m., including “No Ordinary Family” on ABC (5.4 million), Fox’s rerun of “Glee” (4.7 million) and the first hour of “The Biggest Loser” on NBC, which averaged 8.7 million from 8 to 10. CBS led during the 9 and 10 p.m. hours as well as with “NCIS: Los Angeles” (18 million) and “The Good Wife” (12.2 million). NBC was second for the night, ABC was third, and Fox finished in fourth.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘NCIS’ Delivers for CBS', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘NCIS’ Delivers for CBS', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T22:52:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'mid