owns in a Los Angeles building, Joseph Giovannini has created murals that fool the eye into connecting different surfaces.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SOME tenants have to beg their landlords to paint their apartments. Not so the occupants of the lofts in a former garage near downtown Los Angeles, where the owner, Joseph Giovannini, has created murals that fool the eye into connecting distinct surfaces. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13giovannini/13giovannini-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13giovannini/13giovannini-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13giovannini/13giovannini-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13giovannini/13giovannini-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13giovannini/13giovannini-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lofts Painted to Create an Illusion', 'kicker': 'Design Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Living in an Illusion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T23:59:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fred A. Bernstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fred', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Bernstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/141e38c8-fb7a-5900-8543-4c5f9ebdbb00', 'word_count': 491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/141e38c8-fb7a-5900-8543-4c5f9ebdbb00'}, {'abstract': 'Sales on furniture, upholstered pieces and housewares.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/garden/13deals.html', 'snippet': 'Sales on furniture, upholstered pieces and housewares.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Apartment 48 is offering 30 percent to 50 percent off all merchandise, in the store only, through Jan. 31 (Wobbly tumblers, right, originally $8 each, are $5.60; the Hobnail bowl, regularly $98, is $68.60); 12 West 17th Street (Fifth Avenue), (212) 807-1391, apartment48.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13deals/13deals-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13deals/13deals-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 336}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13deals/13deals-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 999, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13deals/13deals-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13deals/13deals-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sales at Apartment 48, Ochre and Design Within Reach', 'kicker': 'Currents | Deals', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sales on Bowls, Bed Skirts and More', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T23:59:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rima Suqi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rima', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Suqi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1b3333e-7cc3-5f9c-bea5-6a177988de19', 'word_count': 351, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1b3333e-7cc3-5f9c-bea5-6a177988de19'}, {'abstract': 'The artist, whose exhibition “Folly” opens soon at the Jane Hartsook Gallery, subverts traditional shapes to her own mischievous ends.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/garden/13qna.html', 'snippet': 'The artist, whose exhibition “Folly” opens soon at the Jane Hartsook Gallery, subverts traditional shapes to her own mischievous ends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Beth Katleman is a ceramic artist who plays with dainty forms and techniques, subverting traditional shapes to her own mischievous ends. Her delicate earthenware reliefs in wild colors recall the 18th-century porcelain rooms of European royalty — except when you look closely, you might see the Campbell Soup kids brandishing a safety razor among the rococo flowers and vines, or the Pillsbury Doughboy tucked inside an ornate doorway.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13wallpaperspan1/13wallpaperspan1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13wallpaperspan1/13wallpaperspan1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13wallpaperspan1/13wallpaperspan1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13wallpaperspan1/13wallpaperspan1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13wallpaperspan1/13wallpaperspan1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beth Katleman on Her Ceramic Curiosities', 'kicker': 'Currents | Q&A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beth Katleman on Her Ceramic Curiosities', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ceramics and Pottery', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T23:59:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Penelope Green', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Penelope', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Green', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d45de6d-b4a9-5c5f-9b67-5185e8cbaf44', 'word_count': 751, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d45de6d-b4a9-5c5f-9b67-5185e8cbaf44'}, {'abstract': 'The new store on Grand Street is a showcase for the designer’s upholstery, case goods and decorative accessories.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/garden/13open.html', 'snippet': 'The new store on Grand Street is a showcase for the designer’s upholstery, case goods and decorative accessories.', 'lead_paragraph': '“When I was 8 years old, I announced to my parents that one day I would have a store with my name on it,” said Tamara Magel, whose childhood prediction came true with the opening of her store in SoHo last week. Ms. Magel, 42, was an auditor for Deloitte & Touche and a software consultant for Price Waterhouse before pursuing her lifelong interest in design. She started her own interior design business nine years ago in Washington; now she is based in New York, and her 2,000-square-foot shop is a showcase for her designs (upholstery, case goods and decorative accessories), along with vintage lighting, 1960s Moroccan carpets and Matouk bedding.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13open/13open-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 994, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13open/13open-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13open/13open-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tamara Magel Opens in SoHo', 'kicker': 'Currents | Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In SoHo, a Dream Realized', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T23:59:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rima Suqi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rima', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Suqi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2458fe6c-656d-53ad-992c-34547489804b', 'word_count': 122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2458fe6c-656d-53ad-992c-34547489804b'}, {'abstract': 'The Mexico City architect Elias Kababie’s Pint table is available in dining and coffee-table sizes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/garden/13furniture.html', 'snippet': 'The Mexico City architect Elias Kababie’s Pint table is available in dining and coffee-table sizes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Elias Kababie’s Pint table looks as if it belongs in a crime scene — albeit a stylish one, with its deep red resin globules dripping precisely toward the floor. Mr. Kababie, a 25-year-old architect, was inspired, he said, by paint on a table (no crime scene involved), and experimented with wood before settling on a Corian-and-resin combination. “Corian and resin are really firm when cold, but when you heat them you can do whatever you like,” he said. And “if you put it outdoors, it will still be in good shape years from now.” Available in dining and coffee-table sizes (for about $5,000 and $3,500, respectively), in black, white, red, pink and blue, at KA, Mr. Kababie’s showroom in Mexico. KA, Juan Vázquez de Mella No. 481, Mexico City; 011-52-55-5280-6679, tienda-ka.com.\xa0', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13furniture/13furniture-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 363, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13furniture/13furniture-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 363}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13furniture/13furniture-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 774, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13furniture/13furniture-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13furniture/13furniture-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Table Dripping With Red Resin', 'kicker': 'Currents | Furniture', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hey, Who Spilled All the Paint?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tables', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T23:59:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rima Suqi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rima', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Suqi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a5fc448-c9ab-50f4-b19d-d2ff33c1d410', 'word_count': 133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a5fc448-c9ab-50f4-b19d-d2ff33c1d410'}, {'abstract': 'A five-part series, organized by Dan Rubinstein, the editor in chief of Surface magazine, explores the current state of furniture design.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/garden/13events.html', 'snippet': 'A five-part series, organized by Dan Rubinstein, the editor in chief of Surface magazine, explores the current state of furniture design.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Home Front: American Furniture Now,” a series of panel discussions at the Museum of Arts and Design in Manhattan, will make its debut tonight. The five-part series, organized by Dan Rubinstein, the editor in chief of Surface magazine, explores the current state of furniture design.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘American Furniture Now’ Panels at the Museum of Arts and Design', 'kicker': 'Currents | Events', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Let’s Pull Up a Chair — and Discuss', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T23:59:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rima Suqi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rima', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Suqi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd3c0b66-e75d-5c6c-b3e5-24808976a3d8', 'word_count': 190, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd3c0b66-e75d-5c6c-b3e5-24808976a3d8'}, {'abstract': 'Even in a sexually liberal culture, the home is still usually off limits for affairs, and the marriage bed remains a sacred object.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/garden/13cheat.html', 'snippet': 'Even in a sexually liberal culture, the home is still usually off limits for affairs, and the marriage bed remains a sacred object.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE woman who came to see Ken Altshuler, a divorce lawyer, had reason to be enraged: her husband was not only having an affair, he was also having an extravagant, money’s-no-concern, fabled-and-faraway-beaches affair. He had taken his girlfriend to Tahiti, he was sending flowers to her. But what infuriated his wife the most was where he had often made love to his girlfriend: their marriage bed.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13cheat1/13cheat1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 314, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13cheat1/13cheat1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 314}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13cheat1/13cheat1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 967, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13cheat1/13cheat1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13cheat1/13cheat1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Try This at Home: Adultery in the Marital Bed', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Don’t Try This At Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADULTERY', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Divorce, Separations and Annulments', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beds and Bedding', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T23:59:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce Wadler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wadler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a387c23-3a22-5941-aa3d-81f93634ee59', 'word_count': 2870, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a387c23-3a22-5941-aa3d-81f93634ee59'}, {'abstract': 'All 46 historic plates in “The Vegetable Garden” have been reproduced, bringing 19th-century garden vegetables to the modern kitchen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/garden/13books.html', 'snippet': 'All 46 historic plates in “The Vegetable Garden” have been reproduced, bringing 19th-century garden vegetables to the modern kitchen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From 1850 to 1895, Vilmorin-Andrieux & Cie, a Parisian seed company, commissioned a series of lavish illustrations as advertisements for its vegetables and fruits. The collection of 46 lithographs, by some of the period’s most notable botanical artists, was eventually published as the “Album Vilmorin (Les Plantes Potagères),” a volume that survives in only a few libraries today. The album showcased the work of the company, started in 1743 by Claude Geoffroy, the daughter of a seed merchant, and Pierre D’Andrieux, a botanist to King Louis XV, and the pioneering work done by their heirs, who were among the first to practice the science of seed breeding.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13books/13books-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 732, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13books/13books-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/13/garden/13books/13books-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Vegetable Garden’ From Taschen', 'kicker': 'Currents | Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vegetables Just Ripe for the Framing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-12T23:59:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sara Barrett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barrett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ed62536-72e9-5cbe-9665-680455c8de03', 'word_count': 140, 'uri': 'nyt://article