 message about the Arizona shootings accused commentators and journalists of “blood libel” in a rush to blame heated political rhetoric for the violence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Sarah Palin broke her silence on Wednesday and delivered a forceful denunciation of her critics in a video message about the Arizona shootings, accusing commentators and journalists of “blood libel” in a frenzied rush to blame heated political speech for the violence.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/us/subPALIN/subPALIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 852, 'width': 767, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/13/us/subPALIN/subPALIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/13/us/subPALIN/subPALIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Palin Joins Debate on Heated Speech With Words That Stir New Controversy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Palin Joins Debate on Heated Speech With Words That Stir New Controversy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tucson Shooting (2011)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libel and Slander', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Speeches and Statements', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palin, Sarah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T02:26:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Zeleny and Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zeleny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37cd6393-7ccf-54e7-b3eb-7631f9fecf56', 'word_count': 885, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37cd6393-7ccf-54e7-b3eb-7631f9fecf56'}, {'abstract': 'What did the Crittenden Compromise promise, and why did it fail?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/last-chance-for-compromise/', 'snippet': 'What did the Crittenden Compromise promise, and why did it fail?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The term “compromise” has paradoxical connotations. We applaud the “spirit of compromise” but shame those who have “compromised their principles.” This dual meaning captures the central problem of our nation’s early history. From the three-fifths compromise embedded in the Constitution, to the Missouri Compromise of 1820 (which divided future Western states between slave and free), to the Compromise of 1850 (which, among other things, created the Fugitive Slave Act), slavery demanded a series of adjustments in order to satisfy two increasingly antagonistic sections.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Last Chance for Compromise', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T02:30:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By SUSAN SCHULTEN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'SCHULTEN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e07dade0-fe24-5508-8596-e9fcb53086d4', 'word_count': 1025, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e07dade0-fe24-5508-8596-e9fcb53086d4'}, {'abstract': 'A Supreme Court ruling on Miranda warnings demonstrates how the original understanding of our Constitution is mediated by modern needs.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/problems-of-democracy/', 'snippet': 'A Supreme Court ruling on Miranda warnings demonstrates how the original understanding of our Constitution is mediated by modern needs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the first day of law school, my constitutional law professor gave the class a homework assignment: go home tonight and read the Constitution.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Problems of Democracy', 'kicker': 'Opinionator', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Miranda v Arizona (Supreme Court Decision)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scalia, Antonin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T02:30:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b09144eb-123e-58ee-80b4-d04a362cd44a', 'word_count': 1209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b09144eb-123e-58ee-80b4-d04a362cd44a'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Doar, the commissioner of the city’s Human Resources Administration, said he did not believe the city had been cavalier in approving home care, the subject of a civil fraud lawsuit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/nyregion/13medicaid.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Doar, the commissioner of the city’s Human Resources Administration, said he did not believe the city had been cavalier in approving home care, the subject of a civil fraud lawsuit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Medicaid fraud conjures up images of shady doctors, pharmacists and businessmen being led away in handcuffs by federal agents as photographers snap away and television cameras roll.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As New York City Defends Medicaid Approvals, Fear of Suit’s Fallout Grows', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As New York City Defends Medicaid Approvals, Health Advocates Fear Suit’s Fallout', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Home Health Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Human Resources Administration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T02:38:51+0000', 'docume