l': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4639a4af-8cf5-501c-9d15-4c1e6b2b27f7', 'word_count': 814, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4639a4af-8cf5-501c-9d15-4c1e6b2b27f7'}, {'abstract': 'The report that first triggered scares that a vaccine to prevent measles, mumps and rubella might cause autism in children has received another devastating blow to its credibility.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/opinion/13thu2.html', 'snippet': 'The report that first triggered scares that a vaccine to prevent measles, mumps and rubella might cause autism in children has received another devastating blow to its credibility.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The report that first triggered scares that a vaccine to prevent measles, mumps and rubella might cause autism in children has received another devastating blow to its credibility. The British Medical Journal has declared that the research was not simply bad science, as has been known for years, but a deliberate fraud.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Autism Fraud', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Autism Fraud', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Autism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wakefield, Andrew', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lancet, The (Journal)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Medical Journal', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T04:18:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/511a06ae-e2b4-5279-923f-df10cf255103', 'word_count': 359, 'uri': 'nyt://article/511a06ae-e2b4-5279-923f-df10cf255103'}, {'abstract': 'President Zine el-Abidine Ben Ali of Tunisia has responded to deadly riots by clamping down even harder on the country’s political life, a course sure to lead to more unrest and bloodshed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/opinion/13thu3.html', 'snippet': 'President Zine el-Abidine Ben Ali of Tunisia has responded to deadly riots by clamping down even harder on the country’s political life, a course sure to lead to more unrest and bloodshed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For 23 years, President Zine el-Abidine Ben Ali of Tunisia has kept a tight clamp on his country’s political life, marketing Tunisia as a tourist oasis while dangerous tensions built up beneath the surface. Now deadly riots have erupted over the bleak economic prospects facing the country’s young people. Mr. Ben Ali’s response has been to clamp down even harder, a course sure to lead to more unrest and bloodshed. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tunisia Seethes', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tunisia Seethes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tunisia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ben Ali, Zine El-Abidine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T04:18:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c73f4d18-f31f-5c9e-9307-fe9cd7017c88', 'word_count': 312, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c73f4d18-f31f-5c9e-9307-fe9cd7017c88'}, {'abstract': 'Among 20 forgotten veterans, one forgotten author.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/opinion/13thu4.html', 'snippet': 'Among 20 forgotten veterans, one forgotten author.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alston Anderson was buried on Saturday for the second time — or the third, if you count the slow sinking that constituted his long but unrewarded life. He was an African-American short-story writer, poet, novelist and jazz critic. He spent three years in the Army in World War II and three months of 1955 at Yaddo, the writers’ colony in Saratoga Springs, N.Y. He studied philosophy at Columbia and the Sorbonne.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Three Burials of Alston Anderson', 'kicker': 'Editorial Observer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Three Burials of Alston Anderson', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yaddo', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Calverton National Cemetery', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T04:18:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lawrence Downes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lawrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4cfc860-8393-565b-930c-bbb892261a00', 'word_count': 768, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4cfc860-8393-565b-930c-bbb892261a00'}, {'abstract': 'Certain types of contingencies suit the temperaments of certain types of presidents especially well, and the Tucson shooting seems to have been one such case for President Obama.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/12/a-few-reflections-on-obamas-speech-in-tucson/', 'snippet': 'Certain types of contingencies suit the temperaments of certain types of presidents especially well, and the Tucson shooting seems to have been one such case for President Obama.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama’s speech in Tucson tonight seems to have won nearly universal praise. I suspect it will be remembered as one of his best moments, almost regardless of what else takes place during the remainder of his presidency.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Few Reflections on Obama’s Speech in Tucson', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T04:25:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/150c509a-300d-549a-ae6f-1de3ef4165ec', 'word_count': 504, 'uri': 'nyt://article/150c509a-300d-549a-ae6f-1de3ef4165ec'}, {'abstract': 'Food price inflation in particular is a sensitive issue in developing nations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/business/global/14asiaecon.html', 'snippet': 'Food price inflation in particular is a sensitive issue in developing nations.', 'lead_paragraph':