r blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thursday Reading: Help with the Heating Bill', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T15:32:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Saranow Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'Saranow', 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b1fd7bf-cdcd-55b1-a4cc-8d479a5338e0', 'word_count': 106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b1fd7bf-cdcd-55b1-a4cc-8d479a5338e0'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s post pairs the poem “For My Wife Cutting My Hair” with a 2007 Modern Love column, “Divorce and a Haircut, Two Bits.”', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/poetry-pairing-jan-13-2011/', 'snippet': 'This week’s post pairs the poem “For My Wife Cutting My Hair” with a 2007 Modern Love column, “Divorce and a Haircut, Two Bits.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In our weekly “Poetry Pairing” series, we collaborate with the Poetry Foundation to feature a work from its American Life in Poetry project alongside content from The Times that somehow echoes, extends or challenges the poem’s themes. Each poem is introduced briefly by Ted Kooser, a former United States poet laureate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poetry Pairing | Jan. 13, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T15:32:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c63990f9-8d4a-534a-b0b5-27fcf89b5ad0', 'word_count': 473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c63990f9-8d4a-534a-b0b5-27fcf89b5ad0'}, {'abstract': 'The third and final set of answers to readers’ questions about getting organized.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/answers-about-getting-organized-part-iii/', 'snippet': 'The third and final set of answers to readers’ questions about getting organized.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Barbara Reich, a professional organizer who was the subject of a recent Metropolitan article by Elissa Gootman, took readers’ questions this week about getting organized. Her third and final set of answers are found below, and previous installments in this Q. and A. can be read here. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Answers About Getting Organized, Part III', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Closets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reich, Barbara', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T15:35:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2577130d-f8fe-533b-97b4-ef9b558a53a4', 'word_count': 1239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2577130d-f8fe-533b-97b4-ef9b558a53a4'}, {'abstract': 'DealBook reporter Ben Protess speaks to Jason Goldberg, a senior U.S. banks analyst at Barclays Capita