tract': 'Audi of America is planning to set the stage for its Super Bowl commercial with a spot that is to begin appearing on Thursday night.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/audi-sets-stage-for-its-super-bowl-spot/', 'snippet': 'Audi of America is planning to set the stage for its Super Bowl commercial with a spot that is to begin appearing on Thursday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Super Bowl XLV on Feb. 6 is going to be crowded with commercials from carmakers. Perhaps as many as eight brands will be vying for viewers in what the trade publication Advertising Age is already calling a traffic jam.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Audi Sets Stage for Its Super Bowl Spot', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Audi Division of Volkswagen AG', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T16:46:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8c45d5a-9e36-5975-bcbf-e0f9af5d7812', 'word_count': 506, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8c45d5a-9e36-5975-bcbf-e0f9af5d7812'}, {'abstract': 'Former U.S. President Jimmy Carter and Nicholas Kristof address a third and final batch or readers’ questions about the referendum in Sudan.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/answering-your-sudan-questions-round-3/', 'snippet': 'Former U.S. President Jimmy Carter and Nicholas Kristof address a third and final batch or readers’ questions about the referendum in Sudan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is a historic week in Sudan, with a referendum underway on secession for the south — and a process that is likely to lead to a new nation of South Sudan (although it may choose a different name for itself). Former President Jimmy Carter is on the ground there, monitoring the voting, and he has been answering questions from readers with me. Read the first and second batches of answers, and here’s the third and last installment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Answering Your Sudan Questions, Round 3', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Referendums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carter, Jimmy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mbeki, Thabo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sudan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T16:51:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a8fd384-d933-532a-bb64-be1b3b71249a', 'word_count': 1013, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a8fd384-d933-532a-bb64-be1b3b71249a'}, {'abstract': 'To a number of older people, personal appearance is still an art.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/style-without-end/', 'snippet': 'To a number of older people, personal appearance is still an art.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Style Without End', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T17:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/16a27558-9d21-5ce0-b48e-cb6a35f68d87', 'word_count': 478, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16a27558-9d21-5ce0-b48e-cb6a35f68d87'}, {'abstract': 'A perfect season has become an outdated notion, as the Duke men’s basketball team found out Wednesday night at Florida State.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/sports/ncaabasketball/14leading.html', 'snippet': 'A perfect season has become an outdated notion, as the Duke men’s basketball team found out Wednesday night at Florida State.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is either charming or a sign of insanity that sports fans go through this particular cycle so often. Some really good team comes along, piles up an impressive winning streak and suddenly it takes on mythical qualities. Stronger than Atlas. Has the smartest coach ever. Can leap tall buildings with a bunny hop. These teams are juggernauts, impervious, absolutely unbeatable ... until they’re not.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Duke Proves Beatable', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Duke Proves Beatable', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krzyzewski, Mike', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Duke University', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T17:02:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a957e23c-8a46-55a9-ab49-c36deb0a51b5', 'word_count': 767, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a957e23c-8a46-55a9-ab49-c36deb0a51b5'}, {'abstract': 'As the Bagger lands in the city of arts and entertainment, the director of the Golden Globes talks about this weekend’s telecast, and the Winkelvoss twins try to get more of that Facebook fortune.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/a-globes-check-in-and-a-crusade-for-credit/', 'snippet': 'As the Bagger lands in the city of arts and entertainment, the director of the Golden Globes talks about this weekend’s telecast, and the Winkelvoss twins try to get more of that Facebook fortune.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES— The Bagger has landed in the city of arts and entertainment, as a billboard at the airport read, as well as the town of celebrities self-involved enough to sign off on their own parodies, never-ending 24-hour donut shops and homeless people who are worried about your karma. Today marks the start of a whirlwind few days of festivities for the Golden Globes, and, like last year, the Bagger has come prepared.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Globes Check-In and a Crusade for Credit', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gervais, Ricky', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuckerberg, Mark E', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T17:03:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/411e0897-4cca-5e1f-be67-849feba23af4', 'word_count': 652, 'uri': 'nyt://article/411e0897-4cca-5e1f-be67-849feba23af4'}, {'abstract': 'To shed more light on the health of America’s poor — and occasionally its rich — the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention compiled details of disproportionate suffering among racial and ethnic groups.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/health/14cdc.html', 'snippet': 'To shed more light on the health of America’s poor — and occasionally its rich — the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention compiled details of disproportionate suffering among racial and ethnic groups.', 'lead_paragraph': 'White people in the United States die of drug overdoses more often than other ethnic groups. Black people are hit proportionately harder by AIDS, strokes and heart disease. And American Indians are more likely to die in car crashes.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/14/us/CDC/CDC-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 737, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/14/us/CDC/CDC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/14/us/CDC/CDC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Broad Racial Disparities Seen in Americans’ Ills', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Broad Racial Disparities\nSeen in Americans’ Ills', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minorities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T17:09:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donald G. McNeil Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donald', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'McNeil', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd967958-2c63-5c7b-9cec-2400a60ea9b5', 'word_count': 694, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd967958-2c63-5c7b-9cec-2400a60ea9b5'}, {'abstract': 'The opera house was sued by the federal government.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/metropolitan-opera-settling-lawsuit-will-increase-wheelchair-access/', 'snippet': 'The opera house was sued by the federal government.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Metropolitan Opera will now be more wheelchair accessible. The opera company on Thursday settled a civil rights lawsuit in Manhattan federal court that had been brought against the Met for failure to comply with the Americans With Disabilities Act. The United States Attorney for the Southern District of New York announced that it had filed, and simultaneously settled, the lawsuit against the Met. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Metropolitan Opera, Settling Lawsuit, Will Increase Wheelchair Access', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMERICANS WITH DISABILITIES ACT', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'v