mes', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Soros Bets the Farm', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Soros, George', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T18:19:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5048da83-e61f-5a00-8696-912fe663fd04', 'word_count': 253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5048da83-e61f-5a00-8696-912fe663fd04'}, {'abstract': 'Concur Technologies, a provider of trip and expense management software, is buying TripIt, a mobile trip manager, in a cash and stock deal that could be worth as much as $120 million.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/concur-takes-off-with-tripit-deal/', 'snippet': 'Concur Technologies, a provider of trip and expense management software, is buying TripIt, a mobile trip manager, in a cash and stock deal that could be worth as much as $120 million.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Concur Technologies, a provider of trip and expense management software, announced Thursday that it had agreed to buy TripIt, a mobile trip manager, in a deal that could be worth as much as $120 million.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Concur Takes Off With TripIt Deal', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Business Travel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Concur Technologies Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T18:22:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6db37ea1-f3cb-5226-a1e0-5e0fb0ba5a7e', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6db37ea1-f3cb-5226-a1e0-5e0fb0ba5a7e'}, {'abstract': 'In “The Dilemma,” the two main characters love each other dearly, but their love is tested by difficult circumstances.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/movies/14dilemma.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Dilemma,” the two main characters love each other dearly, but their love is tested by difficult circumstances.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you have seen the ubiquitous advertisements and trailers for “The Dilemma,” you might be forgiven for expecting a semistupid, semisweet, semioffensive guy comedy. It is entirely possible that Imagine Entertainment and Universal Pictures want you to expect just that. The movie’s stars, Vince Vaughn and Kevin James (of “Couples Retreat,” “Paul Blart: Mall Cop” and much else) seem to grin from every billboard and bus shelter, promising beefy and boisterous wintertime high jinks of a kind moviegoers have learned to love, or at least tolerate.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/14/arts/DILEMMA/DILEMMA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 975, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/14/arts/DILEMMA/DILEMMA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/14/arts/DILEMMA/DILEMMA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Guy Thing? Not That Kind. And Not That Kind Either.', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'The Dilemma'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Guy Thing? Not That Kind. And Not That Kind Either.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vaughn, Vince', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, Kevin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howard, Ron', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Queen Latifah', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T18:25:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4423d396-1ed3-53b6-b55f-a8a46b8bede9', 'word_count': 941, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4423d396-1ed3-53b6-b55f-a8a46b8bede9'}, {'abstract': 'Five days after she was shot in the head, Representative Gabrielle Giffords is able to keep her eyes open for as long as 15 minutes and can move her legs and hands.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/us/14giffords.html', 'snippet': 'Five days after she was shot in the head, Representative Gabrielle Giffords is able to keep her eyes open for as long as 15 minutes and can move her legs and hands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TUCSON \x97 Five days after she was shot in the head at close range, Representative Gabrielle Giffords is able to keep her eyes open for as long as 15 minutes and can move her legs and hands, although her right hand has only slight movement, doctors at University Medical Center here said on Thursday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 's