ed Museum of the Moving Image show where the motion picture has been and where it’s going.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Why have a Museum of the Moving Image at all?” is the question that readily comes to mind before visiting the new, improved, expanded incarnation of this venerable institution in Astoria, Queens, which reopens its doors on Saturday after a $67 million face-lift that might even put Hollywood cosmeticians to shame.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/14/arts/14IMAGE-span/IMAGE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/14/arts/14IMAGE-span/IMAGE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/14/arts/14IMAGE-span/IMAGE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 772, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/14/arts/14IMAGE-span/IMAGE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/14/arts/14IMAGE-span/IMAGE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Pictures Leap to Other Screens', 'kicker': 'Exhibition Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Pictures Leap to Other Screens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of the Moving Image', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T21:55:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Rothstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d79a9cb-b864-52f3-91f9-41beea136635', 'word_count': 1366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d79a9cb-b864-52f3-91f9-41beea136635'}, {'abstract': 'Butcher Block, a market in Sunnyside, Queens, offers much more than just meat; it has become a primary Irish importer in its area.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/butcher-block/', 'snippet': 'Butcher Block, a market in Sunnyside, Queens, offers much more than just meat; it has become a primary Irish importer in its area.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are two entrances to Butcher Block. Open the door on the right and you will see a long butcher’s counter. At the end of it is a sign: “We Sell Boiling Bacon.” Open the other door, just a few feet away, and you quickly pass a wall — probably 10 feet high and 20 feet long — made up entirely of British potato chips and candies, with names like Wispa, Drifter, Catch, Topic.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Love, Bacon, and a Bit of Ireland', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Irish-Americans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meat Packers and Butchers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'SUNNYSIDE (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T21:57:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Saltzstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saltzstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}