, 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ad119cb-3adb-58b7-a1be-0a3159a93bc4', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ad119cb-3adb-58b7-a1be-0a3159a93bc4'}, {'abstract': 'La MaMa, the East Village theater Ellen Stewart created and led, is not a 1960s relic; it continues to produce unexpected and even prophetic art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/theater/14critic.html', 'snippet': 'La MaMa, the East Village theater Ellen Stewart created and led, is not a 1960s relic; it continues to produce unexpected and even prophetic art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was almost exactly 16 years ago that I made my first visit to La MaMa in a professional capacity, as a new theater critic for The New York Times. I’d been there before as a civilian, usually under the influence. (I believe it was where I first saw a foghorn-throated drag queen named Harvey Fierstein.) But even then La MaMa had for me the whiff of another time, the patchouli scent of the 1960s, when downtown theater was longhaired, renegade and rude.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/14/arts/14critic-stewart/14critic-stewart-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/14/arts/14critic-stewart/14critic-stewart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/14/arts/14critic-stewart/14critic-stewart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rocking a Cradle of Experimental Theater', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'AN APPRAISAL; Rocking\nA Cradle\nOf Stage\nInnovation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stewart, Ellen', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'La MaMa ETC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-13T23:04:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/290315b1-53c6-5a20-988f-8d79725cad13', 'word_count': 778, 'uri': 'nyt://article/290315b1-53c6-5a20-988f-8d79725cad13'}, {'abstract': 'The Bleecker Company offers a view of Appalachia through the eyes of its women.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/theater/14hill.html', 'snippet': 'The Bleecker Company offers a view of Appalachia through the eyes of its women.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So often in the small theaters of New York, it seems as if life stops at 30, so it’s great to see a multigenerational cast on the stage at the ArcLight Theater in “Hillbilly Women.” The play, directed by Sondra Lee and staged by the Bleecker Company, has its problems, but it also has one particularly attention-getting performance that leaves you hoping that some casting directors make the trip uptown to see it.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/14/arts/14HILL/HILL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 801, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/14/arts/14HILL/HILL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard