'pub_date': '2011-01-14T01:56:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f70f265a-1437-5b83-a373-bd02c9cf4046', 'word_count': 463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f70f265a-1437-5b83-a373-bd02c9cf4046'}, {'abstract': 'To cut down on spam, governments must make it more expensive to send.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/opinion/14fri4.html', 'snippet': 'To cut down on spam, governments must make it more expensive to send.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a moment, it seemed as if the new year might just deliver us from spam. Last summer, spam volumes started declining around the world, from about 250 billion messages a day in July \x97 almost 40 for each woman, man and child in the world \x97 to about 100 billion a day in November. Then, on Dec. 25, the Rustock botnet, the biggest spam generator, vanished from the Internet. (Two other smaller ones dropped off, too, a few days later.) Volume plummeted to fewer than 30 billion a day. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spam, a Lot', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spam, a Lot', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Spam (Electronic Mail)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T02:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/48dd0492-4dd5-5416-a2b9-c39795223dec', 'word_count': 357, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48dd0492-4dd5-5416-a2b9-c39795223dec'}, {'abstract': 'The United States’ Civil War contributed to the end of slavery in other countries as well.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/13/the-transatlantic-slave-trade-and-the-civil-war/', 'snippet': 'The United States’ Civil War contributed to the end of slavery in other countries as well.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The conclusion of the historian David Brion Davis’s most recent book, “Inhuman Bondage,” traces the significance of the United States Civil War in the ending of slavery elsewhere. It implicitly poses the question: What if the Confederacy had won recognition from Britain in 1862 and had survived the war? Our rather frightening answer is that the three great centers of slavery in the Americas, the United States South, Cuba and Brazil — plus the smaller plantation economy of Dutch Suriname — would not have abolished slavery when they did.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Transatlantic Slave Trade and the Civil War', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T02:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By DAVID ELTIS AND DAVID RICHARDSON', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'ELTIS', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'RICHARDSON', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/70dd5863-b36a-5c79-899d-cacbbc63a72c', 'word_count': 1364, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70dd5863-b36a-5c79-899d-cacbbc63a72c'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Steele, the first black chairman of the Republican Party, faces long odds of winning re-election.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/us/politics/14repubs.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Steele, the first black chairman of the Republican Party, faces long odds of winning re-election.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OXON HILL, Md. \x97 The Republican National Committee was poised to select a new leader on Friday to prepare for the 2012 presidential election, a challenge that begins by paying off a $20 million debt and navigating divisions between the party’s establishment and a newly invigorated grass-roots movement.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/14/us/14repubs1-cnd/14repubs1-cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/14/us/14repubs1-cnd/14repubs1-cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/14/us/14repubs1-cnd/14repubs1-cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/14/us/14repubs1-cnd/14repubs1-cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/14/us/14repubs1-cnd/14repubs1-cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Looking to 2012, G.O.P. Is Set to Select a New Leader', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Looking to 2012, G.O.P. Is Set to Select a New Leader', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican National Committee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wagner, Ann', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Steele, Michael S', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Priebus, Reince R', 'rank': 4, 'major'