book.com', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Flickr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'YouTube.com', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T12:30:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed621427-ce0c-59bc-9738-26e7887be434', 'word_count': 329, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed621427-ce0c-59bc-9738-26e7887be434'}, {'abstract': 'Student Opinion | How much do your parents demand of you, and how have those demands made you the person you are?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/how-permissive-are-your-parents/', 'snippet': 'Student Opinion | How much do your parents demand of you, and how have those demands made you the person you are?', 'lead_paragraph': '“Perhaps the most talked-about story in parenting circles this week is Amy Chua’s essay in The Wall Street Journal titled ‘Why Chinese Mothers Are Superior,’ “ writes Lisa Belkin on the Motherlode blog. Chinese parents, according to Ms. Chua, set very high standards for their children and refuse to compromise those standards. Read the examples Ms. Chua gives below, then tell us how permissive, or how strict, you think your own parents are. What do they demand of you? Do you think their demands are reasonable? Why or why not? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Permissive Are Your Parents?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T12:36:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7a24f81-423b-5afc-a168-b3c696964bc7', 'word_count': 421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7a24f81-423b-5afc-a168-b3c696964bc7'}, {'abstract': 'At a major bookseller’s stores, representations of people of color are scarce, and it is easy to find a van Gogh calendar, but not a Romare Bearden.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/complaint-box-calendars/', 'snippet': 'At a major bookseller’s stores, representations of people of color are scarce, and it is easy to find a van Gogh calendar, but not a Romare Bearden.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It may seem ungrateful of me to lodge this complaint around the time of Martin Luther King’s Birthday. We are, after all, in a purported “postracial” era. But a cherished annual ritual, finding calendars to organize and inspire me during the coming year, was lost to me this past holiday season, and I can’t help wondering if it’s because race is passé now, in these tough-but-equal times.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Complaint Box | Calendars', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Calendars', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T12:36:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thandiwe D. Watts-Jones', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thandiwe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 