rn-day wildcatters have pinpointed what they think is a major oil field. But they have to spend $100 million to see if they’re right.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/magazine/16Drilling-t.html', 'snippet': 'Below the ocean floor off Angola, modern-day wildcatters have pinpointed what they think is a major oil field. But they have to spend $100 million to see if they’re right.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oilmen are optimists, by creed if not always by nature, and early last spring things looked, as those in the industry like to say, prospective. The deepwater rigs in the Gulf of Mexico were steadily drilling, with no suggestion of any impending calamity, and oil was flowing from the vast finds in offshore Brazil. Circumstances looked particularly prospective to a geophysicist named Jim Farnsworth, who works for Cobalt International Energy, a company that held a group of leases 50 miles from the mouth of Angola’s Cuanza River basin. It was at that site, he had come to believe, that an enormous basin of oil lay, beneath an obscuring layer of salt, in rocks deep below the bottom of the sea. The industry had been over this territory a decade earlier; a few scattered wells were drilled into the Cuanza basin, and then, having found little oil, the companies plugged them. In seismic images, you could still see the pipes, running through the sediment of the ocean floor.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16drilling-span/16drilling-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 386, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16drilling-span/16drilling-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 386}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16drilling-span/16drilling-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16drilling-span/16drilling-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16drilling-span/16drilling-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Will to Drill', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'THE WILL TO DRILL', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Offshore Drilling and Exploration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ANGOLA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cobalt International Energy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T15:37:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By BENJAMIN WALLACE-WELLS', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'WALLACE-WELLS', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee19d6c9-3594-5f21-a82a-5d936696b579', 'word_count': 4744, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee19d6c9-3594-5f21-a82a-5d936696b579'}, {'abstract': 'The creators of notable designs at this year’s Detroit auto show seemed determined to offer more space within less length.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/automobiles/autoshow/16DESIGN.html', 'snippet': 'The creators of notable designs at this year’s Detroit auto show seemed determined to offer more space within less length.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT', 'print_section': 'AU', 'p