Dr. Chandan is the global chief economist for Real Capital Analytics, a market research firm in Manhattan that tracks commercial real estate sales and trends worldwide.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dr. Chandan, 37, is the global chief economist for Real Capital Analytics, a market research firm in Manhattan that tracks commercial real estate sales and trends worldwide. He joined the company about a year ago, after his firm, Real Estate Econometrics, was acquired by Real Capital.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/realestate/sq-feet/sq-feet-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 901, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/realestate/sq-feet/sq-feet-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/realestate/sq-feet/sq-feet-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sam Chandan', 'kicker': 'Square Feet | The 30-Minute Interview', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sam Chandan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Office Buildings and Commercial Properties', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T15:56:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivian Marino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2c8178a-159e-5c70-9d6a-e91006a0d297', 'word_count': 779, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2c8178a-159e-5c70-9d6a-e91006a0d297'}, {'abstract': 'An editing award is considered a must for a best picture win – no film has received the top prize without at least an editing nomination since 1981 – and because the American Cinema Editors has a lot of crossover with Academy in its membership, its choices are an excellent predictor.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/oscar-pool-intelligence-the-editors-speak/', 'snippet': 'An editing award is considered a must for a best picture win – no film has received the top prize without at least an editing nomination since 1981 – and because the American Cinema Editors has a lot of crossover with Academy in its membership, it...', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES— Ladies and gentleman, it’s almost time to call it. The American Cinema Editors announced its nominees on Friday, and because an editing award is considered a must for a best picture win – no film has received the top prize without at least an editing nomination since 1981 – and because ACE has a lot of crossover with Academy in its membership, its choices are an excellent predictor. Here’s who to favor in your Oscar pool: “Black Swan,” “The Fighter,” “Inception,” “The King’s Speech” and “The Social Network.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oscar Pool Intelligence: The Editors Speak', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T15:56:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf767055-ec5d-5ab1-b7a5-5c51b5422cd3', 'word_count': 237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf767055-ec5d-5ab1-b7a5-5c51b5422cd3'}, {'abstract': 'How to help a mother who has supported others and now needs some support herself?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/the-mom-blog-jinx/', 'snippet': 'How to help a mother who has supported others and now needs some support herself?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I wonder periodically if there is some sort of mom-blog jinx. I was already an avid reader of blogs even before I was asked to create Motherlode, and now scrolling through several dozen of them each day is my professional routine. When you read so many you can’t help noticing that very bad things have happened to a striking number of their authors. Some of this, of course, is because the Internet is now the place to turn when parents are struggling. That would explain many of the blogs that were started after — after a father was paralyzed, or a mother died in childbirth or a husband drowned.But what strikes me is the number of times that tragedy found a blogger who just happened to be blogging. Stephanie Nielsen wrote mostly about life as the mother of four children until she and her husband were nearly killed, and Stephanie was horrifyingly burned, in a plane crash.\xa0Heather Armstrong was writing about work when she was blindsided by postpartum depression and blogged her way through a psychiatric hospitalization. The comic\xa0Daphne Brogdon’s blog was about her blended family, until she lost her life savings to Bernie Madoff.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Mom Blog Jinx', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T15:57:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Belkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c17b20d6-426b-5af4-b279-f03c370e4851', 'word_count': 2319, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c17b20d6-426b-5af4-b279-f03c370e4851'}, {'abstract': 'Atlanta wins games quietly and efficiently. If this has led to the Falcons flying under the national radar, so be it.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/falcons-have-an-identity-its-called-winning/', 'snippet': 'Atlanta wins games quietly and efficiently. If this has led to the Falcons flying under the national radar, so be it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paul Johnson is a longtime Fifth Down commenter. He answered a request to assess the Falcons, the perception of the Falcons and the mood of the Atlanta area. He lives in Decatur, Ga. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Falcons Have an Identity. It’s Called Winning.', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Atlanta Falcons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T16:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d7c38bd-8e8c-58e0-be24-c28d276922ad', 'word_count': 1068, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d7c38bd-8e8c-58e0-be24-c28d276922ad'}, {'abstract': 'More Americans say China is the world’s leading economic power than say the United States is. We say: Buck up, Americans!', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/the-irrational-awe-of-china/', 'snippet': 'More Americans say China is the world’s leading economic power than say the United States is. We say: Buck up, Americans!', 'lead_paragraph': 'With China’s President, Hu Jintao, coming to the United States next week, Andrew Kohut of the Pew Research Center reports:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Irrational Awe of China', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T16:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/92d44461-df05-5739-942b-dc4ee1c4bb7e', 'word_count': 464, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92d44461-df05-5739-942b-dc4ee1c4bb7e'}, {'abstract': 'PTACs — for package terminal air-conditioners — can be an ugly intrusion, but they also allow residents to control temperature room by room.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/realestate/16posting.html', 'snippet': 'PTACs — for package terminal air-conditioners — can be an ugly intrusion, but they also allow residents to control temperature room by room.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE Pencil Factory, a new condominium building in Greenpoint, Brooklyn, has many selling points. Its public spaces include a crisply contemporary lobby, a spacious gym and a roof terrace with views across the East River to Manhattan that elicit gasps. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '8', 