International Auto Show', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T16:08:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'subsection_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Moran', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moran', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cc85f4e-45da-51a7-a2f2-e0205ab33436', 'word_count': 666, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3cc85f4e-45da-51a7-a2f2-e0205ab33436'}, {'abstract': 'After initially resisting ticket resellers, many teams have allied with them, making it easier for season-ticket holders to resell their tickets and recoup some money.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/sports/15tickets.html', 'snippet': 'After initially resisting ticket resellers, many teams have allied with them, making it easier for season-ticket holders to resell their tickets and recoup some money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One old saw debunked recently was that pro sports are impervious to recessions. During the past few years, attendance has fallen in nearly every league, several franchises declared bankruptcy and a few more were sold for less than expected. Teams cut ticket prices, sold hot dogs for $1 and did anything they could to get customers in the door.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As Economy Sagged, Online Sports Ticket Market Soared', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Economy Has Sagged, the Online Ticket Market Has Taken Flight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ticket Scalping', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'eBay Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'StubHub', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T16:12:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9a78631-5cff-550a-ad89-6540924ff7f4', 'word_count': 959, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9a78631-5cff-550a-ad89-6540924ff7f4'}, {'abstract': 'In the cosseted world of celebrity journalism, the awards-show red carpet is trench warfare.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/poparazzi-carpet-diem/', 'snippet': 'In the cosseted world of celebrity journalism, the awards-show red carpet is trench warfare.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the Romans had wanted to model a gladiatorial event on the traditional wedding reception receiving line, they might have come up with something like the awards-ceremony red carpet. In this brutal contest, two opposing teams face off against each other with competing agendas, borrowed tuxedos and tight smiles concealing deep animosities.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poparazzi | Carpet Diem', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T16:15:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Widdicombe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Widdicombe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/89