ontent_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Identity Dyed in the Wool', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Garment District (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T16:52:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By C. J. Hughes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Hughes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2e8225a-9bfd-5cf1-ba3f-e5f0d6987d2a', 'word_count': 1644, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2e8225a-9bfd-5cf1-ba3f-e5f0d6987d2a'}, {'abstract': 'Like Joyce’s Dubliners, many of the characters in these stories can’t escape their homeland.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/Prose-t.html', 'snippet': 'Like Joyce’s Dubliners, many of the characters in these stories can’t escape their homeland.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Why does the short story lend itself so naturally to the muted but still shattering sentiments of yearning, nostalgia and regret? How many William Trevor tales focus on the moment when a heart is broken or at least badly chipped? Though Mavis Gallant’s work bristles with barbed wit and trenchant social observation, her most moving stories often pivot on romantic ruptures and repressed attraction.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/PROSE/PROSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 732, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/PROSE/PROSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/PROSE/PROSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stories From an Irish Master', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Small Moments', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Toibin, Colm', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T16:57:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francine Prose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Prose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdbe06fa-562e-5b9b-a747-a1f263b1d2a1', 'word_count': 1170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdbe06fa-562e-5b9b-a747-a1f263b1d2a1'}, {'abstract': '“In the parking lot next to the bank, Harry Edmonds saw a piece of gray scrap paper the size of a greeting card”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/excerpt-gryphon.html', 'snippet': '“In the parking lot next to the bank, Harry Edmonds saw a piece of gray scrap paper the size of a greeting card”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Next Building I Plan to Bomb', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Gryphon’', 'kicker': 'Excerpt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T16:58:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Baxter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baxter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'First Chapter', '_id': 'nyt://article/bef4ec1e-bcfb-5f0b-a2de-8b0f767f7fa8', 'word_count': 2491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bef4ec1e-bcfb-5f0b-a2de-8b0f767f7fa8'}, {'abstract': 'Starkness and human isolation lurk beneath the Norman Rockwell contours of Charles Baxter’s Midwest stories.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/Oates-t.html', 'snippet': 'Starkness and human isolation lurk beneath the Norman Rockwell contours of Charles Baxter’s Midwest stories.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reflecting our dazzlingly diverse culture, the contemporary American short story is virtually impossible to define. Where once the “well crafted” short story in the revered tradition of Henry James, Anton Chekhov and James Joyce was the predominant literary model — an essentially realist tradition, subtle in construction and inward rather than dramatic — now the more typical story is likely to be a first-person narration, or monologue: more akin to nonliterary sources like stand-up comedy, performance art, movies and rap music and blogs. Such prose pieces showcase distinctive “voices” as if fictional characters, long restrained by the highly polished language of their creators, have broken free to speak directly and sometimes aggressively to the reader — as in boldly vernacular stories by Junot Díaz, Chuck Palahniuk, Edwidge Danticat, George Saunders, John Edgar Wideman, Denis Johnson and T. C. Boyle, among others. (Yet Edgar Allan Poe, as long ago as 1843, brilliantly gave voice to the manic and utterly convincing murderer of “The Tell-Tale Heart” — perhaps genius is always our contemporary.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/OATES/OATES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/OATES/OATES-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/OATES/OATES-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Charles Baxter’s Midwest', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Small Moments', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baxter, Charles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T16:58:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce Carol Oates', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': 'Carol', 'lastname': 'Oates', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9173636-e408-55a5-a085-e28f76e224f5', 'word_count': 1947, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9173636-e408-55a5-a085-e28f76e224f5'}, {'abstract': 'Roxana Robinson has written three story collections, to go along with four novels and a biography of Georgia O’Keeffe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/Upfront-t.html', 'snippet': 'Roxana Robinson has written three story collections, to go along with four novels and a biography of Georgia O’Keeffe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Roxana Robinson, who reviews Edith Pearlman’s collection “Binocular Vision” as part of our cover package this week, has written three story collections of her own, to go along with four novels and a biography of Georgia O’Keeffe.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/16upfront/16upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 593, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/16upfront/16upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/16upfront/16upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front: Roxana Robinson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robinson, Roxana', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T16:58:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40609802-ff42-5922-adc1-b6153837e21c', 'word_count': 215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40609802-ff42-5922-adc1-b6153837e21c'}, {'abstract': 'Edith Pearlman’s view of the world is large and compassionate, delivered in these stories through small, beautifully precise moments.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/Robinson-t.html', 'snippet': 'Edith Pearlman’s view of the world is large and compassionate, delivered in these stories through small, beautifully precise moments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Why in the world had I never heard of Edith Pearlman? And why, if you hadn’t, hadn’t you? It certainly isn’t the fault of her writing, which is intelligent, perceptive, funny and quite beautiful, as demonstrated in “Binocular Vision: New and Selected Stories.” In the world of literary fiction Pearlman is hardly unknown: she’s the author of three previous collections, “Vaquita,” “Love Among the Greats” and “How to Fall”; she has won several prizes; and her work has appeared repeatedly in “Best American Short Stories.” So she should be known all over the place.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/ROBINSON/ROBINSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 695, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/ROBINSON/ROBINSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/ROBINSON/ROBINSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stories of Dislocation and Relocation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Small Moments', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pearlman, Edith', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T16:58:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roxana Robinson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roxana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robinson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/27c123b0-4585-5bb7-8802-0c65548d41cb', 'word_count': 1283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27c123b0-4585-5bb7-8802-0c65548d41cb'}, {'abstract': 'A pair of French writers debate the universe and their places in it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/Buruma-t.html', 'snippet': 'A pair of French writers debate the universe and their places in it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One way to read this book, a dialogue between two famous French authors, is as a comic novel, a brilliant satire on the vanity of writers. Michel Houellebecq, who won last year’s Prix Goncourt, France’s highest literary award, for his latest novel, “La Carte et le Territoire,” is well known for his provocative black humor. Bernard-Henri Lévy (also known as BHL), though less noted for his wit, likes to play up to his reputation as a comic figure, popping up here, there and everywhere in his fine white shirts, opened halfway down his chest, holding forth on everything from Jean-Paul Sartre to jihad in Pakistan, and generally acting out the role, in a somewhat theatrical fashion, of the great Parisian Intellectual.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/BOOK/BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/BOOK/BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/BOOK/BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 462, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/12/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Guys From Paris', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Guys From Paris', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Levy, Bernard-Henri', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Houellebecq, Michel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T16:58:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Buruma', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buruma', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f370761f-705d-5e90-9ed0-93a30f6873be', 'word_count': 1030, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f370761f-705d-5e90-9ed0-93a30f6873be'}, {'abstract': 'Robb Forman Dew’s novel, set mostly in the 1950s, continues the story of her character Agnes Scofield, a widowed schoolteacher, and her circle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/Wolitzer-t.html', 'snippet': 'Robb Forman Dew’s novel, set mostly in the 1950s, continues the story of her character Agnes Scofield, a widowed schoolteacher, and her circle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If novels were textbooks, this highly original one might be called “America in the 1950s: A Time of Existential Anxiety and Busywork for Women.” Robb Forman Dew covers both the cosmic and the quotidian as she follows a formidably intermingled group of people in the town of Washburn, Ohio. The novel, which resides mostly in the 1950s until an acceleration near the end yanks everyone all the way up to 1973, beautifully chronicles the experiences of a widowed schoolteacher, Agnes Scofield, and those in her midst.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Wolitzer/Wolitzer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 789, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Wolitzer/Wolitzer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Wolitzer/Wolitzer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Woman’s Work', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Woman’s Work', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Fifties', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dew, Robb Forman', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T16:58:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Meg Wolitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wolitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/143555c6-1759-56be-a482-e104e80aced5', 'word_count': 988, 'uri': 'nyt://article/143555c6-1759-56be-a482-e104e80aced5'}, {'abstract': 'The life of Coco Chanel as seen through her famous perfume.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/Jenkins-t.html', 'snippet': 'The life of Coco Chanel as seen through her famous perfume.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Each 30-milliliter bottle of Chanel No. 5 is packed with the essence of a thousand jasmine flowers, the fragrance of a dozen May roses from Grasse and a heaping dose of aldehydes, the molecules that early on gave the scent its modern edge. But as Tilar J. Mazzeo points out in “The Secret of Chanel No. 5,” these are only a few of the many ingredients that turned a perfume into a 20th-century obsession. Into the brew went Coco Chanel’s warm memories of tallow soap and her anguish over lost love, as well as the perfumer Ernest Beaux’s nostalgia for imperial Russia and the Arctic breezes of the White Sea. The result: alchemical magic and unprecedented sales that have led industry insiders to refer to No. 5 as le monstre — the monster.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/SUBB-JENKINS/SUBB-JENKINS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 769, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/SUBB-JENKINS/SUBB-JENKINS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/SUBB-JENKINS/SUBB-JENKINS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Following the Scent', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Following the Scent', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Perfumes and Fragrances', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chanel, Coco', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mazzeo, Tilar J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T16:59:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Kerwin Jenkins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': 'Kerwin', 'lastname': 'Jenkins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a44f3f81-a503-5cfc-a059-c88a3397848f', 'word_count': 790, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a44f3f81-a503-5cfc-a059-c88a3397848f'}, {'abstract': 'A New York Times culture reporter \xadattempts to understand his father’s addiction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/Myerson-t.html', 'snippet': 'A New York Times culture reporter \xadattempts to understand his father’s addiction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dave Itzkoff was 8 years old when he discovered that the father he could never rely on — brimming with loquacious energy one moment, losing his temper the next — was in fact a drug addict. “He’s been addicted to cocaine almost your whole life,” his mother informs him at last, explaining why she’s filing for divorce.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Meyerson/Meyerson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 720, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Addict in the Family', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Addict in the Family', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Itzkoff, Dave', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cocaine and Crack Cocaine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T16:59:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Myerson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Myerson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7733aad7-8824-583e-b35d-582a983e17ef', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7733aad7-8824-583e-b35d-582a983e17ef'}, {'abstract': 'With so much loving going around for vampires, this collected edition was kind of inevitable: at no. 1 on the hardcover list this week is the first volume of the “Vampirella Archives.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/graphic-books-best-seller-lists-vampire-love/', 'snippet': 'With so much loving going around for vampires, this collected edition was kind of inevitable: at no. 1 on the hardcover list this week is the first volume of the “Vampirella Archives.”', 'lead_paragraph': ' With so much loving going around for vampires, this collected edition was kind of inevitable: at No. 1 on the hardcover list this week is the first volume of the “Vampirella Archives,” published by Dynamite Entertainment. The earliest adventures of Vampirella, a good creature of the night who combats evil bloodsuckers, are contained within and date back to 1969. Her very first cover was by Frank Frazetta and the writers and artists who chronicled her stories are the best of the best: Archie Goodwin, Bernie Wrightson, Wally Wood, Neal Adams and more.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Graphic Books Best-Seller Lists: Vampire Love', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'DC Comics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T17:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8e53f34-c97d-5ae7-b6fe-2bb3f60dd2e2', 'word_count': 321, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8e53f34-c97d-5ae7-b6fe-2bb3f60dd2e2'}, {'abstract': 'Peter L. Bergen examines Al Qaeda’s history and motives and America’s ineffective response.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/Ricks-t.html', 'snippet': 'Peter L. Bergen examines Al Qaeda’s history and motives and America’s ineffective response.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For years, I tried to read every new novel about how 9/11 affected our lives. Some were very thoughtful, but I always came away unsatisfied, feeling that the authors had worked hard but had somehow fallen short. As I read the stunning first section of Peter L. Bergen’s new book on the war between the United States and Al Qaeda, I realized I had been looking in the wrong genre. None of the novels were as effective or moving as “The Longest War,” which is a history of our time. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Ricks/Ricks-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Ricks/Ricks-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Ricks/Ricks-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 704, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Ricks/Ricks-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Ricks/Ricks-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Determined to Strike', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Determined to Strike', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T17:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas E. Ricks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Ricks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/368ad9ee-1a81-5eba-b6ed-0b7d6ad3e78f', 'word_count': 1147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/368ad9ee-1a81-5eba-b6ed-0b7d6ad3e78f'}, {'abstract': '“I slide a fishing rod into my kayak as birds begin gathering over our bay.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/excerpt-view-from-lazy-point.html', 'snippet': '“I slide a fishing rod into my kayak as birds begin gathering over our bay.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Prelude', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The View From Lazy Point’', 'kicker': 'Excerpt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T17:07:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Safina', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Safina', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'First Chapter', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0562c14-b9dd-548d-be2e-657ae2d29cbf', 'word_count': 2072, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0562c14-b9dd-548d-be2e-657ae2d29cbf'}, {'abstract': 'From his cottage near Montauk Point, a naturalist explains Earth’s perilous times and delivers an ambitious meditation on nature’s connectedness.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/Browning-t.html', 'snippet': 'From his cottage near Montauk Point, a naturalist explains Earth’s perilous times and delivers an ambitious meditation on nature’s connectedness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This has been a dismal year for the health of our planet. Evidence of human-caused catastrophe mounts daily with grim reports from sea, sky and land: disappearing species, the collapse of fisheries, \xaddeforestation, the shrinking ozone layer, higher concentrations of atmospheric carbon dioxide, oceanic dead zones, warming temperatures, extreme weather, rising sea levels, depleted aquifers, melting glaciers, thawing perma\xadfrost. We have already crossed into an unimaginable new epoch, but we seem unable to unite behind efforts to change, or even slow, our disastrous course.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Browning-t_CA2/Browning-t_CA2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 753, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Browning-t_CA2/Browning-t_CA2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Browning-t_CA2/Browning-t_CA2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Delicate Planet', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Delicate Planet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Safina, Carl', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Montauk (NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T17:07:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dominique Browning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dominique', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/73544c0a-af37-583f-b88f-a306f068775e', 'word_count': 1274, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73544c0a-af37-583f-b88f-a306f068775e'}, {'abstract': 'Perhaps upstaging the notable automotive designers with whom they shared the stage, Pixar’s Jay Schuster and Brad Lewis revealed details of their forthcoming sequel to 2006’s “Cars.”', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/pixars-cars-2-is-previewed-at-detroit-auto-show/', 'snippet': 'Perhaps upstaging the notable automotive designers with whom they shared the stage, Pixar’s Jay Schuster and Brad Lewis revealed details of their forthcoming sequel to 2006’s “Cars.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'After Detroit auto show press previews conclude, the AutoWeek Design Forum commands the spotlight.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pixar’s ‘Cars 2’ Is Previewed at Detroit Auto Show', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMATED FILMS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'North American International Auto Show', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Owen', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pixar Animation Studios', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T17:09:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phil Patton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Patton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8d29eb3-b9fe-5e82-aa68-ab3cf57c8c43', 'word_count': 335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8d29eb3-b9fe-5e82-aa68-ab3cf57c8c43'}, {'abstract': 'Ian Falconer’s rambunctious piglet heroine is back, leading her family on a spree through the City of Bridges.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/Bruder-t.html', 'snippet': 'Ian Falconer’s rambunctious piglet heroine is back, leading her family on a spree through the City of Bridges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If pigs could fly, they’d vacation in Italy. Or at least Olivia would. After a hiatus of several years, Ian Falconer’s rambunctious, curly-tailed heroine is back, and — look out, world! — this time she has a passport. In her latest escapade, “Olivia Goes to Venice,” the piglet leads her family on a spree through the City of Bridges, with frequent pit stops for gelato. They wander among palazzos and canals. They enjoy a gondola ride. They get mobbed by a Hitchcockian flock of pigeons in the Piazza San Marco. Through it all, Olivia runs the show, until her search for the perfect souvenir quite literally brings down the house.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Bruder/Bruder-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 389, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Bruder/Bruder-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 389}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Bruder/Bruder-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Bruder/Bruder-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/Bruder/Bruder-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pig With Passport', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pig With Passport', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pigs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Falconer, Ian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T17:09:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Bruder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4edc0b6f-c7a3-59b7-b1da-a6483f677e95', 'word_count': 540, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4edc0b6f-c7a3-59b7-b1da-a6483f677e95'}, {'abstract': 'This historical fantasy for teenagers follows the adventures of an orphaned girl in a world modeled on 16th-century Poland and Lithuania.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/Posesorksi-t.html', 'snippet': 'This historical fantasy for teenagers follows the adventures of an orphaned girl in a world modeled on 16th-century Poland and Lithuania.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I’ve suffered a loss,” a man says in “Plain Kate,” a historical fantasy for teenagers by Erin Bow. Truly, madly, deeply felt loss saturates the novel, scarring its characters, none more so than Bow’s orphaned heroine, Plain Kate.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Curses in the Air', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Curses in the Air', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T17:09:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sherie Posesorski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sherie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Posesorski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ab1db63-b3ce-5f13-9623-d5969f1dca9e', 'word_count': 743, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ab1db63-b3ce-5f13-9623-d5969f1dca9e'}, {'abstract': 'This book introduces Martha Graham to children with reverence and finesse, recounting how she created the modern-dance classic “Appalachian Spring.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/McDonald-t.html', 'snippet': 'This book introduces Martha Graham to children with reverence and finesse, recounting how she created the modern-dance classic “Appalachian Spring.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was a 9-year-old philistine. Sure, as a dance student I knew my Tchaikovsky and my Petipa. I’d even heard of Stravinsky and Balanchine. But I’d never heard of Martha Graham — not until the day I was made to enter a studio, bare my feet, plant my derrière firmly on the floor and contract my stomach, intensely and repeatedly, in a bout of abdominal torture. After that experience? I wasn’t sure I wanted to know this Graham person.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/McDonald/McDonald-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 975, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/McDonald/McDonald-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/McDonald/McDonald-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rhythms of a New Land', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rhythms of a New Land', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Copland, Aaron', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Noguchi, Isamu', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenberg, Jan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Floca, Brian', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jordan, Sandra', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Graham, Martha', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T17:09:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer B. McDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/468329c7-48b9-5af2-9836-e034a581869e', 'word_count': 663, 'uri': 'nyt://article/468329c7-48b9-5af2-9836-e034a581869e'}, {'abstract': 'One hundred years ago, a libel accusation leveled at a famous novelist ended in the most spectacular crime in American literary history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/Duffy-t.html', 'snippet': 'One hundred years ago, a libel accusation leveled at a famous novelist ended in the most spectacular crime in American literary history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s easy to imagine how a novelist might use a real person as a basis for a fictional character. It’s equally easy to imagine how such a person could notice the similarities and perhaps become offended. After all, the fiction writer has pledged an oath to serve a calling higher than mere feelings. Why should F. Scott Fitzgerald worry over the sensitivities of Max Gerlach in creating Jay Gatsby?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/DUFFY-1294256610036/DUFFY-1294256610036-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 734, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/DUFFY-1294256610036/DUFFY-1294256610036-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/books/review/DUFFY-1294256610036/DUFFY-1294256610036-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Deadliest Book Review', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Character Assassination', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Phillips, David Graham', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libel and Slander', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T17:11:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Duffy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Duffy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7720d926-b0e7-5773-886e-5783708234fa', 'word_count': 1256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7720d926-b0e7-5773-886e-5783708234fa'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/books/review/EdChoice-t.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MARSHALL MCLUHAN: You Know Nothing of My Work!,  by Douglas Coupland (Atlas, $24.) Coupland takes a pop-culture approach to the man known for the phrase “The medium is the message,” a deep thinker who ended up popularized almost in spite of himself.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T17:12:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b46c9a1-9644-5fbb-9296-f4c7f1fb6229', 'word_count': 289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b46c9a1-9644-5fbb-9296-f4c7f1fb6229'}, {'abstract': 'Hubert Dreyfus and Sean Dorrance Kelly’s “All Things Shining” lands on the nonfiction list — not bad for two philosophy professors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/books/review/InsideList-t.html', 'snippet': 'Hubert Dreyfus and Sean Dorrance Kelly’s “All Things Shining” lands on the nonfiction list — not bad for two philosophy professors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DEEP THOUGHTS: Hubert Dreyfus and Sean Dorrance Kelly’s “All Things Shining” lands at No. 22 on the hardcover nonfiction extended list — not bad for two philosophy professors who aim to restore meaning to contemporary life through close encounters with classic Western texts.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/23insidelist/23insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/23insidelist/23insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the