too many pre-fall collections (are they over yet?), but everyone seems ticked off about something in fashion.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/are-you-mad-too/', 'snippet': 'Mabye people are getting back to their pre-Christmas fighting weight, or they’re just peevish from too many pre-fall collections (are they over yet?), but everyone seems ticked off about something in fashion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maybe people are getting back to their pre-Christmas fighting weight, or they’re just peevish from too many pre-fall collections (are they over yet?), but everyone seems ticked off about something in fashion. I love it. There’s actually a lot to debate — though from the tone of some of the remarks, I hear what almost sounds like the us-and-them rhetoric of liberal and conservative pundits. Will I play Maddow to your Beck? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are You Mad, Too?', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McQueen, Alexander', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T19:02:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/76760f13-2640-554e-ae83-f682886e5cd2', 'word_count': 736, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76760f13-2640-554e-ae83-f682886e5cd2'}, {'abstract': 'Eva Gabrielsson talks about the book in a new memoir.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/partner-of-stieg-larsson-wants-to-complete-fourth-millennium-novel/', 'snippet': 'Eva Gabrielsson talks about the book in a new memoir.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eva Gabrielsson, the longtime partner of the Swedish novelist Stieg Larsson, says in a new memoir that she wants to secure the rights to complete Mr. Larsson’s fourth novel in the “Millennium” series, which he was writing when he died suddenly in 2004. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Partner of Stieg Larsson Wants to Complete Fourth ‘Millennium’ Novel', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Larsson, Stieg', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sweden', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T19:04:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/58c0e01d-c120-59ab-8375-0ec9cf02637a', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58c0e01d-c120-59ab-8375-0ec9cf02637a'}, {'abstract': 'Readers weigh in on whether free checking accounts will disappear entirely.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/free-checking-and-its-future/', 'snippet': 'Readers weigh in on whether free checking accounts will disappear entirely.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this weekend’s “Your Money” column, I try to unpack a curious statement from Chase Bank about why it decided to add monthly fees on many of its checking accounts. Let’s just repeat it here for emphasis:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Free Checking and Its Future', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T19:06:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Lieber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lieber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/43dbd441-0dbd-5031-948e-76520a747800', 'word_count': 165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43dbd441-0dbd-5031-948e-76520a747800'}, {'abstract': 'The new three-year deal for the tennis tournament maintains the current schedule, including the Saturday night prime-time women’s final.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/sports/tennis/15open.html', 'snippet': 'The new three-year deal for the tennis tournament maintains the current schedule, including the Saturday night prime-time women’s final.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The United States Open tennis tournament will stay on CBS through 2014 for a slight increase in rights payments under an agreement in principle with the United States Tennis Association.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/15/sports/open/open-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 276, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/15/sports/open/open-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 276}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/15/sports/open/open-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 471, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/15/sports/open/open-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/15/sports/open/open-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Open to Stay on CBS Through 2014', 'kicker': 'Sports Briefing | Tennis ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'CBS Retains U.S. Open', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corp', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McManus, Sean', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T19:09:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/898a2df7-0fd3-59fe-b994-0d2d117a39bd', 'word_count': 506, 'uri': 'nyt://article/898a2df7-0fd3-59fe-b994-0d2d117a39bd'}, {'abstract': 'The Vook is to video reading what the Kindle is to reading reading.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/magazine/16FOB-medium-t.html', 'snippet': 'The Vook is to video reading what the Kindle is to reading reading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A deep fantasy of most readers is that their books will one day come to life. Of course there’s the college-reader hope that reality itself will come to shimmer with the intense meaningfulness of books. But you get over that. The wish that remains is less romantic: the longing for a byte of audio here and there, among the pages of text, so you could hear what Uriah Heep really sounds like when he says, “ ’Umble to this person, and ’umble to that” in “David Copperfield,” or a video clip so you could get a sense of Jack Ryan’s appearance in “Patriot Games.” What if you could give your imagination a break and watch a clip of Harrison Ford’s Jack Ryan and be done with it?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16FOB-medium-span/16FOB-medium-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16FOB-medium-span/16FOB-medium-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16FOB-medium-span/16FOB-medium-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16FOB-medium-span/16FOB-medium-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16FOB-medium-span/16FOB-medium-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Watch Me, Read Me', 'kicker': 'The Medium', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Watch Me, Read Me', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Handheld Computers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronic Books and Readers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vook', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T19:13:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Virginia Heffernan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Virginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heffernan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/715c1aac-6b6c-5750-85b8-abd9f7515d6a', 'word_count': 1034, 'uri': 'nyt://article/715c1aac-6b6c-5750-85b8-abd9f7515d6a'}, {'abstract': 'When your eccentric California neighbor is actually very disturbed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/magazine/16lives-t.html', 'snippet': 'When your eccentric California neighbor is actually very disturbed.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Before we took the apartment on Rialto — a palm-tree-and-bougainvillea-lined avenue a few blocks from the Pacific — the landlord told me about her. He said there was “a kind of daffy older lady” who’d be living above us. He said she was like “the concierge of the building” and had been there a few decades but sometimes drank too much and every so often would “yell.” I was desperate for an apartment in Venice Beach, and I wanted our lives to start rebounding from our disastrous relocation from Maine, but that stopped me in my tracks. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16lives/16lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 900, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16lives/16lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16lives/16lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Crazy Lady Upstairs', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'THE CRAZY LADY UPSTAIRS', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T19:13:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caitlin Shetterly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caitlin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shetterly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aef5b8a9-018b-5fb7-9cc8-8dfa9e8d64d0', 'word_count': 806, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aef5b8a9-018b-5fb7-9cc8-8dfa9e8d64d0'}, {'abstract': 'Antitheft advice; a tipping tip.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/magazine/16FOB-Ethicist-t.html', 'snippet': 'Antitheft advice; a tipping tip.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Traveling in Poland, I visited antique stores offering Jewish items — menorahs, mezuzas — that seemed more than 65 years old, suggesting that they were looted in the Holocaust. I saw things I wished to make part of my own Jewish home but found myself unable to pay for what was probably stolen property. Part of me wishes I had stolen (liberated?) some of them. Would that have been justified? RANDY MALAMUD, ATLANTA', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/03/18/magazine/ethicist75/ethicist75-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2010/03/18/magazine/ethicist75/ethicist75-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tragic Artifacts', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tragic Artifacts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Looting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T19:13:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/bcb2ac56-3df0-58c3-9725-379538324407', 'word_count': 694, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bcb2ac56-3df0-58c3-9725-379538324407'}, {'abstract': 'Does WikiLeaks need a better visual image?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/magazine/16fob-consumed-t.html', 'snippet': 'Does WikiLeaks need a better visual image?', 'lead_paragraph': 'What image comes to mind when you think of Wiki\xadLeaks? Some see it as heroic, others as destructive. Either way, the release of formerly classified documents and diplomatic cables over the last six months have helped transform the site and its founder, Julian Assange, into known entities. But while thinking of Apple or Facebook or even the 2008 Obama campaign calls up certain aesthetic associations or shorthand imagery, WikiLeaks mostly brings to mind the image of Assange’s wan face and silver hair. Perhaps, as Daniel van der Velden of the design studio Metahaven argues, WikiLeaks ought to re-evaluate its “visual identity.” “Does it matter what it looks like?” he asked in a talk sponsored by the Graphic Design Museum in the Netherlands. “Maybe it does.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16fob-consumed/16fob-consumed-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 207, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16fob-consumed/16fob-consumed-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16fob-consumed/16fob-consumed-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Branding Transparency', 'kicker': 'Consumed', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Branding Transparency', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'WikiLeaks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classified Information and State Secrets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T19:13:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Walker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Walker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d76343a0-03b8-5c97-bd6f-8814bf6a5915', 'word_count': 835, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d76343a0-03b8-5c97-bd6f-8814bf6a5915'}, {'abstract': 'Despite the financial woes, Sin City’s mayor tries to keep the good times rolling.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/magazine/16FOB-Q4-t.html', 'snippet': 'Despite the financial woes, Sin City’s mayor tries to keep the good times rolling.', 'lead_paragraph': ' After nearly 12 years as the mayor of Las Vegas, you hardly need to be reminded that what happens in Vegas stays in Vegas. That is, except for families. Nevada has the highest home-foreclosure rate in the country, and the median home price in the Las Vegas area has fallen to $138,000, from $317,000 in 2006. I’m the kind of guy who makes lemonade out of lemons, and I think it’s a terrific opportunity. I’m not sure what you can get for that amount, but you can certainly get a beautiful home with a swimming pool in a gated community for under $200,000. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16FOB-Q4/16FOB-Q4-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16FOB-Q4/16FOB-Q4-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/magazine/16FOB-Q4/16FOB-Q4-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Happens in Vegas', 'kicker': 'Questions for Oscar B. Goodman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Happens in Vegas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Las Vegas (Nev)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goodman, Oscar B', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T19:13:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Deborah Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Deborah', 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/f99db8cb-6757-5587-a9ff-a4a8d6bcf59c', 'word_count': 709, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f99db8cb-6757-5587-a9ff-a4a8d6bcf59c'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to the Jan 2. issue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/magazine/16letters-t-MYFUTURISTIC_LETTERS.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to the Jan 2. issue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Melanie Thernstrom’s account of her difficult road to creating a family was truly heartwarming. For those of us blessed with easy conception, the grief endured by infertile couples is unknowable. Having lost a child to an accident early in my childbearing years, I don’t know how I would have coped if I had not been able to conceive again. Thernstrom was able to give voice and feeling to something that most people cannot imagine. I applaud her openness and her ability to have us see both the clinical and emotional sides of reproductive technology. She has done a great service to all couples valiantly working toward becoming parents.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letters: My Futuristic Insta-Family', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'My Futuristic Insta-Family', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surrogate Motherhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infertility', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T19:13:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3360341-2770-55f9-bff8-b2b022119a0f', 'word_count': 932, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3360341-2770-55f9-bff8-b2b022119a0f'}, {'abstract': 'While prices in Brooklyn have bounced back from the housing crash faster than in Manhattan, and prices in some neighborhoods have crept back up toward boom levels, the high-end market now has more competition.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/big-deal-a-tougher-sell-in-brownstone-brooklyn/', 'snippet': 'While prices in Brooklyn have bounced back from the housing crash faster than in Manhattan, and prices in some neighborhoods have crept back up toward boom levels, the high-end market now has more competition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some people have speculated that a town house at 29 Grace Court in Brooklyn Heights on the market for $5.95 million is not selling because it is too pink.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Deal | A Tougher Sell in Brownstone Brooklyn', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brownstones', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T19:18:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Kershaw', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershaw', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4522da24-c467-52de-9d91-34a1eccbf2d9', 'word_count': 1087, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4522da24-c467-52de-9d91-34a1eccbf2d9'}, {'abstract': 'Here is the statement made by Prime Minister Mohamed Ghannouchi of Tunisia on the country’s state television network on Friday, as recorded and translated from Arabic by The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/world/africa/15tunistext.html', 'snippet': 'Here is the statement made by Prime Minister Mohamed Ghannouchi of Tunisia on the country’s state television network on Friday, as recorded and translated from Arabic by The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here is the statement made by Prime Minister Mohamed Ghannouchi of Tunisia on the country’s state television network on Friday, as recorded and translated from Arabic by The New York Times.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Transcript of Tunisian Prime Minister’s Announcement 