oduction-intended Veloster, aims at an increasingly disillusioned youth market.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/hyundai-curb-hopes-to-join-youth-brigade/', 'snippet': 'The concept, like the brand’s production-intended Veloster, aims at an increasingly disillusioned youth market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Introduced on Monday: Hyundai Curb concept', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hyundai Curb Hopes to Join Youth Brigade', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'North American International Auto Show', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hyundai Motor Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T19:57:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Moran', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moran', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bba2d71-5702-57f9-ba4e-92c52c9b76b9', 'word_count': 297, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bba2d71-5702-57f9-ba4e-92c52c9b76b9'}, {'abstract': 'Imagining the top N.B.A. point guards in some other job illuminates the way in which they go about their business.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/if-they-werent-point-guards-theyd-be/', 'snippet': 'Imagining the top N.B.A. point guards in some other job illuminates the way in which they go about their business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every good company needs leaders in the workplace, just as every basketball team needs an astute floor general. Point guards are the guys on the court who make the first move, the domino tippers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'If They Weren’t Point Guards, They’d Be …', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Billups, Chauncey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harris, Devin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Irving, Kyrie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kidd, Jason', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nash, Steve', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parker, Tony', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Chris', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rondo, Rajon', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rose, Derrick', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Deron', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T20:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jake Appleman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appleman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/357fd37d-7e3a-51a3-912c-308a750c4689', 'word_count': 1042, 'uri': 'nyt://article/357fd37d-7e3a-51a3-912c-308a750c4689'}, {'abstract': 'Arvind Subramanian of the Peterson Institute for International Economics argues that China’s economy is bigger than the American economy.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/on-the-size-of-chinas-economy-a-dissenter/', 'snippet': 'Arvind Subramanian of the Peterson Institute for International Economics argues that China’s economy is bigger than the American economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In my post on China and the United States earlier today, I was careful to say that, by most measures, the United States still has by far the largest economy in the world. The International Monetary Fund, for instance, estimates gross domestic product in the United States to have been about $14.6 trillion last year, while China’s was $10.1 trillion. The World Bank estimates a similar gap.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Size of China’s Economy, a Dissenter', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T20:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/37cf52ac-66a9-5fa6-83f0-f6148210c0c3', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37cf52ac-66a9-5fa6-83f0-f6148210c0c3'}, {'abstract': 'The new N.B.A. commercial starring Kevin Durant is a misguided attempt to market the league.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/n-b-a-s-new-commercial-misfires/', 'snippet': 'The new N.B.A. commercial starring Kevin Durant is a misguided attempt to market the league.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The N.B.A.’s latest marketing foray not only lacks the charm of previous endeavors, but actually borders on creepy in its attempt at capturing the surreal. At the risk of oversimplifying, it doesn’t seem to have much of a point. We, the viewing audience, dive into Kevin Durant’s past to watch an apparent time traveler pretend to inform Durant of his eventual N.B.A. fate. In the lack of real purpose, we’re left to focus solely on the commercial’s aesthetic, which is as bizarre as it is false.It’s silly to ask how we -– along with our mystery traveler -– are transported to Rockville, Maryland circa 2005. After all, beyond a typical suspension of disbelief that accompanies the commercial medium, television ads are typically constructed in a serialized manner. In Television Culture, media scholar John Fiske described the serialized form as “an infinitely extended middle,” and in a sense, that’s where these spots take (and leave) us. The year 2005 isn’t a beginning in any sense for Durant, and we’re not privileged to see the process by which our traveler makes his way into Durant’s past. There’s also no real attempt at ending or closure, marking the images we see as some sort of enduring middleness, a journey which we join in progress. If nothing else, such a structure reinforces that this tale we’re witnessing is hardly our own. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'N.B.A.’s New Commercial Misfires', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Durant, Kevin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oklahoma City Thunder', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T20:07:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Mahoney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mahoney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/470d71b3-7c49-53ed-9e2f-5f72a6f490bb', 'word_count': 750, 'uri': 'nyt://article/470d71b3-7c49-53ed-9e2f-5f72a6f490bb'}, {'abstract': 'As if any more confirmation were needed that Republicans see Chris Christie as a national star, New Jersey’s governor will be the keynote speaker at the National Republican Congressional Committee’s annual dinner March 30.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/christie-will-be-keynote-speaker-at-n-r-c-c-dinner/', 'snippet': 'As if any more confirmation were needed that Republicans see Chris Christie as a national star, New Jersey’s governor will be the keynote speaker at the National Republican Congressional Committee’s annual dinner March 30.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As if any more confirmation were needed that Republicans see Chris Christie as a national star, New Jersey’s governor will be the keynote speaker at the National Republican Congressional Committee’s annual dinner, the committee announced on Friday. The dinner will be held March 30 at the National Building Museum in Washington.\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0In July, when Mr. Christie had been in office for less than six months, the National Governors Association named him to its nine-member executive committee. Over the summer and fall, video clips of him dressing down his critics became YouTube hits. In the stretch run of last year’s campaigns, he was one of the most sought-after surrogates, stumping for Republican candidates around the country.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Christie Will Be Keynote Speaker at Republican Dinner', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T20:09:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Pérez-Peña', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pérez-Peña', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/068ffc4b-6201-5b17-a127-a423b91e331c', 'word_count': 176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/068ffc4b-6201-5b17-a127-a423b91e331c'}, {'abstract': 'We chose a shirt dress that will weather the transition from summer to fall or even see you clear through to December.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/just-one-look-derek-lam-pre-fall-collection/', 'snippet': 'We chose a shirt dress that will weather the transition from summer to fall or even see you clear through to December.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Pre-Fall Collections, being shown in New York over the next several weeks, arrive in stores in May to fill the gap between bikinis and furs.Derek Lam is from California, where the change of seasons is often a nonevent, and he approached pre-fall with a seemingly light hand. There was no shortage of workmanship in his peplumed dresses (designed to look like a top and a skirt) and sharp riding jacket. But depending on which coast you call home, it’s quietly stylish pieces like his guncheck shift dress — photo printed on silk crepe de Chine — or this shirt dress and fur vest that will weather the transition from summer to fall or even see you clear through to December. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Derek Lam Pre-Fall Collection', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T20:15:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alix Browne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a51013e1-4481-5358-94ce-823dc0c7adb9', 'word_count': 125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a51013e1-4481-5358-94ce-823dc0c7adb9'}, {'abstract': 'The country’s new minister in charge of economic and fiscal policy, Kaoru Yosano, is an advocate of raising Japan’s 5 percent sales tax to finance the country’s growing social welfare spending.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/business/global/15yen.html', 'snippet': 'The country’s new minister in charge of economic and fiscal policy, Kaoru Yosano, is an advocate of raising Japan’s 5 percent sales tax to finance the country’s growing social welfare spending.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO \x97 Prime Minister Naoto Kan of Japan chose a longtime fiscal conservative as his economics chief in a Cabinet reshuffle on Friday, signaling a renewed commitment to efforts to rein in the country’s mushrooming public debt.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Cabinet Reshuffle, Japan Names New Economics Chief', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Cabinet Reshuffle, Japan Names New Economics Chief', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yosano, Kaoru', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T20:21:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f69a6edc-fa7f-5da0-9efa-8836cd9384c9', 'word_count': 531, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f69a6edc-fa7f-5da0-9efa-8836cd9384c9'}, {'abstract': 'Down Goes Brown has helpful tips for N.H.L. general managers on the hot seat.', 'w