les of five eating spots in Connecticut, including Valencia Luncheria in Norwalk.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/nyregion/16dinect.html', 'snippet': 'The Food Network program “Diners, Drive-Ins and Dives” has raised the profiles of five eating spots in Connecticut, including Valencia Luncheria in Norwalk.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TO prepare for the first showing of a segment about his tiny Venezuelan restaurant on the Food Network show “Diners, Drive-Ins and Dives,” Michael Young, owner and chef of Valencia Luncheria in Norwalk, said his staff prepped for a week.', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/nyregion/16DINE1CO/16DINE1CO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/nyregion/16DINE1CO/16DINE1CO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/nyregion/16DINE1CO/16DINE1CO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When TV Makes Your Diner Shine', 'kicker': 'Connecticut Dining | Star Turns', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When TV Makes Your Diner Shine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T22:55:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jan Ellen Spiegel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jan', 'middlename': 'Ellen', 'lastname': 'Spiegel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2d463f1-d587-5355-acbc-dc59a19fc0e8', 'word_count': 1314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2d463f1-d587-5355-acbc-dc59a19fc0e8'}, {'abstract': 'Only 77 percent of city schoolteachers showed up Wednesday on the Snow Day That Wasn’t.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/nearly-one-in-four-teachers-stymied-by-snow/', 'snippet': 'Only 77 percent of city schoolteachers showed up Wednesday on the Snow Day That Wasn’t.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While fewer than half of the city’s 1.1 million school children made it to school through the snow on Wednesday, their teachers didn’t earn a gold star for attendance, either.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nearly One in Four Teachers Stymied By Snow', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T22:57:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sharon Otterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sharon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Otterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/62a5cd56-bbcd-5499-b2e4-d3229d3c4e08', 'word_count': 139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62a5cd56-bbcd-5499-b2e4-d3229d3c4e08'}, {'abstract': 'Pier Paolo Piccioli and Maria Grazia Chiuri continue to bring Valentino into the next generation.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/just-one-look-valentino-pre-fall-collection/', 'snippet': 'Pier Paolo Piccioli and Maria Grazia Chiuri continue to bring Valentino into the next generation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Pre-Fall Collections, being shown in New York over the next several weeks, arrive in stores in May to fill the gap between bikinis and furs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Valentino Pre-Fall Collection', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T23:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alix Browne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/244ff8d7-9ecc-5fdf-bfb4-0b08f0373103', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/244ff8d7-9ecc-5fdf-bfb4-0b08f0373103'}, {'abstract': 'A slide show of photographs of cultural events from this week.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/the-week-in-culture-pictures-jan-14/', 'snippet': 'A slide show of photographs of cultural events from this week.', 'lead_paragraph': ' More Photographs', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Week in Culture Pictures, Jan. 14', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T23:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e344d38c-f247-5c3c-9e67-01673f215d38', 'word_count': 13, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e344d38c-f247-5c3c-9e67-01673f215d38'}, {'abstract': 'The main works here were Schumann’s Symphony No. 4 in D minor and Brahms’s Piano Concerto No. 2 in B flat, with the brilliant pianist Yefim Bronfman as soloist, and each received a magisterial and exciting performance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/arts/music/15phil.html', 'snippet': 'The main works here were Schumann’s Symphony No. 4 in D minor and Brahms’s Piano Concerto No. 2 in B flat, with the brilliant pianist Yefim Bronfman as soloist, and each received a magisterial and exciting performance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2008 the young German composer Jörg Widmann wrote “Con Brio,” a concert overture, to open a program by the Bavarian Radio Symphony Orchestra that was to proceed to Beethoven’s Seventh and Eighth Symphonies. Mr. Widmann used the same instrumentation called for in those Beethoven works. And if there are no exact quotations from the symphonies, Mr. Widmann’s overture is run through with Beethovenian riffs, flourishes and humor.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/15/arts/PHIL/PHIL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 648, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/15/arts/PHIL/PHIL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/15/arts/PHIL/PHIL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beethoven’s Varied Descendants', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beethoven’s Varied Descendants', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schumann, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beethoven, Ludwig van', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dohnanyi, Christoph von', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bronfman, Yefim', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T23:03:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/825bf823-4541-523c-98d4-7635749645db', 'word_count': 515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/825bf823-4541-523c-98d4-7635749645db'}, {'abstract': '“Devotion” by Sarah Michelson contemplates discovery and wonder at the Kitchen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/arts/dance/15devotion.html', 'snippet': '“Devotion” by Sarah Michelson contemplates discovery and wonder at the Kitchen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So much art slides out of your mind as soon as you see it: it demands nothing, it gives nothing. Such empty performances engender a particular type of desperation, especially for those who see a lot of shows. At a certain point, trapped in a theater, all you can hear are the seconds ticking away. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/15/arts/devotion/devotion-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 798, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/15/arts/devotion/devotion-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/15/arts/devotion/devotion-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Matters of Faith, Prayer And Physical Exertion', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Matters of Faith, Prayer And Physical Exertion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kitchen, The', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Michelson, Sarah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T23:06:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claudia La Rocco', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claudia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Rocco', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/44a0ab25-64d0-564b-9bb2-949b18ed8266', 'word_count': 870, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44a0ab25-64d0-564b-9bb2-949b18ed8266'}, {'abstract': 'Patrick Pierre-Jerome’s return is in an unlikely setting: a nightclub and restaurant where live music shares the stage with Cajun-spiced Southern food.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/nyregion/16dinenj.html', 'snippet': 'Patrick Pierre-Jerome’s return is in an unlikely setting: a nightclub and restaurant where live music shares the stage with Cajun-spiced Southern food.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PATRICK PIERRE-JEROME made a name for himself in the ’90s, earning raves at his Montclair bistro, Yves, and later at Stage Left in New Brunswick, where he was executive chef. Then, after a tough divorce, he disappeared, cooking in country clubs for several years. His return to the public eye came in an unlikely setting: a nightclub and restaurant, run by a nonprofit group, where live music shares the stage with a succinct menu of moderately priced Cajun-spiced Southern food.', 'print_section': 'NJ', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/nyregion/16dinenj2/16dinenj2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/nyregion/16dinenj2/16dinenj2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cajun-Spiced Food, in a Nonprofit Setting', 'kicker': 'New Jersey  Dining  | Orange', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cajun-Spiced Food, In a Nonprofit Setting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T23:12:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David M. Halbfinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bae3d25e-c705-5768-8286-834b6507d0da', 'word_count': 858, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bae3d25e-c705-5768-8286-834b6507d0da'}, {'abstract': 'Opened in August by Maria Tisdall, a former convent cook, Cheese... Please! carries all kinds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/nyregion/16qbitenj.html', 'snippet': 'Opened in August by Maria Tisdall, a former convent cook, Cheese... Please! carries all kinds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Maria Tisdall cooked for the Benedictine nuns at the St. Walburga Monastery in Elizabeth during the 1990s, she became enough of an expert to write a cookbook, “The Convent Cook: Divine Meals for Families Large and Small” (Ten Speed Press) in 2002.', 'print_section': 'NJ', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Focus on Cheese', 'kicker': 'Quick Bite | Cranford', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Focus on Cheese', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheese', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T23:12:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kelly Feeney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kelly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Feeney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfbaf09a-29f3-5cda-805c-39401f97ce05', 'word_count': 335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfbaf09a-29f3-5cda-805c-39401f97ce05'}, {'abstract': 'A recipe for Cardamom-Scented Oatmeal Pancakes With Apricots and Almonds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/dining/19minirex3.html', 'snippet': 'A recipe for Cardamom-Scented Oatmeal Pancakes With Apricots and Almonds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Time: 30 minutes with cooked oatmeal', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cardamom-Scented Oatmeal Pancakes With Apricots and Almonds', 'kicker': 'Recipe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cardamom-Scented Oatmeal Pancakes with Apricots and Almonds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oatmeal', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pancakes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T23:12:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'recipe', '_id': 'nyt://article/29149b02-038b-56f8-990e-04a9e668a8b1', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29149b02-038b-56f8-990e-04a9e668a8b1'}, {'abstract': 'When handled correctly, whole grains can be used to create superlight pastries, the fastest and easiest of which are pancakes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/dining/19mini.html', 'snippet': 'When handled correctly, whole grains can be used to create superlight pastries, the fastest and easiest of which are pancakes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FAR be it from me to attack an icon (and anyway, I’ve eaten my share), but the common pancake is little more than a vehicle for maple syrup. Without that and careful browning in butter, it’s a total bore.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/19/dining/19mini-span/19mini-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 388, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/19/dining/19mini-span/19mini-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 388}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/19/dining/19mini-span/19mini-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/19/dining/19mini-span/19mini-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Whole-Grain Pancakes, Try a Little Tenderness', 'kicker': 'The Minimalist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Whole-Grain Pancakes, Real Eye-Openers in the Morning', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pancakes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T23:12:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc421cd8-c795-53ac-a1fa-b42b0d99e675', 'word_count': 454, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc421cd8-c795-53ac-a1fa-b42b0d99e675'}, {'abstract': 'Despite a lineup of mostly reruns, consistency carried CBS over rival ABC to top the ratings in prime time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/arts/television/15arts-RERUNSWORKFO_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Despite a lineup of mostly reruns, consistency carried CBS over rival ABC to top the ratings in prime time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Despite a lineup of mostly reruns, consistency carried CBS over rival ABC to top the ratings in prime time on Thursday. According to Nielsen’s estimates the network’s repeats of “CSI” and “The Mentalist” earned 9.7 million and 10.3 million viewers from 9 to 11 p.m. while a new episode of “$#*! My Dad Says” at 8:30 garnered 10.2 million viewers after a repeat of “The Big Bang Theory” at 8 (10.4 million). ABC ranked second on the night despite leading during both the 8 and 9 p.m. time slots with “Wipeout” (10.5 million) and “Grey’s Anatomy” (12.2 million). But at 10 a rebroadcast of the premiere of ABC’s new show “Off the Map” garnered just 6 million viewers, down from this medical drama’s original debut on Wednesday (7.6 million). Fox was third and NBC a distant fourth. President Obama’s speech in Tucson on Wednesday drew a total of 30.8 million viewers, according to Nielsen’s calculation of the television audience watching the four main broadcast networks and three cable news channels.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reruns Work for CBS', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reruns Work for CBS', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corp', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T23:16:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/05153aec-6a0b-57ec-93b2-ca82008c156b', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05153aec-6a0b-57ec-93b2-ca82008c156b'}, {'abstract': 'A meaty season is planned for a new hall in Missouri.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/arts/music/15arts-ORCHESTRAUPT_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'A meaty season is planned for a new hall in Missouri.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A meaty season is planned for a new hall in Missouri. The Kansas City Symphony will move into Helzberg Hall in the new Kauffman Center for the Performing Arts in September, and on Friday the orchestra announced ambitious programming for the 2011-12 season. Michael Stern, the music director, will lead three premieres: new works by Chen Yi, Stephen Hartke and Dan Kellogg. The orchestra will also play Respighi’s “Pines of Rome”; Brahms’s “German Requiem”; Mahler’s Symphony No. 2 (“Resurrection”); Beethoven’s Symphony No. 9; Saint-Saëns’s Symphony No. 3, which will include the hall’s new pipe organ; and works by Messiaen and Schnittke. The musicians should come ready to rehearse: 28 percent of the works in the non-pops season, officials said, are either newly composed or new to the orchestra. The orchestra is also springing for major soloists, including Emanuel Ax, Yo-Yo Ma, Yefim Bronfman, Joshua Bell and the Kansas City native Joyce DiDonato.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Orchestra Up to Date in Kansas City', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Orchestra Up to Date In Kansas City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T23:16:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a48eb463-e314-54f0-8ab2-44aad3a21ea5', 'word_count': 160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a48eb463-e314-54f0-8ab2-44aad3a21ea5'}, {'abstract': '“Brooklyn Kinda Love” on Playboy TV follows four young couples.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/arts/television/15playboy.html', 'snippet': '“Brooklyn Kinda Love” on Playboy TV follows four young couples.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From where I am sitting, writing this review, in a cafe in the white-hot center of brownstone Brooklyn, the phrase “Brooklyn Kinda Love” has a connotation very different from the one that Playboy TV executives intended when they created a series bearing that name.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/15/arts/SUB-PLAYBOY/SUB-PLAYBOY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/15/arts/SUB-PLAYBOY/SUB-PLAYBOY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/15/arts/SUB-PLAYBOY/SUB-PLAYBOY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Expect References to Literary Giants', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Don’t Expect References to Literary Giants', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Playboy Enterprises Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T23:20:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c56fcaa3-9ccf-539d-a18c-fccbfeb3c7b7', 'word_count': 584, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c56fcaa3-9ccf-539d-a18c-fccbfeb3c7b7'}, {'abstract': 'The Winter Farmers Market @ PepsiCo, a joint effort of Purchase College’s Sustainability Department and PepsiCo, is providing home delivery for orders of $25 or more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/nyregion/16qbitewe.html', 'snippet': 'The Winter Farmers Market @ PepsiCo, a joint effort of Purchase College’s Sustainability Department and PepsiCo, is providing home delivery for orders of $25 or more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Farmers’ markets have become so popular that many are staying open — or just beginning operations — in the winter. Now, in a further adaptation, one off-season market in Westchester County is offering a new service: home delivery.', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Market Shopping, From Home', 'kicker': 'Quick Bite | Purchase', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Market Shopping, From Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greenwich (Conn)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Local Food', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Farmers' Markets", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T23:21:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily DeNitto', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DeNitto', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39077010-aeb7-582a-b012-42a65e3af468', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39077010-aeb7-582a-b012-42a65e3af468'}, {'abstract': 'Arthur Cutillo, formerly of the law firm Ropes & Gray, is the latest defendant linked to the Galleon Group to plead guilty.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/another-guilty-plea-in-galleon/', 'snippet': 'Arthur Cutillo, formerly of the law firm Ropes & Gray, is the latest defendant linked to the Galleon Group to plead guilty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A lawyer who was ensnared in the insider trading case involving the Galleon Group pleaded guilty on Friday to charges that he leaked nonpublic information about mergers and acquisitions that his firm was working on. The plea was entered in Federal District Court in Manhattan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lawyer Enters Guilty Plea in Galleon Case', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Galleon Group', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T23:21:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Azam Ahmed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Azam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahmed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c175d2fc-51ec-5b66-9f06-6b40c0867592', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c175d2fc-51ec-5b66-9f06-6b40c0867592'}, {'abstract': 'The Polish trumpeter Tomasz Stanko found a new dynamic with his quartet and Chris Potter at the Jazz Standard.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/arts/music/15quartet.html', 'snippet': 'The Polish trumpeter Tomasz Stanko found a new dynamic with his quartet and Chris Potter at the Jazz Standard.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A bolt of bracing indeterminacy shot through the trumpeter Tomasz Stanko’s late set at the Jazz Standard on Thursday night. Tempos lurched and buckled, or mysteriously dissolved. Melodic lines snaked quickly through oblique intervals, defying resolution. Mr. Stanko framed his solos in long, searching arcs, each note sharply articulated but unpredictable in its aim. His engagement with the other musicians in the band suggested a kind of collective bargaining rather than the resolute charge of a leader and his men.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/15/arts/quartet/quartet-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/15/arts/quartet/quartet-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/15/arts/quartet/quartet-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Setting Leadership Aside For Collective Control', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Setting Leadership Aside For Collective Control', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stanko, Tomasz', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jazz Standard', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T23:24:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/35c24d8f-a399-5b43-b7a7-c9b83ec6ae7c', 'word_count': 461, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35c24d8f-a399-5b43-b7a7-c9b83ec6ae7c'}, {'abstract': 'Cardamom, oatmeal, ricotta, zucchini, carrot and other worthy additions to the batter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/01/14/dining/20110119-pancakes.html', 'snippet': 'Cardamom, oatmeal, ricotta, zucchini, carrot and other worthy additions to the batter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cardamom, oatmeal, ricotta, zucchini, carrot and other worthy additions to the batter.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/14/dining/20110119-pancakes-slide-RYXV/20110119-pancakes-slide-RYXV-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 388, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Recipes for Pancakes, Sweet and Savory', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pancakes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-14T23:25:37+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Food', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/4f4ce7b9-0ca4-540c-8316-5a69fbb74b6d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/4f4ce7b9-0ca4-540c-8316-5a69fbb74b6d'}, {'abstract': 'Arrosto, open for three months, has a sophisticated, polished look and encourages diners to “try a little, try a lot.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/nyregion/16dinewe.html', 'snippet': 'Arrosto, open for three months, has a sophisticated, polished look and encourages diners to “try a little, try a lot.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'OPEN for almost three months, Arrosto, in Port Chester, joins a growing number of urban-chic restaurants in the county. The place is a beauty, intelligently designed to suit the open, cavernous space. At its center is a work station, the front dedicated to the preparation of appetizers and the back a serious cooking stage with a wood-burning oven and a grill, for firing pizzas and roasts.', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/nyregion/16dinewe2/16dinewe2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/nyregion/16dinewe2/16dinewe2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Laid-Back and Indulgent', 'kicker': 'Westchester Dining | Port Chester', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Laid-Back And Indulgent', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Westc