 shopping spree.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/14/the-hellcat/', 'snippet': 'As Lincoln tries to save the union, his wife follows her own better angels to a New York shopping spree.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was not quite a full-blown temper tantrum, but Mary Todd Lincoln’s outburst during a mid-January shopping trip raised eyebrows at a time when her husband did not need any more problems. And it signaled that the president-elect would have his hands full governing his own household, on top of everything else. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hellcat', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Mary Todd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-15T01:30:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ted Widmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ted', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Widmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5856f364-c048-5e16-98e3-8f18409108a8', 'word_count': 1514, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5856f364-c048-5e16-98e3-8f18409108a8'}, {'abstract': 'Sloane Crosley, the writer, may be the most fashionable woman at the library.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/fashion/16WHATIWORE.html', 'snippet': 'Sloane Crosley, the writer, may be the most fashionable woman at the library.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SLOANE CROSLEY, 32, is the author of the essay collections “I Was Told There’d Be Cake,” a best seller, and “How Did You Get This Number,” which will appear in paperback this spring. Currently working on a screenplay and a novel, Ms. Crosley recently left the publicity department of Vintage Books to write full time. She lives in Chelsea but can often be found at the New York Public Library at Fifth Avenue and 42nd Street.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/fashion/16whatiwore/WHATIWORE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 657, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/fashion/16whatiwore/16whatiwore-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/fashion/16whatiwore/16whatiwore-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fashionably Chic for Library Life', 'kicker': 'What I Wore', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fashionably Chic For Library Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crosley, Sloane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-15T01:31:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chloe Malle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chloe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Malle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54f74cac-39d8-5bc0-809f-09841a0831f0', 'word_count': 787, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54f74cac-39d8-5bc0-809f-09841a0831f0'}, {'abstract': 'A record producer was accused of resisting arrest days after he complained about the police in Harlem.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/nyregion/15doll.html', 'snippet': 'A record producer was accused of resisting arrest days after he complained about the police in Harlem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A record producer who complained that he saw the head of a black baby doll on the antenna of an unmarked police car in Harlem was acquitted on Friday of charges that he had later obstructed the police and resisted arrest.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/15/nyregion/DOLL/DOLL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 864, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/15/nyregion/DOLL/DOLL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/15/nyregion/DOLL/DOLL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Acquittal After Reporting Black Doll Head on Police Car', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Man Who Saw Doll Head On Police Car Is Acquitted', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'HARLEM (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-15T01:34:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Eligon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eligon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8683b273-a8d6-543e-8390-fb65ae5256a8', 'word_count': 709, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8683b273-a8d6-543e-8390-fb65ae5256a8'}, {'abstract': 'Postings from craigslist rendered as poetry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/nyregion/16poems.html', 'snippet': 'Postings from craigslist rendered as poetry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONE wonders how many parents are aware that aside from being food critics, amateur psychologists, human alarm clocks and ruiners of financial health, children are — have to say it — powerful romantic magnets. People with kids in tow get lots of attention from the opposite sex, at least according to the following poems “found” last week in the Missed Connections section of newyork.craigslist.org. They are printed verbatim, with only line and stanza breaks added; the titles are the subject headings. ALAN FEUER ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/nyregion/16POEMS/16POEMS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/16/nyregion/16POEMS/16POEMS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 368}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/nyregion/16POEMS/16POEMS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/nyregion/16POEMS/16POEMS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/nyregion/16POEMS/16POEMS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Your Beautiful, Creative Son and Daughter’ (and You, Too)', 'kicker': 'Poetic Connections | Little Aphrodisiacs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Your Beautiful, Creative Son and Daughter’ (and You, Too)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Craigslist', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-15T01:39:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Feuer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Feuer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de59d0c0-c5fe-5d55-8bd9-8c41ec68cd6f', 'word_count': 708, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de59d0c0-c5fe-5d55-8bd9-8c41ec68cd6f'}, {'abstract': 'Rex Ryan said receiver Brad Smith and cornerback Drew Coleman were questionable for a game that he thinks is outweighed in importance only by the Jets’ victory in Super Bowl III.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/sports/football/15jets.html', 'snippet': 'Rex Ryan said receiver Brad Smith and cornerback Drew Coleman were questionable for a game that he thinks is outweighed in importance only by the Jets’ victory in Super Bowl III.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FLORHAM PARK, N.J. \x97 Other than designating the Jets’ playoff game Sunday against the Patriots as “the second-biggest game in the history of the franchise,” Coach Rex Ryan was much more prudent Friday, particularly when discussing injuries.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/15/sports/jets/jets-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 587, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption