5T02:30:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/e62068bf-4911-5279-851c-0cc42b5f1b32', 'word_count': 412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e62068bf-4911-5279-851c-0cc42b5f1b32'}, {'abstract': 'Two nonconformist Russian artists — they refused to work in the state-sanctioned style — are on view in solo shows under one roof in New Brunswick.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/nyregion/16artsnj.html', 'snippet': 'Two nonconformist Russian artists — they refused to work in the state-sanctioned style — are on view in solo shows under one roof in New Brunswick.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Boris Sveshnikov was a 19-year-old art student when he was arrested in 1946, held in Moscow prisons for a year, and finally sentenced to eight years’ hard labor in the northern Ural Mountains. His “crime” and sentence were suitably Kafkaesque. Apparently a classmate had written in a notebook, which he intended to leave out to impress a girl, that he was involved in terrorist activities and wanted to eliminate Stalin; Mr. Sveshnikov was named as the “designated executioner.” Unfortunately, the notebook fell into the hands of the Ministry of State Security.', 'print_section': 'NJ', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/nyregion/16ART1NJ/16ART1NJ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/nyregion/16ART1NJ/16ART1NJ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Abstract and Expressionistic, Yet Based in Grim Reality', 'kicker': 'Art Review | New Jersey', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Abstract and Expressionistic, Yet Based in Grim Reality', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Zimmerli Art Museum', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-15T02:32:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Schwendener', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwendener', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5880059-2691-5ef2-957f-f24cd38de667', 'word_count': 906, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5880059-2691-5ef2-957f-f24cd38de667'}, {'abstract': 'The Haven String Quartet will join St. Luke’s Steel Band and New Haven schoolchildren in a concert tribute to Martin Luther King Jr. at St. Luke’s Episcopal Church.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/nyregion/16musicct.html', 'snippet': 'The Haven String Quartet will join St. Luke’s Steel Band and New Haven schoolchildren in a concert tribute to Martin Luther King Jr. at St. Luke’s Episcopal Church.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN a fitting tribute to Martin Luther King Jr., the Haven String Quartet will be forging new cultural relationships on Monday by sharing a bill \x97 and, at some points, a stage \x97 with its neighbor on Whalley Avenue in New Haven, St. Luke’s Steel Band. ', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/nyregion/16MUSIC1CO/16MUSIC1CO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/16/nyregion/16MUSIC1CO/16MUSIC1CO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subty