 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-15T06:46:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d661f55-6e21-568c-b801-0f22cf394a97', 'word_count': 293, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d661f55-6e21-568c-b801-0f22cf394a97'}, {'abstract': 'Carmelo Anthony produces a profound increase in the efficiency of his supporting cast when he is on the floor.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/why-carmelo-anthony-is-the-ultimate-team-player-and-what-advanced-stats-miss-about-him/', 'snippet': 'Carmelo Anthony produces a profound increase in the efficiency of his supporting cast when he is on the floor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carmelo Anthony, whom the Knicks are considering acquiring in a trade, is sometimes thought of as a selfish player. Indeed, he is the center of the Denver Nuggets’ offense: when he is on the court for them, about 30 percent of their possessions end in Anthony shooting, going to the foul line, or committing a turnover. Nor is Anthony much of a passer: over his career, he’s accumulated 3.1 assists per 36 minutes played, considerably less than that of other high-volume scorers like Kobe Bryant (4.6 assists per 36 minutes) or LeBron James (6.2). ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Carmelo Anthony Is the Ultimate Team Player (and What ‘Advanced’ Stats Miss About Him)', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANTHONY, CARMELO', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Knicks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-15T08:21:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7a07c31-ee4a-56c4-8d1c-d61b8482e9f0', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7a07c31-ee4a-56c4-8d1c-d61b8482e9f0'}, {'abstract': 'The elusive hip-hop mogul finally made good on a 13-month-old promise to appear in an improv comedy show at the Upright Citizens Brigade Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/diddy-did-it-rappers-guest-role-at-the-chris-gethard-show/', 'snippet': 'The elusive hip-hop mogul finally made good on a 13-month-old promise to appear in an improv comedy show at the Upright Citizens Brigade Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometimes it seems like good things can only occur in Chris Gethard’s life if they are balanced out by other inauspicious events. On Friday afternoon, Mr. Gethard, 30, a boyish comedian with a gentle disposition, was playing basketball when another player collided with him, breaking his glasses, cutting his nose and sending him scrambling to LensCrafters for some emergency repair work.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Diddy Did It: Rapper’s Guest Role at ‘The Chris Gethard Show’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Combs, Sean', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Upright Citizens Brigade', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-15T09:12:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d91f36ec-6b4d-5ac8-b29b-fbfcebd1a683', 'word_count': 1295, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d91f36ec-6b4d-5ac8-b29b-fbfcebd1a683'}, {'abstract': 'Republicans choose a new leader for their party, with an eye to 2012.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/the-weekend-word-passing-the-gavel/', 'snippet': 'Republicans choose a new leader for their party, with an eye to 2012.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From Today’s Times:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Weekend Word: Passing the Gavel', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-15T11:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emmarie Huetteman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emmarie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Huetteman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/80358635-f440-548c-bac1-8c58aeac66c5', 'word_count': 334, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80358635-f440-548c-bac1-8c58aeac66c5'}, {'abstract': 'The swift turnabout raised new questions about what kind of government might emerge from the chaos engulfing the country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/world/africa/16tunis.html', 'snippet': 'The swift turnabout raised new questions about what kind of government might emerge from the chaos engulfing the country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TUNIS \x97 Tanks, police officers and gangs of newly deputized young men wielding guns held the deserted streets of Tunis Saturday night after a day of sporadic rioting and gunfire. Power changed hands for the second time in 24 hours, and the swift turnabout raised new questions about what kind of government might emerge from the chaos engulfing Tunisia.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/world/16JPTUNIS1/16JPTUNIS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 709, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/world/16JPTUNIS1/16JPTUNIS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/16/world/16JPTUNIS1/16JPTUNIS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Tunisia, Clashes Continue as Power Shifts a Second Time', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Clashes Go On As Power Shifts Again in Tunis', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ghannouchi, Mohamed', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ben Ali, Zine El-Abidine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tunisia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-15T12:02:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By David D. Kirkpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e49fe5da-b590-595f-8646-4e1feb41424e', 'word_count': 1610, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e49fe5da-b590-595f-8646-4e1feb41424e'}, {'abstract': 'A lot of bad blood was stirred up in the Jets and Patriots camps this week. Will it carry over onto the field?', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/is-jets-patriots-turning-into-a-ravens-steelers-type-rivalry/', 'snippet': 'A lot of bad blood was stirred up in the Jets and Patriots camps this week. Will it carry over onto the field?', 'lead_paragraph': 'When it comes to Ravens-versus-Steelers, what moments do you think of? Is it Bart Scott’s crushing hit on Ben Roethlisberger, from Nov. 26, 2006? The collision between the Steelers’ Ryan Clark and Baltimore’s Willis McGahee in the closing minutes of the A.F.C. championship game in 2009? How about the\xa0 hit by Ravens linebacker Ray Lewis that broke the shoulder of Steelers running back Rashard Mendenhall in 2008.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Jets-Patriots Turning Into a Ravens-Steelers-Type Rivalry?', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baltimore Ravens', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Patriots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pittsburgh Steelers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-15T12:04:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Bretherton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bretherton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2a77381-7535-55e2-a584-1b072d249816', 'word_count': 422, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2a77381-7535-55e2-a584-1b072d249816'}, {'abstract': 'If you’re the underdog in a football game, what are the best tactics to pursue?', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/strategy-for-underdogs-jets-against-the-patriots/', 'snippet': 'If you’re the underdog in a football game, what are the best tactics to pursue?', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’re the underdog in a football game, what is the best strategy to pursue?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Strategy for Underdogs: Jets Against the Patriots', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Patriots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-15T13:30:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1216c25-7243-5ddc-ab44-4298a4bd2118', 'word_count': 541, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1216c25-7243-5ddc-ab44-4298a4bd2118'}, {'abstract': 'This week, Crumbs Bake Shop announced a $66 billion deal to go public.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/is-the-cupcake-bubble-still-inflating/', 'snippet': 'This week, Crumbs Bake Shop announced a $66 billion deal to go public.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In November 2009, we ran an article that explored the difficult economics of opening a cupcake shop, and we asked this question: Are cupcakes a viable business? This week we got an answer — at least for one cupcake chain, Crumbs Bake Shop. Crumbs, which is based in New York, announced plans to go public in a $66 million deal. It claimed 2010 revenue of $31.1 million with earnings of close to $2 million.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is the Cupcake Bubble Still Inflating?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-15T14:47:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': "By You're the Boss Editors", 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/74c232c9-8dd2-5fe2-b401-b616de04f713', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74c232c9-8dd2-5fe2-b401-b616de04f713'}, {'abstract': 'Joseph Gagnon predicts that the world’s financial imbalances — such as China’s trade surplus and the United States’ trade deficit — will probably return to record levels in coming years.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/15/bigger-trade-deficits-coming/', 'snippet': 'Joseph Gagnon predicts that the world’s financial imbalances — such as China’s trade surplus and the United States’ trade deficit — will probably return to record levels in coming years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joseph Gagnon is worried. Mr. Gagnon, a former Federal Reserve economist now at the Peterson Institute, contends in a new paper that the world’s financial imbalances — such as China’s trade surplus and the United States’ trade deficit — will probably return to record levels in coming years. He argues that the imbalances have narrowed recently only because of the recession (which, for example, has reduced American demand for Chinese exports).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bigger Trade Deficits Coming?', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-15T15:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8b73f35-c0f5-5d92-a607-0b4e77837be5', 'word_count': 549, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8b73f35-c0f5-5d92-a607-0b4e77837be5'}, {'abstract': 'The supermarket where the shooting occurred reopened as a gun show went forward in Tucson, and doctors were able to remove Gabrielle Giffords’s breathing tube.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/us/16giffords.html', 'snippet': 'The supermarket where the shooting occurred reopened as a gun show went forward in Tucson, and doctors were able to remove Gabrielle Giffords’s breathing tube.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This article is by William Yardley, Michael Luo and Sam Dolnick. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/16/us/1