n': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Le Pen’s Daughter Elected to Lead His Far-Right Party', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Le Pen’s Daughter Elected to Lead His Far-Right Party', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Le Pen, Marine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Le Pen, Jean-Marie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Front', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T17:26:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Erlanger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Erlanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd8bac4d-fd6c-5bcb-9d5a-a5fdad6e6b71', 'word_count': 272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd8bac4d-fd6c-5bcb-9d5a-a5fdad6e6b71'}, {'abstract': 'It tops the movie box office in its first weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/green-hornet-a-hero-for-sony-pictures/', 'snippet': 'It tops the movie box office in its first weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Green Hornet” was No. 1 at North American theaters over the weekend with about $34 million in ticket sales — proof that a studio, in this case Sony Pictures Entertainment, can fix a troubled movie on the fly. After a sneak peak for “The Green Hornet” was greeted with ridicule from fanboys last summer, Sony performed triage. The film, based on the 1930s radio serial and 1960s television show, was re-edited, new scenes were shot and the advertising plan was reworked. The upshot: While sales were on the squishy side for a 3-D release costing at least $110 million, “The Green Hornet” is far from the flop that many expected. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Green Hornet’ a Hero for Sony Pictures', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howard, Ron', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Pictures Entertainment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Weinstein Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North America', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T17:33:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d5cc84d-8c17-5b25-abe5-02535de17d05', 'word_count': 225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d5cc84d-8c17-5b25-abe5-02535de17d05'}, {'abstract': 'Live analysis and coverage of the N.F.C. divisional round playoff game between the Seattle Seahawks and the Chicago Bears.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/live-analysis-seahawks-at-bears/', 'snippet': 'Live analysis and coverage of the N.F.C. divisional round playoff game between the Seattle Seahawks and the Chicago Bears.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Live analysis and coverage of the N.F.C. divisional round playoff game between the Seattle Seahawks and the Chicago Bears. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Final: Bears 35, Seahawks 24', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cutler, Jay', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hasselbeck, Matt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago Bears', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Seattle Seahawks', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T17:38:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Das', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Das', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4929426-8baf-5ecd-abcf-48d86da2cb27', 'word_count': 2437, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4929426-8baf-5ecd-abcf-48d86da2cb27'}, {'abstract': 'An academic family in Eleanor Brown’s “Weird Sisters” communicates through Shakespearean quotations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/books/17book.html', 'snippet': 'An academic family in Eleanor Brown’s “Weird Sisters” communicates through Shakespearean quotations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eleanor Brown’s debut novel begins charmingly, narrated by three sisters who speak as a single entity. They will explain the event that sets “The Weird Sisters” in motion, “but first we have to explain how our family communicates, and to do that, we have to explain our family.” After that: “Oh, man.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/BOOK/BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 908, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Family Speaks in Someone Else’s Words, but at Least It Chooses the Best', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Family Speaks in Someone Else’s Words, but at Least It Chooses the Best', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Eleanor', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T18:30:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b9ac027-113d-51c3-8a95-edc287f72c0d', 'word_count': 973, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b9ac027-113d-51c3-8a95-edc287f72c0d'}, {'abstract': 'A day before the Open started, the top tennis players rallied together for an exhibition to raise money for the victims of the recent flooding in Australia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/sports/tennis/17iht-TENNIS17.html', 'snippet': 'A day before the Open started, the top tennis players rallied together for an exhibition to raise money for the victims of the recent flooding in Australia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MELBOURNE — It was a sharp contrast. Down below, on a luminous blue court, star tennis players like Novak Djokovic, Andy Roddick and Caroline Wozniacki were in a playful mood, preparing to entertain a sellout crowd in Rod Laver Arena.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/sports/YTENNIS4/YTENNIS4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 750, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/sports/YTENNIS4/YTENNIS4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/17/sports/YTENNIS4/YTENNIS4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Before Australian Open, a Focus on Something Bigger', 'kicker': 'Australian Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Play Hard, Work Hard', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Australian Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nadal, Rafael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Federer, Roger', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T19:54:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Clarey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clarey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92b777e1-4f50-599c-8873-db5560a71c3c', 'word_count': 1087, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92b777e1-4f50-599c-8873-db5560a71c3c'}, {'abstract': 'The filmmakers had more resolved ending in the film originally shown to the studio and test audiences, but they felt that version was dishonest to the story they were trying to tell.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/a-different-ending-for-127-hours/', 'snippet': 'The filmmakers had more resolved ending in the film originally shown to the studio and test audiences, but they felt that version was dishonest to the story they were trying to tell.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES— James Franco might think of “127 Hours” as a Beckett play, as he previously told the Bagger, but it could’ve been a much more traditional Hollywood story. At a party for W Magazine at the Chateau Marmont on Friday, the Bagger spoke with Simon Beaufoy, a writer of the film (and of the Oscar juggernaut “Slumdog Millionaire,” along with his partner, the writer-director Danny Boyle), and he revealed to us that “127 Hours,” a Golden Globe nominee this evening for best screenplay and for Mr. Franco’s performance, nearly had an altogether different finish, even after the scene that everyone watches through splayed fingers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Different Ending for ‘127 Hours’', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golden Globes (Awards)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beaufoy, Simon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boyle, Danny', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Franco, James', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ralston, Aron Lee', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T20:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1a60182-4af0-5bb1-b130-20a3f59c4fcf', 'word_count': 858, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1a60182-4af0-5bb1-b130-20a3f59c4fcf'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Kantrowitz, a financial-aid guru, is answering reader questions on the Free Application for Federal Student Aid, and other topics of the season.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/fafsaq-and-a-part-6/', 'snippet': 'Mark Kantrowitz, a financial-aid guru, is answering reader questions on the Free Application for Federal Student Aid, and other topics of the season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As families of prospective college freshmen begin to tackle the Free Application for Federal Student Aid, or Fafsa, Mark Kantrowitz, a financial aid expert and founder of the Web sites finaid.org and fastweb.com, is answering questions from readers of The Choice this week. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Part 6: Answers to Readers’ Questions on Financial Aid', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T20:05:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Kantrowitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kantrowitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/45718caf-8aba-5686-9151-a959a125b39e', 'word_count': 2246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45718caf-8aba-5686-9151-a959a125b39e'}, {'abstract': 'Live analysis of the Jets’ game against the New England Patriots in the A.F.C. playoffs.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/live-analysis-jets-at-patriots-2/', 'snippet': 'Live analysis of the Jets’ game against the New England Patriots in the A.F.C. playoffs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOXBOROUGH, Mass. — The Jets defeated the Patriots, 28-21, on Sunday to advance to the American Football Conference championship game with a strong defensive effort and an opportunistic offense. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Live Analysis: Jets 28, Patriots 21, Final', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Patriots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T20:45:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Viera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ecdd0beb-4e33-562d-ab6d-a2c0e0a7da84', 'word_count': 2362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ecdd0beb-4e33-562d-ab6d-a2c0e0a7da84'}, {'abstract': 'Early results indicated that perhaps more than 95 percent of voters in the regional capital voted to secede.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/world/africa/17sudan.html', 'snippet': 'Early results indicated that perhaps more than 95 percent of voters in the regional capital voted to secede.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JUBA, Sudan \x97 Southern Sudanese election officials posted early results on Sunday indicating that perhaps more than 95 percent of voters in this regional capital voted to secede from Sudan.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/world/SUDAN/SUDAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/17/world/SUDAN/SUDAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/world/SUDAN/SUDAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/world/SUDAN/SUDAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/17/world/SUDAN/SUDAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Sudan, Early Results Strongly Lean to Secession', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Sudan, Early Results Strongly Lean To Secession', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Referendums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War and Guerrilla Warfare', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sudan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T20:52:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Josh Kron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/651d2a96-67fc-5793-ae68-85eba3abad24', 'word_count': 462, 'uri': 'nyt://article/651d2a96-67fc-5793-ae68-85eba3abad24'}, {'abstract': 'Though the slain civil rights leader is most often connected with Washington and the South, New York also figured significantly in his career.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/dr-king-and-new-york-city/', 'snippet': 'Though the slain civil rights leader is most often connected with Washington and the South, New York also figured significantly in his career.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. is identified mostly with Washington and the South, but he was officially an honorary New Yorker and the city plays a not insignificant role in his biography.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dr. King and New York City', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T21:10:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e33d529c-892c-56bc-8df6-0dac65f62ef8', 'word_count': 344, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e33d529c-892c-56bc-8df6-0dac65f62ef8'}, {'abstract': 'At one of the many Golden Globes weekend parties, Sacha Baron Cohen ducks questions, Johnny Knoxville suggests what a ‘For Your Consideration’ campaign for “Jackass 3D” would look like and Melissa Leo has a grand ol’ time.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/scenes-from-a-very-crowded-party/', 'snippet': 'At one of the many Golden Globes weekend parties, Sacha Baron Cohen ducks questions, Johnny Knoxville suggests what a ‘For Your Consideration’ campaign for “Jackass 3D” would look like and Melissa Leo has a grand ol’ time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES— “You really get to know people real well here,” Jack McGee, who plays Melissa Leo’s husband in “The Fighter,” told the Bagger as he squeezed by us for three seconds at the Paramount party at the Chateau Marmont on Saturday night. The space was jammed as usual, with an array of statuette contenders, other familiar TV and movie faces and those unicorns of the awards season, older Academy members. “Stay out of trouble,” Mark Wahlberg, as always a big draw for fans, told a teenage boy after posing for a photo with him. On this Golden Globes weekend, there was Michael Keaton and Chace Crawford – not together, sadly – Jeff Bridges and the Coen brothers, David O. Russell, Paul Haggis (who told us that Ron Burkle wrote his charity a $100,000 check after their party the other night), the writer-director Mike White in a striped ski hat, and Jason Segel, who charmed us with his down-to-earthness. (He walked to the Chateau from his nearby home.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scenes From a (Very Crowded) Party', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golden Globes (Awards)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Knoxville, Johnny', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leo, Melissa', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T21:11:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a97c59bb-52a0-5565-9e9d-47bbc2864c91', 'word_count': 521, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a97c59bb-52a0-5565-9e9d-47bbc2864c91'}, {'abstract': 'Policy? Go ask Alice.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/when-logic-and-proportion-have-fallen/', 'snippet': 'Policy? Go ask Alice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This, on the other hand, is for tomorrow’s column. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Logic and Proportion Have Fallen', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T21:20:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec1835de-fc7e-5601-8931-a6b5399a39d0', 'word_count': 10, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec1835de-fc7e-5601-8931-a6b5399a39d0'}, {'abstract': '“Sinners and Saints,” a program by the Mannes College the New School for Music, offered scenes from operas by Carlisle Floyd, Robert Ward and Menotti.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/arts/music/17mannes.html', 'snippet': '“Sinners and Saints,” a program by the Mannes College the New School for Music, offered scenes from operas by Carlisle Floyd, Robert Ward and Menotti.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Serious New York opera gourmands know that offerings from the city’s three main conservatories \x97 the Juilliard School, the Manhattan School of Music and Mannes College the New School for Music \x97 are a vital part of any healthy diet. “Sinners and Saints,” mounted by the Mannes Opera in the Kaye Playhouse at Hunter College on Friday evening, proved the point in several ways, any one of which would have argued strongly for attention from aficionados and neophytes alike.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/MANNES1/MANNES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/MANNES1/MANNES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/MANNES1/MANNES1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 557, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Young Voices Rise in American Opera', 'kicker':