ue': 'Tommasini, Anthony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Debussy, Claude', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T22:07:07+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'Gabe Johnson|Ben Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gabe', 'middlename': 'Johnson', 'lastname': 'Ben', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/da9c6902-5e17-5042-87a8-76b19236326a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/da9c6902-5e17-5042-87a8-76b19236326a'}, {'abstract': 'Lance Horne and friends gave an evening of his theater songs as part of Lincoln Center’s American Songbook Series.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/arts/music/17horne.html', 'snippet': 'Lance Horne and friends gave an evening of his theater songs as part of Lincoln Center’s American Songbook Series.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The theater songs of the up-and-coming composer Lance Horne, performed by a cast of his friends at the Allen Room on Friday evening, exemplify the blunt melodic style that has become the new normal of Broadway musicals. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/HORNE/HORNE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 297, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/HORNE/HORNE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 297}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/HORNE/HORNE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 507, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With Some Friends to Help, a Songwriter Reveals His World', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With Some Friends to Help, a Songwriter Reveals His World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'American Songbook (Music Series)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T22:07:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fba0e8f9-ca79-50bd-8c1d-b48a70702c1b', 'word_count': 392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fba0e8f9-ca79-50bd-8c1d-b48a70702c1b'}, {'abstract': 'A starry assemblage of chamber players gathered Saturday evening at Symphony Space for a 60th-anniversary tribute to the Marlboro Music Festival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/arts/music/17lucy.html', 'snippet': 'A starry assemblage of chamber players gathered Saturday evening at Symphony Space for a 60th-anniversary tribute to the Marlboro Music Festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chamber Music America is devoted to keeping the machinery of music making well oiled. One way it does that is by holding an annual conference in New York at which musicians, managers, concert presenters, publishers and record company executives mingle. This year’s took place Thursday through Sunday, and among the highlights was a 60th-anniversary tribute to the Marlboro Music Festival in Vermont, a fountainhead of chamber music in America, on Saturday evening at Symphony Space. (The concert honored Marlboro’s administrators, Anthony P. Checchia and Frank Salomon, who received the organization’s Richard J. Bogomolny National Service Award.) ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/LUCY/LUCY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 675, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bowing and Plucking to Honor a Festival', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bowing and Plucking To Honor a Festival', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T22:09:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/072f46e3-5d93-55e3-9320-5d2ccd6b88dd', 'word_count': 432, 'uri': 'nyt://article/072f46e3-5d93-55e3-9320-5d2ccd6b88dd'}, {'abstract': 'At Le Poisson Rouge, Doveman and friends perform songs both old and barely finished.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/arts/music/17dove.html', 'snippet': 'At Le Poisson Rouge, Doveman and friends perform songs both old and barely finished.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thomas Bartlett, who performs as Doveman, doesn’t hog the spotlight. Alongside his solo albums he has worked as a keyboardist and arranger for the National, Bebel Gilberto, Antony and the Johnsons, and others whose music is infused with pensive drama, and he applies his classical piano training for atmosphere and delicacy, not acrobatics. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/DOVE/DOVE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/DOVE/DOVE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/DOVE/DOVE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 939, 'width': 614, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/DOVE/DOVE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/DOVE/DOVE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How a Club Becomes a Salon for a Night', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How a Club Becomes A Salon For a Night', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T22:12:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/35c4a82b-dd37-5617-b3a0-818ee1a7a4fe', 'word_count': 512, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35c4a82b-dd37-5617-b3a0-818ee1a7a4fe'}, {'abstract': 'In the wake of a murder captured on “The First 48,” the intersection between crime and reality television feels risky, even as a new wave of shows has made the genre more ubiquitous.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/arts/television/17crime.html', 'snippet': 'In the wake of a murder captured on “The First 48,” the intersection between crime and reality television feels risky, even as a new wave of shows has made the genre more ubiquitous.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In May, during a police raid in Detroit, a 7-year-old girl, Aiyana Stanley-Jones, was shot in the head and killed as she slept on a living-room couch. It was an undeniable tragedy, and might have remained a local one but for an unusual circumstance: The police officers involved in the shooting were accompanied by cameras from “The First 48,” the reliably engrossing A&E show that documents the early stages of homicide investigations. Last month the girl’s family filed suit against A&E Television Networks, alleging that filming the raid was tantamount to encouraging it.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/CRIME1/CRIME1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 314, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/CRIME1/CRIME1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 314}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/CRIME1/CRIME1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 536, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/CRIME1/CRIME1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/CRIME1/CRIME1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Squad Cars, Sirens and Gangs, and the Cameras That Love Them', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Squad Cars, Sirens and Gangs, and the Cameras That Love Them', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T22:15:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c726b314-1cda-5453-87ac-bec81e547427', 'word_count': 1122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c726b314-1cda-5453-87ac-bec81e547427'}, {'abstract': 'At the Paramount party on Golden Globes weekend, Josh Brolin (and his wife, Diane Lane) talk about Ethan Coen’s reading habits, hard-to-understand voices and impressive talent of a very young actress.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/behind-a-hedgerow-talking-true-grit/', 'snippet': 'At the Paramount party on Golden Globes weekend, Josh Brolin (and his wife, Diane Lane) talk about Ethan Coen’s reading habits, hard-to-understand voices and impressive talent of a very young actress.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES— The Bagger is in place on the red carpet at the Golden Globes – well, we’re sitting on the floor near it, anyway, eating a cheese sandwich we brought from home; don’t hate us because we’re glamorous – so let’s take a moment to acknowledge the films that were overlooked by the Hollywood Foreign Press Association. There are so many – where to start? Well, the group doesn’t have a documentary category at all, for example. And as has been much noted, it entirely snubbed “True Grit,” the Coen brothers Western. Maybe they just have it in for the Coens? Last year’s “A Serious Man” was also nearly ignored. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Behind a Hedgerow, Talking ‘True Grit’', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golden Globes (Awards)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brolin, Josh', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coen, Ethan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lane, Diane', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hollywood Foreign Press Assn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T22:16:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/955344b8-7f8b-5c1f-9f72-add2fdc4e198', 'word_count': 532, 'uri': 'nyt://article/955344b8-7f8b-5c1f-9f72-add2fdc4e198'}, {'abstract': 'The members of the Screen Actors Guild and the American Federation of Television and Radio Artists have ratified a new three-year agreement with film and television studios and producers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/arts/television/17arts-PERFORMERSUN_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The members of the Screen Actors Guild and the American Federation of Television and Radio Artists have ratified a new three-year agreement with film and television studios and producers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The members of the Screen Actors Guild and the American Federation of Television and Radio Artists, which jointly represent more than 137,000 performers, have ratified a new three-year agreement with film and television studios and producers that calls for a 6 percent raise in wages and a 1.5 percent increase in employer contributions to retirement and medical plans. Ken Howard, president of the screen actors group, described the result, in which 93 percent of those voting favored the accord, as “a huge boon for members” at a time when the film and television industries are experiencing a sharp retrenchment. The Directors Guild of America has also reached a tentative agreement with the Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers, with final approval by members expected as early as this month. But the situation of the Writers Guild, which late in 2007 began a three-month strike that affected production timetables, continues to be uncertain. That group’s contract is scheduled to expire on May 1, but negotiations have not yet been scheduled. In a statement the producers’ alliance praised the performers’ groups for their “commitment to industry stability.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Performers’ Unions Approve Contract', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Performers’ Unions Approve Contract', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Screen Actors Guild', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN FEDERATION OF TELEVISION AND RADIO ARTISTS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T22:32:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbfb6c06-ee61-5b63-b69e-2412ef45a1b9', 'word_count': 187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbfb6c06-ee61-5b63-b69e-2412ef45a1b9'}, {'abstract': 'The Public Theater and LaMaMa are sponsoring an encore benefit performance on Monday night of “Being Harold Pinter,” the play that the Belarus Free Theater has been performing at the Under the Radar festival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/theater/17arts-BENEFITPERFO_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The Public Theater and LaMaMa are sponsoring an encore benefit performance on Monday night of “Being Harold Pinter,” the play that the Belarus Free Theater has been performing at the Under the Radar festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Public Theater and LaMaMa are sponsoring an encore benefit performance on Monday night of “Being Harold Pinter,” the play that the Belarus Free Theater has been performing at the Under the Radar festival, which ended on Sunday. Tom Stoppard and Tony Kushner will host the event, at the Public, which is meant to draw attention to the plight of the Belarussian troupe (including Yana Rusakevich, above), whose members have been subjected to arrest and harassment by the government of Belarus, a former Soviet republic now ruled by Aleksandr G. Lukashenko. Readings by celebrity guests will also be part of the event, which will be followed on Wednesday by a noon protest in front of Belarus’s mission to the United Nations and another evening benefit celebrating the group and its commitment to freedom of expression, this one at Le Poisson Rouge and featuring Mr. Stoppard and the novelists E. L. Doctorow and Don DeLillo.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Benefit Performance For Belarus Troupe', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Benefit Performance For Belarus Troupe', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Belarus Free Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T22:33:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/2392af80-cf41-5232-b922-a5dc627f0d7a', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2392af80-cf41-5232-b922-a5dc627f0d7a'}, {'abstract': 'The expeditions of pioneering naturalists often turned fatal, but their sacrifice was our gain.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/dying-for-discovery/', 'snippet': 'The expeditions of pioneering naturalists often turned fatal, but their sacrifice was our gain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Almost 20 years ago now, in western Ecuador, I traveled with a team of extraordinary biologists studying a remnant of forest as it was being hacked down around us. Al Gentry, a gangling figure in a grimy T-shirt and jeans frayed from chronic tree climbing, was a botanist whose strategy toward all hazards was to pretend that they didn’t exist. At one point, a tree came crashing down beside him after he lost his footing on a slope. Still on his back, he reached out for an orchid growing on the trunk and said, “Oh, that’s Gongora,” as casually as if he had just spotted an old friend on a city street.The team’s birder, Ted Parker, specialized in identifying bird species by sound alone. He started his work day before dawn, standing in the rain under a faded umbrella, his sneakers sunk to their high-tops in mud, whispering into a microcassette recorder about what he was hearing: “Scarlet-rumped cacique … a fasciated antshrike … two more pairs of Myrmeciza immaculata counter-singing. Dysithamnus puncticeps chorus, male and female …”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dying for Discovery', 'kicker': 'Specimens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMALS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birdwatching', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evolution', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Darwin, Charles Robert', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallace, Alfred Russel', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T22:35:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Conniff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Conniff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9635e83a-d980-5b86-84d9-a41987fb19f0', 'word_count': 1148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9635e83a-d980-5b86-84d9-a41987fb19f0'}, {'abstract': 'Dennis Byrd said it was “meaningful” for him to talk to Jets players on the eve of their playoff game against the Patriots.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/for-byrd-reconnecting-with-jets-is-special/', 'snippet': 'Dennis Byrd said it was “meaningful” for him to talk to Jets players on the eve of their playoff game against the Patriots.', 'lead_paragraph': ' FOXBOROUGH, Mass. — Last week, Rex Ryan tweaked Tom Brady and set himself as the protagonist versus New England Coach Bill Belichick. So on Saturday night, on the eve of the Jets’ American Football Conference divisional playoff game against the Patriots, Ryan allowed someone else to get his team motivated for the latest iteration of the rivalry. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Byrd, Reconnecting With Jets Is Special', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Byrd, Dennis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T22:39:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Viera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/27b70757-dd0f-573b-af78-7be053f69126', 'word_count': 466, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27b70757-dd0f-573b-af78-7be053f69126'}, {'abstract': 'The Nashville band Mona played at Union Pool in Williamsburg on Saturday, meshing its brand of internal angst with external pomp.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/arts/music/17mona.html', 'snippet': 'The Nashville band Mona played at Union Pool in Williamsburg on Saturday, meshing its brand of internal angst with external pomp.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The video for “Listen to Your Love,” the first single by the Nashville band Mona, teems with context. Crammed tight are clips of drag-racing muscle cars, buxom real-life Vargas girls in their underwear, Marilyn and Elvis and more. In between are shots of the band members getting their slick hair just so, roughhousing and wearing perfectly tight black jeans: rebels out of time.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/MONA/MONA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 374, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/MONA/MONA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 374}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/MONA/MONA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'w