/nyregion/20110117Church-slide-VNDW/20110117Church-slide-VNDW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/nyregion/20110117Church-slide-VNDW/20110117Church-slide-VNDW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/17/nyregion/20110117Church-slide-VNDW/20110117Church-slide-VNDW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Bronx, a Church on the Mend', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Churches (Buildings)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vandalism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T22:46:31+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'New York', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/f8b7226f-54cc-5dca-9c74-a802ff14e744', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/f8b7226f-54cc-5dca-9c74-a802ff14e744'}, {'abstract': '“Being Human,” a British series, is remade with an American slant for Syfy, not necessarily for the better.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/arts/television/17human.html', 'snippet': '“Being Human,” a British series, is remade with an American slant for Syfy, not necessarily for the better.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The popular British series “Being Human,” about a werewolf, a vampire and a ghost sharing a run-down house in Bristol, isn’t great television, and its use of scary monsters as a metaphor for adolescent (or in this case, 20-something) alienation has been old hat for well over a decade. (Yes, “Buffy” was that long ago.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/HUMAN/HUMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 742, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Relocated to America, Still Bloodthirsty', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Relocated to America, Still Bloodthirsty', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vampires', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Syfy Channel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T22:48:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1a9b126-2c62-52de-a856-033ba383ff22', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1a9b126-2c62-52de-a856-033ba383ff22'}, {'abstract': 'Two new television shows, “Harry’s Law” and “Fairly Legal,” feature female lawyers leaving the flavorless soup kettle of big-firm casework for odder pastures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/arts/television/17law.html', 'snippet': 'Two new television shows, “Harry’s Law” and “Fairly Legal,” feature female lawyers leaving the flavorless soup kettle of big-firm casework for odder pastures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is at least a little amusing to think of Harriet — Harry — Korn as Ally McBeal several decades later, having never shed her menopausal weight gain and only ballooning further, thanks to a diet of grease and packaged cupcakes. Played by Kathy Bates with a likable cynicism, Harry is the fatigued and disaffected lawyer at the center of “Harry’s Law” (beginning Monday on NBC), a woman who has clearly worked too hard at the expense of any real fulfillment, only to conclude that her job was no more thrilling than sorting mail.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/SUBLAW/SUBLAW-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 351, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/SUBLAW/SUBLAW-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 351}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/SUBLAW/SUBLAW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 848, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/SUBLAW/SUBLAW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/SUBLAW/SUBLAW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Law? She’ll Do It Her Way, Thank You', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Law? She’ll Do It Her Way, Thank You', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'USA Network', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T22:58:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/028cc740-1043-50fc-ba3d-c61656bd6a59', 'word_count': 909, 'uri': 'nyt://article/028cc740-1043-50fc-ba3d-c61656bd6a59'}, {'abstract': 'Jason Moran brings together a host of jazz players for a two-night event, most of them, like him, a student of Robert Morgan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/arts/music/17tribeca.html', 'snippet': 'Jason Moran brings together a host of jazz players for a two-night event, most of them, like him, a student of Robert Morgan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In music circles, around the turn of the new century, the phrase “from Houston” started to mean something by consensus. Not just in hip-hop and R&B — that was the time of Swishahouse Records, DJ Screw, UGK and Destiny’s Child — but, strangely enough, in jazz.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/TRIBECA1/TRIBECA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 809, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/TRIBECA1/TRIBECA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/TRIBECA1/TRIBECA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Houston’s Jazz Stars, Celebrated in TriBeCa', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Houston’s Jazz Stars, Celebrated In TriBeCa', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Houston (Tex)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moran, Jason', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T23:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd2e878f-f740-5da2-8d5b-3cf6ef3ec466', 'word_count': 963, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd2e878f-f740-5da2-8d5b-3cf6ef3ec466'}, {'abstract': 'Liz Gorski dreams of a fallen hero.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/missiles/', 'snippet': 'Liz Gorski dreams of a fallen hero.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY’S PUZZLE — Confused by today’s title? It’s part of a quote by a man whose leadership and strength we are celebrating today. The full quote, meant to highlight the folly of our national priorities, is, “We have guided missiles and misguided men.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday: Guided Missiles and Misguided Men', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T23:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4e455a1-5d11-549a-b7fb-7e0ba5c0d78e', 'word_count': 310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4e455a1-5d11-549a-b7fb-7e0ba5c0d78e'}, {'abstract': 'The Carpetbagger and others reported from the 68th annual Golden Globe awards.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/live-blogging-the-golden-globes/', 'snippet': 'The Carpetbagger and others reported from the 68th annual Golden Globe awards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sunday night the Hollywood Foreign Press Association handed out its Golden Globe awards at the Beverly Hilton Hotel. Melena Ryzik (a k a The Carpetbagger), Cathy Horyn, the Hollywood reporters Brooks Barnes and Michael Cieply and the television critic Mike Hale covered the red carpet and the ceremony live. Related Article | List of Winners and Nominees  | Red Carpet Slide Show | More Awards Season Coverage ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Social Network’ and ‘Glee’ Dominate at Golden Globes', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golden Globes (Awards)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-16T23:25:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/528e6b1a-83fe-585e-b56e-330333e64d50', 'word_count': 4082, 'uri': 'nyt://article/528e6b1a-83fe-585e-b56e-330333e64d50'}, {'abstract': 'Jean-Claude Duvalier returned to Haiti after 25 years in exile, but his reason was not immediately clear.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/world/americas/17haiti.html', 'snippet': 'Jean-Claude Duvalier returned to Haiti after 25 years in exile, but his reason was not immediately clear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MEXICO CITY — Haiti’s political crisis took a stunning turn on Sunday when Jean-Claude Duvalier, the dictator known as Baby Doc who was overthrown in 1986, arrived unexpectedly in the capital from exile in 