trayals of exquisite, often fragile young women in British and American films of the 1960s and ‘70s, died on Saturday in London. She was 72 and lived in London.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/OBITYORK1/OBITYORK1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/OBITYORK1/OBITYORK1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/17/arts/OBITYORK1/OBITYORK1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Susannah York, British Actress, Dies at 72', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Susannah York, 72, British Film Actress', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'York, Susannah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T04:14:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margalit Fox', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margalit', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fox', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/534f6023-27c3-54c8-bd40-41cbc42c3a3e', 'word_count': 672, 'uri': 'nyt://article/534f6023-27c3-54c8-bd40-41cbc42c3a3e'}, {'abstract': 'At least three radio stations defied the standards industry council by playing unedited versions of “Money for Nothing” by Dire Straits, which was banned for its use of an antigay slur.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/radio-ban-elicits-protests/', 'snippet': 'At least three radio stations defied the standards industry council by playing unedited versions of “Money for Nothing” by Dire Straits, which was banned for its use of an antigay slur.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OTTAWA — Over the last quarter-century, “Money for Nothing” by Dire Straits has moved from the Top 40 to the playlists of classic rock stations all over the world. But last week, a broadcast industry standards council ruled that the song was unfit for Canada’s airwaves.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Radio Ban Elicits Protests', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dire Straits', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T04:15:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Austen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/79013e8c-cd65-5716-8420-fd9457ad112d', 'word_count': 362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79013e8c-cd65-5716-8420-fd9457ad112d'}, {'abstract': 'Halfway through the term of the nation’s first African-American president, there is little sign of improvement in the dramatically unequal life prospects of black children.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/16/presidents-speech-especially-poignant-on-the-king-holiday/', 'snippet': 'Halfway through the term of the nation’s first African-American president, there is little sign of improvement in the dramatically unequal life prospects of black children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama’s speech after the Tucson shootings earned plaudits for framing the nation’s aspirations through the eyes of a fallen child.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'President’s Speech Especially Poignant on the King Holiday', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Martin Luther Jr', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T04:17:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Harwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f08e70f9-2116-5a14-bdec-311d32f07541', 'word_count': 744, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f08e70f9-2116-5a14-bdec-311d32f07541'}, {'abstract': 'The Congo-born Mr. Ongala, popular in the West from recordings and tours, was not afraid to sing about difficult issues, like the spread of AIDS.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/arts/music/17ongala.html', 'snippet': 'The Congo-born Mr. Ongala, popular in the West from recordings and tours, was not afraid to sing about difficult issues, like the spread of AIDS.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1990, as the AIDS epidemic was gathering strength in Africa, the Tanzanian songwriter, singer, guitarist and bandleader Remmy Ongala released an ebullient dance track called “Mambo Kwa Soksi” (“Things With Socks”). Its lyrics called for men to use condoms (“socks”) to prevent AIDS, and it stirred up controversy; Radio Tanzania refused to play it.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/subONGALA-obit/subONGALA-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 615, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/subONGALA-obit/subONGALA-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/subONGALA-obit/subONGALA-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Remmy Ongala, Tanzanian Musical Star, Dies at 63', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Remmy Ongala, 63, Tanzanian Music Star', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Congo, Democratic Republic of (Congo-Kinshasa)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 3, 'major': '