, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Living Wills', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospice Care', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T18:57:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane E. Brody', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Brody', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60f1a7ff-b981-58b2-87bc-4d435d69fbe7', 'word_count': 1108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60f1a7ff-b981-58b2-87bc-4d435d69fbe7'}, {'abstract': 'Questions about the value of insight get to the heart of how therapy works.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/health/views/18mind.html', 'snippet': 'Questions about the value of insight get to the heart of how therapy works.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is practically an article of faith among many therapists that self-understanding is a prerequisite for a happy life. Insight, the thinking goes, will free you from your psychological hang-ups and promote well-being.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/science/18mind/18mind-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 997, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/science/18mind/18mind-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/18/science/18mind/18mind-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Self-Knowledge Is Only the Beginning', 'kicker': 'Mind', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Self-Knowledge Is Only the Beginning', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Therapy and Rehabilitation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Anxiety and Stress', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T18:59:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Views', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard A. Friedman, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f228b8f-5895-5b60-b3d4-0880365a3043', 'word_count': 831, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f228b8f-5895-5b60-b3d4-0880365a3043'}, {'abstract': 'Labor hoarding, gone with the wind.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/productivity-over-the-business-cycle-wonkish/', 'snippet': 'Labor hoarding, gone with the wind.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alex Tabarrok wonders what I think about a subject that has been obsessing Brad DeLong lately: the apparent shift toward a regime in which productivity growth, which used to decelerate in recessions as firms hoarded workers, now rises instead. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Productivity Over the Business Cycle (Wonkish)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T18:59:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/465f29a4-6c65-502c-a0f9-9518e2e5c1a0', 'word_count': 423, 'uri': 'nyt://article/465f29a4-6c65-502c-a0f9-9518e2e5c1a0'}, {'abstract': 'Government opponents in North Africa have set themselves on fire in recent days in hopes that their protests will lead to the type of demonstrations that toppled the president of Tunisia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/world/africa/18egypt.html', 'snippet': 'Government opponents in North Africa have set themselves on fire in recent days in hopes that their protests will lead to the type of demonstrations that toppled the president of Tunisia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — The self-immolation of a 26-year-old Tunisian man a month ago that set off a popular uprising is inspiring similar acts of gruesome protest across North Africa.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Self-Immolation Protests Spread Across North Africa', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Self-Immolation Protests Spread Across North Africa', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mauritania', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bouteflika, Abdelaziz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALGERIA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hosni', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ben Ali, Zine El-Abidine', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tunisia', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T19:01:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mona El-Naggar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mona', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'El-Naggar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13dbd95b-df27-562a-b5e1-fec4f099eb67', 'word_count': 458, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13dbd95b-df27-562a-b5e1-fec4f099eb67'}, {'abstract': 'Root vegetables may not have the allure of summer produce, but they can be used a variety of nourishing winter dishes. Today: polenta with braised root vegetables.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/health/nutrition/17recipehealth.html', 'snippet': 'Root vegetables may not have the allure of summer produce, but they can be used a variety of nourishing winter dishes. Today: polenta with braised root vegetables.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Food writers tend to be exuberant about spring and summer vegetables, but a bit muted about cold-weather produce. Maybe it’s because summer’s offerings are fleeting in comparison to winter’s enduring, staid roots and tubers. That’s too bad: There are plenty of delicious, comforting dishes to be made with vegetables like kohlrabi, turnips, rutabaga, celeriac, carrots and turnips.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/science/17recipehealth/17recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/18/science/17recipehealth/17recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0,