tha', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Shulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'recipe', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8f0a438-068f-5179-913b-c2b99b5cf43c', 'word_count': 601, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8f0a438-068f-5179-913b-c2b99b5cf43c'}, {'abstract': 'Done properly, an embed requires at least one week with a small unit, usually a platoon or company, and going out with them every day.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/embedistan-outside-the-wire-off-the-message/', 'snippet': 'Done properly, an embed requires at least one week with a small unit, usually a platoon or company, and going out with them every day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AUSTIN, Tex. — At the end of his trip to Kabul in late March, President Obama declared after a high-profile military offensive that the Marines had pushed the Taliban out of the Afghan region of Marja, one of its major strongholds. But even as the president spoke those words, the Marines in Marja were telling a different story to an embedded reporter: The Taliban had retaken the momentum in much of Marja and stymied the troops’ strategy to win hearts and minds. Soon, high-ranking American officials began to acknowledge that Marja was not going nearly as well as hoped.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Embedistan: Outside the Wire, Off the Message', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Halberstam, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Marine Corps', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Marja (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T19:10:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard A. Oppel Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Oppel', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/760e7645-515c-53c7-b7d8-701cdba48adb', 'word_count': 1090, 'uri': 'nyt://article/760e7645-515c-53c7-b7d8-701cdba48adb'}, {'abstract': 'Day 3 at Pitti Uomo included a celebration of Trussardi’s centennial, luxurious sportswear at Brunello Cuccinelli, and the debut of men’s wear by Jane Carr.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/pitti-uomo-group-hug/', 'snippet': 'Day 3 at Pitti Uomo included a celebration of Trussardi’s centennial, luxurious sportswear at Brunello Cuccinelli, and the debut of men’s wear by Jane Carr.', 'lead_paragraph': ' NYTD.fullScreenSlideShowFactory.createSlideshow("#297", 592, 400, true, chameleonData);', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pitti Uomo | Group Hug', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T19:13:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Pask', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pask', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/57833ace-978d-579a-8717-9fd02e8ea52d', 'word_count': 530, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57833ace-978d-579a-8717-9fd02e8ea52d'}, {'abstract': 'The Weinstein Company/Relativity event, drawing on “The King’s Speech” and “Blue Valentine” for its decor, was an early hot spot for the stars.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/a-winner-for-best-party-production-design/', 'snippet': 'The Weinstein Company/Relativity event, drawing on “The King’s Speech” and “Blue Valentine” for its decor, was an early hot spot for the stars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — The Bagger didn’t make it to every party after the Golden Globes last night, but of the four we did attend, the Weinstein Company/Relativity bash earned the highest marks for production design. Drawing on its nominated films, “The King’s Speech” and “Blue Valentine,” the party at the Beverly Hilton Hotel was decorated to look like a British/suburban drawing room, complete with carpeting, vintage-looking tufted couches, chests of drawers painted with the Union Jack and decorated with photos from the movies. (All the extra production work that the director Derek Cianfrance did on “Blue Valentine,” having his actors live together in an upstate New York house for a month, paid off in decorating savings.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Winner for Best Party Production Design', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Douglas, Michael', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weinstein, Harvey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T19:16:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e2bbef5-6f35-57a6-9fc2-35a578794b00', 'word_count': 428, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e2bbef5-6f35-57a6-9fc2-35a578794b00'}, {'abstract': 'On The Lens, Dima Gavrysh documents New York’s coffee bars.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/coffee-on-camera/', 'snippet': 'On The Lens, Dima Gavrysh documents New York’s coffee bars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The photojournalist Dima Gavrysh, who has chronicled Uganda, Chernobyl and Afghanistan, chose a more languid locale for his latest project — the coffee bars of New York. The Lens blog has a slide show of some of the most appealing places he photographed, including Abraço Espresso, Stumptown Coffee Roasters and Blue Bottle Coffee, pictured here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Coffee on Camera', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T19:18:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6615a3d9-6ed0-5fc8-b29d-184e898eb9a1', 'word_count': 56, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6615a3d9-6ed0-5fc8-b29d-184e898eb9a1'}, {'abstract': 'Most people say they’ll pay for genetic tests even if the predictions are sometimes wrong, and most people don’t seem to be traumatized even when they receive bad news.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/science/18tier.html', 'snippet': 'Most people say they’ll pay for genetic tests even if the predictions are sometimes wrong, and most people don’t seem to be traumatized even when they receive bad news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When companies tried selling consumers the results of personal DNA tests, worried doctors and assorted health experts rushed to the public’s rescue. What if the risk assessments were inaccurate or inconsistent? What if people misinterpreted the results and did something foolish? What if they were traumatized by learning they were at high risk for Alzheimer’s or breast cancer or another disease?', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/