ad_paragraph': 'In Tuesday’s Science Times, the writer Nancy Stearns Bercaw describes her father’s decades-long preoccupation with preventing Alzheimer’s disease, which had killed her grandfather. Alzheimer’s was the reason her father became a neurologist in the first place, she writes: he “knew it was coming.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Resisting Alzheimer’s', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Alzheimer's Disease", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T20:03:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3cd5f59-de3c-5a3a-bf60-f4a4ef399312', 'word_count': 184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3cd5f59-de3c-5a3a-bf60-f4a4ef399312'}, {'abstract': 'Normann Copenhagen’s cheery Mormor dinnerware keeps the winter blahs in check.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/feeling-blue-and-white/', 'snippet': 'Normann Copenhagen’s cheery Mormor dinnerware keeps the winter blahs in check.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If winter is already starting to look long and chilly to you, maybe a little home retail therapy will help. Normann Copenhagen’s Mormor ceramic dinnerware — by Gry Fager, a 2008 graduate of the Danish Design School — is covered with a cheery blue-and-white windowpane check that was inspired by a dish towel and by old-school kitchens in general. (“Mormor” is Danish for grandmother.) In addition to Mormor Blue, the pieces are available in two textured white finishes: Mormor Ribbed and Mormor Square, which are also textile-inspired, but with a knitted look. The milk jug ($26) and sugar bowl ($23) give your breakfast table an easy update, and the buttering boards ($26 and $36) make you want to linger over that second slice of toast.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Feeling Blue … and White', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T20:05:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pilar Viladas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pilar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viladas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/531d39bf-09ca-5e2e-8b68-b24c2855ce75', 'word_count': 125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/531d39bf-09ca-5e2e-8b68-b24c2855ce75'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers reported in The Lancet this month that having a circumcised partner reduced a woman’s risk of catching human papillomaviruses by about 25 percent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/health/18global.html', 'snippet': 'Researchers reported in The Lancet this month that having a circumcised partner reduced a woman’s risk of catching human papillomaviruses by about 25 percent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Male circumcision, which has been shown to decrease a man’s risk of contracting the virus that causes AIDS, also appears to help protect his sexual partners against cervical cancer.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/health/18globalspan/18globalspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/18/health/18globalspan/18globalspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', '