ype': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/science/18essay/18essay-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/18/science/18essay/18essay-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Recalling a Fallen Star’s Legacy in High-Energy Particle Physics', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Recalling a Fallen Star’s Legacy In U.S. Particle Physics Quest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Large Hadron Collider', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Physics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fermi National Accelerator Laboratory', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CERN', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Energy Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T22:05:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dennis Overbye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dennis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Overbye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83ca3dd6-1ba5-5d1d-9558-f52e24729bfc', 'word_count': 1230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83ca3dd6-1ba5-5d1d-9558-f52e24729bfc'}, {'abstract': 'NBC’s coverage of the 68th annual Golden Globe Awards drew about 17 million viewers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/arts/television/18arts-GRIDIRONTOPS_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'NBC’s coverage of the 68th annual Golden Globe Awards drew about 17 million viewers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NBC’s coverage of the 68th annual Golden Globe Awards drew about 17 million viewers, an audience virtually identical in size to last year’s show, according to Nielsen’s estimates. Even so, the awards show, which was hosted by Ricky Gervais, above, for the second consecutive year, left NBC in second place for the night, trailing CBS, which led over all largely because of an overrun of its afternoon football playoff coverage. That game, between the New York Jets and the New England Patriots, attracted more than 40 million viewers in the 7 p.m. hour, boosting CBS’s “60 Minutes” at 8 (17.3 million). At 9 and 10, CBS broadcast new episodes of “Undercover Boss” (13.3 million) and “CSI: Miami” (10.3 million). ABC ranked third, garnering its most viewers for “Desperate Housewives” at 9 (10.1 million), while Fox placed fourth with new episodes of its animated comedy lineup.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gridiron Tops Globes', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gridiron Tops Globes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T22:05:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/dcdee3f9-994e-5ebc-a568-bb04522e9d6f', 'word_count': 150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dcdee3f9-994e-5ebc-a568-bb04522e9d6f'}, {'abstract': 'The Finnish pianist Juho Pohjonen chose some difficult works to play at the Sunday Morning Coffee Concert at Lincoln Center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/arts/music/18juho.html', 'snippet': 'The Finnish pianist Juho Pohjonen chose some difficult works to play at the Sunday Morning Coffee Concert at Lincoln Center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Informal concerts at unconventional times are increasingly popular with audiences. One such series, the Sunday Morning Coffee Concerts at Lincoln Center, presents 60-minute programs at the Walter Reade Theater at 11 a.m. The audience gathers afterward for coffee and muffins and a chance to meet the artist. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/JUHO/JUHO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/JUHO/JUHO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/JUHO/JUHO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/JUHO/JUHO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/JUHO/JUHO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Exploring Complex Works, Before You’ve Even Had Your Coffee', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Exploring Complex Works, Before You’ve Even Had Your Coffee', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lincoln Center for the Performing Arts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pohjonen, Juho', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T22:07:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d678ba0b-0e56-5402-a1fe-ae4af145499c', 'word_count': 507, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d678ba0b-0e56-5402-a1fe-ae4af145499c'}, {'abstract': 'In his first interview since major surgery, former Vice President Dick Cheney also predicts that Barack Obama will be a one-term president.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/cheney-considering-heart-transplant/', 'snippet': 'In his first interview since major surgery, former Vice President Dick Cheney also predicts that Barack Obama will be a one-term president.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Former Vice President Dick Cheney said he has not decided whether to seek a heart transplant for what he called his “end-stage heart failure” but said he will make that decision “at some point.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cheney Considering Heart Transplant', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transplants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cheney, Dick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NBC News', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T22:09:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5c8f40e-90a6-503b-bcbb-0554019646ef', 'word_count': 784, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5c8f40e-90a6-503b-bcbb-0554019646ef'}, {'abstract': 'The new year already has a quickening beat for Michael John LaChiusa, the Tony-nominated composer and lyricist of challenging works of musical theater like Broadway’s ‘Marie Christine’ and ‘The Wild Party.’', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/theater/18arts-ONECOMPOSERM_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The new year already has a quickening beat for Michael John LaChiusa, the Tony-nominated composer and lyricist of challenging works of musical theater like Broadway’s ‘Marie Christine’ and ‘The Wild Party.’', 'lead_paragraph': 'The new year already has a quickening beat for Michael John LaChiusa, the Tony-nominated composer and lyricist of challenging works of musical theater like Broadway’s “Marie Christine” and “The Wild Party.” Last week the Public Theater and others held a developmental workshop for his new musical, “Giant,” adapted from Edna Ferber’s epic novel about love, oil, and ranching among Texas alpha males and the women who love them. The Public and the Dallas Theater Center had already announced that they were pursuing a co-production of “Giant,” which had its world premiere at the Signature Theater in Arlington, Va., in 2009. Oskar Eustis, artistic director of the Public, said in an interview that Dallas Theater Center was planning to mount its production of “Giant” next winter. “How fast we’ll go from Dallas to New York City depends on how much progress we make on the show between now and then,” Mr. Eustis said. It seems safe to say that 2012 is likely for the New York run of “Giant.” Mr. LaChiusa wrote the music and lyrics, while the book is by Sybille Pearson (a Tony nominee for best book for the 1983 musical “Baby”). Michael Greif (“Next to Normal,” “Rent”) is the director. In addition to “Giant,” the Off Broadway theater company Transport Group is preparing to start performances in March for a revival of Mr. LaChiusa’s “Hello Again,” a musical featuring a series of interconnected love stories through the 20th century. And Transport Group also plans to stage the world premiere of another LaChiusa musical, “Queen of the Mist,” starting in October. That show centers on a real-life woman who survived a plunge over Niagara Falls. The two-time Tony nominee Mary Testa (“On the Town,” “42nd Street”) is set to star. Both shows will be directed by Jack Cummings III.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'One Composer, Many Productions', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'One Composer, Many Productions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T22:10:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/02224e09-2d41-527f-ae84-970328a503f5', 'word_count': 307, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02224e09-2d41-527f-ae84-970328a503f5'}, {'abstract': 'After donning a nun’s habit for his play “The Divine Sister,” Charles Busch, the writer and actor, will turn to the familiar Jewish rite of the Passover seder for a new work called “Olive and the Bitter Herbs.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/theater/18arts-PRIMARYSTAGE_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'After donning a nun’s habit for his play “The Divine Sister,” Charles Busch, the writer and actor, will turn to the familiar Jewish rite of the Passover seder for a new work called “Olive and the Bitter Herbs.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'After donning a nun’s habit for his play “The Divine Sister,” Charles Busch, the writer and actor, will turn to the familiar Jewish rite of the Passover seder for a new work called “Olive and the Bitter Herbs,” which will open the 2011-12 season of Primary Stages, that Off Broadway theater company said on Monday. In Mr. Busch’s new play, the title character, Olive, finds herself reluctantly hosting a seder for the neighbors in her apartment building while contending with what she thinks is a ghost that she sees in her mirror. Press representatives for Primary Stages said “Olive” would begin preview performances at 59E59 Theaters on July 26, with an opening night of Aug. 9; its limited run is to end on Sept. 3. Other productions planned by Primary Stages for its new season include “The Morini Strad,” a play written by Willy Holtzman about the concert violinist Erica Morini and her Stradivarius. Directed by Casey Childs, that play will begin previews on March 20, 2012, with an opening night of April 3. Another play, called “In Mother Words,” conceived by Susan R. Rose and Joan Stein, and featuring family stories written by Leslie Ayvazian, David Cale, Jessica Goldberg, Beth Henley, Lameece Issaq, Lisa Loomer, Michele Lowe, Marco Pennette, Lisa Ramirez, Theresa Rebeck, Luanne Rice, Annie Weisman and Cheryl L. West, is scheduled for this fall. No casting for the productions was immediately announced.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Primary Stages Lineup Leads With a Seder Play', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Primary Stages Lineup Leads With a Seder Play', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T22:12:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbaf5ff5-f1c6-524c-a43e-2a7c732762e9', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbaf5ff5-f1c6-524c-a43e-2a7c732762e9'}, {'abstract': 'A plucky little troupe is breezily overcoming many of the problems that appear to be plaguing “Spider-Man: Turn Off the Dark,” which is next door.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/theater/18cymbeline.html', 'snippet': 'A plucky little troupe is breezily overcoming many of the problems that appear to be plaguing “Spider-Man: Turn Off the Dark,” which is next door.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Have Julie Taymor and the rest of the team for “Spider-Man: Turn Off the Dark” taken a look at what’s happening right next door to their beleaguered extravaganza? At the tiny New Victory Theater — which sits baby cheek by giant’s jowl to the Foxwoods Theater (lair to the opulent Spider) — a plucky little (and I mean little) troupe is addressing and breezily overcoming many of the same problems that appear to be plaguing Ms. Taymor and company.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/CYMBELINE/CYMBELINE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/CYMBELINE/CYMBELINE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/CYMBELINE/CYMBELINE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 843, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/CYMBELINE/CYMBELINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/CYMBELINE/CYMBELINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Simply Shakespeare, No Tangled Web', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Cymbeline'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Simply Shakespeare, No Tangled Web', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Victory Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fiasco Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T22:15:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c199a331-2099-5be6-9639-319dc52ac421', 'word_count': 965, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c199a331-2099-5be6-9639-319dc52ac421'}, {'abstract': 'T’s features director, Jacob Brown, weighs in on men’s fashions at Milan Fashion Week.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/milan-fashion-week-first-impressions/', 'snippet': 'T’s features director, Jacob Brown, weighs in on men’s fashions at Milan Fashion Week.', 'lead_paragraph': ' NYTD.fullScreenSlideShowFactory.createSlideshow("#475", 592, 400, true, chameleonData);', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Milan Fashion Week | First Impressions', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T22:22:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jacob Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jacob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d62d349c-a8de-5106-9d72-b925a691a016', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d62d349c-a8de-5106-9d72-b925a691a016'}, {'abstract': 'Concerns about accuracy led the History channel not to run “The Kennedys,” a scripted mini-series starring Greg Kinnear.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/arts/television/18kennedy.html', 'snippet': 'Concerns about accuracy led the History channel not to run “The Kennedys,” a scripted mini-series starring Greg Kinnear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A promotional trailer for “The Kennedys,” a multimillion-dollar mini-series prepared for the History channel, suggests it will offer a sweeping inside look at the backrooms and bedrooms of that political clan. There are stylized re-enactments of the life of President John F. Kennedy and his family, and a title card that reads, “Behind the public image lies the story of an American dynasty.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/KENNEDYS/KENNEDYS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/KENNEDYS/KENNEDYS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/KENNEDYS/KENNEDYS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 722, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/KENNEDYS/KENNEDYS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/KENNEDYS/KENNEDYS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dramatizing Camelot', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dramatizing Camelot', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dallek, Robert', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holmes, Katie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennedy, John Fitzgerald', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennedy Family', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenwald, Robert', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'History Channel', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sorensen, Theodore C', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kinnear, Greg', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Surnow, Joel', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T22:28:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba0b01c7-c189-57e0-a273-6b79b372639f', 'word_count': 1538, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba0b01c7-c189-57e0-a273-6b79b372639f'}, {'abstract': 'Keith Jarrett brought his improvisational skills to Carnegie Hall, but his spirit coughed up his muse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/arts/music/18jarrett.html', 'snippet': 'Keith Jarrett brought his improvisational skills to Carnegie Hall, but his spirit coughed up his muse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Keith Jarrett began each piece of his solo concert on Sunday night at Carnegie Hall, he’d sit at the piano, lean over it like someone genuflecting, and cock his ear to the keyboard as if listening to what it might suggest. In his solo concerts since the 1970s, Mr. Jarrett has committed himself to spontaneous improvisation, to ideas that surface in the moment. But on this night the muse was stingy, rarely offering him more than miniatures. And Mr. Jarrett grew increasingly cranky.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Hints of Genius, a Muse Is Expelled', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Hints Of Genius, A Muse Is Expelled', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnegie Hall', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jarrett, Keith', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T22:30:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a42fa638-a350-59d2-9821-91249af8c68f', 'word_count': 592, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a42fa638-a350-59d2-9821-91249af8c68f'}, {'abstract': 'PBS has a new monthly magazine edition of “Frontline,” which features stories about national security and airline safety on its premiere.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/arts/television/18frontline.html', 'snippet': 'PBS has a new monthly magazine edition of “Frontline,” which features stories about national security and airline safety on its premiere.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Presumably PBS calls its new monthly magazine edition of “Frontline” a “fast turnaround” version of that venerable public-affairs program because “somewhat less slow turnaround” didn’t test well with target audiences. Certainly the two opening reports of Tuesday’s three-segment premiere (the only parts provided for review) don’t exactly scream breaking news.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/FRONTLINE/FRONTLINE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 571, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Safety First? All Signs Point to No', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Safety First? All Signs Point to No', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting Service', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Frontline Ltd', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T22:31:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/84c1a644-c66f-59fe-984c-53150377d49c', 'word_count': 572, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84c1a644-c66f-59fe-984c-53150377d49c'}, {'abstract': 'The only real surprise at last night’s Golden Globe awards came on the red carpet: It looked as if Los Angeles was in the grips of a stylist strike.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/it-sure-wasnt-pretty-a-golden-globes-recap/', 'snippet': 'The only real surprise at last night’s Golden Globe awards came on the red carpet: It looked as if Los Angeles was in the grips of a stylist strike.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last night’s Golden Globe awards had the drama of an episode of “This Old House.” David Fincher and Aaron Sorkin’s “The Social Network” swept the ceremony winning best director, screenplay and picture, while favorites Colin Firth and Natalie Portman took the honors in acting. The only real surprise came on the red carpet. Outside of a few young things wearing neutral tones, it looked as if Los Angeles was in the grips of a stylist strike. What’s with the butterfly sleeves? And the French knots? And that gimmicky neckline on one to be forever nameless redhead that made her look like she was wearing half a Snuggie without sleeves. While we were thrilled to see emerald green make a resurgence on the red carpet, wouldn’t it have been nice if the dresses looked a little less like upholstery? Overall, the women looked kind of matronly – ironic in a town known for its desperate attempts to look young. With the Academy Awards about a month away, let’s hope that sartorial slapfest is a little more exciting. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'It Sure Wasn’t Pretty | A Golden Globes Recap', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T22:45:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By T Magazine', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/45901fed-8226-5a88-b38c-80bbec56deec', 'word_count': 179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45901fed-8226-5a88-b38c-80bbec56deec'}, {'abstract': 'As the storm over Rickygate continues, we present a selection of quotes and moments from the press room at the Golden Globes.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/a-grab-bag-of-celebrity-reaction/', 'snippet': 'As the storm over Rickygate continues, we present a selection of quotes and moments from the press room at the Golden Globes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES— As the storm over Rickygate continues – Philip Berk, the president of the Hollywood Foreign Press and a target of Mr. Gervais’s jokes at the Golden Globes, said that Mr. Gervais “definitely crossed the line” in a way that was, oh right, true to form for the comedian, and just happened to slightly bump the ratings – the Bagger would like to offer up a grab bag of quotes and moments from the press room to distract you from the brewing comedy non-troversy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Grab Bag of Celebrity Reaction', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golden Globes (Awards)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bening, Annette', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'De Niro, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Firth, Colin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Portman, Natalie', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rudin, Scott', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T23:12:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f08a0bbe-8eb3-5d40-8746-e49ae97b38cc', 'word_count': 408, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f08a0bbe-8eb3-5d40-8746-e49ae97b38cc'}, {'abstract': 'This Giambattista Valli leopard dress — with its panoply of window panes — stopped us in our tracks.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/just-one-look-giambattista-valli-pre-fall-collection/', 'snippet': 'This Giambattista Valli leopard dress — with its panoply of window panes — stopped us in our tracks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Pre-Fall Collections, being shown in New York over the next several weeks, arrive in stores in May to fill the gap between bikinis and furs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Giambattista Valli Pre-Fall Collection', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T23:15:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alix Browne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/50d421ec-ef0f-5edd-873f-89979a392942', 'word_count': 97, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50d421ec-ef0f-5edd-873f-89979a392942'}, {'abstract': 'Baristas at Starbucks for the first time were given holiday pay for working on Martin Luther King’s Birthday. But a labor group isn’t satisfied.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/starbucks-workers-celebrate-and-protest/', 'snippet': 'Baristas at Starbucks for the first time were given holiday pay for working on Martin Luther King’s Birthday. But a labor group isn’t satisfied.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Each year since 2008, members of the Industrial Workers of the World Starbucks Workers Union had demonstrated on Martin Luther King‘s Birthday, protesting that the company’s baristas who worked on the holiday were not given holiday wages.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Starbucks Workers Celebrate, and Protest', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T23:30:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colin Moynihan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moynihan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/db002d2b-2a31-59a6-ae4c-b75afc71fa6d', 'word_count': 567, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db002d2b-2a31-59a6-ae4c-b75afc71fa6d'}, {'abstract': 'The Mets are on the verge of signing the free agent starting pitcher Chris Young — a 6-foot-10, 280-pound right-handed pitcher with a history of shoulder troubles — according to two major league executives who have been informed of the negotiations.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/mets-reach-agreement-with-chris-young/', 'snippet': 'The Mets are on the verge of signing the free agent starting pitcher Chris Young — a 6-foot-10, 280-pound right-handed pitcher with a history of shoulder troubles — according to two major league executives who have been informed of the negotiation...', 'lead_paragraph': 'In November, 2006, Chris Capuano and Chris Young, two well-educated young pitchers, were teammates on a major league All-Star team traveling in Japan. The two bonded on that trip, have remained friends since, and are now close to being reunited in Flushing on what could be baseball’s smartest pitching staff.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mets Reach Agreement With Chris Young', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Young, Chris', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-17T23:31:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 