': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37b12012-6382-5fa1-b57d-08a6b120bcc2', 'word_count': 755, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37b12012-6382-5fa1-b57d-08a6b120bcc2'}, {'abstract': 'As the Patriots cleaned out their lockers a day after being ousted from the playoffs by the Jets, the tone was understated.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/sports/football/18patriots.html', 'snippet': 'As the Patriots cleaned out their lockers a day after being ousted from the playoffs by the Jets, the tone was understated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOXBOROUGH, Mass. \x97 Bill Belichick was his usual surly self in his year-end assessment of his team, clipped and terse as ever. In the New England locker room, the only sounds came from rustling garbage bags. Even for the say-less, do-more Patriots, it was a quiet afternoon here. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/sports/PATRIOTS/PATRIOTS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 870, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/sports/PATRIOTS/PATRIOTS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/18/sports/PATRIOTS/PATRIOTS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Patriots, Subdued Words and More Questions', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Day After Defeat, Belichick and Patriots Remain Subdued', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Belichick, Bill', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Patriots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Branch, Deion', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-18T00:53:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Viera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfb43ebc-7675-5448-a8a9-cdff6a0077f8', 'word_count': 746, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfb43ebc-7675-5448-a8a9-cdff6a0077f8'}, {'abstract': 'Election observers in southern Sudan said on Monday that the region’s referendum on independence last week was transparent and credible and that southern independence was likely.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/world/africa/18briefs-sudan.html', 'snippet': 'Election observers in southern Sudan said on Monday that the region’s referendum on independence last week was transparent and credible and that southern independence was likely.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Election observers in southern Sudan said on Monday that the region’s referendum on independence last week was transparent and credible and that southern independence was likely. “The referendum process to date is broadly consistent with international standards for democratic elections,” said the Carter Center of Atlanta, based on election monitoring. On Sunday, early polling results released in the capital, Juba, showed voters choosing independence from the north by an average margin of over 95 percent, with a 95 percent voter turnout. Southern independence would take effect July 9. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sudan: Secession Likely, Monitors Say', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | AFRICA', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sudan: Secession Likely, Monitors Say', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Referendums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sudan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting and Voters', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-18T00:55:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Josh Kron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9f22cad-6274-5262-a22e-114539cd4c1c', 'word_count': 89, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9f22cad-6274-5262-a22e-114539cd4c1c'}, {'abstract': 'Echoes of Capra in a Palin book.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/17/exceptionalism-faith-and-freedom-palins-america/', 'snippet': 'Echoes of Capra in a Palin book.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her Facebook reflections on the events in Arizona, Sarah Palin observes that our government was not designed for “perfect men and women.” It is because we are not angels, she says , that we require a system of government that acknowledges “the inevitable conflicts” produced by our “imperfect passions” and yet provides mechanisms — free speech, vigorous debate — that allow us to settle those conflicts without resorting to “dueling pistols.” That, she declares, is the system the Founders designed, and it is part of “why America is exceptional,” an assertion she makes twice.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Exceptionalism, Faith and Freedom: Palin’s America', 'kicker': 'Stanley Fish', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-18T01:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stanley Fish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stanley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6459f171-b745-558d-b4de-8cb3e79a5439', 'word_count': 1436, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6459f171-b745-558d-b4de-8cb3e79a5439'}, {'abstract': 'At first, quarterback Mark Sanchez was asked not to lose; now he is starting to play a leading role in winning.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/sports/football/18sanchez.html', 'snippet': 'At first, quarterback Mark Sanchez was asked not to lose; now he is starting to play a leading role in winning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FLORHAM PARK, N.J. \x97 For most of the last two seasons, the Jets trotted Mark Sanchez onto the field with a simple if somewhat odd directive for a franchise quarterback and the highest-paid player in team history: do not screw this up.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/sports/JPSANCHEZ/JPSANCHEZ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/sports/JPSANCHEZ/JPSANCHEZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/18/sports/JPSANCHEZ/JPSANCHEZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jets’ Mark Sanchez Starts to Lead by Not Losing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Staying Out of Victory’s Way, Jets’ Sanchez Starts to Lead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanchez, Mark', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Rex', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-18T01:02:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Greg Bishop', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Greg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bishop', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a613c364-05c6-58bb-b057-d588ef7a987f', 'word_count': 1041, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a613c364-05c6-58bb-b057-d588ef7a987f'}, {'abstract': 'Speakers honoring the Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King on Monday speculated about the positions he might have had in 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/nyregion/18nyc.html', 'snippet': 'Speakers honoring the Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King on Monday speculated about the positions he might have had in 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As they did in other corners of the city and the country, shadows of Arizona hovered gloomily on Monday over Brooklyn’s annual tribute to the Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/nyregion/SUB-NYC2/SUB-NYC2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, '