Capturing Shifts Between Ecstasy and Anguish', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Capturing Shifts Between Ecstasy and Anguish', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Frick Collection', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Austrian Cultural Forum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-18T23:32:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2abf4f1-93f3-5550-8f54-1242b5a6c8d2', 'word_count': 632, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2abf4f1-93f3-5550-8f54-1242b5a6c8d2'}, {'abstract': 'CBS’s block of comedies delivered their best ratings of the season on Monday night, lifting the network to No. 1 on the night among the major broadcast networks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/arts/television/19arts-ALAUGHERFORC_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'CBS’s block of comedies delivered their best ratings of the season on Monday night, lifting the network to No. 1 on the night among the major broadcast networks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CBS’s block of comedies delivered their best ratings of the season on Monday night, lifting the network to No. 1 on the night among the major broadcast networks. According to Nielsen’s estimates, 15.6 million viewers tuned in at 9 p.m. for “Two and a Half Men” while 13 million watched “Mike & Molly” at 9:30. At 10, CBS’s “Hawaii Five-0” earned 10.6 million viewers but lagged slightly behind the premiere of “Harry’s Law” (11 million), NBC’s new legal drama. Fox ranked second for the night, with NBC and ABC finishing in a virtual tie for third.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Laugher for CBS', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Laugher for CBS', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corp', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-18T23:37:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/867c33d3-b04d-50ac-ac02-b245b2fb8c0f', 'word_count': 99, 'uri': 'nyt://article/867c33d3-b04d-50ac-ac02-b245b2fb8c0f'}, {'abstract': 'The musical comedy team of Michael Garin and Mardie Millit take a whirlwind world tour at the Metropolitan Room.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/arts/music/19garin.html', 'snippet': 'The musical comedy team of Michael Garin and Mardie Millit take a whirlwind world tour at the Metropolitan Room.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Little is sacred to Michael Garin and Mardie Millit, a smirking musical comedy team whose show, “Sleepless in September,” has been playing intermittently at the Metropolitan Room for the last four months. A whirlwind world tour in several languages, it begins in South Africa (“Pata Pata”) and ends on “Route 66,” after visits to Italy, South America, France and Israel.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/19/arts/GARIN/GARIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/19/arts/GARIN/GARIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/19/arts/GARIN/GARIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/19/arts/GARIN/GARIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/19/arts/GARIN/GARIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Songs in Several Languages, Wisecracks Spread Throughout', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Songs in Several Languages, Wisecracks Spread Throughout', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Room', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garin, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-18T23:45:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8ef745c-94d4-5412-8542-48c12ce2b89d', 'word_count': 235, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8ef745c-94d4-5412-8542-48c12ce2b89d'}, {'abstract': 'Milton Rogovin, a social documentarian on a par with Jacob Riis, has died at the age of 101.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/parting-glance-milton-rogovin-101/', 'snippet': 'Milton Rogovin, a social documentarian on a par with Jacob Riis, has died at the age of 101.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Milton Rogovin, an empathetic social documentarian who — like Jacob Riis — put a face on the faceless poor, died Tuesday, a month after celebrating his 101st birthday. Benjamin Genocchio has written the obituary for The New York Times. Mr. Rogovin himself narrated an audio slide show of his pictures, “The Compassionate Eye,” which appeared in April 2009 on Lens, accompanying “Voices Silenced, Faces Preserved,” with text by Randy Kennedy and pictures by Fred R. Conrad, in the Arts & Leisure section.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Parting Glance: Milton Rogovin, 101', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kramer, Hilton', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rogovin, Milton', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Buffalo (NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T00:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Dunlap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dunlap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2aae2e3-1e04-51b3-8a02-d8a01b9f0dac', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2aae2e3-1e04-51b3-8a02-d8a01b9f0dac'}, {'abstract': 'Wall Street loves the fees from break-ups. But investors may want to question the financial alchemy.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/a-test-to-see-if-the-parts-are-worth-more-than-the-whole/', 'snippet': 'Wall Street loves the fees from break-ups. But investors may want to question the financial alchemy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ITT, Fortune Brands and Marathon Oil have all recently announced breakups, and Sara Lee is said to be contemplating one. Cargill announced on Tuesday that it would spin off its Mosaic unit. Market analysts contend that these businesses are too diverse and that separating them will allow the market to fully recognize each company’s inherent value. The real question, though, is whether this will really create that extra value.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Test to See if the Parts Are Worth More Than the Whole', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fortune Brands Incorporated', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sara Lee Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T00:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Davidoff Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Davidoff', 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/592b51c9-3cf0-508a-b69a-ad1fa5c9d108', 'word_count': 977, 'uri': 'nyt://article/592b51c9-3cf0-508a-b69a-ad1fa5c9d108'}, {'abstract': 'Animal Farm in Orwell, Vt., now sells real buttermilk, a rarity that is not as thick and tangy as what is found in supermarkets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/dining/19milk.html', 'snippet': 'Animal Farm in Orwell, Vt., now sells real buttermilk, a rarity that is not as thick and tangy as what is found in supermarkets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Real buttermilk, the liquid that’s left after butter is separated from cream, is a rarity. It’s not as thick or as tangy as what’s sold in supermarkets as buttermilk, which is just milk with added cultures. At Animal Farm in Orwell, Vt., the owner, Diane St. Clair, hand-skims the cream that naturally rises to the top of her Jersey cows’ milk, and cultures and churns it for her butter, which she does not make in hot weather. Now she has started selling the buttermilk that remains, in Vermont and New York. Take out that biscuit recipe, consider pancakes or waffles for the weekend, or plan on fried chicken and coleslaw, for it provides a subtler grace note for cooking and baking than supermarket buttermilk. Before you know it, a quart will be gone, especially if you discover how pleasant it is to drink.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/19/dining/STUFF-milk/STUFF3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 850, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/19/dining/STUFF-milk/STUFF3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/19/dining/STUFF-milk/STUFF3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Buttermilk (the Real Thing)', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Buttermilk (the Real Thing)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dairy Products', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T00:01:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_mat