 York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Screening for Alzheimer’s', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Alzheimer's Disease", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T00:40:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f10564b8-49fc-5f15-8194-9d09af6d9237', 'word_count': 123, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f10564b8-49fc-5f15-8194-9d09af6d9237'}, {'abstract': 'Prime Minister Manmohan Singh, widely viewed as silent amid corruption, inflation and infighting, is expected to reshuffle his cabinet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/world/asia/19delhi.html', 'snippet': 'Prime Minister Manmohan Singh, widely viewed as silent amid corruption, inflation and infighting, is expected to reshuffle his cabinet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW DELHI \x97 Few leaders are more respected globally than Prime Minister Manmohan Singh of India. President Obama has described him as a historic figure, close friend and valued adviser. (So, for that matter, did President George W. Bush.) When Newsweek ranked world leaders, Mr. Singh ranked first, winning praise for his modesty and incorruptibility. He was described as the “leader that other leaders love.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/19/world/DELHI/DELHI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 853, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': Non