f pop, was perhaps best known for his television show “Don Kirshner’s Rock Concert.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/arts/music/19kirshner.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Kirshner, a guiding force during the Brill Building era of pop, was perhaps best known for his television show “Don Kirshner’s Rock Concert.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Don Kirshner, the music publisher of Brill Building hits like “Will You Love Me Tomorrow” and “You’ve Lost That Lovin’ Feelin,’\xa0” who later served as a deadpan Ed Sullivan for Kiss, the Ramones and others with his 1970s television show, “Don Kirshner’s Rock Concert,” died on Monday in Boca Raton, Fla., where he lived. He was 76.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/19/obituaries/19kirshner-cnd/19kirshner-cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 856, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/19/obituaries/19kirshner-cnd/19kirshner-cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/19/obituaries/19kirshner-cnd/19kirshner-cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Don Kirshner, Shaper of Hit Records, Dies at 76', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Don Kirshner, 76, TV Host And Shaper of Hit Records', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T04:15:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/b116117a-6300-5d2f-abb8-1c03b153a6e2', 'word_count': 992, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b116117a-6300-5d2f-abb8-1c03b153a6e2'}, {'abstract': 'The talks scheduled between Iran and the five permanent Security Council members plus Germany should lead to “comprehensive cooperation” to address regional problems, said the Iranian ambassador to the United Nations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/world/19briefs-nations.html', 'snippet': 'The talks scheduled between Iran and the five permanent Security Council members plus Germany should lead to “comprehensive cooperation” to address regional problems, said the Iranian ambassador to the United Nations.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The talks scheduled for Friday between Iran and the five permanent Security Council members plus Germany should lead to “comprehensive cooperation” to address regional problems, Mohammad Khazaee, the Iranian ambassador to the United Nations, said at a news conference. Although previous Iranian attempts to shift the focus away from its nuclear program prompted objections from other negotiators, Mr. Khazaee said the talks, in Istanbul, could lead to cooperation on problems like disarmament and the Arab-Israeli conflict. Mr. Khazaee said the West and other powers should stop making the mistake of ignoring Iran’s status as the regional “heavyweight.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Official Is Optimistic About Iran Talks', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | United Nations', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Official Is Optimistic About Iran Talks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARMS CONTROL AND LIMITATION AND DISARMAMENT', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Security Council (UN)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T04:15:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil MacFarquhar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacFarquhar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef319f9d-19b1-5c32-b0d8-cb3162126d31', 'word_count': 99, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef319f9d-19b1-5c32-b0d8-cb3162126d31'}, {'abstract': 'Corrections appearing in print on January 19, 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/pageoneplus/corrections.html', 'snippet': 'Corrections appearing in print on January 19, 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'INTERNATIONAL', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T04:18:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/c279ea55-7e3b-58ec-a845-6635f31c1001', 'word_count': 960, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c279ea55-7e3b-58ec-a845-6635f31c1001'}, {'abstract': 'The Irish prime minister, Brian Cowen, won a confidence vote among lawmakers for the governing Fianna Fail party, virtually assuring that he will lead the party into a general election that is expected to take place by the end of March.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/world/europe/19briefs-ireland.html', 'snippet': 'The Irish prime minister, Brian Cowen, won a confidence vote among lawmakers for the governing Fianna Fail party, virtually assuring that he will lead the party into a general election that is expected to take place by the end of March.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Irish prime minister, Brian Cowen, won a confidence vote among lawmakers for the governing Fianna Fail party on Tuesday, virtually assuring that he will lead the party into a general election that is expected to take place by the end of March. Uncertainty over the party leadership has grown since the Cowen government accepted harsh terms in December for a $113-billion international bailout of Ireland’s debt-ridden economy. After the ballot, Mr. Cowen accepted the resignation of Micheal Martin, the foreign minister, who had demanded over the weekend that Mr. Cowen resign. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ireland: Backing for Cowen', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ireland: Backing for Cowen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cowen, Brian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ireland', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T04:18:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By John F. Burns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifier': No