or the firm.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/former-d-e-shaw-manager-to-launch-credit-fund-for-golub-capital/', 'snippet': 'Daniel Posner is headed to Golub Capital to start a credit hedge fund — a new line of business for the firm.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The former head of D.E. Shaw’s multibillion-dollar credit investing business, Daniel Posner, is headed to Golub Capital to start a credit hedge fund', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Former D.E. Shaw Manager to Start Credit Fund for Golub Capital', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T14:28:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Azam Ahmed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Azam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahmed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c80bf10-4771-58b6-ad5d-64c306199d15', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c80bf10-4771-58b6-ad5d-64c306199d15'}, {'abstract': 'The Gay and Lesbian Entertainment Critics Association anoints the obvious choice, “Burlesque,” choosing the Cher-Christina Aguilera film over “Sex and the City 2.”', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/and-the-campy-award-goes-to/', 'snippet': 'The Gay and Lesbian Entertainment Critics Association anoints the obvious choice, “Burlesque,” choosing the Cher-Christina Aguilera film over “Sex and the City 2.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here is a category we wish we would see more of: the HitFix blog points us to the Gay and Lesbian Entertainment Critics Association, a group of writers from publications like The Advocate and Us Weekly, which has announced that the winner for “Campy – Intentional or Not – Film of the Year” is “Burlesque.” (Or as the organization puts it: “um, winner.”) It beat out worthwhile contenders like “Sex and the City 2” and “Piranha 3-D.” The win was especially notable because Steven Antin, the director of “Burlesque,” only recently told the Bagger that he didn’t care for the film’s reception. “It really wasn’t what I was aiming for, the camp,” he said of the film starring Cher and Christina Aguilera. “I wanted to make a fun film that was a throwback to Hollywood’s golden age of musicals, and people perceived it as camp for obvious reasons. I was surprised it was perceived that way.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'And The Campy Award Goes To…', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Burlesque', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Antin, Steven', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cher', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rivers, Joan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T14:31:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ecac817-0ee1-5ba5-b6da-b6576845db04', 'word_count': 219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ecac817-0ee1-5ba5-b6da-b6576845db04'}, {'abstract': 'The father and son will work together a book called “Along the Way,” about a journey along an ancient pilgrimage path in Spain.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/those-other-guys-martin-sheen-and-emilio-estevez-plan-memoir/', 'snippet': 'The father and son will work together a book called “Along the Way,” about a journey along an ancient pilgrimage path in Spain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Given recent events it is sometimes easy to forget that there are members of the Sheen family besides Charlie, and that they also live and work and contribute to the artistic community. But there are, and two who have lately avoided the glare of the headlines are collaborating on a memoir. Martin Sheen, the “Apocalypse Now” and “West Wing” star and Sheen family paterfamilias, and his son Emilio Estevez, the “Breakfast Club” actor and “Bobby” director, will write a book called “Along the Way” for the Free Press imprint of Simon & Schuster, the publisher said in a news release. The memoir, which is planned for publication around Father’s Day 2012, is said to chronicle the “physical and spiritual journey” of Mr. Sheen and Mr. Estevez, “along the Camino de Santiago, Spain, the 1,000-year-old pilgrimage path, which\xa0 traverses Galicia, the area from which Sheen’s father emigrated to the U.S., and to which Estevez’s own son has returned.” The book, written with Hope Edelman, will “celebrate their profound bond, give candid insights into the actors’ lives and relationship, examine the triumphs and challenges and inner and outer stages of the journey of a father and son.” (It will also discuss “The Way,” a coming film directed by Mr. Estevez and starring Mr. Sheen.) The release from the Free Press did not immediately mention if “Along the Way” would also address that other, prodigal Sheen sibling.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Martin Sheen and Emilio Estevez Plan a Memoir', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Estevez, Emilio', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheen, Martin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Simon & Schuster Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T14:33:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/424cba89-a9d6-5895-8463-3eddaede2e7b', 'word_count': 234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/424cba89-a9d6-5895-8463-3eddaede2e7b'}, {'abstract': 'The Securities and Exchange Commission announced Tuesday that Eileen Rominger, the global chief investment officer of Goldman Sachs Asset Management, has been named its director of Investment Management.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/goldmans-eileen-rominger-to-join-s-e-c/', 'snippet': 'The Securities and Exchange Commission announced Tuesday that Eileen Rominger, the global chief investment officer of Goldman Sachs Asset Management, has been named its director of Investment Management.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Securities and Exchange Commission has announced that Eileen Rominger, the global chief investment officer of Goldman Sachs Asset Management, has been named its director of investment management. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Goldman’s Eileen Rominger to Join the S.E.C.', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T14:41:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a3ec1e1-2e55-5ab5-8986-e532b8911fed', 'word_count': 319, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a3ec1e1-2e55-5ab5-8986-e532b8911fed'}, {'abstract': 'Are we China, 1958-61?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/great-leaps-backward/', 'snippet': 'Are we China, 1958-61?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve been watching with sympathy as David Beckworth and Scott Sumner discover that their updated monetarism actually puts them on my side of the great ideological divide — cast into the outer darkness along with John Maynard Keynes and Milton Friedman. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Great Leaps Backward', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T14:49:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/206b2e60-dbcb-5ffa-9972-1c33911d3730', 'word_count': 518, 'uri': 'nyt://article/206b2e60-dbcb-5ffa-9972-1c33911d3730'}, {'abstract': 'Europe’s economic woes are creating an opportunity for travelers looking for bargains, with hotels and tour operators offering attractive rates — sometimes as much as 40 percent off — to entice tourists from the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/travel/23prac-europe.html', 'snippet': 'Europe’s economic woes are creating an opportunity for travelers looking for bargains, with hotels and tour operators offering attractive rates — sometimes as much as 40 percent off — to entice tourists from the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For more than a year, the weakest economies in Europe have been struggling: Greece and Ireland have already received bailouts, and Portugal and Spain may be next. Lucky for travelers, the economic woes of these countries can mean opportunities for those who want to visit them. While the weak euro, which recently fell below the $1.30 mark, makes the entire Euro Zone cheaper for Americans, some of the best bargains can be found in the hardest-hit countries. Ireland, Greece and Portugal, in particular, are offering what amount to fire-sale prices as hotels and tour operators slash rates. ', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/travel/Practical/Practical-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 803, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/travel/Practical/Practical-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/travel/Practical/Practical-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Europe’s Troubles Offer Opportunities to Tourists', 'kicker': 'Practical Traveler', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Europe’s Woes Are a Tourist Boon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budget Travel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T15:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michelle Higgins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michelle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Higgins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7544ee24-51a5-5168-8cdc-086938939cf9', 'word_count': 1162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7544ee24-51a5-5168-8cdc-086938939cf9'}, {'abstract': 'Alan Pierson, the artistic director and conductor of the ensemble Alarm Will Sound, will take over.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/brooklyn-philharmonic-hires-music-director-and-aims-for-new-era/', 'snippet': 'Alan Pierson, the artistic director and conductor of the ensemble Alarm Will Sound, will take over.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ailing Brooklyn Philharmonic is hanging in there. Having halted most of its orchestra concerts over the last two seasons and cut staff because of money problems, it said on Wednesday that it had hired a new artistic director, Alan Pierson, and promised a “new era.” Mr. Pierson is the artistic director and conductor of the contemporary-music ensemble Alarm Will Sound. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brooklyn Philharmonic Hires Artistic Director and Aims for ‘New Era’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ALARM WILL SOUND', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Philharmonic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T15:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/08b5db23-afb9-5b39-9ef1-e8fb966c07bb', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08b5db23-afb9-5b39-9ef1-e8fb966c07bb'}, {'abstract': 'Ever wonder how Roger Federer trains?', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/training-with-federer-in-dubai/', 'snippet': 'Ever wonder how Roger Federer trains?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ever wonder how Roger Federer trains? I spoke with Jeremy Bayon, a Frenchman who played college tennis at Mississippi State and is now the assistant coach of the men’s team at the University of Florida, about his experience watching Federer train in Dubai with Jesse Levine, a young American player Bayon coaches. (In the second round of the Australian Open on Wednesday, Federer survived a five-set match against Gilles Simon of France, 6-2, 6-3, 4-6, 4-6, 6-3.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Training With Federer in Dubai', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Federer, Roger', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T15:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Macdonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macdonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6f40ade-af67-5d61-af24-6de536ce7108', 'word_count': 615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6f40ade-af67-5d61-af24-6de536ce7108'}, {'abstract': 'China’s president is welcomed to the White House with an elaborate ceremony and guarded words.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/obama-hu-cite-mutual-aims-as-summit-starts/', 'snippet': 'China’s president is welcomed to the White House with an elaborate ceremony and guarded words.', 'lead_paragraph': '10:47 a.m. | Updated Declaring that China and the United States have ‘’an enormous stake in each other’s success,’’ President Obama welcomed President Hu 