ing their directors a raise.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DealBook has read stories about how America’s teachers are underpaid. We’ve heard arguments that federal judges are underpaid. But we’ve never seen a suggestion that directors of public companies in the United States are underpaid. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Directors May Be Underpaid, Wachtell Lipton Tells Its Clients', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boards of Directors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'WACHTELL LIPTON ROSEN & KATZ', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T15:10:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/608d44aa-4a8d-50e7-bc68-3851127237d3', 'word_count': 503, 'uri': 'nyt://article/608d44aa-4a8d-50e7-bc68-3851127237d3'}, {'abstract': 'Mary Chapin Carpenter presented her amalgamation of folk, country and pop at the Allen Room.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/18/arts/music/18song.html', 'snippet': 'Mary Chapin Carpenter presented her amalgamation of folk, country and pop at the Allen Room.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An acute awareness of time passing is an ever-present theme in the songs of Mary Chapin Carpenter, who gave a quietly spellbinding concert on Saturday evening at the Allen Room as part of Lincoln Center’s American Songbook series. The music of Ms. Carpenter, 52, is an unclassifiable hybrid of pop, folk and country that she performs in the low, steady voice of someone confiding her thoughts in a journal. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/SONG/SONG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 279, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/SONG/SONG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/18/arts/SONG/SONG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ballads, From Literary To Wry to Confessional', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ballads, From Literary To Wry to Confessional', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lincoln Center for the Performing Arts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carpenter, Mary Chapin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T15:14:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a52214e8-ebe7-5845-a912-fa361b419a1e', 'word_count': 434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a52214e8-ebe7-5845-a912-fa361b419a1e'}, {'abstract': 'Greece and Germany denied that such a plan existed, but analysts supported the idea of Greece buying back its bonds on the open market.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/business/global/20euro.html', 'snippet': 'Greece and Germany denied that such a plan existed, but analysts supported the idea of Greece buying back its bonds on the open market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Analysts said on Wednesday that having Greece buy back its own devalued bonds could be an important step toward solving Europe’s sovereign debt crisis.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Greece, Buyback of Bonds Is Floated', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Greece, Buyback Of Bonds Is Floated', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Euro (Currency)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Central Bank', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Bonds', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T15:29:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Ewing', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ewing', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45145c23-bbbc-5b4d-8e5d-7b20220cda9f', 'word_count': 703, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45145c23-bbbc-5b4d-8e5d-7b20220cda9f'}, {'abstract': 'The latest on the effort to repeal the health care overhaul, a frugal Caribbean destination and other consumer-focused items from Wednesday’s Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/wednesday-reading-the-health-care-law-debate/', 'snippet': 'The latest on the effort to repeal the health care overhaul, a frugal Caribbean destination and other consumer-focused items from Wednesday’s Times.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wednesday Reading: The Health Care Law Debate', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T15:30:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Saranow Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'Saranow', 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/388a9f01-b18a-5352-9f4e-36437bf3a886', 'word_count': 142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/388a9f01-b18a-5352-9f4e-36437bf3a886'}, {'abstract': 'The juvenile red-tailed hawk who collided with a window at the headquarters of The Times last week is expected to make a full recovery, but he is not flying yet.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/hawk-on-the-mend-but-still-grounded/', 'snippet': 'The juvenile red-tailed hawk who collided with a window at the headquarters of The Times last week is expected to make a full recovery, but he is not flying yet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A brief update on the juvenile red-tailed hawk who smashed into a window in the glass-walled atrium of The New York Times Building last week: An X-ray showed that his wing is not broken and his prognosis remains good, but six days after the collision, he is still not flying, said the rehabilitator on Long Island who is caring for the bird.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hawk on the Mend, but Still Grounded', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T15:35:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/63df9474-3594-5525-b483-f4d35de849b9', 'word_count': 279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63df9474-3594-5525-b483-f4d35de849b9'}, {'abstract': 'The New York City Central Labor Council has fallen into disarray, and its leader, Jack Ahern, has been chided for doubling his salary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/nyregion/20labor.html', 'snippet': 'The New York City Central Labor Council has fallen into disarray, and its leader, Jack Ahern, has been chided for doubling his salary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two years after Jack Ahern won the presidency of the New York City Central Labor Council on the strength of his pledges to clean up the troubled labor coalition, the council has fallen into disarray, with top aides leaving and union leaders expressing growing frustration over Mr. Ahern’s leadership.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/20/nyregion/LABOR/LABOR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/20/nyregion/LABOR/LABOR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandar