guage film will be whittled down to nine in two stages, and if past experience is any guide, some highly regarded films may be overlooked in Wednesday’s announcement.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Foreign-Language Hopefuls, on Pins and Needles', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bardem, Javier', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Mark', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T16:36:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c10fbfa9-3398-5de4-bf12-6dca59339c3c', 'word_count': 313, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c10fbfa9-3398-5de4-bf12-6dca59339c3c'}, {'abstract': 'Most large countries spend between one and four times as much on computer hardware as they do on computer on computer software. China spends 12 times as much. What’s wrong with that picture?', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/software-piracy-in-china/', 'snippet': 'Most large countries spend between one and four times as much on computer hardware as they do on computer on computer software. China spends 12 times as much. What’s wrong with that picture?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Intellectual property is one of the big economic issues between China and the United States. American business executives and government officials believe Chinese companies steal large amounts of intellectual property from foreign companies. China does have laws against such piracy, but they are often not enforced.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Software Piracy in China', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copyrights and Copyright Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T16:40:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/98edb6cb-9da3-5a0e-9a52-d3eb39718739', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98edb6cb-9da3-5a0e-9a52-d3eb39718739'}, {'abstract': 'Chrissie Morris, a London-based shoe designer, was in a sci-fi frame of mind when she created her Priscilla platform.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/buy-robot/', 'snippet': 'Chrissie Morris, a London-based shoe designer, was in a sci-fi frame of mind when she created her Priscilla platform.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chrissie Morris, a London-based shoe designer, was in a sci-fi frame of mind when she created her Priscilla platform. Named after one of the research robots in the Humanoid Project in Sweden, the showy shagreen and leather shoe combines a strong architectural heel and chunky hardware with soft grass and strawberry hues. Says Morris, “There’s a certain toughness, but the colors celebrate the delicacy of what will bloom come springtime.” The sandal is $1,135 at kirnazabete.com or call (212) 941-9656.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Buy Robot', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'na