ed-ahead-on-deepwater-drilling/', 'snippet': 'Reflecting the expansion, BP has secured permits to drill in the Great Australian Bight basin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ink is barely dry on the White House commission’s investigative report into the Deepwater Horizon accident in the Gulf of Mexico pointing to industrywide deficiencies, but signs are emerging every day that deepwater drilling is expanding around the world.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Full Speed Ahead on Deepwater Drilling', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Offshore Drilling and Exploration', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BP Plc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T19:53:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clifford Krauss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clifford', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krauss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/86667f5a-ceec-5b12-ac3c-06d11a2b53a2', 'word_count': 345, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86667f5a-ceec-5b12-ac3c-06d11a2b53a2'}, {'abstract': '“This is a story about a mother, two daughters, and two dogs. It’s also about Mozart and Mendelssohn, the piano and the violin, and how we made it to Carnegie Hall.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/books/excerpt-battle-hymn-of-the-tiger-mother.html', 'snippet': '“This is a story about a mother, two daughters, and two dogs. It’s also about Mozart and Mendelssohn, the piano and the violin, and how we made it to Carnegie Hall.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is a story about a mother, two daughters, and two dogs. It’s also about Mozart and Mendelssohn, the piano and the violin, and how we made it to Carnegie Hall. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Battle Hymn of the Tiger Mother’', 'kicker': 'Excerpt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T19:54:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Chua', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chua', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'First Chapter', '_id': 'nyt://article/220b5b30-a494-599f-9974-c56a72d9f111', 'word_count': 750, 'uri': 'nyt://article/220b5b30-a494-599f-9974-c56a72d9f111'}, {'abstract': 'Debt doesn’t reduce capacity.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/a-note-on-aggregate-demand-and-aggregate-supply/', 'snippet': 'Debt doesn’t reduce capacity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just a brief note: one thing that keeps appearing in comments is the notion that because we had a bubble, in which some people were borrowing too much, the economic growth of 2000-2007 wasn’t “real” — that it was all a figment of our imagination.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Note on Aggregate Demand and Aggregate Supply', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T19:57:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a9a4a47-9ebd-5d3a-bde1-b7d3ebd33889', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a9a4a47-9ebd-5d3a-bde1-b7d3ebd33889'}, {'abstract': 'Nintendo’s portable 3-D gaming device will go on sale in late March, and the company hopes consumers will do more than play games on it.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/nintendo-says-new-device-will-take-3-d-mainstream/', 'snippet': 'Nintendo’s portable 3-D gaming device will go on sale in late March, and the company hopes consumers will do more than play games on it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nintendo’s new portable 3-D gaming system is in a “category of one,” said Reggie Fils-Aime, president and chief operating officer of the company’s American operation, at an event in New York on Wednesday in which he laid out the details of the product’s introduction. But he acknowledged that even the most novel of gaming devices is now pitted against a range of competitors extending far beyond the traditional gaming world. He said that Nintendo saw the device as more than a hand-held gaming console, and stressed the importance of the device’s ability to display 3-D video without the need for glasses, and its ability to function as a 3-D camera. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nintendo Says New Device Will Take 3-D Mainstream', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Three-Dimensional Devices and Effects', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nintendo Co Ltd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T19:58:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Brustein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brustein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/433aa188-510b-5bfe-bc50-3be41ce32d64', 'word_count': 545, 'uri': 'nyt://article/433aa188-510b-5bfe-bc50-3be41ce32d64'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama and President Hu Jintao of China pledged to nurture their nations’ common interests, but they also acknowledged differences on trade and human rights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/world/asia/20prexy.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama and President Hu Jintao of China pledged to nurture their nations’ common interests, but they also acknowledged differences on trade and human rights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON \x97 President Obama on Wednesday gently but pointedly prodded China to make progress on human rights, but he sought to focus most of the attention during a closely watched state visit with President Hu Jintao on the expanding economic relationship between the United States and its biggest economic rival.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/20/world/Prexy/Prexy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/20/world/Prexy/Prexy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/20/world/Prexy/Prexy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/20/world/Prexy/Prexy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'