19POD.html', 'snippet': 'Pictures from Washington, Sudan, Tunisia and Nepal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pictures from Washington, Sudan, Tunisia and Nepal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/19/blogs/20110119POD-slide-J93X/20110119POD-slide-J93X-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pictures of The Day: Washington and Elsewhere', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T21:51:15+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Blogs', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/db196e0e-83b5-5496-b6a6-376470b2e7e4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/db196e0e-83b5-5496-b6a6-376470b2e7e4'}, {'abstract': 'Another day of runway looks, another Scorecard at your service.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/scorecard-umit-benan-wins/', 'snippet': 'Another day of runway looks, another Scorecard at your service.', 'lead_paragraph': '2011 marks the 90th anniversary since a Florentine craftsman by the name of Guccio Gucci founded a small leather goods house, so Scorecard thinks it seems somehow fitting that alongside the label’s current creative director, Frida Gianinni, designers were casting a long glance back on Monday. Though money may never sleep, with his sly references to “American Pyscho,” the inaugural Who Is on Next? winner Umit Benan provided a helpful gloss on the proceedings: sometimes the past teaches us how to move on.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scorecard | Umit Benan Wins!', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T21:51:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison S. Cohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Cohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/62ba8802-59ef-56c8-8cfc-a2bea99232ce', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62ba8802-59ef-56c8-8cfc-a2bea99232ce'}, {'abstract': 'IAC has hired Bank of America and JPMorgan Chase to advise it on the initial offering and could file an offering prospectus this quarter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/wilbur-ross-iac-aims-to-go-public-this-year/', 'snippet': 'IAC has hired Bank of America and JPMorgan Chase to advise it on the initial offering and could file an offering prospectus this quarter.', 'lead_paragraph': '9:01 a.m. | Updated The International Automotive Components Group, the big auto-parts maker owned by Wilbur L. Ross Jr., plans to hold an initial public offering later this year, a person briefed on the matter told DealBook on Wednesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ross’ IAC Aims to Go Public This Year', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ross, Wilbur L Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T21:54:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e04238ea-d371-5f81-b0fa-0c47079dd28e', 'word_count': 213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e04238ea-d371-5f81-b0fa-0c47079dd28e'}, {'abstract': 'At Union Theological Seminary, Miranda Cuckson stuck to composers true to her taste (Ralph Shapely, Michael Hirsch) but dipped into Bach and Xenakis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/arts/music/20miranda.html', 'snippet': 'At Union Theological Seminary, Miranda Cuckson stuck to composers true to her taste (Ralph Shapely, Michael Hirsch) but dipped into Bach and Xenakis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Miranda Cuckson has quickly become one of a handful of go-to musicians for chamber groups that need a violinist who can play thorny works with undeniable musicality. And though she rarely performs standard repertory, when she does \x97 in the Argento Ensemble’s chamber version of Schumann’s Second Symphony last season, for example \x97 she plays with such beauty and assurance that you wish she would do it more often. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/20/arts/MIRANDA/MIRANDA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 687, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/20/arts/MIRANDA/MIRANDA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/20/arts/MIRANDA/MIRANDA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New-Music Champion Inches Toward the Standard Repertory', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New-Music Champion Inches Toward the Standard Repertory', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Union Theological Seminary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T21:58:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bd3d567-3bdc-5969-a798-a8e8eab806f6', 'word_count': 473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bd3d567-3bdc-5969-a798-a8e8eab806f6'}, {'abstract': 'How did Roger Federer escape Gilles Simon? It wasn’t any one tactical change, but a series of subtle variations that allowed him to wrest control of the match from Simon and his inspired play.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/analysis-how-federer-survived-simon/', 'snippet': 'How did Roger Federer escape Gilles Simon? It wasn’t any one tactical change, but a series of subtle variations that allowed him to wrest control of the match from Simon and his inspired play.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After coming back from a two set deficit to force a fifth set, Gilles Simon looked brazenly confident as he outhit Roger Federer from the baseline. He kept the ball deep to Federer’s backhand, and when Federer changed direction and went down the line, the quick Simon laced into a forehand cross-court that moved Federer hard to his right, and also opened up the backhand side again. He had the tactical hook deep in Federer’s side, and the momentum was on his side.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Analysis: How Federer Survived Simon', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Australian Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T22:00:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Macdonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macdonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d3c3b2b-3830-5da1-9f3f-00adbf610b71', 'word_count': 752, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d3c3b2b-3830-5da1-9f3f-00adbf610b71'}, {'abstract': 'Also in New York Online: the ultimate locksmith nightmare story, untrustworthy bouncers, and antiterror officers watch an anti-Islam movie.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/19/free-money-and-endangered-parrots/', 'snippet': 'Also in New York Online: the ultimate locksmith nightmare story, untrustworthy bouncers, and antiterror officers watch an anti-Islam movie.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The wild parrots of Pelham Bay Park were rendered homeless when the parks department took down the light poles where they nest, but they’re hanging in there. [Brooklyn Parrots]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Free Money and Endangered Parrots', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T22:04:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/118b51d8-027e-5815-ae9d-a86994ebb380', 'word_count': 232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/118b51d8-027e-5815-ae9d-a86994ebb380'}, {'abstract': '“Tom Ryan Thinks...” riffs on Nicholas Ray’s 1956 movie “Bigger Than Life.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/theater/reviews/20tom.html', 'snippet': '“Tom Ryan Thinks...” riffs on Nicholas Ray’s 1956 movie “Bigger Than Life.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let’s get this right out of the way: The full title of Daniel Fish’s new work is “Tom Ryan Thinks He’s James Mason Starring in a Movie by Nicholas Ray in Which a Man’s Illness Provides an Escape From the Pain, Pressure and Loneliness of Trying to Be the Ultimate American Father, Only to Drive Him Further Into the More Thrilling Though Possibly Lonelier Roles of Addict and Misunderstood Visionary.” Sounds kind of like fun, doesn’t it? And you’d think a 53-word title might say it all, right? Well, it isn’t always, and it doesn’t, quite.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/20/arts/TOM/TOM-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/20/arts/TOM/TOM-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 359}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/20/arts/TOM/TOM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/20/arts/TOM/TOM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/20/arts/TOM/TOM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Drug-Fueled Rants From a ’50s Film', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Tom Ryan Thinks . . .'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drug-Fueled Rants From a ’50s Film', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rouner, Christina', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Thomas Jay', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ray, Nicholas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T22:09:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cf717f1-ea03-584d-b7aa-7519cd01fefc', 'word_count': 476, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3cf717f1-ea03-584d-b7aa-7519cd01fefc'}, {'abstract': 'Nives Widauer spent five months renovating her apartment in a 19th-century Austrian mansion that now houses the police.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/01/20/greathomesanddestinations/20110120-vienna-ss.html', 'snippet': 'Nives Widauer spent five months renovating her apartment in a 19th-century Austrian mansion that now houses the police.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nives Widauer spent five months renovating her apartment in a 19th-century Austrian mansion that now houses the police.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': "In Austria, an Artist's Home in a Police Station", 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vienna (Austria)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-19T22:15:54+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Great Homes & Destinations', 'section_name': 'Great Homes & Destinations', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/3c70a59f-8278-5560-8c34-6c657fa92d57', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/3c70a59f-8278-5560-8c34-6c657fa92d57'}, {'abstract': '“Perfect Couples,” a new NBC series, has a Judd Apatow take on men and women.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/arts/television/20couples.html', 'snippet': '“Perfect Couples,” a new NBC series, has a Judd Apatow take on men and women.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you have seen a movie by Judd Apatow or Todd Phillips — which is to say, if you have so much as swiped a pinky across the frosting of popular culture this millennium — then you know that there is an oceanic divide between the sexes, and that it has nothing to do with glass ceilings, wage gaps or other benighted concerns of the 1970s. What keeps men and women at war is male laziness, stupidity, buffoonery, disorganization, bad taste and fantasy baseball. Women are competent, dull models of efficiency — like human versions of Staples. The model of marriage portrayed is pure old-school sitcom as though the institution had evolved not a millimeter since “The Honeymooners.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/20/arts/COUPLES/COUPLES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/20/arts/COUPLES/COUPLES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/20/arts/COUPLES/COUPLES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 966, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/20/arts/COUPLES/COUPLES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbSt