0T00:04:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pete Wells', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pete', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wells', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'recipe', '_id': 'nyt://article/0051843b-e4ec-55bb-b4e0-dc06e03d4830', 'word_count': 58, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0051843b-e4ec-55bb-b4e0-dc06e03d4830'}, {'abstract': 'Five out of five children will tell you: Whether it’s a processed nugget or a golden-fried drumstick, form takes a back seat to flavor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/magazine/23Food-t-000.html', 'snippet': 'Five out of five children will tell you: Whether it’s a processed nugget or a golden-fried drumstick, form takes a back seat to flavor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the opening salvos of the Christmas blizzard that would buckle New York City, I drove to the nearest decent butcher to buy beef for a classic French daube. On a night when swirling snow raked against the windows, who could resist egg noodles topped with melting meat, carrots and onions? A 3-year-old, that’s who. When I filled his bowl with noodles, Elliot, my younger son, called out with joy, “P’sghetti!” And then, as I ladled on the stew, he rebelled. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23food-span/23food-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 377, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23food-span/23food-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 377}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23food-span/23food-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75