1/01/20/chinese-president-to-hear-earful-from-lawmakers/', 'snippet': 'Chinese President Hu Jintao heads Thursday to Capitol Hill, where he confront lawmakers who have been highly critical of his country’s economic policies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chinese President Hu Jintao may be on a bit of a high after Wednesday night’s elaborate state dinner in his honor at the White House, complete with Maine lobster and aged rib-eye.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chinese President to Hear Earful From Lawmakers', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hu Jintao', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T13:44:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4aaee832-2f48-5e2f-9a8f-9a24ed278c3a', 'word_count': 678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4aaee832-2f48-5e2f-9a8f-9a24ed278c3a'}, {'abstract': 'Fake finance, but real growth.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/growth-in-the-naughties/', 'snippet': 'Fake finance, but real growth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yesterday I posted a brief note about how to think about the 2000-2007 expansion, now that we know that there was an unsustainable housing-and-debt bubble. My point was that this doesn’t mean that the growth was somehow fake; real output of goods and services did indeed rise, even if the legacy of that growth was debt that create macroeconomic problems now.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Growth in the Naughties', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T13:44:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2cbb4e47-cd0d-5a8d-9157-cd870dbe975d', 'word_count': 336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2cbb4e47-cd0d-5a8d-9157-cd870dbe975d'}, {'abstract': 'The street artist Banksy did not, of course, show up to accept his Cinema Eye documentary award, having his producer do the honors with a speech that mocked his fellow filmmakers.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/a-very-banksy-acceptance-speech/', 'snippet': 'The street artist Banksy did not, of course, show up to accept his Cinema Eye documentary award, having his producer do the honors with a speech that mocked his fellow filmmakers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO— For those still wondering about the legitimacy of the Banksy film “Exit Through the Gift Shop,” the Cinema Eye documentary award the film just won might help. The pseudonymous British artist even worked up an acceptance speech for the ceremony – although Banksy did not, of course, turn up to read it. That fell to the movie’s producer, Jaimie D’Cruz. Might have been a good thing, too, as Banksy went a little Gervais in mocking his fellow filmmakers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Very Banksy Acceptance Speech', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Banksy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T13:53:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b42629c3-72ee-5db4-bab3-ce9e65764a48', 'word_count': 258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b42629c3-72ee-5db4-bab3-ce9e65764a48'}, {'abstract': 'A London-based fashion journalist and consultant deconstructs the politics of fashion (or is that the fashion of politics?) on a global level.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/speak-softly-and-wear-big-shoulder-pads/', 'snippet': 'A London-based fashion journalist and consultant deconstructs the politics of fashion (or is that the fashion of politics?) on a global level.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sarah Palin’s infamous $150,000 shopping spree during the last presidential elections underscored the fact that in politics, a woman’s wardrobe can all too easily eclipse her ideology. Whether she’s working a trailer park-friendly plaid shirt at a campaign stop or an ethereal Dior gown at a state dinner, there is no escaping sartorial scrutiny.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Speak Softly and Wear Big Shoulder Pads', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T14:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', '