eadly North Korean artillery attack on a South Korean island in November.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea \x97 South Korea said Thursday that it had agreed to hold low-level defense talks with North Korea. The talks would be the first dialogue between the Koreas since a deadly North Korean artillery attack on a South Korean island in November seemed to push the peninsula to the brink of war.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South Korea Agrees to Low-Level Talks With North', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Korea Agrees to Low-Level Talks with North', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T15:22:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Fackler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fackler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9dcba681-b5e9-58a2-9a81-b2e15a3fb876', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9dcba681-b5e9-58a2-9a81-b2e15a3fb876'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s post pairs the poem “The Thrift Shop Dresses” with a 2010 “Modern Love” essay, “In Grief, a Mother and a Wife Bond.”', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/poetry-pairing-jan-20-2011/', 'snippet': 'This week’s post pairs the poem “The Thrift Shop Dresses” with a 2010 “Modern Love” essay, “In Grief, a Mother and a Wife Bond.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In our weekly “Poetry Pairing” series, we collaborate with the Poetry Foundation to feature a work from its American Life in Poetry project alongside content from The Times that somehow echoes, extends or challenges the poem’s themes. Each poem is introduced briefly by Ted Kooser, a former United States poet laureate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poetry Pairing | Jan. 20, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T15:24:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5945dc46-fb0c-5bf2-829c-5b5b8cf8a0a0', 'word_count': 621, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5945dc46-fb0c-5bf2-829c-5b5b8cf8a0a0'}, {'abstract': '“O: A Presidential Novel” by Anonymous is speculative fiction about the 2012 campaign that is trite, implausible and decidedly unfunny.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/books/21book.html', 'snippet': '“O: A Presidential Novel” by Anonymous is speculative fiction about the 2012 campaign that is trite, implausible and decidedly unfunny.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Well, now we know why the author of this much gossiped about, heavily marketed new book wanted to remain anonymous: “O: A Presidential Novel” is a thoroughly lackadaisical performance \x97 trite, implausible and decidedly unfunny. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/BOOK/BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 671, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/BOOK/BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Dishy Take on the 2012 Race, by ... Somebody', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dishy Take On 2012 Race, By ... Somebody', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T15:26:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michiko Kakutani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michiko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kakutani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a1df65d-5c96-5413-9387-113faea0072d', 'word_count': 888, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a1df65d-5c96-5413-9387-113faea0072d'}, {'abstract': 'The omission of “Of Gods and Men” from the Oscar shortlist for best foreign-language film was accompanied by another Academy decision that seems even more outrageous to some in France: the inclusion of Algeria’s entry, “Outside the Law.”', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/french-fury-over-academy-shortlist/', 'snippet': 'The omission of “Of Gods and Men” from the Oscar shortlist for best foreign-language film was accompanied by another Academy decision that seems even more outrageous to some in France: the inclusion of Algeria’s entry, “Outside the Law.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even in ordinary circumstances, the failure of France’s “Of Gods and Men” to make the Oscar shortlist for best-foreign language film announced Wednesday would have been controversial, especially in France. The film received rapturous reviews there, was a box office success and has won numerous awards, including that of best foreign-language film of 2010 as voted by the National Board of Review in the United States. “Grand prize winner at the last Cannes festival, Xavier Beauvois’s ‘Of Gods and Men’ doesn’t even qualify for the preliminary list,” was the incredulous reaction of the magazine Paris Match on its Web site. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'French Fury Over Academy Shortlist', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beauvois, Xavier', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bouchareb, Rachid', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T15:28:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a286644-15cd-559e-a6e1-4d1a897e2e5a', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a286644-15cd-559e-a6e1-4d1a897e2e5a'}, {'abstract': 'Clothing that can be tweaked as the day passes, naming a price for getting bumped from a flight and other consumer-focused items from Thursday’s Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/thursday-reading-convertible-clothing-for-the-cost-conscious/', 'snippet': 'Clothing that can be tweaked as the day passes, naming a price for getting bumped from a flight and other consumer-focused items from Thursday’s Times.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thursday Reading: Convertible Clothing for the Cost-Conscious', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T15:29:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Saranow Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'Saranow', 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a041e798-3888-53cd-be31-d67ba0cce995', 'word_count': 124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a041e798-3888-53cd-be31-d67ba0cce995'}, {'abstract': 'The long-running design partnership of Evelyn and Jerome Ackerman, who created innovative home furnishings in a variety of media, is the subject of an exhibition that opens at the Craft and Folk Art Museum in Los Angeles.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/seeing-things-marriage-by-design/', 'snippet': 'The long-running design partnership of Evelyn and Jerome Ackerman, who created innovative home furnishings in a variety of media, is the subject of an exhibition that opens at the Craft and Folk Art Museum in Los Angeles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the course of their more than 50-year design partnership, Evelyn and Jerome Ackerman created ceramics, tiles, wood carvings and textiles (among many other design items for the home) that helped shape what we now know as midcentury California modernism. While many of us grew up with the Ackermans’ work, the couple was less well known (although no less productive) than their contemporaries Charles and Ray Eames. The Ackermans and their work will be celebrated when a long-overdue retrospective, featuring more than 200 examples of their work, opens on Jan. 23 at the Craft and Folk Art Museum in Los Angeles.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeing Things | Marriage by Design', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T15:30:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Hodge', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a1d4e56-3f38-56d4-b2cc-286bf7ccaeb9', 'word_count': 357, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a1d4e56-3f38-56d4-b2cc-286bf7ccaeb9'}, {'abstract': 'Ron Franklin filed a wrongful termination lawsuit in a Texas county court Wednesday saying that he could be dismissed only for a “failure to perform” his job.', 'web_url': 'https://thequad.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/fired-sportscaster-sues-espn/', 'snippet': 'Ron Franklin filed a wrongful termination lawsuit in a Texas county court Wednesday saying that he could be dismissed only for a “failure to perform” his job.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ron Franklin, the sportscaster fired last month by ESPN for remarks he made to a sideline reporter in a hotel lobby before the Chick-fil-A Bowl, filed a wrongful termination lawsuit in a Texas county court Wednesday saying that he could be dismissed only for a “failure to perform” his job.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fired Sportscaster Sues ESPN', 'kicker': 'The Quad', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Edwards, Jeannine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Franklin, Ron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ESPN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T15:30:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/30bd72f3-78c8-5ff7-8f75-a9a74b153187', 'word_count': 195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30bd72f3-78c8-5ff7-8f75-a9a74b153187'}, {'abstract': 'Trajectories for energy demand and CO2 lead to tough math for limiting climate risk.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/tough-climate-math-in-the-face-of-co2-and-energy-forecasts/', 'snippet': 'Trajectories for energy demand and CO2 lead to tough math for limiting climate risk.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s hard to find projections for both global energy demand and emissions of carbon dioxide from fuel burning that don’t have a sustained upward trajectory for decades to come. The latest such forecasts were issued on Wednesday by Exxon Mobil, at the World Future Energy Summit in Abu Dhabi, and by BP. I’ll focus more on the BP report because it’s posted online, while Exxon’s is still awaiting publication.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tough Climate Math in the Face of CO2 and Energy Forecasts', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T15:37:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/691766d2-c102-5e8e-8d4b-5ac8e66188ae', 'word_count': 558, 'uri': 'nyt://article/691766d2-c102-5e8e-8d4b-5ac8e66188ae'}, {'abstract': 'Spotify, the popular British music service, has made its first deal in the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/music-service-spotify-closes-deal-with-sony/', 'snippet': 'Spotify, the popular British music service, has made its first deal in the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In what could be a breakthrough for the next phase of digital music, Sony Music Entertainment has signed a deal with Spotify, a sleek British online service, for distribution in the United States. Spotify, which streams music and has a smartphone app, has been a hit in Europe but its move across the Atlantic has been long delayed. Sony is the first major label group to sign for American distribution.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Music Service Spotify Closes Deal With Sony', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Spotify', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T15:44:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d716620-aa75-5141-83a2-3b67376f3ba0', 'word_count': 276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d716620-aa75-5141-83a2-3b67376f3ba0'}, {'abstract': 'Fear was the No. 1 reason older adults gave for not getting a colonoscopy to screen for colon cancer, a new report says.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/why-people-arent-screened-for-colon-cancer/', 'snippet': 'Fear was the No. 1 reason older adults gave for not getting a colonoscopy to screen for colon cancer, a new report says.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is fear holding you back from a potentially lifesaving screening test?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why People Aren’t Screened for Colon Cancer', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colonoscopy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests and Testing', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T15:44:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roni Caryn Rabin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roni', 'middlename': 'Caryn', 'lastname': 'Rabin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e3d7fd2-93da-5b55-9f18-e11377d6948f', 'word_count': 760, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e3d7fd2-93da-5b55-9f18-e11377d6948f'}, {'abstract': 'On this week’s program, Sherry Turkle discusses her book  on technology and loneliness, and Claire Cain Miller of The Times talks about the state of communications privacy laws.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/tech-talk-podcast-evolving-relationships-and-privacy/', 'snippet': 'On this week’s program, Sherry Turkle discusses her book  on technology and loneliness, and Claire Cain Miller of The Times talks about the state of communications privacy laws.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Technology can make it easier for people to connect with others, but it can also take a toll on relationships. On this week’s Tech Talk show, J.D. Biersdorfer chats with Sherry Turkle, a professor at M.I.T. whose new book is “Alone Together: Why We Expect More From Technology and Less From Each Other.” Ms. Turkle, the founder and director of the M.I.T. Initiative on Technology and the Self, says that hyperconnectivity can make people feel lonely.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tech Talk Podcast: Evolving Relationships and Privacy', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T15:45:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/125474e2-e894-5f93-85cb-409efc1e5d70', 'word_count': 305, 'uri': 'nyt://article/125474e2-e894-5f93-85cb-409efc1e5d70'}, {'abstract': 'The headliners include Bon Jovi, Kid Rock, Arcade Fire, Jimmy Buffett, and John Mellencamp.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/new-orleans-jazz-festival-adds-more-pop-and-rock/', 'snippet': 'The headliners include Bon Jovi, Kid Rock, Arcade Fire, Jimmy Buffett, and John Mellencamp.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 42nd edition of the New Orleans Jazz and Heritage Festival will take place over two weekends this spring, from April 29 to May 1 and May 5 to 8, and its lineup continues a long trend toward ever-more-mainstream rock and pop. The headliners include Bon Jovi, Kid Rock, Arcade Fire, Jimmy Buffett, John Mellencamp, Tom Jones, Wilco, the Strokes, Robert Plant and John Legend and the Roots, festival organizers announced on Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Orleans Jazz Festival Adds More Pop and Rock', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat',