ocument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': '<h5>By Warren Weaver, Jr. </h5>\n<h5>Special to The New York Times </h5>', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef79b172-5ed1-5561-a967-2534c54a0b32', 'word_count': 1206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef79b172-5ed1-5561-a967-2534c54a0b32'}, {'abstract': '13th Chief Justice of the United States', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/learning/general/onthisday/bday/0122.html', 'snippet': '13th Chief Justice of the United States', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fred M. Vinson enjoyed a noteworthy career in each of the three branches of the Federal Government--legislative, executive and judicial. In twelve years in the House of Representatives he won wide renown as a tax and fiscal expert. Then, in World War II, he served in top administrative posts, as Economic Stabilizer, Federal Loan Administrator, Assistant President, and Secretary of the Treasury. Finally, in 1946, he was appointed by President Truman as Chief Justice of the United States. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vinson Excelled In Federal Posts', 'kicker': 'OBITUARY', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T16:13:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By THE NEW YORK TIMES', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9ee6b81-6db4-596d-87fa-f45a11f66d82', 'word_count': 3006, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9ee6b81-6db4-596d-87fa-f45a11f66d82'}, {'abstract': 'Nine years after the murder of the American reporter Daniel Pearl in Pakistan, a dozen of the militants involved in his murder remain at large.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/world/asia/21pearl.html', 'snippet': 'Nine years after the murder of the American reporter Daniel Pearl in Pakistan, a dozen of the militants involved in his murder remain at large.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISLAMABAD, Pakistan \x97 Nine years after the American reporter Daniel Pearl was captured and killed by operatives of Al Qaeda in Pakistan, more than a dozen of the militants involved in his murder remain at large, a testament to the lack of will by Pakistani authorities to prosecute the cases, according to a report released Thursday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/world/21PEARL2/21PEARL2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 922, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/world/21PEARL2/21PEARL2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/21/world/21PEARL2/21PEARL2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Report Says Militants in Pearl Killing Still at Large', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Militants Linked to Killing of U.S. Reporter in Pakistan Remain at Large, Report Says', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hostages', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Georgetown University', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pearl, Daniel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wall Street Journal', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T16:15:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreig