Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ad2b049-a6f1-52c8-966e-78f224824869', 'word_count': 617, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ad2b049-a6f1-52c8-966e-78f224824869'}, {'abstract': 'The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration’s report showed that out of 20.3 million recalled vehicles, a record 14.9 million were recalled directly by their manufacturers.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/2010-a-record-year-for-voluntary-recalls/', 'snippet': 'The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration’s report showed that out of 20.3 million recalled vehicles, a record 14.9 million were recalled directly by their manufacturers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The auto industry recalled about 20.3 million vehicles last year, and three of the five automakers with the most recalls were Japanese, according to figures released by the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration on Wednesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '2010 a Record Year for ‘Voluntary’ Recalls', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recalls and Bans of Products', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T16:33:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b8db5ff-691c-5652-9f8c-a736ab029820', 'word_count': 505, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b8db5ff-691c-5652-9f8c-a736ab029820'}, {'abstract': 'The Iraqi prime minister issued a ban on gunshot celebrations after soccer victories to keep people from being injured or killed. But there are some things that governments just do not have the power to stop.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/take-away-celebratory-gunfire-from-my-cold-victorious-hand/', 'snippet': 'The Iraqi prime minister issued a ban on gunshot celebrations after soccer victories to keep people from being injured or killed. But there are some things that governments just do not have the power to stop.', 'lead_paragraph': ' BAGHDAD – Jubilant Iraqi soccer fans ignored government orders and lit up the Baghdad night sky with gunshots on Wednesday, after the national team beat North Korea 1-0 in the Asian Cup tournament to qualify for the quarterfinals.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Take Away Celebratory Gunfire? From My Cold, Victorious Hand.', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Firearms', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maliki, Nuri Kamal al-', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Baghdad (Iraq)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T16:34:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Omar Al-Jawoshy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Omar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Al-Jawoshy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f55e5dd-5bab-5ac6-a3b1-456ec7df9013', 'word_count': 719, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f55e5dd-5bab-5ac6-a3b1-456ec7df9013'}, {'abstract': 'As Michael R. Bloomberg proposes overhauling federal law, critics say his record on the issue is mixed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/nyregion/21bloomberg.html', 'snippet': 'As Michael R. Bloomberg proposes overhauling federal law, critics say his record on the issue is mixed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On “Meet the Press,” Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg declared, “We have to go and get the immigrants here.” To a group of business leaders in Brooklyn, he extolled “the economic power” of immigration. And in his State of the City address on Wednesday, he interrupted a litany of local issues to urge Americans “to fix our broken immigration system.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/nyregion/SUB-BLOMBERG/SUB-BLOMBERG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/nyregion/SUB-BLOMBERG/SUB-BLOMBERG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/21/nyregion/SUB-BLOMBERG/SUB-BLOMBERG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Even Bloomberg Can’t Escape Complexity of Immigration', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Even Mayor Cannot Escape Complexity of Immigration Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T16:37:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Dolnick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dolnick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1d97173-d859-5358-b0d8-0b7cba313c7b', 'word_count': 1311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1d97173-d859-5358-b0d8-0b7cba313c7b'}, {'abstract': 'This year “The King’s Speech” has been targeted by a self-proclaimed, but anonymous, Academy member, who claims that the movie is losing Academy voters because of its main subject’s anti-Semitic and pro-Nazi leanings.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/kings-nazis-and-the-oscars/', 'snippet': 'This year “The King’s Speech” has been targeted by a self-proclaimed, but anonymous, Academy member, who claims that the movie is losing Academy voters because of its main subject’s anti-Semitic and pro-Nazi leanings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO— You know a film is a front-runner when the charges of smear campaigns begin. The latest victim? “The King’s Speech,” the subject of an email circulated a few weeks ago in which the writer claims the film is losing Academy voters because of its subject’s apparent anti-Semitic and pro-Nazi proclivities. The email, from a person who claimed Academy membership, was sent last month to the blogger Scott Feinberg, who published it after checking its sources, which ultimately led back to a 2002 article in The Guardian. (Mr. Feinberg was unable to determine if the letter writer was in fact an Academy member.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kings, Nazis and the Oscars', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Firth, Colin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T16:39:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/af3aeb94-5f05-5f2c-b9c8-ea2cfe394d4d', 'word_count': 287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af3aeb94-5f05-5f2c-b9c8-ea2cfe394d4d'}, {'abstract': 'Ratings for the season premiere of “American Idol” were down almost 20 percent.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/idol-fewer-bad-singers-fewer-viewers/', 'snippet': 'Ratings for the season premiere of “American Idol” were down almost 20 percent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The big question looming over the new season of “American Idol” has been: How much will Simon be missed? The initial answer: A lot.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': "‘Idol': Fewer Bad Singers, Fewer Viewers", 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'American Idol (TV Program)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cowell, Simon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Randy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lopez, Jennifer', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tyler, Steven', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T16:44:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c2264a0-3e53-51b0-bd1b-3549e257000c', 'word_count': 299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c2264a0-3e53-51b0-bd1b-3549e257000c'}, {'abstract': 'One Police Plaza: With memories of the Arizona shooting fresh, preparing for the “active shooter scenario.”', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/planning-for-the-unfathomable/', 'snippet': 'One Police Plaza: With memories of the Arizona shooting fresh, preparing for the “active shooter scenario.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The frenzied violence unfolded abruptly, on a Saturday morning, at a suburban shopping center in Tucson. But it has happened elsewhere: aboard a Long Island Rail Road train; at a beverage distributor in Connecticut; at a cable television network in Maryland; on a college campus in Virginia.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Planning for the Unfathomable', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Raymond W', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Selleck, Tom', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tucson (Ariz)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T16:46:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liz Robbins and Al Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robbins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Al', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/415d1bb8-65e9-5d0a-ae93-c5a9be6ff32e', 'word_count': 1430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/415d1bb8-65e9-5d0a-ae93-c5a9be6ff32e'}, {'abstract': 'Members of Congress raise issues of trade, human rights and abortion in meetings with President Hu of China, but some of them can only listen without speaking up.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/as-lawmakers-meet-chinas-hu-some-can-only-listen/', 'snippet': 'Members of Congress raise issues of trade, human rights and abortion in meetings with President Hu of China, but some of them can only listen without speaking up.', 'lead_paragraph': '2:13 p.m. | Updated There was not much give and take as President Hu Jintao of China met with House members this morning, according to those who were in attendance. Representative Kevin Brady, a Republican of Texas, called it “mostly speechifying.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As Lawmakers Meet China’s Hu, Some Can Only Listen', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T16:50:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helene Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/375c560f-5a3f-5b9c-a140-b314f794ea82', 'word_count': 536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/375c560f-5a3f-5b9c-a140-b314f794ea82'}, {'abstract': 'A roundup of sites for managing the details of digital footprints after death.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/managing-online-accounts-after-death/', 'snippet': 'A roundup of sites for managing the details of digital footprints after death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a recent New York Times magazine article, “Cyberspace When You’re Dead,” the writer Rob Walker mentioned three Web sites that help ensure consumers still have a say in what happens to their online presences after they die.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Managing Online Accounts After Death', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T17:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Saranow Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'Saranow', 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6dadaf22-00df-5561-b654-3df7ffe93d71', 'word_count': 915, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6dadaf22-00df-5561-b654-3df7ffe93d71'}, {'abstract': 'The tenth season premiere of “American Idol” drew 26 million viewers, down about 14 percent from the 30 million who tuned in for last year’s debut.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/idol-debut-ratings-down/', 'snippet': 'The tenth season premiere of “American Idol” drew 26 million viewers, down about 14 percent from the 30 million who tuned in for last year’s debut.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the start of its 10th season on Wednesday night, “American Idol” gained two new judges in the form of Steven Tyler and Jennifer Lopez. But that monolithic Fox singing contest lost about 14 percent of the total number of viewers who tuned in for the debut of Season 9, drawing 26 million this year versus 30 million last year. Is ex-judge Simon Cowell smiling somewhere? Bill Carter has more at Media Decoder.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Idol’ Debut Ratings Down', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T17:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf22a704-c64a-5e4d-9791-f53a971c0602', 'word_count': 73, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf22a704-c64a-5e4d-9791-f53a971c0602'}, {'abstract': 'Interest rises in an index that examines university policies and programs for gay students, in part a response to reports of bullying.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/gay-friendly/', 'snippet': 'Interest rises in an index that examines university policies and programs for gay students, in part a response to reports of bullying.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Blogger’s Note: The following article was prepared through the New York Times Student Journalism Institute, a workshop that was recently held in Miami. Mr. Candido is a student at Florida Atlantic University. –Jacques Steinberg', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Students Seek Avenues to Gay-Friendly Colleges', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T17:03:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sergio N. Candido', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sergio', 'middlename': 'N.', 'lastname': 'Candido', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5cf1f77-9f0a-512f-b4ac-7ab0914e1ca2', 'word_count': 1124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5cf1f77-9f0a-512f-b4ac-7ab0914e1ca2'}, {'abstract': 'Celtics center Shaquille O’Neal still can be effective, as he showed in a great fourth quarter against the Pistons.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/oneal-turns-back-the-clock-for-a-night/', 'snippet': 'Celtics center Shaquille O’Neal still can be effective, as he showed in a great fourth quarter against the Pistons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once, Shaquille O’Neal was the most feared basketball player on the floor. He was a catastrophic, 7-foot-1 wrecking machine, blocking shots, throwing down rim-rattling dunks, and doing it all with a wink.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'O’Neal Turns Back the Clock, For a Night', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Neal, Shaquille", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Celtics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T17:05:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne C. Gerstner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Gerstner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3503bf2-d947-5a94-b20b-49eaffdc40b3', 'word_count': 388, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3503bf2-d947-5a94-b20b-49eaffdc40b3'}, {'abstract': 'Was the Nets owner Mikhail D. Prokhorov’s decision not to pursue Carmelo Anthony bold and gutsy, or did it just add to the melodrama of his franchise?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/sports/basketball/21leading.html', 'snippet': 'Was the Nets owner Mikhail D. Prokhorov’s decision not to pursue Carmelo Anthony bold and gutsy, or did it just add to the melodrama of his franchise?', 'lead_paragraph': 'You do have to hand it to the N.B.A. Just when it concluded one big off-season divafest that everyone thought could not be topped for sheer audacity and narcissism, it simply rolled on to a big in-season divafest. What the Carmelo Anthony story lacks in hourlong television debacles, it gains in dragging an oligarch all the way from Russia to proclaim the situation a circus.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Closing the Door on Carmelo Anthony', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Closing the Door on Carmelo Anthony', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANTHONY, CARMELO', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Jersey Nets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Prokhorov, Mikhail D', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T17:07:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1874c3ad-b3fa-5b4a-9608-ccc939fac708', 'word_count': 711, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1874c3ad-b3fa-5b4a-9608-ccc939fac708'}, {'abstract': 'After studying the pattern of the veins on his hand, American investigators concluded that Khalid Shaikh Mohammed, a senior operative for Al Qaeda, wielded the blade that killed Daniel Pearl.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/new-report-on-daniel-pearls-murder-reveals-forensic-analysis-of-killers-veins/', 'snippet': 'After studying the pattern of the veins on his hand, American investigators concluded that Khalid Shaikh Mohammed, a senior operative for Al Qaeda, wielded the blade that killed Daniel Pearl.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After studying the pattern of the veins on his hand, American investigators concluded that Khalid Shaikh Mohammed, a senior operative for Al Qaeda, wielded the blade that killed Daniel Pearl, a Wall Street Journal reporter who was kidnapped and executed before a video camera in Pakistan in 2002, according to a new report.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Report on Daniel Pearl’s Murder Reveals Forensic Analysis of Killer’s Veins', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T17:07:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4158dc12-2963-5259-b7bc-96d9b742ae23', 'word_count': 1003, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4158dc12-2963-5259-b7bc-96d9b742ae23'}, {'abstract': 'An entrance into Tiananmen Square in Beijing, China.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/photo-of-the-week-77/', 'snippet': 'An entrance into Tiananmen Square in Beijing, China.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An entrance into Tiananmen Square in Beijing, China.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Photo of the Week', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T17:16:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e0d9ad8-5ad1-562f-a850-1240b0bc39a9', 'word_count': 8, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e0d9ad8-5ad1-562f-a850-1240b0bc39a9'}, {'abstract': 'T’s features director, Jacob Brown, weighs in on men’s fashions at Paris Fashion Week.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/paris-fashion-week-rick-owens-and-juun-j/', 'snippet': 'T’s features director, Jacob Brown, weighs in on men’s fashions at Paris Fashion Week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In some ways, a Rick Owens show changes little from season to season, and you’ll occasionally hear grumblings of this from editors. But then, men change their style little from season to season anyway. And Owens does have a distinct idea that he constantly pushes further along. One significant aspect of that idea is the dropped, skirtlike waistline — often created with multiple draped layers of fabric. Occasionally an actual skirt appears, but usually some variation of a long shirt hem shows below the bottom edge of a suit jacket. Sometimes it just shows in the back, approximating the visual effect of wearing a shirt tied around the waist — an easy jumping off point for understanding how a skirt can look masculine on a guy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paris Fashion Week | Rick Owens and Juun.J', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T17:20:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jacob Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jacob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ce72e76-857e-5a43-9191-2ad1f389670b', 'word_count': 289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ce72e76-857e-5a43-9191-2ad1f389670b'}, {'abstract': 'When “Spider-Man: Turn Off the Dark” opens after well over 100 preview performances, it will break a record set by Mr. Mason’s comedy “A Teaspoon Every Four Hours,” which played 97 previews – and closed on its opening night – in 1969.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/whats-not-to-love-jackie-mason-waits-for-spider-man-to-break-his-dubious-record/', 'snippet': 'When “Spider-Man: Turn Off the Dark” opens after well over 100 preview performances, it will break a record set by Mr. Mason’s comedy “A Teaspoon Every Four Hours,” which played 97 previews – and closed on its opening night – in 1969.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When (or perhaps if) “Spider-Man: Turn Off the Dark” opens on March 15, it will be a day of emotion and fulfillment for that long-delayed musical’s cast, creative team and producers, and also for Jackie Mason.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s Not to Love? Jackie Mason Waits for ‘Spider-Man’ to Break His Dubious Record', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T17:26:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/30bf1f1b-4cd7-5d52-be0c-ffad3b21aff8', 'word_count': 755, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30bf1f1b-4cd7-5d52-be0c-ffad3b21aff8'}, {'abstract': 'The Carpetbagger visits the P.S. 22 Chorus in Staten Island, where 65 fifth-graders are preparing to sing at the Academy Awards.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/video-a-little-time-with-oscars-bound-school-choir/', 'snippet': 'The Carpetbagger visits the P.S. 22 Chorus in Staten Island, where 65 fifth-graders are preparing to sing at the Academy Awards.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video: A Little Time With Oscars-Bound School Choir', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Staten Island (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T17:31:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy Beiler and Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beiler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/10ba5f98-f202-5061-ae88-4463e6e6637b', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10ba5f98-f202-5061-ae88-4463e6e6637b'}, {'abstract': 'The Securities and Exchange Commission approved new regulations aimed at curbing the risks of asset-backed securities and bringing more clarity to the still struggling market.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/s-e-c-approves-new-rules-for-asset-backed-securities/', 'snippet': 'The Securities and Exchange Commission approved new regulations aimed at curbing the risks of asset-backed securities and bringing more clarity to the still struggling market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Securities and Exchange Commission approved new regulations on Thursday aimed at curbing the risks posed by asset-backed securities.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'S.E.C. Approves New Rules for Asset-Backed Securities', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T17:33:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6069618e-da88-50bd-bfe4-9130c81df91d', 'word_count': 530, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6069618e-da88-50bd-bfe4-9130c81df91d'}, {'abstract': 'Another study confirms that most of the warmest years in the historical record since 1880 have come in the past decade or so and that 2010 was so warm it came close to setting the all-time record.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/new-figures-confirm-that-2010-was-a-hot-year/', 'snippet': 'Another study confirms that most of the warmest years in the historical record since 1880 have come in the past decade or so and that 2010 was so warm it came close to setting the all-time record.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New figures issued on Thursday in Britain show that 2010 was the second-warmest year in the historical record. That comes on the heels of reports last week from two American agencies that 2010 was tied for the warmest year in the record books.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Figures Confirm That 2010 Was a Hot Year', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Aeronautics and Space Administration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of East Anglia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Meteorological Organization', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T17:40:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Gillis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gillis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/78fb53df-6934-53ae-8efc-7ffa1ce71fd6', 'word_count': 289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78fb53df-6934-53ae-8efc-7ffa1ce71fd6'}, {'abstract': '“The last campaign had tested his self-control. The eighteen-hour days, the frenetic travel, the bad food, the loss of autonomy, and the silly expectations imposed on him.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/books/excerpt-o-a-presidential-novel.html', 'snippet': '“The last campaign had tested his self-control. The eighteen-hour days, the frenetic travel, the bad food, the loss of autonomy, and the silly expectations imposed on him.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'August 2012', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘O: A Presidential Novel’', 'kicker': 'Excerpt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T17:45:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By ANONYMOUS', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anonymous', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'First Chapter', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f68559f-722d-53bc-b77e-a5edaee16ca7', 'word_count': 1173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f68559f-722d-53bc-b77e-a5edaee16ca7'}, {'abstract': 'The wounded congresswoman is able to stand without assistance but cannot yet speak, her husband said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/us/21giffords.html', 'snippet': 'The wounded congresswoman is able to stand without assistance but cannot yet speak, her husband said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TUCSON \x97 In the nearly two weeks since a gunshot passed through her brain, Representative Gabrielle Giffords has continued to make what doctors say is remarkable progress and is now able to distinguish particular colors and bear her own weight on her feet. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/us/21giffords/21giffords-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/us/21giffords/21giffords-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/21/us/21giffords/21giffords-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Doctor Cautions That Rehabilitation Process for Giffords Is Likely to Be a Long One', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Doctor Cautions That Rehabilitation Process for Giffords Is Likely to Be a Long One', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tucson Shooting (2011)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'nam