ned the hard way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The week before Christmas, I nearly killed my German shepherd.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How the Doctor Almost Killed Her Dog', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T18:22:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randi Hutter Epstein, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randi', 'middlename': 'Hutter', 'lastname': 'Epstein', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf044989-fca8-554b-8ff3-eeaf921662a4', 'word_count': 916, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf044989-fca8-554b-8ff3-eeaf921662a4'}, {'abstract': 'Pay fell in 2010 — but education still paid, according to a new Bureau of Labor Statistics report.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/education-still-pays/', 'snippet': 'Pay fell in 2010 — but education still paid, according to a new Bureau of Labor Statistics report.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Bureau of Labor Statistics is out with a new report today showing that pay trends in 2010 were not as good as they had been in 2009. In 2009, real weekly pay had risen for every educational group — high-school dropouts, high-school graduates, people with some college and four-year college graduates. In 2010, pay fell for every group.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Education Still Pays', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bureau of Labor Statistics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}],