experimental line of Ermengildo Zegna men’s fashion empire takes the win for the final day of shows in Milan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Head spinning from four days of nonstop fashion in Milan? Paris is still to come. But not to fear: Scorecard is here to cut through the shades of gray — and blue-gray and grayish-green — for you. Though the Caten brother duo surprised critics by going back to black, it was Alessandro Sartori who proved through his non-flannel suits for the experimental line of the 100-year-old Ermengildo Zegna men’s fashion empire that the presence of the past can be felt in techno tailoring.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scorecard | Z Zegna Wins!', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T18:48:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison S. Cohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Cohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/772768ef-464c-547d-a753-40ae45e9b07d', 'word_count': 581, 'uri': 'nyt://article/772768ef-464c-547d-a753-40ae45e9b07d'}, {'abstract': 'At a press event in Manhattan, T-Mobile executives outlined their plans to stay competitive with rival carriers.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/t-mobile-tries-to-counter-the-iphones-spread/', 'snippet': 'At a press event in Manhattan, T-Mobile executives outlined their plans to stay competitive with rival carriers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the big questions lingering in the wake of the news that Verizon Wireless would begin selling the iPhone is how the smaller wireless carriers in the United States, like Sprint and T-Mobile, will fare without it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'T-Mobile Tries to Counter the iPhone’s Spread', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'T-Mobile', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T18:50:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6363529-c703-5ac7-a2d8-eb042f09ce8b', 'word_count': 436, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6363529-c703-5ac7-a2d8-eb042f09ce8b'}, {'abstract': 'An exhibition at Cornell University explores — and explodes — the $2,500 subcompact to see what makes it tick, both mechanically and culturally.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/tata-nano-a-peoples-car-piece-by-piece/', 'snippet': 'An exhibition at Cornell University explores — and explodes — the $2,500 subcompact to see what makes it tick, both mechanically and culturally.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Its North American sales prospects remain as nebulous as New Delhi’s evening smog, but the Tata Nano has made some notable stateside cameos: at the Detroit Science Center during that city’s 2010 auto show; at New York’s Cooper-Hewitt National Design Museum shortly thereafter; and now, as the marquee attraction at a Cornell University exhibition in Ithaca, N.Y. — albeit in heavily modified form.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tata Nano: A People’s Car, Piece by Piece', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cornell University', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tata Motors Ltd', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ithaca (NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T18:51:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ed7014f-4f25-581e-a66e-23b5f32b0881', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ed7014f-4f25-581e-a66e-23b5f32b0881'}, {'abstract': 'Four in 10 Americans said that job creation should be the top priority for the new Congress, according to the latest New York Times/CBS News poll.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/poll-congresss-top-focus-to-should-be-job-creation/', 'snippet': 'Four in 10 Americans said that job creation should be the top priority for the new Congress, according to the latest New York Times/CBS News poll.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even though Congress’s first order of business under Republican control was Wednesday’s vote to repeal the health care law, most Americans said that they do not understand how the law affects them, and the public thinks job creation rather than health care should be the top priority for Congress, according to the latest New York Times/CBS News poll.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poll: Congress’s Top Focus Should Be Job Creation', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T19:00:59+0000', 'document_type': 'a