813d53', 'word_count': 556, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1ef76d6-42e2-5d6c-896b-757358813d53'}, {'abstract': 'Stefanie Zadravec’s “Honey Brown Eyes,”set in Bosnia in 1992, explores the terrors of war.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/theater/reviews/21honey.html', 'snippet': 'Stefanie Zadravec’s “Honey Brown Eyes,”set in Bosnia in 1992, explores the terrors of war.', 'lead_paragraph': '“War teaches you the value of an onion,” one character explains while preparing dinner in the harrowing but credulity-straining “Honey Brown Eyes.” And while the promising young playwright Stefanie Zadravec makes it clear that such values can be almost meaningless in a world where soldiers discuss the relative sexual merits of 10- versus 12-year-old girls, her shrewd juxtaposition of domesticity and atrocity very nearly overcomes the play’s narrative faults.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/HONEY/HONEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/HONEY/HONEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/HONEY/HONEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/HONEY/HONEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/HONEY/HONEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When War Begins, Nothing Is Normal', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Honey Brown Eyes'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When War Begins, Nothing Is Normal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schmidt, Erica', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Working Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zadravec, Stefanie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T22:13:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Grode', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grode', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dcf80031-38be-53b1-99b7-af23c6ee580a', 'word_count': 400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dcf80031-38be-53b1-99b7-af23c6ee580a'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Kleier is a co-president of Gumley Haft Kleier, a boutique brokerage firm that specializes in luxury properties and developments in Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/realestate/23sqft.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Kleier is a co-president of Gumley Haft Kleier, a boutique brokerage firm that specializes in luxury properties and developments in Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ms. Kleier is a co-president of Gumley Haft Kleier, a boutique brokerage firm that specializes in luxury properties and developments in Manhattan.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/realestate/sq-feet/sq-feet-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 901, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/realestate/sq-feet/sq-feet-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/realestate/sq-feet/sq-feet-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Michele Kleier', 'kicker': 'Square Feet | The 30-Minute Interview', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Michele Kleier', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T22:16:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivian Marino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b76db88-bf17-5252-9877-c5b1445de6f5', 'word_count': 847, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b76db88-bf17-5252-9877-c5b1445de6f5'}, {'abstract': '“Johnny Mad Dog” is a film based on a novel by the Congolese writer Emmanuel Dongala.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/movies/21roundup-JOHNNYMADDOG_RVW.html', 'snippet': '“Johnny Mad Dog” is a film based on a novel by the Congolese writer Emmanuel Dongala.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like a rifle butt to the head, “Johnny Mad Dog” leaves you reeling. Shot in Liberia but set in Anyplace, Africa, Jean-Stéphane Sauvaire’s morally complex, visually horrifying tale of child soldiers on an adult rampage never lets up.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Child Soldiers on an Adult Rampage', 'kicker': 'Movie Review | ‘Johnny Mad Dog’', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T22:17:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeannette Catsoulis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeannette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Catsoulis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d0badbd-5b5e-5567-8c89-a7ca5d6c3b9b', 'word_count': 379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d0badbd-5b5e-5567-8c89-a7ca5d6c3b9b'}, {'abstract': 'Kiran Rao’s moody film is set in Bombay.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/movies/21roundup-DHOBIGHATMUM_RVW.html', 'snippet': 'Kiran Rao’s moody film is set in Bombay.', 'lead_paragraph': '“My muse, my whore, my beloved”: that’s what Arun, a gloomy artist, calls Bombay in Kiran Rao’s “Dhobi Ghat (Mumbai Diaries).” (Despite the title’s “Mumbai,” everyone here says Bombay.) It’s certainly Ms. Rao’s muse: she seems mesmerized by its hurly-burly and has tried to find a narrative that can contain its multitudes.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Love Versus Class Barriers', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Dhobi Ghat (Mumbai Diaries)'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T22:18:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Saltz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saltz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a2104ef-e15a-5f6e-b444-82fc01bfbb04', 'word_count': 353, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a2104ef-e15a-5f6e-b444-82fc01bfbb04'}, {'abstract': '“Gabi on the Roof in July” is a film directed by Lawrence Michael Levine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/movies/21roundup-GABIONTHEROO_RVW.html', 'snippet': '“Gabi on the Roof in July” is a film directed by Lawrence Michael Levine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lawrence Michael Levine’s “Gabi on the Roof in July,” with a title that evokes Eric Rohmer and a creative process that resembles Mike Leigh’s long-term structured improvisations, is a kind of negative argument in favor of the auteur theory. Talented performers have developed credible characters and realistic scenes of life among the young and confused in bohemian Brooklyn, but there doesn’t seem to have been anyone involved who could shape the material, give it a point of view and a pulse, and make it add up to something more than a sharply observed acting exercise. The shock of recognition dissipates, to be replaced by the glance at the wristwatch.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Life Among the Young and Confused', 'kicker': 'Movie Review | ‘Gabi on the Roof in July’', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T22:19:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/25445063-4c49-5661-91f8-9b32fd91a348', 'word_count': 380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25445063-4c49-5661-91f8-9b32fd91a348'}, {'abstract': 'The Whitney’s latest exhibition feels like a harbinger of what the museum will do with its new downtown space.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/arts/design/21visions.html', 'snippet': 'The Whitney’s latest exhibition feels like a harbinger of what the museum will do with its new downtown space.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The main attraction at the Whitney Museum of American Art right now (after the Edward Hopper show) is a parakeet named Peetie. This live, chirping bird, the only replaceable component of Edward Kienholz’s 1964-65 assemblage “The Wait,” animates a comically morbid tableau of old bones, faded photographs and musty wallpaper. An elderly woman \x97 her thin frame fashioned from a cow’s skeleton \x97 holds a taxidermic cat on her lap; above her is a portrait of a young man, a dear departed husband or absent son.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/VISIONS-1/VISIONS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/VISIONS-1/VISIONS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/VISIONS-1/VISIONS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/VISIONS-1/VISIONS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/VISIONS-1/VISIONS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Haunting Tour, One Room at a Time', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Haunting Tour, One Room at a Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Whitney Museum of American Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T22:19:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/79ea9d59-f858-51e3-a30f-ce5888a829fa', 'word_count': 827, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79ea9d59-f858-51e3-a30f-ce5888a829fa'}, {'abstract': '“Lemmy” is a film about the singer, bassist and central piston behind the band Motörhead.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/movies/21roundup-LEMMY_RVW.html', 'snippet': '“Lemmy” is a film about the singer, bassist and central piston behind the band Motörhead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the worlds of hard rock and heavy metal, Ian Fraser Kilmister, the singer, bassist and central piston behind the band Motörhead, is not only a musical influence but also a paragon of hard living, like Keith Richards, only without the pretty-boy band mate, Top 40 hits and hippie associations. No, Lemmy — as he is known, the subject of this diverting if hagiographic documentary with an unprintable subtitle — specializes in industrial crunch served at missile velocity, a sound that once flourished in the factory precincts of Birmingham, England; and Detroit.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Paragon of Hard Living', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Lemmy'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metallica', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T22:20:03+0000