0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/99e7e746-2617-5135-a510-ae309dc9a0d4'}, {'abstract': 'The social historian Stephanie Coontz re-evaluates “The Feminine Mystique” and its author, Betty Friedan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/books/review/Traister-t.html', 'snippet': 'The social historian Stephanie Coontz re-evaluates “The Feminine Mystique” and its author, Betty Friedan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Halfway through “A Strange Stirring,” the social historian Stephanie Coontz — parsing the reception of “The Feminine Mystique,” Betty Friedan’s 1963 examination of middle-class female repression and despair — confesses to feeling some ambivalence over Friedan’s project, and hence her own.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Traister/Traister-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Traister/Traister-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Traister/Traister-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 808, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Traister/Traister-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Traister/Traister-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mad Women', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mad Women', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature',