 built-in cushion. Imagine all the foot...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Call it extreme quilting, but a number of designers fancy a beefed up version of the technique—at Mugler, Jean Paul Gaultier and Louis Vuitton. Some of the coats at Vuitton had a channeled flap that offered a built-in cushion. Imagine all the football fans who would appreciate something like that on a cold day. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fashion Channel', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T23:33:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/636767d0-b2f1-5c89-a5a1-dc440f8e10d0', 'word_count': 57, 'uri': 'nyt://article/636767d0-b2f1-5c89-a5a1-dc440f8e10d0'}, {'abstract': 'Two eminent philosophy professors take aim at contemporary nihilism in this idiosyncratic tour of the classics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/books/review/Neiman-t.html', 'snippet': 'Two eminent philosophy professors take aim at contemporary nihilism in this idiosyncratic tour of the classics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By the time they contemplate an application to graduate school, philosophy students have learned that it isn’t merely tacky to display an interest in questions about the meaning of life; it’s a major professional risk. For many decades, British and American students were urged to turn their attention instead to the meaning of language, while European philosophers were engaged in more portentous-sounding but equally arcane diversions. There were occasional, notable exceptions — Stanley Cavell’s work showed how the meaning of one’s words and the meaning of one’s life might coincide — but then Cavell was long considered to stand outside the real business of philosophy. Even more fatally, students were introduced to the history of philosophy as a series of epistemological puzzles decontaminated from troubling concerns about what it means to be human, a question that had moved the great philosophers of the canon as surely as any thoughtful 18-year-old.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/23neiman/23neiman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 906, 'width': 920, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/23neiman/23neiman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/23neiman/23neiman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What It All Means', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What It All Means', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dreyfus, Hubert L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Sean Dorrance', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T23:35:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Neiman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Neiman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/356b2089-7d31-5854-b960-ef7b9a0e13a4', 'word_count': 1563, 'uri': 'nyt://article/356b2089-7d31-5854-b960-ef7b9a0e13a4'}, {'abstract': 'Can fiction be philosophical? Even novelists trained in philosophy have sometimes insisted no.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/books/review/Ryerson-t.html', 'snippet': 'Can fiction be philosophical? Even novelists trained in philosophy have sometimes insisted no.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Can a novelist write philosophically? Even those novelists most commonly deemed “philosophical” have sometimes answered with an emphatic no. Iris Murdoch, the longtime Oxford philosopher and author of some two dozen novels treating highbrow themes like consciousness and morality, argued that philosophy and literature were contrary pursuits. Philosophy calls on the analytical mind to solve conceptual problems in an “austere, unselfish, candid” prose, she said in a BBC interview broadcast in 1978, while literature looks to the imagination to show us something “mysterious, ambiguous, particular” about the world. Any appearance of philosophical ideas in her own novels was an inconsequential reflection of what she happened to know. “If I knew about sailing ships I would put in sailing ships,” she said. “And in a way, as a novelist, I would rather know about sailing ships than about philosophy.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/arts/ryerson/ryerson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/arts/ryerson/ryerson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/arts/ryerson/ryerson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Philosophical Novel', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Thinkers and Dreamers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallace, David Foster', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldstein, Rebecca Newberger', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-20T23:35:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Ryerson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryerson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7814352f-ac70-58fe-ba72-a85f3fc05f0c', 'word_count': 1225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7814352f-ac70-58fe-ba72-a85f3fc05f0c'}, {'abstract': 'James Miller argues that philosophers’ willingness to reflect on their own petty failings makes their lives more, not less, worth studying.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/books/review/Bakewell-t.html', 'snippet': 'James Miller argues that philosophers’ willingness to reflect on their own petty failings makes their lives more, not less, worth studying.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the proof of a pudding is in the eating, and the proof of a rule is in the exceptions, where should we look for the proof of a philosophy?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Bakewell-t_CA0/Bakewell-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 919, 'width': 692, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'l