ct', 'value': 'Festivals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Park City (Utah)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T02:22:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de999f98-dd91-56b3-931f-ae3c00f5f6ce', 'word_count': 969, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de999f98-dd91-56b3-931f-ae3c00f5f6ce'}, {'abstract': 'China has stumbled into a monetary muddle that’s getting worse with each passing month.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/opinion/21krugman.html', 'snippet': 'China has stumbled into a monetary muddle that’s getting worse with each passing month.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With Hu Jintao, China’s president, currently visiting the United States, stories about growing Chinese economic might are everywhere. And those stories are entirely true: although China is still a poor country, it’s growing fast, and given its sheer size it’s well on the way to matching America as an economic superpower.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'China Goes to Nixon', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China Goes To Nixon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Yuan (Currency)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hu Jintao', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inflation (Economics)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T02:25:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/6582ee4f-5408-5ccd-939c-706a7b6becd7', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6582ee4f-5408-5ccd-939c-706a7b6becd7'}, {'abstract': 'Ahmet Davutoglu, the tireless, talkative foreign minister, is the architect of a foreign policy designed to (peacefully) restore his country to greatness. But whose side is he really on?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/magazine/23davutoglu-t.html', 'snippet': 'Ahmet Davutoglu, the tireless, talkative foreign minister, is the architect of a foreign policy designed to (peacefully) restore his country to greatness. But whose side is he really on?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the fall of 2009, relations between Serbia and Bosnia — never easy since the savage civil war of the 1990s — were slipping toward outright hostility. Western mediation efforts had failed. Ahmet Davutoglu, the foreign minister of Turkey, offered to step in. It was a complicated role for Turkey, not least because Bosnia is, like Turkey, a predominantly Muslim country and Serbia is an Orthodox Christian nation with which Turkey had long been at odds. But Davutoglu had shaped Turkey’s ambitious foreign policy according to a principle he called “zero problems toward neighbors.” Neither Serbia nor Bosnia actually shares a border with Turkey. Davutoglu, however, defined his neighborhood expansively, as the vast space of former Ottoman dominion. “In six months,” Davutoglu told me in one of a series of conversations this past fall, “I visited Belgrade five times, Sarajevo maybe seven times.” He helped negotiate names of acceptable diplomats and the language of a Serbian apology for the atrocities in Srebrenica. Bosnia agreed, finally, to name an ambassador to Serbia. To seal the deal, as Davutoglu tells the tale, he met late one night at the Sarajevo airport with the Bosnian leader Haris Silajdzic. The Bosnian smoked furiously. Davutoglu, a pious Muslim, doesn’t smoke — but he made an exception: “I smoked; he smoked.” Silajdzic accepted the Serbian apology. Crisis averted. Davutoglu calls this diplomatic style “smoking like a Bosnian.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23turkey-span/23turkey-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23turkey-span/23turkey-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23turkey-span/23turkey-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23turkey-span/23turkey-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Turkey’s Rules', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Turkey’s Rules', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davutoglu, Ahmet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T02:25:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Traub', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Traub', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be386739-02ea-5acb-b04d-5c961ba8c37e', 'word_count': 4705, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be386739-02ea-5acb-b04d-5c961ba8c37e'}, {'abstract': 'Judge Lewis A. Kaplan left little doubt that he would reject a request by the lawyers for the former Guantánamo detainee Ahmed Khalfan Ghailani to reverse a single guilty verdict.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/nyregion/21ghailani.html', 'snippet': 'Judge Lewis A. Kaplan left little doubt that he would reject a request by the lawyers for the former Guantánamo detainee Ahmed Khalfan Ghailani to reverse a single guilty verdict.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal judge in Manhattan suggested on Thursday that he believed there was strong evidence to support the conviction last fall of a former Guantánamo detainee on a single count of conspiracy stemming from the 1998 bombings of two American Embassies in East Africa.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Judge Suggests Evidence in Terror Case Supports Conviction', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Judge Suggests Evidence in Terror Trial Supports Single Conviction', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Detainees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaplan, Lewis A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ghailani, Ahmed Khalfan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T02:27:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Weiser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f974304-1526-5d06-acd9-1b5f2f0d6089', 'word_count': 400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f974304-1526-5d06-acd9-1b5f2f0d6089'}, {'abstract': 'The authorities are exploring the possibilities of pursuing a criminal case in the abduction of Carlina White from a Harlem 