e': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business Day', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/dbe013e9-f4b2-597f-a82c-54b357cccf0c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/dbe013e9-f4b2-597f-a82c-54b357cccf0c'}, {'abstract': 'Other than the Yankees, the city’s major sports teams have not had much success winning championships.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/sports/21loserville.html', 'snippet': 'Other than the Yankees, the city’s major sports teams have not had much success winning championships.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been 42 years since the Jets won a championship \x97 four decades of failure and fiasco that, with any luck, might come to an end with a victory over the Pittsburgh Steelers on Sunday and another in the Super Bowl in Arlington, Tex., on Feb. 6.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/sports/METSrefer/METSrefer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/sports/METSrefer/METSrefer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/21/sports/METSrefer/METSrefer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New York: A Champ When It Comes to Losing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New York: A Champ When It Comes to Losing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Rangers', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Knicks', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T02:49:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e92b7fde-ea2e-526e-9c81-d8038de686d2', 'word_count': 1028, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e92b7fde-ea2e-526e-9c81-d8038de686d2'}, {'abstract': '“I was aghast when Deborah Solomon asked Benjamin how her brother contracted AIDS.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/magazine/23letters-t-QUESTIONSFOR_LETTERS.html', 'snippet': '“I was aghast when Deborah Solomon asked Benjamin how her brother contracted AIDS.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was aghast when Deborah Solomon asked Benjamin how her brother contracted AIDS. I thought we had moved well beyond the day when people cared how somebody contracted the disease. In the early days of the epidemic, the questions about how somebody acquired H.I.V. or AIDS were asked to find out if the person living with it was an “innocent victim” or somebody who “deserved” what he (most frequently at that point) got. Thus, if the person acquired the virus because he was a homosexual or a drug user, it was thought to be his fault for engaging in risky behavior. However, if somebody — like Ryan White — acquired the virus through tainted blood, our hearts ached, even if we kept them at a distance. To ask that question now harks back to that dark day in a most distasteful way.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letter: Questions for Regina Benjamin', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'LETTER; Questions For Regina Benjamin', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benjamin, Regina M', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T02:49:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/df25c963-e602-5f04-a6ad-023beacfd58f', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df25c963-e602-5f04-a6ad-023beacfd58f'}, {'abstract': 'Some say Jean-Claude Duvalier returned to Haiti to get around a new law that will make it harder for him to access millions frozen in Switzerland.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/world/americas/21haiti.html', 'snippet': 'Some say Jean-Claude Duvalier returned to Haiti to get around a new law that will make it harder for him to access millions frozen in Switzerland.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PORT-AU-PRINCE, Haiti \x97 When Jean-Claude Duvalier suddenly emerged from exile last weekend, saying he was here “to help” his devastated homeland, fears immediately spread across the country that the former dictator had come to stake a new claim to power. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/world/HAITI/HAITI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 630, 'width': 630, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/world/HAITI/HAITI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/21/world/HAITI/HAITI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Some See a Cash Motive in Duvalier’s Return', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Duvalier’s Return to Haiti, Some See a Cash Motivation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Haiti', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duvalier, Jean-Claude', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embezzlement', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T02:51:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginger Thompson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginger', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thompson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a431be9e-281c-5cb9-bf3a-2094a3df5406', 'word_count': 1039, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a431be9e-281c-5cb9-bf3a-2094a3df5406'}, {'abstract': '“While attention-grabbing, the subheadline that headphones may be ‘making you deaf’ is not only unnecessarily alarmist, it is terminologically inaccurate as well.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/magazine/23letters-t-THEMEDIUM_LETTERS.html', 'snippet': '“While attention-grabbing, the subheadline that headphones may be ‘making you deaf’ is not only unnecessarily alarmist, it is terminologically inaccurate as well.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'While I appreciate any article that seeks to educate the public about hearing loss and its possible causes, I was dismayed to see the word “deaf” used to describe the “slight to severe” hearing loss that headphones can cause in Virginia Heffernan’s column “Against Headphones.” Deafness, versus any hearing impairment short of that, signifies an inability to hear anything at all; for those of us who can hear certain sounds but are unable to distinguish others (the author cites the plinking of raindrops as an example), “hearing-impaired” or “hard of hearing” are the proper descriptive terms for our condition. While attention-grabbing, the subheadline that headphones may be “making you deaf” is not only unnecessarily alarmist, it is terminologically inaccurate as well.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letters: The Medium', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'LETTERS; The Medium', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Headphones and Headsets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T02:52:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/151d0bbf-8ec6-5b87-8159-8dc3ebb2cebd', 'word_count': 220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/151d0bbf-8ec6-5b87-8159-8dc3ebb2cebd'}, {'abstract': 'Tax increment financing is a leading topic of discussion among candidates. One proposal is to put it to use toward affordable housing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/us/21cnctif.html', 'snippet': 'Tax increment financing is a leading topic of discussion among candidates. One proposal is to put it to use toward affordable housing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nearly 300 church members, community activists and other area residents gathered in the auditorium of a Rogers Park school last Sunday to celebrate the legacy of the Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. — and to rally around a proposal to create a special taxing district that would finance neighborhood rental housing exclusively.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/us/sub21TIF1/sub21TIF1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Candidates Question the Ways Tax Increment Financing Is Used', 'kicker': 'Chicago News Cooperative', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Candidates Question the Ways Tax Increment Financing Is Used ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Area Planning and Renewal', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T02:54:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mick Dumke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dumke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e75df9d-8858-5d70-bf2e-fdfdcf627cde', 'word_count': 1277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e75df9d-8858-5d70-bf2e-fdfdcf627cde'}, {'abstract': '“Is Judith Warner really going to equate upper-middle-class white women’s doing yoga to the struggles of the 1970s feminist movement?”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/magazine/23letters-t-FEARAGAINOFF_LETTERS.html', 'snippet': '“Is Judith Warner really going to equate upper-middle-class white women’s doing yoga to the struggles of the 1970s feminist movement?”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is Judith Warner really going to equate upper-middle-class white women’s doing yoga to the struggles of the 1970s feminist movement? Women, like my single mother, who fought to build communities of equality are not the same as women who do yoga to find a better self.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letters: Fear (Again) of Flying', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'LETTERS; Fear (Again) of Flying', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Feminist Movement', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T02:55:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/0cd6cc0a-5c48-5e1f-887a-e8774e45fef9', 'word_count': 236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0cd6cc0a-5c48-5e1f-887a-e8774e45fef9'}, {'abstract': '“The digital immortality described in this article appears to be the latest cyberspace craze devoted to self-delusional egotism.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/magazine/23letters-t-THINGSTODOIN_LETTERS.html', 'snippet': '“The digital immortality described in this article appears to be the latest cyberspace craze devoted to self-delusional egotism.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Unlike some of the people in Rob Walker’s article on Internet existence after death, I’m not too concerned about the information floating around the Web. I would like my hard drive and backup to delete themselves upon learning of my death. And I would like this to happen immediately, as news of my ceasing to breathe is received. This needs to be remote and wireless, of course, and automatic. And when I buy this off-the-shelf solution that someone will create, I will set the preferences to protect certain files (financial mainly) for my executor. I’m hoping someone will soon be able to say, “There’s an app for that.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letters: Things to Do in Cyberspace When You’re Dead', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'LETTERS; Things To Do In Cyberspace When You’re Dead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T02:57:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/38981491-9e96-5f80-b071-c2eb48690e7e', 'word_count': 230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38981491-9e96-5f80-b071-c2eb48690e7e'}, {'abstract': 'Is Pete Mitchell suggesting that my coif is not as it should be?', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/20/growth/', 'snippet': 'Is Pete Mitchell suggesting that my coif is not as it should be?', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRIDAY’S PUZZLE — Is it me, or is Pete Mitchell trying to tell me something? Mind you, I don’t believe for a second that people are trying to send me messages through their puzzles. Because that would sound deranged, right?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday: Basis of Growth', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T03:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/18b248db-3c99-55f3-8d3f-6bad92dbf104', 'word_count': 209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18b248db-3c99-55f3-8d3f-6bad92dbf104'}, {'abstract': '“The Walk Across America for Mother Earth” is a playful but perceptive comedy by the downtown writer and performer Taylor Mac.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/theater/reviews/21walk.html', 'snippet': '“The Walk Across America for Mother Earth” is a playful but perceptive comedy by the downtown writer and performer Taylor Mac.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What to wear when you’re about to chain yourself to a bowling alley to protest the nuclear arms race? Why, sequins, of course. Head to toe!', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/WALK/WALK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/WALK/WALK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 359}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/WALK/WALK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 655, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/WALK/WALK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/21/arts/WALK/WALK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Protesters Armed With Wigs and Sequins', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'The Walk Across America for Mother Earth'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Protesters Armed With Wigs and Sequins', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mac, Taylor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T03:01:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/205f0b6c-43a0-5eed-b676-5f1c40c959bf', 'word_count': 1394, 'uri': 'nyt://article/205f0b6c-43a0-5eed-b676-5f1c40c959bf'}, {'abstract': 'Banks should not be allowed to evade rules on abusive debit-card practices by switching to a less rigorous federal regulator.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/opinion/21fri2.html', 'snippet': 'Banks should not be allowed to evade rules on abusive debit-card practices by switching to a less rigorous federal regulator.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Congress outlawed many of the credit card industry’s most abusive practices in 2009. The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, which regulates about 4,800 state-chartered banks, recently did the same thing with the debit card industry. The banks, which are predictably crying foul, should not be allowed to evade the rules by switching to a less rigorous federal regulator. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Debit Card Predators', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Debit Card Predators', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Deposit Insurance Corp', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debit Cards', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Protection', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T03:02:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/990336b4-613c-58fd-982f-e2eb2d0d85e7', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/990336b4-613c-58fd-982f-e2eb2d0d85e7'}, {'abstract': 'The next moves of the Islamists, the only credible opposition movement in Tunisia, are a significant variable in the country’s post-revolutionary future.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/world/africa/21islamist.html', 'snippet': 'The next moves of the Islamists, the only credible opposition movement in Tunisia, are a significant variable in the country’s post-revolutionary future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TUNIS \x97 Ali Larayedh was imprisoned and tortured for 14 years for his role as a leader of the outlawed Islamist movement here, then hounded for the past six years by the omnipresent Tunisian secret police.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/world/21ISLAMIST1/21ISLAMIST1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 673, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/world/21ISLAMIST1/21ISLAMIST1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/21/world/21ISLAMIST1/21ISLAMIST1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Opposition in Tunisia Finds Chance for Rebirth', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Opposition In Tunisia Finds Chance For Rebirth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tunisia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Islam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ben Ali, Zine El-Abidine', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T03:04:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By David D. Kirkpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7088d5b2-ba88-5338-89bb-9afa8cb48907', 'word_count': 1115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7088d5b2-ba88-5338-89bb-9afa8cb48907'}, {'abstract': 'Tunisia’s people have overthrown a dictator, but their achievement is not yet secure.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/opinion/21fri3.html', 'snippet': 'Tunisia’s people have overthrown a dictator, but their achievement is not yet secure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tunisia’s people have achieved something rare and inspiring. Through a month of grass-roots protests, they overthrew a dictator, Zine el-Abidine Ben Ali. At a crucial moment, the Tunisian Army leader, Gen. Rachid Ammar, ordered his troops not to fire on their own people and then resisted the temptation to seize power for himself. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tunisia, After Ben Ali', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tunisia, After Ben Ali', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tunisia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ben Ali, Zine El-Abidine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'