ws_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ce36294-c5e2-5736-aa64-c554aa95eb6a', 'word_count': 42, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ce36294-c5e2-5736-aa64-c554aa95eb6a'}, {'abstract': 'Williams Formula One team is planning to sell shares in the team publicly.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/williams-team-contemplating-going-public/', 'snippet': 'Williams Formula One team is planning to sell shares in the team publicly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Williams Formula One team, the third most successful in the series’ history behind Ferrari and McLaren, announced on Friday that it was contemplating going public with the company in order to ensure its long-term future.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Williams Team Contemplating Going Public', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T12:57:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Spurgeon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spurgeon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/270b0a05-7c4e-5889-ac53-fbb491c7db5a', 'word_count': 361, 'uri': 'nyt://article/270b0a05-7c4e-5889-ac53-fbb491c7db5a'}, {'abstract': 'Williams Formula One team is planning to sell shares in the team publicly.', 'web_url': 'https://formulaone.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/williams-team-contemplating-going-public/', 'snippet': 'Williams Formula One team is planning to sell shares in the team publicly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Williams Formula One team, the third most successful in the series’ history behind Ferrari and McLaren, announced on Friday that it was contemplating going public with the company in order to ensure its long-term future.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Williams Team Contemplating Going Public', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T12:57:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Spurgeon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spurgeon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae4415f6-2858-5690-882c-3fed2d720823', 'word_count': 361, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae4415f6-2858-5690-882c-3fed2d720823'}, {'abstract': 'The basketball world is in dire need of a new positional system, as showcased by the ridiculous ambiguity of the small forward position.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/positional-revolution-turns-to-small-forwards/', 'snippet': 'The basketball world is in dire need of a new positional system, as showcased by the ridiculous ambiguity of the small forward position.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With each passing season, the need to reclassify basketball’s positional system becomes more obvious. Waves of unique players come into the N.B.A. every year, and many pay the price for its reluctance to adapt; some of the league’s most intriguing talents are poorly utilized, buried deep on the bench, or cast off because they don’t fit neatly into the game’s positional molds. The lines that separate positions are arbitrary and limiting, and though a willingness to cross those lines is sometimes lauded (Hedo Turkoglu can run an offense!), just as often a cross-positional player is maligned for some transgression against the game (Monta Ellis isn’t a real point guard! Burn him at the stake!).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Positional Revolution Turns to Small Forwards', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, LeBron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Chris', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turkoglu, Hedo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T13:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Mahoney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mahoney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f85173cd-0c72-5b07-9bff-51645af0c2df', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f85173cd-0c72-5b07-9bff-51645af0c2df'}, {'abstract': 'On Thursday, an advisory committee to the Food and Drug Administration recommended approval of a new dye that can reveal brain plaques characteristic of Alzheimer’s disease in living patients.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/alzheimers-test-clears-a-hurdle/', 'snippet': 'On Thursday, an advisory committee to the Food and Drug Administration recommended approval of a new dye that can reveal brain plaques characteristic of Alzheimer’s disease in living patients.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An advisory committee to the Food and Drug Administration recommended unanimously Thursday that the agency approve the first test able to diagnose Alzheimer’s disease in the brain of a living person, The Times reports this morning. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Alzheimer’s Test Clears a Hurdle', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Alzheimer's Disease", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T13:04:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d71d82e-b648-547d-948f-95fd2cf48f57', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d71d82e-b648-547d-948f-95fd2cf48f57'}, {'abstract': 'Also in the news: New York as city of sports losers, and a steep drop in car accidents on a Brooklyn street where a controversial bike lane was built.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/morning-buzz-case-not-closed-23-years-later/', 'snippet': 'Also in the news: New York as city of sports losers, and a steep drop in car accidents on a Brooklyn street where a controversial bike lane was built.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Grumble grumble winter wonderland grumble grumble. Here’s the news.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Buzz | Case Not Closed, 23 Years Later', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T13:26:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/982cfb2c-c169-532c-8bff-11dce979538d', 'word_count': 571, 'uri': 'nyt://article/982cfb2c-c169-532c-8bff-11dce979538d'}, {'abstract': 'Jean-Marie Messier and Edgar M. Bronfman Jr. were both fined and given suspended sentences.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/business/global/22vivendi.html', 'snippet': 'Jean-Marie Messier and Edgar M. Bronfman Jr. were both fined and given suspended sentences.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The media tycoons Jean-Marie Messier and Edgar Bronfman Jr. were convicted Friday of criminal charges and given suspended sentences in a case stemming from their tenure at the top of the French conglomerate Vivendi Universal and its near collapse eight years ago. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/business/dbpix-vivendi1/dbpix-vivendi1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/22/business/dbpix-vivendi1/dbpix-vivendi1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/business/dbpix-vivendi1/dbpix-vivendi1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/business/dbpix-vivendi1/dbpix-vivendi1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/22/business/dbpix-vivendi1/dbpix-vivendi1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'French Court Convicts Executives in Vivendi Case', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'French Court Convicts Executives in Vivendi Case', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vivendi', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Messier, Jean-Marie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T13:31:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Saltmarsh and Eric Pfanner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saltmarsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pfanner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46171c6b-2a61-552b-8407-3023fb743fe6', 'word_count': 556, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46171c6b-2a61-552b-8407-3023fb743fe6'}, {'abstract': 'Coral reefs were severely damaged by elevated sea temperatures last year.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/thailand-closes-diving-sites-to-protect-corals/', 'snippet': 'Coral reefs were severely damaged by elevated sea temperatures last year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some of Thailand’s most popular diving sites are now off-limits to tourists: Thai officials announced on Thursday that they were restricting access to seven marine national parks for up to a year to prevent further harm to coral reefs severely damaged by a long period of elevated sea temperatures last year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Coral Bleaching Prompts Thailand to Close Diving Sites', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coral', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reefs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Thailand', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T13:34:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Collins Rudolf', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'Collins', 'lastname': 'Rudolf', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e49042c3-d3f3-50f9-8a18-1b3796d1f94a', 'word_count': 237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e49042c3-d3f3-50f9-8a18-1b3796d1f94a'}, {'abstract': 'Brooklyn may get a lot of buzz, but it lacks meaningful local government and media organizations, letting the powers that be evade scrutiny.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/complaint-box-powerless-in-brooklyn/', 'snippet': 'Brooklyn may get a lot of buzz, but it lacks meaningful local government and media organizations, letting the powers that be evade scrutiny.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of the boroughs outside Manhattan, Brooklyn gets the most buzz — as a tourist attraction, a “hipster brand” and an incubator of art and artisanal products. That has provoked a backlash from longtime Brooklynites and others wary of smugness from the borough’s Brownstone Belt.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Complaint Box | Powerless in Brooklyn', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T13:38:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Norman Oder', 'person': [{'firstnam