es of his erratic behavior.', 'web_url': 'https://gambit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/its-bobby-fischer-season/', 'snippet': 'The former world champion is the subject of a new documentary, a new biography and an article about what have the causes of his erratic behavior.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bobby Fischer, the mercurial former world chess champion, died nearly three years ago, but lately his story is making a comeback.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s Bobby Fischer Season', 'kicker': 'Gambit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chess', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brady, Frank', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fischer, Bobby', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garbus, Liz', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T13:57:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Chess', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dylan Loeb McClain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dylan', 'middlename': 'Loeb', 'lastname': 'McClain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b178ddb6-8eb0-5cbe-87b8-5ba93054be84', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b178ddb6-8eb0-5cbe-87b8-5ba93054be84'}, {'abstract': 'An architecture professor urges that failing shopping malls be “retrofitted” with services to help the suburban elderly stay in their neighborhoods.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/new-lives-for-dead-suburban-malls/', 'snippet': 'An architecture professor urges that failing shopping malls be “retrofitted” with services to help the suburban elderly stay in their neighborhoods.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The suburban shopping mall has fallen on hard times, and Ellen Dunham-Jones, co-author of “Retrofitting Suburbia: Urban Design Solutions for Redesigning Suburbs,” is thrilled, frankly.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Lives for ‘Dead’ Suburban Malls', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T14:00:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Stabiner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stabiner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3e7700d-db82-5ed8-a94e-0f8b786f06cf', 'word_count': 702, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3e7700d-db82-5ed8-a94e-0f8b786f06cf'}, {'abstract': 'A valuable lesson learned while at sea: Cruise-ship food doesn’t have to be bad.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/out-and-about-cruising-the-caribbean/', 'snippet': 'A valuable lesson learned while at sea: Cruise-ship food doesn’t have to be bad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My latest adventure at sea — a cruise around the eastern Caribbean with my partner aboard the Celebrity Eclipse — taught me two valuable lessons. First, some people are just addicted to cruising. We met several passengers who were well beyond their 25th voyage, a milestone we may never get to. But this cruise, which began in Miami and had ports of call in Puerto Rico, St. Maarten and St. Kitts, whetted our appetite for more. Which brings me to Lesson No. 2: Cruise-ship food doesn’t have to be bad. In fact, the Eclipse’s was quite memorable.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Out and About | Cruising the Caribbean', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T14:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d548b2e-28b5-5166-832b-9ff0230de301', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d548b2e-28b5-5166-832b-9ff0230de301'}, {'abstract': 'Authorities have backed off a threat to seize custody of the 3-year-old son of an imprisoned opposition leader, ruling he could stay with his grandmother.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/world/europe/22belarus.html', 'snippet': 'Authorities have backed off a threat to seize custody of the 3-year-old son of an imprisoned opposition leader, ruling he could stay with his grandmother.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW \x97 Authorities in Belarus have backed off a threat to seize custody of the 3-year-old son of an imprisoned opposition leader there, ruling that the boy’s grandmother was fit to take care of him. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/world/22BELARUS/22BELARUS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/world/22BELARUS/22BELARUS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/22/world/22BELARUS/22BELARUS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Belarus Won’t Take Custody of Leader’s Son', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Belarus Decides to Leave Boy With Grandmother', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sannikov, Andrei', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sannikov, Danil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Belarus', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Custody and Support', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T14:01:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Schwirtz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwirtz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/862605b3-2636-538d-8a49-fc608414f32d', 'word_count': 699, 'uri': 'nyt://article/862605b3-2636-538d-8a49-fc608414f32d'}, {'abstract': 'The trip to sing at the Oscars for a bunch of adorable school kids from Staten Island almost didn’t happen, but a matter of connections made all right in the world.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/pulling-strings-for-an-oscar-performance/', 'snippet': 'The trip to sing at the Oscars for a bunch of adorable school kids from Staten Island almost didn’t happen, but a matter of connections made all right in the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Park City, Utah— As adorable as the P.S. 22 chorus is – and if you haven’t watched the school kids sing Katy Perry and Bon Jovi, prepare to be overwhelmed with cuteness – its forthcoming trip to sing at the Academy Awards almost didn’t happen. As with everything Hollywood-related, it was all about who you know.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pulling Strings for an Oscar Performance', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T14:13:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6a3ff5b-a3ce-5571-bfaf-4e9ca4e44e1e', 'word_count': 588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6a3ff5b-a3ce-5571-bfaf-4e9ca4e44e1e'}, {'abstract': 'The N.H.L.’s waiver rules may keep goalie Evgeni Nabokov from joining Detroit even though the Red Wings signed him to a contract.', 'web_url': 'https://slapshot.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/detroit-signs-nabokov-but-doesnt-have-him-yet/', 'snippet': 'The N.H.L.’s waiver rules may keep goalie Evgeni Nabokov from joining Detroit even though the Red Wings signed him to a contract.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Now that his agent has finalized a deal with the Detroit Red Wings, Evgeni Nabokov is close to returning to the N.H.L. However, there’s a good chance the 35-year-old Russian will not get to tend goal for Detroit.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Detroit Signs Nabokov, But Doesn’t Have Him Yet', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davidson, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nabokov, Evgeni', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Detroit Red Wings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Hockey League', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'St Louis Blues', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T14:30:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Botta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Botta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/55e17961-36c9-5a46-9c17-8787fd267395', 'word_count': 461, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55e17961-36c9-5a46-9c17-8787fd267395'}, {'abstract': 'The marketplace appears perkier than in recent years, the ambush marketers are out in force and festival founder Robert Redford says he has no intention of retiring.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/sundance-kicks-off-commerce-tentatively-in-the-air/', 'snippet': 'The marketplace appears perkier than in recent years, the ambush marketers are out in force and festival founder Robert Redford says he has no intention of retiring.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARK CITY, Utah— The Bagger has landed at the Sundance Film Festival, where we hope to discover some of the films we’ll be writing up next year. As our colleague Brooks Barnes writes in Friday’s Times, executives expect a perkier marketplace than in recent years, though nowhere near the irrational exuberance of the years before the Great Recession (when the likes of “Hamlet 2″ went for $10 million). This year, along with a focus on even indier films that were born out of the Sundance Labs, also may see tickets harder to come by because of renovations putting one theater out of commission. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sundance Kicks Off, Commerce Tentatively in the Air', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Redford, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T14:46:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d172f9a9-f819-5f9b-aabf-7c9c42272bf8', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d172f9a9-f819-5f9b-aabf-7c9c42272bf8'}, {'abstract': 'In Schenectady, where G.E. has its roots, President Obama announced Jeffrey R. Immelt’s new role on an advisory board as its focus shifts to job creation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/business/economy/22immelt.html', 'snippet': 'In Schenectady, where G.E. has its roots, President Obama announced Jeffrey R. Immelt’s new role on an advisory board as its focus shifts to job creation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SCHENECTADY, N.Y. \x97 President Obama is traveling here on Friday to name Jeffrey R. Immelt to run his outside panel of economic advisers, succeeding Paul A. Volcker, the former Federal Reserve chairman, who is stepping down, the White House said. Mr. Immelt is chairman and chief executive of General Electric, the giant conglomerate with deep roots in this somewhat battered industrial city near Albany.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/us/22immelt2/22immelt2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/22/us/22immelt2/22immelt2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewi