 her previous 250 Grand Slam singles matches.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MELBOURNE, Australia — The Williams sisters have won Grand Slam titles, won this very tournament, with aches, strains and wraps around their legs. But there will be no mind over matter run to the trophy, or even the second week, this time.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/sports/venus/venus-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 256, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/22/sports/venus/venus-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 256}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/sports/venus/venus-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 437, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hip Injury Forces Venus Williams to Withdraw', 'kicker': 'Australian Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hip Injury Forces Venus Williams to Withdraw', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Venus', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Australian Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T16:09:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Clarey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clarey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/497e3631-5e80-57df-9402-bfdd19cac10c', 'word_count': 756, 'uri': 'nyt://article/497e3631-5e80-57df-9402-bfdd19cac10c'}, {'abstract': 'An American teenager who was detained for several weeks in Kuwait landed on Friday morning at Dulles Airport, where he was briefly questioned.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/us/22detain.html', 'snippet': 'An American teenager who was detained for several weeks in Kuwait landed on Friday morning at Dulles Airport, where he was briefly questioned.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON \x97 An American teenager who was detained for several weeks in Kuwait and placed on an American no-fly list landed Friday morning at Dulles International Airport, where he was briefly questioned by the Federal Bureau of Investigation and then released to his family. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/us/DETAIN_span/DETAIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/22/us/DETAIN_span/DETAIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/us/DETAIN_span/DETAIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 710, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/us/DETAIN_span/DETAIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/22/us/DETAIN_span/DETAIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Detained Teenager Questioned by F.B.I.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Detained Teenager Questioned by F.B.I.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mohamed, Gulet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Security and Warning Systems', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T16:10:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Mazzetti', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mazzetti', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1119add-bdff-5ca9-a359-38b49b6e84c0', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1119add-bdff-5ca9-a359-38b49b6e84c0'}, {'abstract': '“For a snow event, it was fairly uneventful,” said a transit spokesman. But Friday morning’s storm didn’t stop the city’s plows and salt spreaders from rolling.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/nyregion/22snow.html', 'snippet': '“For a snow event, it was fairly uneventful,” said a transit spokesman. But Friday morning’s storm didn’t stop the city’s plows and salt spreaders from rolling.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is tempting to say something like it’s all over but the shoveling.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/nyregion/SNOW/SNOW-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/22/nyregion/SNOW/SNOW-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/nyregion/SNOW/SNOW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/nyregion/SNOW/SNOW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/22/nyregion/SNOW/SNOW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As Snowstorms Go, This Was a Breeze', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Snowstorms Go, This Was a Breeze', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snow and Snowstorms', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sanitation Department (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T16:15:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Barron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ddf110c-1e8e-5a7d-ad99-ae2ea369fbf2', 'word_count': 358, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ddf110c-1e8e-5a7d-ad99-ae2ea369fbf2'}, {'abstract': 'Books by Roland Barthes, Mahmoud Darwish, Xiaoda Xiao and Mark Slouka.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/books/review/Soderlind-t.html', 'snippet': 'Books by Roland Barthes, Mahmoud Darwish, Xiaoda Xiao and Mark Slouka.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOURNING DIARY  Oct. 26, 1977 – Sept. 15, 1979.  By Roland Barthes. Translated by Richard Howard.  Hill & Wang, $25.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Soderlind-t_CA0/Soderlind-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 833, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nonfiction Chronicle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nonfiction Chronicle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Slouka, Mark', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howard, Richard', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T16:21:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lori Soderlind', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lori', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soderlind', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/67631ada-f6d0-549c-b2cc-ec2326027c73', 'word_count': 1132, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67631ada-f6d0-549c-b2cc-ec2326027c73'}, {'abstract': 'Amy Chua’s paean to take-no-prisoners hyperparenting roars onto the hardcover nonfiction list this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/books/review/InsideList-t.html', 'snippet': 'Amy Chua’s paean to take-no-prisoners hyperparenting roars onto the hardcover nonfiction list this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HEAR ME ROAR: “Battle Hymn of the Tiger Mother,” Amy Chua’s paean to take-no-prisoners hyperparenting, cruises to No. 5 on the hardcover nonfiction list this week, trailing charges of stereotype-baiting, shameless hucksterism and worse. An excerpt that appeared in The Wall Street Journal under the fire-breathing title “Why Chinese Mothers Are Superior” generated more than 7,000 comments, more than any other article in the history of that paper’s Web site. Since then, Chua — a second-generation Chinese-American and Yale law professor previously known for scholarly volumes like “Day of Empire: How Hyperpowers Rise to Global Dominance, and Why They Fall” — has been backpedaling hard, insisting that readers, along with The Journal’s headline writers, had missed her self-mockery. The novelist and self-described “Bad Mother” Ayelet Waldman certainly didn’t see any. Waldman delivered a counterblast on The Journal’s Web site, though she found a silver lining in her two teenage children’s outraged response to Chua’s article. “I say with confidence that neither of my children has ever before bothered to read a single word of The Wall Street Journal,” she wrote. “I don’t think that I could have screamed or threatened them into doing so, not even if I’d tossed them outside in the middle of winter, to cower barefoot and freezing on the front step. So to Ms. Chua I express my gratitude. It seems to take a Chinese mother to force my Western kids to read the paper.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/30insidelist/30insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/30insidelist/30insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T16:21:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/388a2fd5-8113-518c-a13c-5528534dca1f', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/388a2fd5-8113-518c-a13c-5528534dca1f'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/books/review/EdChoice-t.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GRYPHON: New and Selected Stories,  by Charles Baxter (Pantheon, $27.95.) Beneath the shadowless Norman Rockwell contours of Baxter’s Midwest lurks something like the chilling starkness and human isolation in the bleakly beautiful work of Edward Hopper.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice: Recent books of particular interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T16:21:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9ffdea5-da2b-5033-a582-4ef99e9b5356', 'word_count': 277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9ffdea5-da2b-5033-a582-4ef99e9b5356'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/books/review/PaperRow-t.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AMERICAN BABYLON: Notes of a Christian Exile,  by Richard John Neuhaus (Basic Books, $16.95.) In these posthumously published essays Neuhaus (1936-2009), a Roman Catholic priest and the founder and editor of the conservative journal First Things, states the case for a natural-law approach to politics and morals, which he insists are inextricably linked. From Roe v. Wade to questions of atheism and citizenship, “ ‘American Babylon’ displays Neuhaus in all his virtues — elegantly argued and written, fair-minded and with a formidable range of reference,” Charles R. Morris wrote in the Book Review.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/23paperrow/23paperrow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/23paperrow/23paperrow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T16:21:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab4f4135-698c-54c9-a6ad-14c332473b84', 'word_count': 793, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab4f4135-698c-54c9-a6ad-14c332473b84'}, {'abstract': 'Letters in response to David Carr’s review of Douglas Coupland’s biography “Marshall McLuhan.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/books/review/Letters-t-MARSHALLMCLU_LETTERS.html', 'snippet': 'Letters in response to David Carr’s review of Douglas Coupland’s biography “Marshall McLuhan.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Marshall McLuhan’', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Marshall Mcluhan’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T16:23:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3c119cd-b0da-5523-8d81-d4cdcecaf190', 'word_count': 374, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3c119cd-b0da-5523-8d81-d4cdcecaf190'}, {'abstract': 'Letter in response to Raymond Arsenault’s review of Eugene Robinson’s “Disintegration.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/books/review/Letters-t-COMMONVALUES_LETTERS.html', 'snippet': 'Letter in response to Raymond Arsenault’s review of Eugene Robinson’s “Disintegration.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Common Values', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Common Values', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T16:23:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/09e57b61-8849-514f-ab2c-f0e729da6ff8', 'word_count': 315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09e57b61-8849-514f-ab2c-f0e729da6ff8'}, {'abstract': 'Ahmad Chalabi’s daughter offers an absorbing social history of Iraq through her family story.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/books/review/Robinson-t.html', 'snippet': 'Ahmad Chalabi’s daughter offers an absorbing social history of Iraq through her family story.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I thought this book might test my ability to make an assessment on the merits — the cardinal rule of reviewing — since the author is the daughter of Ahmad Chalabi, the Iraqi politician who, she acknowledges on the very first page, is accused of being a charlatan, a triple agent and the source of faulty intelligence that led the United States into war in Iraq. I reported on his activities during the war and met Tamara (briefly) in the desert outside Nasiriya during the early days of the American invasion. Father and daughter arrived on a plane full of Iraqis who were not politicians but a ragtag group of fighters named the “Free Iraqi Fighting Force.” The Special Forces unit in which I was embedded as a war correspondent for U.S. News & World Report was instructed by its command to train, arm and field these forces to put “an Iraqi face” on the American-led invasion. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Robinson/Robinson-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Robinson/Robinson-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Robinson/Robinson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 561, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Robinson/Robinson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Robinson/Robinson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'By the Banks of the Tigris', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'By the Banks of the Tigris', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chalabi, Ahmad', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T16:23:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Robinson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robinson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a86d0ec7-7d93-5082-ac6a-49bae5cdc846', 'word_count': 1222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a86d0ec7-7d93-5082-ac6a-49bae5cdc846'}, {'abstract': 'A tour of the hyper-feminine, commercialized world of young girls.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/books/review/Paul-t.html', 'snippet': 'A tour of the hyper-feminine, commercialized world of young girls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The “princess phase.” So inevitable is this period in the maturation of girls today that it should qualify as an official developmental stage, worthy of an entry in Leach or Brazelton: first crawling, then walking, then the urgent desire to wear something pink and spark\xadly. Whether we smile indulgently or roll our eyes at the drifts of tulle and chiffon that begin accumulating in our daughters’ rooms around age 4, participation in these royal rituals has come to seem necessary, even natural.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Paul/Paul-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1009, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Paul/Paul-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Paul/Paul-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Pink Necessary?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Is Pink Necessary?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beauty Contests', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dolls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Orenstein, Peggy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Youth', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Toys', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T16:24:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Murphy Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': 'Murphy', 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/db91a5d6-01cf-5469-90e2-6ea418b91163', 'word_count': 1254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db91a5d6-01cf-5469-90e2-6ea418b91163'}, {'abstract': 'A story collection inspired by true stories of German jurisprudence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/books/review/Steinhauer-t.html', 'snippet': 'A story collection inspired by true stories of German jurisprudence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the preface to this mesmerizing story collection, Ferdinand von Schirach recalls his uncle, a judge who lost his left arm and right hand in World War II. The uncle, he tells us, began his own stories by stating, “Most things are complicated, and guilt always presents a bit of a problem.” This line also began his suicide letter.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Steinhauer-t_CA0/Steinhauer-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1011, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Steinhauer-t_CA0/Steinhauer-t_CA0-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Steinhauer-t_CA0/Steinhauer-t_CA0-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Guilty Hearts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Guilty Hearts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T16:24:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Olen Steinhauer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Olen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinhauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/459ed93b-69dd-523d-a0fd-35a9490e0ec1', 'word_count': 856, 'uri': 'nyt://article/459ed93b-69dd-523d-a0fd-35a9490e0ec1'}, {'abstract': 'Peter Mandelson, a longtime Labour party power broker, will become a senior adviser to Lazard.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.