cle/c88a53bb-cdbc-5ea5-86d1-256a33d9f936'}, {'abstract': 'Nearly 99 percent of southern Sudanese voted to split off from northern Sudan and form their own country, according to preliminary results released on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/world/africa/22sudan.html', 'snippet': 'Nearly 99 percent of southern Sudanese voted to split off from northern Sudan and form their own country, according to preliminary results released on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JUBA, Sudan \x97 Nearly 99 percent of southern Sudanese voters have chosen to split off from northern Sudan and form their own country, according to preliminary results of an independence referendum conducted this month.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South Sudanese Vote Overwhelmingly for Secession', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Sudanese Vote Overwhelmingly for a Split', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Referendums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sudan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:07:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Josh Kron and Jeffrey Gettleman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gettleman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/49984f06-7d0f-5be3-a6a2-7a564b259e98', 'word_count': 377, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49984f06-7d0f-5be3-a6a2-7a564b259e98'}, {'abstract': 'Sophia Gimenez, a high school senior from Denver, wonders if she can balance the college search with her mother’s diagnosis of multiple sclerosis.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/sophia-gimenez-part-3/', 'snippet': 'Sophia Gimenez, a high school senior from Denver, wonders if she can balance the college search with her mother’s diagnosis of multiple sclerosis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last month, the thrill of my acceptance to Knox College and Mills College was cut short by my mother’s diagnosis of multiple sclerosis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Ailing Mother Eases Her Daughter’s Pain, and Conscience', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:12:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sophia Gimenez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sophia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gimenez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/33790f5e-bb16-5f81-aa71-d7ada03bf188', 'word_count': 782, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33790f5e-bb16-5f81-aa71-d7ada03bf188'}, {'abstract': 'Sherry Turkle once saw technology as a tool for playing with identity. Now she fears it is replacing identity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/books/review/Lehrer-t.html', 'snippet': 'Sherry Turkle once saw technology as a tool for playing with identity. Now she fears it is replacing identity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1995, Sherry Turkle, a professor of the “social studies of science” at M.I.T., published a book about identity in the digital age called “Life on the Screen.” It was a mostly optimistic account, as Turkle celebrated the freedom of online identity. Instead of being constrained by the responsibilities of real life, Turkle argued, people were using the Web to experiment, trying on personalities like pieces of clothing. As one online user told her, “You are who you pretend to be.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Lehrer-t_CA0/Lehrer-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 894, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Lehrer-t_CA0/Lehrer-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/books/review/Lehrer-t_CA0/Lehrer-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'We, Robots', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'We, Robots', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turkle, Sherry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robots and Robotics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:13:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonah Lehrer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lehrer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/70b82b3e-1f93-5bfa-a330-7a9cb906d636', 'word_count': 1177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70b82b3e-1f93-5bfa-a330-7a9cb906d636'}, {'abstract': 'The gallery, Park Life, has asked a federal court for a declaratory judgment that states the canine shape cannot be copyrighted.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/gallery-challenges-jeff-koons-balloon-dog-claim/', 'snippet': 'The gallery, Park Life, has asked a federal court for a declaratory judgment that states the canine shape cannot be copyrighted.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You may not have realized that the balloon dog you bought for a buck from a street clown might be a collector’s item. But the artist Jeff Koons, who constructed a 10-foot tall “Balloon Dog” that has been exhibited in the Metropolitan Museum of Art and elsewhere, sent a cease-and-desist letter to a Canadian manufacturer and San Francisco gallery for producing and selling “Balloon Dog” bookends. Now the gallery, Park Life, has asked a federal court for a declaratory judgment that states the canine shape cannot be copyrighted, according to Courthouse News Service. “As virtually any clown can attest, no one owns the idea of making a balloon dog, and the shape created by twisting a balloon into a dog-like form is part of the public domain,” the gallery says in its federal complaint. “Any similarities between the Balloon Dog Bookend compared with the Balloon Dog Structure are driven by the wholly unprotectable idea of depicting the shape of a balloon dog in a solid form.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gallery Challenges Jeff Koons’s Balloon Dog Claim', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koons, Jeff', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:14:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e44abbc-8db1-5094-92b8-a9122630c043', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e44abbc-8db1-5094-92b8-a9122630c043'}, {'abstract': 'The fossil of a species of flying reptile that coexisted with dinosaurs is the first female of its kind to be associated with an egg.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/science/25obegg.html', 'snippet': 'The fossil of a species of flying reptile that coexisted with dinosaurs is the first female of its kind to be associated with an egg.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scientists have found a 160-million-year-old preserved fossil of a pterosaur, along with one of its eggs. Pterosaurs were flying reptiles of various sizes that coexisted with dinosaurs from the late Triassic to the Cretaceous period. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/25/science/25obegg1/25obegg1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Tell Fossil’s Sex? Well, the Egg Is a Clue', 'kicker': 'Observatory', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Tell Fossil’s Sex? Well, the Egg Is a Clue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paleontology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pterosaurs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eggs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fossils', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:16:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sindya N. Bhanoo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sindya', 'middlename': 'N.', 'lastname': 'Bhanoo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26cac7a4-b0be-5705-953e-d3c587b39e1a', 'word_count': 255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26cac7a4-b0be-5705-953e-d3c587b39e1a'}, {'abstract': 'The former chess champion lived such a peripatetic life that no one person could provide the key to explaining him to the filmmaker Liz Garbus.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/a-fractured-bobby-fischer/', 'snippet': 'The former chess champion lived such a peripatetic life that no one person could provide the key to explaining him to the filmmaker Liz Garbus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The documentary filmmaker Liz Garbus has taken on some dark and difficult topics in her work, including prisons (“The Farm: Angola U.S.A.”) and brain injuries (“Coma”). Her latest subject, Bobby Fischer, was in many ways no different.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Fractured Bobby Fischer', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chess', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fischer, Bobby', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garbus, Liz', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:18:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dylan Loeb McClain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dylan', 'middlename': 'Loeb', 'lastname': 'McClain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/61701cf6-20b3-58af-8831-5751c6ccf40a', 'word_count': 357, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61701cf6-20b3-58af-8831-5751c6ccf40a'}, {'abstract': 'A novel recreates Lucie Duff Gordon’s escape to Egypt and how her Englishness slowly melted away.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/books/review/Fugard-t.html', 'snippet': 'A novel recreates Lucie Duff Gordon’s escape to Egypt and how her Englishness slowly melted away.', 'lead_paragraph': '“A hot dusty complicity between Lady, maid and manservant” — this is the tantalizing dynamic Kate Pullinger places at the center of her novel “The Mistress of Nothing.” The lady is a figure from real life, the Victorian author Lucie Duff Gordon, best known for her “Letters From Egypt.” Stricken with tuberculosis and in search of a more benign climate, she left her husband and family back in Esher, England, and traveled to Egypt with her maid, Sally Naldrett, whom Pullinger has chosen as her narrator. Little is known about Sally, save for the fact that she became pregnant and that the child’s father was said to be an Arab man. Duff Gordon’s response to the birth was to try to separate Sally from the child and send her back to England.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/arts/fugard/fugard-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/arts/fugard/fugard-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Orientalists', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Orientalists', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:20:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Fugard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fugard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7f6c17f-25d6-5518-ab62-13dd61c46bdc', 'word_count': 580, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7f6c17f-25d6-5518-ab62-13dd61c46bdc'}, {'abstract': 'The women in Joyce Carol Oates’s latest collection display a powerful and self-destructive need for love.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/books/review/Thompson-t.html', 'snippet': 'The women in Joyce Carol Oates’s latest collection display a powerful and self-destructive need for love.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this story collection, Joyce Carol Oates, master of the American gothic, revisits territory she has long claimed as her own, a fictional realm where mild-mannered men turn into monsters and the maternal embrace is more often crushing than comforting. Her effusive, exclamatory prose can be — and has been — mocked, but there’s little doubt that Oates is a well-practiced storyteller. Too well practiced, perhaps: the impact of these precisely turned tales of violation and violence tends to be more mechanical than visceral. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reckless Abandon', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reckless Abandon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oates, Joyce Carol', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:23:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrea Thompson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thompson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ddceab80-1dbc-5ea5-a4bb-c82268121149', 'word_count': 633, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ddceab80-1dbc-5ea5-a4bb-c82268121149'}, {'abstract': 'U.S. Bank hints that it may impose a fee for debit cards, among other pricing changes to its checking accounts.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/u-s-bank-hints-at-checking-and-debit-card-fees/', 'snippet': 'U.S. Bank hints that it may impose a fee for debit cards, among other pricing changes to its checking accounts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The days of the free debit card may be coming to an end at some banks.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Bank Hints at Checking and Debit Card Fees', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:24:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Saranow Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'Saranow', 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6b439a8-9da0-5058-95e9-91a402e70b76', 'word_count': 620, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6b439a8-9da0-5058-95e9-91a402e70b76'}, {'abstract': 'After winning her third-round match, Caroline Wozniacki, responding to criticism that her news conferences are boring, provided answers before there were questions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/sports/tennis/22iht-TENNIS22.html', 'snippet': 'After winning her third-round match, Caroline Wozniacki, responding to criticism that her news conferences are boring, provided answers before there were questions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MELBOURNE, Australia \x97 The burdens of being No. 1 can be enough to make even a young woman nicknamed Sunshine a little gloomy, particularly when that No. 1 player, Caroline Wozniacki, has yet to win a Grand Slam tournament and has started the season with a new racket and some uninspiring performances. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/sports/tennis/tennis-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 693, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/sports/tennis/tennis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/22/sports/tennis/tennis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No. 1? No Doubt. Predictable? No Way.', 'kicker': 'Australian Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No. 1? No Doubt. Predictable? No Way.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Australian Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wozniacki, Caroline', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:24:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Clarey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clarey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0a6d04b-c6b8-5270-ae5a-2fa90f19a8d6', 'word_count': 1116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0a6d04b-c6b8-5270-ae5a-2fa90f19a8d6'}, {'abstract': 'The fourth volume of “Stepping on Roses,” published by VIZ Media, enters our manga list at No. 8 this week.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/graphic-books-best-sellers-manga-romance-and-honors-from-gladd/', 'snippet': 'The fourth volume of “Stepping on Roses,” published by VIZ Media, enters our manga list at No. 8 this week.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The fourth volume of “Stepping on Roses,” published by VIZ Media, enters our manga list at No. 8 this week. At the center of the series is Sumi Kitamura, who is having immense financial troubles. Her prayers seem to be answered when she meets the wealthy Soichiro Ashida, who offers her money to marry him and enter high society. “Stepping on Roses” asks, can Sumi pass as a member of the elite and is the sham marriage worth it? Vol. 2 featured a childhood friend of Soichiro stirring up feelings in Sumi; the latest volume has someone from her husband’s corporation spying on the household. Will somebody eventually find out the truth? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Graphic Books Best Sellers: Manga Romance and Honors from GLAAD', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Marvel Comics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:30:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/226e4d3d-19bb-55bc-8b9a-4e6a52e12a77', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/226e4d3d-19bb-55bc-8b9a-4e6a52e12a77'}, {'abstract': 'Courthouse Confidential: A dispute over legal fees stemming from 9/11 litigation, and recalling the day Robert F. Kennedy learned that his brother had been assassinated.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/its-not-the-money-its-the-principle/', 'snippet': 'Courthouse Confidential: A dispute over legal fees stemming from 9/11 litigation, and recalling the day Robert F. Kennedy learned that his brother had been assassinated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lawyers, not surprisingly, are 