], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1969b55-b67a-5fc3-b7ad-4ec1a2d1ae7c', 'word_count': 399, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1969b55-b67a-5fc3-b7ad-4ec1a2d1ae7c'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Aldredge created the clothes for more than 300 stage and film productions.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/theoni-v-aldredge-costume-designer-for-chorus-line-annie-has-died/', 'snippet': 'Ms. Aldredge created the clothes for more than 300 stage and film productions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Theoni V. Aldredge, the designer of the fabulously feathered transvestites in the original stage version of “La Cage aux Folles” and Little Orphan Annie’s red dress on Broadway, died Friday morning in Connecticut, said her husband, Tom Aldredge. She was 88. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Theoni  V. Aldredge, Costume Designer for ‘Chorus Line’ and ‘Annie,’ Dies', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:46:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2a8cee2-bb45-56ce-a1a4-4cd8505ba831', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2a8cee2-bb45-56ce-a1a4-4cd8505ba831'}, {'abstract': 'A large private equity firm is seen as a likely suitor of the company.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/pep-boys-auto-parts-chains-weighs-sale/', 'snippet': 'A large private equity firm is seen as a likely suitor of the company.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pep Boys-Manny, Moe & Jack is considering a sale of the company, according to a person with direct knowledge of the matter. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pep Boys Auto Parts Chain Weighs Sale', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pep Boys', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:47:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/95373b69-8a46-5398-99f8-4125a4f41ea1', 'word_count': 301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95373b69-8a46-5398-99f8-4125a4f41ea1'}, {'abstract': 'How Palin-hatred drives the media-Palin symbiosis.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/the-coverage-of-sarah-palin/', 'snippet': 'How Palin-hatred drives the media-Palin symbiosis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I see that Nate Silver has brought his usual statistical acumen to bear on my suggestion, in this week’s column on the media’s dysfunctional relationship to Sarah Palin, that a “majority of Americans …are neither Palinoiacs nor Palinistas.” Not so fast, he writes, citing a recent Politico survey on American attitudes toward various public figures:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Coverage of Sarah Palin', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:47:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f665c4e-e865-5de5-b449-c86812ae6eb8', 'word_count': 900, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f665c4e-e865-5de5-b449-c86812ae6eb8'}, {'abstract': 'Gabrielle Giffords, Sarah Palin and America’s obsession with the rootin’-tootin’ female politician.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/magazine/23fob-wwln-t.html', 'snippet': 'Gabrielle Giffords, Sarah Palin and America’s obsession with the rootin’-tootin’ female politician.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Whether or not you believe there is any connection between the first assassination attempt ever made on an American female politician and the gun-slinging rhetoric of the first Republican woman ever nominated for the vice presidency, what’s undeniably true is that despite the vast philosophical and intellectual chasms between them, Representative Gabrielle Giffords of Arizona and the former Alaska governor Sarah Palin have something in common: they are both cowgirl politicians. In this, they are symptomatic of the too-narrow ways in which the United States is willing to accept women as leaders.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23wwln-span/23wwln-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 453, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23wwln-span/23wwln-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 453}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23wwln-span/23wwln-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23wwln-span/23wwln-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cowgirl Country', 'kicker': 'The Way We Live Now', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cowgirl Country', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Giffords, Gabrielle', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wayne, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oakley, Annie', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gillibrand, Kirsten E', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palin, Sarah', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:48:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca Traister', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Traister', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f004037-b4a3-5280-a988-e7d9ca80841a', 'word_count': 1041, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f004037-b4a3-5280-a988-e7d9ca80841a'}, {'abstract': 'Brock Enright and his cast of players offer “reality adventures” — chase scenes, unidentified assailants, romantic rivalries — to paying clients. Some call it therapy; he claims it’s art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/magazine/23Videogame-t.html', 'snippet': 'Brock Enright and his cast of players offer “reality adventures” — chase scenes, unidentified assailants, romantic rivalries — to paying clients. Some call it therapy; he claims it’s art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brock Enright rents a studio in an old Catholic school in Greenpoint, Brooklyn, a former classroom that is hard to navigate without stepping on a drawing or kicking a sculpture, but he makes much of his art beyond his studio’s walls — in bars, fast-food joints, Chinatown shopping plazas, rooftops and other locations throughout Manhattan. One evening not long ago, I joined him at the Sixth Ward, a bar on the Lower East Side, to watch him work on a new piece.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23movies-popup/23movies-popup-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 251, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23movies-popup/23movies-popup-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 251}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23movies-popup/23movies-popup-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 428, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23movies-popup/23movies-popup-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23movies-popup/23movies-popup-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'If Your Life Were a Movie', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'If Your Life Were a Movie', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:48:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonah Weiner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8df74895-ce0f-5ebb-8407-d99cba3cbd0b', 'word_count': 3003, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8df74895-ce0f-5ebb-8407-d99cba3cbd0b'}, {'abstract': 'Will an invasive procedure based on a doctor’s hunch stop a patient’s relentless deterioration?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/magazine/23diagnosis-t.html', 'snippet': 'Will an invasive procedure based on a doctor’s hunch stop a patient’s relentless deterioration?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The woman mumbled and ran a shaking hand through her dark mass of tangled curls. Her husband could barely make out what she was saying over the din of the emergency room at the John Muir Medical Center in Walnut Creek, Calif. “Rewind it,” she said, raising her voice, looking at her husband. “Rewind it,” she pleaded. Her husband squeezed her hand and forced a smile. Over the last four months she often repeated this phrase when she was confused. He wasn’t sure what she meant, but he wished they could rewind their lives and return to the way they were half a year earlier, before his previously healthy 64-year-old wife became sick.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23diagnosis-span/23diagnosis-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 306, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23diagnosis-span/23diagnosis-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 306}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23diagnosis-span/23diagnosis-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23diagnosis-span/23diagnosis-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Wasting Woman', 'kicker': 'Diagnosis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Wasting Woman', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ovaries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tumors', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:48:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Sanders, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sanders', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cd60aec-182f-5305-ac03-615505758ab6', 'word_count': 1356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3cd60aec-182f-5305-ac03-615505758ab6'}, {'abstract': 'No virtual relief for the 25-year itch.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/magazine/23lives-t.html', 'snippet': 'No virtual relief for the 25-year itch.', 'lead_paragraph': ' He friended me just after Thanksgiving, Week 13 of regular-season football this year. He wanted to know if I was the same Lisa who studied marine science at a certain East Coast college for a summer semester back in the early ’80s. I was. I am. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23lives/23lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/magazine/23lives/23lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Brief Electronic Affair', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A BRIEF ELECTRONIC AFFAIR', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T17:48:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa K. Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88f32da5-68e6-566c-b8e1-faf667fda89e', 'word_count': 776, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88f32da5-68e6-566c-b8e1-faf667fda89e'}, {'abstract': 'House call, house rules.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/magazine/23FOB-Ethicist-t.html', 'snippet': 'House call, house rules.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My elderly aunt became ill and phoned me, a physician, to ask if she should call an ambulance. I surmised that she was severely dehydrated. From my hospital, I took a bag of saline, IV tubing, an IV lock and a needle. An unsuspecting nurse handed me the tape that secures the needle. I gave my aunt these fluids at home, and she soon felt better, as did I: my stealing $50 worth of medical supplies saved the taxpayers more than a thousand dollars for an E.R. visit. Did I do right? E.G., NEW YORK', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/03/18/magazine/ethicist75/ethicist75-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2010/03/18/magazine/ethicist75/ethicist75-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Swiping Supplies', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Swiping Supplies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [