us is the host. (Podcast Archive) // ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Book Review Podcast: Philosophy Edition', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T21:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5150791-2848-5410-a129-8ecdd9c5362b', 'word_count': 76, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5150791-2848-5410-a129-8ecdd9c5362b'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs from Albania, Tunisia, Brooklyn and California.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/01/21/blogs/20110121POD.html', 'snippet': 'Photographs from Albania, Tunisia, Brooklyn and California.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photographs from Albania, Tunisia, Brooklyn and California.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/21/blogs/20110121POD-slide-ABNT/20110121POD-slide-ABNT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 701, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pictures of the Day: Albania and Elsewhere', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T21:02:47+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Blogs', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/6980173b-c528-54a3-b3e9-e903d1bbe709', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/6980173b-c528-54a3-b3e9-e903d1bbe709'}, {'abstract': 'Ip Man, the martial artist master and mentor to Bruce Lee, is the subject of four movies, one from the auteur Wong Kar-wai.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/movies/23ipman.html', 'snippet': 'Ip Man, the martial artist master and mentor to Bruce Lee, is the subject of four movies, one from the auteur Wong Kar-wai.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEARLY four decades after his death, Bruce Lee remains one of the most potent symbols of Hong Kong cinema’s golden age. The original crossover martial artist, a local child star turned celebrated creator of Jeet Kune Do, the art of the intercepting fist, Lee made only a handful of films after his international breakthrough. (He died in 1973 at the age of 32.) But kung fu classics like “Fist of Fury” (1972) and “Enter the Dragon” (1973) are the stuff of countless homages and imitations, and Lee’s life and career have been chronicled in dozens of books and films. While the Hong Kong martial arts drama has long been in decline, it’s only fitting that the latest stirrings of a revival are connected to Lee: the most popular kung fu screen hero of the moment is none other than Lee’s mentor, Ip Man. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/arts/IPMAN-1/IPMAN-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/23/arts/IPMAN-1/IPMAN-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/arts/IPMAN-1/IPMAN-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/arts/IPMAN-1/IPMAN-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/arts/IPMAN-1/IPMAN-1-thumbS