he New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A With Hofstra’s Charles Jenkins', 'kicker': 'The Quad', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cassara, Mo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jenkins, Charles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hofstra University', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-21T21:31:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Styczynski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Styczynski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f43a3f9-cec6-5041-801e-45afb550db3e', 'word_count': 866, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f43a3f9-cec6-5041-801e-45afb550db3e'}, {'abstract': 'Working with low budgets, Samuel Fuller turned necessity into idiosyncratic invention with “Shock Corridor” and “The Naked Kiss.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/movies/homevideo/23kehr.html', 'snippet': 'Working with low budgets, Samuel Fuller turned necessity into idiosyncratic invention with “Shock Corridor” and “The Naked Kiss.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN the early 1960s, after spending more than a decade working for major studios, the director and writer Samuel Fuller found himself back where he had started in Hollywood, working for one of the small, budget-minded studios collectively known as Poverty Row.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/arts/KEHR-1/KEHR-1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarg