', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/teaching-civil-war-history-2-0/', 'snippet': 'The Web can be both a blessing and a curse in teaching Civil War history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Civil War Sesquicentennial is upon us. Over the next four years Americans will be reminded of and engage in debates about every aspect of a war that fundamentally transformed the nation and that set us on a path we are still working to come to terms with. America went through the same process at the centennial. What’s different this time around is the focus on race and slavery, both of which have the potential to divide Americans and obscure the boundaries between the present and the past; that, and the ability for anyone to access millions of pages of information about the war, its causes and consequences through the Internet. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Teaching Civil War History 2.0', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'History', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Textbooks', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Robert E', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T01:51:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin M. Levin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fcfedcf7-ce54-5b79-8e43-61ddb70bcd63', 'word_count': 1368, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fcfedcf7-ce54-5b79-8e43-61ddb70bcd63'}, {'abstract': 'Eric Nadelstern followed a trajectory that took him from the classroom to the principal’s office and eventually to leadership in the department.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/nyregion/22deputy.html', 'snippet': 'Eric Nadelstern followed a trajectory that took him from the classroom to the principal’s office and eventually to leadership in the department.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A deputy schools chancellor who was one of the last old-guard educators to hold a top position at the Education Department and commanded several elements of the system’s reform efforts announced on Friday that he would retire at the end of the month.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Top Overseer of City Schools Plans to Retire', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Top Overseer Of City Schools Plans to Retire', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nadelstern, Eric', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Polakow-Suransky, Shael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T01:51:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fernanda Santos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fernanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Santos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3508eef-abac-5382-930b-ca3e46368fd8', 'word_count': 407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3508eef-abac-5382-930b-ca3e46368fd8'}, {'abstract': 'A statement from Viacom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/business/22viacom_statement.html', 'snippet': 'A statement from Viacom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'About 90% of the 2010 compensation is long-term equity awards that align our executives’ interests with those of our stockholders.\xa0 The majority of\xa0 2010 compensation is one-time equity grants related to the previously announced long-term extensions of employment agreements, which vest over the life of those agreements.\xa0\xa0 In 2010 Viacom achieved outstanding operational and financial results, including double-digit growth in operating income, adjusted net earnings and total shareholder return.\xa0 Viacom shares appreciated 33% during calendar year 2010, versus the S&P 500 gain of 12%.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Statement from Viacom', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Viacom', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T01:54:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Text', '_id': 'nyt://article/e41e8f65-e84c-5d93-8f77-2e85ec46155c', 'word_count': 84, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e41e8f65-e84c-5d93-8f77-2e85ec46155c'}, {'abstract': 'The dishes at Restaurant North can be urbane yet rough and tumble, and the owners know just where all the ingredients come from.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/nyregion/23dinewe.html', 'snippet': 'The dishes at Restaurant North can be urbane yet rough and tumble, and the owners know just where all the ingredients come from.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN early January, a friend called to say that he had bailed off of Interstate 684 in a snowstorm and wondered what the name of that restaurant in Armonk was — the one I’d mentioned to him a few weeks ago. “Restaurant North,” I said. “You are a lucky man.”', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/nyregion/23dinewe3/23dinewe3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/nyregion/23dinewe3/23dinewe3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Farm-to-Table Menu That Plays the Edge', 'kicker': 'Westchester Dining | Armonk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Farm-to-Table Menu That Plays the Edge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Westchester County (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T01:56:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alice Gabriel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alice', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gabriel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/138a24ae-790d-560d-975d-d0afe765c574', 'word_count': 1124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/138a24ae-790d-560d-975d-d0afe765c574'}, {'abstract': 'A Florida pastor leads his flock in fasting that he advocates as a way of drawing closer to God.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/us/22beliefs.html', 'snippet': 'A Florida pastor leads his flock in fasting that he advocates as a way of drawing closer to God.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JACKSONVILLE, Fla. — After last Sunday night’s service at Celebration Church, where a 10-piece band played Christian rock for 1,600 worshipers, Stovall Weems, the pastor, met me in his “green room” and offered nibbles from a tray of kiwi, pineapple and melon slices.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/us/BELIEFS/BELIEFS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/us/BELIEFS/BELIEFS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/22/us/BELIEFS/BELIEFS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Many Choices on the Menu of Religious Fasts', 'kicker': 'Beliefs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Many Choices on the Menu of Religious Fasts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bible', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jacksonville (Fla)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T01:58:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Oppenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oppenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4c5e479-2c2d-5b45-9194-2a8f27d8dc65', 'word_count': 923, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4c5e479-2c2d-5b45-9194-2a8f27d8dc65'}, {'abstract': 'John Galliano revisited the familiar territory of the Russian Revolution, this time with Rudolf Nureyev in tow.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/the-russians-are-coming-again/', 'snippet': 'John Galliano revisited the familiar territory of the Russian Revolution, this time with Rudolf Nureyev in tow.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Galliano revisited the familiar territory of the Russian Revolution, this time with Rudolf Nureyev in tow. Don’t ask me how Mr. Galliano got from 1917 to 1961, the year that Nureyev defected (in Paris), but he did, and you sort of go along with the reconstructed history and the homage to the ballet star. But it was strange: Once the show dispensed with the Russian émigrés and their tired coats, the models’ faces acquired the waxy perfection of mannequins or Rudy lookalikes. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Russians Are Coming (Again?)', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T01:59:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/67ec7e08-8438-50f0-acd2-d73668dd69f4', 'word_count': 84, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67ec7e08-8438-50f0-acd2-d73668dd69f4'}, {'abstract': 'There are a handful of prisoner rehabilitation programs that really work. So why aren’t there more of them?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/removing-the-roadblocks-to-rehabilitation/', 'snippet': 'There are a handful of prisoner rehabilitation programs that really work. So why aren’t there more of them?', 'lead_paragraph': 'What works and what doesn’t work to solve a social problem is often no mystery.\xa0 The mystery is why we so often persist in doing what doesn’t work.\xa0 The topic of Tuesday’s column — prisoner re-entry into the community — offers myriad examples.\xa0\xa0 One is the practice of dropping people getting out of jail or prison right back into the neighborhoods where they got in trouble in the first place.\xa0 Intuition tells us that this is a bad idea: the old street corners and the old friends seem like a recipe for the old troubles.\xa0 Research on this idea is rare and hard to do — it’s tough to get around the problem that the person who chooses not to go home may have other qualities that make him successful.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Removing the Roadblocks to Rehabilitation', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vocational Training', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fortune Society', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T02:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2dc9467-b394-5cdd-9281-351666c07771', 'word_count': 1424, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2dc9467-b394-5cdd-9281-351666c07771'}, {'abstract': 'A year after a losing season, the Bears decided to push all their chips in, staking the season, and perhaps several future years, on a handful of risky bets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/sports/football/22bears.html', 'snippet': 'A year after a losing season, the Bears decided to push all their chips in, staking the season, and perhaps several future years, on a handful of risky bets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LAKE FOREST, Ill. \x97 It was almost exactly a year ago and Lovie Smith was having a colder-than-usual January in Chicago. After a third successive non-playoff season \x97 the 2009 schedule ended with a 7-9 record \x97 Smith was considered a lame-duck coach.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/sports/bears-1/bears-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 714, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/22/sports/bears-1/bears-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/22/sports/bears-1/bears-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bears’ Plan For Recovery Included Stimulus', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bears’ Plan For Recovery Included Stimulus', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Lovie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago Bears', 'rank': 4, 'major': '