ry 22, 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRONT PAGE', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T02:56:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f649ea8-0baa-5e9a-9a57-b3c2c18ea7ee', 'word_count': 502, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f649ea8-0baa-5e9a-9a57-b3c2c18ea7ee'}, {'abstract': 'Victor Fleming orders up his usual fun puzzle.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/21/stick-with-it/', 'snippet': 'Victor Fleming orders up his usual fun puzzle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SATURDAY’S PUZZLE — Was this a tough one for you? Saturdays are the cruellest day for me, to paraphrase T.S. Eliot. It’s very difficult for me to get even a toehold in Saturday puzzles, but perhaps with more practice things will get easier.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saturday: Stick With It', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T03:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/65216ea8-9ff1-598d-b4ba-e782931f1e16', 'word_count': 275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65216ea8-9ff1-598d-b4ba-e782931f1e16'}, {'abstract': 'The trade tensions with China sometimes seem like a rerun of the 1980s rivalry with Japan. But can Washington use its old strategies?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/business/23japan.html', 'snippet': 'The trade tensions with China sometimes seem like a rerun of the 1980s rivalry with Japan. But can Washington use its old strategies?', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN the 1980s, the United States faced an unnerving challenge from a rising economic powerhouse and export dynamo. It was an impressive challenger, to be sure, but one that often bent rules of global competition unfairly to its advantage by handing out financial subsidies to domestic companies, discriminating against foreign suppliers in government contracts, pilfering Western technology and keeping its currency cheap.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/business/JP-JAPAN-1/JP-JAPAN-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/business/JP-JAPAN-1/JP-JAPAN-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/business/JP-JAPAN-1/JP-JAPAN-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Maybe Japan Was Just a Warm-Up', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Maybe Japan Was Just a Warm-Up', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Investments', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T03:01:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Lohr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lohr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/21a9ccf9-db3f-5638-8769-0b67b415c1df', 'word_count': 1563, 'uri': 'nyt://article/21a9ccf9-db3f-5638-8769-0b67b415c1df'}, {'abstract': 'After 40 Central Americans vanished, the United Nations high commissioner for human rights wants to know whether the military and police were complicit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/world/americas/22mexico.html', 'snippet': 'After 40 Central Americans vanished, the United Nations high commissioner for human rights wants to know whether the military and police were complicit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MEXICO CITY \x97 The United Nations top human rights official pressed Mexico on Friday to investigate the disappearance of 40 Central American migrants last month and determine whether the military and the police were complicit.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.N. Urges Inquiry of Migrants’ Disappearance in Mexico', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.N. Urges Inquiry of Migrants’ Disappearance in Mexico', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kidnapping', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Central America', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T03:03:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randal C. Archibold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randal', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Archibold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/344bf53a-5aa0-59c6-a551-d70f62cab757', 'word_count': 393, 'uri': 'nyt://article/344bf53a-5aa0-59c6-a551-d70f62cab757'}, {'abstract': 'The Industrial and Commercial Bank of China signed an agreement to obtain an 80 percent stake in the American arm of the Bank of East Asia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/world/asia/22beijing.html', 'snippet': 'The Industrial and Commercial Bank of China signed an agreement to obtain an 80 percent stake in the American arm of the Bank of East Asia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Industrial and Commercial Bank of China signed an agreement on Friday to obtain an 80 percent stake in the American arm of the Bank of East Asia, according to a person briefed on the agreement. The deal, if approved by regulators, would mean that for the first time, a state-owned Chinese bank would have a controlling stake in a United States financial institution. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chinese Bank to Buy Stake in U.S. Arm of Bank of East Asia', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chinese Bank to Buy Stake in U.S. Arm of Bank of East Asia', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Industrial and Commercial Bank o