 '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T11:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emmarie Huetteman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emmarie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Huetteman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/47db9786-2298-50ae-9839-9a4ed5ab9489', 'word_count': 371, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47db9786-2298-50ae-9839-9a4ed5ab9489'}, {'abstract': 'An N.F.L. season, like life and the occasional takeout order, is full of regrets. Let’s take a little inventory of a few things — both writers and commenters — got wrong on the Fifth Down this season.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/perils-of-n-f-l-forecasting/', 'snippet': 'An N.F.L. season, like life and the occasional takeout order, is full of regrets. Let’s take a little inventory of a few things — both writers and commenters — got wrong on the Fifth Down this season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An N.F.L. season, like life and the occasional takeout order, is full of regrets. Jerry Jones probably regrets his slow hook on Wade Phillips. Mike Shanahan might lament his premature one on Donovan McNabb. And Matt Dodge, well … let’s just leave him alone for a few months.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Perils of N.F.L. Forecasting', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago Bears', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Bay Packers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pittsburgh Steelers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T12:05:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Das', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Das', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/473e0108-e855-5209-99a6-3adf84f45c67', 'word_count': 793, 'uri': 'nyt://article/473e0108-e855-5209-99a6-3adf84f45c67'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Sullivan’s Wealth Matters column looks at how Wall Street’s highfliers are investing their bonus money.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/where-wall-street-bonus-money-is-going/', 'snippet': 'Paul Sullivan’s Wealth Matters column looks at how Wall Street’s highfliers are investing their bonus money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paul Sullivan’s Wealth Matters column this week notes that bonus season on Wall Street always evokes the image of highfliers blowing their bonus money on fast cars and penthouse apartments. But, he writes, while that certainly happens, most of the bonus money has long been split between the boring and exotic.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Wall Street Bonus Money Is Going', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bonuses', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T12:30:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bucks Editors', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bucks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Editors', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f79e7245-a93d-5bc1-9816-4b39155acd5e', 'word_count': 125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f79e7245-a93d-5bc1-9816-4b39155acd5e'}, {'abstract': 'Experts question a contention by Canadian property owners that elevated levels of carbon dioxide on their property can be attributed to gas from a carbon-capture reservoir.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/a-carbon-storage-leak-not-so-fast-experts-caution/', 'snippet': 'Experts question a contention by Canadian property owners that elevated levels of carbon dioxide on their property can be attributed to gas from a carbon-capture reservoir.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Has the world’s largest carbon-capture project sprung a leak?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Carbon Storage Leak?  Not So Fast, Experts Caution', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Pollution', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T13:08:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Collins Rudolf', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'Collins', 'lastname': 'Rudolf', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/96c9ed19-21e4-5e7c-a904-df2e2d8c1bb0', 'word_count': 522, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96c9ed19-21e4-5e7c-a904-df2e2d8c1bb0'}, {'abstract': 'What some readers proposed to jettison from the regulatory rulebook.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/reader-response-dumb-regulations/', 'snippet': 'What some readers proposed to jettison from the regulatory rulebook.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Friday, we asked you to name dumb regulations. Some of the policies you described in response certainly would seem like excellent candidates for a second look by rule makers. We haven’t tried to verify these accounts, nor have we asked the agencies for comment. These are simply three notes that piqued our interest.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reader Response: ‘Dumb’ Regulations', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T14:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Binyamin Appelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Binyamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec553960-fca4-5369-8f5d-4ebd58bc5f36', 'word_count': 473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec553960-fca4-5369-8f5d-4ebd58bc5f36'}, {'abstract': 'In a fascinating article in “The Atlantic,” Joshua S. Speckman writes about the tremendous amount of spin being generated by  copoly strings.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/racket-strings-and-topspin/', 'snippet': 'In a fascinating article in “The Atlantic,” Joshua S. Speckman writes about the tremendous amount of spin being generated by  copoly strings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was an advertisement in the 1970’s that featured the Argentinian Guillermo Vilas touting a brand of tennis string. It appeared in tennis magazines, and, perhaps to lend it some authenticity, was written in an awkward, broken English. My friends and I found it funny enough to commit to memory. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Racket Strings and Topspin', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Australian Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T14:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Macdonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macdonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/65d992f9-7696-57a9-96f6-9d1f247d19a1', 'word_count': 343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65d992f9-7696-57a9-96f6-9d1f247d19a1'}, {'abstract': 'Scenes from the Paris Fashion Week photo diary of Sonny Vandevelde.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/photos-of-the-moment-givenchy-2/', 'snippet': 'Scenes from the Paris Fashion Week photo diary of Sonny Vandevelde.', 'lead_paragraph': ' NYTD.fullScreenSlideShowFactory.createSlideshow("#338", 592, 490, true, chameleonData);', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Photos of the Moment | Givenchy', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T14:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sonny Vandevelde', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sonny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vandevelde', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b3493ff-313b-54ae-9722-b073792304db', 'word_count': 35, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b3493ff-313b-54ae-9722-b073792304db'}, {'abstract': 'The Anaheim Ducks were headed to  Montreal for a rare visit Saturday night, but more significant  is the return of Saku Koivu, who had mixed reviews as the Canadiens’ captain.Koivu’s tenure in that role was mixed.', 'web_url': 'https://slapshot.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/for-ducks-koivu-a-return-to-montreal/', 'snippet': 'The Anaheim Ducks were headed to  Montreal for a rare visit Saturday night, but more significant  is the return of Saku Koivu, who had mixed reviews as the Canadiens’ captain.Koivu’s tenure in that role was mixed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Anaheim Ducks were headed to Montreal for a rare visit Saturday night, but more significant for Canadiens fans is the return of Saku Koivu, the Canadiens’ former captain.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Ducks’ Koivu, a Return to Montreal', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koivu, Saku', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Montreal Canadiens', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T14:25:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein and Stu Hackel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Stu', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hackel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2ff9dac-a4c2-5baa-970d-68e4893b8ef5', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2ff9dac-a4c2-5baa-970d-68e4893b8ef5'}, {'abstract': 'The return of a hackneyed misconception.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/competitiveness/', 'snippet': 'The return of a hackneyed misconception.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sigh. So it appears that President Obama is going to make “competitiveness” his main economic theme. To be fair, he could (and may well) do worse. But this is hackneyed stuff, and involves a fundamental misconception about the nature of our economic problems.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '“Competitiveness”', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T14:34:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a7cf106-2cfe-530d-b444-e2190b853151', 'word_count': 315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a7cf106-2cfe-530d-b444-e2190b853151'}, {'abstract': 'Chickweed — that tiny-leafed, low-to-the-ground, corn-tasting weed — thrives through the winter, come freeze or thaw.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/urban-forager-under-snow-she-flourishes/', 'snippet': 'Chickweed — that tiny-leafed, low-to-the-ground, corn-tasting weed — thrives through the winter, come freeze or thaw.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Blizzards, snowstorms and freezing temperatures have no effect on springy, delicate-seeming chickweed, which for the past few months has been growing stealthily across the city. Even when most plants are lying dormant, one can often find this tenacious plant thriving under a blanket of snow.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Urban Forager | A Green Queen in Winter', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weeds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T14:39:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ava Chin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ava', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ced6fee-6652-592f-9d57-c6d728baf1eb', 'word_count': 408, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ced6fee-6652-592f-9d57-c6d728baf1eb'}, {'abstract': 'Activists have been harassed, beaten and killed when they have used a right-to-know law to make inquiries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/world/asia/23india.html', 'snippet': 'Activists have been harassed, beaten and killed when they have used a right-to-know law to make inquiries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KODINAR, India \x97 Amit Jethwa had just left his lawyer’s office after discussing a lawsuit he had filed to stop an illicit limestone quarry with ties to powerful local politicians. That is when the assassins struck, speeding out of the darkness on a roaring motorbike, pistols blazing. He died on the spot, blood pouring from his mouth and nose. He was 38. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/world/23DOGINDIA1/23DOGINDIA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/world/23DOGINDIA1/23DOGINDIA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/world/23DOGINDIA1/23DOGINDIA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'High Price for India’s Information Law', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Indians Use Information Law, at a Deadly Risk', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bribery and Kickbacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T14:55:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lydia Polgreen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lydia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Polgreen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bbb2965f-c342-5969-babd-b25dd9879745', 'word_count': 1207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bbb2965f-c342-5969-babd-b25dd9879745'}, {'abstract': 'This Sunday’s games may go a long way in answering the question of who’s the third-best quarterback in the N.F.L.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/whos-no-3-roethlisberger-or-rodgers/', 'snippet': 'This Sunday’s games may go a long way in answering the question of who’s the third-best quarterback in the N.F.L.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brady vs. Manning? Who cares.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who’s No. 3? Roethlisberger or Rodgers?', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodgers, Aaron', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roethlisberger, Ben', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T15:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Luis DeLoureiro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Luis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DeLoureiro', 'qualifier'