'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T18:48:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Job Market', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Oliversmith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oliversmith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72ac1fb6-92a9-5ac9-b490-6097c8e10422', 'word_count': 933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72ac1fb6-92a9-5ac9-b490-6097c8e10422'}, {'abstract': '“I told myself early on that I wasn’t going to make gender an issue in my career or allow other people to do so,” says Judy McReynolds, C.E.O. of the freight company Arkansas Best.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/jobs/23boss.html', 'snippet': '“I told myself early on that I wasn’t going to make gender an issue in my career or allow other people to do so,” says Judy McReynolds, C.E.O. of the freight company Arkansas Best.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GROWING up, I spent many weekends on my grandparents’ farm an hour from us in Oklahoma. I remember digging potatoes and visiting the newborn piglets. My grandfather had eight brothers, and I learned to be comfortable around men at an early age. You had better be smiling or you’d be teased.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/business/BOSS/BOSS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 794, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/business/BOSS/BOSS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/business/BOSS/BOSS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheigh