ght end Bart Starr would calmly seek out when the dirt-stained jerseys of the Bears or the Giants writhed in front of all his other options.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/sports/broadcast1/broadcast1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 882, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/sports/broadcast1/broadcast1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/sports/broadcast1/broadcast1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Childhood Stories From the Inside of a Dynasty', 'kicker': 'Cheering Section', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Childhood Stories From the Inside Of a Dynasty', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Super Bowl', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Bay Packers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T20:26:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Glanz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glanz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04780099-1102-5eca-b81e-7d7808a6a467', 'word_count': 985, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04780099-1102-5eca-b81e-7d7808a6a467'}, {'abstract': 'A network of spies run by Duane R. Clarridge shows how private citizens can exploit the chaos of rivalries inside the government to carry out their agenda.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/world/23clarridge.html', 'snippet': 'A network of spies run by Duane R. Clarridge shows how private citizens can exploit the chaos of rivalries inside the government to carry out their agenda.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON \x97 Duane R. Clarridge parted company with the Central Intelligence Agency more than two decades ago, but from poolside at his home near San Diego, he still runs a network of spies. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/world/23clarridge-span/23clarridge-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 351, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/23/world/23clarridge-span/23clarridge-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 351}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/world/23clarridge-span/23DOGJPCLARRIDGE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 691, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/world/23clarridge-span/23clarridge-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/world/23clarridge-span/23clarridge-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Former Spy With Agenda Operates a Private C.I.A.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'THE SHADOW WAR; A FORMER SPY, NOW OPERATING HIS OWN C.I.A.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Furlong, Michael D', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Military Contractors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karzai, Ahmed Wali', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karzai, Hamid', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T20:30:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Mazzetti', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mazzetti', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c783644-73de-5ced-b825-f89f3ff88e96', 'word_count': 2707, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c783644-73de-5ced-b825-f89f3ff88e96'}, {'abstract': 'John Hawkes, a Sundance darling, has proved very good at playing bad in movies like “Winter’s Bone” and this year’s “Martha Marcy May Marlene.”', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/sundances-go-to-bad-guy/', 'snippet': 'John Hawkes, a Sundance darling, has proved very good at playing bad in movies like “Winter’s Bone” and this year’s “Martha Marcy May Marlene.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARK CITY, Utah— Walking up Main Street here on Friday afternoon, John Hawkes was recognized by some fans and happily posed for a few photos. Mr. Hawkes has always been a Sundance darling, but now the rest of the world is taking note. As the menacing addict and familyman Teardrop in Debra Granik’s Ozarks drama “Winter’s Bone,” he’s received awards buzz and nominations, the most attention of a career that has included roles in “Deadwood” and Miranda July’s 2005 Sundance hit, “Me and You and Everyone We Know.” Mr. Hawkes is back at Sundance with two films: “Martha Marcy May Marlene,” in which he plays a cult leader; and “Higher Ground,” Vera Farmiga’s directorial debut. (He also voiced a talking cat in Ms. July’s second feature, “The Future,” which had its premiere here on Friday, though she eventually replaced him with her own voice.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sundance’s Go-To Bad Guy', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hawkes, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'July, Miranda', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T20:44:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/22812a9f-5819-5e43-9f01-90769dfa778b', 'word_count': 420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22812a9f-5819-5e43-9f01-90769dfa778b'}, {'abstract': 'Playing for FC Barcelona, the Spanish prodigy drafted by the Minnesota Timberwolves has made only a third of his field goal attempts this season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/sports/basketball/23rubio.html', 'snippet': 'Playing for FC Barcelona, the Spanish prodigy drafted by the Minnesota Timberwolves has made only a third of his field goal attempts this season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rarely would a 20-year-old basketball player be considered over the hill. ', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/sports/RUBIO/RUBIO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 937, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/sports/RUBIO/RUBIO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/sports/RUBIO/RUBIO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'He’s 20. Is Rubio’s Fine Future in the Past?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Rubio, 20, It May Be Time To Leave Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Minnesota Timberwolves', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rubio, Ricky', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'FC Barcelona', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T20:47:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Givony', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Givony', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d10dc33e-0102-5786-bc35-cebd3fd637c1', 'word_count': 1037, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d10dc33e-0102-5786-bc35-cebd3fd637c1'}, {'abstract': 'How a band of Upper South Congressmen tried to keep their states in the Union.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/no-better-southern-man/', 'snippet': 'How a band of Upper South Congressmen tried to keep their states in the Union.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The featureless flat plains of West Tennessee seemed an unlikely locale to harbor a “Southern Black Republican.” But his political opponents routinely hung this epithet — and worse — on Rep. Emerson Etheridge. That’s because, unlike many Southern antisecessionists in early 1861, Etheridge continued to proclaim unconditional loyalty to the Union. Even worse, he ridiculed the idea that the Republican Party intended to interfere with slavery. Secessionists and Southern Rights supporters sputtered in frustration. Etheridge, they said, had fallen into “the depths of disgrace and infamy.” He appealed only to the “ignorant and blind lick-spittles” rather than to “the slaveholding and enlightened portion of the people.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘No Better Southern Man’', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southern States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tennessee', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T20:48:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By daniel w. crofts', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b81f54d3-cead-5ea4-a19d-1234637c78a2', 'word_count': 1460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b81f54d3-cead-5ea4-a19d-1234637c78a2'}, {'abstract': 'The N.F.L. is selling several thousand $200 tickets for fans to watch the Super Bowl —   not in  Cowboys Stadium, but outside the $1.12 billion behemoth, on television.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/n-f-l-offers-tickets-to-super-bowl-party-outside-cowboys-stadium/', 'snippet': 'The N.F.L. is selling several thousand $200 tickets for fans to watch the Super Bowl —   not in  Cowboys Stadium, but outside the $1.12 billion behemoth, on television.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The N.F.L. is stretching the meaning of a ticket to the Super Bowl, the most prized ticket in sports.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'N.F.L. Offers Tickets to Super Bowl Party Outside Cowboys Stadium', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Super Bowl', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T21:18:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8bb1001e-1316-5f99-b6dc-00fde84e4e25', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8bb1001e-1316-5f99-b6dc-00fde84e4e25'}, {'abstract': 'A fight to lead a state party through the 2012 race for president ended with an upset by a conservative activist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/us/politics/23hampshire.html', 'snippet': 'A fight to lead a state party through the 2012 race for president ended with an upset by a conservative activist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DERRY, N.H. \x97 A fight to lead the New Hampshire Republican Party through next year’s presidential race ended Saturday with an upset victory by a conservative candidate backed by members of the Tea Party and other grass-roots groups. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/us/23kimball-span/23kimball-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 339, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/23/us/23kimball-span/23kimball-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 339}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/us/23kimball-span/23kimball-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 659, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/us/23kimball-span/23kimball-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/us/23kimball-span/23kimball-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tea Party Activist Takes Over New Hampshire G.O.P.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Hampshire G.O.P. Picks Newcomer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea Party Movement', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T21:19:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abby Goodnough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodnough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/704ef26e-7359-5ccd-a54f-fcc97fb73d11', 'word_count': 840, 'uri': 'nyt://article/704ef26e-7359-5ccd-a54f-fcc97fb73d11'}, {'abstract': '“True Grit” has unalloyed faith in values antithetical to those of the 21st century America so deftly skewered in “The Social Network.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/opinion/23rich.html', 'snippet': '“True Grit” has unalloyed faith in values antithetical to those of the 21st century America so deftly skewered in “The Social Network.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A month before John Wayne won the 1969 Best Actor Oscar for “True Grit,” Richard Nixon wrote him a “Dear Duke” fan letter from the Oval Office: “I saw it in the W.H. with my family and for once we agree with the critics — you were great!” Some four decades later, his rave was echoed by another Republican warrior, this time in praise of the “True Grit” remake with Jeff Bridges in the role of the old, fat, hard-drinking, half-blind 19th-century United States marshal Rooster Cogburn. Shortly after New Year’s, Liz Cheney told The Times that her parents saw “True Grit” at the Teton Theater in Jackson, Wyo., and gave it “two thumbs up.”', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/opinion/23blittcover/23blittcover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/23/opinion/23blittcover/23blittcover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 420}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/opinion/23blittcover/23blittcover-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/opinion/23blittcover/23blittcover-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/opinion/23blittcover/23blittcover-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The One-Eyed Man Is King', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The One-Eyed Man Is King', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coen, Ethan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuckerberg, Mark E', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coen, Joel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fincher, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T21:39:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Rich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c20986e-b3e7-5cba-bca1-af318c9b39bb', 'word_count': 1563, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c20986e-b3e7-5cba-bca1-af318c9b39bb'}, {'abstract': 'We are still waiting for President Obama to fulfill his promises on gun safety.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/opinion/23sun1.html', 'snippet': 'We are still waiting for President Obama to fulfill his promises on gun safety.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is widely believed in Washington that there is no chance the gun lobby and the new Republican majority in the House would ever permit passage of the modest ideas for tightening America’s absurdly lax gun laws that have surfaced since the massacre in Tucson.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saner Gun Laws', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Saner Gun Laws', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Rifle Assn', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'School Shootings', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T21:56:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf55fbdc-2466-540e-b76a-ddf5266d1369', 'word_count': 611, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf55fbdc-2466-540e-b76a-ddf5266d1369'}, {'abstract': 'The momentary infatuation with a “virtual fence” with Mexico resulted in changes to the separation of powers that raise constitutional questions.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/legacy-of-a-fence/', 'snippet': 'The momentary infatuation with a “virtual fence” with Mexico resulted in changes to the separation of powers that raise constitutional questions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I fell in love withJanet Napolitano before I ever met her, back in 2005, when she was governor of Arizona and had this to say about the proposal in Washington to build a fence along the Mexican border:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Legacy of a Fence', 'kicker': 'Opinionator', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitution (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fences', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chertoff, Michael', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Napolitano, Janet', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARIZONA', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T22:07:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d510e424-cc2e-5ffd-97f8-2821009c3cb3', 'word_count': 770, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d510e424-cc2e-5ffd-97f8-2821009c3cb3'}, {'abstract': 'If the federal government is going to end homelessness among veterans, it will have to find a way to help the hard cases. One program is pointing the way.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/opinion/23sun2.html', 'snippet': 'If the federal government is going to end homelessness among veterans, it will have to find a way to help the hard cases. One program is pointing the way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the federal government is going to end homelessness among veterans in five years, as Veterans Affairs Secretary Eric Shinseki has vowed to do, then it will have to find a way to help the hard cases. These are the long-term street people \x97 men mostly, many fragile in mind or body, often addicted to alcohol or drugs. They shuttle from shelter to street to emergency room, burning up caregivers’ energy and social-service dollars. ', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Housing and a Chance', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Housing and a Chance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Housing and Urban Development Department', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Veterans Affairs Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T22:09:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a86e0ea-3d30-5cfc-98dd-4ffaa76a69f3', 'word_count': 383, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a86e0ea-3d30-5cfc-98dd-4ffaa76a69f3'}, {'abstract': 'Killing the publicly subsidized presidential campaign finance system, adopted in the wake of the Watergate scandals, is the last thing the House should do.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/opinion/23sun3.html', 'snippet': 'Killing the publicly subsidized presidential campaign finance system, adopted in the wake of the Watergate scandals, is the last thing the House should do.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the Republicans’ thumbs up-or-down version of lawmaking, the House majority leader, Eric Cantor, is targeting for extinction the publicly subsidized presidential campaign finance system adopted in the wake of the Watergate scandals. ', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Watergate? What’s That?', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Watergate? What’s That?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cantor, Eric', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T22:11:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/12890da6-b6f0-534d-804d-b2f7da7330e8', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12890da6-b6f0-534d-804d-b2f7da7330e8'}, {'abstract': 'It used to be uncommon for senators to walk away from their exclusive club. But remaining now entails months and months of hard work, often with no guarantee of a return.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/us/politics/23cong.html', 'snippet': 'It used to be uncommon for senators to walk away from their exclusive club. But remaining now entails months and months of hard work, often with no guarantee of a return.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON \x97 As veteran members of the Senate contemplate the future, they find themselves pondering a question memorably and musically posed by the Clash: Should I stay or should I go?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Incumbent Senators Weigh Options', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Watching for Clues As Senators Weigh Options', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lieberman, Joseph I', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lugar, Richard G', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Conrad, Kent', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nelson, Bill', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hutchison, Kay Bailey', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T22:12:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Hulse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hulse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/057ee633-4155-533b-b404-da225bbadc51', 'word_count': 1001, 'uri': 'nyt://article/057ee633-4155-533b-b404-da225bbadc51'}, {'abstract': 'On the Burr family cemetery, a little graveyard in the middle of a Home Depot parking lot.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/opinion/23sun4.html', 'snippet': 'On the Burr family cemetery, a little graveyard in the middle of a Home Depot parking lot.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Commack, on Long Island, a little graveyard lies surrounded by chain-link fence and buried in snow. Inside are nine headstones, some from the 1800s, many cracked and tilted, under spin