ale lead, a young British actress named Felicity Jones, is this year’s Carey Mulligan (“An Education”).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sundance Embraces the Latest ‘It’ Girl', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Doremus, Drake', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mulligan, Carey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T22:34:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a7d111d-30b3-55fd-8929-3c6c9cbe06d7', 'word_count': 240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a7d111d-30b3-55fd-8929-3c6c9cbe06d7'}, {'abstract': 'A response to an article about the growing and influential lawsuit-lending industry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/opinion/lweb23loans.html', 'snippet': 'A response to an article about the growing and influential lawsuit-lending industry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lawsuit Loans', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lawsuit Loans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lobbying and Lobbyists', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Bankruptcies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T22:35:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/60d2528b-6aa9-519e-84fa-d4ad1e8b50b7', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60d2528b-6aa9-519e-84fa-d4ad1e8b50b7'}, {'abstract': 'Evan Lysacek, winner of the gold medal in men’s figure skating last year, discussed his charity work, future plans and interest in acting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/sports/olympics/23seconds.html', 'snippet': 'Evan Lysacek, winner of the gold medal in men’s figure skating last year, discussed his charity work, future plans and interest in acting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A year ago, Evan Lysacek won the gold medal in the men’s figure skating competition at the Vancouver Olympics. He was the first American to win the event since Brian Boitano in 1988. These days, besides headlining the Stars on Ice tour, he is a member of the board of Figure Skating in Harlem, which teaches girls academic and life skills through ice skating, and serves on the celebrity board of Ronald McDonald House Charities, which aims to improve the health and well-being of children. He will be honored at the Thurman Munson Awards dinner on Feb. 1 by the AHRC New York City Foundation, an organization that benefits handicapped children. JOE BRESCIA', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/sports/seconds/seconds-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/23/sports/seconds/seconds-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Undecided on Olympics', 'kicker': 'Q. and A. With Evan Lysacek', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Undecided On Olympics', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lysacek, Evan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Figure Skating', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T22:37:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Brescia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brescia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/471ce9fb-1dee-53de-98cb-ceb284c6f9ef', 'word_count': 1239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/471ce9fb-1dee-53de-98cb-ceb284c6f9ef'}, {'abstract': 'Tunisian democracy has a chance, but won’t likely spread.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/opinion/23kaplan.html', 'snippet': 'Tunisian democracy has a chance, but won’t likely spread.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE West stands captivated by Tunisia, where a month of peaceful protests by secular working- and middle-class Arabs has toppled a dictator, raising hopes that this North African country of 10 million will set off democracy movements throughout a region of calcified dictatorships. But before we envision a new Middle East remade in the manner of Europe 1989, it is worth cataloguing the pivotal ways in which Tunisia is unique.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/opinion/23opedimg/23opedimg-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/23/opinion/23oped