None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Plains Giants Establishing Tiny Foothold On Tables', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ranches', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bison', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meat', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-22T22:49:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kirk Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kirk', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec9039a8-06ad-58c3-95d6-b5edf7caeaaa', 'word_count': 1085, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec9039a8-06ad-58c3-95d6-b5edf7caeaaa'}, {'abstract': 'Here’s what my Chinese blogs looked like, before censors arrived.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/22/blogs-interrupted/', 'snippet': 'Here’s what my Chinese blogs looked like, before censors arrived.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My Sunday column is a show-and-tell of an experiment I conducted in Beijing to look for the boundaries of what is permissible on the Chinese Internet. A Chinese editor I much admired, Qin Benli, taught me a wonderful expression in Chinese, 擦边球, or cabianqiu, meaning a ping pong ball that just nicks the edge of table. It’s legal, but with no room to spare. Qin told me that that is exactly what a good Chinese journalist aims to do with each article. So I tried to construct a blog and micro-blog (the Chinese equivalent of Twitter) to find out where the cabianqiu are.\xa0 Below are pictures of a micro-blog and blog that I started in Beijing in December:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Blogs Interrupted', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywo