amined the deadly raid on a flotilla bound for Gaza last May concluded Sunday that Israel had acted in accordance with international law when its military enforced its naval blockade by intercepting the ships in international waters.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Israeli Panel Rules Flotilla Raid Legal', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Panel Finds Israel’s Actions Justified in Fatal Flotilla Raid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gaza Strip', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Free Gaza Movement', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T17:28:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isabel Kershner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isabel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eada7bea-850b-555b-9947-0932ed31a0a5', 'word_count': 734, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eada7bea-850b-555b-9947-0932ed31a0a5'}, {'abstract': 'But still it moves.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/nobody-expects-the-macroeconomic-inquisition/', 'snippet': 'But still it moves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A correspondent directs me to this article:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nobody Expects the Macroeconomic Inquisition', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T17:58:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9818f0f6-c017-53b3-9469-4dc4a453cf78', 'word_count': 78, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9818f0f6-c017-53b3-9469-4dc4a453cf78'}, {'abstract': 'Scenes from the Paris Fashion Week photo diary of Sonny Vandevelde.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/photos-of-the-moment-dior/', 'snippet': 'Scenes from the Paris Fashion Week photo diary of Sonny Vandevelde.', 'lead_paragraph': ' NYTD.fullScreenSlideShowFactory.createSlideshow("#393", 592, 490, true, chameleonData);', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Photos of the Moment | Dior', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T18:00:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sonny Vandevelde', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sonny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vandevelde', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/cecc8a87-6486-5162-9a48-72947c5869ae', 'word_count': 35, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cecc8a87-6486-5162-9a48-72947c5869ae'}, {'abstract': 'Paramount picked up the giggly romantic drama “Like Crazy” while Lionsgate and Roadshow Attractions bought “Margin Call” for a price estimated in the low seven figures.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/the-market-begins-to-move-at-sundance/', 'snippet': 'Paramount picked up the giggly romantic drama “Like Crazy” while Lionsgate and Roadshow Attractions bought “Margin Call” for a price estimated in the low seven figures.', 'lead_paragraph': '10:29 p.m. | Updated PARK CITY, Utah— And on the fourth day, the deals started to pick up steam. As Sundance awoke to a particularly frosty morning on Sunday — 9 degrees by our thermometer’s estimation — word broke that Paramount and Lionsgate had both snapped up movies overnight. Paramount paid about $4 million for “Like Crazy,” a giggly romantic drama starring Anton Yelchin and Felicity Jones. Reigning indie queen Jennifer Lawrence (“Winter’s Bone”) has a supporting role in the film, which directed by Drake Doremus. About 50 percent of the movie’s dialogue is improvised.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Market Begins to Move at Sundance', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spacey, Kevin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yelchin, Anton', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T18:33:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9261af5d-d44f-5902-a97b-a4274bdf81c8', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9261af5d-d44f-5902-a97b-a4274bdf81c8'}, {'abstract': 'For fall 2011, Dior Homme is soft, draped, plain, and the boys are wearing flat, wide-brimmed hats.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/amish-fashion-week/', 'snippet': 'For fall 2011, Dior Homme is soft, draped, plain, and the boys are wearing flat, wide-brimmed hats.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Five years ago Dior Homme was hyper-cool boys in the latest wispy jeans and haircuts. For fall 2011, the clothes are soft, draped, plain, and the boys are wearing flat, wide-brimmed hats. I had a sudden flashback to Alexander Godunov as an Amish dreamboat farmer in “Witness,” though some of the loose monochromatic suits and unadorned double-faced coats also made me think, on second thought, of vintage Yohji Yamamoto and more recent Phoebe Philo of Céline —her influence anyway.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Amish Fashion Week', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Christian Dior SA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T18:43:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/055dbc96-0bef-5e0a-8cf6-8dd30871c26e', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/055dbc96-0bef-5e0a-8cf6-8dd30871c26e'}, {'abstract': 'Yael Averbuch checks in from China, where she is playing with the U.S. women’s national team in the Four Nations Tournament.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/in-china-plugged-in-but-unplugged/', 'snippet': 'Yael Averbuch checks in from China, where she is playing with the U.S. women’s national team in the Four Nations Tournament.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Midfielder Yael Averbuch, a Montclair, N.J., native by way of the University of North Carolina, will be contributing to the Goal blog in the runup to the third season of Women’s Professional Soccer and as the United States women’s national team prepares to the FIFA Women’s World Cup in Germany later this year. Now, she checks in from China, where the U.S. is getting ready to face the hosts in the Four Nations Tournament.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In China, Plugged In, but Unplugged', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coaches and Managers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sundhage, Pia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook.com', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'YouTube.com', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T18:44:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Yael Averbuch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Yael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Averbuch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd673f29-f323-5b25-b2de-600e67ea0df2', 'word_count': 575, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd673f29-f323-5b25-b2de-600e67ea0df2'}, {'abstract': 'Caroline Wozniacki wins in such a workmanlike, undramatic way that many tennis observers overlook what she does do well.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/analysis-how-wozniacki-keeps-winning/', 'snippet': 'Caroline Wozniacki wins in such a workmanlike, undramatic way that many tennis observers overlook what she does do well.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Caroline Wozniacki wins in such a workmanlike, undramatic way that many tennis observers overlook what she does do well. She wears down her opponents with consistency, calm, and a complete understanding of what she can and can’t do on a tennis court. If playing a tennis match is akin to managing money, Wozniacki would earn you a solid return, year in and year out, but she would never stand out.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Analysis: How Wozniacki Keeps Winning', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Australian Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T18:47:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Macdonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macdonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e209b16-6066-5f6a-8c92-c4a2d5fb382a', 'word_count': 618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e209b16-6066-5f6a-8c92-c4a2d5fb382a'}, {'abstract': 'Mitch McConnell, the Senate’s top Republican, said that his party will vigorously oppose the spending initiatives President Obama plans to include in his State of the Union address.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/us/politics/24repubs.html', 'snippet': 'Mitch McConnell, the Senate’s top Republican, said that his party will vigorously oppose the spending initiatives President Obama plans to include in his State of the Union address.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Senate’s top Republican, Mitch McConnell of Kentucky, said on Sunday that his party will vigorously oppose the spending initiatives President Obama plans to include in his State of the Union address on Tuesday because “it’s not a time to be looking at pumping up government spending.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Senate Republicans to Oppose Obama’s Spending Plans', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Senate Republicans to Oppose Obama's Spending Plans", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McConnell, Mitch', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T18:47:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Berger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42808871-7236-58b5-aa46-19e879cb56b5', 'word_count': 992, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42808871-7236-58b5-aa46-19e879cb56b5'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Tilson Thomas and Frank Gehry joined visions to create a new home for the New World Symphony in Miami Beach.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/arts/design/24gehry.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Tilson Thomas and Frank Gehry joined visions to create a new home for the New World Symphony in Miami Beach.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MIAMI BEACH \x97 Can an architect save classical music? That seems to be what Michael Tilson Thomas, the artistic director of the New World Symphony, was counting on when he hired Frank Gehry to design him a new music center.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/GEHRY-2/GEHRY-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': '