culate that Penn’s Jerome Allen could be, as he eventually was.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Coach Leaves the Bench, but Not the Team', 'kicker': 'The Quad', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T19:52:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Styczynski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Styczynski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/68a047e6-b270-53d2-a271-044d97810fb1', 'word_count': 519, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68a047e6-b270-53d2-a271-044d97810fb1'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Bock, who died in November, is best known for “Fiddler on the Roof,” which won nine Tony Awards and became a theater staple throughout the world.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/23/to-life-song-studded-remembrance-for-the-fiddler-composer-jerry-bock/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Bock, who died in November, is best known for “Fiddler on the Roof,” which won nine Tony Awards and became a theater staple throughout the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Friends and colleagues will gather on Monday to celebrate the life and work of Jerry Bock, who wrote the music to some of the most memorable songs in musical-theater history. The public is invited, too.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Life! Song-Studded Remembrance for the ‘Fiddler’ Composer Jerry Bock', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T20:15:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New