y fish.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you have one of those precocious, somewhat creepy preschoolers who read at a 10th-grade level and build life-size, working reproductions of the space shuttle out of Legos, expect the child to have a few questions if plunked down in front of “Bubble Guppies.” This new animated show from Nickelodeon, which stars six perky fish and has its premiere on Monday morning, may not mesh well with the kid’s overly logical brain.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/GUPPIES/GUPPIES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/GUPPIES/GUPPIES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/GUPPIES/GUPPIES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 548, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/GUPPIES/GUPPIES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/GUPPIES/GUPPIES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Splish Splash: Fantastical Fish Tales', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Splish Splash: Fantastical Fish Tales', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nickelodeon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T23:02:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb6b97b3-8632-5f4e-826e-dc41d4564a11', 'word_count': 453, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb6b97b3-8632-5f4e-826e-dc41d4564a11'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Twain impersonators are doing very well, thanks to “The Autobiography of Mark Twain,” published in 2010.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/books/24twain.html', 'snippet': 'Mark Twain impersonators are doing very well, thanks to “The Autobiography of Mark Twain,” published in 2010.', 'lead_paragraph': 'INCLINE VILLAGE, Nev. \x97 McAvoy Layne climbed the stage chomping on an unlighted cigar, his shock of spray-painted white hair bright in the spotlights. “You can’t beat an audience that’s been waiting 100 years,” he said to the crowd.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/TWAIN/TWAIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 980, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/TWAIN/TWAIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/TWAIN/TWAIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mark Twain, Now a Career for the Mustachioed', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mark Twain, Now a Career for the Mustachioed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clemens, Samuel Langhorne', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Impersonations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T23:08:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malia Wollan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wollan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6724d8b5-9689-5814-b2c1-2bfbc93e67a5', 'word_count': 1024, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6724d8b5-9689-5814-b2c1-2bfbc93e67a5'}, {'abstract': 'In “Endgame,” a longtime associate of Bobby Fischer recounts the chess superstar’s rise and fall from grace.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/books/24book.html', 'snippet': 'In “Endgame,” a longtime associate of Bobby Fischer recounts the chess superstar’s rise and fall from grace.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s no exaggeration to call Bobby Fischer both one of the most admired and one of the most reviled figures in American history. The admiration is prompted by his precocious rise to the pinnacle of the chess-playing world and his galvanizing 1972 cold-war-era triumph over Boris Spassky, the Soviet champion. The vilification stems from the monstrousness he exhibited in later years. On Sept. 11, 2001, he told a radio interviewer, “Yes, well, this is all wonderful news,” and, “It’s time to finish off the U.S. once and for all.” Thanks to the Internet, those comments will live forever.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/JPBOOK2/JPBOOK2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 989, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/JPBOOK2/JPBOOK2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/JPBOOK2/JPBOOK2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Odd, Odd Case of Bobby Fischer', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Odd, Odd Case Of Bobby Fischer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fischer, Bobby', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brady, Frank', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chess', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T23:14:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bb25df6-7899-5dc4-8c1b-16e7ddef46bf', 'word_count': 1037, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bb25df6-7899-5dc4-8c1b-16e7ddef46bf'}, {'abstract': 'Commissioners did not vote on the recommendation that a higher standard of fiduciary duty be applied.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/business/24sec.html', 'snippet': 'Commissioners did not vote on the recommendation that a higher standard of fiduciary duty be applied.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON \x97 Investment advisers and stockbrokers should be subject to the same fiduciary standard of conduct \x97 putting a customer’s interests above their own \x97 rather than the different governance regimes that currently apply to the two groups, the Securities and Exchange Commission recommended on Saturday.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'S.E.C. Study Recommends More Oversight of Brokers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'S.E.C. Study Recommends More Oversight of Brokers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Financial Brokers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T23:17:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wyatt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wyatt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/424f4d40-3acc-5650-a830-7809e2c546f6', 'word_count': 408, 'uri': 'nyt://article/424f4d40-3acc-5650-a830-7809e2c546f6'}, {'abstract': 'With government subsidies shrinking, European arts institutions are finding ways to bring in cash. And to some arts-world types, the strategies seem a little cheesy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/arts/24squeeze.html', 'snippet': 'With government subsidies shrinking, European arts institutions are finding ways to bring in cash. And to some arts-world types, the strategies seem a little cheesy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS \x97 On the Left Bank of the Seine, the facade of the glass-roofed Beaux-Arts building that houses the Musée d’Orsay is draped with a huge advertisement for Chanel No. 5. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/SQUEEZE/SQUEEZE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/SQUEEZE/SQUEEZE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/SQUEEZE/SQUEEZE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 582, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/SQUEEZE/SQUEEZE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/24/arts/SQUEEZE/SQUEEZE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘This Space for Rent’: In Europe, Arts Now Must Woo Commerce', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘This Space for Rent’: In Europe, Arts Now Must Woo Commerce', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Finances', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T23:21:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Doreen Carvajal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Doreen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carvajal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5af68e9a-cd75-5de7-b581-9f55831c0006', 'word_count': 911, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5af68e9a-cd75-5de7-b581-9f55831c0006'}, {'abstract': 'From the Lederer Memorial Trophy, an educated guess about the trump break brings a brilliancy prize.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/crosswords/bridge/24card.html', 'snippet': 'From the Lederer Memorial Trophy, an educated guess about the trump break brings a brilliancy prize.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Few top tournaments award brilliancy prizes these days. But the Lederer Memorial Trophy in London last October did. (The event is named for Richard and Tony Lederer, father and son. The Official Encyclopedia of Bridge says that Richard was “perhaps the first great figure of British bridge.”)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Well-Executed Finesse, but Was It Worthy of a Prize?', 'kicker': 'Bridge', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Well-Executed Finesse, But Was It Worthy of a Prize?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T23:28:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b34dae1d-85c1-54c5-8f0a-b44b714ce6d7', 'word_count': 358, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b34dae1d-85c1-54c5-8f0a-b44b714ce6d7'}, {'abstract': 'Natalie Portman helped power the romantic comedy “No Strings Attached” from Paramount Pictures to No. 1 at the North American box office over the weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/movies/24arts-MOREMOVIEGOL_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Natalie Portman helped power the romantic comedy “No Strings Attached” from Paramount Pictures to No. 1 at the North American box office over the weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you’re hot, you’re hot. Natalie Portman, steaming toward the Academy Awards for her disturbing portrayal of a deranged ballerina in “Black Swan,” helped power the romantic comedy “No Strings Attached” from Paramount Pictures to No. 1 at the North American box office over the weekend. The movie, which also stars Ashton Kutcher (above with Ms. Portman), sold an estimated $20.3 million in tickets, an unexpectedly firm performance. All of the others in the top five were holdovers. “The Green Hornet” (Sony Pictures Entertainment) was second with about $18 million for a two-week total of $63.4 million, according to Hollywood.com, which compiles box-office statistics. “The Dilemma,” a buddy comedy from Universal Pictures, sold about $9.7 million in its second weekend ($33.4 million total) for third place. “The King’s Speech” (the Weinstein Company) was a close fourth with roughly $9.2 million ($58.6 million total), while “True Grit” (Paramount) chugged away in fifth place with $8 million, lifting its five-week total to $138.7 million.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Movie Gold for Natalie Portman', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More Movie Gold for Natalie Portman', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Portman, Natalie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T23:38:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/08c5e9fc-58ad-520c-b24a-96a4d396a3c2', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08c5e9fc-58ad-520c-b24a-96a4d396a3c2'}, {'abstract': 'Best-selling authors crowded the list of finalists for the National Book Critics Circle awards.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/books/24arts-PRIZENOMINAT_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Best-selling authors crowded the list of finalists for the National Book Critics Circle awards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Best-selling authors crowded the list of finalists for the National Book Critics Circle awards, announced on Saturday at the Jerome L. Greene Performance Space in the South Village. The finalists for fiction included Jennifer Egan for “A Visit From the Goon Squad” and Jonathan Franzen for “Freedom,” books that were conspicuously overlooked in the National Book Awards last year. Other fiction finalists were David Grossman for “To the End of the Land,” Hans Keilson for “Comedy in a Minor Key” and Paul Murray for “Skippy Dies.” In the autobiography category, Patti Smith was a finalist for “Just Kids,” a memoir that won the National Book Award for nonfiction in 2010. Other categories were biography, criticism, nonfiction and poetry. The annual awards will be presented on March 10 at The New School in Manhattan.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Prize Nominations for Best-Selling Authors', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prize Nominations For Best-Selling Authors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Book Critics Circle', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-23T23:40:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2da97cb-cb39-51ae-8b74-d1d40983db16', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2da97cb-cb39-51ae-8b74-d1d40983db16'}, {'abstract': 'John Goddard, a Rhode Island furnituremaker who died bankrupt in 1785 after British troops had driven off his clientele, brightened the weekend’s Americana sales.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/arts/design/24arts-GODDARDFURNI_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'John Goddard, a Rhode Island furnituremaker who died bankrupt in 1785 after British troops had driven off his clientele, brightened the weekend’s Americana sales.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Goddard, a Rhode Island furnituremaker who died bankrupt in 1785 after British troops had driven off his clientele, brightened the weekend’s Americana sales. Despite a longtime decline in demand for what the trade calls “brown furniture,” lots attributed to Goddard carried six- and seven-figure prices. At Sotheby’s, the dealer Leigh Keno, on behalf of an unnamed collector, paid $230,500 for two of Goddard’s mahogany side chairs with flared legs, petal carvings and spiny claw feet, above. Christie’s took in $5.682 million for a kneehole mahogany desk that Goddard apparently made as a wedding gift for his daughter Catherine, one of his 16 children. According to Maine Antique Digest, the buyer was the Pennsylvania dealer C. L. Prickett, on behalf of an unnamed collector. The price is the fourth highest for American furniture at auction, the estimate was just $700,000 to $900,000, and the desk brought $940,000 at Sotheby’s in 2004.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Goddard Furniture Lifts Americana Sales', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Goddard Furniture Lifts Americana Sales', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Furniture', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'},