/01/24/business/media/24indie.html', 'snippet': 'Take to the Internet? Sell coffee and muffins? Independent bookstores are looking for the right balance as even a giant like Borders finds it difficult.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ARLINGTON, Va. \x97 Get on Twitter. Add a wine bar. Do as one bookstore owner did and rent out space for birthday parties.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/business/24Indie/24Indie-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 812, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/business/24Indie/24Indie-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/24/business/24Indie/24Indie-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Small Bookstores Struggle for Niche in Shifting Times', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Small Bookstores Struggle for Niche in Shifting Times', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Borders Group Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T02:19:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbb7bfb4-851c-539f-8e0c-e4a92917624f', 'word_count': 1047, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbb7bfb4-851c-539f-8e0c-e4a92917624f'}, {'abstract': 'Two years ago, Mark Steinhubl began the recovery process that lies ahead for Gabrielle Giffords, at the rehabilitation center she will enter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/us/24rehab.html', 'snippet': 'Two years ago, Mark Steinhubl began the recovery process that lies ahead for Gabrielle Giffords, at the rehabilitation center she will enter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COLLEGE STATION, Tex. — Mark Steinhubl does not remember being shot. The bullet tore into his skull just above the right eye and cut through the right side of his brain. The slug obliterated some memories.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/us/ALT-REHAB/ALT-REHAB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/24/us/ALT-REHAB/ALT-REHAB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/us/ALT-REHAB/ALT-REHAB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Shot in the Head, but Getting Back on His Feet and on With His Life', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shot in the Head, But Getting Back on His Feet and on With His Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tucson (Ariz)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Therapy and Rehabilitation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tucson Shooting (2011)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Giffords, Gabrielle', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T02:20:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7074c15f-6872-51d6-aa25-8daabb84f988', 'word_count': 1542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7074c15f-6872-51d6-aa25-8daabb84f988'}, {'abstract': 'Cases in which the Supreme Court has “original jurisdiction” show how the court’s understanding of the Constitution is changing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/opinion/24mon2.html', 'snippet': 'Cases in which the Supreme Court has “original jurisdiction” show how the court’s understanding of the Constitution is changing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During oral argument about Montana v. Wyoming this month, the Supreme Court was doing something rare: it was in session as a trial court and its task was to decide how to divvy up water from tributaries to the Yellowstone River. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Evolving Court', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Evolving Court', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitution (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Montana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wyoming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T02:20:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/34dd7b1d-6d60-5b85-9a58-86813b271a5e', 'word_count': 456, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34dd7b1d-6d60-5b85-9a58-86813b271a5e'}, {'abstract': 'The country’s second-largest teachers’ union has wisely chosen to help shape reform rather than block it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/opinion/24mon3.html', 'snippet': 'The country’s second-largest teachers’ union has wisely chosen to help shape reform rather than block it.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Education officials across the country are increasingly focused on the two critical reform tasks: developing more effective teacher evaluation systems and speeding up the glacial pace of disciplinary hearings for teachers charged with misconduct. The American Federation of Teachers, the country’s second-largest teachers’ union, has wisely chosen to work with state legislatures and local school districts to help shape these new systems rather than try to block them. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reform and the Teachers’ Unions', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reform and the Teachers’ Unions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Education Assn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weingarten, Randi', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN FEDERATION OF TEACHERS', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T02:21:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b9f2aff-4734-53ad-9ed2-b929fa0931d7', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b9f2aff-4734-53ad-9ed2-b929fa0931d7'}, {'abstract': 'Many Scots are upset with a bill that could institute permanent daylight saving time throughout Britain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/opinion/24mon4.html', 'snippet': 'Many Scots are upset with a bill that could institute permanent daylight saving time throughout Britain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Time zones are laid out longitudinally and, with a few exceptions, the clock changes by an hour for every 15 degrees you move east or west. But the length of the day \x97 the time the sun spends above the horizon \x97 varies by latitude and the farther north you go in winter, the less daylight there is. That explains why many Scots are upset with a bill making its way through Parliament that could institute permanent daylight saving time throughout all of Britain.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Time Banditry', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Time Banditry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scotland', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Daylight Saving Time', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T02:22:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a0f19ab-25f7-5de0-a38a-8b045ecc48e7', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a0f19ab-25f7-5de0-a38a-8b045ecc48e7'}, {'abstract': 'A woman charged with vending without a license in New York could not wait to return to her life of crime.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/nyregion/24diary.html', 'snippet': 'A woman charged with vending without a license in New York could not wait to return to her life of crime.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DEAR DIARY:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Metropolitan Diary', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'METROPOLITAN DIARY', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vendors, Street', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T02:24:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e34e1b85-39bb-554c-abe3-e30f91b31501', 'word_count': 642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e34e1b85-39bb-554c-abe3-e30f91b31501'}, {'abstract': 'First-person accounts from three Americans recruited by the Georgian government to teach English.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/world/europe/24teachers.html', 'snippet': 'First-person accounts from three Americans recruited by the Georgian government to teach English.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The former Soviet republic of Georgia is trying to improve the quality of the English instruction in its classrooms with a program that has hired 1,000 people from the United States, Canada and other English-speaking countries to teach in its schools. Here are three teachers’ observations so far.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'American Voices, Far From Home', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'American Voices, Far From Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia (Georgian Republic)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 