eef946-a78c-57c1-9484-4758310cfd61'}, {'abstract': 'The health of the American arts sector reached a 12-year low in 2009, according to a report from Americans for the Arts, a national advocacy group.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/arts/24arts-NATIONALARTS_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The health of the American arts sector reached a 12-year low in 2009, according to a report from Americans for the Arts, a national advocacy group.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The health of the American arts sector reached a 12-year low in 2009, according to a report from Americans for the Arts, a national advocacy group. The 2010 National Arts Index, which strives for a comprehensive picture of how the arts fared during the recession, shows that the vitality of the arts sector dropped a record 3.6 points, to 97.7. The losses from 2007 to 2009 were nearly double the gains made from 2003 to 2007, according to the report. At the same time, the number of nonprofit arts institutions grew by 3,000 during that period. As a result, the United States arts sector now includes 109,000 nonprofit organizations, 550,000 for-profit businesses and 2.2 million artists. But more organizations and individuals have struggled to make ends meet; in 2009, 41 percent of nonprofit arts organizations reporting to the I.R.S. failed to achieve a balanced budget, up from 36 percent in 2008.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'National Arts Index Shows Struggles', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'National Arts Index Shows Struggles', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICANS FOR THE ARTS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T14:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/605f7dd3-3868-5329-b955-30b45b75dd0c', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/605f7dd3-3868-5329-b955-30b45b75dd0c'}, {'abstract': 'Wynton Marsalis and Eric Clapton are expected to team up for three Jazz at Lincoln Center concerts in April, according to a publicist for the event.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/arts/music/24arts-MARSALISANDC_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Wynton Marsalis and Eric Clapton are expected to team up for three Jazz at Lincoln Center concerts in April, according to a publicist for the event.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wynton Marsalis and Eric Clapton are expected to team up for three Jazz at Lincoln Center concerts in April, according to a publicist for the event. Titled “Wynton Marsalis and Eric Clapton Play the Blues,” with a seven-piece band drawn largely from the ranks of the Jazz at Lincoln Center Orchestra, the collaboration is to start with the organization’s annual fund-raising gala on April 7, followed by regular ticketed concerts at Rose Hall on April 8 and 9. The two musicians performed together during a Jazz at Lincoln Center gala in 2003, and Mr. Marsalis appeared on Mr. Clapton’s recent album, “Clapton,” but the April performances are to be their first full concerts together. Tickets are at jalc.org.   ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Together Onstage: Marsalis and Clapton', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Together Onstage: Marsalis and Clapton', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marsalis, Wynton', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jazz at Lincoln Center', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clapton, Eric', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T14:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/bbf955d3-0db6-5fae-a30f-8980e3fc19a1', 'word_count': 119, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bbf955d3-0db6-5fae-a30f-8980e3fc19a1'}, {'abstract': 'At this year’s International Möbel Messe, the annual furniture fair in Cologne, Germany, young designers demonstrated new and ingenious ways to work with that old standby, wood.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/design-fairs-a-woodsy-cologne/', 'snippet': 'At this year’s International Möbel Messe, the annual furniture fair in Cologne, Germany, young designers demonstrated new and ingenious ways to work with that old standby, wood.', 'lead_paragraph': ' NYTD.fullScreenSlideShowFactory.createSlideshow("#777", 592, 400, true, chameleonData); ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Design Fairs | A Woodsy Cologne', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T14:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felix Burrichter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burrichter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ef48805-bec4-5ece-844c-663310576148',