': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d181a8b-6ed6-518b-9e54-67b9c3fd739e', 'word_count': 436, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d181a8b-6ed6-518b-9e54-67b9c3fd739e'}, {'abstract': 'Car-charging stations in Tennessee will rely on a mix of solar power and batteries, and some of the energy will flow to and from the grid.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/if-you-build-it-will-they-charge/', 'snippet': 'Car-charging stations in Tennessee will rely on a mix of solar power and batteries, and some of the energy will flow to and from the grid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A transition to electric cars isn’t just a matter of the cars, but also of the infrastructure that goes with them, including public charging stations. The Electric Power Research Institute and the Tennessee Valley Authority plan to cut the ribbon on Tuesday on a prototype of a new kind of charging station, one that uses solar cells and batteries. But they do not work together in quite the way the public might expect.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'If You Build It, Will They Charge?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Batteries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Vehicles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Solar Energy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tennessee Valley Authority', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chattanooga (Tenn)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Knoxville (Tenn)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nashville (Tenn)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tennessee', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T17:03:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/c869534c-b3ab-5d07-907f-efd04fbb39c9', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c869534c-b3ab-5d07-907f-efd04fbb39c9'}, {'abstract': 'MagicJack, a $40 device which lets you makes calls over the Internet using a regular phone, is offering a free service.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/magicjack-now-offers-free-calls/', 'snippet': 'MagicJack, a $40 device which lets you makes calls over the Internet using a regular phone, is offering a free service.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Insomniacs are no doubt familiar with the infomercials for magicJack, a device that lets you plug your home phone into a computer to make calls over the Internet using VoIP. Marketed as “the most exciting deal we’ve ever had” on the “Saving Shopping Network,” the magicJack is $40 for the device and the first year’s service with a $20 annual fee for each year after.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'MagicJack Now Offers Free Calls', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Internet Telephony', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T17:07:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'first