strode into the international arrivals hall at Moscow’s busiest airport and set off an enormous explosion, killing more than 30 people, Russian officials said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/01/24/world/europe/20110125-MOSCOW.html', 'snippet': 'A suicide bomber strode into the international arrivals hall at Moscow’s busiest airport and set off an enormous explosion, killing more than 30 people, Russian officials said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A suicide bomber strode into the international arrivals hall at Moscow’s busiest airport and set off an enormous explosion, killing more than 30 people, Russian officials said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/world/europe/20110125-MOSCOW-slide-PEKV/20110125-MOSCOW-slide-PEKV-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 714, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/24/world/europe/20110125-MOSCOW-slide-PEKV/20110125-MOSCOW-slide-PEKV-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/24/world/europe/20110125-MOSCOW-slide-PEKV/20110125-MOSCOW-slide-PEKV-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Suicide Bombing at Moscow Airport', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moscow (Russia)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRPORTS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Domodedovo Airport', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T21:30:22+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'World', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/3d884592-b48f-59b5-9fb8-c31435535e3c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/3d884592-b48f-59b5-9fb8-c31435535e3c'}, {'abstract': 'New York releases statistics on its new inspection system for the first time.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/city-says-57-of-graded-restaurants-have-gotten-as/', 'snippet': 'New York releases statistics on its new inspection system for the first time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the matter of its new letter-grade system that rates the cleanliness of restaurants, the New York City Department of Health and Mental Hygiene has given itself an A.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'City Says 57 Percent of Graded Restaurants Have Gotten A’s', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Health and Mental Hygiene Department (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T21:33:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/114d389b-d996-5f5f-a7bb-dd66cbb07215', 'word_count': 438, 'uri': 'nyt://article/114d389b-d996-5f5f-a7bb-dd66cbb07215'}, {'abstract': 'In Navajo culture, talking about death is thought to bring it about, so it is not discussed. Now health workers are trying to find a comfortable way to begin the conversation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/health/25navajo.html', 'snippet': 'In Navajo culture, talking about death is thought to bring it about, so it is not discussed. Now health workers are trying to find a comfortable way to begin the conversation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FORT DEFIANCE, Ariz. \x97 Mitzie Begay, an elegant 76-year-old Navajo, can interpret the nuances of her language and traditions with contemporary verve and understated wit \x97 qualities that make her a good fit for a job that could hardly have been imagined in the Navajo Nation a generation ago. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/25/science/25navajospan/25navajospan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/25/science/25navajospan/25navajospan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/25/science/25navajospan/25navajo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/25/science/25navajospan/25navajospan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/25/science/25navajospan/25navajospan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With Poem, Broaching the Topic of Death', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Navajos Confront Life’s End With Poem', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospice Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Navajo Indians', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Living Wills', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T21:34:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Daitz, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Daitz', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/44c47024-8305-5485-80ea-d1d8b9084414', 'word_count': 1030, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44c47024-8305-5485-80ea-d1d8b9084414'}, {'abstract': 'This week, A. O. Scott looks back at “Billy Liar,” John Schlesinger’s 1963 drama about a young daydreamer.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/critics-picks-video-billy-liar/', 'snippet': 'This week, A. O. Scott looks back at “Billy Liar,” John Schlesinger’s 1963 drama about a young daydreamer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, A. O. Scott looks back at “Billy Liar,” John Schlesinger’s 1963 drama about a young daydreamer (Tom Courtenay), who escapes from the boredom of his life into a fantasy world. Mr. Scott appreciates the film for its pathos and comedy, which stems, he says, from “the tension between two different kinds of imagination. One kind involves making up things that aren’t true. The other kind, which is no less creative, involves recognizing the world for what it is.” Have you seen “Billy Liar?” Please share your thoughts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Critics’ Picks Video: ‘Billy Liar’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T21:40:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/67b20bd7-66d1-5675-9706-3547ac392f36', 'word_count': 89, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67b20bd7-66d1-5675-9706-3547ac392f36'}, {'abstract': 'The busy model and recent mom on how she is gearing up for New York Fashion Week.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/keeping-up-with-arizona-muse/', 'snippet': 'The busy model and recent mom on how she is gearing up for New York Fashion Week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last season the model Arizona Muse clocked 26 runway shows — and could have done more had she not had an exclusive with Prada in Milan. This season, you can bet the 22-year-old Muse, a recent mom, will be practically ubiquitous. Four cities (New York, London, Milan and Paris) and about 100 shows per — well, you do the math. “Fashion Week is the busiest time of each season,” she told us as she geared up for New York. “Whether or not I actually get any free time beforehand remains to be seen, ha!” Right now, she fills her “idle” hours with running and Pilates. If she’s lucky, she will be able to take a short break between cities. “I plan to spend some time in London,” she says. (She is from New Mexico, but her mom is English.) “I have dear dear family and friends there.” More than anything, she tells us that she’s ready for the shows to get going. “I like how busy it is, running around from one place to another. It’s quite exhilarating,” she says. “I know that it will end. And when it does I think I’ll go home to Santa Fe, where I grew up, and eat lots of green chili.” Hot stuff.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Keeping Up With Arizona Muse', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T21:42:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f29ad2b5-647f-5798-b310-788449e2ec64', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f29ad2b5-647f-5798-b310-788449e2ec64'}, {'abstract': 'Yes, it is miserably cold, but there are some upsides.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/outside-seeing-a-glass-as-half-full-yes-its-still-ice/', 'snippet': 'Yes, it is miserably cold, but there are some upsides.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A curious phenomenon swept the frigid streets of New York City today, where temperatures plummeted to 8 degrees: people looked where they were going. The lure of warm destinations, the primal urge to rush indoors and a deep reluctance to expose tender fingers to the brutal cold meant that iPhones, Blackberrys and other P.D.A.’s were tucked away. Strolling the streets, seeing so many people untethered to various electronic distractions or jabbering away in halfalogues, one could be forgiven for thinking it was 2002 all over again.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Outside, Seeing a Glass as Half Full (Yes, It’s Still Ice)', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Clubs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Sports Club', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Montreal (Quebec)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T21:48:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cara Buckley', 'person': [{