s-come-fast-and-furious/', 'snippet': 'A flurry of Sundance deals closed on Monday, with a psychological thriller, a sweet teen drama and a documentary about The New York Times among the films being snapped up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARK CITY, Utah— And the money keeps rolling in. A flurry of Sundance deals closed on Monday, with Fox Searchlight, Magnolia Pictures, Participant Media, Sundance Selects/IFC Films, and National Geographic all snapping up movies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sundance Deals Come Fast and Furious', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Olsen, Elizabeth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T22:37:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/caa64391-bf1d-51b3-b5ed-09bbdf2de949', 'word_count': 287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/caa64391-bf1d-51b3-b5ed-09bbdf2de949'}, {'abstract': 'Corey Dargel, who pushes at the margins of modern classical music and adventurous pop, put together a program called “Falling Apart and Coming Together” at the Brooklyn Museum.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/arts/music/25dargel.html', 'snippet': 'Corey Dargel, who pushes at the margins of modern classical music and adventurous pop, put together a program called “Falling Apart and Coming Together” at the Brooklyn Museum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nearly two years have passed since the Brooklyn Philharmonic fielded a full complement of players in a concert of its own. That this institution, long a feisty steward for offbeat repertory and unorthodox programming, had disappeared almost completely made its continued community-outreach programs seem poignant and slightly quixotic. Until last week, that is, when the orchestra announced the appointment of a new artistic director: Alan Pierson, leader of the vital chamber ensemble Alarm Will Sound.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/25/arts/DARGEL/DARGEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 862, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fragments of Egyptian Statuary Set Stage for Songs of Fragmentation', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fragments of Egyptian Statuary Set Stage for Songs of Fragmentation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Museum', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dargel, Corey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Philharmonic', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T22:37:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/61ab4db4-4729-5f6c-bb8c-aae727e59f01', 'word_count': 470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61ab4db4-4729-5f6c-bb8c-aae727e59f01'}, {'abstract': 'An unnatural abstinence threatens to sap the grape’s genetic health and the future pleasure of millions of oenophiles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/science/25wine.html', 'snippet': 'An unnatural abstinence threatens to sap the grape’s genetic health and the future pleasure of millions of oenophiles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the last 8,000 years, the wine grape has had very little sex. This unnatural abstinence threatens to sap the grape’s genetic health and the future pleasure of millions of oenophiles.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lack of Sex Among Grapes Tangles a Family Vine', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lack of Sex Among Grapes Tangles a Family Vine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE DEPARTMENT', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T22:48:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Wade', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wade', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02f401c9-abf0-5b1e-99b8-b6ae0da057bd', 'word_count': 1139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02f401c9-abf0-5b1e-99b8-b6ae0da057bd'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs from Russia, Hong Kong, and New York.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/pictures-of-the-day-moscow-and-elsewhere/', 'snippet': 'Photographs from Russia, Hong Kong, and New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photographs from Russia, Hong Kong, and New York.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pictures of the Day: Moscow and Elsewhere', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T22:51:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michele McNally, Lisa Iaboni and Karly Domb Sadof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michele', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McNally', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Iaboni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Karly', 'middlename': 'Domb', 'lastname': 'Sadof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/88301b5b-271c-561b-8d29-ea74dc782404', 'word_count': 8, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88301b5b-271c-561b-8d29-ea74dc782404'}, {'abstract': 'Reports of Verizon Wireless network failures flooded Twitter and Facebook as well as Verizon’s own support boards.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/network-problems-hit-verizon-wireless-customers/', 'snippet': 'Reports of Verizon Wireless network failures flooded Twitter and Facebook as well as Verizon’s own support boards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many Verizon Wireless customers turned to social networks and message boards on Monday to complain that they were unable to make calls or use data applications on their phones and other devices. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Network Trouble for Verizon Wireless in Virginia', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Verizon Communications Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T22:53:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/49e89487-073e-5cdb-8ac4-90fd56c09a1c', 'word_count': 242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49e89487-073e-5cdb-8ac4-90fd56c09a1c'}, {'abstract': 'A group of Republican lawmakers has called for the elimination of the National Endowment for the Arts and the National Endowment for the Humanities, each of which received $167.5 million in the last federal budget.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/arts/design/25arts-REPUBLICANST_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'A group of Republican lawmakers has called for the elimination of the National Endowment for the Arts and the National Endowment for the Humanities, each of which received $167.5 million in the last federal budget.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A group of Republican lawmakers has called for the elimination of the National Endowment for the Arts and the National Endowment for the Humanities, each of which received $167.5 million in the last federal budget. The group, called the Republican Study Committee and comprising about 165 House Republicans, also called for eliminating the Corporation for Public Broadcasting, which got $430 million. The proposal, made Thursday, is part of the Spending Reduction Act of 2011, which aims to reduce federal spending by $2.5 trillion over 10 years. Senator Jim DeMint, above, a South Carolina Republican who is chairman of the Senate Steering Committee, said in a statement that the bill “begins the difficult task of shrinking the federal bureaucracy that threatens our future prosperity.” Robert L. Lynch, president of Americans for the Arts, a lobbying group, said in an interview on Monday that the arts provide 5.7 million jobs in the United States that generate about $30 billion in taxes, nearly $13 billion of which goes to the federal government. “If they’re serious about jobs and they’re serious about income, they would invest more in the arts,” Mr. Lynch said.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Republicans Try to Abolish Arts Groups', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Republicans Try to Abolish Arts Groups', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T22:54:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/64405091-f66a-568f-b2bf-7162ab55d254', 'word_count': 193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64405091-f66a-568f-b2bf-7162ab55d254'}, {'abstract': 'Alfred E. Mann, the brother of Robert Mann — a founding member of the Juilliard String Quartet — has donated $6 million to endow chairs in his brother’s name.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/arts/music/25arts-FOOTNOTE_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'Alfred E. Mann, the brother of Robert Mann — a founding member of the Juilliard String Quartet — has donated $6 million to endow chairs in his brother’s name.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alfred E. Mann, the brother of Robert Mann \x97 a founding member of the Juilliard String Quartet \x97 has donated $6 million to endow chairs in his brother’s name. The money will establish chairs at the Manhattan School of Music, where Robert Mann, 90, has taught since 2006, and at the Thornton School of Music at the University of Southern California.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Footnote', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Footnote', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T22:58:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d632250c-7075-5fbd-af61-75d24decd8ec', 'word_count': 63, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d632250c-7075-5fbd-af61-75d24decd8ec'}, {'abstract': 'CBS dominated the ratings on Sunday as the American Football Conference championship game delivered its highest ratings in 24 years', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/arts/television/25arts-CBSWINS_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'CBS dominated the ratings on Sunday as the American Football Conference championship game delivered its highest ratings in 24 years', 'lead_paragraph': 'CBS dominated the ratings on Sunday as the American Football Conference championship game delivered its highest ratings in 24 years. Although Nielsen’s final audience figures were not available on Monday, CBS said the preliminary overnight estimates for the Pittsburgh Steelers’ victory over the New York Jets were roughly 10 percent higher than those for last year’s A.F.C. title game. That broadcast, in the late afternoon, attracted 46.9 million viewers when final ratings were released. CBS capitalized on its high ratings on Sunday with a special 10 p.m. broadcast of its new series “Hawaii Five-0,” which drew an impressive 19.2 million viewers, the crime show’s highest ratings of the season. Fox ranked second on the night with mostly new episodes of its comedy lineup, followed by ABC and NBC.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CBS Wins', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'CBS Wins', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T23:02:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d73156db-4391-5197-aa73-88ae066410ac', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d73156db-4391-5197-aa73-88ae066410ac'}, {'abstract': 'The number of low-power FM radio stations will soon increase, leading to more local voices and topics on the air.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/arts/25radio.html', 'snippet': 'The number of low-power FM radio stations will soon increase, leading to more local voices and topics on the air.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OPELOUSAS, La. \x97 When John Freeman turned on his car radio one recent day and tuned to KOCZ, the voice he heard was a 2-year-old girl’s.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/25/arts/RADIO/RADIO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 760, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/25/arts/RADIO/RADIO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/25/arts/RADIO/RADIO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Low-Power FM Radio to Gain Space on the Dial', 'kicker': 