', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oh fashion, you fickle beast. On the second day of Paris fashion week, the premier maître de ballet John Galliano outdid himself with more than the usual four grand acts of love, death, regret and absolution. Yet Scorecard knows that Riccardo Tisci then went and let the dogs out — and made streetwear look the height of chic.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scorecard | Givenchy Wins!', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T23:33:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison S. Cohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Cohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc1327c1-28d2-5e09-971c-252a8b580836', 'word_count': 673, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc1327c1-28d2-5e09-971c-252a8b580836'}, {'abstract': 'Dior featured pale tones and Giorgio Armani’s Privé startled for its futuristic shapes at the spring haute couture shows in Paris.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/and-now-couture/', 'snippet': 'Dior featured pale tones and Giorgio Armani’s Privé startled for its futuristic shapes at the spring haute couture shows in Paris.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not even 24 hours after the Paris men’s shows had ended they were a dim memory. The women had moved in for the spring haute couture shows, like a temperance league. The tent for Dior today was so big, set up behind the Musée Rodin, you could have run three men’s shows simultaneously. I saw writers who had sprawled easily on benches at the men’s shows now squeezed delicately into gold spindle chairs. The mood became tense, watchful. And you have the clients and the paparazzi, who must get the same pictures every year but don’t seem to care. It’s kind of like family. You show up out of a mixture of duty and curiosity — and fear, fear that someone will get a bigger bowl of porridge.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'And Now Couture', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T23:39:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/30c47d15-2dc7-514e-b402-6f80b47f824a', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30c47d15-2dc7-514e-b402-6f80b47f824a'}, {'abstract': 'Here’s why Canon says using lower-megapixel sensors in its top consumer camcorders will improve the video quality.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/in-camcorder-megapixels-can-less-be-more/', 'snippet': 'Here’s why Canon says using lower-megapixel sensors in its top consumer camcorders will improve the video quality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If there were an award for marketing bravery, I would give it to Canon.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Camcorder Megapixels, Can Less Be More?', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Camcorders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T23:40:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e53d3aa-3334-5630-9eef-1ca62cad4210', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e53d3aa-3334-5630-9eef-1ca62cad4210'}, {'abstract': 'The Oscar nominations are just hours away, so the Bagger took a tour of our fellow prognosticators’ predictions.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/a-bonanza-of-oscar-predictions/', 'snippet': 'The Oscar nominations are just hours away, so the Bagger took a tour of our fellow prognosticators’ predictions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARK CITY, Utah— O.K. folks, we know what you’re thinking: enough with the ambush marketing and It girl reports, let’s get back to the race. The Oscar nominations are just hours away, so the Bagger took a tour of our fellow prognosticators’ predictions (you can see ours here).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Bonanza of Oscar Predictions', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bardem, Javier', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kunis, Mila', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manville, Lesley', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moore, Julianne', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-24T23:48:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8659deee-611d-5fd3-9fd4-c8b342b3c49e', 'word_count': 374, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8659deee-611d-5fd3-9fd4-c8b342b3c49e'}, {'abstract': 'Readers voice their opi