ame': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T01:25:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a79ef9d9-6a66-537e-abf2-cfbb0e02607c', 'word_count': 965, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a79ef9d9-6a66-537e-abf2-cfbb0e02607c'}, {'abstract': 'Most of the costs were for overtime pay in two agencies and lost ridership revenue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/nyregion/25blizzard.html', 'snippet': 'Most of the costs were for overtime pay in two agencies and lost ridership revenue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The enormous blizzard of Dec. 26 embarrassed the mayor, stranded buses and cars, and left hundreds of subway riders stuck on frozen trains in the outer reaches of New York City. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'December Blizzard Will Cost Over $68 Million', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'December Blizzard Will Cost More Than $68 Million, and That’s Before a Final Reckoning', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Transit Authority', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Transportation Authority', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snow and Snowstorms', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T01:31:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc11e524-e8f5-5902-90bf-19f199a50d96', 'word_count': 375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc11e524-e8f5-5902-90bf-19f199a50d96'}, {'abstract': 'After a week of political turmoil, Ireland’s main political parties reached agreement on Monday on steps needed to stabilize the economy and move to a general election, probably on Feb. 25.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/world/europe/25webbrfs-Ireland.html', 'snippet': 'After a week of political turmoil, Ireland’s main political parties reached agreement on Monday on steps needed to stabilize the economy and move to a general election, probably on Feb. 25.', 'lead_paragraph': ' After a week of political turmoil, Ireland’s main political parties reached agreement on Monday on steps needed to stabilize the economy and move to a general election, probably on Feb. 25. Finance Minister Brian Lenihan said the parties had agreed to set this Saturday as a deadline for passing legislation required to implement a $25 billion package of austerity measures needed to secure a $114 billion international bailout for the country’s battered economy. Prime Minister Brian Cowen, whose efforts to cling to office had precipitated a crisis, did not attend the Dublin talks, and his Fianna Fail party said it would choose a new leader by Wednesday. Left undecided was who will serve as prime minister during the election period, a role Mr. Cowen has earmarked for himself. The agreement followed a statement by the European Commission saying it was “crucial” for Ireland’s stability and credibility that the austerity package be approved before the election. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ireland: Agreement Reached on Austerity Measures', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ireland: Agreement Reached on Austerity Measures', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ireland', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lenihan, Brian J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T01:32:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By John F. Burns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/290b6314-b0f8-5262-910d-5c9aab5ddc40', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/290b6314-b0f8-5262-910d-5c9aab5ddc40'}, {'abstract': 'Shares rise in after-hours trading on news of Steven Cohen’s passive stake.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/24/sac-takes-a-slice-of-dominos/', 'snippet': 'Shares rise in after-hours trading on news of Steven Cohen’s passive stake.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steven Cohen’s hedge fund, SAC Capital, disclosed on Monday that it had acquired a 5.3 percent stake in Domino’s Pizza. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'SAC Takes a Slice of Domino’s', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Domino's Pizza Inc", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T01:34:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d1a1819-c82e-57af-b50b-35f064efea80', 'word_count': 201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d1a1819-c82e-57af-b50b-35f064efea80'}, {'abstract': 'The head of the Italian bishops conference said that Italians felt a “moral unease” toward their public figures, an indirect reference to accusations that Prime Minister Silvio Berlusconi compensated “a significant number” of women for sex.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/world/europe/25webbrfs-Italy.html', 'snippet': 'The head of the Italian bishops conference said that Italians felt a “moral unease” toward their public figures, an indirect reference to accusations that Prime Minister Silvio Berlusconi compensated “a significant number” of women for sex.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The head of the Italian bishops conference said Monday that Italians felt a “moral unease” toward their public figures, an indirect reference to a sex scandal in which magistrates accuse Prime Minister Silvio Berlusconi of compensating “a significant number” of women for sex. “Whoever accepts a public position must understand the sobriety, personal discipline, sense of measure and honor that come with it,” Cardinal Angelo Bagnasco said. His remarks were a sign of the rising pressure on Mr. Berlusconi. The Vatican secretary of state said last week that the Vatican was watching the scandal “with concern,” and the head of the leading industrialists’ organization said Sunday that she would support Finance Minister Giulio Tremonti as a possible replacement for Mr. Berlusconi. Mr. Berlusconi denies all wrongdoing and has vowed to fight on. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Italy: Bishop Cites ‘Moral Unease’ Over Public Figures', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Italy: Bishop Cites ‘Moral Unease’ Over Public Figures', 'name': 