/', 'snippet': 'The British culture minister says he is inclined to refer the News Corporation’s bid to buy British Sky Broadcasting to the competition regulator, while adding that Rupert Murdoch’s media company may still have an opportunity to avert an extended ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jeremy Hunt, the British culture minister, said on Tuesday that he was inclined to refer the News Corporation‘s £7.8 billion offer to buy British Sky Broadcasting to the nation’s competition regulator for a full review, but added that Rupert Murdoch‘s media company might still have an opportunity to avert an extended inquiry.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Britain May Extend Scrutiny of News Corp. Bid for BSkyB', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Sky Broadcasting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T13:37:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris V. Nicholson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': 'V.', 'lastname': 'Nicholson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7523c847-e83c-5c45-aa98-913eca33d389', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7523c847-e83c-5c45-aa98-913eca33d389'}, {'abstract': 'The Illinois Supreme Court may now take up the matter of Rahm Emanuel’s residency, even as Chicago begins to print ballots for the mayoral election next month.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/emanuel-keeps-campaigning-as-he-fights-to-get-back-on-the-ballot/', 'snippet': 'The Illinois Supreme Court may now take up the matter of Rahm Emanuel’s residency, even as Chicago begins to print ballots for the mayoral election next month.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHICAGO – With Rahm Emanuel’s mayoral hopes resting in the hands of the Illinois Supreme Court, Chicago’s newspapers weighed in loudly – and grumpily — on Tuesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Emanuel Keeps Campaigning, as He Fights to Get Back on the Ballot', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T13:44:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Davey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/22fd392a-41cb-530a-9b3c-8d70304c4118', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22fd392a-41cb-530a-9b3c-8d70304c4118'}, {'abstract': 'Anxiety is mounting, however, about which countries — and whose companies — will benefit from the clean-energy boom, a potential topic of interest for business leaders at Davos. China has been pouring state resources into the sector.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/efforts-to-halt-climate-change-provoke-new-optimism/', 'snippet': 'Anxiety is mounting, however, about which countries — and whose companies — will benefit from the clean-energy boom, a potential topic of interest for business leaders at Davos. China has been pouring state resources into the sector.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For years, China was seen as a major obstacle to global efforts to combat climate change because of its refusal to reduce emissions under the Kyoto Protocol.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Efforts to Fight Climate Change Revive Optimism', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Renewable Energy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wind Power', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koch-Weser, Caio', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Economic Forum', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T13:50:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Kanter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kanter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/730210b6-b70b-5a99-be0a-d9bf51c0aa4c', 'word_count': 973, 'uri': 'nyt://article/730210b6-b70b-5a99-be0a-d9bf51c0aa4c'}, {'abstract': 'The 83rd annual Oscar nominations.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/the-83rd-annual-oscar-nominations/', 'snippet': 'The 83rd annual Oscar nominations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Below is the list of the 83nd Annual Oscar Nominations, which were announced Tuesday morning:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 83rd Annual Oscar Nominations', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T13:54:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a5fb816-3555-5baf-b320-dd62ca3fed5e', 'word_count': 709, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a5fb816-3555-5baf-b320-dd62ca3fed5e'}, {'abstract': '“Black Swan,” “The Fighter,” “Inception,” “The Kids Are All Right,” “The King’s Speech,” “127 Hours,” “The Social Network,” “Toy Story 3,” “True Grit” and “Winter’s Bone” were announced as the 10 nominees for best picture.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/academy-award-nominations-announced-2/', 'snippet': '“Black Swan,” “The Fighter,” “Inception,” “The Kids Are All Right,” “The King’s Speech,” “127 Hours,” “The Social Network,” “Toy Story 3,” “True Grit” and “Winter’s Bone” were announced as the 10 nominees for best picture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sorry, “Burlesque” fans: the 10 films that will be vying for best motion picture at the 83rd Academy Awards are “Black Swan,” “The Fighter,” “Inception,” “The Kids Are All Right,” “The King’s Speech,” “127 Hours,” “The Social Network,” “Toy Story 3,” “True Grit” and “Winter’s Bone,” the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences announced on Tuesday morning. The nominees for best director are Darren Aronofsky (for “Black Swan”), David O. Russell (“The Fighter”), Tom Hooper (“The King’s Speech”), David Fincher (“The Social Network”) and Joel and Ethan Coen (“True Grit”). “The King’s Speech” led the pack with 12 nominations total, including Colin Firth (nominated for best actor), Helena Bonham Carter (best supporting actress) and Geoffrey Rush (best supporting actor). ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Academy Award Nominations Announced', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ACADEMY OF MOTION PICTURE ARTS AND SCIENCES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T13:54:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea17817f-0c65-56f4-84e1-8a8a4af54e5d', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea17817f-0c65-56f4-84e1-8a8a4af54e5d'}, {'abstract': 'Getting a call correct should be a top priority in the replay challenge system, so why should a coach lose a challenge if he’s right?', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/should-teams-be-penalized-for-challenging-bad-calls/', 'snippet': 'Getting a call correct should be a top priority in the replay challenge system, so why should a coach lose a challenge if he’s right?', 'lead_paragraph': 'N.F.L. Rule, Section 9 Instant Replay, Coaches’ Challenge In each game, a team will be permitted two challenges that will initiate Referee Replay reviews. Each challenge will require the use of a team timeout. If a challenge is upheld, the timeout will be restored to the challenging team. A challenge will only be restored if a team is successful on both of its challenges, in which case it shall be awarded a third challenge, but a fourth challenge will not be permitted under any circumstances. No challenges will be recognized from a team that has exhausted its timeouts. A team that is out of timeouts or has used all of its available challenges may not attempt to initiate an additional challenge.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Should Teams Be ‘Penalized’ for Challenging Bad Calls?', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T14:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Bretherton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bretherton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5651116-f67f-5337-b6e7-754b623c7e03', 'word_count': 771, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5651116-f67f-5337-b6e7-754b623c7e03'}, {'abstract': 'The new horoscope is too confusing — trade your kitschy zodiac gems for Solange Azagury-Partridge’s Chinese New Year rings.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/animal-house-solange-azagury-partridge/', 'snippet': 'The new horoscope is too confusing — trade your kitschy zodiac gems for Solange Azagury-Partridge’s Chinese New Year rings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Solange Azagury-Partridge was born in the Chinese Year of the Ox, which by definition means she’s not only incredibly strong-willed but a firm believer in tradition. Her 12 hand-carved and painted lacquer critters set on 18-karat-gold bands and pendants share a similar dualism, serving double duty as conventional commemorative gifts for the coming Chinese New Year and as unconventional tokens of love for Valentine’s Day, which falls in the same month. “You can feel love emanating from these sweet creatures, and they are incredibly personalized,” Ms. Azagury-Partridge says of the designs, which start at $2,450 and include a plump purple poodle for those born in the Year of the Dog. “I definitely like unconventional gifts.” Admirers, take note. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Animal House | Solange Azagury-Partridge', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T14:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/be978d83-0ada-56ca-ab03-1e635e195506', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be978d83-0ada-56ca-ab03-1e635e195506'}, {'abstract': 'The Hot Wheels Video Racer is a tiny rolling camcorder, complete with a preview screen, that can replay a trip around the track from the viewpoint of an ant sitting on the hood.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/the-video-view-from-a-hot-wheels-car/', 'snippet': 'The Hot Wheels Video Racer is a tiny rolling camcorder, complete with a preview screen, that can replay a trip around the track from the viewpoint of an ant sitting on the hood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From our colleagues at Gadgetwise:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Video View From a Hot Wheels Car', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mattel Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T14:04:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Warren Buckleitner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Warren', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckleitner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ceb4361-4ebc-5b63-930a-b12c59b6625b', 'word_count': 230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ceb4361-4ebc-5b63-930a-b12c59b6625b'}, {'abstract': 'A “go shop