 viability.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/the-future-of-algae-fuels-is-when/', 'snippet': 'Advocates of algae-based fuels disagree with a new study on the technology’s near-term commercial viability.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I write in Tuesday’s Times, a new study from the Rand Corporation, the global policy think tank based in Santa Monica, Calif., and formed more than 60 years ago to advise the American government on military issues, suggests that Department of Defense is wasting its time exploring alternative fuels. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Future of Algae Fuels Is … When?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALGAE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Renewable Energy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biofuels', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rand Corp', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Navy', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T19:50:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Zeller Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zeller', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/13493416-6cb7-5143-8cb6-9fa6c6482e6a', 'word_count': 336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13493416-6cb7-5143-8cb6-9fa6c6482e6a'}, {'abstract': 'There is no silver lining to the Cavaliers’ descent to the N.B.A.’s worst team.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/cavaliers-lay-claim-to-leagues-worst/', 'snippet': 'There is no silver lining to the Cavaliers’ descent to the N.B.A.’s worst team.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Monday night, the Cleveland Cavaliers fell, 101-103, to the Nets, losing their 17th straight game. Losing is a way of life in Cleveland these days; the most surprising part of the Cavaliers’ most recent defeat wasn’t the result, but the fact that the game was close at all. Cleveland has become so spectacularly awful that even competitive games are a rarity. In fact, prior to last night’s game, the Cavaliers hadn’t lost a game by fewer than five points since the day after Christmas. This Cleveland team doesn’t just lose. They’ve raised incompetence to high art, and use each contest as a creative outlet through which to uniquely express their profound failures as a basketball team.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cavaliers Lay Claim to League’s Worst', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hollins, Ryan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott, Byron', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cleveland Cavaliers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cleveland (Ohio)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T19:51:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Mahoney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mahoney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6ec7c55-0e88-5386-beb7-fb364e4acfe7', 'word_count': 765, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6ec7c55-0e88-5386-beb7-fb364e4acfe7'}, {'abstract': 'Parking in Union City, N.J., remains an adventure.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/street-scenes-angle-parking/', 'snippet': 'Parking in Union City, N.J., remains an adventure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Union City, N.J., nearly a month after the great blizzard of 2010. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Street Scenes: Angle Parking', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Union City (NJ)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T20:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/074ca1e2-0491-5fe5-817b-1d165a15b3cb', 'word_count': 13, 'uri': 'nyt://article/074ca1e2-0491-5fe5-817b-1d165a15b3cb'}, {'abstract': 'Envisioning the World Economic Forum’s annual meeting in January 2020.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/davos-10-years-into-the-future/', 'snippet': 'Envisioning the World Economic Forum’s annual meeting in January 2020.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Imagine it is January 2020.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Davos, 9 Years Into the Future', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gates, Bill', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Simon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putin, Vladimir V', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roubini, Nouriel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Economic Forum', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T20:02:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katrin Bennhold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katrin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bennhold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f214819d-78f9-5ab7-9c43-6be53b12ae87', 'word_count': 1006, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f214819d-78f9-5ab7-9c43-6be53b12ae87'}, {'abstract': 'Comic book stories, like soap operas, are a never-ending series of twists and turns. And fans of super-heroes have learned that seemingly no death is permanent.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/the-life-death-and-life-of-superheroes/', 'snippet': 'Comic book stories, like soap operas, are a never-ending series of twists and turns. And fans of super-heroes have learned that seemingly no death is permanent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So the Human Torch, one quarter of the Fantastic Four, is dead today. And while Jonathan Hickman, the writer of Fantastic Four, had fans feverishly wondering which member of the quartet would meet his or her end, the smarter question is, how long before the deceased returns?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Life, Death and Life of Superheroes', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T20:06:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/18d78cfe-04d4-57c0-a88b-07bdef05e6f8', 'word_count': 551, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18d78cfe-04d4-57c0-a88b-07bdef05e6f8'}, {'abstract': 'Former Balenciaga studio director Bouchra Jarrar went beyond the veil with a pared-down vision of haute minimalism — that turned out to be a conceptual proposition critics could fully support.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/couture-scorecard-bouchra-jarrar-wins/', 'snippet': 'Former Balenciaga studio director Bouchra Jarrar went beyond the veil with a pared-down vision of haute minimalism — that turned out to be a conceptual proposition critics could fully support.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hooray, couture is back! (And Scorecard doesn’t just mean because Christian Lacroix is designing for Desigual.) With daughters of Dubai a calling, Monday John Galliano hauled out the old wasp-waists from the Dior atelier to an 800-seat marquee on the grounds of the Musée Rodin. Meanwhile former Balenciaga studio director Bouchra Jarrar went beyond the veil with a pared-down vision of haute minimalism — that turned out to be a new look critics could fully support.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Couture Scorecard | Bouchra Jarrar Wins!', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T20:07:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison S. Cohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Cohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3635e601-36ae-57b6-a347-c3cee1578288', 'word_count': 764, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3635e601-36ae-57b6-a347-c3cee1578288'}, {'abstract': 'On the perverse dual legacy of the Great Recession.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/expanding-or-maybe-shrinking-the-social-safety-net/', 'snippet': 'On the perverse dual legacy of the Great Recession.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Was last year’s health care reform the final stitch needed to secure America’s social safety net? Jim Kessler, vice president for Third Way, has given an emphatic ‘yes,’ but Lane Kenworthy, a University of Arizona professor who studies inequality, begs to differ.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Expanding, or Maybe Shrinking, the Social Safety Net', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T20:10:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d220dc6e-a75e-5b15-b415-2227841c3c29', 'word_count': 327, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d220dc6e-a75e-5b15-b415-2227841c3c29'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama called on Americans to unleash their creative spirits and join in the goal of outcompeting other nations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/us/politics/26speech.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama called on Americans to unleash their creative spirits and join in the goal of outcompeting other nations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON \x97 President Obama challenged Americans on Tuesday night to unleash their creative spirit, set aside their partisan differences and come together around a common goal of outcompeting other nations in a rapidly shifting global economy.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/26/us/26sotu_511/26sotu_511-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/26/us/26sotu_511/26sotu_511-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/26/us/26sotu_511/26sotu_511-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/26/us/26sotu_511/26sotu_511-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/26/us/26sotu_511/26sotu_511-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Calls for Bipartisan Effort to Fight for U.S. Jobs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'OBAMA PROPOSING\nBIPARTISAN EFFORT\nTO WIN THE FUTURE', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T20:11:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheryl Gay Stolberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheryl', 'middlename': 'Gay', 'lastname': 'Stolberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e8670d8-ad1e-51c9-9a00-5126b36d532f', 'word_count': 1439, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e8670d8-ad1e-51c9-9a00-5126b36d532f'}, {'abstract': 'Davos participants will focus on the widening gap between the super rich and the rest of the world.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/working-wealthy-predominate-the-new-global-elite/', 'snippet': 'Davos participants will focus on the widening gap between the super rich and the rest of the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DAVOS, Switzerland — “The next 10 years is going to be the most exciting time in our lives!” said Tejpreet Singh Chopra, an Indian entrepreneur. “The Indian economy will double! It will be incredible!”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Working Wealthy Predominate the New Global Elite', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buffett, Warren E', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cowen, Tyler', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gates, Bill', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Simon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nooyi, Indra K', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schwab, Klaus', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Economic Forum', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Davos (Switzerland)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T20:20:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chrystia Freeland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chrystia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Freeland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/955776fc-f1b1-55ed-834b-980540858010', 'word_count': 2382, 'uri': 'nyt://article/955776fc-f1b1-55ed-834b-980540858010'}, {'abstract': 'Simon Beaufoy and Danny Boyle’s ending for their inspired-by-true-events tale almost got upended by real life.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/realitys-near-collision-with-127-hours/', 'snippet': 'Simon Beaufoy and Danny Boyle’s ending for their inspired-by-true-events tale almost got upended by real life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARK CITY, Utah— Looks like rejecting a conventional Hollywood ending paid off for Danny Boyle and Simon Beaufoy of “127 Hours,” which nabbed best adapted screenplay, best picture, best actor and other nods from the Academy. Of course, as with any film based on a true story, reality nearly intruded on the tale the filmmakers wanted to tell.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reality’s Near-Collision With ‘127 Hours’', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T20:22:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ccf2c4ad-30a6-5ead-a0ba-ffb773932184', 'word_count': 359, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ccf2c4ad-30a6-5ead-a0ba-ffb773932184'}, {'abstract': 'Lesson Plan | Researching local or national black history, choosing significant artifacts and creating a museum display.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/stories-to-tell-curating-an-african-american-history-exhibition/', 'snippet': 'Lesson Plan | Researching local or national black history, choosing significant artifacts and creating a museum display.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Overview | Given that history is composed of many interwoven stories, how do curators and other historians decide which stories to tell? How can key historical events, people, places and themes best be represented in a meaningful, engaging exhibition to teach others? In this lesson, students consider the messages sent by artifacts and then develop an African-American history exhibition. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stories to Tell: Curating an African-American History Exhibition', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Museum of African American History and Culture', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T20:25:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Kavanagh and Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kavanagh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/98e4dbec-f5dc-5d64-8fc8-3512361f6d94', 'word_count': 2357, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98e4dbec-f5dc-5d64-8fc8-3512361f6d94'}, {'abstract': 'A rundown of how some highly rated charities spent donations to help Haiti.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/how-charities-used-donations-for-haiti/', 'snippet': 'A rundown of how some highly rated charities spent donations to help Haiti.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last January, after the devastating earthquake struck in Haiti, Bucks published a number of posts about how to help the victims, including tips for safe and efficient giving, how to have your donation doubled and how to donate reward points.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Charities Used Donations for Haiti', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Humanitarian Aid', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Charity Navigator', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Haiti', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T20:25:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Saranow Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'Saranow', 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e5594c8-cb67-5407-a691-3c5600e812a9', 'word_count': 330, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e5594c8-cb67-5407-a691-3c5600e812a9'}, {'abstract': 'The coalition government and the Labour opposition both refuse to compromise on a measure dividing them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/world/europe/26lords.html', 'snippet': 'The coalition government and the Labour opposition both refuse to compromise on a measure dividing them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON \x97 It was nearly midnight on Day 12 of the most grueling debate in recent House of Lords memory, and not all the Lords present were, strictly speaking, awake. But the Right Honorable Lord Davies of Oldham was warming to the question of the hour: a proposal to change “may” to “should” on Page 10, Line 7, of the Parliamentary Voting System and Constituencies Bill. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/26/world/LORDS/LORDS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 647, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Election Bill Erodes Decorum in House of Lords', 'kicker': 'London Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'All-Night Debates Leave a House Divided', 'name': None, 'seo'