e, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4dd441b-1367-5cb2-9d02-287b1b145b24', 'word_count': 421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4dd441b-1367-5cb2-9d02-287b1b145b24'}, {'abstract': 'As Iraqis watched televised street demonstrations from Lebanon, some wondered how far they were from destabilizing conditions of their own.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/world/middleeast/26iraq.html', 'snippet': 'As Iraqis watched televised street demonstrations from Lebanon, some wondered how far they were from destabilizing conditions of their own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGHDAD \x97 Iraq’s new government has been in office for just over one month, but already it has seen the toppling of governments in Tunisia and Lebanon, antigovernment protests in Egypt, and a rise in violence and unrest at home. On Tuesday, as Iraqis watched televised street demonstrations from Lebanon, some here wondered how far they were from destabilizing conditions of their own. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Some Iraqis See Symptoms of Region’s Tumult at Home', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Some Iraqis See Symptoms of Region’s Tumult at Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hezbollah', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lebanon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maliki, Nuri Kamal al-', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sadr, Moktada Al-', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nasrallah, Hassan', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T20:45:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Leland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f90d74ee-db1e-54da-bdb5-f4c1f0e301a8', 'word_count': 549, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f90d74ee-db1e-54da-bdb5-f4c1f0e301a8'}, {'abstract': 'The issue of transparency has become more than just an organizational matter at Davos this year, but a topic of discussion in and of itself.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/in-a-wikileaks-era-balancing-public-and-private/', 'snippet': 'The issue of transparency has become more than just an organizational matter at Davos this year, but a topic of discussion in and of itself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DAVOS, Switzerland — At a meeting of the World Economic Forum in China last year, Adrian Monck, the group’s chief spokesman, moderated a panel on social networking. Because the Internet can be a sensitive topic in China, he asked the audience whether the discussion should be kept private, to promote a freer exchange.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In a Wikileaks Era, Balancing Public and Private', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classified Information and State Secrets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assange, Julian P', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schwab, Klaus', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'WikiLeaks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wo