8:44+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'ContinuousNews', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'Zena Barakat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barakat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/a46cb09e-f4a8-590f-b6cc-bda35d183517', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/a46cb09e-f4a8-590f-b6cc-bda35d183517'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs from Lebanon, Egypt, Romania and Russia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/01/25/blogs/20110125POD.html', 'snippet': 'Photographs from Lebanon, Egypt, Romania and Russia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photographs from Lebanon, Egypt, Romania and Russia.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/25/blogs/20110125POD-slide-IZPL/20110125POD-slide-IZPL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 644, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pictures of the Day: Lebanon and Elsewhere', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T20:49:13+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Blogs', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/b6d5b9db-dcab-586c-8f54-51650bfde152', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/b6d5b9db-dcab-586c-8f54-51650bfde152'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Arndt, the screenwriter of “Toy Story 3,” says he understands little about the writing process except that starting at the end works for him.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/the-hard-slog-to-writing-gratification/', 'snippet': 'Michael Arndt, the screenwriter of “Toy Story 3,” says he understands little about the writing process except that starting at the end works for him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Arndt won an original screenplay Oscar in 2007 for “Little Miss Sunshine” and now finds himself nominated again, for best adapted screenplay for “Toy Story 3.” But, as he said on Tuesday morning, writing remains a mysterious, difficult process. One of the few things that works for him: He starts by writing the end of the story and figuring out how he wants the audience to feel. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hard Slog to Writing Gratification', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arndt, Michael', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pixar Animation Studios', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T20:57:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/64217b02-0e79-543c-92df-9d1c8b2119c3', 'word_count': 228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64217b02-0e79-543c-92df-9d1c8b2119c3'}, {'abstract': 'Uyanga Tamir, a high school senior from Denver, always assumed she’d be able to exhale in her second semester. “How naive I was,” she says now, as she tackles applications to several colleges abroad.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/cherry-creek-uyanga-tamir-3/', 'snippet': 'Uyanga Tamir, a high school senior from Denver, always assumed she’d be able to exhale in her second semester. “How naive I was,” she says now, as she tackles applications to several colleges abroad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As time goes on, I only look back and exclaim, “How naïve I was.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A January Deadline Met, but the Applications Continue', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T20:58:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Uyanga Tamir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Uyanga', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tamir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/611683cf-df7a-5904-b795-ae2f60b7d918', 'word_count': 475, 'uri': 'nyt://article/611683cf-df7a-5904-b795-ae2f60b7d918'}, {'abstract': 'Four young student innovators get prime seating at tonight’s State of the Union address.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/student-innovators-featured-during-state-of-the-union-speech/', 'snippet': 'Four young student innovators get prime seating at tonight’s State of the Union address.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The White House blog has posted some details about four young innovators who will sit with the first lady as President Obama delivers his State of the Union message tonight.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Student Innovators Featured During State of the Union Speech', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Engineering and Engineers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Solar Energy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Michelle', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Energy Department', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T20:59:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4001a567-2132-571f-bdf9-2ba2839bbb45', 'word_count': 544, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4001a567-2132-571f-bdf9-2ba2839bbb45'}, {'abstract': 'The artist and train hopper Swampy, known for his graffiti, talks with the Moment about his work, his plans for a new book and his thoughts on art.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/asked-and-answered-swampy/', 'snippet': 'The artist and train hopper Swampy, known for his graffiti, talks with the Moment about his work, his plans for a new book and his thoughts on art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the “bootleg artist” known as Swampy, the world is an endless living canvas. Variations on his signature “swampdonkey” tag — the skull of some imaginary tusked creature — can be found in almost every imaginable setting across America: dilapidated skyscrapers in Oakland, Calif.; train trestles in Ciudad Juarez, Mexico; gallery walls in Atlanta or Brooklyn. Though he’s best known for his graffiti, Swampy’s output varies wildly and incorporates a kaleidoscope of materials, styles and settings. His studio is almost entirely mobile, his materials are liberated as needed from large art supply chains, and he travels entirely for free by hopping freight trains. Trains, and train hopping, are a central theme in the hundreds of works he displays on Flickr and at swmpdnky.com. T caught up with Swampy, who had just spent several months traveling by train through Mexico taking photos for a new book, from his home in Oakland. This is his first published interview.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Asked and Answered | Swampy', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T21:05:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jordan Michelman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jordan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Michelman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/1058a764-dcf3-57d1-8b87-ba63186951d9', 'word_count': 847, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1058a764-dcf3-57d1-8b87-ba63186951d9'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s X Games will feature three events — ski halfpipe, and snowboard and ski slopestyle — that appear set to become Olympic additions at the 2014 Winter Games.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/sports/26xgames.html', 'snippet': 'This week’s X Games will feature three events — ski halfpipe, and snowboard and ski slopestyle — that appear set to become Olympic additions at the 2014 Winter Games.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For 15 years, the Winter X Games have acted as an incubator for hip, new Olympic sports that tend to lure larger and younger television audiences. Although officials at the International Olympic Committee do not credit the X Games directly with influencing what winds up in their program, many recent additions \x97 and the athletes who star in them \x97 earned their start at the Winter X Games, which begin Thursday on Buttermilk Mountain in Aspen, Colo.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/26/sports/26xgames1/26xgames1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'wi