ht in Havana” depicts the final hours before the end of the Cuban revolution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ever attend a party only to learn that you missed out on a much better time across town? Try Havana on New Year’s Eve, 1958, the last hurrah of the Fulgencio Batista regime before Fidel Castro’s rebel troops entered the capital.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Last Call for Cabaret Nights in Cuba', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Midnight in Havana'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Last Call for Cabaret Nights in Cuba', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dominguez, Robert', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T21:33:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Grode', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grode', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b977310-64d4-5d5f-8eb7-208c5169aae7', 'word_count': 360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b977310-64d4-5d5f-8eb7-208c5169aae7'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs from Lebanon, Egypt, Romania and Russia.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/pictures-of-the-day-lebanon-and-elsewhere-2/', 'snippet': 'Photographs from Lebanon, Egypt, Romania and Russia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photographs from Lebanon, Egypt, Romania and Russia.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pictures of the Day: Lebanon and Elsewhere', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T21:33:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michele McNally, Merrill D. Oliver, Craig Allen and Karly Domb Sadof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michele', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McNally', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Merrill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b5bea98-e2b3-5d25-8424-960367d77cca', 'word_count': 7, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b5bea98-e2b3-5d25-8424-960367d77cca'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama delivers his State of the Union address tonight at 9 p.m. Eastern time, and The Caucus will be live blogging it starting at 8.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/the-state-of-the-union-what-to-watch/', 'snippet': 'President Obama delivers his State of the Union address tonight at 9 p.m. Eastern time, and The Caucus will be live blogging it starting at 8.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama delivers his State of the Union address tonight at 9 p.m. Eastern time, and The Caucus will be live blogging it, starting an hour ahead. Come back here at 8 p.m. for the latest reporting about what we expect Mr. Obama to say. And then stay with us as we offer context to his speech and take note of the atmospherics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The State of the Union: What to Watch', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T21:35:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7946b65-5fb4-5dc8-a394-ccda44be8648', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7946b65-5fb4-5dc8-a394-ccda44be8648'}, {'abstract': 'House Republicans, joined by a handful of Democrats, voted to instruct the Budget Committee to come up with proposed cuts in federal spending that would take many programs back to the levels of 2008.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/house-orders-budget-trimmed/', 'snippet': 'House Republicans, joined by a handful of Democrats, voted to instruct the Budget Committee to come up with proposed cuts in federal spending that would take many programs back to the levels of 2008.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The House voted on Tuesday to direct the chairman of the Budget Committee to cut federal spending to 2008 levels on a broad array of programs, as they sought to play offense in what is expected to be a long battle with the Obama administration on fiscal policy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'House Orders Budget Trimmed', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T21:36:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By David M. Herszenhorn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4970495b-46a1-5c48-b5dc-fafdb19e1dc2', 'word_count': 520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4970495b-46a1-5c48-b5dc-fafdb19e1dc2'}, {'abstract': 'Does Wall Street Read?| Davos Color Code| Jim Simons Speaks', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/buzz-tracker-16/', 'snippet': 'Does Wall Street Read?| Davos Color Code| Jim Simons Speaks', 'lead_paragraph': 'Do Wall Streeters Read? Well, other than DealBook, of course. But Elizabeth Minkel on The New Yorker’s Book Bench blog notes that while there are a few examples of financial fiction in recent years, finding fiction readers in finance may be rarer. Some investment bank recruiters are apparently reluctant to hire someone seen as a “bookworm.” Still, she finds evidence of intellectual activity elsewhere, including a hedge fund where people suggest reading Heidegger. (“Being and Time” as trading manual?) The New Yorker', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Buzz Tracker', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T21:39:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/94e91c63-ced3-5943-ae32-745361c6114e', 'word_count': 413, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94e91c63-ced3-5943-ae32-745361c6114e'}, {'abstract': 'Claims that the confinement of Pfc. Bradley Manning is unnecessarily harsh bring to mind similar arguments made on behalf of Capt. James Yee, a Muslim chaplain at Guantánamo in 2003.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/dispute-over-confinement-of-wikileaks-suspect-echoes-guantanamo-chaplain-case/', 'snippet': 'Claims that the confinement of Pfc. Bradley Manning is unnecessarily harsh bring to mind similar arguments made on behalf of Capt. James Yee, a Muslim chaplain at Guantánamo in 2003.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The controversy over the harsh confinement the military has imposed on Bradley Manning, the Army private\xa0 accused of leaking hundreds of thousands of classified documents to WikiLeaks, holds echoes of a similar dispute over the pretrial detention of a Muslim Army chaplain at the prison in Guantánamo Bay, Cuba, seven years ago.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dispute Over Confinement of WikiLeaks Suspect Echoes Guantánamo Chaplain Case', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-25T21:39:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlie Savage', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Savage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb121169-214f-597c-b198-b8e72488c3db', 'word_count': 634, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb121169-214f-597c-b198-b8e72488c3db'}, {'abstract': 'While Viswanathan Anand and Hikaru Nakamura, the co-leaders after Round 8, drew in Round 9, Vladimir Kramnik and Levon Aronian won. All four are tied are for the lead with four rounds to play.', 'web_url': 'https://gambit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/four-tied-for-the-lead-of-tata-steel-chess-tournament/', 'snippet': 'While Viswanathan Anand and Hikaru Nakamura, the co-leaders after Round 8, drew in Round 9, Vladimir Kramnik and Levon Aronian won. All four are tied are for the lead with four rounds to play.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Four Tied for the Lead of Tata Steel Chess Tournament', 'kicker': 'Gambit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANAND, VISWANATHAN', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aronian, Levon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carlsen, Magnus', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kramnik, Vladimir', 'rank': 4, 'maj