_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/9d4ccad3-483a-5bf7-a87f-fe803afa2e40', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/9d4ccad3-483a-5bf7-a87f-fe803afa2e40'}, {'abstract': 'A descendant of the Alabama city’s founders, trained in urban planning, has bought up properties with the goal of creating a design-centered district.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/realestate/commercial/26alabama.html', 'snippet': 'A descendant of the Alabama city’s founders, trained in urban planning, has bought up properties with the goal of creating a design-centered district.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BIRMINGHAM, Ala. \x97 This city was founded, quite deliberately, in the late 19th century by a group of industrialists who wanted to capitalize on proximity to vast deposits of coal, iron and limestone. But after a period of prosperity and growth, Birmingham was brought to its knees by the Depression and wracked by the end of segregation. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/26/business/ALABAMA/ALABAMA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/26/business/ALABAMA/ALABAMA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/26/business/ALABAMA/ALABAMA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 601, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/26/business/ALABAMA/ALABAMA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/26/business/ALABAMA/ALABAMA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumb