redient is Parmesan cheese,” he said. “It’s the expensive kind from Italy, Reggiano.” Consider a few quarts of the soup to fill mugs for your Super Bowl party. And in summer, try it chilled.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/26/dining/26STUFF-3/STUFF3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 289, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/26/dining/26STUFF-3/STUFF3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 289}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/26/dining/26STUFF-3/STUFF3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 493, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/26/dining/26STUFF-3/26STUFF-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/26/dining/26STUFF-3/26STUFF-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Soup, Jersey Tomatoes Last Through the Year', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spoonfuls of Summer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soups', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tomatoes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T02:31:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a8ba941-6fae-57e9-98e1-e8bb78b98b01', 'word_count': 108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a8ba941-6fae-57e9-98e1-e8bb78b98b01'}, {'abstract': 'The owners of the Spanish food store on Broome Street have opened a compact but well-stocked wine shop next door.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/dining/26spanish.html', 'snippet': 'The owners of the Spanish food store on Broome Street have opened a compact but well-stocked wine shop next door.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The newest expansion of the Spanish food store Despaña, owned by Angelica and Marcos Intriago, pictured, is a compact but extremely well-stocked wine shop next door. It’s a no-frills but appealing installation with floor-to-ceiling shelves holding some 400 different wines grouped according to region, about 30 sherries, more than 30 sparkling wines and several ciders. Prices start at around $8, with more than 60 percent of the wines selling for less than $25. At the high end, up to $695, are bottles of Pingus and Vega Sicilia Unico. The adjacent food shop makes it easy for the Intriagos to provide tastings with cheeses, cured meats and other tidbits.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/26/dining/26STUFF/STUFF1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 745, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/26/dining/26STUFF/STUFF1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/26/dining/26STUFF/STUFF1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Despaña Opens a Wine Shop', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Despaña Opens a Wine Shop', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T02:32:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/08ca1e33-7e34-5323-b029-77905507bde4', 'word_count': 147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08ca1e33-7e34-5323-b029-77905507bde4'}, {'abstract': 'After sitting fallow for 43 years, a desolate stretch of parking lots along Delancey Street is closer than ever to being transformed into housing and shops.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/nyregion/26seward.html', 'snippet': 'After sitting fallow for 43 years, a desolate stretch of parking lots along Delancey Street is closer than ever to being transformed into housing and shops.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After sitting fallow for 43 years as the Lower East Side’s popularity soared, a desolate stretch of parking lots along Delancey Street is closer than ever to being transformed into housing and shops, marking the end of a long and bitter stalemate over the future of the sites.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Board Backs Development of Site on Lower East Side With Housing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Board Backs Development of Site on Lower East Side With Housing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Seward Park Urban Renewal Area (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing and Real Estate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Affordable Housing', 'rank': 3, 'major'