al Court in The Hague, which wanted him on charges of rape, murder, torture and other atrocities committed during a terror campaign against civilians in the Democratic Republic of Congo’s Kivu region. The rebel, Callixte Mbarushimana, who fled Rwanda, was living in Paris as a political refugee until the French police arrested him in October. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rwandan Rebel Handed Over to Court', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rwandan Rebel Handed Over to Court', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Congo, Democratic Republic of (Congo-Kinshasa)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rwanda', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mbarushimana, Callixte', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Criminal Court', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T04:13:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marlise Simons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marlise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Simons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4190d679-e2d8-5b0a-870f-a061ab027503', 'word_count': 72, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4190d679-e2d8-5b0a-870f-a061ab027503'}, {'abstract': 'The two political parties sat next to each other during the State of the Union, but signs of partisanship did not fade.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/us/politics/26scene.html', 'snippet': 'The two political parties sat next to each other during the State of the Union, but signs of partisanship did not fade.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — It was a new day in Congress. Or at least a new night. In a powerful break with tradition, lawmakers crossed party lines to sit Republican and Democrat, side by side, as the president addressed them.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Seating Chart, but Same Divides', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For One Night, Lawmakers Forgo Seating by Party Affiliation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T04:19:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Steinhauer and Carl Hulse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinhauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hulse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e34e0281-5a79-5569-9b21-f7892438643a', 'word_count': 990, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e34e0281-5a79-5569-9b21-f7892438643a'}, {'abstract': 'While New Haven Line riders acted nonchalant at the malfunction Monday morning, railroad officials promised a full inspection.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/nyregion/26metro.html', 'snippet': 'While New Haven Line riders acted nonchalant at the malfunction Monday morning, railroad officials promised a full inspection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Icy weather can be a challenge for the decades-old commuter trains that run along Metro-North’s New Haven Line. Brakes freeze; motors struggle. Cancellations and delays are common. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video of Open Door on Moving Train Shakes Metro-North', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Video of Open Door on Moving Train Shakes Metro-North', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroads', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metro-North Commuter Railroad', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Commuting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T04:32:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be86adb2-771c-5c3d-917c-5d2488cec3df', 'word_count': 418, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be86adb2-771c-5c3d-917c-5d2488cec3df'}, {'abstract': 'Winning lottery numbers for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut for Jan. 25, 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/nyregion/26lottery.html', 'snippet': 'Winning lottery numbers for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut for Jan. 25, 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jan. 25, 2011', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lottery Numbers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lottery Numbers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lotteries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T04:34:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/a64fbcdc-e567-57fb-b6e5-ffb749f48d18', 'word_count': 159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a64fbcdc-e567-57fb-b6e5-ffb749f48d18'}, {'abstract': 'Barack Obama and Paul Ryan punt on the deficit.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/25/the-politics-of-evasion/', 'snippet': 'Barack Obama and Paul Ryan punt on the deficit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you were a visitor from Mars, watching tonight’s State of the Union address and Paul Ryan’s Republican response, you would have no reason to think that the looming insolvency of our entitlement system lies at the heart of the economic challenges facing the United States over the next two decades. From President Obama, we heard a reasonably eloquent case for center-left technocracy and industrial policy, punctuated by a few bipartisan flourishes, in which the entitlement issue felt like an afterthought: He took note of the problem, thanked his own fiscal commission for their work without endorsing any of their recommendations, made general, detail-free pledges to keep Medicare and Social Security solvent (but “without slashing benefits for future generations”), and then moved swiftly on to the case for tax reform. Tax reform is important, of course, and so are education and technological innovation and infrastructure and all the other issues that the president touched on in this speech. But it was still striking that in an address organized around the theme of American competitiveness, which ran to almost 7,000 words and lasted for an hour, the president spent almost as much time talking about solar power as he did about the roots of the nation’s fiscal crisis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Politics of Evasion', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T04:46:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2565049-3d17-5e69-815e-260b063368c4', 'word_count': 421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2565049-3d17-5e69-815e-260b063368c4'}, {'abstract': 'The day in sports from Australian Open tennis to Asian Cup soccer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/01/25/sports/PHOTO-REPLAY.html', 'snippet': 'The day in sports from Australian Open tennis to Asian Cup soccer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The day in sports from Australian Open tennis to Asian Cup soccer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/25/sports/PHOTO-REPLAY-slide-96YY/PHOTO-REPLAY-slide-96YY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 724, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/25/sports/PHOTO-REPLAY-slide-96YY/PHOTO-REPLAY-slide-96YY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/25/sports/PHOTO-REPLAY-slide-96YY/PHOTO-REPLAY-slide-96YY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Photo Replay: Jan. 25', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T04:48:44+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/90002679-f3da-537d-9975-5c371f339c70', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/90002679-f3da-537d-9975-5c371f339c70'}, {'abstract': 'Times reporters factcheck President Obama’s State of the Union address and find the gray areas.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/us/politics/26fact.html', 'snippet': 'Times reporters factcheck President Obama’s State of the Union address and find the gray areas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON \x97 President Obama’s State of the Union address was chock-full of the sort of simple declarative statements that make him one of the most engaging political orators of his generation. But gauging the accuracy of those statements is not quite so straightforward, and some of the points in his speech shaded facts to help deliver his desired political message. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Some Difficulties Unnoted, And Some Facts Shaded', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Some Difficulties Unnoted, And Some Facts Shaded', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Renewable Energy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T04:50:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By David M. Herszenhorn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e06bc95-98a8-5ff0-b07f-c99197233d3d', 'word_count': 912, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e06bc95-98a8-5ff0-b07f-c99197233d3d'}, {'abstract': 'Many lawmakers broke the tradition of sitting with their own parties at the State of the Union address.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/01/25/us/politics/sotu-closer-look.html', 'snippet': 'Many lawmakers broke the tradition of sitting with their own parties at the State of the Union address.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many lawmakers broke the tradition of sitting with their own parties at the State of the Union address.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Sat Where: The State of the Union Seating Chart', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T04:58:23+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/494d4858-fbde-5061-8ecb-ffde17b27e2e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/494d4858-fbde-5061-8ecb-ffde17b27e2e'}, {'abstract': 'Recent studies cast doubt on whether platelet-rich plasma therapy — the expensive and controversial treatment known as P.R.P. — actually helps in the recovery of sports injuries.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/phys-ed-does-platelet-rich-plasma-therapy-really-work/', 'snippet': 'Recent studies cast doubt on whether platelet-rich plasma therapy — the expensive and controversial treatment known as P.R.P. — actually helps in the recovery of sports injuries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tiger Woods, Chris Canty (a defensive tackle for the New York Giants)\xa0and the Phillies’ pitcher Cliff Lee have in the past year or so greatly boosted the popularity of a controversial therapy by employing it to combat a sore knee (Woods), hamstring (Canty) and abdomen (Lee). The treatment, platelet-rich plasma therapy, or P.R.P., involves centrifuging a person’s own blood until it contains a concentrated mix of plasma cells and growth factors and then injecting the resulting substance directly into the injured tissue. In theory, the distilled growth factors (a protein or substance that helps stimulate growth) should speed healing and improve the tissue’s health, which has happened in the lab. When scientists surgically created lesions in animal tendons and other tissues, P.R.P. therapy nudged the injured tissues to rapidly create new collagen and blood vessels. Meanwhile, testimonials from professional athletes suggested that the shots work in humans as well. Today, recreational athletes reportedly clamor for P.R.P. to treat everything from tennis elbow to back pain, even though the procedure rarely is covered by insurance and can cost $1,000 a shot. “It has buzz,” said Dennis Cardone, a clinical associate professor at New York University’s Langone Medical Center who has written about the use of P.R.P. in sports medicine.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Phys Ed: Does Platelet-Rich Plasma Therapy Really Work?', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T05:01:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 