 in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wednesday Reading: Avoiding Vacation Rental Scams', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T15:29:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Saranow Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'Saranow', 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c001d58-2b54-519d-a035-6ea6ebcfb157', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c001d58-2b54-519d-a035-6ea6ebcfb157'}, {'abstract': 'Chapter 9 and Chapter 11 could be harmonized with regard to labor contracts, argues Stephen J. Lubben.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/financial-distress-and-the-bankruptcy-code/', 'snippet': 'Chapter 9 and Chapter 11 could be harmonized with regard to labor contracts, argues Stephen J. Lubben.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Now that the issue of states filing for Chapter 9 relief is in the spotlight, it seems appropriate to revisit this topic.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Financial Distress and the Bankruptcy Code', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T15:32:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen J. Lubben', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Lubben', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/738acaf2-3d86-5731-b8b2-48836c63fd2e', 'word_count': 438, 'uri': 'nyt://article/738acaf2-3d86-5731-b8b2-48836c63fd2e'}, {'abstract': 'This time he’s hired by a publisher.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/writer-for-hire-gets-a-book-deal-to-write-about-helping-students-cheat/', 'snippet': 'This time he’s hired by a publisher.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When an anonymous pen-for-hire wrote a tell-all article in The Chronicle of Higher Education about his booming career writing papers for cheating undergraduate and graduate students it caused a stir across college campuses. Now Bloomsbury USA has signed the writer, who lives on the East Coast and uses the pen name Ed Dante, for a book about cheating in higher education. He has quit his job to work on the project and plans to reveal his identity when the book is published in 2012. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Writer-for-Hire Gets a Book Deal to Write About Helping Students Cheat', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T15:35:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/844311b3-3e8b-5911-847f-8df812679d95', 'word_count': 231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/844311b3-3e8b-5911-847f-8df812679d95'}, {'abstract': 'The children’s invention, intended to discourage texting while driving, caught the attention of Transportation Secretary Ray LaHood and President Obama.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/smartwheel-earns-n-h-children-a-beltway-audience/', 'snippet': 'The children’s invention, intended to discourage texting while driving, caught the attention of Transportation Secretary Ray LaHood and President Obama.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tackling distracted driving is a matter of national debate, but few would expect six home-schooled children from Londonderry, N.H., to propose a solution so promising they’ve already met with President Obama and Transportation Secretary Ray LaHood.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'SMARTwheel Earns N.H. Children a Beltway Audience', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Text Messaging', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'LaHood, Ray', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transportation Department (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T15:43:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanya Mohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d976ed2-08aa-589c-9073-8e267f78fc1a', 'word_count': 548, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d976ed2-08aa-589c-9073-8e267f78fc1a'}, {'abstract': 'The makeup exec and social fixture shares her tips on everything from finding the best coffee in Amagansett to avoiding “super shiny face” on the red carpet.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/the-insider-olivia-chantecaille/', 'snippet': 'The makeup exec and social fixture shares her tips on everything from finding the best coffee in Amagansett to avoiding “super shiny face” on the red carpet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By all appearances, Olivia Chantecaille is living a charmed life. A society fixture, the 36-year-old divides her time between New York, East Hampton, Paris, Capri and other envy-inducing locales. In 2009, she wed a hedge fund trader named Warren G. Grady III on St. Barts, wearing a custom-made Giambattista Valli gown. When she’s not bouncing around the social circuit or promoting her family’s botanical-based cosmetics in stores around the world, Chantecaille is engaged in a variety of charitable pursuits, many of which are in support of the arts. And her privileged perch has informed her taste — in everything from finding the best coffee in Amagansett, N.Y., to avoiding “super-shiny face” on the red carpet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Insider | Olivia Chantecaille', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T15:47:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Heyman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ef2fa18-f659-5c18-abb4-7f79696828d5', 'word_count': 572, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ef2fa18-f659-5c18-abb4-7f79696828d5'}, {'abstract': 'Obama pushes for a clean energy quest but leaves out the reasons it’s needed.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/the-c-word-vanishes/', 'snippet': 'Obama pushes for a clean energy quest but leaves out the reasons it’s needed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The “C-word,” climate, appears to have become to the Beltway what\xa0the “P-word,” population, has been in climate treaty negotiations for a long time — unmentionable.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Ducks and Covers on Climate', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T15:55:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f057b95a-7daf-5e69-b417-cb9369d4d9ba', 'word_count': 1151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f057b95a-7daf-5e69-b417-cb9369d4d9ba'}, {'abstract': 'Many veterans said they feared that seeking treatment  would lead colleagues or bosses to lose respect for them and would hurt their careers.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/veterans-like-active-duty-troops-hesitant-to-seek-mental-health-care/', 'snippet': 'Many veterans said they feared that seeking treatment  would lead colleagues or bosses to lose respect for them and would hurt their careers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In recent years, the military has been encouraging its active duty troops to acknowledge the emotional and psychological stress of deployment, hoping to break through the resistance some people have to seeking help. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Veterans, Like Active Duty Troops, Are Hesitant to Seek Mental Health Care', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rand Corp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Veterans Affairs Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T15:55:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Dao', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dao', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2261c57-2657-5a47-9893-9c8e62b853c7', 'word_count': 551, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2261c57-2657-5a47-9893-9c8e62b853c7'}, {'abstract': 'An aggregation of links by the reporters and editors of Diner’s Journal.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/what-were-reading-105/', 'snippet': 'An aggregation of links by the reporters and editors of Diner’s Journal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An aggregation of links by the reporters and editors of Diner’s Journal. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T15:59:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/be07c077-461c-5ea5-958c-fc579c850fe9', 'word_count': 276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be07c077-461c-5ea5-958c-fc579c850fe9'}, {'abstract': 'After the Bagger’s pundits began speculating about who will prevail on Oscar night, a different kind of expert opinion stepped in.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/the-professional-oddsmakers-move-in/', 'snippet': 'After the Bagger’s pundits began speculating about who will prevail on Oscar night, a different kind of expert opinion stepped in.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARK CITY, Utah — Only moments after the Oscar nominations were announced the Bagger’s fellow pundits began speculating about who would prevail. “Original Screenplay: it’s over, people. ‘The King’s Speech’ wins,” the blogger Sasha Stone wrote on Twitter. She also predicted that “Inception,” whose director, Christopher Nolan, was snubbed in his category, will win for art direction, visual effects, sound and sound editing, and that, among documentaries, another category with a lot of cold-shoulders, “Inside Job” would triumph. “Cake walk,” she wrote.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Professional Oddsmakers Move In', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bale, Christian', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fincher, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leo, Melissa', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nolan, Christopher', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T16:01:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/62e7f39c-f533-537b-83fc-3b2110791ca0', 'word_count': 257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62e7f39c-f533-537b-83fc-3b2110791ca0'}, {'abstract': 'TED, known for exclusive events and online videos of speeches by celebrities like Al Gore, Bono and Malcolm Gladwell, plans to publish its own short e-books, beginning with three that went on sale Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/ted-starts-an-e-books-line/', 'snippet': 'TED, known for exclusive events and online videos of speeches by celebrities like Al Gore, Bono and Malcolm Gladwell, plans to publish its own short e-books, beginning with three that went on sale Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The conference superstar TED is getting into the e-book business.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'TED Starts an E-Books Line', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronic Books and Readers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANDERSON, CHRIS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TED (Technology, Entertainment, Design)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T16:05:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a897ccea-224f-5d78-a9de-ff10c6fcea9e', 'word_count': 354, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a897ccea-224f-5d78-a9de-ff10c6fcea9e'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Louvin achieved his greatest fame with the Louvin Brothers, the popular duo who modernized the close-harmony singing of Depression-era acts like the Blue Sky Boys and the Delmore Brothers.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/charlie-louvin-country-musician-dies-at-83/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Louvin achieved his greatest fame with the Louvin Brothers, the popular duo who modernized the close-harmony singing of Depression-era acts like the Blue Sky Boys and the Delmore Brothers.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Charlie Louvin, a member of one of the pre-eminent brother acts in country music and an inspiration to several generations of rock musicians, died Wednesday at his home in Wartrace, Tenn. He was 83. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Charlie Louvin, Country Musician, Dies at 83', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Louvin, Charlie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T16:15:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Friskics-Warren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Friskics-Warren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8ed110a-f22c-53af-b860-d197330aeb94', 'word_count': 220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8ed110a-f22c-53af-b860-d197330aeb94'}, {'abstract': 'N.Y. See: Broadway between 34th and 35th Streets.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/herald-square-953-a-m/', 'snippet': 'N.Y. See: Broadway between 34th and 35th Streets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Broadway between 34th and 35th Streets. The National Weather Service has issued a Winter Storm Warning for the region.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Herald Square, 9:53 A.M.', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'HERALD SQUARE (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T16:16:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a10551e0-4b99-5c91-b99b-084170a5615b', 'word_count': 19, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a10551e0-4b99-5c91-b99b-084170a5615b'}, {'abstract': 'A plate on which Pablo Picasso rendered the dachshund of the photographer David Douglas Duncan has been given to the Harry Ransom Center at the University of Texas.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/man-in-this-case-picasso-paints-dog/', 'snippet': 'A plate on which Pablo Picasso rendered the dachshund of the photographer David Douglas Duncan has been given to the Harry Ransom Center at the University of Texas.', 'lead_paragraph': ' When forgotten Picassos aren’t turning up in the most unlikely places, they’re depicting the most unlikely subjects. A piece of dinnerware he painted on and gave to the photojournalist David Douglas Duncan, which Mr. Duncan has donated to the Harry Ransom Center at the University of Texas at Austin, may not compare to the cache of nearly 300 works that a French electrician says he was given by Picasso’s second wife. But the plate is noteworthy for what it depicts: a dachshund named Lump, owned by Mr. Duncan in the 1950s. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Man (In This Case, Picasso) Paints Dog', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duncan, David Douglas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Picasso, Pablo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Texas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T16:20:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5b2b729-db59-593c-a21e-613267084db7', 'word_count': 228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5b2b729-db59-593c-a21e-613267084db7'}, {'abstract': 'Barnaby Conrad has written a novel about John Wilkes Booth 60 years after promising Sinclair Lewis he would do so.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/books/27conrad.html', 'snippet': 'Barnaby Conrad has written a novel about John Wilkes Booth 60 years after promising Sinclair Lewis he would do so.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CARPINTERIA, Calif. — The last time Barnaby Conrad saw Sinclair Lewis, three years after he served as Lewis’s personal secretary, they were at a bar in Paris and, by Mr. Conrad’s account, Lewis was thoroughly drunk. But not so drunk that he couldn’t chastise his former secretary for failing to execute a book idea that Lewis had handed him one morning at breakfast: a novel based on the conceit that John Wilkes Booth had escaped capture after assassinating Lincoln and had embarked on a secret life in the American frontier.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/27/arts/CONRADALT/CONRADALT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 733, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/27/arts/CONRADALT/CONRADALT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'t