pdated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Retirements Swallowed by Debt', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T17:27:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sherisse Pham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sherisse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7be6581b-ba8c-5c8b-9b54-b6b97ed2f93a', 'word_count': 1002, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7be6581b-ba8c-5c8b-9b54-b6b97ed2f93a'}, {'abstract': 'In the late 1990’s, hundreds of anti-Semitic comments appeared on the Web under the name of Mansour Baghamian, a chess organizer than living in Houston. Mr. Baghamian said he did not write them. Who did?', 'web_url': 'https://gambit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/anti-semitic-comments-found-under-a-chess-organizers-name/', 'snippet': 'In the late 1990’s, hundreds of anti-Semitic comments appeared on the Web under the name of Mansour Baghamian, a chess organizer than living in Houston. Mr. Baghamian said he did not write them. Who did?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the late 1990’s, someone using the name Mansour Bighamian posted more than 1,000 messages on Web bulletin boards about political and religious topics. Many of the postings were screeds attacking Jews and Israel, as well as gays. Among the most vitriolic suggested that the “time is ripe to build more ovens in EVERY country in the world for the D-DAY.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Anti-Semitic Comments Found Under a Chess Organizer’s Name', 'kicker': 'Gambit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTI-SEMITISM', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T17:28:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Chess', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dylan Loeb McClain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dylan', 'middlename': 'Loeb', 'lastname': 'McClain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb739ec2-342d-5386-b290-f19fda4347a5', 'word_count': 609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb739ec2-342d-5386-b290-f19fda4347a5'}, {'abstract': 'The British Museum has a new mobile app that allows visitors and armchair travelers to get up-close views of Galileo’s letters, the original version of “Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland” and other works from its collections.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/an-app-for-browsing-the-british-library/', 'snippet': 'The British Museum has a new mobile app that allows visitors and armchair travelers to get up-close views of Galileo’s letters, the original version of “Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland” and other works from its collections.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Visitors to the British Library have a new tool to help guide them: a mobile app that \xa0highlights more than 100 works from its collections. Included are portions of the Codex Sinaiticus, a handwritten manuscript of the Christian Bible written in the middle of the fourth century; Galileo’s letters; Leonardo da Vinci’s notebooks; the original version of “Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland,” and Jane Austen’s teenage writings.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An App for Browsing the British Library', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libraries and Librarians', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Manuscripts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Library', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T17:28:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanya Mohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fa7cba3-ab43-52ee-8eba-fcca21c90b2a', 'word_count': 190, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fa7cba3-ab43-52ee-8eba-fcca21c90b2a'}, {'abstract': 'Any vehicle blocking a roadway “or impeding the ability to plow streets” would be “subject to towing at the owner’s expense.”', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/city-declares-weather-emergency-again/', 'snippet': 'Any vehicle blocking a roadway “or impeding the ability to plow streets” would be “subject to towing at the owner’s expense.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 4:11 p.m. | With snow swirling outside, Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg declared a weather emergency on Wednesday, something officials did a few weeks ago for a minor storm but did not do during the post-Christmas blizzard that snarled the city.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'City Declares Weather Emergency. Again.', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snow and Snowstorms', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T17:32:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Barron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/04c09015-d1bf-5aca-ac31-7c18cced975e', 'word_count': 431, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04c09015-d1bf-5aca-ac31-7c18cced975e'}, {'abstract': 'Sue de Beer, an artist known for dabbling in darkness, has taken on a ghostly video project.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/arts/design/30debeer.html', 'snippet': 'Sue de Beer, an artist known for dabbling in darkness, has taken on a ghostly video project.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SURVEYING the row of door buzzers outside the hulking Brooklyn building where the artist Sue de Beer works, it somehow seems fitting to find a lone occupant listed on the building’s top floor, with no further explanation: “GOD.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/arts/DEBEER/DEBEER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/30/arts/DEBEER/DEBEER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 371}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'imag