t a doctored Lincoln manuscript says about the 16th president’s enduring myths.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/a-blot-on-lincoln-historians/', 'snippet': 'What a doctored Lincoln manuscript says about the 16th president’s enduring myths.', 'lead_paragraph': 'These facts remain true: on April 14, 1864, President Abraham Lincoln sent a routine, one-sentence, handwritten order to Joseph Holt, the judge advocate general, pardoning a California soldier, Private Patrick Murphy, who had been sentenced to be shot for desertion. “This man is pardoned, and hereby ordered to be discharged from the service,” the president wrote.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Blot on Lincoln Historians', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Amnesties, Commutations and Pardons', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Archives and Records Administration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T20:23:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harold Holzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harold', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/37118866-62f8-571a-9fc3-e8f6b913f4b2', 'word_count': 928, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37118866-62f8-571a-9fc3-e8f6b913f4b2'}, {'abstract': 'Two important spokesmen for business leaders and laborers applaud the call for investment in infrastructure.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/chamber-and-union-leaders-praise-obama/', 'snippet': 'Two important spokesmen for business leaders and laborers applaud the call for investment in infrastructure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two men who hardly agree on anything – the president of the U.S. Chamber of Commerce, Tom Donohue, and the president of the A.FL-C.I.O., Richard Trumka – have issued a joint statement praising President Obama’s call for new infrastructure.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chamber of Commerce and A.F.L.-C.I.O. Praise Obama', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T20:23:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ab9f22a-0ac3-5812-b334-19e4dbc3c09a', 'word_count': 305, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ab9f22a-0ac3-5812-b334-19e4dbc3c09a'}, {'abstract': 'In a blunt rebuke to Susana Martinez, the state’s Supreme Court reinstates two regulations whose publication she had blocked.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/new-mexico-high-court-reverses-governor-on-environmental-rules/', 'snippet': 'In a blunt rebuke to Susana Martinez, the state’s Supreme Court reinstates two regulations whose publication she had blocked.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New Mexico Supreme Court’s rebuke to Susana Martinez, the state’s new governor was blunt. “No one is above the law,” the state’s chief justice, Charles W. Daniels, said Wednesday morning as he announced the high court’s decision to reinstate two environmental regulations that the governor had unilaterally blocked upon taking office earlier this month. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Court Reverses New Mexico Governor on Environmental Rules', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martinez, Susana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Mexico', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T20:29:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicity Barringer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barringer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8806ccb2-28c3-5eba-a52d-bfe410813793', 'word_count': 334, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8806ccb2-28c3-5eba-a52d-bfe410813793'}, {'abstract': 'Several major banks are expected to give presentations to Ally Financial this week in an effort to win a leading role in the impending sale of the government’s stake in the company.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/banks-prepare-to-compete-for-ally-financial-stock-sale/', 'snippet': 'Several major banks are expected to give presentations to Ally Financial this week in an effort to win a leading role in the impending sale of the government’s stake in the company.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Another week, another “bake-off” for a company majority-owned by the government.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Banks Prepare to Compete for Ally Financial Stock Sale', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ally Financial Inc.', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T20:29:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/673cd3be-2cd2-5ec6-a6d3-e521d0b3c735', 'word_count': 250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/673cd3be-2cd2-5ec6-a6d3-e521d0b3c735'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Ebersole, I was reminded with renewed force, is a rarity among musical comedy stars.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/theater-talkback-a-master-class-with-christine-ebersole-a-singer-of-many-voices/', 'snippet': 'Ms. Ebersole, I was reminded with renewed force, is a rarity among musical comedy stars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ever since last Saturday night – well, around the time Saturday night turned into Sunday morning – I’ve been hearing voices. One of them is a clear, righteous soprano that recalls Joan Baez, the balladeer of my childhood; another is a lilting, silvery curlicue like that of the soubrette in an operetta from the gaslight era; then there’s the sassy “Hello, boys” trumpet of a girl singer with a big band; and, most hauntingly, there’s the glasslike voice that seems to exist expressly as a container for the shimmering pain within.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Theater Talkback: A Master Class With Christine Ebersole, a Singer of Many Voices', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'NIGHTCLUBS AND CABARETS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'