erators of the now-closed restaurant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Donald J. Trump and the head of the powerful union that represented the 400 workers at Tavern on the Green say they have come to an agreement that could revive the shuttered landmark restaurant that is now home to a food-truck court and a visitor center.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Trump Says He and Union Have a Deal on Tavern on the Green', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T22:24:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5c62dd4-7175-5ab5-87bc-44e6a13cb796', 'word_count': 1021, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5c62dd4-7175-5ab5-87bc-44e6a13cb796'}, {'abstract': 'Donald Trump said he would spend $20 million of his own money to rebuild Tavern on the Green and make it “the highest-grossing restaurant on the planet.’’', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/trumps-big-plans-for-tavern-on-the-green/', 'snippet': 'Donald Trump said he would spend $20 million of his own money to rebuild Tavern on the Green and make it “the highest-grossing restaurant on the planet.’’', 'lead_paragraph': ' Donald J. Trump and the head of the powerful union that represented the 400 workers at Tavern on the Green say they have come to an agreement that would revive the shuttered landmark restaurant in Central Park that is now home to a food-truck court and a visitor center. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Trump’s Big Plans for Tavern on the Green', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T22:26:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/591422d7-ee8a-556e-a3bd-3453fe98039e', 'word_count': 119, 'uri': 'nyt://article/591422d7-ee8a-556e-a3bd-3453fe98039e'}, {'abstract': 'The Sundance Film Festival announced partnerships with Facebook and Kickstarter.com to help Sundance artists connect with audiences.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/sundance-kickstarts-audience-connections/', 'snippet': 'The Sundance Film Festival announced partnerships with Facebook and Kickstarter.com to help Sundance artists connect with audiences.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES— The Sundance Institute is expanding its efforts to help filmmakers use the Web to find funding and distribution for their work. On Wednesday the organization, best known for its film festival, announced partnerships with Facebook and Kickstarter.com a relatively new site that aids aspiring movie directors, musicians, playwrights and other emerging artists find funding. The goal: to help connect Sundance artists with audiences.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sundance Kickstarts Audience Connections', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T22:26:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/20c08819-efd9-5644-9fb9-40fdf00eee40', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20c08819-efd9-5644-9fb9-40fdf00eee40'}, {'abstract': 'At least three camps will be very happy that the Decemberists, the indie-folk band from Portland, Ore., known for its high-concept songs and SAT-level vocabulary, has reached No. 1 on the latest Billboard.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/arts/music/27arts-DECEMBERISTS_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'At least three camps will be very happy that the Decemberists, the indie-folk band from Portland, Ore., known for its high-concept songs and SAT-level vocabulary, has reached No. 1 on the latest Billboard.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At least three camps will be very happy that the Decemberists, the indie-folk band from Portland, Ore., known for its high-concept songs and SAT-level vocabulary, has reached No. 1 on the latest Billboard album chart: the band and its record company; its fans; and the hand wringers and observers of the music industry who have recently seen two historic lows in an important sales statistic. The Decemberists’ latest, “The King Is Dead” (Capitol), sold 94,000 copies in its first week, according to Nielsen SoundScan, sending the album straight to No. 1. That’s a much healthier number than any other album has had recently. Last week the band Cake’s “Showroom of Compassion” (Upbeat) set a record for the fewest sales for a No. 1 album, with 44,000. It beat the record set the week before by Taylor Swift’s “Speak Now” (Big Machine), which had 52,000. The next four slots on the Top 200 this week are taken up by new albums. “Kidz Bop 19” (Razor & Tie), the latest album in the “Kidz Bop” franchise of family-friendly versions of pop hits, opened at No. 2, with 70,000; and “Science & Faith” (Epic), by the Irish alternative-rock band the Script, bowed at No. 3, with 49,000. Social Distortion, a Los Angeles band that plays rockabilly-tinged punk rock, has reached the Top 10 for the first time in its long career with “Hard Times and Nursery Rhymes” (Epitaph), at No. 4, with 46,000; and Gregg Allman’s new solo album, “Low Country Blues” (Rounder), bows at No. 5, with 36,000. (Cake’s album plunged to No. 25 this week with 15,000 sales, a 67 percent decline.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Decemberists Reign on Billboard Chart', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Decemberists Reign on Billboard Chart', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T22:31:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/5187bd4c-662e-5849-8654-9b8646930974', 'word_count': 274, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5187bd4c-662e-5849-8654-9b8646930974'}, {'abstract': 'The Roundabout Theater Company is extending the run of “The Importance of Being Earnest.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/theater/27arts-FOOTNOTES_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The Roundabout Theater Company is extending the run of “The Importance of Being Earnest.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Roundabout Theater Company is extending the run of “The Importance of Being Earnest” on Broadway by an unusually long 17 weeks, until July 3, following wide critical acclaim for the production. As a result of the extended run, Roundabout announced that its new musical “The People in the Picture,” which was to have followed “Earnest” into the American Airlines Theater this spring, would now be mounted at Studio 54, one of the company’s three Broadway houses. ... The Broadway revival of “Born Yesterday,” starring Jim Belushi and Nina Arianda, is to start previews on March 31 at the Cort Theater, with opening night set for April 24, the show’s producers announced.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Footnotes', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Footnotes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T22:32:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9ffe07a-89bd-5ce1-a678-d3e3ef4353e2', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9ffe07a-89bd-5ce1-a678-d3e3ef4353e2'}, {'abstract': 'Suggestions for storing and displaying artwork when the refrigerator door can’t hold any more of little Egbert’s masterpieces.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/garden/27artbox.html', 'snippet': 'Suggestions for storing and displaying artwork when the refrigerator door can’t hold any more of little Egbert’s masterpieces.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHY hang children’s art on the refrigerator door? Because it’s there. And as an exhibition venue, it’s better than nothing. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/27/garden/17artbox-span/17artbox-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 348, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/27/garden/17artbox-span/17artbox-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 348}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/27/garden/17artbox-span/ArtBox-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 630, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_na