ion_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Hesser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hesser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e67da109-e4d6-5ed4-bfc0-0e9495f655cf', 'word_count': 522, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e67da109-e4d6-5ed4-bfc0-0e9495f655cf'}, {'abstract': 'The Food and Drug Administration said that 60 cases of anaplastic large-cell lymphoma had been reported in women with breast implants.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/health/research/27implant.html', 'snippet': 'The Food and Drug Administration said that 60 cases of anaplastic large-cell lymphoma had been reported in women with breast implants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Breast implants may cause a small but significant increase in the risk of an extremely rare but treatable type of cancer, the Food and Drug Administration said on Wednesday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Breast Implants Are Linked to Rare but Treatable Cancer, F.D.A. Finds', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Breast Implants Are Linked to Rare but Treatable Cancer, F.D.A. Finds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Breasts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Implants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T23:02:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Research', 'byline': {'original': 'By Denise Grady', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Denise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grady', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9895d548-6cd4-57a7-929e-ddf89ea42da9', 'word_count': 683, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9895d548-6cd4-57a7-929e-ddf89ea42da9'}, {'abstract': 'Richard Wilson’s opera “Aethelred the Unready” tells the story of an English king who wants history to treat him more kindly.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/arts/music/27unready.html', 'snippet': 'Richard Wilson’s opera “Aethelred the Unready” tells the story of an English king who wants history to treat him more kindly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Poor King Aethelred of England. Plagued by intrigues in his court, disunity among his subjects and frequent attacks by the Vikings, he had a long but miserable reign, from 978 to 1016. After his death he was saddled with the epithet the Unrede, which is generally rendered the Unready in modern English, though it actually meant the Ill Advised. And he is the subject of a one-act comic opera by Richard Wilson, who toys with that mistranslation and Aethelred’s unfortunate reputation. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/27/arts/UNREADY/UNREADY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/27/arts/UNREADY/UNREADY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/27/arts/UNREADY/UNREADY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Long-Dead King Sets Out to Upgrade His Brand’s Image', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Long-Dead King Sets Out to Upgrade His Brand’s Image', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Richard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Symphony Space', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T23:03:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/844582f2-9b1b-5ff9-acbc-24effc679acc', 'word_count': 488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/844582f2-9b1b-5ff9-acbc-24effc679acc'}, {'abstract': 'The Collegiate Chorale and the American Symphony Orchestra performed a concert version of “Knickerbocker Holiday,” Kurt Weill and Maxwell Anderson’s 1938 Broadway show.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/arts/music/27knick.html', 'snippet': 'The Collegiate Chorale and the American Symphony Orchestra performed a concert version of “Knickerbocker Holiday,” Kurt Weill and Maxwell Anderson’s 1938 Broadway show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Much of the breezy score for “Knickerbocker Holiday,” the 1938 Broadway show by Kurt Weill (music) and Maxwell Anderson (book and lyrics), effervesces like a Gilbert and Sullivan operetta. To hear it vibrantly performed by the Collegiate Chorale and the American Symphony Orchestra (conducted by James Bagwell) at Alice Tully Hall on Tuesday evening was to be returned to a quaint, lilting musical-theater ethos in which frivolity trumps gravity. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/27/arts/KNICK/KNICK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 808, 'width': 1024, 'subT