f the Year', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T23:56:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa1ea01f-b5fd-5b82-a79a-ed1d5b8a13e4', 'word_count': 434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa1ea01f-b5fd-5b82-a79a-ed1d5b8a13e4'}, {'abstract': 'After Carl Icahn extended his $5.50 a share bid for Dynegy, Seneca Capital, the energy company’s second largest shareholder, said it would reject a $6 a share offer for the energy company — should such a bid materialize.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/seneca-pre-emptively-rejects-dynegy-offer-at-6-a-share/', 'snippet': 'After Carl Icahn extended his $5.50 a share bid for Dynegy, Seneca Capital, the energy company’s second largest shareholder, said it would reject a $6 a share offer for the energy company — should such a bid materialize.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Seneca Capital, the second-largest shareholder of Dynegy, said on Wednesday that it would reject a $6 a share offer for the energy company — should such a bid materialize.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seneca Pre-Emptively Rejects Dynegy Offer at $6 a Share', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Icahn, Carl C', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dynegy Inc.', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T23:56:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8b4a571-0deb-5b35-9bef-708c741ff00c', 'word_count': 325, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8b4a571-0deb-5b35-9bef-708c741ff00c'}, {'abstract': 'At Valentino, Maria Grazia Chiuri and Pier Paolo Piccioli offered very pretty clothes, focusing on maxi lengths, simple day dresses in double ivory crepe with faggoting, some very cool halter dresses in delicate lace, and shirtwaists.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/26/paris-couture-valentino/', 'snippet': 'At Valentino, Maria Grazia Chiuri and Pier Paolo Piccioli offered very pretty clothes, focusing on maxi lengths, simple day dresses in double ivory crepe with faggoting, some very cool halter dresses in delicate lace, and shirtwaists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The spring haute couture shows, which ended tonight with Valentino, revealed a creative energy that ought to give pause to designers of ready-to-wear. Not that the two worlds really have anything in common, except a respect for craft and beauty and certain high-profile clients. But ready-to-wear has had more and more limitations placed upon it, like the availability of fabrics, pricing issues and competitive pressures from fast-fashion chains. Haute couture has none of these concerns, and it has enough masters — Karl Lagerfeld, John Galliano, Jean Paul Gaultier — and, of course, sympathetic corporate owners to create exceptional, even relevant fashion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paris Couture: Valentino', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Couture (Fashion)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-26T23:58:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e183e599-99b8-5b9c-814a-829b25c691c2', 'word_count': 274, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e183e599-99b8-5b9c-814a-829b25c691c2'}, {'abstract': 'Reporter Mona El-Naggar describes the second day of rioting in Cairo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/world/1248069590856/protesters-defy-egypt-s-govermnent.html', 'snippet': 'Reporter Mona El-Naggar describes the second day of rioting in Cairo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reporter Mona El-Naggar describes the second day of rioting in Cairo.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/26/world/11115_1_tc-012611-monaegypt_75x75.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/26/world/11115_1_tc-012611-monaegypt_75x75.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Protesters Defy Egypt’s Government', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-27T00:02:51+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'Ben Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/c512a28c-cb5f-5ba5-a10f-f8629edec426', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/c512a28c-cb5f-5ba5-a10f-f8629edec426'}, {'abstract': 'An 18-year-old who was born in New York and then sent to live in South Africa returned home two years ago, only to be forced to fend for herself before she finished high school.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/nyregion/27neediest.html', 'snippet': 'An 18-year-old who was born in New York and then sent to live in South Africa returned home two years ago, only to be forced to fend for herself before she finished high school.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Using the accepted social metrics of teenagers in this country, Thakane Masondo should have plenty of friends. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/27/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/27/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/27/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In High School and Alone in a Homeless Shelter', 'kicker': 'The Neediest Cases', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Outwardly, She’s Like Any High School Senior. Secretly, She’s Living Alone in a Shelter.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times Neediest Cases Fund', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'HARLEM (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Catholic Charities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-27T00:15:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Keh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/22a34f71-2606-592f-8d22-cd0191e6af48', 'word_count': 646, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22a34f71-2606-592f-8d22-cd0191e6af48'}, {'abstract': 'Stephen C. Barr, 25, a junior literary agent at Writers House, searches for hidden masterpieces and says he is “freakishly, dangerously ambitious.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/nyregion/27entry.html', 'snippet': 'Stephen C. Barr, 25, a junior literary agent at Writers House, searches for hidden masterpieces and says he is “freakishly, dangerously ambitious.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'An insatiable reader, Stephen C. Barr, 25, is a junior literary agent at Writers House. When he is not searching the slush pile \x97 the trove of unsolicited submissions \x97 for hidden masterpieces, Mr. Barr searches blogs for worthy book projects. A 2007 graduate of the University of California, Los Angeles, he switched his career objective from editor to agent early on, winning internships first at Hotchkiss and Associates and then at Writers House, where he was promoted to junior agent from agent assistant last year. Mr. Barr lives in Hell’s Kitchen.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/27/nyregion/ENTRY/ENTRY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/27/nyregion/ENTRY/ENTRY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargehei