turned to Cairo on Thursday ahead of major demonstrations set to follow Friday prayers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO \x97 Mohamed ElBaradei, the Nobel laureate who has become a leading opponent of President Hosni Mubarak of Egypt, returned to Cairo on Thursday in an attempt to galvanize youth-led street protests that extended into a third day across the country.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/world/28egypt2-cnd/28egypt2-cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/28/world/28egypt2-cnd/28egypt2-cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/world/28egypt2-cnd/28egypt2-cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/world/28egypt2-cnd/28egypt2-cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/28/world/28egypt2-cnd/28egypt2-cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Opposition in Egypt Gears Up for Major Friday Protest', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'ElBaradei Returns as Egypt Braces for New Protests', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ElBaradei, Mohamed', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hosni', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-27T12:57:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kareem Fahim and Liam Stack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kareem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fahim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Liam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c668570-733d-5d71-8d12-405e8057f351', 'word_count': 845, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c668570-733d-5d71-8d12-405e8057f351'}, {'abstract': 'Conservatives have questioned whether the president believes in American exceptionalism. His speech Tuesday seemed designed to prove he does.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/obama-used-speech-to-address-americas-greatness-and-his-critics/', 'snippet': 'Conservatives have questioned whether the president believes in American exceptionalism. His speech Tuesday seemed designed to prove he does.', 'lead_paragraph': 'According to Sarah Palin and other conservatives, President Obama does not recognize America’s greatness. Among his failings, they say, is a tendency to talk the country down, minimize its place in the world and underestimate its uniqueness.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Used Speech to Address America’s Greatness — and His Critics', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-27T12:57:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b0aeb56-757b-5fe6-9a4a-8bfb000318ba', 'word_count': 958, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b0aeb56-757b-5fe6-9a4a-8bfb000318ba'}, {'abstract': 'The Jets will have several difficult decisions to make as they reshape their roster for next season.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/did-the-jets-have-a-successful-season/', 'snippet': 'The Jets will have several difficult decisions to make as they reshape their roster for next season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'They went 11-5, but they didn’t win the division. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Did the Jets Have a Successful Season?', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-27T13:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6774de4c-9067-50f3-9ad4-e30a01c2a830', 'word_count': 211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6774de4c-9067-50f3-9ad4-e30a01c2a830'}, {'abstract': 'Sonos’ S5 music system, coupled with free iPad and iPhone apps, create a near-perfect way of listening to your digital audio content.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/sonos-finds-its-missing-links/', 'snippet': 'Sonos’ S5 music system, coupled with free iPad and iPhone apps, create a near-perfect way of listening to your digital audio content.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s easy to forget that Apple’s iPod was not an instant success. First offered in 2001, and compatible with the Mac only, it was not available for Windows computers until 2002, and then as a separate model. Apple’s iTunes Music Store, which gave consumers access to millions of songs, came into existence only in mid-2003.\xa0 And ultimately it was the store that gave the iPod the ammunition it needed to become the center of popular culture.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sonos Finds Its Missing Link(s)', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sonos', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-27T13:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric A. Taub', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Taub', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e8c71fa-bbb9-5f0f-8be7-d6f5812d3e3e', 'word_count': 741, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e8c71fa-bbb9-5f0f-8be7-d6f5812d3e3e'}, {'abstract': 'A panel manufactured by Petra Solar can slice and dice voltage to prevent a surplus of solar energy from crippling power lines.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/for-excess-solar-flow-a-veg-o-matic-for-voltage/', 'snippet': 'A panel manufactured by Petra Solar can slice and dice voltage to prevent a surplus of solar energy from crippling power lines.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Until very recently, the solar industry was mainly concerned with getting a toehold in the production of electricity at a utility-level scale. Now a New Jersey company is looking for its niche in a different field — how to handle a system that is saturated with solar energy, sometimes enough to destabilize the electric grid.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Excess Solar Flow, a Veg-O-Matic for Voltage', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Solar Energy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Service Electric and Gas Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-27T13:01:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/912191c7-40e4-5e7d-bece-bea2bb78eb56', 'word_count': 1032, 'uri': 'nyt://article/912191c7-40e4-5e7d-bece-bea2bb78eb56'}, {'abstract': 'Camera traps set at a salt lick in the Ecuadorian jungle catch troops of spider and howler monkeys.', 'web_url': 'https://scientistatwork.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/camera-traps-catch-lots-of-monkeys/', 'snippet': 'Camera traps set at a salt lick in the Ecuadorian jungle catch troops of spider and howler monkeys.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anthony Di Fiore, a primate biologist at New York University, writes from Ecuador, where he is using GPS technology and camera traps to study the behavior of male spider monkeys.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Camera Traps Catch Lots of Monkeys', 'kicker': 'Scientist at Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monkeys and Apes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank'