s on a charge, were acquired by the car-sharing company a year before they become commercially available.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Thursday, eight plug-in Toyota Prius hybrids became available to members of Zipcar, the car-sharing service that began in the Boston area 11 years ago. The plug-in Prius will not be made available to individual customers until 2012.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Zipcar Adds Toyota Prius Plug-In Hybrids to Fleet', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hybrid Vehicles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toyota Motor Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ZipCar', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-27T20:56:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8814dc41-51a4-5051-919e-af646ddcf5c8', 'word_count': 407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8814dc41-51a4-5051-919e-af646ddcf5c8'}, {'abstract': 'Still a bargain, Lisbon is newly alive with upstart museums and renovated industrial districts, along with innovative restaurants, nightclubs and hotels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/travel/30hours-lisbon.html', 'snippet': 'Still a bargain, Lisbon is newly alive with upstart museums and renovated industrial districts, along with innovative restaurants, nightclubs and hotels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHEAP. That’s the label usually slapped across the forehead of the Portuguese capital. Around the Continent, the waterside city is mostly seen as the charmingly faded seat of a centuries-gone trade empire where you can plunk down some coins to ride an old yellow cable car, visit Baroque churches and squares, fill up on cut-rate seafood meals, sip 2-euro glasses of Portuguese red and retire to your budget hotel. But Lisbon is getting fancier every month. By day, ambitious upstart museums and renovated industrial districts offer an infusion of contemporary art and design. By night, a fledgling wave of neo-Portuguese restaurants, stylish night spots and innovatively designed hotels provide happening places to play. The best part? The city remains a terrific bargain.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/travel/36HOURS-span/36HOURS-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/30/travel/36HOURS-span/36HOURS-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/travel/36HOURS-span/36HOURS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/travel/36HOURS-span/36HOURS-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/travel/36HOURS-span/36HOURS-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '36 Hours in Lisbon', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '36 HOURS: Lisbon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lisbon (Portugal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-27T21:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Sherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a50f12e-b7e9-50c6-9501-123942753bc4', 'word_count': 1459, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a50f12e-b7e9-50c6-9501-123942753bc4'}, {'abstract': 'Many New Yorkers headed home from work on Thursday will barely notice the effects of Wednesday night’s snowstorm.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/afternoon-commute-outlook-somewhat-improved/', 'snippet': 'Many New Yorkers headed home from work on Thursday will barely notice the effects of Wednesday night’s snowstorm.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many New Yorkers headed home from work on Thursday will barely notice the aftereffects of Wednesday night’s snowstorm: the vast majority of the city’s subway system, along with the PATH train (latest info ») and New Jersey Transit (latest info ») , will operate close to a normal weekday schedule. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Afternoon Commute Outlook: Somewhat Improved', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snow and Snowstorms', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-27T21:04:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/87bd00ad-3a16-5018-a759-962ada81a160', 'word_count': 225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87bd00ad-3a16-5018-a759-962ada81a160'}, {'abstract': 'The Chinese artist Wang Qingsong, who has moved from painting to producing staged photographs and videos, is the subject of a small survey at the International Center of Photography.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/arts/design/28qingsong.html', 'snippet': 'The Chinese artist Wang Qingsong, who has moved from painting to producing staged photographs and videos, is the subject of a small survey at the International Center of Photography.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Beijing artist Wang Qingsong, born at the beginning of the Cultural Revolution, has seen China morph from an insular, rural society to a globally engaged dynamo. His art has evolved just as rapidly, from Gaudy Painting (a Chinese variation on Pop) to giant photographs staged in movie studios and short, performance-based videos. All of these works regard recent changes in Chinese culture \x97 the proliferation of McDonald’s, overcrowded cities, even a booming art scene \x97 from an ironic stance that needs no translation.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/28QINGSONG-span/QINGSONG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/28QINGSONG-span/QINGSONG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/28QINGSONG-span/QINGSONG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 580, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/28QINGSONG-span/QINGSONG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/28QINGSONG-span/QINGSONG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As China Evolves, So Does an Artist', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As China Evolves, So Does An Artist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Center of Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-27T21:05:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/86524e81-83f3-5e7e-ba92-c2f8ce10e08b', 'word_count': 910, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86524e81-83f3-5e7e-ba92-c2f8ce10e08b'}, {'abstract': 'Health care experts tout accountable care organizations as the system’s best hope for the future, but engaging patients is crucial to their success.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/health/views/27chen.html', 'snippet': 'Health care experts tout accountable care organizations as the system’s best hope for the future, but engaging patients is crucial to their success.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This past week while spending time with nonmedical friends, I found myself referring to what health care experts have been touting as the system’s best hope for the future. My friends, eager to learn more and always game for any clarification of the health care system, leaned in to hear me expound on accountable care organizations, or A.C.O.’s.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2008/09/15/health/chen_190/chen_190-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2008/09/15/health/chen_190/chen_190-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2008/09/15/health/chen_190/chen_190-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2008/09/15/health/chen_190/chen_190-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2008/09/15/health/chen_190/chen_190-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Missing Ingredient in Accountable Care', 'kicker': 'Doctor and Patient', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Missing Ingredient in Accountable Care', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reform and Reorganization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-27T21:05:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Views', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pauline W. Chen, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pauline', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a06514eb-4b82-5bf0-90d6-128fae5a609a', 'word_count': 1013, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a06514eb-4b82-5bf0-90d6-128fae5a609a'}, {'abstract': 'Accountable care organizations, or A.C.O.’s, are hailed as the best hope for the future of health care, but one critical ingredient is missing from the discussion: the patient.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/making-patients-part-of-accountable-care/', 'snippet': 'Accountable care organizations, or A.C.O.’s, are hailed as the best hope for the future of health care, but one critical ingredient is missing from the discussion: the patient.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Accountable care organizations, or A.C.O.’s, are quickly becoming the “it” girl of the health care overhaul,\xa0 garnering admiration from policy makers, researchers, third-party payers and politicians of all stripes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Making Patients Part of Accountable Care', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reform and Reorganization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-27T21:06:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pauline W. Chen, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pauline', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfdc233f-06ae-5d66-837d-2b41ba5dc147', 'word_count': 214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfdc233f-06ae-5d66-837d-2b41ba5dc147'}, {'abstract': 'Ursula von Rydingsvard’s giant wooden works at SculptureCenter evoke a raw, primeval age.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/arts/design/28sculpture.html', 'snippet': 'Ursula von Rydingsvard’s giant wooden works at SculptureCenter evoke a raw, primeval age.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Legions of modern artists have been attracted to things prehistoric, archaic and primordial. Gauguin painted fantasies of a South Pacific Eden. Picasso drew inspiration from African masks, which, misguidedly or not, he took for signs of timeless human ferocity. A traveler happening on Robert Smithson’s “Spiral Jetty” could mistake it for a ceremonial maze built by ancient pagans.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/28SCULPTURE-span/SCULPTURE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/28SCULPTURE-span/SCULPTURE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/28SCULPTURE-span/SCULPTURE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/28SCULPTURE-span/SCULPTURE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/28SCULPTURE-span/SCULPTURE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Artifacts of an Imagined Time', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Artifacts of an Imagined Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub