': None, 'lastname': 'Graeber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/e642f2ec-3933-505b-8bbe-7af33af9f6e5', 'word_count': 3661, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e642f2ec-3933-505b-8bbe-7af33af9f6e5'}, {'abstract': 'Using fabrics and texture, the Danish artist Sergej Jensen, exhibiting at MoMA P.S. 1, achieves what other artists do with paint.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/arts/design/28jensen.html', 'snippet': 'Using fabrics and texture, the Danish artist Sergej Jensen, exhibiting at MoMA P.S. 1, achieves what other artists do with paint.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Sergej Jensen’s shy, luminous, physically scant paintings, often made of little more than scraps of linen and other found fabrics, are tightrope walkers that travel light. Balancing gracefully between reverence and irreverence while deftly eluding irony, they jettison so much of painting’s traditional baggage that they almost question themselves out of existence. But not quite. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/28JENSEN-1/JENSEN-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 898, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/28JENSEN-1/JENSEN-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/28JENSEN-1/JENSEN-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Textured Palette Without Much Paint', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Textured Palette Without Much Paint', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords