.com/2011/01/27/the-other-clippers/', 'snippet': 'Blake Griffin and Eric Gordon may steal the show, but the Clippers’ future is bright in part due to three other intriguing young players.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s become something of a sports media ritual to pay daily tribute to Blake Griffin, and with the resurgent Clippers now winning their fair share of games, the importance of Eric Gordon has become a bit more celebrated. Together, Griffin and Gordon comprise a nucleus with enormous promise; both players are top-15 scorers on a per-game basis (and very nearly on a per-minute basis, where Gordon ranks 10th and Griffin 16th among all players with at least 500 minutes played), and their overall efficiency creates a stable foundation on which the Clippers can build. For the first time in a long while, Los Angeles’ other team can be approached with optimism.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Other Clippers', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bledsoe, Eric', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gordon, Eric', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Griffin, Blake', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Clippers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T00:16:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Mahoney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mahoney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1f8997e-91a1-5dde-972b-4e8f679e7403', 'word_count': 1487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1f8997e-91a1-5dde-972b-4e8f679e7403'}, {'abstract': 'A behavioral economist and a sportswriter take on some of the most cherished myths in sports.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/books/review/Weber-t.html', 'snippet': 'A behavioral economist and a sportswriter take on some of the most cherished myths in sports.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Defense doesn’t win championships. Teamwork isn’t what it’s cracked up to be. Momentum is a myth. And the Chicago Cubs aren’t cursed; they just stink.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Weber-t_CA0/Weber-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Weber-t_CA0/Weber-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Weber-t_CA0/Weber-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Game Theory', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Game Theory', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Statistics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wertheim, L Jon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T00:18:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Weber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': N