lican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T02:55:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/aadae9e6-10bd-5594-90cf-2298958d818e', 'word_count': 810, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aadae9e6-10bd-5594-90cf-2298958d818e'}, {'abstract': 'We find it curious that the Conan Doyle Estate has commissioned a new novel featuring the character Sherlock Holmes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/opinion/28fri4.html', 'snippet': 'We find it curious that the Conan Doyle Estate has commissioned a new novel featuring the character Sherlock Holmes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At a mid-January meeting of the Sherlock Holmes Society in London \x97 held in the Members’ Dining Room at the House of Commons, no less \x97 the English writer Anthony Horowitz announced that he has been commissioned by the Conan Doyle Estate to write a new novel featuring the venerable detective. According to Mr. Horowitz’s publisher, the title is a “closely guarded secret” and the prologue is “under lock and key.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Authorized Sleuth', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Authorized Sleuth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holmes, Sherlock (Fictional Character)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DOYLE, SIR ARTHUR CONAN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Horowitz, Anthony', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T02:55:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb8d0a19-01fa-501f-b916-0e6aaa2be7f0', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb8d0a19-01fa-501f-b916-0e6aaa2be7f0'}, {'abstract': 'Every year, the Green Bay Packers release a financial report that tells the story of a small-market team that benefits from a huge fan base and the league’s big deals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/sports/football/28packers.html', 'snippet': 'Every year, the Green Bay Packers release a financial report that tells the story of a small-market team that benefits from a huge fan base and the league’s big deals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every year, the Green Bay Packers do something unique in the N.F.L.: they release a financial report. The document is brief but revealing, telling the story of a small-market team that benefits enormously from the league’s huge television deals and the devotion of its fans.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/sports/JP-PACKERS/JP-PACKERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/sports/JP-PACKERS/JP-PACKERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/28/sports/JP-PACKERS/JP-PACKERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'N.F.L. Finances, as Seen Through Packers’ Records', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'N.F.L. Finances, As Seen Through Packers’ Records', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Bay Packers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T02:57:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ee55f08-e5f5-5f76-9e0e-571483bd62b3', 'word_count': 893, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ee55f08-e5f5-5f76-9e0e-571483bd62b3'}, {'abstract': 'To the moon, Kevin Der.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/27/rainbows/', 'snippet': 'To the moon, Kevin Der.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRIDAY’S PUZZLE — Ready for this? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday: Like the Bay of Rainbows', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T03:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/789335b9-8c50-5fe6-8f79-5fa3e9397875', 'word_count': 407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/789335b9-8c50-5fe6-8f79-5fa3e9397875'}, {'abstract': 'How to make a megafortune as a painter? Go where competition is scarce but vanity and new money are not.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/magazine/30Vicari-t.html', 'snippet': 'How to make a megafortune as a painter? Go where competition is scarce but vanity and new money are not.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The thing about being a painter,” Andrew Vicari, who has claims to being the most lavishly rewarded painter in the world, was saying, “is that every night you go to bed thinking the work you have done that day is fabulous. And then you wake up the next morning and look at your canvas and think it is worthless, a piece of junk, and you start again.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/magazine/30Vicari-span/30Vicari-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 382, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/30/magazine/30Vicari-span/30Vicari-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 382}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/magazine/30Vicari-span/30Vicari-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/magazine/30Vicari-span/30Vicari-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/magazine/30Vicari-span/30Vicari-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rembrandt of Riyadh', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Rembrandt of Riyadh', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Riyadh (Saudi Arabia)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T03:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Adams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Adams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae67a69d-b3c8-58d3-ad5a-8d979fa0ff22', 'word_count': 2785, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae67a69d-b3c8-58d3-ad5a-8d979fa0ff22'}, {'abstract': '“The Old Masters,” by Simon Gray, depicts a showdown in Italy in 1937 between the art historian Bernard Berenson and the leading art dealer Joseph Duveen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/theater/reviews/28old.html', 'snippet': '“The Old Masters,” by Simon Gray, depicts a showdown in Italy in 1937 between the art historian Bernard Berenson and the leading art dealer Joseph Duveen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW HAVEN — Simon Gray’s drama “The Old Masters,” which is having its American premiere at the Long Wharf Theater here, could be described as the quintessential middlebrow West End play. The attributes of this durable genre include characters drawn from the learned classes, preferably by way of the history books; dialogue that’s sophisticated, amusing and well turned without being too vulgar or, well, challenging; swanky sets and period costumes capable of inspiring reveries about antiquing; and nothing in the way of innovation. A scene featuring the serving of tea is preferable, if optional.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/28Old/Old-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/28Old/Old-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/28Old/Old-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Showdown at the Italian Villa, Easels Drawn', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'The Old Masters'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Showdown at the Italian Villa, Easels Drawn', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Long Wharf Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gray, Simon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T03:01:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0f9b43b-af80-5de5-a5bb-8b5e7bdb3a9d', 'word_count': 1010, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0f9b43b-af80-5de5-a5bb-8b5e7bdb3a9d'}, {'abstract': 'Citizen suits against the City of Chicago have decreased since the city adopted a policy to fight all suits seeking less than $100,000.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/us/28cncpolice.html', 'snippet': 'Citizen suits against the City of Chicago have decreased since the city adopted a policy to fight all suits seeking less than $100,000.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After a Chicago police officer fatally shot Beverly Thurman’s dog, Roscoe, after getting a call about a loose pit bull, Ms. Thurman filed a lawsuit alleging that the officer had acted wrongfully.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fighting Suits Saves Money for Chicago', 'kicker': 'Chicago News Cooperative', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fighting Suits Saves Money For Chicago', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T03:08:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kari Lydersen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lydersen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/91734a1a-44e5-5908-af15-8321b7db2b41', 'word_count': 581, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91734a1a-44e5-5908-af15-8321b7db2b41'}, {'abstract': 'The Chicago Bears were never in danger of beating the Green Bay Packers on Sunday even if substitute quarterback Caleb Hanie had played the entire second half.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/sports/football/28cncsports.html', 'snippet': 'The Chicago Bears were never in danger of beating the Green Bay Packers on Sunday even if substitute quarterback Caleb Hanie had played the entire second half.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So Caleb Hanie can play, maybe, and Todd Collins cannot, beyond all shadow of any doubt. The Bears were hoping for more than that tepid revelation from Sunday’s heralded showdown with the Green Bay Packers, what with the N.F.C. Championship and a trip to the Super Bowl on the line, but on the bright side, the backup quarterback discovery kept their 21-14 defeat from being a total loss.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25B', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/us/28CNCSPORTS/28CNCSPORTS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 709, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reality Finally Caught Up to the Bears. Now, Moving On.', 'kicker': 'Chicago News Cooperative', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'SPORTS; Reality Finally Caught Up to the Bears. Now, Moving On.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago Bears', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T03:09:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa249699-9e88-5560-8ff1-942b9518aefb', 'word_count': 903, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa249699-9e88-5560-8ff1-942b9518aefb'}, {'abstract': 'Mississippi State and its sophomore star center, Renardo Sidney, have failed to live up to expectations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/sports/ncaabasketball/28sidney.html', 'snippet': 'Mississippi State and its sophomore star center, Renardo Sidney, have failed to live up to expectations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ATHENS, Ga. \x97 Renardo Sidney resembled a standby, somebody to call into action every once in a while, just not on every possession. Listed at 6 feet 10 inches and 2