dia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Buzz | Fight for Life After BMW Sale Goes Bad', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T13:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb57e4b5-4fc9-5409-be4c-f02398fd9dac', 'word_count': 883, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb57e4b5-4fc9-5409-be4c-f02398fd9dac'}, {'abstract': 'Advertisers like Visa are buying commercial time before, during and after Super Bowl XLV, reflecting the improvement in the economy.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/super-bowl-advertisers-are-super-busy/', 'snippet': 'Advertisers like Visa are buying commercial time before, during and after Super Bowl XLV, reflecting the improvement in the economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Each year, it seems that the efforts by marketers to gain attention for what they are doing on Super Bowl Sunday start earlier than the year before. It is not unlike how reporters write each year that retailers seem to be starting the Christmas shopping season earlier than the year before.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Super Bowl Advertisers Are Super Busy', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Super Bowl', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T13:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ff86298-b4e5-59a2-8b50-9cfb60c8933b', 'word_count': 596, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ff86298-b4e5-59a2-8b50-9cfb60c8933b'}, {'abstract': 'Caregiving obligations are deeply felt in many Hispanic families, but tradition is running up against harsh modern realities, experts say.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/caregiving-struggles-for-hispanic-families/', 'snippet': 'Caregiving obligations are deeply felt in many Hispanic families, but tradition is running up against harsh modern realities, experts say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fabiola Santiago lives in the Miami suburbs, two miles from her 81-year-old mother and 87-year-old father, whom she looks after. As the single mother of three grown daughters, she might live in the city or in Miami Beach if she had no family responsibilities — but to her, that’s like saying she’d fly if she woke up tomorrow with wings. It’s a fantasy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Strains for Hispanic Caregivers', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hispanic-Americans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T13:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Stabiner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stabiner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/118376f8-1a3e-5021-907d-485bb080e6c6', 'word_count': 724, 'uri': 'nyt://article/118376f8-1a3e-5021-907d-485bb080e6c6'}, {'abstract': 'Rank Group of New Zealand bought the parent of Prestone antifreeze in a $950 million deal.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/honeywell-sells-consumer-auto-products-business/', 'snippet': 'Rank Group of New Zealand bought the parent of Prestone antifreeze in a $950 million deal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Honeywell announced on Friday that had agreed to sell its automotive consumer products group to the Rank Group, a New Zealand private investment firm, in cash deal valued at $950 million. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Honeywell Sells Consumer Auto Products Business', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Honeywell International Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rank Group', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T13:08:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/74615202-1bcc-5350-a183-5e3258749029', 'word_count': 183, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74615202-1bcc-5350-a183-5e3258749029'}, {'abstract': 'Athletes have been known to complain that reporters misquote them. With Twitter, athletes can cut out the middleman and avoid being misunderstood. No downside, right?', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/athlete-thine-enemy-is-twitter/', 'snippet': 'Athletes have been known to complain that reporters misquote them. With Twitter, athletes can cut out the middleman and avoid being misunderstood. No downside, right?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Athletes have been known to complain that reporters misquote them or spin their statements.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Athlete, Thine  Enemy Is Twitter', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cromartie, Antonio', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T13:17:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1400e9e-f36c-59a4-af2d-f16a3d406f7e', 'word_count': 227, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1400e9e-f36c-59a4-af2d-f16a3d406f7e'}, {'abstract': 'The dam will produce electricity for about 23 million people in  major cities but flood roughly 200 square miles of land in the Amazon, displacing indigenous peoples.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/brazilian-dam-clears-hurdle/', 'snippet': 'The dam will produce electricity for about 23 million people in  major cities but flood roughly 200 square miles of land in the Amazon, displacing indigenous peoples.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A significant hurdle in the construction of a huge dam on a tributary of the Amazon river was removed this week when Brazilian authorities approved the clearing of hundreds of acres of forest at the dam site and the construction of new roads to service the project.', 'source': 'Th