urce': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wheeee! It’s the Sled-Cam!', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T15:04:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/46d52f4a-21f8-5dda-9c6d-7fc8653109a0', 'word_count': 24, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46d52f4a-21f8-5dda-9c6d-7fc8653109a0'}, {'abstract': 'Don’t run on.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/mega-comments/', 'snippet': 'Don’t run on.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just a reminder: very long comments (rough rule is 3 inches on my screen, but it’s going to be subjective) will not be posted. I’m only saying this because the mega-comments, both positive and negative, seem to be making a comeback. If your comment wasn’t posted, that’s probably the reason.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mega-comments', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T15:08:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ae1c160-390e-5f31-aa7f-3dd2b2368484', 'word_count': 50, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ae1c160-390e-5f31-aa7f-3dd2b2368484'}, {'abstract': 'M.A.C. cosmetics has teamed up with Warner Bros. to sell a “Wonder Woman”-inspired collection, now exclusively available at M.A.C.’s Spring Street pop-up store.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/vain-glorious-hero-product/', 'snippet': 'M.A.C. cosmetics has teamed up with Warner Bros. to sell a “Wonder Woman”-inspired collection, now exclusively available at M.A.C.’s Spring Street pop-up store.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hot on the (red, pleather) heels of NBC’s announcement that it has picked up David E. Kelley’s “Wonder Woman”-based television pilot, M.A.C. cosmetics just opened a pop-up store on Spring Street to exclusively sell its new “Wonder Woman”-inspired collection, a partnership with Warner Bros. Outfitted with giant renderings of the character and reproductions of vintage comic books, the space is the only place to shop the limited-edition line until Feb. 10, when it hits M.A.C. locations nationwide. Pick up a nail polish matched to the shade worn by the superhero, false lashes, reflex glitter pigments or a supersize Lipglass, all bearing the “Wonder Woman” logo. “There’s something about ‘Wonder Woman’ that speaks to a lot of generations,” says the creative director and senior vice president of M.A.C. worldwide, James Gager, pointing out that the character graced the premiere cover of Ms. Magazine. “She’s an icon.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vain Glorious | Hero Product', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T15:21:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Siegel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Siegel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e19c7215-7d0f-5c93-89a1-54769a2db9fe', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e19c7215-7d0f-5c93-89a1-54769a2db9fe'}, {'abstract': 'Downgrades past, and their irrelevance.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/japanese-bond-amnesia/', 'snippet': 'Downgrades past, and their irrelevance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amid all the stories about the S&P downgrade of Japan, I’ve seen hardly any mentions of the fact that Japan was downgraded below Botswana in 2002. And here was the effect on the interest rate:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Japanese Bond Amnesia', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T15:23:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/72463d3d-ccdd-53a5-938c-564c37b33896', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72463d3d-ccdd-53a5-938c-564c37b33896'}, {'abstract': 'More free Internet calling options, a fierce defense of the banks and other consumer-focused items from Friday’s Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/friday-reading-more-internet-calling-options/', 'snippet': 'More free Internet calling options, a fierce defense of the banks and other consumer-focused items from Friday’s Times.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Reading: More Internet Calling Options', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T15:23:43+0000'