metaphor that applies best is, obviously, the crunchy taco.', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/automobiles/Hyundai-A-National/Hyundai-A-National-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 294, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/30/automobiles/Hyundai-A-National/Hyundai-A-National-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 294}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/automobiles/Hyundai-A-National/Hyundai-A-National-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 501, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/automobiles/Hyundai-A-National/Hyundai-A-National-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/automobiles/Hyundai-A-National/Hyundai-A-National-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Crowd-Pleasing Composition in 3 Movements', 'kicker': 'Behind the Wheel | 2011 Hyundai Sonata', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Crowd-Pleasing Composition in 3 Movements', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reviews', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hyundai Motor Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T15:37:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'subsection_name': 'New Cars', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Pearley Huffman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'Pearley', 'lastname': 'Huffman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/19c37d1e-a2f1-5df3-bd46-f83930f4dff9', 'word_count': 1310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19c37d1e-a2f1-5df3-bd46-f83930f4dff9'}, {'abstract': 'At what point in the election cycle does a president’s approval rating become a reliable predictor of his or her re-election chances? We aren’t there yet  — but there is still much to glean from examining the data.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/approval-ratings-and-re-election-odds/', 'snippet': 'At what point in the election cycle does a president’s approval rating become a reliable predictor of his or her re-election chances? We aren’t there yet  — but there is still much to glean from examining the data.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Earlier this month, we posted the simple version of a finding, based on the historical record, that is worth keeping in mind when you read articles about how Barack Obama’s presidency has (or has not been) been revitalized: It’s just too soon for his approval ratings to tell us very much about his re-election prospects for 2012.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Approval Ratings and Re-Election Odds', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T15:40:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/52863139-f452-5ce6-81b6-035b6a2b479a', 'word_count': 1561, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52863139-f452-5ce6-81b6-035b6a2b479a'}, {'abstract': 'Comprehensive auto-management apps can help you save money.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/app-smart-extra-auto-management/', 'snippet': 'Comprehensive auto-management apps can help you save money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You can save a few pennies per gallon (or maybe much more) with the apps featured in Thursday’s App Smart column. But drivers who are diligent enough to shop around for gas will likely find even greater use in a pair of more comprehensive auto-management apps for iPhone and Android users.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'App Smart Extra: Auto-Management', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T15:43:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bob Tedeschi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tedeschi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c9d4cc2-be63-5a30-aca6-c908813998f7', 'word_count': 164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c9d4cc2-be63-5a30-aca6-c908813998f7'}, {'abstract': 'Lighthouse International is once again showing all 10 nominated live-action and animated shorts nominated the week before the Oscars.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/big-themes-in-small-packages/', 'snippet': 'Lighthouse International is once again showing all 10 nominated live-action and animated shorts nominated the week before the Oscars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With all the hoopla over Oscar nominations for “The King’s Speech” and “True Grit,” the nominees in the shorter film categories may have escaped your attention, but not that of movie executives. For one thing, a nominated film can launch a career. Consider John Lasseter, who received a nomination for best animated short in 1987 for “Luxo Jr.,” about a young lamp and his father, which was Pixar’s first attempt at animation, or Taylor Hackford, who won the live action short in 1979 for “Teenage Father” and went on to a successful career and Oscar nominations for best picture and direction for “Ray.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Themes in Small Packages', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hackford, Taylor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lasseter, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lighthouse International', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T15:50:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ab01719-678f-5069-ae7d-1edb2eb916b0', 'word_count': 554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ab01719-678f-5069-ae7d-1edb2eb916b0'}, {'abstract': 'Try pronouncing your acronyms before committing.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/obamas-creep-problem/', 'snippet': 'Try pronouncing your acronyms before committing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As an aging baby boomer, I remember the 1970s; and one of the things I remember is that it was the era of unfortunate acronyms. Nixon had the Committee to Re-Elect the President, which everyone, and I mean everyone, referred to as CREEP. Ford had the Council on Wage and Price Stability, which everyone referred to as COWPS, pronounced phonetically.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama’s CREEP Problem', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T15:53:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5710f762-3d68-5b84-b637-71c2bf0636d2', 'word_count': 98, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5710f762-3d68-5b84-b637-71c2bf0636d2'}, {'abstract': 'One hundred fun and creative ideas for celebrating the 100th day of school, with The New York Times and other resources.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/100-ways-to-celebrate-the-100th-day-of-school-with-the-new-york-times/', 'snippet': 'One hundred fun and creative ideas for celebrating the 100th day of school, with The New York Times and other resources.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Depending on when you started the academic year — and how many snow days you’ve had so far — the 100th day of school should be coming up any day now.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '100 Ways to Celebrate the 100th Day of School With The Times', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T15:54:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten, Shannon Doyne and Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/94fed261-220e-5463-a2c1-df828818d24a', 'word_count': 3096, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94fed261-220e-5463-a2c1-df828818d24a'}, {'abstract': 'Andy Murray’s 4-6, 7-6 (2), 6-1, 7-6 (2) victory was filled with extended rallies and memorable defense, but the differences in the end were Murray’s bigger serve and bolder tactics', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/29/sports/tennis/29tennis.html', 'snippet': 'Andy Murray’s 4-6, 7-6 (2), 6-1, 7-6 (2) victory was filled with extended rallies and memorable defense, but the differences in the end were Murray’s bigger serve and bolder tactics', 'lead_paragraph': 'MELBOURNE, Australia — Andy Murray and Novak Djokovic were 13 when they first played tennis against each other. It was in the southern French city of Tarbes in the junior tournament called Les Petits As. Murray won in a hurry.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/sports/29murray2/29murray2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 694, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/sports/29murray2/29murray2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/29/sports/29murray2/29murray2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Murray Beats Ferrer to Reach Final', 'kicker': 'Australian Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Grand Slam Final Disguised as a Friendly Rivalry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murray, Andy (1987- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Australian Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ferrer, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T15:58:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Clarey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clarey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68749fa5-4754-5964-a88f-479d76462d2b', 'word_count': 1002, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68749fa5-4754-5964-a88f-479d76462d2b'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Connery, the actor and James Bond portrayer, was honored with a bust in his likeness, unveiled Thursday in the Estonian capital, Tallinn.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/from-estonia-with-love-a-sculpture-honoring-sean-connery/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Connery, the actor and James Bond portrayer, was honored with a bust in his likeness, unveiled Thursday in the Estonian capital, Tallinn.', 'lead_paragraph': ' There is, apparently, a distinction even rarer than being granted a license to kill by Her Majesty’s Secret Service, and that is having a bronze sculpture of your likeness displayed in the Estonian capital, Tallinn. Sean Connery is now that most exceptional of actors who can claim both honors: on Thursday, a bust depicting the well-preserved star of seven James Bond films (not to mention “The Hunt for Red October,” “Highlander” and “The Untouchables”) was unveiled outside Tallinn’s Scottish Club, an organization for Scottish expatriates and admirers of all things Scottish in Estonia, The Associated Press reported. Peter Carter, Britain’s ambassador to Estonia, said at the unveiling of the $14,000 sculpture that Mr. Connery was “without a doubt, an icon,” adding: “He is variously known as James Bond or the sexiest man of the century. He’s a great British actor, a great Scot actor and a great symbol for Scotland.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Estonia With Love: A Sculpture Honoring Sean Connery', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bond, James (Fictional Character)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Connery, Sean', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Estonia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tallinn (Estonia)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T16:02:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/196122f2-43ec-5696-8a74-a275ccd9cc53', 'word_count': 209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/196122f2-43ec-5696-8a74-a275ccd9cc53'}, {'abstract': 'In its report to the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration, the air-conditioner manufacturer attributed the problem to a loose fuse.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/carrier-bus-air-conditioners-are-recalled-after-fires/', 'snippet': 'In its report to the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration, the air-conditioner manufacturer attributed the problem to a loose fuse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After receiving reports of fires, the Carrier Corporation is recalling almost 45,000 air-conditioning units used on school and shuttle buses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Carrier Bus Air-Conditioners Are Recalled After Fires', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ducati', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LEXUS DIVISION OF TOYOTA MOTOR SALES USA', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Highway Traffic Safety Administration', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T16:07:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/4132998c-3bfe-53fa-909d-ed10fa028d04', 'word_count': 468, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4132998c-3bfe-53fa-909d-ed10fa028d04'}, {'abstract': 'In the late 19th century, a cluster of buildings along or near Park Row, close to City Hall and the courts, housed almost every daily newspaper in town.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/29/nyregion/29metjournal.html', 'snippet': 'In the late 19th century, a cluster of buildings along or near Park Row, close to City Hall and the courts, housed almost every daily newspaper in town.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the late 19th century, a cluster of buildings along or near Park Row, close to those busy news engines, City Hall and the city’s courts, housed almost every daily new