 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fb7ab31-4a98-50e5-a5ad-93eee2c6a785', 'word_count': 1224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fb7ab31-4a98-50e5-a5ad-93eee2c6a785'}, {'abstract': 'The United States economy is larger than it has ever been, thanks in large part to government spending. As measured by G.D.P., no major member of the European Union has managed to do that.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/the-american-economy-bigger-than-ever/', 'snippet': 'The United States economy is larger than it has ever been, thanks in large part to government spending. As measured by G.D.P., no major member of the European Union has managed to do that.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’re back.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The American Economy: Bigger Than Ever', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T16:36:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/016c04a5-dff0-588e-9a95-925e03308a2c', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/016c04a5-dff0-588e-9a95-925e03308a2c'}, {'abstract': 'The United States economy is larger than it has ever been, thanks in large part to government spending. As measured by G.D.P., no major member of the European Union has managed to do that.', 'web_url': 'https://norris.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/the-american-economy-bigger-than-ever/', 'snippet': 'The United States economy is larger than it has ever been, thanks in large part to government spending. As measured by G.D.P., no major member of the European Union has managed to do that.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’re back.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The American Economy: Bigger Than Ever', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T16:36:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/40f2dd6f-bf40-54bc-990f-bb5d7315fda8', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40f2dd6f-bf40-54bc-990f-bb5d7315fda8'}, {'abstract': 'Slacking off; cleaning out.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/magazine/30FOB-Ethicist-t.html', 'snippet': 'Slacking off; cleaning out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My recycling is collected weekly, almost always by the same driver. I actually watch the pickup about 50 percent of the time, as I have two young boys who like to watch the truck. The driver frequently stops to talk on his cellphone — one time talking for five minutes in the truck while it idled in front of my house — and has taken magazines from my recycling and spent a minute or two reading them. Should I report this to the carting company, or is it none of my business how he gets his job done? M.S., HIGHLAND PARK, ILL.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/03/18/magazine/ethicist75/ethicist75-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2010/03/18/magazine/ethicist75/ethicist75-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Doubting Others’ Job Performance', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Doubting Others’ Job Performance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T16:38:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/558de133-d86d-5daa-a274-c9be0dc7cbc4', 'word_count': 708, 'uri': 'nyt://article/558de133-d86d-5daa-a274-c9be0dc7cbc4'}, {'abstract': 'Why “Sunday Night Football” is the third-most-popular program on television — among women.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/magazine/30FOB-wwln-t.html', 'snippet': 'Why “Sunday Night Football” is the third-most-popular program on television — among women.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the N.F.L. season began this past September, it appeared as if the sport’s cultural prominence — no other league is anywhere close to as popular or rich — might be in jeopardy. The league was plagued by scandal. The Pittsburgh Steelers, for one, were forced to play their first four games without quarterback Ben Roethlisberger, who was disciplined after being accused of sexually assaulting a college student in the bathroom of a bar. The New York Jets, meanwhile, were busy hooting at the television journalist Inés Sainz on the field and in the locker room — a flap that, in hindsight, seems charming relative to the lewd text messages that onetime Jets employees received from former quarterback Brett Favre (reportedly involving a photographed penis) and the supposed foot-fetishism of the Jets’ current head coach, Rex Ryan.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/magazine/30FOB-wwln-span/30FOB-wwln-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 441, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/30/magazine/30FOB-wwln-span/30FOB-wwln-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 441}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/magazine/30FOB-wwln-span/30FOB-wwln-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 854, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/magazine/30FOB-wwln-span/30FOB-wwln-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/magazine/30FOB-wwln-span/30FOB-wwln-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gridiron Girls', 'kicker': 'The Way We Live Now', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The XX Blitz', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T16:40:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1bd53bef-bf4d-5966-a12b-ae41b56f7b22', 'word_count': 978, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bd53bef-bf4d-5966-a12b-ae41b56f7b22'}, {'abstract': 'Why some fans of a new gizmo are eager to misuse it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/magazine/30FOB-Consumed-t.html', 'snippet': 'Why some fans of a new gizmo are eager to misuse it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last September, at one of those periodic events where Apple offers up a new product with much fanfare, Steve Jobs invited the audience to join him in admiring the latest iteration of the iPod Nano. With the touch-screen interface associated with the iPhone and the iPad, the music player had shrunk to a square, weighing less than an ounce and almost comically wee. Some observers took it for a joke when Jobs said an Apple board member intended to sport the thing as a watch. Others didn’t laugh; they sprang into action.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/magazine/30FOB-Consumed/30FOB-Consumed-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 206, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/magazine/30FOB-Consumed/30FOB-Consumed-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/magazine/30FOB-Consumed/30FOB-Consumed-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The iPod Gets the Dick Tracy Treatment', 'kicker': 'Consumed', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Time-Honored', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kickstarter', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPod', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T16:41:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Walker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Walker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60d0778f-b580-5c72-85aa-38db7e3381b7', 'word_count': 859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60d0778f-b580-5c72-85aa-38db7e3381b7'}, {'abstract': 'Despite upheaval, a Goldman Sachs partner holds her cards close.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/magazine/30FOB-Q4-t.html', 'snippet': 'Despite upheaval, a Goldman Sachs partner holds her cards close.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a partner at Goldman Sachs and one of the best-known market analysts in this country, you’re surely aware that women continue to be thinly represented among the senior ranks on Wall Street. Why is that? Let’s think about it in the following way: We can only deal with the pool of young women who make themselves available and express interest in investment banking.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/magazine/30FOB-Q4/30FOB-Q4-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 767, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Steady Hand', 'kicker': 'Questions for Abby Joseph Cohen', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Steady Hand', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohen, Abby Joseph', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T16:41:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'INTERVIEW BY Deborah Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Deborah', 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/a355706f-66df-55a8-b366-0f182ec4d433', 'word_count': 664, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a355706f-66df-55a8-b366-0f182ec4d433'}, {'abstract': 'What explains the zealous debate over screen protectors?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/magazine/30FOB-medium-t.html', 'snippet': 'What explains the zealous debate over screen protectors?', 'lead_paragraph': 'You never know what will hit a nerve with technophiles. The screen protector — a filmy, staticky sheet that covers the screen of a tablet or smartphone — would seem to be a fairly noncontroversial accessory. But people who love their smartphones and tablets are divided over it. The screen protectors, manufactured by companies like Zagg and Splash, really bring out the emotions in gadget fans, especially those of ardent devotees of a certain hallowed tech company based in Cupertino, Calif.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/magazine/30FOB-medium-span/30FOB-medium-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/30/magazine/30FOB-medium-span/30FOB-medium-t