ork Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/arts/Counter/Counter-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 906, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/arts/Counter/Counter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/29/arts/Counter/Counter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Vigilance Never Sleeps', 'kicker': "Television Review | 'Counterterror NYC'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where Vigilance Never Sleeps', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Geographic Channel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:07:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf3fe8c6-0133-5837-bd39-d645a7410288', 'word_count': 583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf3fe8c6-0133-5837-bd39-d645a7410288'}, {'abstract': 'The Florida lender, whose public offering was priced at $27, received a warm reception on Friday morning, as shares increased to $28.25 in the first few hours of trading.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/bankunited-shares-rise-5-on-first-day/', 'snippet': 'The Florida lender, whose public offering was priced at $27, received a warm reception on Friday morning, as shares increased to $28.25 in the first few hours of trading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BankUnited’s public offering received a warm reception on Friday morning, as shares rose nearly 5 percent to around $28.25 in the first few hours of trading.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'BankUnited Shares Rise 5% on First Day', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ross, Wilbur L Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BankUnited', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Blackstone Group, The', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carlyle Group', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:08:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/219165b8-2bf0-59cd-8688-ef3412ddcbc3', 'word_count': 373, 'uri': 'nyt://article/219165b8-2bf0-59cd-8688-ef3412ddcbc3'}, {'abstract': 'The Hyundai Sonata Hybrid was engineered to level the playing field a bit for drivers who spend more time commuting on the highway than sitting in stop-and-go traffic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/automobiles/30HYBRID.html', 'snippet': 'The Hyundai Sonata Hybrid was engineered to level the playing field a bit for drivers who spend more time commuting on the highway than sitting in stop-and-go traffic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HIGHWAY driving is not where hybrids shine. Typically, their fuel economy gains are most impressive in town, where low-speed driving is powered by electricity and efficiency is aided by a regenerative braking system. ', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/automobiles/Hybrid/Hybrid-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 585, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Hybrid Made for the Highway', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Hybrid Made for the Highway', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hyundai Motor Co', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hybrid Vehicles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:16:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tudor Van Hampton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tudor', 'middlename': 'Van', 'lastname': 'Hampton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e1470c4-1516-5ca2-9692-6da7d1429db3', 'word_count': 503, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e1470c4-1516-5ca2-9692-6da7d1429db3'}, {'abstract': 'Taking stock of four new memoirs — and of the motives for adding to an already crowded genre.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/books/review/Genzlinger-t.html', 'snippet': 'Taking stock of four new memoirs — and of the motives for adding to an already crowded genre.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A moment of silence, please, for the lost art of shutting up.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/30genzlinger/30genzlinger-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 158, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/30genzlinger/30genzlinger-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 158}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/30genzlinger/30genzlinger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/30genzlinger/30genzlinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/30genzlinger/30genzlinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Problem With Memoirs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Center of Attention', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shawn, Allen', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bell, Anthea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:16:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d09a5e0-3721-59c3-83a5-5dfbc7d2dee0', 'word_count': 1491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d09a5e0-3721-59c3-83a5-5dfbc7d2dee0'}, {'abstract': 'Looking back at their teenage years, a novel’s narrators expose a town’s collective guilt.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/books/review/Gilmore-t.html', 'snippet': 'Looking back at their teenage years, a novel’s narrators expose a town’s collective guilt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oh, the infectious nature of a “we” narration, the perils of a collective voice. Almost by its very nature this is a point of view infused with nostalgia, looking back to a time when “we” were not yet the individual “I” the adult world demands we become. Faulkner’s “we” in the story “A Rose for Emily” conjures up a South long gone. And in Jeffrey Eugenides’s novel “The Virgin Suicides,” which circles around a tragic event — five sisters have killed themselves when the book opens — the communal reflection on childhood veils these harrowing deaths with a gauzy filter.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Gilmore-t_CA0/Gilmore-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 779, 'width': 964, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Gilmore-t_CA0/Gilmore-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Gilmore-t_CA0/Gilmore-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Boys to Men', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Boys to Men', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:18:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Gilmore', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gilmore', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c10f648-d229-5959-a68a-b31806648398', 'word_count': 898, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c10f648-d229-5959-a68a-b31806648398'}, {'abstract': 'The CNN reporter Soledad O’Brien looks back on 22 years in TV news.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/books/review/Moaveni-t.html', 'snippet': 'The CNN reporter Soledad O’Brien looks back on 22 years in TV news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The CNN reporter Soledad O’Brien is the daughter of a white Australian father and a black Cuban mother. This heritage bequeathed her an immigrant’s ideals about America’s promise, as well as a striking appearance that has shaped her experience of “the land of possibilities.” Both these themes — her roots and her looks — run through O’Brien’s memoir, “The Next Big Story,” as she charts her lifelong effort to forge a singular identity out of her biracial background. Her account, written with Rose Marie Arce, is also a reporter’s reflection on the television news business, which in the span of her 22-year career has swung from largely ignoring issues of race to often stoking prejudice with bombastic, personality-driven coverage. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/30moaveni/30moaveni-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/30moaveni/30moaveni-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/28/arts/30moaveni/30moaveni-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Anchor', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Anchor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Brien, Soledad", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:18:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Azadeh Moaveni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Azadeh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moaveni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae1a2e3a-b142-55b2-94c2-28fec0ff9988', 'word_count': 710, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae1a2e3a-b142-55b2-94c2-28fec0ff9988'}, {'abstract': 'An exceptionally inventive neuroscientist sees the workings of the mind in evolutionary terms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/books/review/Gottlieb-t.html', 'snippet': 'An exceptionally inventive neuroscientist sees the workings of the mind in evolutionary terms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The men of old, reported Socrates, saw madness as a gift that provides knowledge or inspiration. “It was when they were mad that the prophetess at Delphi and the priestesses at Dodona achieved so much; . . . when sane they did little or nothing.” Today, insanity can still bring the gift of knowledge, but in a different manner. Much of what we know about the brain comes from seeing what happens when it is damaged, or affected in unusual ways. If the Delphic seer were to turn up tomorrow, neuroscientists would whisk her straight off into a brain scanner. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Gottlieb/Gottlieb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 815, 'width': 719, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Gottlieb/Gottlieb-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Gottlieb/Gottlieb-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Lion in the Undergrowth', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Lion in the Undergrowth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capgras Syndrome', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ramachandran, V S', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:19:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Gottlieb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gottlieb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0692e201-e113-5302-8255-99e9abddfcad', 'word_count': 1184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0692e201-e113-5302-8255-99e9abddfcad'}, {'abstract': 'Biographical sketches of early American counterfeiters and their colorful, roguish lives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/books/review/Washburn-t.html', 'snippet': 'Biographical sketches of early American counterfeiters and their colorful, roguish lives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the midst of the current economic chaos, our faith in that most modest of financial instruments — the dollar bill — has remained unchanged. Not the mass dollar, that abstract plurality, ebbing and ebbing again, but the Federal Reserve notes in your pocket. Your dollars may lose value, but at least they are real. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hot Off the Presses', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hot Off the Presses', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Counterfeit Money and Financial Instruments', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gold', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:19:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Washburn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Washburn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4de2490d-cc51-5cb0-be39-e8d3e73d71b8', 'word_count': 622, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4de2490d-cc51-5cb0-be39-e8d3e73d71b8'}, {'abstract': 'The structure in Central Park will remain a visitors’ center for a while, Bloomberg said. If Tavern reopens as a restaurant one day, it might be smaller and more modest than before.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/mayor-in-no-rush-to-respond-to-trumps-tavern-on-the-green-plan/', 'snippet': 'The structure in Central Park will remain a visitors’ center for a while, Bloomberg said. If Tavern reopens as a restaurant one day, it might be smaller and more modest than before.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At a news conference on Thursday, as reported in The New York Post, Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg threw cold water on Donald J. Trump’s plans to run Tavern on the Green. Tavern is now a visitors’ center with a few food trucks, and Mayor Bloomberg said that might be what it remains.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mayor in No Rush to Respond to Trump’s Tavern on the Green Plan', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tavern on the Green', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:20:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Fox', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fox', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a4b13dd-ea81-5f4b-87d3-4f338877b892', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a4b13dd-ea81-5f4b-87d3-4f338877b892'}, {'abstract': 'This rich and lively novel’s hero is a chronicler of Moroccan lore.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/books/review/Tepper-t.html', 'snippet': 'This rich and lively novel’s hero is a chronicler of Moroccan lore.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hassan, the “storyteller” of Joydeep Roy-Bhattacharya’s new novel, is more than just a narrator: he is a guide, a witness, a showman, a chronicler of Moroccan legend and lore. His stage is the central square of Marrakesh, Djemaa el Fna, where the myriad wonders of this great, red-walled city surround and inspire him. “All around me the city spreads out its wares — its many narratives — and I survey them as if from a high place,” he proclaims. Indeed, his vantage point allows him to draw freely the raw materials of his craft: “It is a landscape filled with allegories, where the imagination is law, and storytellers can spend entire days resuscitating mysteries.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Tepper/Tepper-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 827, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Tepper/Tepper-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Tepper/Tepper-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Walled City', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Walled City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Morocco', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Marrakesh (Morocco)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:20:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anderson Tepper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anderson', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tepper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa90f55b-9324-5c88-b9ea-4a03a767a1e3', 'word_count': 607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa90f55b-9324-5c88-b9ea-4a03a767a1e3'}, {'abstract': 'To ensure that the growing array of electronics in new cars do not become obsolete too quickly, engineers are creating vehicles that can adapt to new technologies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/automobiles/30GRADE.html', 'snippet': 'To ensure that the growing array of electronics in new cars do not become obsolete too quickly, engineers are creating vehicles that can adapt to new technologies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COMPUTERS can be upgraded with software and peripherals, and smartphones are able to add new functions by downloading an app. So why not a similar capability for the device that wields more processing power than either of those? ', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/automobiles/Upgrade-2/Upgrade-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 623, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/automobiles/Upgrade-2/Upgrade-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/automobiles/Upgrade-2/Upgrade-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pursuing Cars That Won’t Be Outdated', 'kicker': 'Technology', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pursuing Cars That Won’t Be Outdated', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:20:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', '