tion': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'One Small Step Backward', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'One Small Step Backward', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:40:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/94dd27a4-4ad1-531a-98ae-aab76ff2a7e2', 'word_count': 160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94dd27a4-4ad1-531a-98ae-aab76ff2a7e2'}, {'abstract': 'Letter in response to Melanie Thernstrom’s review of Mira Bartok’s memoir, “The Memory Palace.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/books/review/Letters-t-LIFELESSONS_LETTERS.html', 'snippet': 'Letter in response to Melanie Thernstrom’s review of Mira Bartok’s memoir, “The Memory Palace.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Life Lessons', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Life Lessons', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:40:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ff30339-b9b7-52c1-8af1-41e03319057d', 'word_count': 106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ff30339-b9b7-52c1-8af1-41e03319057d'}, {'abstract': 'Letter in response to David Orr’s review of Richard Wilbur’s new poetry collection, “Anterooms.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/books/review/Letters-t-AFORMALVIRTU_LETTERS.html', 'snippet': 'Letter in response to David Orr’s review of Richard Wilbur’s new poetry collection, “Anterooms.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Formal Virtuoso', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Formal Virtuoso', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:41:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b1e3e7c-3972-5101-9777-c7a9dedb7f96', 'word_count': 117, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b1e3e7c-3972-5101-9777-c7a9dedb7f96'}, {'abstract': 'We acquire the books on our shelves for all sorts of reasons, but try explaining that to a G-man.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/books/review/Nicholson-t.html', 'snippet': 'We acquire the books on our shelves for all sorts of reasons, but try explaining that to a G-man.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s probably time to update the list on my Facebook profile for the books I “like.” If you think that “liking” a book is a fairly nebulous and meaningless concept, you’ll get no argument from me. I made the list a couple of years back and jotted down the first few titles that came into my head (“Gravity’s Rainbow,” “The Big Sleep,” “More Pricks Than Kicks” and “The Anatomy of Melancholy,” if you must know). They weren’t selected entirely at random — they’re all books I think are great — but I didn’t spend much time pondering the selection, and on another day I might well have chosen four completely different titles.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Nicholson-t_CA0/Nicholson-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 615, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Nicholson-t_CA0/Nicholson-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Nicholson-t_CA0/Nicholson-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Perils of Literary Profiling', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Are We What We Read?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:42:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Nicholson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nicholson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5cb1e549-f22d-5978-9afe-0e74d4ff485c', 'word_count': 1178, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5cb1e549-f22d-5978-9afe-0e74d4ff485c'}, {'abstract': 'Mystery novels by C. J. Sansom, Richard A. Thompson, Michael Koryta and Erin Kelly.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/books/review/Crime-t.html', 'snippet': 'Mystery novels by C. J. Sansom, Richard A. Thompson, Michael Koryta and Erin Kelly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cold, hard truth about historical mysteries comes down to this: If you can’t trust the history, you won’t enjoy the mystery. C. J. Sansom, a British lawyer with a Ph.D. in history, proved himself the most trustworthy of authors with “Dissolution,” a mystery set during the tumultuous period when Henry VIII was dismantling England’s great monasteries and systematically pillaging their earthly treasures. Sansom has gone on to write additional historically persuasive Tudor mysteries featuring Matthew Shardlake, a hunchbacked lawyer prone to “melancholy humors,” whose official court duties are interrupted by the investigations he conducts for influential patrons.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Crime-t_CA0/Crime-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 982, 'width': 957, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Crime-t_CA0/Crime-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Crime-t_CA0/Crime-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Court of Corruption', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Court of Corruption', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sansom, C J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Erin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koryta, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:42:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fa947d3-e8c6-52df-847a-74037db064d3', 'word_count': 903, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fa947d3-e8c6-52df-847a-74037db064d3'}, {'abstract': 'A family history by a ceramics artist; memoirs by a young Oxford graduate who became a military officer and from NPR’s Michele Norris; and an academic biography of the poet Andrew Marvell.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/books/review/Buskey-t.html', 'snippet': 'A family history by a ceramics artist; memoirs by a young Oxford graduate who became a military officer and from NPR’s Michele Norris; and an academic biography of the poet Andrew Marvell.', 'lead_paragraph': ' THE HARE WITH AMBER EYES  A Family’s Century of Art and Loss.  By Edmund de Waal.  Farrar, Straus & Giroux, $26.  ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Buskey-t_CA0/Buskey-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 802, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nonfiction Chronicle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nonfiction Chronicle ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marvell, Andrew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Waal, Edmund', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:42:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Megan Buskey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Megan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buskey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6558e01e-7ca4-57b2-8db8-46bc7ae03e68', 'word_count': 1080, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6558e01e-7ca4-57b2-8db8-46bc7ae03e68'}, {'abstract': 'Claire Dederer’s “Poser,” in its third week on the nonfiction list, has lots to say about yoga and modern parenting. Just don’t ask her to demonstrate Tiger Mother pose.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/02/06/books/review/InsideList-t.html', 'snippet': 'Claire Dederer’s “Poser,” in its third week on the nonfiction list, has lots to say about yoga and modern parenting. Just don’t ask her to demonstrate Tiger Mother pose.', 'lead_paragraph': 'UPWARD TIGER: Amy Chua, whose “Battle Hymn of the Tiger Mother” climbs to No. 2 on the hardcover nonfiction list in its second week, remains Topic A in many circles. Chua recently got the obligatory roasting by Stephen Colbert, who warned any children in his audience, “Get back to that Mendelssohn concerto before she drowns your bunny!”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/06/books/review/06insidelist/06insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/06/books/review/06insidelist/06insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:42:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9610b99-789d-5adc-b40f-16342dad3c97', 'word_count': 524, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9610b99-789d-5adc-b40f-16342dad3c97'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/books/review/EdChoice-t.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EXAMINED LIVES: From Socrates to Nietzsche,  by James Miller (Farrar, Straus & Giroux, $28.) Miller shows philosophers becoming ever more inclined to reflect on their own petty failings, and suggests this makes their lives more, not less, worth studying.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice: Recent books of particular interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:42:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/68361cb2-2468-56f9-9ce9-1a650521b3b2', 'word_count': 281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68361cb2-2468-56f9-9ce9-1a650521b3b2'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/books/review/PaperRow-t.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NOAH’S COMPASS,  by Anne Tyler (Ballantine, $15.) In a succession of novels (“The Accidental Tourist,” “A Patchwork Planet”), Tyler’s male protagonists have become instantly recognizable: lonely and defeated, lost souls whose lives have stalled. Liam Pennywell, the hero of Tyler’s 18th novel, is a retired teacher who realizes that despite two marriages and three daughters, he has “experienced only the most glancing relationship with his own life.” After he is attacked by an intruder in his Baltimore home, Liam becomes fixated on recovering a few hours of lost memory.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/30paperrow/30paperrow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/30paperrow/30paperrow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:42:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/b03c4d20-b308-593e-910c-7e2cff1abf19', 'word_count': 813, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b03c4d20-b308-593e-910c-7e2cff1abf19'}, {'abstract': '“From Prada to Nada,” a retread of “Sense and Sensibility,” relies on ethnic heritage to supplant the 19th-century mores that created the tension in Jane Austen’s original story.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/29/movies/29prada.html', 'snippet': '“From Prada to Nada,” a retread of “Sense and Sensibility,” relies on ethnic heritage to supplant the 19th-century mores that created the tension in Jane Austen’s original story.', 'lead_paragraph': '“From Prada to Nada,” a rueful, occasionally touching and entirely predictable romantic comedy with a terrible title, recasts Jane Austen’s “Sense and Sensibility” as a contemporary story of Chicana empowerment. When their father’s death forces Nora and Mary Dominguez to leave the family mansion, they don’t land in a country cottage, they move into their tía Aurelia’s East Los Angeles Victorian.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/movies/29prada-span/29prada-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/29/movies/29prada-span/29prada-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/movies/29prada-span/29prada-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/movies/29prada-span/29prada-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/29/movies/29prada-span/29prada-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jane Austen, Transmigrated To Modern-Day East L.A.', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'From Prada to Nada'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jane Austen, Transmigrated To Modern-Day East L.A.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:46:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/03334a62-0cac-5b40-bea8-a3d2504681a9', 'word_count': 390, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03334a62-0cac-5b40-bea8-a3d2504681a9'}, {'abstract': 'The volunteering rate — as measured by the portion of Americans who volunteered through or for an organization at least once over the course of a year — was 26.3 percent in the year ending in September 2010.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/volunteering-rate-fell-in-2010/', 'snippet': 'The volunteering rate — as measured by the portion of Americans who volunteered through or for an organization at least once over the course of a year — was 26.3 percent in the year ending in September 2010.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The volunteering rate — as measured by the portion of Americans who volunteered through or for an organization at least once over the course of a year — fell 0.5 percentage points in the year ended September 2010, to 26.3 percent, according to a Labor Department report released this week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Volunteering Rate Fell in 2010', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Volunteers and Community Service', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:47:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5049fb7-e64f-52ad-a9e9-c78afd115217', 'word_count': 142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5049fb7-e64f-52ad-a9e9-c78afd115217'}, {'abstract': 'Davos participants like the economist Nouriel Roubini and Barry Silbert, chief executive of SecondMarket, share their views on issues like the lack of global leadership and why the I.P.O. market is languishing.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/voices-from-davos/', 'snippet': 'Davos participants like the economist Nouriel Roubini and Barry Silbert, chief executive of SecondMarket, share their views on issues like the lack of global leadership and why the I.P.O. market is languishing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland, DealBook’s Andrew Ross Sorkin chatted with attendees like the economist Nouriel Roubini and Barry Silbert, chief executive of SecondMarket, about their thoughts on important issues raised at the conference.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Voices From Davos', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Liveris, Andrew N', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roubini, Nouriel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dow Chemical Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SecondMarket Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Economic Forum', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Davos (Switzerland)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Switzerland', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:50:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/0967c9ac-3fe7-5beb-9c01-ee941338c008', 'word_count': 57, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0967c9ac-3fe7-5beb-9c01-ee941338c008'}, {'abstract': 'The trustee who represents victims of Bernard L. Madoff’s Ponzi scheme is alleging that the team’s owners knew or should have known that Madoff was operating a fraud.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/29/sports/baseball/29mets.html', 'snippet': 'The trustee who represents victims of Bernard L. Madoff’s Ponzi scheme is alleging that the team’s owners knew or should have known that Madoff was operating a fraud.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The trustee representing the victims of Bernard L. Madoff’s multibillion dollar Ponzi scheme is seeking hundreds of millions of dollars from the owners of the Mets, alleging that they, as longtime and successful investors, knew or should have known Madoff was operating a fraud, according to two lawyers involved in the case.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/sports/Mets/Mets-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/29/sports/Mets/Mets-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/sports/Mets/Mets-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/sports/Mets/Mets-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/29/sports/Mets/Mets-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Trustee Faults Mets Owners Over Madoff Fraud', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Trustee Faults Mets Owners Over Madoff Fraud', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Picard, Irving H', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Madoff, Bernard L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ponzi Schemes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilpon, Fred', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:55:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Leigh Cowan, Peter Lattman, Serge F. Kovaleski and David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': 'Leigh', 'lastname': 'Cowan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Serge', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Kovaleski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}, {'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 4}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30872dae-2a0e-56e5-99af-18c32d68b40e', 'word_count': 1253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30872dae-2a0e-56e5-99af-18c32d68b40e'}, {'abstract': 'A Yale law professor argues that under President Obama the current war on terror still betrays the principles of “just war” theory.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/books/review/Traub-t.html', 'snippet': 'A Yale law professor argues that under President Obama the current war on terror still betrays the principles of “just war” theory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the chief roles of the philosopher, from the time of Socrates, has been to discomfit citizens by forcing them to think about matters from which they would prefer to avert their eyes. Stephen L. Carter, a professor of law at Yale as well as a successful novelist, has written a book about “just war” theory in which he concludes that Barack Obama, a president he clearly admires, has prosecuted the war on terror with no more regard for the theory’s ancient principles than George W. Bush (whom many of Carter’s readers no doubt consider a war criminal). The really discomfiting part is that Carter doesn’t advance this claim as a criticism, but rather as an acknowledgment of the way America’s leaders fight wars, and are quite likely to continue fighting this one.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Traub-t_CA0/Traub-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 606, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Traub-t_CA0/Traub-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/books/review/Traub-t_CA0/Traub-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The War Presidents', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The War Presidents', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carter, Stephen L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T17:55:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Traub', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Traub', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e898519f-0267-5be2-8685-8bed7ae88521', 'word_count': 1238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e898519f-0267-5be2-8685-8bed7ae88521'}, {'abstract': 'After the suicide bombing at a Moscow airport, Parliament on Friday approved an alert system similar to the one the United States adopted after 9/11, then dropped.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/29/world/europe/29russia.html', 'snippet': 'After the suicide bombing at a Moscow airport, Parliament on Friday approved an alert system similar to the one the United States adopted after 9/11, then dropped.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW \x97 Under intense pressure to ratchet up security measures after the terrorist attack Monday at the airport here, Russian lawmakers on Friday fast-tracked the introduction of a color-coded alert system similar to the one adopted by the United States after the Sept. 11 attacks. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': []