nd Competition: Whiskers on Kittens', 'kicker': 'Schott&#039;s Vocab', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T18:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Schott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/eafcec90-37fa-57da-9ebc-69f67b2d0a73', 'word_count': 88, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eafcec90-37fa-57da-9ebc-69f67b2d0a73'}, {'abstract': 'Over 30 years, a strain of Streptococcus pneumoniae spread across the world and turned over about three quarters of its genome, researchers say.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/02/01/science/01obpneumonia.html', 'snippet': 'Over 30 years, a strain of Streptococcus pneumoniae spread across the world and turned over about three quarters of its genome, researchers say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Researchers from seven countries have collaborated to analyze how a single strain of Streptococcus pneumoniae bacteria has morphed over 30 years and spread across the world, in an attempt to overcome the development of antibiotics and vaccines. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pneumonia DNA Morphs To Dodge Vaccines', 'kicker': 'Observatory', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pneumonia DNA Morphs To Dodge Vaccines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pneumonia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T18:07:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sindya N. Bhanoo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sindya', 'middlename': 'N.', 'lastname': 'Bhanoo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1312606e-c501-5333-896e-3573d213290d', 'word_count': 278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1312606e-c501-5333-896e-3573d213290d'}, {'abstract': 'Two of the dirtiest types of heating oil would be phased out under city rules proposed on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/new-rules-would-phase-out-two-dirty-heating-oils/', 'snippet': 'Two of the dirtiest types of heating oil would be phased out under city rules proposed on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Two of the dirtiest types of heating oil would be phased out under city rules proposed on Friday: No. 6, the cheapest but most viscous type pumped into aging boilers, and another heavy oil, No. 4, which is only slightly less noxious. Both exacerbate asthma and emphysema and increase the risk of heart attack and premature death. The fuels are currently used by about 10,000 buildings in New York: Read about the move toward cleaner oil on the Green blog. »', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Rules Would Phase Out Two Dirty Heating Oils', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T18:19:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fd98084-b2d1-5c12-9867-c45dabe53e4f', 'word_count': 82, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fd98084-b2d1-5c12-9867-c45dabe53e4f'}, {'abstract': 'A patient who produces a surprising response to a time-tested question teaches a valuable lesson: It can be hard to distinguish truth from a perfectly good answer.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/a-life-lesson-learned-in-medical-school/', 'snippet': 'A patient who produces a surprising response to a time-tested question teaches a valuable lesson: It can be hard to distinguish truth from a perfectly good answer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was in my second year of medical school that I learned one of the most important lessons of my career: That it can be hard to distinguish truth from a perfectly good answer. Certainty was what I craved the most back then, poised as I was on the threshold of my medical career. But my first patient would cure me of certainty forever.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Life Lesson Learned in Medical School', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medical Schools', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T18:20:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maggie Kozel, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maggie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozel', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/892929c0-80e8-5275-846f-379f251371c5', 'word_count': 845, 'uri': 'nyt://article/892929c0-80e8-5275-846f-379f251371c5'}, {'abstract': 'How Obama can build on his State of the Union message with a speech explaining why an energy quest is necessary.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/obamas-climate-speech/', 'snippet': 'How Obama can build on his State of the Union message with a speech explaining why an 