ndscape is dramatic, but why does it compel people to rip about in boats, roll down hills in plastic balls and jump off bridges?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sure, the landscape is dramatic, but why does it compel people to rip about in boats, roll down hills in plastic balls and jump off bridges?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/travel/30COVER-slide-FQ6P/30COVER-slide-FQ6P-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/travel/30COVER-slide-FQ6P/30COVER-slide-FQ6P-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/travel/30COVER-slide-FQ6P/30COVER-slide-FQ6P-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'License to Thrill', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Zealand', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T19:55:25+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/d035488b-49dd-5d20-9588-1ae5b80db9e6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/d035488b-49dd-5d20-9588-1ae5b80db9e6'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Landesman, the chairman of the National Endowment for the Arts, addressed the problem of struggling theaters.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/landesman-comments-on-theater/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Landesman, the chairman of the National Endowment for the Arts, addressed the problem of struggling theaters.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Count on Rocco Landesman to stir the pot. Speaking at a conference about new play development at Arena Stage in Washington on Thursday, Mr. Landesman, the chairman of the National Endowment for the Arts, addressed the problem of struggling theaters. “You can either increase demand or decrease supply,” he said. “Demand is not going to increase, so it is time to think about decreasing supply.” His comment reverberated through the blogosphere. “What does he mean there’s too much supply?!?” wrote Trisha Mead, the public relations and publications manager at Portland Center Stage in Oregon. “What does he mean we can’t increase demand?!? Who determines which theater companies are wheat and which are chaff?!?” In another post, Durango Miller, a playwright and director, said: “Why not just increase funding? Maybe the N.E.A. is outdated and should be replaced by another system for funding the arts in the United States. Or maybe the people who are running the N.E.A. should be replaced.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Landesman Comments on Theater', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T19:59:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/15f9c87e-e34e-5e5e-897d-edf1ff804079', 'word_count': 315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15f9c87e-e34e-5e5e-897d-edf1ff804079'}, {'abstract': 'The indie studio, all but invisible so far at the festival, has bought the coming-of-age drama “Pariah.”', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/focus-gets-into-the-sundance-game/', 'snippet': 'The indie studio, all but invisible so far at the festival, has bought the coming-of-age drama “Pariah.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES— Focus Features, all but invisible at Sundance this year (especially in contrast to rival Fox Searchlight), finally popped up on Friday with an acquisition in the $800,000 range. Focus, which may or may not be for sale itself, bought worldwide rights to “Pariah,” a coming-of-age story centered on a 17-year-old black girl trying to come to terms with her homosexuality. Dee Rees directed the film; as part of the deal, Ms. Rees will write another script for Focus with the goal of directing it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Focus Gets into the Sundance Game', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Focus Features', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T20:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb135fd5-7d18-5686-bc4f-d8918289a1cd', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb135fd5-7d18-5686-bc4f-d8918289a1cd'}, {'abstract': 'Donald Fehr, the new executive director of the N.H.L. Players Association, speaks in depth for the first time since last summer about his legacy in baseball and labor relations, and about what lies ahead for the hockey union and the N.H.L.', 'web_url': 'https://slapshot.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/the-fehr-interview-looking-to-civil-talks-and-no-shots-across-the-bow/', 'snippet': 'Donald Fehr, the new executive director of the N.H.L. Players Association, speaks in depth for the first time since last summer about his legacy in baseball and labor relations, and about what lies ahead for the hockey union and the N.H.L.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his first in-depth interview since announcing his candidacy for the job last summer, Donald Fehr, the new executive director of the N.H.L. Players Association, said that last week’s hiring by the union of a Toronto lawyer who has opposed the N.H.L. in previous battles over franchise relocations was not the “shot across the bow” it has been described as in some quarters.In a wide-ranging discussion before he left New York for Raleigh, N.C., on Friday to attend the N.H.L.’s All-Star weekend, Fehr traced his ascendancy to the job, stressing his admiration for the N.H.L. players he has met since coming over from the directorship of the Major League Baseball Players Association, a post he held for 26 years until stepping down in 2009.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fehr Interview: Looking to Civil Talks, and No ‘Shots Across the Bow’', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bettman, Gary', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fehr, Donald', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Langenbrunner, Jamie', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Marvin', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Major League Baseball Players Assn', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Hockey League', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Raleigh (NC)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sochi (Russia)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Toronto (Ontario)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T20:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ced87508-d3db-5ec9-b2cc-6020c71f2493', 'word_count': 2931, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ced87508-d3db-5ec9-b2cc-6020c71f2493'}, {'abstract': 'An entourage of stuff follows the writer wherever she goes, and she admits that it’s bizarrely self-defeating.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/fashion/30Mirror.html', 'snippet': 'An entourage of stuff follows the writer wherever she goes, and she admits that it’s bizarrely self-defeating.', 'lead_paragraph': '“YOU need to declare independence from stuff,” my boyfriend, who travels with a single carry-on bag, one change of clothes and a lone paperback that he leaves behind on the plane, recently announced. We were standing in the street, and I had asked him to hold my store-bought cup of tea, so I could mine for my cellphone in the rubble at the bottom of my distended bag, while clutching a stack of mail with the armpit of my bagless arm. ', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/fashion/30themirror/SUB-THEMIRROR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/fashion/30themirror/30themirror-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/0