ked “like” on the Orlando Magic’s Facebook post about the release of Jason Williams 53 times in four minutes Wednesday, though what they meant may not be that clear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been a complicated career for Jason Williams, the point guard known to some as “White Chocolate,” who has morphed from troubled youngster in Sacramento, to reborn star in Memphis, to key championship cog in Miami to struggling veteran in Orlando.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s to ‘Like’ About Williams’s Release by Orlando?', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Jason', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Orlando Magic', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Florida', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:00:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Hoffman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoffman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f2549cc-0b2a-5294-af1c-8556834423ab', 'word_count': 315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f2549cc-0b2a-5294-af1c-8556834423ab'}, {'abstract': 'A slide show of photographs of cultural events from this week.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/the-week-in-culture-pictures-jan-28/', 'snippet': 'A slide show of photographs of cultural events from this week.', 'lead_paragraph': ' More Photographs', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Week in Culture Pictures, Jan. 28', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbed4959-ac42-5ccb-9c26-03fefe865216', 'word_count': 13, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbed4959-ac42-5ccb-9c26-03fefe865216'}, {'abstract': 'John Pearley Huffman found plenty to praise in the all-new Sonata.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/reviewing-the-hyundai-sonata/', 'snippet': 'John Pearley Huffman found plenty to praise in the all-new Sonata.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Sunday’s Automobiles section, John Pearley Huffman reviews the 2011 Hyundai Sonata in its three forms: hybrid, above; conventional gasoline; and turbocharged versions. Mr. Huffman found lots to praise in the all-new Sonata:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reviewing the Hyundai Sonata', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reviews', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hyundai Motor Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:01:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/a011b713-6549-5ef1-8e14-4450e64607b9', 'word_count': 90, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a011b713-6549-5ef1-8e14-4450e64607b9'}, {'abstract': 'Claudia Purita’s One Woman Wines and Vineyards in Southold produces about 2,000 cases annually. She has one full-time field worker, and friends and family members help at harvest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/nyregion/30vinesli.html', 'snippet': 'Claudia Purita’s One Woman Wines and Vineyards in Southold produces about 2,000 cases annually. She has one full-time field worker, and friends and family members help at harvest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Memories of her family’s farm and vineyard in Calabria, in southern Italy, prompted Claudia Purita to buy a 28-acre potato farm in Southold in 2002. Mrs. Purita had spent 14 years working in the kitchens of Long Island restaurants that she and her husband, Frank, owned, including Paradiso, in Rockville Centre.', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Not Quite a One-Woman Show', 'kicker': 'Long Island Vines', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Not Quite a One-Woman Show', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:02:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard G. Goldberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Goldberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e8d69e9-ce8c-50ba-9244-6f04ec52f2e1', 'word_count': 306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e8d69e9-ce8c-50ba-9244-6f04ec52f2e1'}, {'abstract': 'Ching Valdes-Aran reprises her role as an elderly immigrant missing the old country in the Ma-Yi Theater production of “Flipzoids.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/29/theater/reviews/29flip.html', 'snippet': 'Ching Valdes-Aran reprises her role as an elderly immigrant missing the old country in the Ma-Yi Theater production of “Flipzoids.”', 'lead_paragraph': '“Flipzoids,” Ralph B. Peña’s empathetic dissection of the Filipino immigrant experience, manages the neat trick of being both elegiac and stirring. These seemingly contradictory qualities stem partly from Mr. Peña’s uneven script, but primarily from a towering performance by Ching Valdes-Aran, the actress at its center.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/arts/FLIP/FLIP-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 796, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/arts/FLIP/FLIP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/29/arts/FLIP/FLIP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Their Minds, an Ocean Keeps Them Apart', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Flipzoids'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Their Minds, an Ocean Keeps Them Apart', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ma-Yi Theater Co', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Valdes-Aran, Ching', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:02:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Grode', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grode', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/717a1bab-189a-5555-8d13-14d14c366232', 'word_count': 395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/717a1bab-189a-5555-8d13-14d14c366232'}, {'abstract': 'Dilma Rousseff, the Brazilian president, said the government was committed to building 6,000 new homes to relocate hillside communities devastated by landslides this month.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/29/world/americas/29brazil.html', 'snippet': 'Dilma Rousseff, the Brazilian president, said the government was committed to building 6,000 new homes to relocate hillside communities devastated by landslides this month.', 'lead_paragraph': 'RIO DE JANEIRO \x97 Brazil’s president has committed the government to building 6,000 homes for displaced residents of the hillside communities devastated by landslides that killed at least 842 people this month.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brazil to Rebuild Houses Lost in Rains', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Homes for Displaced in Brazil', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rousseff, Dilma', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disasters and Emergencies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:06:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexei Barrionuevo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexei', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barrionuevo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/636c8d22-c45f-579b-a8a1-57e84ebd591d', 'word_count': 266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/636c8d22-c45f-579b-a8a1-57e84ebd591d'}, {'abstract': 'Every day is Valentine’s Day at Swallow, where standouts include calamari, cheese-steak sandwiches, steamed mussels and a satiny crème brûlée.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/nyregion/30dineli.html', 'snippet': 'Every day is Valentine’s Day at Swallow, where standouts include calamari, cheese-steak sandwiches, steamed mussels and a satiny crème brûlée.', 'lead_paragraph': 'YOU don’t have to wait until Feb. 14 to have a romantic dinner out. At Swallow, which opened in Huntington in October, every day is Valentine’s Day.', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/nyregion/30dineli1/30dineli1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/30/nyregion/30dineli1/30dineli1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/nyregion/30dineli1/30dineli1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 673, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Spot to Share Dishes and Romance', 'kicker': 'Long Island Dining |  Huntington', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Spot to Share Dishes and Romance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:06:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne Starkey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Starkey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd8d7c6f-5d37-53e0-91d4-a2848cd430ef', 'word_count': 709, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd8d7c6f-5d37-53e0-91d4-a2848cd430ef'}, {'abstract': 'In Queenstown, New Zealand, a high demand for extreme sports has created a booming industry for adrenaline junkies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/travel/1248069596254/adrenaline-in-new-zealand.html', 'snippet': 'In Queenstown, New Zealand, a high demand for extreme sports has created a booming industry for adrenaline junkies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Queenstown, New Zealand, a high demand for extreme sports has created a booming industry for adrenaline junkies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/28/travel/11137_1_nzadventure_75x75.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/28/travel/11137_1_nzadventure_75x75.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Adrenaline in New Zealand', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Adventure Travel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Zealand', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parachutes and Parachute Jumping', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:07:16+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'Ben Solomon | Ethan Todras-Whitehill', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': 'Solomon', 'lastname': 'Ethan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/5e37ec5b-2020-58aa-a81e-2e1dc819348b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/5e37ec5b-2020-58aa-a81e-2e1dc819348b'}, {'abstract': 'The Budapest Festival Orchestra played works by Haydn and Stravinsky at Avery Fisher Hall on Tuesday and Wednesday nights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/29/arts/music/29festival.html', 'snippet': 'The Budapest Festival Orchestra played works by Haydn and Stravinsky at Avery Fisher Hall on Tuesday and Wednesday nights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ranking artists is a risky and contentious sport. When The New York Times’s chief music critic, Anthony Tommasini, thoughtfully and painstakingly created a list of the 10 greatest composers recently, reader delight and outrage were fairly evenly mixed. A similar furor erupted in 2008 when the British classical-music magazine Gramophone ranked the top 20 orchestras in the world. After all, orchestral playing is a mix of chemistry and chance; ensembles have on and off nights in good halls and bad, and sound different depending on who is on the podium.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/arts/festival/festival-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/arts/festival/festival-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/29/arts/festival/festival-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Serious ‘Rite,’ Sultry Tango and Skillful Solos', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Serious ‘Rite,’ Sultry Tango and Skillful Solos', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Budapest Festival Orchestra', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:11:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/af62db90-d342-5205-a105-dbd66320ab6c', 'word_count': 615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af62db90-d342-5205-a105-dbd66320ab6c'}, {'abstract': 'The former president says he has no plans to raise money, campaign or appear on talk shows.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/bush-says-hes-done-with-politics/', 'snippet': 'The former president says he has no plans to raise money, campaign or appear on talk shows.', 'lead_paragraph': ' President George W. Bush says he has left politics for good and has no plans to raise money and work for Republican candidates.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bush Says He’s Done With Politics', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:11:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Southall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Southall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a7824d4-f120-5ec5-8df5-8a83cbdefe00', 'word_count': 277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a7824d4-f120-5ec5-8df5-8a83cbdefe00'}, {'abstract': 'Rustic, dark-stained pine banquettes and window casements contribute to the convivial, pubby atmosphere of Wood Creek Bar and Grill.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/nyregion/30qbitect.html', 'snippet': 'Rustic, dark-stained pine banquettes and window casements contribute to the convivial, pubby atmosphere of Wood Creek Bar and Grill.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Wood Creek Bar and Grill occupies a prominent place in the Royal Arcanum Building, a 100-year-old brick gem and one of Norfolk’s most distinctive structures. Rustic, dark-stained pine banquettes and window casements, globe-shaped sconces and walls decorated with a smattering of French bistro art all contribute to the convivial, pubby atmosphere. Yet the outfitting of white-paper-covered tables with fresh flowers and crayons underscores the family-friendly nature of the establishment.', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Focus Is on Families', 'kicker': 'Quick Bite | Norfolk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Focus Is on Families', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:12:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ded07b37-3048-5ba1-b92b-da8df61dd171', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ded07b37-3048-5ba1-b92b-da8df61dd171'}, {'abstract': '“American Idol” easily topped the night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/29/arts/television/29arts-IDOLASUSUAL_BRF.html', 'snippet': '“American Idol” easily topped the night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even with CBS and ABC broadcasting mostly reruns on Thursday, NBC and its new three-hour comedy block struggled to gain much traction in the ratings as Fox and “American Idol” easily topped the night. According to Nielsen’s estimates, “Idol” led over all with 22.3 million viewers at 8 p.m., boosting Fox’s “Bones” into the lead at 9 with 12.2 million viewers. CBS ranked second. Although NBC fared better than CBS and ABC in the demographic of viewers 18 to 49, it trailed in third place in total viewers, tied with ABC.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Idol,’ as Usual', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Idol,’ as Usual', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:13:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Toff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5d4496a-3588-5ee5-b970-a5dffefff5a5', 'word_count': 91, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5d4496a-3588-5ee5-b970-a5dffefff5a5'}, {'abstract': 'Sails American Grill has a new owner, but the chef, Nathan Kramer, is from the site’s previous occupant, the River Cat Grill.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/nyregion/30dinect.html', 'snippet': 'Sails American Grill has a new owner, but the chef, Nathan Kramer, is from the site’s previous occupant, the River Cat Grill.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’M sure I wasn’t the only one saddened by the closing of the River Cat Grill last March — or gladdened by the opening of Sails American Grill in the same spot less than nine months later. This new restaurant in Rowayton’s center has a freshly painted, crisp white interior; an understated nautical motif; and a new owner, Arthur Hooper. But Nathan Kramer, the former River Cat chef, is back at the tiller.', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/nyregion/30dinect1/30dinect1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/30/nyregion/30dinect1/30dinect1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/nyregion/30dinect1/30dinect1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 635, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fire in the Hearth and a Nautical Theme', 'kicker': 'Connecticut Dining | Rowayton', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fire in the Hearth And a Nautical Theme', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:14:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/82c625cb-d2da-5525-b4eb-fe457d1e63af', 'word_count': 785, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82c625cb-d2da-5525-b4eb-fe457d1e63af'}, {'abstract': 'The Brooklyn Rundfunk Orkestrata’s album, “The Hills Are Alive,” takes “The Sound of Music” into a new era.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/29/arts/music/29hills.html', 'snippet': 'The Brooklyn Rundfunk Orkestrata’s album, “The Hills Are Alive,” takes “The Sound of Music” into a new era.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The harmony of the Jackson 5’s hit song “ABC” is instantly recognizable as a falsetto-like voice starts “a buh-BUH buh buh-BUH,” echoed by a male chorus. But then little Michael Jackson and his brothers give way to another group of family singers, the seven Von Trapp children and their governess, Maria, and the lyrics from the Swiss Alps plow into those from Gary, Ind.:', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/arts/Jp-hills/Jp-hills-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 329, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/29/arts/Jp-hills/Jp-hills-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 329}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/arts/Jp-hills/Jp-hills-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/arts/Jp-hills/Jp-hills-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/29/arts/Jp-hills/Jp-hills-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Band Updates Some of Its Favorite Things', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Band Updates Some of Its Favorite Things', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodgers, Richard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hammerstein, Oscar II', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:16:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ce8a703-9f50-51fa-a47b-de03258a2f83', 'word_count': 1025, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ce8a703-9f50-51fa-a47b-de03258a2f83'}, {'abstract': 'Ernest Borgnine, the Oscar-winning star of “Marty” who’s getting a SAG honor this weekend, waxes nostalgic about his love of Westerns, the benefits of the studio system and his decidedly non-Method acting style.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/uncle-ernie-takes-a-bow/', 'snippet': 'Ernest Borgnine, the Oscar-winning star of “Marty” who’s getting a SAG honor this weekend, waxes nostalgic about his love of Westerns, the benefits of the studio system and his decidedly non-Method acting style.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES— On Sunday, the Screen Actors Guild will honor Ernest Borgnine for “career achievement and humanitarian accomplishment.” Mr. Borgnine, who just turned 94, has been acting for nearly 70 years, most recently appearing in “RED” opposite Bruce Willis and Helen Mirren; he won an Oscar in 1955 for “Marty.” He spoke to the Bagger from the kitchen of his home in Beverly Hills, punctuating nearly every answer with avuncular laughter, but became circumspect when discussing how the experience of movie-making has changed over the years. “Everything became money-oriented,” he said. “Hurry up, hurry up, hurry up, we don’t have any time for gaiety or anything else, let’s make the picture, make the picture and that’s it. It’s changed from being a family kind of thing to everybody trying to make a buck.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Uncle Ernie Takes a Bow', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:18:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ec42902-28ef-5734-84c5-aaae77fa4452', 'word_count': 957, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ec42902-28ef-5734-84c5-aaae77fa4452'}, {'abstract': 'CBS, Warner Brothers Television and the show’s executive producer, Chuck Lorre, said, “We are profoundly concerned for his health and well-being, and support his decision.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/two-and-a-half-men-on-hiatus-as-sheen-enters-rehab/', 'snippet': 'CBS, Warner Brothers Television and the show’s executive producer, Chuck Lorre, said, “We are profoundly concerned for his health and well-being, and support his decision.”', 'lead_paragraph': '8:52 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Two and a Half Men’ on Hiatus as Sheen Enters Rehab', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheen, Charlie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:19:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff and Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/20892535-284c-5db0-8832-bb0437d174c8', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20892535-284c-5db0-8832-bb0437d174c8'}, {'abstract': 'Chefs like Kevin Sbraga, a “Top Chef” winner, say that though appearing on reality-TV shows can be incredibly hard and life-changing, they are grateful for the opportunity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/nyregion/30dinenj.html', 'snippet': 'Chefs like Kevin Sbraga, a “Top Chef” winner, say that though appearing on reality-TV shows can be incredibly hard and life-changing, they are grateful for the opportunity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KEVIN SBRAGA, the last chef standing on last season’s installment of the Bravo cooking series ”Top Chef” and, until recently, executive chef at Rat’s Restaurant at the Grounds for Sculpture art park in Hamilton, likes being a winner.', 'print_section': 'NJ', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/nyregion/30DINENJ3/30DINENJ3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/nyregion/30DINENJ3/30DINENJ3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/nyregion/30DINENJ3/30DINENJ3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Cooks Who Compete, the Challenges of Fame', 'kicker': 'New Jersey Dining |Tv Chefs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Cooks Who Compete, The Challenges of Fame', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chefs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-28T23:20:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tammy La Gorce', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tammy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Gorce', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe491c0d-fbc6-5fbf-935b-7f5970db064e', 'word_count': 908, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe491c0d-fbc6-5fbf-935b-7f5970db064e'}, {'abstract': '“Mosheh,” which its creator Yoav Gal calls a videOpera, gets its first full production at Here Arts Center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/29/arts/music/29gal.html', 'snippet': '“Mosheh,” which its creator Yoav Gal calls a videOpera, gets its first full production at Here Arts Center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The composer Yoav Gal has been working for about a decade on “Mosheh,” his compellingly idiosyncratic meditation on Moses and his relationship to the women in his life and to God, in that order. The opera was commissioned by Merkin Concert Hall, where Mr. Gal offered a weird but tantalizing excerpt in 2003, and since then, bits of the work have turned up at Galapagos, Makor and other halls, and at New York City Opera’s Vox showcase in 2009. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/arts/gal/gal-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 614, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': No