 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rice, Susan E', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirk, Ron', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carney, Jay', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T00:40:21+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/4ed48185-34d8-5f5c-8d8f-2d2a4df33781', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/4ed48185-34d8-5f5c-8d8f-2d2a4df33781'}, {'abstract': 'CBS, Warner Brothers Television and the show’s executive producer, Chuck Lorre, said, “We are profoundly concerned for his health and well-being, and support his decision.”', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/from-artsbeat-two-and-a-half-men-on-hiatus-as-sheen-enters-rehab/', 'snippet': 'CBS, Warner Brothers Television and the show’s executive producer, Chuck Lorre, said, “We are profoundly concerned for his health and well-being, and support his decision.”', 'lead_paragraph': ' Dave Itzkoff and Bill Carter write about CBS and Warner Brothers Television’s announcement on Friday that their sitcom “Two and a Half Men” would take a production hiatus, as co-star Charlie Sheen enters a rehabilitation center. In a brief statement issued by the network and the studio, as well as\xa0Chuck Lorre, the comedy’s creator and executive producer, they said, “We are profoundly concerned for his health and well-being, and support his decision.” The statement did not disclose the reason for Mr. Sheen’s entering rehab. On Thursday, the actor was taken by ambulance to Cedars-Sinai Medical Center in Los Angeles. His publicist said at the time that Mr. Sheen had severe abdominal pains resulting from a hernia condition, and a friend of the actor’s said he had been laughing too hard at a television show.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From ArtsBeat: ‘Two and a Half Men’ on Hiatus', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheen, Charlie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Warner Brothers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T00:44:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc1008b3-56a8-5343-ae29-557405c2e734', 'word_count': 141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc1008b3-56a8-5343-ae29-557405c2e734'}, {'abstract': 'Big boxes and productivity.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/the-wal-mart-decade/', 'snippet': 'Big boxes and productivity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve been putting some material together for textbook revision, and found myself looking at European versus US productivity performance over the past couple of decades. I sort of knew the facts here, but this recent paper by Bart van Ark (pdf) seemed to me to make the points especially clearly, and I found myself rolling my own versions of some of his numbers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Wal-Mart Decade', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T00:46:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname