 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T00:57:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9e61421-4dfc-5984-8ab8-e7867363e3f6', 'word_count': 892, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9e61421-4dfc-5984-8ab8-e7867363e3f6'}, {'abstract': 'Studies show short-term benefits for severely depressed patients, but no evidence of any benefit after 30 days.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/29/health/29shock.html', 'snippet': 'Studies show short-term benefits for severely depressed patients, but no evidence of any benefit after 30 days.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A federal advisory panel was sharply divided on Friday about whether to recommend that regulators designate electroshock devices as high risk. The change could increase oversight on a controversial treatment used by about 100,000 Americans a year.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'F.D.A. Panel Is Split on Electroshock Risks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'F.D.A. Panel Is Split On Electroshock Risks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Depression (Mental)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Food and Drug Administration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electroshock Therapy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T00:58:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Duff Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Duff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0e4ec8f-c234-5aeb-a889-5a58247ebcbc', 'word_count': 776, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0e4ec8f-c234-5aeb-a889-5a58247ebcbc'}, {'abstract': 'At Helen Keller Services for the Blind, one longtime employee is taking her leave after 49 years, while a newcomer takes her place.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/nyregion/30open.html', 'snippet': 'At Helen Keller Services for the Blind, one longtime employee is taking her leave after 49 years, while a newcomer takes her place.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SALLY DIMICELI’S CAREER began 49 years ago at the Helen Keller Services for the Blind on Willoughby Street in Downtown Brooklyn, when, at age 18, she got a job as a cashier. She worked in that capacity until 1994, when she was promoted into the fund-raising and development position she has now.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/nyregion/30shut/30shut-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chronicle of a Changing City', 'kicker': 'Shut & Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chronicle of a Changing City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Keller, Helen, Services for the Blind', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Downtown Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T01:02:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corey Kilgannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilgannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96e575d0-7b88-5a67-8c41-f2df69b5a725', 'word_count': 382, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96e575d0-7b88-5a67-8c41-f2df69b5a725'}, {'abstract': 'Louisville running back Bilal Powell gained 1,405 yards and averaged 6.1 yards per carry in his first year as a full-time starter.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/28/q-and-a-with-louisvilles-bilal-powell/', 'snippet': 'Louisville running back Bilal Powell gained 1,405 yards and averaged 6.1 yards per carry in his first year as a full-time starter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Louisville running back Bilal Powell emerged on the college football scene as a senior, gaining 1,405 yards and averaging 6.1 yards per carry in his first year as a full-time starter. As physical as he is on the field, he’s equally soft-spoken away from the game. The Footballguys.com staff writer and Rookie Scouting Portfolio author Matt Waldman thought Powell was one of the more impressive runners during Senior Bowl practices in Mobile, Ala. This is his interview with Powell for Draftguys.com. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q. and A. With Louisville’s Bilal Powell', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Draft and Recruitment (Sports)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Louisville', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T01:02:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Waldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/44750d79-2c19-506f-b164-47f4b42c86ee', 'word_count': 882, 'uri': 'nyt://article