Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By William C. Rhoden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Rhoden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c16b7b7-67e4-5eb6-a895-c946291d7ae5', 'word_count': 1073, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c16b7b7-67e4-5eb6-a895-c946291d7ae5'}, {'abstract': 'Apple has managed logic-defying growth via its combination of popular hardware, software and services. But its formula may face longer-term challenges.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/business/30unbox.html', 'snippet': 'Apple has managed logic-defying growth via its combination of popular hardware, software and services. But its formula may face longer-term challenges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SIZE is the enemy of growth. It is one of the unwritten laws of business, a matter of simple percentages. After all, when a company has $1 billion in yearly sales, an extra $1 billion doubles its size. Add $1 billion in new business to a $10 billion-a-year company, and it amounts to just 10 percent growth. The size-growth tradeoff seems inevitable, an inescapable force like gravity.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/business/UNBOX/UNBOX-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 662, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/business/UNBOX/UNBOX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/business/UNBOX/UNBOX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Power of the Platform at Apple', 'kicker': 'Unboxed', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Power Of the Platform', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T19:13:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Lohr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lohr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3c1806a-e0bf-588b-8b35-8283122f558c', 'word_count': 1092, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3c1806a-e0bf-588b-8b35-8283122f558c'}, {'abstract': 'The return to Haiti of the former dictator Jean Claude "Baby Doc" Duvalier has encouraged former political prisoners to file charges for illegal imprisonment and torture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/world/1248069597874/tortured-under-baby-doc.html', 'snippet': 'The return to Haiti of the former dictator Jean Claude "Baby Doc" Duvalier has encouraged former political prisoners to file charges for illegal imprisonment and torture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The return to Haiti of the former dictator Jean Claude "Baby Doc" Duvalier has encouraged former political prisoners to file charges for illegal imprisonment and torture.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/29/world/11132_1_duval_75x75.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/29/world/11132_1_duval_75x75.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': "Tortured Under 'Baby Doc'", 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duvalier, Jean-Claude', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Haiti', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duvalier, Francois', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T19:13:43+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'John Fiege|Lara Petusky Coger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'Fiege', 'lastname': 'Lara', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/a34c15e7-3883-5984-b34c-789bdd05c6b6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/a34c15e7-3883-5984-b34c-789bdd05c6b6'}, {'abstract': 'Looking back on a meeting with the “most out” gay Ugandan, the country’s leading gay rights crusader, before his death.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/weekinreview/30gettleman.html', 'snippet': 'Looking back on a meeting with the “most out” gay Ugandan, the country’s leading gay rights crusader, before his death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NAIROBI, Kenya — It was late at night and totally dark when I first met David Kato. He had been described to me as “the most out” gay Ugandan and the country’s leading gay rights crusader, reviled by many, revered by a small few \x97 but definitely well known. So I was a bit surprised when he suggested that we conduct our interview in an empty lot behind a disco, down a dark gravel road.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/weekinreview/GETTLEMAN/GETTLEMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 688, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/weekinreview/GETTLEMAN/GETTLEMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/weekinreview/GETTLEMAN/GETTLEMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Remembering David Kato, a Gay Ugandan and a Marked Man', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'AFRICA: Remembering a Marked Man', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hate Crimes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kato, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Uganda', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T19:14:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeffrey Gettleman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gettleman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81659b67-3b42-580f-933c-10db127e8c91', 'word_count': 679, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81659b67-3b42-580f-933c-10db127e8c91'}, {'abstract': 'In the wider world of sports, Shaun White is the second most powerful athlete, but he is favored to win a fourth consecutive gold medal at the X Games on Sunday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/sports/30xgames.html', 'snippet': 'In the wider world of sports, Shaun White is the second most powerful athlete, but he is favored to win a fourth consecutive gold medal at the X Games on Sunday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ASPEN, Colo. \x97 In the wider world of sports, Shaun White is the second most powerful athlete, ahead of Tiger Woods, Tom Brady and LeBron James, according to Bloomberg BusinessWeek. Only Peyton Manning ranked higher than White, a two-time Olympic gold medalist, in the magazine’s annual Power 100 survey published last week.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/sports/XGAMES1/XGAMES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/30/sports/XGAMES1/XGAMES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/sports/XGAMES1/XGAMES1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/sports/XGAMES1/XGAMES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/sports/XGAMES1/XGAMES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'White Is Adapting to Less Than Perfect', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'White Is Adapting To Less Than Perfect', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lago, Scotty', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snowboarding', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'X Games', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'White, Shaun', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T19:25:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Higgins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Higgins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/53f12c2c-022d-5275-b817-8b791ea0656c', 'word_count': 989, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53f12c2c-022d-5275-b817-8b791ea0656c'}, {'abstract': 'Optimism about the state of the union is all well and good, but presidents can’t risk appearing ignorant of reality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/weekinreview/30stolberg.html', 'snippet': 'Optimism about the state of the union is all well and good, but presidents can’t risk appearing ignorant of reality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON —  Hours before President Obama delivered his State of the Union address, a senior adviser to the president confided that his tone would be “self-consciously optimistic.” And so it was. ', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/weekinreview/STOHL1/STOHL1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 154, 'width': 265, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/weekinreview/STOHL1/STOHL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/weekinreview/STOHL1/STOHL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'And Now, the Cheerleader in Chief', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'And Now, the Cheerleader in Chief', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T19:26:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheryl Gay Stolberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheryl', 'middlename': 'Gay', 'lastname': 'Stolberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f2e89c0-bee1-5b83-903b-cf485f91ce56', 'word_count': 1201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f2e89c0-bee1-5b83-903b-cf485f91ce56'}, {'abstract': 'Some see a conflict of interest for the ESPN broadcaster Erin Andrews in her deal to endorse Reebok.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/sports/30espn.html', 'snippet': 'Some see a conflict of interest for the ESPN broadcaster Erin Andrews in her deal to endorse Reebok.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When ESPN’s Erin Andrews reported that Texas Christian players were slipping in their new Nike cleats during the Rose Bowl on New Year’s Day, the detail seemed typical of the colorful tidbits that sideline reporters often provide to viewers.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/sports/SUB-ESPN/SUB-ESPN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 838, 'width': 886, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Andrews Faces Questions About a Deal With Reebok', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Andrews Faces Questions About a Deal With Reebok', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Andrews, Erin (1978- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosenstiel, Tom', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Reebok International Ltd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dorfman, Bob', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NIKE Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nantz, Jim', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ESPN', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T19:37:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Thomas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aea8f1f1-7ddd-5ebb-92cb-cbf7793bf3f8', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aea8f1f1-7ddd-5ebb-92cb-cbf7793bf3f8'}, {'abstract': 'A bombing in Moscow yanks Russia’s president back from efforts to seek a bright future with the West.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/weekinreview/30levy.html', 'snippet': 'A bombing in Moscow yanks Russia’s president back from efforts to seek a bright future with the West.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — Last week was to have heralded a defining moment for Russia’s forward-looking, Obama-admiring, iPad-toting president, Dmitri A. Medvedev. He would escape the shadow of his bare-knuckled mentor, Prime Minister Vladimir V. Putin, by giving the keynote speech at the World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland. What better way for Mr. Medvedev to show that his country had an alluring future, while also burnishing his own credentials as a reformer?', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/weekinreview/LEVYSUB/LEVYSUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 450, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/weekinreview/LEVYSUB/LEVYSUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/weekinreview/LEVYSUB/LEVYSUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Medvedev, From Davos to Reality', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'RUSSIA: From Davos to Reality', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Davos (Switzerland)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Economic Forum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Medvedev, Dmitri A', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moscow (Russia)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T19:43:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clifford J. Levy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clifford', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Levy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab2cdd23-3895-5be9-b24b-de40e58a2d00', 'word_count': 1056, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab2cdd23-3895-5be9-b24b-de40e58a2d00'}, {'abstract': 'Jokes from TV comedy show monologues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/weekinreview/30laugh.html', 'snippet': 'Jokes from TV comedy show monologues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JAY LENO', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Laugh Lines', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'LAUGH LINES', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T19:47:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f264ad0-3863-525a-9a7a-b8c012eff9c1', 'word_count': 194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f264ad0-3863-525a-9a7a-b8c012eff9c1'}, {'abstract': 'Twenty-five years ago on Friday, on an unusually cold morning, the space shuttle Challenger exploded shortly after liftoff, killing all seven astronauts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/weekinreview/30backthen.html', 'snippet': 'Twenty-five years ago on Friday, on an unusually cold morning, the space shuttle Challenger exploded shortly after liftoff, killing all seven astronauts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Twenty-five years ago on Friday, on an unusually cold morning, the space shuttle Challenger exploded shortly after liftoff, killing all seven astronauts. Richard Feynman, the Nobel Prize-winning physicist and a member of the presidential commission investigating the disaster, quickly zeroed in the cause: a failure of the O-rings, seals designed to prevent hot gases from leaking through the joint on the rocket booster. Here’s how he did it, as The Times’s Philip M. Boffey reported two weeks after the accident.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Back Then: 1986', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'BACK THEN: 1986', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T19:49:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/912bd28e-144e-52cf-9f6b-14f4fdf13a9c', 'word_count': 150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/912bd28e-144e-52cf-9f6b-14f4fdf13a9c'}, {'abstract': '11,500: The average annual number of people treated in hospital emergency rooms from 1990 to 2006 for injuries suffered while shoveling snow.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/weekinreview/30primenumber.html', 'snippet': '11,500: The average annual number of people treated in hospital emergency rooms from 1990 to 2006 for injuries suffered while shoveling snow.', 'lead_paragraph': '11,500: The average annual number of people treated in hospital emergency rooms from 1990 to 2006 for injuries suffered while shoveling snow, a study in this month’s issue of The American Journal of Emergency Medicine reports. Men were most frequently hurt. Injuries to the lower back accounted for slightly more than one-third of emergency room visits. Twenty percent of the injuries were the result of slips and falls; 15 percent, from being struck by a shovel. Nearly 7 percent of the visits were “ cardiac-related,: including all 1,647 deaths.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Prime Number', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'PRIME NUMBER', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T19:50:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'WeekInReview', 'section_name': 'Week in Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fac562cb-dd39-5dc8-aa07-c1ab8d5f10b9', 'word_count': 89, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fac562cb-dd39-5dc8-aa07-c1ab8d5f10b9'}, {'abstract': 'Scientists and government officials involved in studying gun use say the influence of the National Rifle Association has all but choked off money for such work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/weekinreview/30fight.html', 'snippet': 'Scientists and government officials involved in studying gun use say the influence of the National Rifle Association has all but choked off money for such work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scientists and government officials involved in studying gun use say the influence of the National Rifle Association has all but choked off money for such work.', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', '