 Party-fueled fiscal austerity, have discovered that most Americans prefer compromise to confrontation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/opinion/30rich.html', 'snippet': 'The Republicans, who sold themselves as the uncompromising champions of Tea Party-fueled fiscal austerity, have discovered that most Americans prefer compromise to confrontation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ANY lingering doubts about Barack Obama’s determination to appropriate Ronald Reagan’s political spirit evaporated just before the State of the Union. No American brand is more associated with Reagan than General Electric, and it was that corporation’s chief executive, Jeffrey Immelt, who popped up as the president’s new wingman when the White House rolled out its latest jobs initiative on Jan. 21. Obama’s speech on Tuesday, with its celebration of the nation’s can-do capitalist ingenuity, moved him still closer to Reagan’s sweet spot as a national cheerleader. The president even offered a remix of the old Reagan-era G.E. jingle “We bring good things to life” \x97 now traded up to the grander “We do big things.” ', 'print_section': 'WK', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/opinion/30richimg/30richimg-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/30/opinion/30richimg/30richimg-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/opinion/30richimg/30richimg-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/30/opinion/30richimg/30richimg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/30/opinion/30richimg/30richimg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Tea Party Wags the Dog', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Tea Party Wags the Dog', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea Party Movement', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bachmann, Michele M', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T21:42:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Rich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4b8b63f-89f7-5175-b915-92c19d0c0ee2', 'word_count': 1570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4b8b63f-89f7-5175-b915-92c19d0c0ee2'}, {'abstract': 'The consensus among the Arab elite at the World Economic Forum was that the protests in Egypt would end the nation’s near monarchical regime.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/business/global/30davos.html', 'snippet': 'The consensus among the Arab elite at the World Economic Forum was that the protests in Egypt would end the nation’s near monarchical regime.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DAVOS, Switzerland — The unrest engulfi