stan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-29T23:55:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Livingston, Alicia Cheng and Sarah Gephart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Livingston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Alicia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cheng', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gephart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d14f72bd-46d0-5726-b600-cabc4f726e63', 'word_count': 252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d14f72bd-46d0-5726-b600-cabc4f726e63'}, {'abstract': 'Abraham Lincoln’s stepmother, Sarah Lincoln, helped make him what he was.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/29/lincolns-other-mother/', 'snippet': 'Abraham Lincoln’s stepmother, Sarah Lincoln, helped make him what he was.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the evening of Jan. 30, 1861, a slow freight train chugged into the small hamlet of Charleston, Ill., having completed a 12-mile run from Mattoon. Or nearly 12 miles — the train didn’t quite make it all the way to the station. A few people straggled out of the caboose and trudged through slush and ice toward the depot, where a gaggle of townsfolk loitered. To their astonishment, they realized that the tall man coming toward them, wearing a shawl, was Abraham Lincoln. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lincoln’s Other Mother', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-30T00:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ted Widmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ted', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Widmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/68eea9a0-7648-5a17-9f90-9f9df5837642', 'word_count': 1724, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68eea9a0-7648-5a17-9f90-9f9df5837642'}, {'abstract': 'For some cyclists, snow is no reason to lock up the bike; riding in it is a challenge and even, dare they say it, fun.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/29/spokes-two-wheels-no-fear-in-slush-or-powder/', 'snippet': 'For some cyclists, snow is no reason to lock up the bike; riding in it is a challenge and even, dare they say it, fun.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pascal Sauvayre hopped his front tire through several inches of freshly fallen powder, stopping in front of a small group of cyclists gathered in the morning darkness at the southeast end of Central Park.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spokes | Two Wheels, No Fear, in Slush or Powder', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snow and Snowstorms', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': 