Symphony presented its first Wallcast concert at the New World Center in Miami Beach.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/arts/music/31park.html', 'snippet': 'The New World Symphony presented its first Wallcast concert at the New World Center in Miami Beach.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MIAMI BEACH \x97 On Friday night at the New World Center here the New World Symphony, a top-notch training orchestra, presented its first free Wallcast concert. Remember that name, Wallcast, because it is going to catch on.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/31/arts/PARK/PARK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 362, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/31/arts/PARK/PARK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 362}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/31/arts/PARK/PARK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 581, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dutchman Flies Right Off the Wall', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Dutchman Flies Right Off the Wall', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thomas, Michael Tilson', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New World Symphony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-30T23:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dbdb303-c087-547e-918f-5d5db1c937d7', 'word_count': 607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dbdb303-c087-547e-918f-5d5db1c937d7'}, {'abstract': 'The third India Art Summit finds a lot of money changing hands, and a patron opens a new museum.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/arts/design/31summit.html', 'snippet': 'The third India Art Summit finds a lot of money changing hands, and a patron opens a new museum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW DELHI \x97 The third India Art Summit, an art fair that ended last week, took up more than 90,000 square feet of Pragati Maidan, the main public exhibition hall here in India’s capital. Eighty-four galleries were represented, showing the work of 500 mostly Indian modern and contemporary artists. Organizers reported 128,000 visitors over the fair’s three-day run, and millions of dollars changed hands, with a couple of buyers each taking home more than $2 million in art. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/31/arts/SUMMIT/SUMMIT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/31/arts/SUMMIT/SUMMIT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/31/arts/SUMMIT/SUMMIT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 619, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/31/arts/SUMMIT/SUMMIT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/31/arts/SUMMIT/SUMMIT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In India, a Busy Fair and a Spirited Art Scene', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In India, a Busy Fair and a Spirited Art Scene', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-30T23:03:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Somini Sengupta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Somini', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sengupta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c306e35d-0a57-54b4-9032-b387db6e8c9e', 'word_count': 1155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c306e35d-0a57-54b4-9032-b387db6e8c9e'}, {'abstract': '“Like Crazy,” a romance directed by Drake Doremus and starring Felicity Jones, won the grand jury prize for an American dramatic film at the Sundance Film Festival awards.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/movies/31arts-SUNDANCEFILM_BRF.html', 'snippet': '“Like Crazy,” a romance directed by Drake Doremus and starring Felicity Jones, won the grand jury prize for an American dramatic film at the Sundance Film Festival awards.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Like Crazy,” a romance directed by Drake Doremus and starring Felicity Jones, above, (and purchased by Paramount Pictures and Indian Paintbrush), won the grand jury prize for an American dramatic film at the Sundance Film Festival awards, announced on Saturday night. Peter D. Richardson’s “How to Die in Oregon,” a searing look at assisted suicide, won the grand jury prize for an American documentary. In the world categories Anne Sewitsky’s drama “Happy, Happy,” about a homemaker who struggles to keep her emotions in check, and Danfung Dennis’s Afghanistan documentary “Hell and Back Again” won the jury prizes. The audience awards for American films went to Maryam Keshavarz’s “Circumstance,” a look at a teenage lesbian in modern-day Iran, and Cindy Meehl’s “Buck,” a documentary about the cowboy who was the real-life inspiration for the 1998 drama “The Horse Whisperer.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sundance Films Honored', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sundance Films Honored', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-30T23:13:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/668b57f1-1fdd-5134-b48d-0a3885e5e56c', 'word_count': 141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/668b57f1-1fdd-5134-b48d-0a3885e5e56c'}, {'abstract': '“So I shot him.” That is the first line and the title of a 19-page unpublished short story by Dashiell Hammett that Strand Magazine is planning to publish next month.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/books/31arts-UNKNOWNHAMME_BRF.html', 'snippet': '“So I shot him.” That is the first line and the title of a 19-page unpublished short story by Dashiell Hammett that Strand Magazine is planning to publish next month.', 'lead_paragraph': '“So I shot him.” That is the first line and the title of a 19-page unpublished short story by Dashiell Hammett that Strand Magazine is planning to publish next month. “Despite being a thriller, this story has some psychological elements that are not seen in a typical Hammett,” said Andrew F. Gulli, the Strand’s publisher, who found 15 unpublished Hammett stories in the archives of the Harry Ransom Center at the University of Texas, Austin. Hammett, who died in 1961, is best known for hard-boiled thrillers like “The Thin Man” and “The Maltese Falcon,” which were made into Hollywood movies. Mr. Gulli specializes in finding unknown works by writers; in the past two years he has published stories by Mark Twain, P. G. Wodehouse, Graham Greene and Agatha Christie.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Unknown Hammett Story Sees the Light of Day', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unknown Hammett Story Sees the Light of Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hammett, Dashiell', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-30T23:13:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/f287ff6c-78ca-5326-a6df-35763866ce1f', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f287ff6c-78ca-5326-a6df-35763866ce1f'}, {'abstract': 'The horror film “The Rite,” starring Anthony Hopkins as an exorcist, was the top performer at the North American box office over the weekend with about $15 million in ticket sales.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/movies/31arts-THERITEHASAS_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'The horror film “The Rite,” starring Anthony Hopkins as an exorcist, was the top performer at the North American box office over the weekend with about $15 million in ticket sales.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The horror film “The Rite,” starring Anthony Hopkins, left, as an exorcist, was the top performer at the North American box office over the weekend with about $15 million in ticket sales. Unlike some horror movies, the film mostly attracted viewers over the age of 25, The Associated Press reported. “No Strings Attached,” starring Natalie Portman and Ashton Kutcher, was second with sales of roughly $13.7 million for a new total of $39.7 million, according to Hollywood.com, which tracks box office results. “The Mechanic” and “The Green Hornet” tied for third place with $11.5 million in sales. Other films got a bump from last week’s Oscar nominations, including “The King’s Speech,” which earned $11.1 million in its 10th week of release for a new total of $72.2 million; the film was shown in an additional 900 theaters. And “True Grit,” another Oscar hopeful, sold $7.6 million in tickets as it approached a total of $150 million.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Rite’ Has a Solid Opening at the Box Office', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘The Rite’ Has a Solid Opening at the Box Office', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-30T23:16:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd8a129a-51c1-528b-bebc-f073cbdc14e8', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd8a129a-51c1-528b-bebc-f073cbdc14e8'}, {'abstract': 'St. Martin’s Press, part of Macmillan, has converted President Obama’s speech at the Tucson memorial service on Jan. 12 into e-book form.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/books/31arts-OBAMASTUCSON_BRF.html', 'snippet': 'St. Martin’s Press, part of Macmillan, has converted President Obama’s speech at the Tucson memorial service on Jan. 12 into e-book form.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anyone who missed President Obama’s speech at the Tucson memorial service on Jan. 12 can read it online, watch a video clip on YouTube or listen to snippets on NPR. And those glued to their Nooks and Kindles can now download it as an e-book. St. Martin’s Press, part of Macmillan, has converted the speech into e-book form, making it available through Amazon, BN.com and other retailers for 99 cents. A spokesman for St. Martin’s said the e-book, for sale on Monday, would be the only downloadable version of the speech, which is in the public domain and available to any publisher. St. Martin’s has decided to name the e-book “What Is Best in America,” after Mr. Obama’s description of the people who were killed by a gunman on Jan. 8. “The six people who lost their lives on Saturday,” Mr. Obama said in the speech, “they too represented what is best in America.” Publishers have experimented in recent months with shorter digital offerings in e-book form, including essays and short stories.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama’s Tucson Speech Is Published as E-Book', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obama’s Tucson Speech Is Published as E-Book', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronic Books and Readers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "St Martin's Press", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Speeches and Statements', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-30T23:18:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/84e96e32-685d-54d1-9359-26931dcf930f', 'word_count': 174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84e96e32-685d-54d1-9359-26931dcf930f'}, {'abstract': 'Aaron Rodgers isn’t the first Chico native to appear on his or her sport’s biggest stage.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/rodgerss-town-has-history-of-success/', 'snippet': 'Aaron Rodgers isn’t the first Chico native to appear on his or her sport’s biggest stage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHICO, Calif. — The Chico native Aaron Rodgers isn’t the only former Pleasant Valley High School quarterback to have graced an N.F.L. roster.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rodgers’s Town Has History of Success', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodgers, Aaron', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-30T23:22:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf3416ba-06be-5013-bbd4-2891ac2fb7b0', 'word_count': 327, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf3416ba-06be-5013-bbd4-2891ac2fb7b0'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Alvarado’s last-second shot from well beyond halfcourt gave the Jaspers a stunning victory at Marist.', 'web_url': 'https://thequad.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/30/manhattan-wins-from-way-downtown/', 'snippet': 'Michael Alvarado’s last-second shot from well beyond halfcourt gave the Jaspers a stunning victory at Marist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was an exciting weekend in college basketball, with No. 1 Ohio State and No. 2 Pittsburgh barely escaping on the road and No. 3 Duke getting manhandled by St. John’s at Madison Square Garden. But the most astonishing moment came at the end of the game between Manhattan and Marist. That’s right, Manhattan and Marist. The Red Foxes held a 59-57 lead in the final seconds when Manhattan’s Michael Alvarado got the ball on an inbounds pass with two seconds left, began dribbling up the court and let go of the ball well before the halfcourt line. It banked in off the glass, giving the Jaspers a 60-59 victory and Alvarado a shining moment in January.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Manhattan Wins, From Way Downtown', 'kicker': 'The Quad', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-30T23:22:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Connor Ennis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Connor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ennis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/057a9923-34a9-5464-8789-6b66d1c4d276', 'word_count': 117, 'uri': 'nyt://article/057a9923-34a9-5464-8789-6b66d1c4d276'}, {'abstract': 'Photos of Christopher Wheeldon’s “Polyphonia” and “For the Love of Duke” by Susan Stroman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/01/31/arts/dance/20110131-ballet.html', 'snippet': 'P