t', 'value': 'Reform and Reorganization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T03:10:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/a57eb835-e3f7-5ba6-ae35-3f0e37b641aa', 'word_count': 515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a57eb835-e3f7-5ba6-ae35-3f0e37b641aa'}, {'abstract': 'Retailers are increasingly offering free shipping, which encourages consumers to spend a little on this site, a little on that.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/business/media/31drill.html', 'snippet': 'Retailers are increasingly offering free shipping, which encourages consumers to spend a little on this site, a little on that.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the most recent holiday season, consumers who clicked on search ads, like those accompanying Google results, performed 87 percent more transactions with online retailers than they had a year before. But the average online shopping basket was 10 percent smaller, according to Kenshoo, a maker of search-engine marketing software that analyzed three million transactions with its clients, generally large online retailers. In other words, people are spreading their money thinner online.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/31/business/31Drill/31Drill-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 826, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'More Transactions, on Smaller Orders', 'kicker': 'Drilling Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More Transactions, on Smaller Orders', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Behavior', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T03:14:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Mindlin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mindlin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92dd2fc9-067f-52eb-847b-4e78ce09b1a7', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92dd2fc9-067f-52eb-847b-4e78ce09b1a7'}, {'abstract': 'Opponents in Northern California say the smart meters, seen as a crucial step toward creating a “green grid,” are a threat to privacy and health.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/science/earth/31meters.html', 'snippet': 'Opponents in Northern California say the smart meters, seen as a crucial step toward creating a “green grid,” are a threat to privacy and health.', 'lead_paragraph': 'INVERNESS PARK, Calif. — Pacific Gas and Electric’s campaign to introduce wireless smart meters in Northern California is facing fierce opposition from an eclectic mix of Tea Party conservatives and left-leaning individualists who say the meters threaten their liberties and their health.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/31/us/Meters1_span/Meters-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/01/31/us/Meters1_span/Meters-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/31/us/Meters1_span/Meters-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, 