dd Gray Wolf to List of Canines in Africa', 'kicker': 'Observatory', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Add Gray Wolf to List Of Canines in Africa', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wolves', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T17:54:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sindya N. Bhanoo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sindya', 'middlename': 'N.', 'lastname': 'Bhanoo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd8b5d56-9e6d-5618-b0ef-588b299c0681', 'word_count': 270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd8b5d56-9e6d-5618-b0ef-588b299c0681'}, {'abstract': 'The Great Recession hasn’t been a springtime for authoritarians or theocrats.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/egypt-a-case-for-optimism/', 'snippet': 'The Great Recession hasn’t been a springtime for authoritarians or theocrats.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the midst of last year’s Iranian uprisings, I made the following point about the likely political consequences of the Great Recession:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt: A Case For Optimism', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T17:58:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ffa81067-471e-512a-a746-4b96a4d4d37d', 'word_count': 783, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ffa81067-471e-512a-a746-4b96a4d4d37d'}, {'abstract': 'A new campaign for Mount Gay rum carries the theme “Sailing spoken here,” which doubles as the Web address for a microsite.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/business/media/31adnewsletter1.html', 'snippet': 'A new campaign for Mount Gay rum carries the theme “Sailing spoken here,” which doubles as the Web address for a microsite.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Given how fiercely wintry winter has been in much of the country, the idea of sailing away someplace sounds pretty inviting. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/31/business/media/31advertisingnewsletter1-inline/31advertisingnewsletter1-inline-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 837, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/01/31/business/media/31advertisingnewsletter1-inline/31advertisingnewsletter1-inline-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/01/31/business/media/31advertisingnewsletter1-inline/31advertisingnewsletter1-inline-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Yo-Ho-Ho and a Bottle of ...You Know', 'kicker': 'Campaign Spotlight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Yo-Ho-Ho and a Bottle of ...You Know', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rum', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sailboats and Sailing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T17:59:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8154a05-9481-5270-aa6e-ae48f83e458a', 'word_count': 1221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8154a05-9481-5270-aa6e-ae48f83e458a'}, {'abstract': 'The agency did not admit any wrongdoing and accusations of bias were left unaddressed.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/s-b-a-settles-discrimination-suit/', 'snippet': 'The agency did not admit any wrongdoing and accusations of bias were left unaddressed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back in April, The Agenda reported on a lawsuit against the Small Business Administration, brought by a would-be venture capitalist seeking access to government-backed investment funds. The S.B.A. recently reached a settlement — actually, it was in November, but I’ve been on paternity leave (twins!) — with Diamond Ventures, an Atlanta-based fund run by C. Earl Peek, an African-American who claimed discrimination after the S.B.A. rejected Diamond’s application for a small-business investment company license. But while the settlement is good news for Mr. Peek and his partners, it does not address what Diamond claimed was a far-reaching pattern of bias that affected a whole class of black and other minority investors.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'S.B.A. Settles Discrimination Suit', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minorities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venture Capital', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Small Business Administration', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T17:59:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robb Mandelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mandelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca53c3d8-4e10-592b-92f9-7e8ef7a0543c', 'word_count': 649, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca53c3d8-4e10-592b-92f9-7e8ef7a0543c'}, {'abstract': 'A seemingly serviceable and unsecured motor scooter has been parked for months on Sutton Place South without being driven away, ticketed, towed or, miraculously, stolen.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/the-curious-case-of-the-unscathed-scooter/', 'snippet': 'A seemingly serviceable and unsecured motor scooter has been parked for months on Sutton Place South without being driven away, ticketed, towed or, miraculously, stolen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 4:33 p.m. | It’s the stuff of urban legends: A seemingly serviceable and unsecured motor scooter has been parked for months on Sutton Place South without being driven away, ticketed, towed or, miraculously, stolen. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Curious Case of the Unscathed Scooter', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Motorcycles, Motor Bikes and Motorscooters', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parking', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T17:59:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/39a892df-5d09-5a4f-8911-2b268a71a651', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39a892df-5d09-5a4f-8911-2b268a71a651'}, {'abstract': 'The first ultra-trivial Super Bowl story to gain sentience, sprout legs and start toppling buildings last week was the drama surrounding the Packers’ team photo.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/nick-barnett-unfriends-america/', 'snippet': 'The first ultra-trivial Super Bowl story to gain sentience, sprout legs and start toppling buildings last week was the drama surrounding the Packers’ team photo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first ultra-trivial Super Bowl story to gain sentience, sprout legs and start toppling buildings last week was the drama surrounding the Packers’ team photo. The story so far: players on injured reserve were scheduled to arrive in Dallas too late to appear in the photo, the shoot was rescheduled, Aaron Rodgers criticized injured teammates who chose to rehabilitate outside of Green Bay, Jermichael Finley and Nick Barnett responded via Twitter, and the silliest controversy ever to swallow the football world whole was spawned.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nick Barnett Unfollows America', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Super Bowl', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodgers, Aaron', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Bay Packers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T18:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Tanier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fa5d722-7c77-5cda-b5cb-9113819b568c', 'word_count': 520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fa5d722-7c77-5cda-b5cb-9113819b568c'}, {'abstract': 'Egypt belongs with the Middle Eastern countries that have relatively few fossil fuel resources, rather than those that have them in abundance.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/egypt-oil-and-democracy/', 'snippet': 'Egypt belongs with the Middle Eastern countries that have relatively few fossil fuel resources, rather than those that have them in abundance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Revolutions are, by their very nature, difficult to predict. The unrest that gripped Eastern Europe and the Soviet Union from 1989 to 1992, and led to the fall of Communist governments there, was anticipated by few policymakers and political scientists in advance.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt, Oil and Democracy', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T18:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d245348e-b2a8-55b0-a2c9-2a45dd5cad05', 'word_count': 1239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d245348e-b2a8-55b0-a2c9-2a45dd5cad05'}, {'abstract': 'The planned retirement of the chief executive could be the catalyst for a deal, Credit Suisse analysts say.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/pall-shares-surge-on-sale-talk/', 'snippet': 'The planned retirement of the chief executive could be the catalyst for a deal, Credit Suisse analysts say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Might love lead to a takeover? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pall Shares Surge on Sale Talk', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pall Corp', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T18:02:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e01fbd12-5c66-59f7-84f4-1b23189e4792', 'word_count': 215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e01fbd12-5c66-59f7-84f4-1b23189e4792'}, {'abstract': 'A curator at the Victoria and Albert Museum in London has announced the discovery of performance footage on a production house’s Web site.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/found-footage-of-the-original-ballets-russes/', 'snippet': 'A curator at the Victoria and Albert Museum in London has announced the discovery of performance footage on a production house’s Web site.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our knowledge of Diaghilev’s Ballets Russes troupe — not the versions after his death in 1929 — comes from texts, sketches, photographs and of course dances handed down over the years. But no film of a performance by the original company was thought to exist. Now a curator at the Victoria and Albert Museum in London, where an exhibition on the company closed this month, has announced the discovery of less than a minute of performance footage on the Web site of the production house British Pathé. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Found: Footage of the Original Ballets Russes', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T18:03:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By THE NEW YORK TIMES', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/44b92b3a-ae5d-561b-9982-4bce99028441', 'word_count': 217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44b92b3a-ae5d-561b-9982-4bce99028441'}, {'abstract': 'A curator at the Victoria and Albert Museum in London has announced the discovery of performance footage on a production house’s Web site.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/found-footage-of-the-original-ballets-russes/', 'snippet': 'A curator at the Victoria and Albert Museum in London has announced the discovery of performance footage on a production house’s Web site.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our knowledge of Diaghilev’s Ballets Russes troupe — not the versions after his death in 1929 — comes from texts, sketches, photographs and of course dances handed down over the years. But no film of a performance by the original company was thought to exist. Now a curator at the Victoria and Albert Museum in London, where an exhibition on the company closed this month, has announced the discovery of less than a minute of performance footage on the Web site of the production house British Pathé. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Found: Footage of the Original Ballets Russes', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diaghilev, Serge', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T18:03:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/477f24d9-259f-54c7-9c62-40e54d6d957f', 'word_count': 217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/477f24d9-259f-54c7-9c62-40e54d6d957f'}, {'abstract': 'Cranberry juice is a popular home remedy for treating and preventing bladder infections. But exactly how and why it seems to help remains a scientific mystery, explains Dr. Abigail Zuger.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/studying-cranberry-juice-for-infections/', 'snippet': 'Cranberry juice is a popular home remedy for treating and preventing bladder infections. But exactly how and why it seems to help remains a scientific mystery, explains Dr. Abigail Zuger.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cranberry juice is a popular home remedy for treating and preventing bladder infections. But exactly how and why it seems to help remains a scientific mystery, explains Dr. Abigail Zuger.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Studying Cranberry Juice for Infections', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Complementary Medicine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T18:07:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/d560d012-d376-5b09-a163-8432d00a0f5f', 'word_count': 177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d560d012-d376-5b09-a163-8432d00a0f5f'}, {'abstract': 'The Steelers unearth them even in the draft’s late rounds, keep plenty in the pipeline and make them sit until they absorb the details of a difficult defense.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/02/01/sports/football/01linebackers.html', 'snippet': 'The Steelers unearth them even in the draft’s late rounds, keep plenty in the pipeline and make them sit until they absorb the details of a difficult defense.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DALLAS \x97 Before he became one of the best linebackers in football, James Harrison was just another Pittsburgh Steelers project, a raw player plucked from practice squad anonymity so confused by the complexities of Dick LeBeau’s defense that he would stop cold in the middle of a play, with no idea where to run as the action continued around him.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/01/sports/y-jp-linebacker/y-jp-linebacker-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/02/01/sports/y-jp-linebacker/y-jp-linebacker-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/01/sports/y-jp-linebacker/y-jp-linebacker-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 721, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/01/sports/y-jp-linebacker/y-jp-linebacker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/01/sports/y-jp-linebacker/y-jp-linebacker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Grooming Linebackers the Steeler Way', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'FORGING LINEBACKERS\nTHE STEELER WAY', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Super Bowl', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Free Agents (Sports)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pittsburgh Steelers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Draft and Recruitment (Sports)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T18:08:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judy Battista', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Battista', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c34ce0e-56a0-5f66-8111-86c4848187e9', 'word_count': 1418, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c34ce0e-56a0-5f66-8111-86c4848187e9'}, {'abstract': 'Core inflation, expanded.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/screw-your-coreage-to-the-sticking-place/', 'snippet': 'Core inflation, expanded.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back when I first tried to explain the logic of focusing on core inflation rather than headline inflation, I emphasized the distinction between goods whose prices fluctuate all the time and those whose prices are revised only occasionally; it’s the latter category that is subject to inflation inertia, and therefore where you have to worry that inflation or deflation can get baked into the economy, and become hard to undo.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Screw Your Coreage to the Sticking Place', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T18:12:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e12b9de7-ca12-59fb-a51f-073350936b5b', 'word_count': 283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e12b9de7-ca12-59fb-a51f-073350936b5b'}, {'abstract': 'Following up readers’ comments and questions about the shingles vaccine.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/readers-questions-the-shingles-vaccine/', 'snippet': 'Following up readers’ comments and questions about the shingles vaccine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I didn’t realize how lucky I was, more than a year ago, when I simply trotted over to my family doctor’s office and got vaccinated against shingles. Having seen too many friends, younger and older, suffering with this painful disease, I considered immunization one of the benefits of turning 60, the age at which the vaccine is recommended. Happily, I didn’t have to wait: My doctor had a supply on hand, and my insurance paid for the shot.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Readers’ Questions: The Shingles Vaccine', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shingles (Disease)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T18:14:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/16795954-7056-5e23-a689-40a2d1112bb3', 'word_count': 997, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16795954-7056-5e23-a689-40a2d1112bb3'}, {'abstract': 'The new cars, equipped with the latest regenerative-braking technology, will begin competing even before two teams’ claims to the Lotus name are heard in court.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/ferrari-sauber-and-a-pair-of-lotuses-unveil-formula-one-cars/', 'snippet': 'The new cars, equipped with the latest regenerative-braking technology, will begin competing even before two teams’ claims to the Lotus name are heard in court.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the eve of the 2011 Formula One racing season, four teams have issued gleaming letters of intent.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ferrari, Sauber and a Pair of Lotuses Unveil Formula One Cars', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ferrari SpA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Formula One', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Group Lotus', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lotus Cars Ltd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Renault SA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T18:28:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qua