ph': 'PALM BEACH, Fla. — So you’re a hedge fund manager. You’ve been around for five years, have solid returns but took a beating during the financial crisis that reduced your assets base to a little more than $1 billion from $2 billion. How do you raise money?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Fund Managers, a Class in Raising Money', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T19:27:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Azam Ahmed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Azam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahmed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/e299fe9d-1123-552c-8f4a-de65436703c2', 'word_count': 412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e299fe9d-1123-552c-8f4a-de65436703c2'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers are trying to figure out how the New Caledonian crow became such an outlier, an avian savant.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/02/01/science/01angier.html', 'snippet': 'Researchers are trying to figure out how the New Caledonian crow became such an outlier, an avian savant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amid all the psychosocial caterwauling these days over the relative merits of tiger mothers and helicopter dads, allow me to make a pitch for the quietly dogged parenting style of the New Caledonian crow. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/01/science/01angierspan/01angierspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 318, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/02/01/science/0