ittle resemblance to the MoMA exhibition “On Line.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/02/01/arts/dance/01line.html', 'snippet': 'When dancers speak of line, they generally speak in terms that bear little resemblance to the MoMA exhibition “On Line.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Line in dance has two main meanings: one in space, the other in time. The Museum of Modern Art’s exhibition “On Line: Drawing Through the Twentieth Century,” which prominently reflects the institution’s new interest in dance, illustrates that. You’ve hardly arrived when you see a film of Loie Fuller’s 1897-99 “Danse Serpentine (II).” In this the performer is never static; the billowing lines she makes with her costume are in perpetual change. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/01/arts/JPLINE/JPLINE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 195, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/02/01/arts/JPLINE/JPLINE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 195}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/01/arts/JPLINE/JPLINE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 332, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/01/arts/JPLINE/JPLINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/01/arts/JPLINE/JPLINE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Line in Dance? Let’s Go Back to the Drawing Board', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Line in Dance? Let’s Go Back To the Drawing Board', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Modern Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T23:48:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e85266e-f63e-54b7-812c-11b553541d82', 'word_count': 1105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e85266e-f63e-54b7-812c-11b553541d82'}, {'abstract': 'A solid performance of a Prokofiev concerto by Vadim Gluzman was overshadowed by an account of Krzysztof Penderecki’s Serenade.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/02/01/arts/music/01orpheus.html', 'snippet': 'A solid performance of a Prokofiev concerto by Vadim Gluzman was overshadowed by an account of Krzysztof Penderecki’s Serenade.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ostensible drawing card at the Orpheus Chamber Orchestra’s concert at Carnegie Hall on Saturday evening was an account of Prokofiev’s Violin Concerto No. 2, with Vadim Gluzman as soloist. Yet as fine as Mr. Gluzman’s performance was, a workout that balanced thoughtfulness and visceral power, the most memorable performance of the evening came in an outlier: Krzysztof Penderecki’s Serenade (1997). ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/01/arts/sub-Orpheus/sub-Orpheus-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 656, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Violin Concerto Was the Attraction, but a Serenade Had Seductive Power', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Violin Concerto Was the Attraction, But a Serenade Had Seductive Power', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Orpheus Chamber Orchestra', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T23:51:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/699eec14-a931-5f15-87dd-4570a5f7c344', 'word_count': 379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/699eec14-a931-5f15-87dd-4570a5f7c344'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Plant and his Band of Joy played a wide-ranging set at the Beacon Theater, setting recent songs alongside traditional tunes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/02/01/arts/music/01plant.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Plant and his Band of Joy played a wide-ranging set at the Beacon Theater, setting recent songs alongside traditional tunes.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Revisiting musical roots, as maturing musicians often do, can lead to dutiful, preservationist renditions of old songs. But not for Robert Plant, 62, whose Band of Joy played on Sunday night at the Beacon Theater as if twanging and stomping were the route to revelation. The concert had nothing to do with period style and everything to do with spirit. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/01/arts/PLANT/PLANT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 658, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/01/arts/PLANT/PLANT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/01/arts/PLANT/PLANT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Offering a Range of Songs, While Prizing Spirit Over Style', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Offering a Range of Songs, While Prizing Spirit Over Style', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Plant, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T23:53:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/267a045c-ba3b-56ca-ad07-0882a3c39862', 'word_count': 471, 'uri': 'nyt://article/267a045c-ba3b-56ca-ad07-0882a3c39862'}, {'abstract': '“Post Mortem,” a “Frontline” report on PBS, highlights the widespread incompetence of coroners and the failure of governments to demand sound forensic pathology.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/02/01/arts/television/01postmortem.html', 'snippet': '“Post Mortem,” a “Frontline” report on PBS, highlights the widespread incompetence of coroners and the failure of governments to demand sound forensic pathology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let’s imagine that an intruder entered your home one night and fatally poisoned you. Let’s say that during the previous weeks or months you had evinced signs of emotional volatility. Perhaps you were even depressed. Let’s go further and presume that you lived in one of the many places in America experiencing state and municipal budget crises. The police suspect suicide, but because the coroner’s office is underfinanced and understaffed — maybe there isn’t even a place to refrigerate your body until someone can get around to dissecting your corpse — no autopsy is performed to confirm or invalidate those suspicions. A murderer is free and on to the next house and victim.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/01/arts/sub-postmortem/sub-postmortem-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/02/01/arts/sub-postmortem/sub-postmortem-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/01/arts/sub-postmortem/sub-postmortem-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bumbling in the Morgue: Blind, Drunk or Merely Incompetent Coroners', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bumbling in the Morgue: Blind, Drunk or Merely Incompetent Coroners', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Autopsies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forensic Science', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T23:55:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8d20be1-2fc8-5848-b4ad-dcee1ea1ba95', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8d20be1-2fc8-5848-b4ad-dcee1ea1ba95'}, {'abstract': '“It’s an important moment in the land of Lincoln,” Governor Patrick J. Quinn said as the standing-room only crowd cheered and snapped pictures. “We believe in civil rights and we believe in civil unions.”', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/01/31/illinois-governor-signs-civil-union-law/', 'snippet': '“It’s an important moment in the land of Lincoln,” Governor Patrick J. Quinn said as the standing-room only crowd cheered and snapped pictures. “We believe in civil rights and we believe in civil unions.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHICAGO – Hundreds of people packed into a downtown ballroom on Monday afternoon to watch Governor Patrick J. Quinn, a Democrat, sign a law making civil unions legal for same-sex couples in this state.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Illinois Governor Signs Civil Union Law', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quinn, Patrick J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Illinois', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-01-31T23:57:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Davey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://article/60e8ccc0-eed5-58d1-ace9-ef976b89f31b', 'word_count': 462, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60e8c